/* AP Psychology Practice Bank: 1333 original questions. Data file for practice.html */
window.AP_BANK = [{"id":"U0-001","section":"0.1","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Dr. Okafor is studying why some students become unusually irritable during exam season. She collects saliva samples to measure cortisol levels and uses brain scans to compare activity in the amygdala before and after exams. Dr. Okafor's approach best reflects which psychological perspective?","options":["Sociocultural","Behavioral","Humanistic","Biological"],"answer":3,"rationale":"Measuring stress hormones and brain activity explains behavior through physical processes in the body, which is the biological perspective. A sociocultural psychologist would focus on social expectations around exams instead."},{"id":"U0-002","section":"0.1","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Mariam regularly leaves her homework until the night before it is due. Four psychologists offer explanations for her behavior. Which explanation best reflects the cognitive perspective?","options":["Mariam's delaying expresses an unconscious conflict about authority figures that began in childhood.","Mariam believes she produces her best work under pressure, so she interprets starting early as wasted effort.","Mariam's friendship group treats last-minute work as normal, and she follows their expectations.","Mariam's delaying has been strengthened by the relief she feels each time she puts a task off."],"answer":1,"rationale":"The cognitive perspective focuses on how thoughts, beliefs and interpretations guide behavior. The explanation about relief strengthening the habit is behavioral, the unconscious conflict is psychodynamic, and the friendship-group explanation is sociocultural."},{"id":"U0-003","section":"0.1","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Researchers find that children in many different countries learn to fear spiders and heights after very little experience, but rarely develop strong fears of electrical sockets, even though sockets are a more common danger in modern homes. Which perspective would best explain this pattern?","options":["Behavioral","Psychodynamic","Evolutionary","Humanistic"],"answer":2,"rationale":"The evolutionary perspective explains behavior in terms of traits that helped ancestors survive and reproduce, so readiness to fear ancient threats such as spiders and heights would have been naturally selected. A behavioral explanation would predict more fear of the objects children are most often exposed to."},{"id":"U0-004","section":"0.1","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"A school counselor working with Luis, a student who feels unmotivated, avoids diagnosing him or analyzing his past. Instead she listens without judgment, emphasizes that Luis can choose his own direction, and encourages him to pursue goals that help him grow into the person he wants to become. The counselor's approach is most consistent with which perspective?","options":["Cognitive","Psychodynamic","Biological","Humanistic"],"answer":3,"rationale":"The humanistic perspective stresses free will, personal growth and reaching one's potential, supported by a nonjudgmental relationship. A psychodynamic counselor would focus on uncovering unconscious conflicts from Luis's past."},{"id":"U0-005","section":"0.1","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"A doctor treating Rashid for frequent tension headaches notes that headaches run in his family, that Rashid tends to think every small mistake at work will lead to disaster, and that he cares for his younger siblings while working long hours. The doctor plans treatment that addresses all three factors. Which approach is the doctor using?","options":["The sociocultural perspective","The evolutionary perspective","The biopsychosocial model","The behavioral perspective"],"answer":2,"rationale":"The biopsychosocial model combines biological influences (family history), psychological influences (catastrophic thinking) and social influences (family and work demands). The sociocultural perspective alone would capture only the social demands."},{"id":"U0-006","section":"0.1","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which research question would a psychologist working from the sociocultural perspective be most likely to investigate?","options":["Which brain regions become most active when teenagers feel embarrassed in front of others?","How do rewards and punishments from teachers shape how often students raise their hands?","How do hidden wishes and fears appear in the daydreams of teenagers?","How do the expectations of a teenager's community influence how openly the teenager discusses feelings with others?"],"answer":3,"rationale":"The sociocultural perspective examines how culture, community norms and social context shape behavior. The other questions reflect the biological, behavioral and psychodynamic perspectives."},{"id":"U0-007","section":"2.2","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Before a football final, Haruto told his friends the favored team would win easily. The underdog won by two goals. The next day Haruto said, \"Honestly, it was obvious the underdog was going to win. Their defense was clearly better.\" Haruto's statement best illustrates which concept?","options":["Confirmation bias","Overconfidence","Illusory correlation","Hindsight bias"],"answer":3,"rationale":"Hindsight bias is the tendency to believe, after learning an outcome, that one would have predicted it. Haruto had actually predicted the opposite result, so his \"it was obvious\" claim reflects hindsight bias rather than overconfidence in a prediction made beforehand."},{"id":"U0-008","section":"2.2","skill":"Data Interpretation","difficulty":2,"stimulus":{"type":"table","caption":"Average confidence and actual accuracy on three question sets","headers":["Question set","Average confidence that answer was correct (%)","Actual percentage correct (%)"],"rows":[["Set 1: Geography","90","61"],["Set 2: History","85","58"],["Set 3: Science","92","66"]]},"stem":"After answering each question in a general-knowledge quiz, students rated how confident they were that their answer was correct. Based on the data in the table, the students' judgments best illustrate which concept?","options":["Confirmation bias","Regression toward the mean","Hindsight bias","Overconfidence"],"answer":3,"rationale":"Across all three sets, students' average confidence was far higher than their actual accuracy, which is overconfidence. Hindsight bias would involve claiming after the fact that they knew the answers all along."},{"id":"U0-009","section":"0.1","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Dr. Lindqvist spent ten years developing a theory about how music improves concentration. When three careful studies from her own lab failed to support the theory, she published the results and revised the theory instead of searching for reasons to dismiss the data. Which part of the scientific attitude does her response best show?","options":["Humility","Curiosity","Hindsight bias","Overconfidence"],"answer":0,"rationale":"Humility in science means being willing to be wrong and letting evidence change one's views, even about a favored theory. Curiosity is the drive to ask questions, which does not capture her acceptance of disconfirming results."},{"id":"U0-010","section":"0.2","skill":"Research Methods and Design","difficulty":3,"stimulus":{"type":"passage","text":"Dr. Nakamura compared 40 students at a secondary school who had taken music lessons for at least three years with 40 students at the same school who had never taken music lessons. All 80 students completed the same standardized memory test, and the music students scored higher on average."},"stem":"Which statement best describes the design of Dr. Nakamura's study?","options":["It is non-experimental, because the students were not randomly assigned to the music and no-music groups.","It is an experiment, because memory was measured with the same standardized test for everyone.","It is a case study, because it focused on a specific group of students who play instruments.","It is an experiment, because the researcher compared the scores of two separate groups."],"answer":0,"rationale":"Students chose for themselves whether to take music lessons, so there was no manipulated independent variable and no random assignment. Comparing two groups is not enough to make a study an experiment, since the groups may differ in many other ways."},{"id":"U0-011","section":"0.2","skill":"Research Methods and Design","difficulty":2,"stimulus":{"type":"passage","text":"Dr. Banda gave a single online questionnaire to 300 office workers in Abu Dhabi. The questionnaire asked how many minutes each worker spent commuting each day and asked them to complete a 20-item life satisfaction scale. She found that workers with longer commutes tended to report lower life satisfaction."},"stem":"Which research method did Dr. Banda use?","options":["An experiment","A correlational study","A meta-analysis","A case study"],"answer":1,"rationale":"Dr. Banda measured two existing variables (commute time and life satisfaction) and examined how they were related, without manipulating either one. That is a correlational study."},{"id":"U0-012","section":"0.2","skill":"Research Methods and Design","difficulty":2,"stimulus":{"type":"passage","text":"A neuropsychologist spent 18 months studying a 52-year-old patient, Mr. T, who lost the ability to recognize familiar objects by sight after a rare type of brain infection. She conducted weekly interviews, gave him dozens of perception tests, examined his brain scans, and spoke with his family about changes in his daily life."},"stem":"Which is the most important limitation of the method used in this research?","options":["Findings about one individual may not generalize to other people.","The method requires random assignment, which was impossible with only one participant.","The method produces only numerical data, which cannot describe the patient's experiences.","The method cannot include more than one type of data, such as interviews and brain scans."],"answer":0,"rationale":"This is a case study: an in-depth investigation of one person. Case studies give rich detail, but one unusual individual may not represent the wider population. Case studies often combine many kinds of data, including qualitative descriptions."},{"id":"U0-013","section":"0.2","skill":"Research Methods and Design","difficulty":2,"stem":"Researchers sit in a busy mall food court and record how often diners check their phones while eating. They do not speak to the diners or change anything about the setting. Which statement best describes this study?","options":["It is a naturalistic observation, which can describe behavior but cannot show what causes it.","It is a survey, which relies on diners' own reports of how often they check their phones.","It is a case study, which provides in-depth information about one diner's phone habits.","It is an experiment, which can show that phone checking causes people to eat more slowly."],"answer":0,"rationale":"Recording behavior in a real-world setting without interfering is naturalistic observation. It describes what people do, but because nothing is manipulated, it cannot establish cause and effect."},{"id":"U0-014","section":"0.2","skill":"Research Methods and Design","difficulty":2,"stem":"The student council surveyed classmates with this item: \"Don't you agree that the school should lengthen the lunch break, since most students are too rushed to eat properly?\" Which is the most serious problem with this survey item?","options":["It collects qualitative data, which cannot be summarized in any way.","Its wording is likely to push respondents toward agreeing.","It is a closed question, so it cannot measure opinions.","It cannot be answered without an independent variable."],"answer":1,"rationale":"The item is a leading question: it states a reason to agree and invites agreement, so responses would reflect the wording as much as true opinions. Neutral wording would give a more accurate picture."},{"id":"U0-015","section":"0.2","skill":"Research Methods and Design","difficulty":2,"stem":"A team of researchers tested the reading fluency of the same 120 children when the children were 6, 10 and 14 years old to see how reading skills change over time. Which research design did the team use?","options":["Naturalistic observation","Case study","Longitudinal","Cross-sectional"],"answer":2,"rationale":"A longitudinal study follows and retests the same participants over a long period. A cross-sectional study would instead test different children of different ages at one time."},{"id":"U0-016","section":"0.2","skill":"Research Methods and Design","difficulty":3,"stem":"A researcher tested how quickly 25-year-olds, 50-year-olds and 75-year-olds could learn a new smartphone app, testing all three groups during the same week. The 75-year-olds took the longest. Why should the researcher be cautious about concluding that aging itself slows app learning?","options":["No correlation can be calculated when participants are grouped by age.","A cross-sectional design always requires a placebo group to rule out expectations.","The age groups grew up with very different exposure to technology, so the differences may reflect generational experiences rather than aging.","The same participants were tested repeatedly, so practice effects made later scores higher."],"answer":2,"rationale":"This is a cross-sectional design, which compares different age groups at one time. Its main weakness is cohort effects: each age group has had different life experiences, which could explain the differences instead of age. Repeated testing of the same people is a feature of longitudinal designs."},{"id":"U0-017","section":"0.2","skill":"Research Methods and Design","difficulty":2,"stimulus":{"type":"table","caption":"Dr. Varga gathered 38 published studies on aerobic exercise and mood, converted each result into an effect size, and statistically combined all 38. A sample of the studies is shown.","headers":["Study","Country","Number of participants","Effect size"],"rows":[["Study 1","Brazil","64","0.41"],["Study 2","Japan","210","0.28"],["Study 3","Nigeria","95","0.52"],["Study 4","Canada","150","0.33"],["All 38 studies combined","Various","6,870","0.38"]]},"stem":"Based on the description and table, which research method is Dr. Varga using?","options":["Naturalistic observation","Longitudinal study","Meta-analysis","Replication"],"answer":2,"rationale":"Dr. Varga is not collecting new data; she is statistically combining the results of many earlier studies to estimate an overall effect, which is a meta-analysis. Replication would mean repeating one study with new participants."},{"id":"U0-018","section":"0.2","skill":"Research Methods and Design","difficulty":2,"stem":"A researcher interviews 15 families who recently moved to the UAE, asking open-ended questions such as \"What was the most difficult part of your first month here?\" She then reads the transcripts and groups the answers into common themes. Which statement correctly classifies the data she collected and explains why?","options":["Quantitative data, because more than ten families took part","Qualitative data, because the responses are non-numerical descriptions of experiences","Quantitative data, because the interview questions were planned in advance","Qualitative data, because the study had no hypothesis to test"],"answer":1,"rationale":"Qualitative data are descriptions in words rather than numbers, such as interview answers sorted into themes. Planning questions in advance makes an interview structured, but it does not make the answers numerical."},{"id":"U0-019","section":"0.2","skill":"Research Methods and Design","difficulty":3,"stimulus":{"type":"passage","text":"Study 1: A researcher surveyed 400 students and found that those who said they used a weekly planner had higher end-of-term grades. Study 2: A different researcher randomly assigned 200 students either to a four-week planner-training program or to a four-week study-skills video series of the same length. Students in the planner group earned higher end-of-term grades."},"stem":"Which statement about the two studies is most accurate?","options":["Both studies show that using a planner caused higher grades, because both found the same pattern.","Only Study 1 shows cause and effect, because it measured students' real habits outside a research setting.","Neither study shows any relationship between planner use and grades, because grades vary from term to term.","Only Study 2 supports the conclusion that using a planner caused higher grades."],"answer":3,"rationale":"Study 2 manipulated planner training and randomly assigned students, so it is an experiment that can support a causal conclusion. Study 1 is correlational: students who choose to use planners may differ in other ways, such as motivation."},{"id":"U0-020","section":"0.2","skill":"Research Methods and Design","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which of the following studies is an example of correlational research?","options":["Following one patient with a rare memory disorder for five years","Watching children on a playground and recording each act of helping without interacting with them","Randomly assigning students to study with or without background music and comparing their test scores","Measuring the number of siblings each child has and the child's score on a sharing task"],"answer":3,"rationale":"A correlational study measures two existing variables to see whether they are related, such as number of siblings and sharing scores. Random assignment to music conditions is an experiment, following one patient is a case study, and watching the playground is naturalistic observation."},{"id":"U0-021","section":"0.2","skill":"Research Methods and Design","difficulty":1,"stem":"Before a study on sleep and memory is published in a scientific journal, the editor sends the manuscript to three independent experts, who check whether the methods, analysis and conclusions are sound. This process is called","options":["replication","meta-analysis","peer review","debriefing"],"answer":2,"rationale":"Peer review is the evaluation of research by other experts in the field before publication. Replication is different: it means repeating a study to see whether the results hold."},{"id":"U0-022","section":"0.2","skill":"Research Methods and Design","difficulty":2,"stem":"A company claims its new phone app reduces test anxiety. Which research plan would best allow a researcher to determine whether the app causes a reduction in test anxiety?","options":["Randomly assign students to use either the app or a similar app with no anxiety content for four weeks, then compare their anxiety scores.","Observe students in an exam hall and count visible signs of anxiety, such as foot tapping.","Interview the app's most enthusiastic user in depth about how the app changed her experience of tests.","Survey students who already use the app and compare their anxiety scores with those of students who do not."],"answer":0,"rationale":"Only an experiment, with a manipulated independent variable and random assignment, can show cause and effect. Comparing existing users and non-users is correlational, because people who choose the app may differ in other ways."},{"id":"U0-023","section":"0.3","skill":"Research Methods and Design","difficulty":2,"stimulus":{"type":"passage","text":"Dr. Al Mansoori randomly assigned 60 students to read the same 900-word article either in a quiet room or in a room playing recorded cafe noise. Afterward, all students completed a 20-question comprehension test about the article."},"stem":"What is the independent variable in Dr. Al Mansoori's experiment?","options":["The article that all participants read","The 60 students who took part in the study","The level of background noise in the room during reading","The score on the 20-question comprehension test"],"answer":2,"rationale":"The independent variable is the factor the researcher manipulates: here, quiet room or recorded cafe noise. The comprehension score is the dependent variable, the outcome that is measured."},{"id":"U0-024","section":"0.3","skill":"Research Methods and Design","difficulty":2,"stem":"Dr. Chen plans to compare how long high school students persist on difficult tasks after receiving praise for effort or praise for ability. Each student will work alone on a set of puzzles, some of which have no solution. Which way of recording persistence would let other researchers measure it in exactly the same way?","options":["The researcher's overall impression of how determined each student seemed","The number of seconds each student keeps working on an unsolvable puzzle before asking to move on","How much each student appears to enjoy challenging tasks during the session","Each student's general attitude toward difficult schoolwork"],"answer":1,"rationale":"An operational definition states exactly how a variable will be observed and measured, so the study can be replicated. Timing how long a student keeps working on an unsolvable puzzle is specific and observable; impressions, enjoyment and general attitudes are vague and depend on the observer's judgment."},{"id":"U0-025","section":"0.3","skill":"Research Methods and Design","difficulty":3,"stimulus":{"type":"table","caption":"Results from two labs studying speech rehearsal and anxiety","headers":["Lab","How anxiety was measured","Rehearsal group","No-rehearsal group"],"rows":[["Nairobi","Heart rate during the speech (beats per minute)","92","104"],["Manila","Self-rated anxiety after the speech (1 to 10 scale)","6.1","6.3"]]},"stem":"Two labs ran the same experiment on whether rehearsing a speech reduces anxiety, randomly assigning students to rehearse or not before giving a short speech. Based on the table, what is the best explanation for why the labs reached different conclusions?","options":["The Manila lab measured the independent variable instead of the dependent variable.","Heart rate produces qualitative data, so it cannot be compared across groups.","The labs used different operational definitions of anxiety, so they may have measured different aspects of it.","The Nairobi lab did not have an independent variable, so its results are not valid."],"answer":2,"rationale":"Both labs manipulated the same independent variable (rehearsal), but one measured anxiety as heart rate and the other as a self-rating. Different operational definitions can capture different aspects of a concept, which can lead to different results and makes direct replication harder."},{"id":"U0-026","section":"0.3","skill":"Research Methods and Design","difficulty":2,"stem":"To test new typing software, a researcher has Group 1 practice with the new software at 8:00 a.m. and Group 2 practice with the old software at 8:00 p.m. Both groups then complete the same typing-speed test. Which factor in this design is a confounding variable?","options":["The time of day each group practiced","The score on the typing-speed test","The type of typing software used","The use of the same typing test for both groups"],"answer":0,"rationale":"A confounding variable is a factor other than the independent variable that differs between groups and could explain the results. Because the groups practiced at different times of day, differences in alertness, not the software, could explain any difference. The software is the independent variable and typing speed is the dependent variable."},{"id":"U0-027","section":"0.3","skill":"Research Methods and Design","difficulty":3,"stimulus":{"type":"passage","text":"Ms. Reyes, a school psychologist, randomly selected 200 names from her school's roster of 1,400 students. For each selected student, she flipped a coin: heads meant the student would receive written feedback on a practice essay, and tails meant the student would receive spoken feedback. She then compared the two groups' scores on a later essay."},"stem":"What is the main purpose of the coin flip in Ms. Reyes's study?","options":["To provide a precise operational definition of the dependent variable","To make the two groups similar at the start, so that differences afterward can be attributed to the type of feedback","To make sure the results can be generalized to every student in the school","To keep participants from knowing which type of feedback they will receive"],"answer":1,"rationale":"The coin flip is random assignment, which spreads participant differences evenly across conditions and so controls for confounding variables. Choosing 200 names at random from the roster was random sampling, which is what supports generalizing to the school."},{"id":"U0-028","section":"0.3","skill":"Research Methods and Design","difficulty":2,"stem":"A researcher wants to know how much time high school students across Dubai spend on screens each day. She gives her survey to the members of the gaming club at her own school. Which is the most serious problem with her sampling method?","options":["She should have randomly assigned club members to experimental and control groups.","Her sample is too large to give accurate information about the population.","It is a convenience sample that is unlikely to represent the population of Dubai high school students.","A survey cannot measure how many hours a person spends on a daily activity."],"answer":2,"rationale":"Club members at one school were chosen because they were easy to reach, and gamers are likely to spend more time on screens than typical students. This sampling bias means the results would not generalize. A random sample from many schools would be more representative."},{"id":"U0-029","section":"0.3","skill":"Research Methods and Design","difficulty":2,"stem":"In a study of a vitamin supplement and concentration, one group of adults takes the supplement daily while a second group takes an identical-looking capsule containing no active ingredient. What is the main purpose of the identical-looking capsule?","options":["To serve as the independent variable that is measured at the end of the study","To control for participants' expectations, so any extra improvement in the supplement group can be credited to the supplement","To reduce social desirability bias by keeping all of the participants' answers anonymous","To make sure the supplement group receives a larger dose than usual"],"answer":1,"rationale":"The capsule is a placebo. People may improve simply because they expect a pill to work, so comparing against a placebo group separates the supplement's real effect from the placebo effect."},{"id":"U0-030","section":"0.3","skill":"Research Methods and Design","difficulty":2,"stem":"In a study of a new relaxation exercise, participants do not know whether they are doing the real exercise or a similar-looking stretching routine, but the researchers who lead the sessions and record the results do know. This study uses","options":["a single-blind procedure","a naturalistic observation","a double-blind procedure","random sampling"],"answer":0,"rationale":"When only the participants are unaware of their condition, the study is single-blind. In a double-blind procedure, the researchers who interact with participants and record data would also not know who is in which group."},{"id":"U0-031","section":"0.3","skill":"Research Methods and Design","difficulty":3,"stem":"Mr. Haddad tests whether a creative-writing workshop improves story quality. He knows which students attended the workshop, and he personally rates every student's story on a 1 to 10 scale. The workshop students receive higher ratings. Which problem most threatens his conclusion, and how could it be reduced?","options":["Sampling bias; Mr. Haddad should use a larger group of students from the same school.","Social desirability bias; students should complete the writing task anonymously.","Hindsight bias; Mr. Haddad should predict the results before collecting data.","Experimenter bias; the stories should be rated by someone who does not know which students attended the workshop."],"answer":3,"rationale":"Because Mr. Haddad knows who attended and expects the workshop to help, his expectations may influence his ratings. Using raters who are blind to the conditions, as in a double-blind procedure, reduces experimenter bias."},{"id":"U0-032","section":"0.3","skill":"Research Methods and Design","difficulty":2,"stem":"A teacher asks each of her students, one at a time and face to face, how many times this year they have copied a classmate's homework. Nearly every student answers \"never.\" The teacher's results are most likely affected by","options":["the placebo effect","social desirability bias","experimenter bias","regression toward the mean"],"answer":1,"rationale":"Social desirability bias occurs when people answer in ways that make them look good to others, which is especially likely when the question is sensitive and the person asking is their teacher. An anonymous survey would likely give more honest answers."},{"id":"U0-033","section":"0.3","skill":"Research Methods and Design","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which of the following hypotheses is falsifiable?","options":["Dreams contain hidden wishes that the dreamer can never become aware of.","Students who sleep at least 8 hours the night before a quiz will score higher than students who sleep fewer than 6 hours.","People sometimes feel happier or sadder for many different reasons.","An invisible energy that no instrument can detect influences students' moods."],"answer":1,"rationale":"A falsifiable hypothesis makes a specific prediction that data could show to be wrong. Sleep hours and quiz scores can both be measured, so the sleep prediction could be disproven; the other statements either cannot be measured or are too vague to be contradicted."},{"id":"U0-034","section":"0.3","skill":"Research Methods and Design","difficulty":2,"stem":"A study in Seoul found that students remembered more vocabulary when they tested themselves than when they reread their notes. Researchers in Mexico, Kenya and Germany then repeated the study with new participants and found the same result. Which process do the later studies represent?","options":["Debriefing","Meta-analysis","Replication","Peer review"],"answer":2,"rationale":"Replication means repeating a study, often with new participants and settings, to see whether the findings hold. Successful replications in several countries increase confidence that the original result was not a fluke."},{"id":"U0-035","section":"0.3","skill":"Research Methods and Design","difficulty":2,"stem":"Dr. Ndlovu has a theory that actively retrieving information strengthens memory more than passively reviewing it. Which of the following is a hypothesis that could be used to test this theory?","options":["Researchers have studied memory and forgetting for more than a century.","Some students prefer to study alone, while others prefer to study with friends.","Memory is a complex process that involves many regions of the brain.","Students who take three practice quizzes on a vocabulary list will recall more words one week later than students who reread the list for the same amount of time."],"answer":3,"rationale":"A theory is a broad explanation, while a hypothesis is a specific, testable prediction derived from it. The practice-quiz statement names the conditions and the measurable outcome, so it directly tests the retrieval theory."},{"id":"U0-036","section":"0.4","skill":"Research Methods and Design","difficulty":3,"stimulus":{"type":"passage","text":"Researchers propose a study with 15-year-old volunteers. Each student would take an intelligence test and then be falsely told that they scored in the bottom 5 percent of students their age. The researchers would measure each student's self-esteem every week for four months before telling the students the truth about their scores."},"stem":"An institutional review board (IRB) would most likely require major changes to this proposal for which reason?","options":["Self-esteem cannot be operationally defined, so the study would produce no usable data.","The study should use a random sample of all 15-year-olds in the country instead of volunteers.","Research with adolescents can never include any form of deception, even with approval.","The months of false feedback could cause lasting psychological harm, and the risk to participants outweighs the benefit of the study."],"answer":3,"rationale":"A key job of the IRB is to protect participants from harm. Believing for four months that you scored in the bottom 5 percent could seriously damage a teenager's well-being, and a short debriefing at the end may not undo it. Mild, approved deception with minors is possible, so a total ban is not the reason."},{"id":"U0-037","section":"0.4","skill":"Research Methods and Design","difficulty":2,"stem":"At a mall, a researcher asks shoppers to sign a form that says only \"I agree to take part in this research study.\" When Kofi asks what the study involves and how long it will take, the researcher replies that she cannot tell him until he signs. Which ethical guideline is the researcher failing to follow?","options":["Debriefing","Confidentiality","Informed consent","Random assignment"],"answer":2,"rationale":"Informed consent requires that people understand what a study involves, including its procedures, risks and their right to withdraw, before agreeing to take part. A signature without that information is not informed."},{"id":"U0-038","section":"0.4","skill":"Research Methods and Design","difficulty":2,"stem":"A researcher plans to study problem-solving in 11-year-olds. Each child's parents have signed consent forms. What else should the researcher obtain before each child takes part?","options":["A written promise from the child to complete every task, even ones the child dislikes","Permission from the child's teacher to keep the purpose of the study secret from the child","Nothing more, because parental consent removes the need for the child's agreement","The child's own agreement to participate, explained in language the child can understand"],"answer":3,"rationale":"Children cannot give legal consent, so parents or guardians consent, but researchers should also obtain the child's assent: the child's own willing agreement after an age-appropriate explanation. Children, like adults, keep the right to stop."},{"id":"U0-039","section":"0.4","skill":"Research Methods and Design","difficulty":2,"stem":"Participants in a study on test anxiety gave informed consent and were fully debriefed afterward. However, the researcher later posted a results table on a public website listing each participant's full name next to his or her anxiety score. Which ethical guideline did the researcher violate?","options":["The use of deception","Debriefing","Confidentiality","Informed consent"],"answer":2,"rationale":"Researchers must keep participants' personal information and results private, for example by using code numbers instead of names. Consent and debriefing were handled properly here; publishing names with scores breaks confidentiality."},{"id":"U0-040","section":"0.4","skill":"Research Methods and Design","difficulty":2,"stem":"In a study on group decision-making, Dr. Santos uses a confederate who pretends to be another participant and argues for an obviously wrong answer. What should Dr. Santos do at the end of each session?","options":["Debrief the participant by explaining the true purpose of the study and the confederate's role, and address any distress","Keep the deception secret permanently so that the participant's memory of the session is not disturbed","Ask for informed consent for the first time, now that the session is complete","Say nothing about the confederate, because participants agreed to take part before the session began"],"answer":0,"rationale":"When a study uses deception, such as a confederate, researchers must debrief participants afterward: reveal the deception, explain why it was needed and make sure participants leave without lasting distress. Informed consent must come before participation, not after."},{"id":"U0-041","section":"0.4","skill":"Research Methods and Design","difficulty":2,"stem":"Under typical ethical guidelines, under which condition is an institutional review board most likely to approve a study that uses deception?","options":["The study could not be done without deception, the risk of harm is minimal, and participants will be debriefed afterward.","Participants will be paid enough money to make up for being misled.","The lead researcher has already published several well-known studies on the topic.","The study will take place in a public setting such as a park or shopping center."],"answer":0,"rationale":"Deception is allowed only when it is necessary for answering the research question, poses minimal risk and is followed by debriefing. Payment, setting and a researcher's reputation do not justify misleading participants."},{"id":"U0-042","section":"0.4","skill":"Research Methods and Design","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which statement best describes ethical guidelines for psychological research with nonhuman animals?","options":["Animals may never be used in psychological research under any circumstances.","Researchers may cause any level of pain as long as the results might be useful to humans.","Studies must be reviewed by an oversight committee, and researchers must minimize the animals' pain and discomfort.","Animal studies do not require ethical review, because animals cannot give informed consent."],"answer":2,"rationale":"Animal research is permitted, but it must be approved by a review committee and must ensure humane care, minimizing pain and stress. Because animals cannot consent, oversight is more important, not less."},{"id":"U0-043","section":"0.4","skill":"Research Methods and Design","difficulty":2,"stem":"Halfway through a puzzle-solving study, Ananya becomes uncomfortable and tells the researcher she would like to stop. The researcher tells her she must finish all of the puzzles or she will lose the course credit she was promised. Which ethical principle has the researcher violated?","options":["The requirement that participants be selected through random sampling","The right of participants to withdraw at any time without penalty","The requirement that participants' data be kept confidential","The requirement that participants be debriefed at the end of a study"],"answer":1,"rationale":"Participants must be free to stop at any point without being punished, and threatening to remove promised credit is a penalty. This right is usually explained during informed consent."},{"id":"U0-044","section":"0.5","skill":"Data Interpretation","difficulty":3,"stimulus":{"type":"table","caption":"Minutes spent reading for pleasure on one school night","headers":["Student","Minutes"],"rows":[["Aisha","25"],["Ben","90"],["Chen","15"],["Dalia","40"],["Emeka","10"],["Farah","30"],["Gabriel","15"],["Hina","35"],["Ivan","20"]]},"stem":"The table shows how many minutes nine students read for pleasure on a school night. Which statement about these data is accurate?","options":["The median is 25 minutes, and it describes a typical student better than the mean does because of one extreme score.","The mode is 20 minutes, which is the score that occurs most often.","The mean is 25 minutes, and it is the best summary because it uses every score.","The range is 70 minutes, which shows that the scores are closely clustered."],"answer":0,"rationale":"In order, the scores are 10, 15, 15, 20, 25, 30, 35, 40 and 90, so the median (middle score) is 25. The mean is 280 divided by 9, about 31.1, pulled upward by the outlier of 90. The mode is 15 and the range is 90 minus 10, which is 80."},{"id":"U0-045","section":"0.5","skill":"Data Interpretation","difficulty":2,"stimulus":{"type":"bar","title":"Hours of sleep before the test","xLabel":"Hours of sleep","yLabel":"Number of students","categories":["5","6","7","8","9"],"series":[{"name":"Students","values":[2,5,9,6,3]}],"yMax":10},"stem":"A class of 25 students reported how many hours they slept the night before a test. Based on the graph, which statement is accurate?","options":["The mode and the median are both 7 hours.","The mode is 9 hours, and the median is 7 hours.","The mode is 7 hours, and the range is 7 hours.","The mode is 7 hours, and the median is 8 hours."],"answer":0,"rationale":"The tallest bar is 7 hours (9 students), so the mode is 7. With 25 scores, the median is the 13th; counting up gives 2 students at 5 hours, 7 through 6 hours and 16 through 7 hours, so the 13th score is 7 hours. The range is 9 minus 5, which is 4 hours."},{"id":"U0-046","section":"0.5","skill":"Data Interpretation","difficulty":3,"stimulus":{"type":"bar","title":"Scores on the challenge test","xLabel":"Score range","yLabel":"Number of students","categories":["0-20","21-40","41-60","61-80","81-100"],"series":[{"name":"Students","values":[14,10,4,2,1]}]},"stem":"A teacher gave a very difficult challenge test to 31 students. The graph shows the distribution of scores. Which statement best describes this distribution?","options":["It is approximately normal, and the mean and median are likely equal.","It is negatively skewed, and the mean is likely higher than the median.","It is positively skewed, and the mean is likely lower than the median.","It is positively skewed, and the mean is likely higher than the median."],"answer":3,"rationale":"Most scores are bunched at the low end with a tail stretching toward the few high scores, which is a positive (right) skew. The small number of high scores pulls the mean upward, above the median."},{"id":"U0-047","section":"0.5","skill":"Data Interpretation","difficulty":2,"stimulus":{"type":"bar","title":"Time to solve the puzzle","xLabel":"Time (seconds)","yLabel":"Number of students","categories":["20-29","30-39","40-49","50-59","60-69","70-79"],"series":[{"name":"Students","values":[2,9,4,3,8,2]}]},"stem":"A researcher timed 28 students solving the same math puzzle. The graph shows how long the students took. Which term best describes this distribution, and what might it suggest?","options":["It is positively skewed, which suggests that most students finished more slowly than average.","It is bimodal, which may suggest that the students fall into two groups, such as those using two different strategies.","It is negatively skewed, which suggests that a few students finished very quickly.","It is a normal distribution, which suggests that most students finished near the mean time."],"answer":1,"rationale":"The graph has two separate peaks (30 to 39 seconds and 60 to 69 seconds), which makes it bimodal. Two peaks often mean the data combine two different subgroups."},{"id":"U0-048","section":"0.5","skill":"Data Interpretation","difficulty":2,"stimulus":{"type":"table","caption":"History test results","headers":["Class","Number of students","Mean score","Standard deviation"],"rows":[["Class X","24","75","3"],["Class Y","24","75","12"]]},"stem":"Two classes took the same history test. Based on the table, which statement is accurate?","options":["Scores in Class X vary more from the mean than scores in Class Y.","Because the means are equal, the two sets of scores must be distributed in the same way.","Scores in Class Y are more spread out around the mean than scores in Class X.","Students in Class Y scored higher on average than students in Class X."],"answer":2,"rationale":"Standard deviation measures how much scores vary around the mean. Both classes averaged 75, but Class Y's standard deviation of 12 is much larger than Class X's 3, so Class Y's scores are more spread out."},{"id":"U0-049","section":"0.5","skill":"Data Interpretation","difficulty":3,"stem":"Scores on a national reasoning test are normally distributed with a mean of 50 and a standard deviation of 10. Fatima scored 70. Approximately what percentage of test takers scored lower than Fatima?","options":["About 68 percent","About 98 percent","About 95 percent","About 84 percent"],"answer":1,"rationale":"A score of 70 is two standard deviations above the mean. In a normal curve, 50 percent of scores fall below the mean, 34 percent fall between the mean and one standard deviation above, and about 13.5 percent fall between one and two standard deviations above, for a total of about 97.5 percent. The 95 percent figure is the share within two standard deviations on both sides of the mean."},{"id":"U0-050","section":"0.5","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Omar learns that his score on a college admissions test places him at the 80th percentile. What does this mean?","options":["He scored higher than about 80 percent of the people who took the test.","He scored lower than about 80 percent of the people who took the test.","He answered about 80 percent of the questions correctly.","His score was 80 points higher than the mean score."],"answer":0,"rationale":"Percentile rank shows the percentage of scores that fall below a given score. It is not the same as the percentage of questions answered correctly."},{"id":"U0-051","section":"0.5","skill":"Data Interpretation","difficulty":3,"stimulus":{"type":"table","caption":"Correlations with weekly hours of part-time work (N = 500)","headers":["Variable","Correlation coefficient with hours worked"],"rows":[["Grade point average","-0.48"],["Hours of sleep per night","-0.71"],["Money saved per month","+0.62"],["Number of school clubs joined","+0.05"]]},"stem":"A researcher surveyed 500 high school students who have part-time jobs. The table shows the correlation between the number of hours each student works per week and four other variables. Which statement is best supported by the data?","options":["Hours worked are more strongly related to sleep than to savings.","Hours worked are most strongly related to savings, because that correlation is positive.","Hours worked have a moderate positive relationship with the number of school clubs joined.","Working more hours causes students to earn lower grades."],"answer":0,"rationale":"The strength of a correlation depends on its absolute value, not its sign, so negative 0.71 (sleep) is stronger than positive 0.62 (savings). A correlation of 0.05 shows almost no relationship, and correlational data cannot show that working causes lower grades."},{"id":"U0-052","section":"0.5","skill":"Data Interpretation","difficulty":2,"stimulus":{"type":"table","caption":"Practice time and performance errors","headers":["Pianist","Hours of practice","Number of errors"],"rows":[["Pianist 1","1","18"],["Pianist 2","2","17"],["Pianist 3","3","14"],["Pianist 4","4","15"],["Pianist 5","5","11"],["Pianist 6","6","9"],["Pianist 7","7","8"],["Pianist 8","8","5"]]},"stem":"Eight beginner pianists recorded how many hours they practiced a new piece and how many errors they made when performing it. If these data were plotted on a scatterplot, which description would best fit the relationship?","options":["No correlation","A strong positive correlation","A strong negative correlation","A weak negative correlation"],"answer":2,"rationale":"As practice hours rise from 1 to 8, errors fall fairly steadily from 18 to 5, so the points would cluster tightly around a line sloping downward. That pattern is a strong negative correlation."},{"id":"U0-053","section":"0.5","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":3,"stem":"Mr. Tanaka gives his class a weekly quiz. Each time a student earns their lowest score of the term, he gives that student a stern talk. He notices that these students almost always score higher on the next quiz, and he concludes that stern talks improve performance. Which concept offers a better explanation for the improvement?","options":["The placebo effect","Regression toward the mean","Illusory correlation","Hindsight bias"],"answer":1,"rationale":"An unusually low score is partly due to chance factors, such as a bad day, so the next score is likely to be closer to the student's average whether or not a talk happens. This is regression toward the mean, and it can make an ineffective action seem to work."},{"id":"U0-054","section":"0.5","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"A shop owner in Al Ain is convinced that customers are ruder on very hot days. When she checks a year of her own customer complaint records against daily temperatures, she finds no relationship between the two. Her original belief is an example of","options":["a negative correlation","an illusory correlation","statistical significance","regression toward the mean"],"answer":1,"rationale":"An illusory correlation is a perceived relationship between two variables that does not actually exist. People tend to notice and remember the cases that fit their belief, such as one rude customer on a hot day, and overlook the cases that do not."},{"id":"U0-055","section":"0.6","skill":"Data Interpretation","difficulty":2,"stimulus":{"type":"bar","title":"Mean words recalled on the final test","xLabel":"Group","yLabel":"Mean words recalled (out of 20)","categories":["Memory-training app","Puzzle game"],"series":[{"name":"Mean words recalled","values":[14.2,11.6]}],"yMax":20},"stem":"Researchers randomly assigned 80 students either to use a memory-training app for three weeks or to use a puzzle game for the same amount of time. The graph shows the mean number of words each group recalled on a final test. The difference between the groups was statistically significant (p = .01). Which interpretation is most accurate?","options":["The app improved memory for 99 percent of the students who used it.","The app had a very large effect, because the result was statistically significant.","The results prove that the app will improve memory for every person who uses it.","The difference between the groups is unlikely to have occurred by chance alone."],"answer":3,"rationale":"Statistical significance means the difference is unlikely to be due to chance. A p value of .01 does not describe what percentage of individuals improved, does not prove anything for everyone, and does not by itself show how large the effect is; that requires an effect size."},{"id":"U0-056","section":"0.6","skill":"Data Interpretation","difficulty":2,"stimulus":{"type":"table","caption":"Effect sizes for two sleep programs","headers":["Program","Statistically significant?","Effect size"],"rows":[["Program A: sleep-tips emails","Yes","0.15"],["Program B: evening screen-free routine","Yes","0.90"]]},"stem":"Two school programs designed to improve students' sleep were each tested in a separate experiment. Both produced statistically significant improvements. Based on the table, which conclusion is most accurate?","options":["Both programs had medium effects, because both results were statistically significant.","Program A had a large effect, because its results were statistically significant.","Neither program was effective, because both effect sizes are below 1.0.","Program A had a small effect, and Program B had a large effect."],"answer":3,"rationale":"Effect size shows how big a difference is. By common guidelines, values around 0.2 are small and values of 0.8 or above are large, so 0.15 is small and 0.90 is large. Statistical significance alone does not tell how big an effect is."},{"id":"U0-057","section":"0.6","skill":"Research Methods and Design","difficulty":2,"stem":"A survey of 1,200 families finds that teenagers who eat breakfast with their families more often tend to earn higher grades. Which statement best explains why the researchers cannot conclude that family breakfasts cause higher grades?","options":["A third variable, such as how involved parents are in their children's daily lives, could lead to both more family breakfasts and higher grades.","Surveys produce only qualitative data, which cannot be used to examine relationships.","Grades cannot be operationally defined, so they cannot be measured in a survey.","The correlation is positive, and only negative correlations can show cause and effect."],"answer":0,"rationale":"In correlational research, an outside factor may cause both variables, which is the third variable problem. Highly involved parents might both organize family meals and support schoolwork. Positive and negative correlations are equally unable to show causation."},{"id":"U0-058","section":"0.6","skill":"Research Methods and Design","difficulty":3,"stem":"A researcher finds a positive correlation between teenagers' anxiety scores and the number of hours they spend on social media each day. Which statement best describes the directionality problem in this study?","options":["The sample might include more anxious teenagers than the general population does.","Anxiety might lead teenagers to spend more time on social media, rather than social media use leading to anxiety.","The correlation might be too weak to show any relationship between the two variables.","A third factor, such as lack of sleep, might increase both anxiety and social media use."],"answer":1,"rationale":"The directionality problem means we cannot tell which variable influences which: social media could raise anxiety, or anxious teens could turn to social media. A third factor causing both is a different issue, the third variable problem."},{"id":"U0-059","section":"0.6","skill":"Research Methods and Design","difficulty":2,"stem":"A researcher studied 40 volunteers, all aged 19 to 21 and all enrolled in the same university psychology class, and found that a breathing exercise improved their focus. She claimed the exercise will improve focus for adults of all ages around the world. What is the main weakness of her claim?","options":["Her sample is not representative of all adults, so the results may not generalize beyond students like those she studied.","Her study had no independent variable, so she cannot claim any effect.","She should have used a case study instead, because case studies generalize more easily.","Her results were based on volunteers, so they cannot be statistically significant."],"answer":0,"rationale":"Generalizing requires a sample that represents the population. A small group of young adults from one class may differ from older adults or people in other cultures, so the findings need testing in more diverse samples."},{"id":"U0-060","section":"0.6","skill":"Data Interpretation","difficulty":3,"stimulus":{"type":"passage","text":"A technology company randomly assigned 20,000 users to type on either a standard keyboard or a new curved keyboard for one month. Users of the curved keyboard responded to on-screen prompts an average of 2 milliseconds faster. The difference was statistically significant (p < .05), and the effect size was 0.05."},"stem":"Which conclusion is best supported by the study described?","options":["Effect sizes cannot be calculated for experiments, so the effect size should be ignored.","Because the result is statistically significant, the effect of keyboard type must be large.","The difference must be due to chance, because the effect size is so small.","The difference is unlikely to be due to chance, but it is too small to matter much in practice."],"answer":3,"rationale":"With a very large sample, even a tiny difference can be statistically significant, meaning it is probably real rather than chance. The effect size of 0.05 shows the difference is very small, so it has little practical importance."},{"id":"U1-001","section":"1.1","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Nurses in a maternity ward notice that babies only a few hours old relax their faces and suck eagerly when a drop of sugar water is placed on their lips. Which of the following is the most likely explanation for this reaction?","options":["Observational learning from watching family members enjoy sweet foods","An inborn preference for sweet tastes, which in our ancestors' environment signaled safe, energy-rich food","A classically conditioned response formed by pairing sugar with feeding times","A cultural food tradition passed on by the infants' families"],"answer":1,"rationale":"Because the babies are only hours old and have had almost no chance to learn, the reaction is best explained as an inborn, evolved preference. Sweetness usually signaled ripe, calorie-rich food, so ancestors who liked it gained energy and survived. Conditioning, imitation and cultural traditions all require experience the newborns have not had."},{"id":"U1-002","section":"1.2","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":1,"stem":"After a long school day, Ahmed lies down on the sofa to rest, and his parasympathetic nervous system becomes more active. Which of the following changes in his body is most likely?","options":["His heart rate slows and his breathing becomes slower and deeper","His liver releases stored glucose into his bloodstream for quick energy","His palms begin to sweat more than usual","Blood flow shifts away from his stomach toward his leg muscles"],"answer":0,"rationale":"The parasympathetic nervous system calms the body and conserves energy, so heart rate and breathing slow down. Releasing glucose, sweating and sending blood to the muscles are all sympathetic, fight-or-flight changes."},{"id":"U1-003","section":"1.2","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":1,"stem":"In the seconds before the starting signal of a 400-meter race, Valentina's sympathetic nervous system becomes highly active. Which of the following changes is a direct result of this activation?","options":["Her salivary glands produce more saliva","Her stomach and intestines speed up digestion","Her pupils narrow to sharpen close-up vision","Her breathing speeds up so more oxygen reaches her muscles"],"answer":3,"rationale":"Sympathetic activation prepares the body for action by increasing breathing rate and heart rate so the muscles get more oxygen. Increased saliva, faster digestion and narrowing pupils are parasympathetic, rest-and-digest effects."},{"id":"U1-004","section":"1.1","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Infants in very different cultures, from Manila to Nairobi to Oslo, tend to sit up, crawl and take their first steps in roughly the same order and at similar ages. A psychologist who strongly favors the nature side of the nature-nurture debate would most likely explain this pattern by pointing to","options":["socialization, as parents in every culture teach the same motor skills","reinforcement, as caregivers reward each new movement with praise","maturation, the biological growth process that unfolds according to a genetic plan","observational learning, as infants copy older children who are already walking"],"answer":2,"rationale":"Supporters of nature emphasize maturation, the genetically guided sequence of biological growth that unfolds with relatively little influence from experience. Socialization, reinforcement and observational learning all stress the environment, which is the nurture side of the debate."},{"id":"U1-005","section":"1.1","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"At a large family gathering, Hessa notices that her younger cousins become upset and anxious whenever they are left out of the group games. An evolutionary psychologist would most likely explain the strong human need to belong by arguing that","options":["people seek belonging mainly as a step toward reaching their full personal potential","ancestors who stayed connected to a group were more likely to be protected, find food and survive long enough to raise children","children want group membership only because parents reward them for joining in","the need to belong grows out of unconscious conflicts formed in early childhood"],"answer":1,"rationale":"The evolutionary perspective explains traits by how they helped ancestors survive and reproduce, and living in a group offered protection and shared resources. The other explanations reflect the humanistic, behavioral and psychodynamic perspectives."},{"id":"U1-006","section":"1.1","skill":"Concept Recall","difficulty":1,"stem":"Two psychologists disagree about a gifted young violinist. One argues that her talent comes mostly from abilities she inherited from her parents, while the other argues that it comes mostly from years of lessons and daily practice at home. Their disagreement is an example of which long-standing debate in psychology?","options":["Nature versus nurture","Stability versus change","Continuity versus stages","Person versus situation"],"answer":0,"rationale":"The nature-nurture debate asks how much traits and behaviors are shaped by heredity (nature) compared with environment and experience (nurture). Stability versus change and continuity versus stages are developmental debates about how people change over time, not about where a trait comes from."},{"id":"U1-007","section":"0.6","skill":"Research Methods and Design","difficulty":2,"stem":"A researcher surveys 500 families and finds that teenagers who eat dinner with their families most nights report fewer behavior problems at school than teenagers who rarely eat with their families. He concludes that eating family dinners prevents behavior problems. Which of the following best explains why his conclusion is not justified?","options":["He did not use a double-blind procedure.","He confused a negative correlation with a positive correlation.","He did not use a large enough sample.","He drew a cause-and-effect conclusion from correlational data."],"answer":3,"rationale":"A survey can show only that two variables are related. Other factors, such as parents' working hours or family stress, might affect both family dinners and behavior, so only an experiment with random assignment could show that the dinners cause the difference. A sample of 500 is reasonably large, and the direction of a correlation does not solve the causation problem."},{"id":"U1-008","section":"1.2","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"During a family camping trip in the desert, Ravi lifts a rock and sees a large scorpion just centimeters from his hand. His heart pounds, his mouth goes dry and his breathing quickens before he even steps back. These changes are most directly produced by which of the following?","options":["The parasympathetic nervous system","The somatic nervous system","The sympathetic nervous system","The cerebellum"],"answer":2,"rationale":"A racing heart, dry mouth and fast breathing are classic fight-or-flight responses, controlled by the sympathetic division of the autonomic nervous system. The parasympathetic division calms the body afterward, and the somatic system controls voluntary movements such as stepping back."},{"id":"U1-009","section":"1.2","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":1,"stem":"Grace knows the answer to her teacher's question, so she decides to raise her hand. Which division of the nervous system carries the commands from her central nervous system to the skeletal muscles in her arm?","options":["The autonomic nervous system","The somatic nervous system","The parasympathetic nervous system","The endocrine system"],"answer":1,"rationale":"The somatic nervous system is the part of the peripheral nervous system that controls voluntary movement of the skeletal muscles. The autonomic nervous system, including its parasympathetic branch, controls involuntary functions such as heart rate, and the endocrine system communicates through hormones rather than nerves."},{"id":"U1-010","section":"1.1","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":3,"stem":"A psychologist wants evidence that a child's home environment influences vocabulary size. Which of the following findings would provide the strongest support for this idea?","options":["Identical twins raised in the same home have more similar vocabulary scores than identical twins raised in different homes.","Identical twins have more similar vocabulary scores than fraternal twins, whether they are raised together or apart.","Adopted children's vocabulary scores are more similar to their biological parents' scores than to their adoptive parents' scores.","Identical twins raised in different homes have more similar vocabulary scores than fraternal twins raised in the same home."],"answer":0,"rationale":"Comparing identical twins raised together with identical twins raised apart holds genes constant, so any extra similarity in the twins raised together must come from their shared environment. The other findings all point to heredity, because in each case the more genetically similar people are more alike."},{"id":"U1-011","section":"1.1","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"On his first day at a new international school, Mateus notices that students quickly sort newcomers into 'people like us' and 'people not like us' within minutes of meeting them. According to evolutionary psychologists, this tendency to rapidly form in-groups and out-groups most likely developed because it","options":["helped ancestors measure how heritable different traits were within their group","allowed ancestors to divide food fairly among close family members","made it easier for ancestors to teach language to young children","helped ancestors quickly judge whether unfamiliar people were likely to be allies or possible threats"],"answer":3,"rationale":"Evolutionary psychologists argue that quickly categorizing strangers helped our ancestors decide whom to trust and whom to be wary of, which improved survival. The other options describe tasks that do not require sorting unfamiliar people into groups."},{"id":"U1-012","section":"1.1","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"During a class debate on the evolutionary perspective, Arjun says that psychologists should be aware of an ethical problem in the history of these ideas. Which of the following statements best describes that problem?","options":["Evolutionary research is unethical because it can be carried out only on animals rather than on humans.","Studying evolved behavior always requires deceiving participants, which is never allowed.","Some theorists misused evolutionary ideas to label certain groups of people as inferior and to justify discriminating against them.","Evolutionary theories cannot be studied ethically because they do not use random assignment."],"answer":2,"rationale":"Some theorists applied evolutionary principles in ways that discriminated against others, a movement known as eugenics. A lack of random assignment limits cause-and-effect conclusions, but it does not make a study unethical, and evolutionary research does not depend on deception or on animals only."},{"id":"U1-013","section":"1.1","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Dr. Lindqvist finds that children between the ages of 2 and 6 are much more likely to refuse unfamiliar vegetables and fruits than younger infants are. She proposes that this wariness of new foods appears just as children begin walking and exploring on their own because it would have protected young ancestors from eating poisonous plants. A researcher from which of the following perspectives would be most interested in her explanation?","options":["Behavioral","Evolutionary","Humanistic","Sociocultural"],"answer":1,"rationale":"The explanation focuses on how a behavior (avoiding unfamiliar foods) may have been naturally selected because it helped young children survive, which is the core idea of the evolutionary perspective. A behavioral explanation would focus on rewards and punishments at mealtimes, and a sociocultural one on cultural food practices."},{"id":"U1-014","section":"1.1","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Ancestral humans who were cautious near cliff edges and steep drops were less likely to fall and more likely to live long enough to have children. Over many generations, a wariness of heights became common in the human population. This process is best described as","options":["natural selection","heritability","maturation","assimilation"],"answer":0,"rationale":"Natural selection is the process by which traits that improve survival and reproduction become more common over generations. Heritability describes how much of the variation in a trait is due to genes, and maturation is the biological growth of one individual, not change across generations."},{"id":"U1-015","section":"1.1","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Four psychology students each propose a research question for a class project. Which question would an evolutionary psychologist be most likely to investigate?","options":["How quickly does a neural impulse travel along a myelinated axon?","How do teachers' rewards shape students' study habits over a semester?","How does unconditional positive regard from parents affect a child's self-concept?","Why do young children in many cultures learn to fear snakes and spiders more quickly than flowers or butterflies?"],"answer":3,"rationale":"Evolutionary psychologists ask why certain fears and behaviors might have helped ancestors survive, and readily fearing dangerous animals such as snakes and spiders fits that question. The other questions belong to biological, behavioral and humanistic research."},{"id":"U1-016","section":"1.2","skill":"Concept Recall","difficulty":1,"stem":"A textbook diagram shows a human figure in which only the brain and the spinal cord are shaded. What is one of the main jobs of the system shown?","options":["Carrying messages along the nerves that branch out from the spine to the skin, muscles and organs","Sending hormones through the bloodstream to regulate growth and metabolism","Processing incoming sensory information and sending out commands that direct the body's responses","Producing antibodies that defend the body against infection"],"answer":2,"rationale":"The brain and spinal cord form the central nervous system, which takes in sensory messages, makes sense of them and sends out commands to the body. The nerves that branch out to the skin, muscles and organs belong to the peripheral nervous system, hormones are the endocrine system's job, and antibodies come from the immune system."},{"id":"U1-017","section":"1.1","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":3,"stem":"Researchers want to know whether heredity influences how sociable and outgoing people are. Which of the following findings would provide the strongest evidence for a genetic influence?","options":["Identical twins raised in the same home are more alike in sociability than identical twins raised in different homes.","Identical twins raised in different homes are more alike in sociability than fraternal twins raised in the same home.","Adopted siblings raised in the same home are more alike in sociability than strangers of the same age.","Fraternal twins raised in the same home are more alike in sociability than fraternal twins raised in different homes."],"answer":1,"rationale":"Identical twins raised apart share all their genes but not their home, while fraternal twins raised together share their home but only about half their genes. If the identical twins are still more alike, genes are the best explanation. The other findings show that sharing a home increases similarity, which is evidence for environment."},{"id":"U1-018","section":"1.1","skill":"Data Interpretation","difficulty":2,"stimulus":{"type":"bar","title":"Similarity of pitch-discrimination scores within pairs raised in the same home (hypothetical data)","xLabel":"Type of pair","yLabel":"Correlation between the two people's scores","categories":["Identical twins","Fraternal twins","Nontwin siblings","Adopted siblings"],"series":[{"name":"Correlation","values":[0.78,0.52,0.44,0.18]}],"yMax":1},"stem":"A music researcher measured the ability to tell apart two very similar musical notes in different types of pairs who were all raised in the same home. Which conclusion is best supported by the graph?","options":["The more genetically related a pair is, the more similar their scores tend to be.","Sharing a home has no effect on pitch-discrimination scores.","Identical twins have better pitch discrimination than people in the other groups.","Adopted siblings are more alike in pitch discrimination than nontwin siblings."],"answer":0,"rationale":"Similarity rises as genetic relatedness rises, from adopted siblings (no shared genes) up to identical twins (all genes shared). The graph shows correlations, which describe how alike the two members of a pair are, not how high anyone scored. Adopted siblings still show some similarity, so the shared home appears to matter a little."},{"id":"U1-019","section":"1.2","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Just before she walks on stage for the final of a school debate competition, Mei Ling notices that her mouth feels dry, her hands are cold and clammy, and her stomach feels tight because digestion has slowed. What is producing these changes in her body?","options":["Activity of her parasympathetic nervous system","Activity of her somatic nervous system","A spinal reflex arc","Activity of her sympathetic nervous system"],"answer":3,"rationale":"Stress before a performance triggers the sympathetic division, which reduces saliva and digestion and pulls blood away from the skin, making the hands feel cold. The parasympathetic division would do the opposite, and the somatic system controls voluntary muscle movement rather than these involuntary changes."},{"id":"U1-020","section":"1.1","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"A psychologist is writing a chapter on how nature and nurture shape development and wants an example that clearly highlights the role of nurture. Which of the following examples should she choose?","options":["Lucas has the same unusual eye color as his biological grandmother, whom he has never met.","Baby Theo began crawling at about the same age as most infants around the world.","Omar grew up hearing his grandparents speak Urdu and his parents speak Arabic, and by age six he speaks both languages fluently.","Twins Aisha and Mariam, raised in different countries, both became nearsighted at around age ten."],"answer":2,"rationale":"Nurture refers to environmental influences such as the languages a child hears at home, so learning two languages from family members is a clear example. Inherited eye color, crawling on a typical maturational timetable and separated twins developing the same condition all point to genetic influences."},{"id":"U1-021","section":"1.2","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Tomás is sitting outside the examiner's office, waiting to be called for his driving test. He can feel his pulse pounding in his neck, and he keeps taking quick, short breaths. Which of the following best explains these changes?","options":["His parasympathetic nervous system is working to conserve his body's energy.","His sympathetic nervous system is preparing his body to respond to a situation he sees as threatening.","His somatic nervous system is voluntarily speeding up his breathing and heart rate.","A spinal reflex arc is speeding up his heart without any involvement of his brain."],"answer":1,"rationale":"Worry about the test activates the sympathetic nervous system, which speeds up heart rate and breathing as part of the fight-or-flight response. The parasympathetic system slows these functions, heart rate is controlled involuntarily rather than by the somatic system, and the reaction starts with his brain's appraisal of the test rather than a spinal reflex."},{"id":"U1-022","section":"1.2","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"When a large dog suddenly barks and runs toward Amira in the park, her heart begins to race. Which of the following pairs of changes is most likely happening in her body at the same time, controlled by the same part of the nervous system?","options":["Her pupils widen and her digestion slows.","Her pupils narrow and her digestion speeds up.","Her saliva increases and her breathing slows.","Her bladder contracts and her blood pressure drops."],"answer":0,"rationale":"A racing heart in a frightening moment signals sympathetic activation, which also widens the pupils and slows digestion. The other pairs include parasympathetic changes, such as narrowed pupils, increased saliva, slower breathing and bladder contraction."},{"id":"U1-023","section":"1.2","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"When Maryam walks into a dark room, her friends jump out and shout 'Surprise!' for her birthday. She gasps and her heart pounds, but over the next few minutes her heart rate slows and her breathing returns to normal. Which part of the nervous system most directly brings her body back to this calm state?","options":["The sympathetic nervous system","The somatic nervous system","Increased release of epinephrine from her adrenal glands","The parasympathetic nervous system"],"answer":3,"rationale":"The parasympathetic nervous system calms the body and conserves energy after a stressful moment, slowing heart rate and breathing. The sympathetic nervous system caused the initial startle response, and releasing more epinephrine would keep her aroused rather than calm her down."},{"id":"U1-024","section":"1.2","skill":"Concept Recall","difficulty":1,"stem":"A biology teacher lists several bodily responses on the board and asks students to circle the one controlled by the parasympathetic nervous system. Which response should they circle?","options":["Widening of the airways in the lungs","Increased activity of the sweat glands","Increased production of saliva","Stronger, faster contractions of the heart"],"answer":2,"rationale":"The parasympathetic nervous system supports rest-and-digest functions, such as producing saliva to help break down food. Widened airways, sweating and a stronger heartbeat are sympathetic changes that prepare the body for action."},{"id":"U1-025","section":"1.2","skill":"Concept Recall","difficulty":1,"stem":"A sudden power cut plunges a crowded mall into darkness, and a loud alarm starts blaring. Which branch of the nervous system springs into action first in the shoppers who feel a jolt of fear?","options":["The parasympathetic nervous system","The sympathetic nervous system","The somatic nervous system","The hippocampus"],"answer":1,"rationale":"Sudden fear immediately triggers the sympathetic nervous system, the fight-or-flight branch of the autonomic nervous system. The parasympathetic system becomes more active later, as the body calms down."},{"id":"U1-026","section":"1.2","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":1,"stem":"Ji-woo is playing a fast piano piece at a school concert. Which of the following most directly carries the messages from her spinal cord to the muscles that move her fingers?","options":["The peripheral nervous system","The parasympathetic nervous system","The corpus callosum","The endocrine system"],"answer":0,"rationale":"Nerves of the peripheral nervous system, specifically its somatic division, carry motor commands from the central nervous system to the skeletal muscles of the fingers. The parasympathetic system controls involuntary functions, the corpus callosum connects the two brain hemispheres, and the endocrine system uses hormones."},{"id":"U1-027","section":"1.1","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Researchers in many countries find that people spot an angry face in a crowd of happy faces faster than they spot a happy face in a crowd of angry faces. Some psychologists suggest this happens because quickly noticing possible danger from others helped our ancestors survive. Which psychological perspective best fits this explanation?","options":["Behavioral","Psychodynamic","Humanistic","Evolutionary"],"answer":3,"rationale":"The explanation focuses on how a tendency helped ancestors survive and was passed on, which is the evolutionary perspective. A behavioral explanation would focus on learning through rewards and punishments, and the psychodynamic and humanistic perspectives focus on unconscious conflicts and personal growth."},{"id":"U1-028","section":"0.1","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"set":"u1_12_twins","stimulus":{"type":"table","caption":"Hypothetical study: 40 pairs of twins in which at least one twin was diagnosed with persistent childhood stuttering. Identical twins share all of their genes; fraternal twins share about half, like any pair of siblings.","headers":["Twin type","Pairs studied","Pairs in which both twins stutter","Percentage of pairs"],"rows":[["Identical","20","13","65%"],["Fraternal","20","3","15%"]]},"stem":"Researchers want to understand how heredity and environment interact in the development of persistent stuttering, using the twin data shown in the table. Which psychological perspective would be most useful for answering this question?","options":["Behavioral","Humanistic","Biological","Sociocultural"],"answer":2,"rationale":"The biological perspective examines how genes and other physical factors, interacting with the environment, influence behavior and mental processes. Twin studies are a key tool of this perspective because they compare people who differ in genetic similarity."},{"id":"U1-029","section":"1.1","skill":"Data Interpretation","difficulty":2,"set":"u1_12_twins","stimulus":{"type":"table","caption":"Hypothetical study: 40 pairs of twins in which at least one twin was diagnosed with persistent childhood stuttering. Identical twins share all of their genes; fraternal twins share about half, like any pair of siblings.","headers":["Twin type","Pairs studied","Pairs in which both twins stutter","Percentage of pairs"],"rows":[["Identical","20","13","65%"],["Fraternal","20","3","15%"]]},"stem":"Which of the following conclusions is best supported by the data in the table?","options":["Stuttering in one twin causes the other twin to stutter through imitation.","Genetic similarity appears to be related to the likelihood that both twins stutter.","Genes are the only cause of persistent stuttering.","Shared home environment explains stuttering better than genes, because fraternal twins also grow up together."],"answer":1,"rationale":"Both twins stuttered in 65% of identical pairs but only 15% of fraternal pairs, so the more genetically similar twins were more often alike. Because the data are correlational, they cannot show that one twin causes the other's stuttering, and since identical pairs were not 100% alike, genes cannot be the only cause."},{"id":"U1-030","section":"0.2","skill":"Research Methods and Design","difficulty":2,"set":"u1_12_twins","stimulus":{"type":"table","caption":"Hypothetical study: 40 pairs of twins in which at least one twin was diagnosed with persistent childhood stuttering. Identical twins share all of their genes; fraternal twins share about half, like any pair of siblings.","headers":["Twin type","Pairs studied","Pairs in which both twins stutter","Percentage of pairs"],"rows":[["Identical","20","13","65%"],["Fraternal","20","3","15%"]]},"stem":"Which of the following explains why the researchers in this study could not use an experimental design?","options":["The researchers could not randomly assign participants to be identical or fraternal twins.","The researchers could not measure stuttering in both members of each twin pair.","The sample was too small to calculate percentages.","Twins cannot be observed in their everyday environments."],"answer":0,"rationale":"An experiment requires the researcher to manipulate the independent variable and randomly assign participants to conditions. Twin type is fixed before birth, so the groups already exist and the study is correlational. The table shows that stuttering was measured in both twins and that percentages could be calculated."},{"id":"U1-031","section":"1.2","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which of the following situations is the sympathetic nervous system most directly responsible for?","options":["Lina's breathing slows and her stomach begins digesting food after a big family lunch.","Diego deliberately kicks the ball toward the corner of the goal during a match.","Priya's lower leg jerks forward when the doctor taps just below her kneecap.","Yusuf's heart pounds and his muscles tense as a sandstorm suddenly sweeps toward his family's camp."],"answer":3,"rationale":"The sympathetic nervous system prepares the body for fight or flight in response to danger, as in Yusuf's reaction to the sandstorm. Lina's calm digestion is parasympathetic, Diego's kick is a voluntary somatic action, and Priya's knee jerk is a spinal reflex."},{"id":"U1-032","section":"1.1","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"A teacher asks students to write a question that captures the nature-nurture issue. Which student's question best does this?","options":["Does memory work more like a video recording or more like a reconstruction of past events?","Do children develop through distinct stages or through gradual, continuous change?","Is a child's skill at mathematics mainly the result of inherited ability or of the teaching and practice the child receives?","Does personality stay stable across adulthood, or does it keep changing with age?"],"answer":2,"rationale":"The nature-nurture issue asks how much heredity and environment each contribute to a trait. Only the mathematics question contrasts inherited ability with environmental experience. The questions about stages and stability are separate developmental debates, and the memory question concerns how memory works."},{"id":"U1-033","section":"4.6","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which theory of motivation would most likely suggest that parents are strongly driven to protect and provide for their children, even at great cost to themselves, because ancestors who did so passed on more of their genes to future generations?","options":["Drive-reduction theory","Evolutionary theory","Arousal theory","Incentive theory"],"answer":1,"rationale":"Evolutionary theory explains motives in terms of survival and passing on genes, and protecting one's children helps those shared genes survive. Drive-reduction theory focuses on reducing internal needs such as hunger, arousal theory on seeking an ideal level of stimulation, and incentive theory on external rewards."},{"id":"U1-034","section":"1.2","skill":"Concept Recall","difficulty":1,"stem":"After a school fire drill, the nurse explains how students' bodies return to normal once they realize there is no real fire. Which statement best sums up what the parasympathetic nervous system does in this situation?","options":["It restores the body to a calm, balanced state after a fight-or-flight response.","It speeds up heart rate and breathing so the body can respond to danger.","It carries voluntary movement commands from the brain to the skeletal muscles.","It processes sensory information within the brain and spinal cord."],"answer":0,"rationale":"The parasympathetic division conserves energy and returns the body to homeostasis once a threat has passed, for example by slowing heart rate and breathing. Speeding up the body is the sympathetic division's job, voluntary movement is the somatic system's job, and processing within the brain and spinal cord is the central nervous system's job."},{"id":"U1-035","section":"5.3","skill":"Data Interpretation","difficulty":2,"stimulus":{"type":"bar","title":"Twin pairs in which both twins have ADHD (hypothetical data)","xLabel":"Twin type","yLabel":"Pairs in which both twins have the diagnosis (%)","categories":["Identical twins","Fraternal twins"],"series":[{"name":"Both twins diagnosed","values":[70,32]}],"yMax":100},"stem":"A research team studied twin pairs in which at least one twin has an ADHD diagnosis. Identical twins share all of their genes, while fraternal twins share about half. The pattern in the graph is most consistent with which psychological approach to explaining ADHD?","options":["Behavioral","Humanistic","Psychodynamic","Biological"],"answer":3,"rationale":"Identical twins, who share more genes, were much more likely than fraternal twins to share the diagnosis, which suggests a genetic contribution. Explaining a disorder in terms of genes and brain functioning is the focus of the biological approach."},{"id":"U1-036","section":"1.1","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which of the following research findings provides the strongest support for a biological basis of behavior?","options":["Children who see an older sibling praised for sharing begin to share their toys more often.","Students recall more vocabulary words when they are tested in the same room where they studied.","Children adopted as newborns are more similar in anxiety levels to their biological parents than to the adoptive parents who raised them.","A child who earns stickers for reading at home begins to read more books each week."],"answer":2,"rationale":"Adopted children share genes with their biological parents but their environment with their adoptive parents, so greater similarity to the biological parents points to heredity. The other findings show learning and memory effects that depend on experience."},{"id":"U1-037","section":"1.1","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Babies around the world tend to cry and protest when separated from the caregivers who feed and protect them, and they calm down when the caregiver returns. Which psychological approach would suggest that this distress serves an adaptive function by keeping infants close to the adults who keep them safe?","options":["Humanistic","Evolutionary","Cognitive","Sociocultural"],"answer":1,"rationale":"The evolutionary approach explains behaviors by how they helped our ancestors survive. Infants who stayed close to their protectors were safer from danger, so distress at separation may have been naturally selected."},{"id":"U1-038","section":"1.2","skill":"Concept Recall","difficulty":1,"stem":"Which of the following correctly matches a division of the human nervous system with the two subdivisions it contains?","options":["Peripheral nervous system: somatic and autonomic","Central nervous system: sympathetic and parasympathetic","Autonomic nervous system: brain and spinal cord","Somatic nervous system: central and peripheral"],"answer":0,"rationale":"The peripheral nervous system is divided into the somatic system, which controls voluntary movement, and the autonomic system, which controls involuntary functions. The sympathetic and parasympathetic divisions belong to the autonomic system, and the brain and spinal cord make up the central nervous system."},{"id":"U1-101","section":"1.3","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Hamdan drinks several cups of strong coffee before a late study session and notices that he feels alert and cannot sit still. Which of the following substances would most likely have the opposite general effect on his central nervous system?","options":["Nicotine","Amphetamines","Alcohol","Cocaine"],"answer":2,"rationale":"Caffeine is a stimulant that speeds up central nervous system activity. Alcohol is a depressant that slows it down, partly by strengthening the inhibitory effects of GABA. Nicotine, amphetamines and cocaine are all stimulants, so they would push in the same direction as caffeine."},{"id":"U1-102","section":"1.3","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":1,"stem":"After a shoulder injury, a physical therapist explains to Camila that the signals from her motor neurons are not triggering her biceps muscle properly. Which neurotransmitter do motor neurons release onto muscle fibers to cause them to contract?","options":["Serotonin","Dopamine","GABA","Acetylcholine"],"answer":3,"rationale":"Acetylcholine is released at the junction between motor neurons and muscle fibers and triggers voluntary muscle contraction. Serotonin is linked mainly to mood and sleep, dopamine to reward and movement control in the brain, and GABA is the main inhibitory neurotransmitter."},{"id":"U1-103","section":"1.3","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":1,"stem":"During a school health talk, a doctor explains that alcohol slows activity in brain regions involved in judgment and self-control. As a result, people who have been drinking often say or do things they would normally hold back. This effect is best described as","options":["increased alertness","hallucinations","improved memory formation","reduced inhibition"],"answer":3,"rationale":"Because alcohol is a depressant that slows brain areas involved in judgment, it commonly lowers inhibitions, so people act on impulses they would usually control. It does not increase alertness or improve memory, and hallucinations are typical of hallucinogens rather than alcohol."},{"id":"U1-104","section":"0.4","skill":"Research Methods and Design","difficulty":2,"stem":"A graduate student studying test anxiety publishes a research article that lists each participant's full name, class schedule and anxiety score in a table. Which ethical guideline did the student most clearly fail to follow?","options":["Confidentiality","Debriefing","Informed consent","Protection from harm"],"answer":0,"rationale":"Confidentiality requires researchers to keep participants' identities and personal data private. Names and class schedules make participants easy to identify. Debriefing happens after a study ends, informed consent is agreement given before taking part, and protection from harm concerns physical and psychological risk."},{"id":"U1-105","section":"1.3","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Nicotine fits into receptor sites normally used by acetylcholine and activates them, producing effects similar to those of acetylcholine. In this role, nicotine is best described as","options":["an antagonist","an agonist","a reuptake inhibitor","an inhibitory neurotransmitter"],"answer":1,"rationale":"A drug that binds to a receptor and activates it, mimicking the natural neurotransmitter, is an agonist. An antagonist would occupy the receptor but block its activation. A reuptake inhibitor leaves the neurotransmitter in the synapse longer, and nicotine is not a neurotransmitter made by the body."},{"id":"U1-106","section":"1.3","skill":"Concept Recall","difficulty":1,"stem":"A pharmacology student is sorting psychoactive substances into categories based on how they affect the central nervous system. Alcohol slows reaction time, impairs coordination and decreases neural activity. In which category does alcohol belong?","options":["Stimulants","Hallucinogens","Depressants","Opioids"],"answer":2,"rationale":"Depressants such as alcohol reduce neural activity and slow body functions, which explains the slower reaction time and poor coordination. Stimulants increase neural activity, hallucinogens mainly distort perception, and opioids mainly relieve pain."},{"id":"U1-107","section":"1.3","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Ms. Kapoor has been taking a prescribed opioid painkiller for back pain for several months. She tells her doctor that the dose that once relieved her pain completely now barely helps, and she feels she needs more to get the same relief. Ms. Kapoor is most likely experiencing","options":["withdrawal","tolerance","the placebo effect","reuptake inhibition"],"answer":1,"rationale":"Tolerance develops when the body adapts to a drug with repeated use, so larger doses are needed to produce the same effect. Withdrawal refers to the unpleasant symptoms that appear when a person stops taking a drug, which is not what she describes."},{"id":"U1-108","section":"1.3","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Nadia steps on a sharp pebble and her foot jerks upward before she is even aware of the pain. Which of the following best explains why her foot moved so quickly?","options":["The motor cortex predicted the pebble and sent a movement command in advance.","Interneurons in the spinal cord linked the sensory input to a motor response without waiting for the brain.","Motor neurons carried the pain signal to the brain, which then triggered the movement.","The hypothalamus released a hormone that made the foot muscles contract."],"answer":1,"rationale":"In a spinal reflex, sensory neurons send the signal to interneurons in the spinal cord, which pass it straight to motor neurons. The brain is informed at about the same time, but it is not needed to start the movement. Motor neurons carry messages away from the central nervous system, not toward the brain."},{"id":"U1-109","section":"0.2","skill":"Research Methods and Design","difficulty":1,"stem":"A researcher recruits 80 volunteers and uses a random number generator to assign half of them to listen to slow, calm music for ten minutes and half to sit in silence for ten minutes. She then measures each participant's heart rate. Which of the following best describes this research method?","options":["An experiment","Naturalistic observation","A correlational study","A survey"],"answer":0,"rationale":"The researcher manipulated the independent variable (music or silence), used random assignment to create comparable groups, and measured the dependent variable (heart rate). Those features define an experiment, which is the method that can support cause-and-effect conclusions."},{"id":"U1-110","section":"1.3","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"A health teacher explains that nicotine raises heart rate, increases alertness and speeds up activity in the central nervous system. Based on these effects, nicotine belongs to the same class of drugs as which of the following?","options":["Caffeine","Morphine","Alcohol","Marijuana"],"answer":0,"rationale":"Nicotine and caffeine are both stimulants, drugs that increase neural activity and arousal. Morphine is an opioid, alcohol is a depressant, and marijuana is usually classified as a hallucinogen."},{"id":"U1-111","section":"1.3","skill":"Concept Recall","difficulty":2,"stem":"While gardening, Paolo pricks his finger on a sharp thorn and pulls his hand back before he even notices the pain. Which sequence correctly traces the path of the signal in this reflex?","options":["Skin receptors, then interneurons in the spinal cord, then sensory neurons, then motor neurons, then arm muscles","Skin receptors, then sensory neurons, then the sensory cortex in the brain, then motor neurons, then arm muscles","Arm muscles, then motor neurons, then interneurons in the spinal cord, then sensory neurons, then skin receptors","Skin receptors, then sensory neurons, then interneurons in the spinal cord, then motor neurons, then arm muscles"],"answer":3,"rationale":"In a reflex arc, receptors detect the stimulus, sensory neurons carry the signal to the spinal cord, interneurons relay it, and motor neurons carry the command out to the muscles. The brain is not needed to start a spinal reflex, which is why Paolo moved before he noticed the pain."},{"id":"U1-112","section":"1.3","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"During surgery, doctors sometimes use a medication that attaches to acetylcholine receptors on muscle fibers without activating them. Because acetylcholine can no longer bind to those receptors, the patient's muscles stay completely relaxed. This medication is acting as","options":["an agonist","a stimulant","an antagonist","an endorphin"],"answer":2,"rationale":"An antagonist occupies a receptor so the natural neurotransmitter cannot activate it, which here prevents acetylcholine from triggering muscle contraction. An agonist would activate the receptor and mimic acetylcholine instead."},{"id":"U1-113","section":"1.3","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Bilal's 78-year-old grandfather has started forgetting conversations from earlier in the day, asking the same questions again and again, and getting lost on the way to a bakery he has visited for years. His doctor diagnoses Alzheimer's disease. A loss of neurons that produce which neurotransmitter is most closely linked to these memory symptoms?","options":["Dopamine","Acetylcholine","Norepinephrine","Endorphins"],"answer":1,"rationale":"Alzheimer's disease involves the loss of neurons that produce acetylcholine, a neurotransmitter important for learning and memory. Dopamine is more closely linked to reward and movement, norepinephrine to alertness and arousal, and endorphins to pain relief."},{"id":"U1-114","section":"1.3","skill":"Concept Recall","difficulty":1,"stem":"Serotonin, glutamate and GABA are all best described as","options":["chemical messengers released by neurons that send excitatory or inhibitory signals across synapses","hormones released into the bloodstream by endocrine glands","psychoactive drugs that block the reuptake of other chemicals","types of glial cells that insulate axons with myelin"],"answer":0,"rationale":"Serotonin, glutamate and GABA are neurotransmitters: neurons release them into the synapse, where they make the receiving neuron more or less likely to fire. Hormones travel through the bloodstream, and glial cells are support cells, not chemicals."},{"id":"U1-115","section":"1.3","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"After major knee surgery, Mateo is given a hospital medication that binds to the same receptors as endorphins and strongly reduces his pain. This medication most likely belongs to which class of drugs?","options":["Stimulants","Hallucinogens","Antagonists that block endorphin receptors","Opioids"],"answer":3,"rationale":"Opioids, such as morphine, mimic the body's endorphins and are used medically to relieve severe pain. A drug that blocked endorphin receptors would be an antagonist and would not relieve pain, stimulants increase neural activity, and hallucinogens distort perception."},{"id":"U1-116","section":"1.3","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"A pharmaceutical team is designing a medication that slows the breakdown of a particular neurotransmitter in the synapse so that more of it remains available. Their goal is to ease the memory problems of people in the early stages of Alzheimer's disease. Which neurotransmitter should they target?","options":["Serotonin","Substance P","Acetylcholine","GABA"],"answer":2,"rationale":"Memory loss in Alzheimer's disease is linked to the loss of acetylcholine-producing neurons, so medications that keep more acetylcholine in the synapse can ease symptoms for a time. Substance P is involved in pain, and serotonin and GABA are not the main targets for Alzheimer's memory problems."},{"id":"U1-117","section":"1.3","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Karim, a 28-year-old, begins to experience numbness and weakness in his legs. His doctor explains that his immune system is damaging the fatty coating around axons in his central nervous system. Which of the following is the most likely effect of this damage on his neurons?","options":["Neurotransmitters will no longer be able to bind to receptors on the receiving neuron.","Electrical signals will travel more slowly or become disrupted as they move along the axon.","The neuron's cell body will stop producing neurotransmitters.","The neuron will remain permanently depolarized."],"answer":1,"rationale":"The fatty coating is the myelin sheath, which insulates the axon and speeds up conduction of the action potential. When myelin is damaged, as in multiple sclerosis, signals slow down or fail, which leads to problems such as numbness and weakness. The other options describe problems with receptors, the cell body or the neuron's resting state, none of which is caused by loss of myelin."},{"id":"U1-118","section":"0.5","skill":"Data Interpretation","difficulty":1,"stem":"Sana tracks how many practice questions she completes each day over nine days: 4, 9, 12, 4, 7, 4, 15, 9, 20. What is the mode of this set of values?","options":["4","9","9.3","20"],"answer":0,"rationale":"The mode is the value that appears most often. The number 4 appears three times and 9 appears twice. The value 9 is the median, 9.3 is the mean (rounded to one decimal place), and 20 is simply the highest value in the set."},{"id":"U1-119","section":"1.2","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"After a fall from a ladder, Kwame's spinal cord was damaged in his lower back. His brain is unharmed, yet he can no longer feel touch on his legs or move them on purpose. This case best illustrates which primary function of the central nervous system?","options":["Releasing hormones into the bloodstream that control growth and metabolism","Relaying sensory messages up to the brain and motor commands down to the body","Storing the memories needed to recognize familiar people and places","Carrying signals between the left and right hemispheres of the brain"],"answer":1,"rationale":"The spinal cord is part of the central nervous system and relays sensory information up to the brain and motor commands down to the body, so damage to it cuts this pathway even though the brain is unharmed. Memory storage and communication between the hemispheres happen within the brain, and hormones come from the endocrine system."},{"id":"U1-120","section":"1.3","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Ms. Reyes compares the nervous system to a city's telephone network to help her class remember the roles of neurons and glial cells. Which analogy best captures the difference between the two types of cells?","options":["Neurons are the phone lines that carry messages, and glial cells are the maintenance crews that insulate the lines, hold them in place and clear away debris.","Glial cells are the phone lines that carry messages, and neurons are the maintenance crews that insulate and repair them.","Neurons and glial cells are both phone lines, but glial cells carry only emergency calls from the sense organs.","Glial cells are the callers who decide what to say, and neurons are the recording machines that store old messages."],"answer":0,"rationale":"Neurons generate and transmit electrical messages, much like phone lines. Glial cells support neurons by providing structure, forming insulating myelin and removing waste, much like maintenance crews."},{"id":"U1-121","section":"1.3","skill":"Concept Recall","difficulty":1,"stem":"During a sprint, the motor neurons controlling Akosua's leg muscles fire again and again. However, right after each action potential, a neuron cannot fire again for a very brief moment, no matter how strong the incoming signal is. This brief recovery interval is called","options":["the threshold","the resting potential","reuptake","the refractory period"],"answer":3,"rationale":"The refractory period is the short recovery time after an action potential when the neuron cannot fire again. The threshold is the level of stimulation needed to fire, and the resting potential is the neuron's charge when it is not firing."},{"id":"U1-122","section":"1.3","skill":"Concept Recall","difficulty":1,"stem":"While walking barefoot on the beach, Jae-won feels cool, wet sand under his feet. Which type of neuron carries this touch information from his feet toward his spinal cord and brain?","options":["Motor neurons","Interneurons","Sensory neurons","Glial cells"],"answer":2,"rationale":"Sensory neurons carry information from the body's receptors toward the central nervous system. Motor neurons carry commands in the opposite direction, interneurons connect neurons within the brain and spinal cord, and glial cells support neurons rather than carrying messages."},{"id":"U1-123","section":"1.2","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"At a sports physical, the doctor taps the tendon just below Hiro's kneecap with a small rubber hammer, and his lower leg kicks forward before he has time to think about it. Which statement best explains where this response came from?","options":["The motor cortex planned the kick and sent the command down to his leg.","The spinal cord produced the kick directly, without waiting for a command from the brain.","The cerebellum triggered the kick from a motor habit it had stored.","Hiro learned to kick this way through reinforcement during earlier checkups."],"answer":1,"rationale":"The knee-jerk response is a spinal reflex: the signal travels to the spinal cord, which sends a motor command straight back to the leg muscles without waiting for the brain. Because it is automatic and present without training, it is not planned by the motor cortex, stored by the cerebellum or learned through reinforcement."},{"id":"U1-124","section":"1.3","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"A psychiatrist prescribes a patient with major depressive disorder a medication from the most commonly prescribed class of antidepressants. The medication blocks the reuptake of one particular neurotransmitter, keeping more of it in the synapse. Which neurotransmitter is this medication most likely targeting?","options":["Serotonin","Acetylcholine","GABA","Endorphins"],"answer":0,"rationale":"Low serotonin activity is the neurotransmitter pattern most closely linked to major depressive disorder, and the most common antidepressants block serotonin reuptake. Acetylcholine is linked mainly to muscle movement and memory, GABA to inhibition and calming, and endorphins to pain relief."},{"id":"U1-125","section":"1.3","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Rashid started running in the evenings to get fit. After a few weeks he notices that he feels calm, happy and less bothered by aches at the end of every long run, so he keeps going even on days when he feels tired. Which of the following best explains the good feeling that keeps him running?","options":["Running increases the release of substance P.","Running blocks the release of acetylcholine.","Running raises his levels of ghrelin.","Running increases the release of endorphins in his brain."],"answer":3,"rationale":"Endorphins are natural pain-relieving chemicals released during intense exercise, producing a pleasant, calm feeling sometimes called a runner's high. Substance P increases pain perception, blocking acetylcholine would interfere with muscle movement, and ghrelin signals hunger."},{"id":"U1-126","section":"1.3","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"In a laboratory, a neuron receives a large burst of glutamate from several neighboring neurons at the same moment. Glutamate is the brain's main excitatory neurotransmitter. What is the most likely effect on the receiving neuron?","options":["It becomes less likely to fire, because the signals add up to inhibition.","It fires an action potential that is stronger than its usual action potential.","It becomes more likely to reach threshold and fire an action potential.","Its resting charge becomes more negative, moving it further from threshold."],"answer":2,"rationale":"Excitatory neurotransmitters such as glutamate make the inside of the receiving neuron less negative, moving it toward threshold and making firing more likely. Because of the all-or-none principle, a stronger input does not make an individual action potential stronger."},{"id":"U1-127","section":"1.3","skill":"Data Interpretation","difficulty":2,"stimulus":{"type":"line","title":"Membrane potential of one neuron after a strong stimulus is applied at 1 ms","xLabel":"Time (ms)","yLabel":"Membrane potential (mV)","series":[{"name":"Membrane potential","points":[[0,-70],[1,-70],[1.3,-55],[1.5,-10],[1.8,40],[2.2,25],[2.7,-30],[3.2,-80],[3.8,-76],[4.5,-70],[6,-70]]}],"bands":[{"from":1.3,"to":1.8,"label":"X"},{"from":1.8,"to":3.2,"label":"Y"},{"from":3.2,"to":4.5,"label":"Z"}],"hlines":[{"y":-55,"label":"Threshold"}]},"stem":"Which of the following is occurring during the interval labeled Y on the graph?","options":["Sodium ions are rapidly entering the axon, making the inside less negative.","The neuron is at rest, and the charge inside the cell equals the charge outside.","Neurotransmitters are being reabsorbed by the sending neuron.","Potassium ions are leaving the axon, moving the inside of the cell back toward its negative resting charge."],"answer":3,"rationale":"Interval Y is the fall from the peak, called repolarization, when potassium ions leave the axon and the inside of the cell becomes negative again. The sodium option describes depolarization (interval X). A resting neuron is negative inside, not equal to the outside. Reuptake happens in the synapse and does not appear on a graph of membrane potential."},{"id":"U1-128","section":"1.3","skill":"Concept Recall","difficulty":1,"stem":"Morphine and heroin relieve pain because they bind to receptor sites that are normally used by which of the body's own chemicals?","options":["Dopamine","Endorphins","Glutamate","Serotonin"],"answer":1,"rationale":"Endorphins are the body's natural painkillers. Opioid drugs such as morphine and heroin have a similar shape, so they bind to endorphin receptors and act as agonists, reducing pain."},{"id":"U1-129","section":"1.3","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"After months of studying abroad, Noor returns home and shares a long hug with her grandmother at the airport. She feels a strong rush of warmth and closeness. Which change in her body is most likely contributing to this feeling?","options":["A rise in oxytocin","A drop in endorphins","A rise in ghrelin","A rise in melatonin"],"answer":0,"rationale":"Oxytocin is a hormone linked to social bonding, trust and feelings of closeness, and it rises during warm physical contact such as hugging. Ghrelin signals hunger, melatonin promotes sleep, and a drop in endorphins would not create a pleasant feeling."},{"id":"U1-130","section":"1.3","skill":"Concept Recall","difficulty":1,"stem":"Kofi and Leila are making a poster that matches each hormone studied in AP Psychology with its main function. Which description should they place under oxytocin?","options":["Signals hunger to the brain when the stomach is empty","Rises in the evening to help the body prepare for sleep","Triggers a surge of energy and a faster heartbeat during emergencies","Promotes bonding and trust between people, such as the attachment between parents and newborns"],"answer":3,"rationale":"Oxytocin is associated with social bonding, including parent and infant attachment and trust. Signaling hunger describes ghrelin, preparing for sleep describes melatonin, and the emergency energy surge describes adrenaline."},{"id":"U1-131","section":"1.3","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Daniel usually drinks four cans of cola a day. During a week-long school trip where no caffeinated drinks are available, he has headaches, feels very tired and becomes irritable for the first few days. Daniel's experience is best described as","options":["tolerance","the placebo effect","withdrawal","habituation"],"answer":2,"rationale":"Withdrawal is the set of uncomfortable symptoms that appear when a person stops using a substance the body has adapted to, and caffeine withdrawal commonly causes headaches and fatigue. Tolerance is the need for more of a drug to get the same effect, which is not what Daniel experienced."},{"id":"U1-132","section":"1.3","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":1,"stem":"During a football match, Chidi takes a hard kick to the shin but keeps playing and barely notices the pain until the final whistle. Which natural chemicals produced by his brain most likely reduced his pain during the match?","options":["Substance P","Endorphins","Melatonin","Acetylcholine"],"answer":1,"rationale":"Endorphins are the brain's natural painkillers and are released during stress and intense physical activity, which helps explain why Chidi felt little pain during the match. Substance P does the opposite, increasing pain perception."},{"id":"U1-133","section":"1.2","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":3,"stem":"On a desert hike, Sebastián brushes his hand against a cactus spine and jerks it away before he consciously feels the sting. Which statement correctly describes the roles of the central and peripheral nervous systems in this spinal reflex?","options":["Both systems are involved: peripheral sensory and motor neurons carry the signals in and out, while an interneuron in the spinal cord, part of the central nervous system, links them.","Only the central nervous system is involved, because the spinal cord detects the cactus spine directly.","The autonomic nervous system moves the hand, because the reaction happens without conscious control.","Interneurons in the peripheral nervous system near the hand link the sensory and motor neurons, so the spinal cord is not needed."],"answer":0,"rationale":"A spinal reflex arc requires both systems working together: peripheral sensory and motor neurons carry signals to and from the spinal cord, where interneurons of the central nervous system connect them. The movement uses skeletal muscles, so it is a somatic response rather than an autonomic one, even though it is automatic."},{"id":"U1-134","section":"0.3","skill":"Research Methods and Design","difficulty":2,"stem":"A school nurse helps test a new herbal supplement meant to improve focus. Each student receives a capsule that looks identical, but only some of the capsules contain the supplement. The students and the staff members who score the attention tests do not know which capsules contain the supplement until all the data are collected. This procedure is an example of","options":["random sampling","a single-blind design","the placebo effect","a double-blind design"],"answer":3,"rationale":"In a double-blind design, neither the participants nor the people collecting the data know who is in which condition, which reduces both participant expectations and experimenter bias. A single-blind design hides the condition only from participants. Random sampling describes how participants are chosen, and the placebo effect is an outcome rather than a design."},{"id":"U1-135","section":"1.3","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Dr. Al Mansoori examines brain tissue under a microscope and identifies cells that wrap around axons to form an insulating layer and help clear away waste products. These cells do not generate action potentials themselves. Which type of cell is she most likely observing?","options":["Motor neurons","Sensory neurons","Glial cells","Interneurons"],"answer":2,"rationale":"Glial cells support neurons by providing structure, forming insulating myelin, and removing waste. Motor neurons, sensory neurons and interneurons are all neurons, and neurons are the cells that generate and transmit action potentials."},{"id":"U1-136","section":"1.3","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"A researcher tests a compound that blocks the transporters on the sending neuron that normally pull dopamine back out of the synapse after it is released. Which of the following is the most likely immediate result in the synapse?","options":["Dopamine will be broken down faster, so the signal will end sooner.","Less dopamine will be released from the sending neuron.","Dopamine will stay in the synapse longer and keep stimulating receptors on the receiving neuron.","The receiving neuron will lose its receptors for dopamine."],"answer":2,"rationale":"Reuptake normally clears neurotransmitter from the synapse. Blocking it, as a reuptake inhibitor does, leaves dopamine in the gap where it can keep binding to receptors, which strengthens and prolongs its effect on the receiving neuron."},{"id":"U1-137","section":"1.3","skill":"Concept Recall","difficulty":1,"stem":"After a sending neuron releases serotonin into the synapse, some of the leftover serotonin is drawn back into the sending neuron's terminal buttons so that it can be reused. This process is called","options":["depolarization","the refractory period","the all-or-none principle","reuptake"],"answer":3,"rationale":"Reuptake is the reabsorption of a neurotransmitter by the sending neuron after it has done its job in the synapse. Depolarization and the refractory period are stages of the action potential, and the all-or-none principle describes how a neuron fires either completely or not at all."},{"id":"U1-138","section":"1.3","skill":"Concept Recall","difficulty":1,"stem":"A neuroscience video shows a brief electrical impulse traveling down an axon as the inside of each section of the cell quickly becomes positive and then returns to negative. This traveling impulse is called","options":["a resting potential","the threshold","an action potential","a neurotransmitter"],"answer":2,"rationale":"An action potential is the brief series of electrical charge changes, depolarization followed by repolarization, that travels down the axon when a neuron fires. The resting potential is the charge when the neuron is not firing, and neurotransmitters are chemicals, not electrical impulses."},{"id":"U1-139","section":"1.3","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":1,"stem":"Every time Zara beats a difficult level in a new video game, she feels a rush of pleasure that makes her want to keep playing. Which neurotransmitter is most closely tied to this feeling of reward?","options":["Glutamate","Dopamine","Substance P","Acetylcholine"],"answer":1,"rationale":"Dopamine is the neurotransmitter most closely linked to the brain's reward pathways, and its release makes rewarding activities feel good and motivates people to repeat them. Substance P is linked to pain, and acetylcholine mainly to muscle movement and memory."},{"id":"U1-140","section":"1.3","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"While helping her family prepare dinner, Kiri accidentally cuts her finger with a kitchen knife. The cut throbs and stings for several minutes. What role does substance P play in what she feels?","options":["Substance P helps transmit pain signals, which increases the pain she feels from the cut.","Substance P acts like endorphins, blocking the pain signals from her finger.","Substance P is a hormone that signals the brain when the stomach is empty.","Substance P speeds healing by activating her parasympathetic nervous system."],"answer":0,"rationale":"Substance P is a neurotransmitter involved in sending pain signals, so it contributes to the stinging pain of an injury. Endorphins, not substance P, reduce pain, and the hormone that signals hunger is ghrelin."},{"id":"U1-141","section":"1.3","skill":"Concept Recall","difficulty":1,"stem":"A neuron receives several small excitatory signals from neighboring neurons. Each signal alone is too weak, but when they add together and the charge inside the neuron reaches a certain minimum level, the neuron fires. This minimum level of stimulation is called the","options":["resting potential","refractory period","synaptic gap","threshold"],"answer":3,"rationale":"The threshold is the level of stimulation required to trigger an action potential, and once it is reached the neuron fires completely. The resting potential is the charge before stimulation, and the refractory period comes after firing."},{"id":"U1-142","section":"1.3","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"In a fictional study, Dr. Okafor gives volunteers a nasal spray containing either a hormone or plain salt water before they play a cooperative game with strangers. Volunteers who received the hormone shared more points with strangers, rated them as more trustworthy and felt closer to their team. Which hormone was most likely in the spray?","options":["Leptin","Adrenaline","Oxytocin","Melatonin"],"answer":2,"rationale":"Oxytocin is a hormone associated with trust, social bonding and feelings of closeness to others. Leptin signals fullness, adrenaline prepares the body for emergencies, and melatonin helps regulate sleep."},{"id":"U1-143","section":"1.3","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":1,"stem":"A health textbook describes the typical effects of different classes of drugs. Which description is most consistent with the effects of a hallucinogen?","options":["Feeling more alert, with a faster heart rate and less need for sleep","Seeing colors and shapes shift and hearing sounds that are not actually present","Strong relief from severe pain along with drowsiness","Slowed reaction time, poor coordination and slurred speech"],"answer":1,"rationale":"Hallucinogens distort perception and cognition, which can lead to seeing or hearing things that are not based on real sensory input. Increased alertness describes stimulants, pain relief with drowsiness describes opioids, and slowed reactions with slurred speech describe depressants."},{"id":"U1-144","section":"0.3","skill":"Research Methods and Design","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which of the following research findings best illustrates the placebo effect?","options":["Patients who were told a sugar pill was a strong headache medicine reported less pain afterward than patients who received no pill.","Patients who took a real pain medication reported less pain than patients who took nothing.","Workers became more productive simply because they knew researchers were watching them.","A researcher who expected a treatment to work rated the treated patients as more improved than an independent observer did."],"answer":0,"rationale":"The placebo effect occurs when people improve because they expect a treatment to work, even though the treatment has no active ingredient. A real medication working is a true drug effect, and a researcher's expectations shaping ratings is experimenter bias."},{"id":"U1-145","section":"0.3","skill":"Research Methods and Design","difficulty":2,"stem":"Dr. Haddad is testing whether a new energy drink improves reaction time. She worries that some volunteers already drink a lot of coffee every day, which could make them respond differently to the energy drink. Which procedure would best ensure that heavy and light coffee drinkers are spread evenly across her experimental and control groups?","options":["Randomly selecting volunteers from the general population","Using a double-blind procedure so that no one knows who received which drink","Operationally defining reaction time before the study begins","Randomly assigning volunteers to the energy-drink group or the placebo-drink group"],"answer":3,"rationale":"Random assignment gives every participant an equal chance of being in each group, so preexisting differences such as coffee habits tend to balance out across conditions. Random selection affects how well the sample represents the population, and a double-blind procedure controls expectations, not preexisting differences."},{"id":"U1-146","section":"1.3","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"A road safety video explains why people should never drive after drinking alcohol. Which explanation of alcohol's effects is most accurate?","options":["Alcohol speeds up neural firing, which causes drivers to overreact to small events on the road.","Alcohol blocks endorphin receptors, causing pain that distracts the driver.","Alcohol slows activity in the central nervous system, which delays reaction time and impairs coordination.","Alcohol produces vivid hallucinations in most people who drink it."],"answer":2,"rationale":"Alcohol is a depressant that reduces central nervous system activity, partly by increasing the effects of GABA, so drivers react more slowly and lose coordination. Speeding up neural firing describes stimulants, and vivid hallucinations are typical of hallucinogens."},{"id":"U1-147","section":"1.3","skill":"Concept Recall","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which condition involves the immune system damaging the myelin sheath around neurons in the brain and spinal cord, slowing neural messages and causing symptoms such as blurred vision, numbness and muscle weakness?","options":["Myasthenia gravis","Multiple sclerosis","Alzheimer's disease","Parkinson's disease"],"answer":1,"rationale":"In multiple sclerosis, the immune system attacks myelin in the central nervous system, which slows or disrupts the signals traveling along axons. Myasthenia gravis also involves the immune system, but it attacks acetylcholine receptors at the muscles rather than myelin."},{"id":"U1-148","section":"1.3","skill":"Concept Recall","difficulty":1,"stem":"A neuron in Laila's visual cortex is not currently sending a message. Which statement best describes the electrical state of its membrane?","options":["It is polarized, with the inside of the cell negative compared with the outside.","It has no difference in charge between the inside and the outside.","The inside of the cell is positive compared with the outside.","It is in its refractory period and cannot respond to stimulation."],"answer":0,"rationale":"At rest, a neuron is polarized: the inside of the membrane is negative relative to the outside, at about negative 70 millivolts. The inside becomes positive only briefly during an action potential."},{"id":"U1-149","section":"1.3","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"An emergency room doctor examines a patient whose heart is racing, who cannot sit still and whose brain activity appears greatly increased after taking an unknown drug. Which of the following drugs most likely caused these effects?","options":["Heroin","Alcohol","Marijuana","Cocaine"],"answer":3,"rationale":"Cocaine is a stimulant, a drug that increases nervous system activity, raising heart rate and alertness. Heroin is an opioid and alcohol is a depressant, both of which slow the body, and marijuana mainly distorts perception."},{"id":"U1-201","section":"1.4","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Over six months, Bilal, a 34-year-old engineer, gains a large amount of weight. He reports feeling hungry almost constantly, even shortly after finishing a full meal. A scan reveals a small tumor pressing on a structure that monitors the body's internal state. Where is the tumor most likely located?","options":["In the hippocampus","In the hypothalamus","In the cerebellum","In the primary visual cortex"],"answer":1,"rationale":"The hypothalamus helps regulate hunger and satiety, so a tumor pressing on it can disrupt the signals that tell a person they are full. The hippocampus is involved in forming memories, not in controlling appetite."},{"id":"U1-202","section":"1.4","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"In an approved animal study, Dr. Mensah implants a tiny electrode in a rat's amygdala. The rat is resting calmly in a familiar cage when she switches on a mild current to that region. Which behavior should Dr. Mensah most expect to see next?","options":["The rat freezes and then tries to escape from the cage","The rat falls into a deep, dreamless sleep","The rat begins grooming itself calmly","The rat immediately starts searching for food"],"answer":0,"rationale":"The amygdala is central to detecting threats and producing fear, so stimulating it tends to trigger defensive behaviors such as freezing and fleeing. Sudden searching for food would be more consistent with stimulating the hypothalamus."},{"id":"U1-203","section":"1.4","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"After a skiing accident, Ingrid speaks in long, smooth sentences, but her words are often the wrong ones and her sentences make little sense. When her doctor asks her simple questions, she seems confused about what he means. Which brain area was most likely damaged?","options":["Wernicke's area","Broca's area","The cerebellum","The reticular formation"],"answer":0,"rationale":"Wernicke's area supports language comprehension, so damage leaves speech fluent but meaningless and makes it hard to understand others. Damage to Broca's area would instead make speech slow and effortful while comprehension stays mostly intact."},{"id":"U1-204","section":"2.5","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Before a minor dental procedure, Takeshi is given a sedative. He chats with the nurse during the procedure, but afterward he has no memory of the conversation, even though he can still recall events from earlier that morning. The sedative most likely disrupted his ability to form new memories by affecting which structure?","options":["Cerebellum","Occipital lobe","Medulla","Hippocampus"],"answer":3,"rationale":"The hippocampus is essential for forming new explicit memories, so a drug that disrupts it can block memory for events that happen while the drug is active. Memories formed before the drug was given remain available."},{"id":"U1-205","section":"1.4","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Rafael had his corpus callosum cut to treat severe epilepsy. In a laboratory task, he stares at a dot in the center of a screen while a picture of a key is flashed briefly to the left of the dot, in his left visual field. Where is the picture of the key first processed?","options":["In the visual cortex of the left hemisphere","Equally in both hemispheres through the corpus callosum","In the cerebellum before reaching either hemisphere","In the visual cortex of the right hemisphere"],"answer":3,"rationale":"Each visual field is sent to the opposite hemisphere, so an image in the left visual field goes to the right hemisphere's visual cortex. Because Rafael's corpus callosum is cut, the image cannot be shared with the left hemisphere."},{"id":"U1-206","section":"1.4","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"A nurse visits a patient who has a lesion in Wernicke's area. Which of the following tasks is the patient most likely to struggle with?","options":["Recognizing a familiar coin by touch with eyes closed","Following the nurse's spoken instruction to point to the window","Telling apart the colors of two pens","Keeping balance while standing up from a chair"],"answer":1,"rationale":"Wernicke's area supports understanding of language, so following a spoken instruction depends on it. Identifying an object by touch relies on the somatosensory cortex in the parietal lobe instead."},{"id":"U1-207","section":"2.5","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"When Lucas was nine, a cycling accident injured his cerebellum. Which type of memory would most likely be affected by this injury?","options":["Episodic memory, such as recalling his last birthday","Semantic memory, such as knowing the capital of Japan","Procedural memory, such as learning to swim or play the guitar","Iconic memory, such as a brief visual image of a scene"],"answer":2,"rationale":"The cerebellum contributes to procedural learning, the memory for motor skills and habits. Recalling personal events and facts depends more on the hippocampus and cortex."},{"id":"U1-208","section":"1.4","skill":"Research Methods and Design","difficulty":1,"stem":"In a study on meditation, Dr. Banerjee places a cap of small metal sensors on each volunteer's scalp. The sensors record the overall electrical activity of the brain, moment by moment, while volunteers meditate. Which research tool is Dr. Banerjee using?","options":["Functional magnetic resonance imaging (fMRI)","Computed tomography (CT)","Electroencephalography (EEG)","Lesioning"],"answer":2,"rationale":"An EEG records electrical activity of the brain through electrodes on the scalp. An fMRI measures changes in blood flow inside a scanner rather than recording electrical signals at the scalp."},{"id":"U1-209","section":"1.4","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"A neurologist reviews brain scans from four stroke patients. Which patient is most likely to have aphasia?","options":["Patient whose damage is in the cerebellum","Patient whose damage is in Broca's area of the left frontal lobe","Patient whose damage is in the occipital lobe","Patient whose damage is in the medulla"],"answer":1,"rationale":"Aphasia is an impairment of language, and it most often follows damage to the language areas of the left hemisphere, such as Broca's area or Wernicke's area. Occipital damage affects vision and cerebellar damage affects coordination."},{"id":"U1-210","section":"1.4","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":1,"stem":"Which of the following would most likely be observed in a person whose Broca's area has been damaged?","options":["Speaking fluently but without being able to understand what others say","Being unable to see objects on one side of the room","Speaking in short, effortful phrases while still understanding what others say","Losing the ability to form new memories"],"answer":2,"rationale":"Broca's area is responsible for speech production, so damage causes slow, labored speech while comprehension is largely preserved. Fluent speech with poor comprehension is the pattern seen after damage to Wernicke's area."},{"id":"U1-211","section":"1.4","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":1,"stem":"Amara is a free diver who tries to hold her breath for as long as possible. Eventually, an automatic urge to breathe becomes so strong that she must surface, even though she wants to stay underwater longer. Which brainstem structure is most responsible for this automatic control of breathing?","options":["Amygdala","Cerebellum","Occipital lobe","Medulla"],"answer":3,"rationale":"The medulla, located in the brainstem, controls automatic survival functions such as breathing and heart rate. The cerebellum coordinates movement and balance rather than breathing."},{"id":"U1-212","section":"1.4","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"At age four, Yusuf had a stroke that damaged a large area of his left hemisphere, and doctors warned that he might have lasting problems with speech and with movement on his right side. Two years later, scans show regions of his right hemisphere becoming active when he talks, and he speaks and runs as well as his classmates. Which concept best explains his recovery?","options":["Hemispheric lateralization","Reuptake","Brain plasticity","Contralateral control"],"answer":2,"rationale":"Brain plasticity is the brain's ability to reorganize, which allowed undamaged areas, especially in his right hemisphere, to take over functions lost in the left. Lateralization describes the usual specialization of the hemispheres, which would predict lasting deficits rather than recovery."},{"id":"U1-213","section":"1.4","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Before a car accident, Dimitri was known as a careful, polite manager. Since the accident damaged part of his brain, he makes rude comments in meetings, spends money on impulse and starts projects without thinking through the steps. Which brain area was most likely injured?","options":["Hippocampus","Frontal lobe","Occipital lobe","Cerebellum"],"answer":1,"rationale":"The frontal lobe, especially the prefrontal cortex, supports executive functions such as self-control, planning and judgment. Damage to the hippocampus would mainly affect forming new memories rather than personality and impulse control."},{"id":"U1-214","section":"1.4","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"After a severe brainstem injury, a patient's eyes remain closed and she cannot be roused by loud voices, touch or bright light, even though scans show her cerebral cortex is largely undamaged. Damage to which structure best explains her lack of arousal?","options":["Hippocampus","Reticular formation","Broca's area","Occipital lobe"],"answer":1,"rationale":"The reticular formation, a network running through the brainstem, controls alertness and arousal, so serious damage to it can cause a lasting coma. The hippocampus is involved in memory, and its damage would not prevent a person from waking."},{"id":"U1-215","section":"1.4","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":3,"stem":"Two stroke patients are assessed by a speech therapist. Patient Kwame understands every question perfectly but can only produce a few slow, broken words in reply. Patient Leila speaks quickly and smoothly, but her sentences make no sense and she does not understand the questions. Which pairing best identifies the area most likely damaged in each patient?","options":["Kwame: Broca's area; Leila: Wernicke's area","Kwame: Wernicke's area; Leila: Broca's area","Kwame: cerebellum; Leila: occipital lobe","Kwame: corpus callosum; Leila: hippocampus"],"answer":0,"rationale":"Broca's area controls speech production, so Kwame understands but cannot produce speech. Wernicke's area controls comprehension, so Leila speaks fluently but without meaning and cannot understand others."},{"id":"U1-216","section":"1.4","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"A labeled brain diagram has an arrow pointing to a small structure deep inside the brain. It sits just below the thalamus and is connected by a thin stalk to the pituitary gland. Which function is most associated with the structure the arrow points to?","options":["Forming new long-term memories","Regulating body temperature, hunger and thirst","Coordinating balance and smooth movement","Producing fluent, grammatical speech"],"answer":1,"rationale":"The description fits the hypothalamus, which lies below the thalamus, controls the pituitary gland and maintains internal balance such as temperature, hunger and thirst. Memory formation is a function of the nearby hippocampus."},{"id":"U1-217","section":"1.4","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Since a fall from a ladder at work damaged part of his brain, Aliyu, a hotel receptionist, can still greet guests and answer simple questions. However, he loses track when he must carry out a sequence of tasks, such as checking a guest in, assigning a room and then arranging an airport pickup, and he struggles to plan his shifts ahead of time. Which brain lobes are most associated with the abilities he has lost?","options":["Frontal lobes","Temporal lobes","Occipital lobes","Parietal lobes"],"answer":0,"rationale":"Planning, organizing a sequence of steps and keeping track of goals are executive functions controlled mainly by the frontal lobes. The temporal lobes are more involved in hearing and language comprehension, which Aliyu still manages well."},{"id":"U1-218","section":"1.4","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":1,"stem":"Months after a stroke damaged part of her motor cortex, Lucia gradually regains movement in her hand as neighboring regions of her brain begin to take over control of it. This recovery is an example of","options":["lateralization","reuptake","brain plasticity","contralateral control"],"answer":2,"rationale":"Plasticity is the brain's ability to adapt and reorganize after damage, which allows healthy regions to take over lost functions. Contralateral control describes each hemisphere controlling the opposite side of the body, which does not explain recovery."},{"id":"U1-219","section":"1.4","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":1,"stem":"On a family camping trip in the desert, Khalid lifts a rock and sees a scorpion with its tail raised. His heart pounds and he jumps back. Which brain structure is most closely associated with Khalid's fear reaction?","options":["Hippocampus","Cerebellum","Parietal lobe","Amygdala"],"answer":3,"rationale":"The amygdala detects threats and triggers fear responses. The hippocampus may later help Khalid remember the event, but it is not the main source of the fear reaction."},{"id":"U1-220","section":"1.4","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Brain imaging studies have found that experienced violinists tend to have a larger region of somatosensory cortex devoted to the fingers of their left hand, the hand that presses the strings, than people who do not play an instrument. This difference most likely reflects","options":["contralateral control","the split-brain effect","reuptake of neurotransmitters","brain plasticity"],"answer":3,"rationale":"Years of practice change the brain's structure, an example of plasticity in response to experience. Contralateral control explains why the left hand is mapped in the right hemisphere, but not why the region becomes larger with training."},{"id":"U1-221","section":"0.2","skill":"Research Methods and Design","difficulty":2,"stem":"Over two years, neuropsychologist Dr. Pereira gives dozens of memory tests to a former chef whose hippocampus was damaged by an infection, and she reviews all of his medical records. She finds that he can still learn new cooking techniques, but he cannot remember any of the lessons in which he learned them. In a textbook chapter, she writes that all people with hippocampal damage keep the ability to learn new skills. Which evaluation of her claim is most appropriate?","options":["The claim is justified because two years of testing makes the findings true for everyone.","The claim is justified because medical records are objective data.","The claim is limited because memory tests cannot measure learning.","The claim is limited because it rests on a single case study, and one patient may not represent others."],"answer":3,"rationale":"A case study gives rich, detailed information about one person, but that person may not be representative, so more cases are needed before generalizing. Testing one patient for a long time or using objective records does not make the results apply to everyone."},{"id":"U1-222","section":"0.5","skill":"Data Interpretation","difficulty":2,"stimulus":{"type":"table","caption":"Contralateral control quiz scores (out of 20)","headers":["Student","Score"],"rows":[["Aisha","6"],["Ben","7"],["Chidi","8"],["Dana","8"],["Emre","9"],["Fatima","10"],["Gabriel","19"]]},"stem":"Ms. Farah gives a 20-point quiz on contralateral control to seven students. The scores are shown in the table. Which statement best describes this set of scores?","options":["The distribution is negatively skewed because one unusually low score pulls the mean below the median.","The distribution is normal because the mean and the median are equal.","The distribution is positively skewed because one unusually high score pulls the mean above the median.","The median is higher than the mode, so the distribution is symmetrical."],"answer":2,"rationale":"The mean is about 9.6 and the median is 8, so the mean is higher than the median. A single extreme high score (19) pulls the mean upward, which creates a positive skew."},{"id":"U1-223","section":"1.4","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":1,"stem":"A small injury damages a patch of cortex at the very back of Haruto's brain. Which problem is he most likely to notice afterward?","options":["Trouble finding the right words when he speaks","A gap or blurry patch in part of what he sees","Weakness in the muscles of his legs","Muffled hearing in one ear"],"answer":1,"rationale":"The very back of the cortex is the occipital lobe, which processes visual information, so a small lesion there can cause a loss in part of the visual field. Hearing problems would be linked to the temporal lobes instead."},{"id":"U1-224","section":"1.4","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Since her stroke, Mrs. Santos follows her grandchildren's conversations easily and laughs at their jokes at the right moments. When she tries to reply, though, her words come out slowly and with great effort, in short phrases such as \"Want... water... now.\" Which part of her brain was most likely damaged?","options":["Broca's area","Wernicke's area","The hippocampus","The primary visual cortex"],"answer":0,"rationale":"Broca's area controls speech production, so damage leads to slow, effortful speech with simplified grammar while comprehension remains good. Damage to Wernicke's area would mainly affect her understanding of language."},{"id":"U1-225","section":"1.4","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Dr. Okafor shows a patient a series of photographs of faces. The patient correctly names every familiar person in the photos and describes their hair and clothing in detail. However, she consistently rates frightened faces as calm or neutral. Damage to which structure best explains this pattern?","options":["Occipital lobe","Hippocampus","Amygdala","Cerebellum"],"answer":2,"rationale":"The amygdala helps detect the emotional meaning of stimuli, especially signs of fear, so damage can impair recognition of fearful expressions. Her vision and memory for faces are intact, which rules out the occipital lobe and hippocampus as the main problem."},{"id":"U1-226","section":"1.4","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":1,"stem":"After a stroke affecting his cerebellum, which problem is Mr. Abubakar most likely to experience?","options":["Inability to follow a conversation with his family","Loss of the ability to feel fear","Constant hunger even after large meals","Unsteady walking and jerky, poorly coordinated movements"],"answer":3,"rationale":"The cerebellum coordinates muscle movement and balance, so damage leads to clumsy, unsteady movement. Following a conversation depends on language areas such as Wernicke's area, not the cerebellum."},{"id":"U1-227","section":"1.4","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":1,"stem":"Which of the following tasks would most likely be the hardest for a person with damage to Wernicke's area?","options":["Recognizing a friend's face across the room","Catching a ball thrown gently toward her","Understanding a short story read aloud by a friend","Noticing the smell of coffee brewing"],"answer":2,"rationale":"Wernicke's area is responsible for language comprehension, so understanding a spoken story would be most difficult. Face recognition, catching a ball and smelling depend on other brain areas."},{"id":"U1-228","section":"1.4","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Anika, a concert pianist, can still press each key she aims for after a head injury, but her playing has become jerky and uneven, and her fingers no longer move smoothly from one note to the next. Which brain structure was most likely injured?","options":["Cerebellum","Hypothalamus","Amygdala","Wernicke's area"],"answer":0,"rationale":"The cerebellum coordinates the timing and smoothness of skilled movements, so damage makes practiced actions like playing piano jerky. The hypothalamus regulates internal states such as hunger and temperature, not movement."},{"id":"U1-229","section":"1.4","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Sergio sleeps through traffic noise and the hum of the air conditioner, yet he wakes up instantly when his baby sister starts crying in the next room. Which brain structure plays the biggest role in filtering incoming stimuli and switching him from sleep to alertness?","options":["Reticular formation","Cerebellum","Occipital lobe","Hippocampus"],"answer":0,"rationale":"The reticular formation in the brainstem controls arousal and helps filter which stimuli are important enough to wake us. The hippocampus forms memories and does not control the shift from sleep to wakefulness."},{"id":"U1-230","section":"1.4","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":1,"stem":"Joon-ho is climbing an indoor rock wall. Each time he reaches for a new hold, his body makes dozens of tiny, well-timed corrections so that he does not swing away from the wall. Which brain structure is mainly responsible for these smooth, precise adjustments?","options":["Cerebellum","Thalamus","Amygdala","Medulla"],"answer":0,"rationale":"The cerebellum coordinates voluntary movement and balance, allowing smooth, precise adjustments. The medulla controls automatic functions such as breathing and heart rate, and the thalamus relays sensory information, but neither coordinates balance."},{"id":"U1-231","section":"1.4","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"In an approved animal study, a researcher electrically stimulates one brain region in a calm, friendly cat. Within seconds, the cat arches its back, hisses and attacks a toy in its cage. When the stimulation stops, the cat becomes calm again. Which structure was most likely stimulated?","options":["Amygdala","Cerebellum","Occipital lobe","Medulla"],"answer":0,"rationale":"The amygdala is involved in emotional responses such as aggression and fear, so stimulating it can produce sudden attack behavior. The medulla controls automatic functions like breathing and heart rate rather than emotional behavior."},{"id":"U1-232","section":"1.4","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"After a head injury, Kofi can hear people's voices clearly, but the words no longer have meaning to him and he cannot understand what is being said. Damage to which region of the brain most likely explains Kofi's difficulty?","options":["Left temporal lobe","Right temporal lobe","Left occipital lobe","Right frontal lobe"],"answer":0,"rationale":"Wernicke's area, which supports language comprehension, is located in the temporal lobe of the left hemisphere in most people. The right temporal lobe is less involved in understanding the meaning of words."},{"id":"U1-233","section":"1.4","skill":"Research Methods and Design","difficulty":2,"stem":"Researchers recruit adults aged 60 to 75 and teach half of them a challenging new skill, such as juggling or learning a new language, over three months. Brain scans taken before and after training show increased gray matter in task-related areas for the trained group only. Which conclusion is best supported by this kind of research?","options":["Learning experiences can physically reshape the brain even late in adulthood.","The brain can change its structure only during infancy.","Experience changes how the brain functions but never its physical structure.","The brain stops forming new connections after early adulthood."],"answer":0,"rationale":"Changes in gray matter after training in older adults show that environmental experiences continue to reshape the brain physically across the life span. The finding contradicts the claim that structural change is limited to infancy or early adulthood."},{"id":"U1-234","section":"1.4","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":3,"stem":"A patient recovering from a stroke is given a sheet of paper covered with short lines scattered evenly across the page and asked to cross out every line. She carefully crosses out every line on the right half of the page but leaves every line on the left half untouched, and she insists that she has finished the task. Damage to which brain area most likely explains this pattern?","options":["Left parietal lobe","Cerebellum","Broca's area","Right parietal lobe"],"answer":3,"rationale":"Ignoring the left side of space, called neglect, typically follows damage to the right hemisphere, especially the right parietal lobe, because each hemisphere attends to the opposite side of space. Left hemisphere damage would more likely affect the right side or language."},{"id":"U1-235","section":"1.4","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"A neurosurgeon is explaining risks to two patients who need surgery on the cerebral cortex. For most people, surgery on which part of the brain carries the greatest risk of losing the ability to speak?","options":["The right hemisphere","The corpus callosum","The left hemisphere","The cerebellum"],"answer":2,"rationale":"For most people, speech production and language areas such as Broca's area and Wernicke's area are located in the left hemisphere. The corpus callosum connects the hemispheres but does not itself produce speech."},{"id":"U1-236","section":"1.4","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Researchers record brain activity while volunteers perform different tasks. For most people, which of the following tasks would most likely show greater activity in the right hemisphere than in the left?","options":["Writing a short text message to a friend","Reading a paragraph aloud","Understanding a question spoken by the researcher","Mentally rotating a 3D block to see whether it matches another shape"],"answer":3,"rationale":"For most people, the right hemisphere shows an advantage for spatial tasks such as mental rotation. Writing, reading aloud and understanding speech are language tasks that rely mainly on the left hemisphere."},{"id":"U1-237","section":"1.4","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Mei is choosing between three phone plans. She compares the monthly cost, data limits and contract length of each, predicts which plan will save her the most money over two years, and then signs up for that plan. Which brain region is Mei relying on most as she makes this decision?","options":["The cerebellum","The medulla","The prefrontal cortex","The somatosensory cortex"],"answer":2,"rationale":"Weighing options, predicting outcomes and making judgments are executive functions of the prefrontal cortex in the frontal lobe. The medulla controls automatic functions such as breathing and does not support reasoning."},{"id":"U1-238","section":"1.4","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"A diagram of the left side of the brain labels four regions. Region 1 is just behind the forehead. Region 2 is on the side of the brain, below a deep groove and near the ear. Region 3 is at the top of the brain toward the back of the crown. Region 4 is at the very back of the brain. After a stroke, Olumide can understand others perfectly but has great difficulty controlling his lips and tongue to form words. Which labeled region was most likely damaged?","options":["Region 2, the temporal lobe","Region 3, the parietal lobe","Region 1, the frontal lobe","Region 4, the occipital lobe"],"answer":2,"rationale":"Broca's area and the motor cortex, which control speech production and the muscles used to speak, are in the frontal lobe just behind the forehead. Intact comprehension suggests the temporal lobe, where Wernicke's area is found, was spared."},{"id":"U1-239","section":"1.4","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":3,"stem":"When she was eight months old, Sofia had her whole left cerebral hemisphere surgically removed to stop life-threatening seizures. By age eight, she speaks in full sentences and understands language well. Which explanation best accounts for her recovery of language?","options":["Her left hemisphere regrew within a few years of the surgery.","Language is controlled equally by both hemispheres in all people, so nothing was lost.","Her cerebellum took over the role of Broca's area and Wernicke's area.","Her right hemisphere formed new connections and took over language functions."],"answer":3,"rationale":"Because of plasticity, especially strong in early childhood, the remaining right hemisphere was able to reorganize and assume language functions usually handled by the left. Brain tissue that is removed does not regrow, and language is normally concentrated in the left hemisphere."},{"id":"U1-240","section":"1.4","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Dr. Ruiz measures brain activity in two groups of rats: one that has gone without food for 12 hours and one that has just eaten. She is looking for the brain structure whose activity differs most between the groups because it regulates when the animals start and stop eating. Which structure should she expect to show this difference?","options":["The amygdala","The hypothalamus","The occipital lobe","The cerebellum"],"answer":1,"rationale":"The hypothalamus regulates hunger and eating by monitoring internal signals such as blood sugar and hormones. The amygdala is involved in emotions such as fear rather than the basic regulation of hunger."},{"id":"U1-241","section":"1.4","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"After a stroke, Ines can no longer feel touch or pain on her right hand, although she can still move it. Damage to which area of the brain most likely explains this loss?","options":["Left parietal lobe","Right parietal lobe","Left occipital lobe","Right frontal lobe"],"answer":0,"rationale":"The somatosensory cortex, which processes touch and pain, is in the parietal lobe, and each hemisphere receives sensation from the opposite side of the body. Loss of feeling in the right hand therefore points to the left parietal lobe."},{"id":"U1-242","section":"0.5","skill":"Data Interpretation","difficulty":1,"stem":"Mr. Haddad gives 200 students a test on the lobes of the brain. The scores form a normal distribution. About how many students would be expected to score above the mean?","options":["50","100","68","136"],"answer":1,"rationale":"A normal distribution is symmetrical around the mean, so half of the scores fall above it and half fall below it. Half of 200 students is 100."},{"id":"U1-243","section":"1.4","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"A neurosurgeon recommends cutting the corpus callosum of a 16-year-old patient after years of medication failed to control his condition. The goal of the surgery is to stop abnormal electrical activity from spreading from one hemisphere to the other. Which condition does the patient most likely have?","options":["Severe epilepsy","Major depressive disorder","Insomnia","Generalized anxiety disorder"],"answer":0,"rationale":"Cutting the corpus callosum is a last-resort treatment for severe epilepsy because it prevents seizures from spreading between hemispheres. It is not a treatment for mood, sleep or anxiety disorders."},{"id":"U1-244","section":"1.4","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Mr. Delgado, who is right-handed, has a stroke that damages part of his right hemisphere. Which task is he most likely to find more difficult than before?","options":["Spelling a list of common words","Copying a drawing of a cube with the correct angles and proportions","Following a spoken instruction from his doctor","Naming everyday objects shown to him"],"answer":1,"rationale":"For most right-handed people, the right hemisphere has an advantage for spatial tasks such as copying shapes and judging proportions. Spelling, following speech and naming objects are language tasks that depend mainly on the left hemisphere."},{"id":"U1-245","section":"1.4","skill":"Research Methods and Design","difficulty":1,"stem":"To find out which area of the cerebellum is needed for balance, a research team first trains rats to cross a narrow beam. Under a protocol approved by their university's ethics board, the team then destroys a tiny, precisely located patch of cerebellar tissue in each rat and compares how well the rats cross the beam before and after the surgery. Which research method is the team using?","options":["Case study","Lesioning","Electroencephalography (EEG)","Naturalistic observation"],"answer":1,"rationale":"Lesioning involves deliberately damaging or removing a specific brain area to see which behaviors are affected. A case study is an in-depth look at one individual and does not involve purposely damaging brain tissue."},{"id":"U1-246","section":"1.4","skill":"Research Methods and Design","difficulty":2,"stem":"Dr. Kimura's lab plans to electrically stimulate the lateral hypothalamus of rats that have just finished a full meal and then watch the rats for the next hour. Which hypothesis is the lab most likely testing?","options":["The rats will forget a maze they learned last week.","The rats will start eating again even though they were recently fed.","The rats will stop responding to sounds in the room.","The rats will lose their sense of balance."],"answer":1,"rationale":"The lateral hypothalamus helps trigger hunger, so stimulating it would be expected to make even well-fed animals eat. Forgetting a learned maze would be linked to the hippocampus instead."},{"id":"U1-247","section":"1.4","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":3,"stem":"Three patients with life-threatening seizures need the same surgery to remove a large section of one cerebral hemisphere: a 4-year-old girl, a 25-year-old man and a 60-year-old woman. Assuming the surgery is equally successful in stopping the seizures, which patient is most likely to recover the most function in the long run?","options":["The 4-year-old, because a young brain has greater plasticity","The 25-year-old, because his brain is fully developed","The 60-year-old, because she has more life experience to draw on","All three equally, because removed brain tissue grows back"],"answer":0,"rationale":"Plasticity is greatest in early childhood, so a young child's remaining brain tissue is best able to reorganize and take over lost functions. Removed brain tissue does not regrow, and a fully developed adult brain is less able to reorganize."},{"id":"U1-248","section":"1.4","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":1,"stem":"Ayesha is sorting a box of beads into separate piles by their color and shape. Which lobe of her brain is most responsible for processing the colors and shapes she sees?","options":["Frontal lobe","Temporal lobe","Cerebellum","Occipital lobe"],"answer":3,"rationale":"The occipital lobe contains the primary visual cortex, which processes visual features such as color and shape. The temporal lobe is mainly involved in hearing and language comprehension."},{"id":"U1-249","section":"1.4","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Sixteen-year-old Tariq has been told many times by his older brother that riding an e-scooter in traffic without a helmet is dangerous. Tariq understands the risk when he talks about it, but he keeps doing it with his friends. Which explanation is most consistent with research on the adolescent brain?","options":["His prefrontal cortex, which supports judgment and impulse control, is still developing.","His occipital lobes are not yet able to see traffic clearly.","He scores unusually high on the personality trait of agreeableness.","His medulla is overactive, which raises his heart rate."],"answer":0,"rationale":"The prefrontal cortex, which supports judgment and weighing consequences, continues to mature into the mid-twenties, which helps explain risky choices in adolescence. High agreeableness would not predict ignoring warnings about danger."},{"id":"U1-250","section":"1.4","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which situation shows the medulla at work most clearly?","options":["Priya picks out her cousin's face in a crowded mall.","While Omar sleeps, his heart keeps beating and his breathing stays steady without any conscious effort.","Diego keeps his balance when the metro train suddenly brakes.","Aiko remembers what she ate for breakfast yesterday."],"answer":1,"rationale":"The medulla controls automatic survival functions such as heartbeat and breathing, which continue even during sleep. Keeping balance on a braking train depends mainly on the cerebellum."},{"id":"U1-251","section":"1.4","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":1,"stem":"As Rohan walks past a neighbor's gate, a large dog suddenly lunges at the fence, barking loudly. Rohan jumps back with his heart racing. Which brain structure is primarily responsible for triggering Rohan's fear?","options":["Amygdala","Cerebellum","Hippocampus","Motor cortex"],"answer":0,"rationale":"The amygdala rapidly detects threats and triggers the fear response. The motor cortex helps carry out the jump backward, but it does not produce the feeling of fear."},{"id":"U1-252","section":"1.4","skill":"Concept Recall","difficulty":1,"stem":"On a classroom brain model, Hana's teacher has painted the amygdala, the hippocampus and the hypothalamus the same color to show that they work together in emotion, motivation and memory. The color most likely marks which system?","options":["The limbic system","The reticular activating system","The motor cortex","The somatosensory cortex"],"answer":0,"rationale":"The amygdala, hippocampus and hypothalamus are parts of the limbic system, a network involved in emotion, motivation and memory. The reticular activating system controls arousal and alertness."},{"id":"U1-253","section":"1.4","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which of the following is the best example of experience reshaping the physical structure of the brain?","options":["Each hemisphere of Dev's brain controls movement on the opposite side of his body.","After months of learning to read Braille, more of Dev's somatosensory cortex responds to touch on his reading fingertip.","Dev's leg jerks forward when a doctor taps just below his knee.","A neurotransmitter is reabsorbed by the neuron that released it after Dev touches a hot cup."],"answer":1,"rationale":"Plasticity means the brain's structure and connections change with experience, as when Braille practice enlarges the cortical area devoted to the reading finger. Contralateral control is a fixed feature of brain organization, not a change caused by experience."},{"id":"U1-254","section":"1.4","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Zara checks the time during her morning coffee and suddenly realizes that her driving test began ten minutes ago. Her chest tightens and she feels a wave of panic as she grabs her keys. Which brain structure is primarily responsible for this anxious reaction?","options":["Occipital lobe","Cerebellum","Amygdala","Somatosensory cortex"],"answer":2,"rationale":"The amygdala produces emotional reactions to threats, including anxiety and fear about a situation like missing an important test. The occipital lobe allows her to see the clock but does not create the emotional response."},{"id":"U1-255","section":"1.4","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Years after severe damage to his frontal lobes, Mr. Kowalski can still see clearly, hear well and feel touch normally. Which ability is he least likely to have regained?","options":["Recognizing the colors of traffic lights","Hearing a doorbell ring","Organizing and following through on a multistep plan, such as preparing for a trip","Feeling the texture of a towel"],"answer":2,"rationale":"The frontal lobes support executive functions such as planning, organizing and following through on goals, and severe damage often leaves lasting problems with these skills. Seeing, hearing and touch are processed in the occipital, temporal and parietal lobes."},{"id":"U1-256","section":"0.2","skill":"Research Methods and Design","difficulty":1,"stem":"Dr. Nakamura is studying how families experience a relative's recovery from a brain injury. She interviews family members, asks them to describe in their own words how their relative has changed, and later groups their answers into common themes. Which type of data is Dr. Nakamura collecting?","options":["Quantitative data","Electroencephalography (EEG) data","Likert-scale survey data","Qualitative data"],"answer":3,"rationale":"Qualitative data are non-numerical descriptions, such as interview answers, used to understand people's experiences in depth. Likert-scale ratings produce numbers and are a form of quantitative data."},{"id":"U1-257","section":"1.3","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"A medication for depression blocks the sending neuron from reabsorbing serotonin after it is released. As a result, serotonin stays in the synaptic gap longer and continues to stimulate the receiving neuron. Which term best describes how this medication works?","options":["Antagonist","Tolerance","Reuptake inhibitor","Withdrawal"],"answer":2,"rationale":"A reuptake inhibitor prevents a neurotransmitter from being taken back into the sending neuron, so it remains active in the synapse longer. An antagonist would block receptor sites and reduce the neurotransmitter's effect."},{"id":"U1-258","section":"1.4","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":3,"stem":"A stroke damages only the primary visual cortex in Aditi's left occipital lobe, while both of her eyes remain healthy. Which part of her vision is she most likely to lose?","options":["The left visual field, as seen by both eyes","Everything seen by her left eye only","The right visual field, as seen by both eyes","Everything seen by her right eye only"],"answer":2,"rationale":"The left occipital lobe receives input from the left half of each retina, which captures the right visual field of both eyes. Damage there therefore affects the right visual field in both eyes, not all of one eye."},{"id":"U1-259","section":"1.4","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stimulus":{"type":"passage","text":"Dr. Haddad wants to know whether watching a roller-coaster video through a virtual reality headset is more frightening than watching the same video on a flat screen. He writes each volunteer's name on a slip of paper and draws the slips from a box to assign half of the volunteers to the headset condition and half to the flat-screen condition. While they watch, a sensor tracks each volunteer's heart rate, and afterward each volunteer rates how frightening the video was on a scale from 1 (not at all) to 10 (extremely)."},"stem":"Based on the study described, which brain structure is mainly responsible for speeding up participants' heartbeat while they watch the frightening video?","options":["Broca's area","Hippocampus","Occipital lobes","Medulla"],"answer":3,"rationale":"The medulla in the brainstem controls automatic functions such as heart rate and breathing, which speed up in response to a frightening stimulus. The occipital lobes process the video's images but do not control heart rate."},{"id":"U1-260","section":"1.4","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":3,"stem":"Kalinda lost her sight at age two because both corneas became cloudy. At age 40, she receives corneal transplants, and doctors confirm that her eyes now work normally. Even so, she has great difficulty recognizing faces and judging how far away objects are. Her difficulties most likely come from limited processing in her","options":["visual cortex","corneas","cones","auditory cortex"],"answer":0,"rationale":"Because the visual cortex did not receive normal input during the years when it develops, it never learned to process complex visual information such as faces and depth. Her new corneas work properly, so the problem is in the brain rather than the eye."},{"id":"U1-261","section":"1.4","skill":"Data Interpretation","difficulty":2,"stem":"A map of the somatosensory cortex shows that the lips and fingertips have very large areas of cortex devoted to them, while the back and thighs have much smaller areas, even though the back and thighs are much larger body parts. Which conclusion is best supported by this map?","options":["Larger body parts have more somatosensory cortex devoted to them.","Body parts with greater touch sensitivity have more somatosensory cortex devoted to them.","Body parts closer to the brain always have more somatosensory cortex devoted to them.","Body parts used least often have the most somatosensory cortex devoted to them."],"answer":1,"rationale":"The amount of somatosensory cortex devoted to a body part reflects its sensitivity, not its size. The fingertips are far from the brain yet have a large area, which rules out distance from the brain as the explanation."},{"id":"U1-262","section":"1.4","skill":"Data Interpretation","difficulty":3,"stem":"A split-brain patient stares at the center of a screen. When the word CUP is flashed to his right visual field, he reads it aloud easily. When the word PEN is flashed to his left visual field, he says he saw nothing, yet his left hand reaches under a cloth and picks out a pen by touch. Which conclusion is best supported by these results?","options":["In most people, the right hemisphere controls speech.","After split-brain surgery, information from each visual field still reaches both hemispheres.","The left hand is controlled mainly by the left hemisphere.","In most people, the left hemisphere controls speech, while the right hemisphere can still recognize objects without naming them."],"answer":3,"rationale":"Words in the right visual field reach the left hemisphere, which can name them, while words in the left visual field reach the right hemisphere, which cannot speak but can guide the left hand to pick the object by touch. This shows that language is mostly located in the left hemisphere."},{"id":"U1-263","section":"0.4","skill":"Research Methods and Design","difficulty":2,"stem":"Dr. Chen video-records a session in which Amal, a participant in her study of hemispheric specialization, completes several visual field tasks. A year later, Dr. Chen wants to show the video at an international conference. Which step is most necessary before she shows the video?","options":["Confirm that the conference audience is made up only of trained psychologists","Edit the video to remove the dates on which it was recorded","Tell the audience afterward that Amal took part voluntarily","Obtain Amal's informed consent for the video to be shown at the conference"],"answer":3,"rationale":"Showing an identifiable recording of a participant to a new audience requires the participant's informed consent for that specific use. A professional audience or a statement that she volunteered for the original study does not replace her permission for this new use."},{"id":"U1-264","section":"0.5","skill":"Data Interpretation","difficulty":2,"stimulus":{"type":"table","caption":"Brain structures test results","headers":["Class","Number of students","Mean score","Standard deviation"],"rows":[["Class 1","25","78","3"],["Class 2","25","78","15"]]},"stem":"Two classes took the same test on brain structures. Based on the results in the table, which conclusion is most accurate?","options":["Students in Class 2 scored higher on average than students in Class 1.","The highest and lowest scores in Class 2 differ by exactly 15 points.","Scores in Class 2 are more strongly correlated with each other than scores in Class 1.","Scores in Class 2 are spread out farther from the mean than scores in Class 1."],"answer":3,"rationale":"Standard deviation measures how much scores in a group differ from the group's mean, so the larger value for Class 2 shows more spread. The means are equal, so neither class scored higher on average, and the difference between highest and lowest scores is the range, not the standard deviation."},{"id":"U1-265","section":"1.4","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"During awake brain surgery, a surgeon gently stimulates the upper part of a patient's right temporal lobe to map its functions. Which experience is the patient most likely to report?","options":["Seeing flashes of light","Hearing a buzzing tone","Feeling tingling in the left hand","Feeling a twitch in the left leg"],"answer":1,"rationale":"The temporal lobes contain the auditory cortex, so stimulating it can produce the experience of hearing sounds. Flashes of light would come from stimulating the occipital lobe, and tingling in the hand from the parietal lobe."},{"id":"U1-266","section":"0.2","skill":"Research Methods and Design","difficulty":2,"stem":"Researchers review hospital records for 350 adults who had strokes. They find that patients who received more hours of physical therapy each week tended to regain the ability to walk sooner. The researchers did not decide how much therapy any patient received. Which research method best describes this study?","options":["Correlational","Experimental","Case study","Naturalistic observation"],"answer":0,"rationale":"The researchers measured two existing variables, therapy hours and recovery time, to see whether they are related, which is a correlational method. It is not an experiment because no variable was manipulated and patients were not randomly assigned."},{"id":"U1-267","section":"1.4","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Dr. Lindqvist is comparing two ways of studying Arabic vocabulary. Volunteers in one group listen through headphones as a native speaker pronounces each new word, while volunteers in the other group study the same words silently on flashcards. An fMRI scanner records brain activity during the study session. Which part of the brain should show greater activity in the listening group than in the flashcard group?","options":["Temporal lobes","Occipital lobes","Parietal lobes","Cerebellum"],"answer":0,"rationale":"The temporal lobes contain the auditory cortex, which processes sounds such as spoken words. The occipital lobes would be more active in the flashcard group, because reading depends on visual processing."},{"id":"U1-268","section":"1.4","skill":"Concept Recall","difficulty":2,"stem":"The thalamus relays incoming sensory information to the appropriate areas of the cortex. Which of the following sensory experiences reaches the cortex without first being relayed through the thalamus?","options":["The sound of a phone ringing","The aroma of cardamom coffee","The sight of a desert sunset","The feel of warm sand under bare feet"],"answer":1,"rationale":"Smell is the only sense whose signals travel to the cortex without first passing through the thalamus. Hearing, vision and touch are all relayed through the thalamus."},{"id":"U1-269","section":"1.4","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":1,"stem":"A patient has damage to several structures of the limbic system. Which of the following is most likely to be disrupted?","options":["Sharpness of vision","Fine control of finger movements","The grammar of spoken sentences","Emotional reactions such as fear and pleasure"],"answer":3,"rationale":"The limbic system, which includes the amygdala and hypothalamus, is closely associated with emotion and motivation. Fine finger control depends on the motor cortex and cerebellum, and grammar on Broca's area."},{"id":"U1-270","section":"1.4","skill":"Concept Recall","difficulty":1,"stem":"Which of the following correctly pairs a lobe of the brain with a region it contains?","options":["Parietal lobe: primary auditory cortex","Temporal lobe: motor cortex","Occipital lobe: primary visual cortex","Frontal lobe: somatosensory cortex"],"answer":2,"rationale":"The occipital lobe, at the back of the brain, contains the primary visual cortex. The auditory cortex is in the temporal lobe, the motor cortex in the frontal lobe and the somatosensory cortex in the parietal lobe."},{"id":"U1-271","section":"1.4","skill":"Concept Recall","difficulty":1,"stem":"Because it sends signals that direct the activity of the pituitary gland, which brain structure is often described as a key link between the nervous system and the endocrine system?","options":["Hippocampus","Thalamus","Hypothalamus","Cerebellum"],"answer":2,"rationale":"The hypothalamus controls the pituitary gland, which in turn influences other glands, linking the brain to the endocrine system. The thalamus is a sensory relay station and does not control the pituitary."},{"id":"U1-272","section":"0.3","skill":"Research Methods and Design","difficulty":2,"stem":"Dr. Al Suwaidi is conducting a correlational study on weekly hours of video gaming and speed on a visual search task. She recruits participants only from members of her university's esports club who volunteer, and tests them in a campus laboratory. Why might her results not apply to the general population?","options":["Correlational studies cannot detect whether two variables are related.","The study measures more than one variable for each participant.","A laboratory setting always makes results less accurate than a field setting.","Volunteers from one esports club may differ in important ways from the wider population."],"answer":3,"rationale":"Generalizing to a population requires a representative sample, ideally obtained through random selection, and volunteers from one club may differ from most people. Detecting relationships is exactly what correlational studies do, and laboratory research can still produce accurate results."},{"id":"U1-273","section":"1.4","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"A woman has a rare condition that has destroyed her amygdala on both sides of the brain, while the rest of her brain is healthy. Which behavior is she most likely to show?","options":["Difficulty coordinating her steps while walking","Inability to see colors","Walking right up to a growling, unfamiliar dog without hesitation","Trouble forming grammatical sentences"],"answer":2,"rationale":"The amygdala is essential for detecting threats and feeling fear, so damage to it reduces fear responses to dangerous situations. Coordinating steps depends on the cerebellum, not the amygdala."},{"id":"U1-274","section":"2.5","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Since brain surgery two years ago, Mr. Bose can still describe his years at university and his first job in rich detail. However, he cannot remember the names of the nurses who care for him every day, and he forgets conversations within minutes. Which structure was most likely damaged during the surgery?","options":["Cerebellum","Hippocampus","Broca's area","Hypothalamus"],"answer":1,"rationale":"The hippocampus is critical for forming new explicit memories, so damage leaves older memories intact but prevents new ones from being stored. The cerebellum supports procedural skills rather than memories of new events."},{"id":"U1-275","section":"2.5","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Mariam's family has many photos and videos of her second birthday party, but she has no memory of the event at all. One biological explanation for this kind of early forgetting points to the immaturity of which brain structure during the first few years of life?","options":["Medulla","Hippocampus","Cerebellum","Occipital lobe"],"answer":1,"rationale":"The hippocampus, which is needed to form lasting explicit memories, is not fully developed in the first few years of life, which helps explain infantile amnesia. The medulla is mature early because it controls survival functions such as breathing."},{"id":"U1-276","section":"1.4","skill":"Concept Recall","difficulty":1,"stem":"Which of the following body parts has one of the largest areas of the somatosensory cortex devoted to it?","options":["Back","Lips","Thigh","Upper arm"],"answer":1,"rationale":"The lips are extremely sensitive to touch, so they have a very large area of somatosensory cortex. The back and thigh are large body parts but are much less sensitive, so they receive smaller areas."},{"id":"U1-277","section":"1.4","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":1,"stem":"A researcher wants to measure brain activity linked specifically to fear while volunteers watch short clips of a shark approaching a diver. Which structure should she focus on?","options":["Amygdala","Cerebellum","Occipital lobe","Broca's area"],"answer":0,"rationale":"The amygdala is the brain structure most closely associated with fear. The occipital lobe will be active because volunteers are watching the clips, but its activity reflects vision rather than fear."},{"id":"U1-278","section":"4.6","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Rania's doctor explains why she felt so hungry on a long hiking trip when she skipped lunch. When the stomach is empty, it releases the hormone ghrelin, which increases hunger. Fat tissue releases the hormone leptin, which over the long term signals that the body has enough stored energy and helps reduce appetite. Which statement best describes what the hypothalamus contributes to this process?","options":["It reacts only to external cues, such as seeing or smelling a meal.","It can switch hunger on but has no role in reducing it.","It responds to hormones such as ghrelin and leptin to increase or decrease hunger.","It controls hunger only by sensing how full the stomach is."],"answer":2,"rationale":"The hypothalamus receives hormonal signals such as ghrelin, which increases hunger, and leptin, which reduces appetite, and uses them to regulate hunger and fullness. It responds to internal signals, not only to outside cues, and it helps both start and stop eating."},{"id":"U1-279","section":"1.4","skill":"Concept Recall","difficulty":1,"stem":"Which of the following statements about the reticular activating system is accurate?","options":["It is the main area for understanding spoken language.","It stores long-term memories of facts and events.","It controls the fine coordination of finger movements.","It helps control alertness and the shift between sleep and wakefulness."],"answer":3,"rationale":"The reticular activating system, which runs through the brainstem, regulates arousal and alertness. Understanding spoken language depends on Wernicke's area, and finger coordination on the cerebellum and motor cortex."},{"id":"U1-280","section":"1.4","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"After a stroke, Mr. Ito cannot see anything, yet eye examinations show that his eyes, retinas and optic nerves are completely healthy. Damage to which part of the cerebral cortex best explains his loss of vision?","options":["Temporal lobe","Parietal lobe","Frontal lobe","Occipital lobe"],"answer":3,"rationale":"The occipital lobe contains the primary visual cortex, so damage there can cause blindness even when the eyes work normally. The temporal lobe processes sound and language rather than basic visual input."},{"id":"U1-281","section":"1.4","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which of the following activities depends most on the cerebral cortex?","options":["Keeping the heart beating during deep sleep","Maintaining body temperature on a cold night","Weighing the pros and cons of two job offers before choosing one","Breathing automatically while reading a book"],"answer":2,"rationale":"Decision-making, reasoning and weighing options are higher-order processes carried out by the cerebral cortex, especially the frontal lobes. Heartbeat and breathing are controlled by the medulla, and temperature by the hypothalamus."},{"id":"U1-282","section":"1.4","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"A patient is brought to the hospital after a serious injury to the base of the skull that damaged the medulla. Which problem should the medical team be most concerned about?","options":["Difficulty understanding what others are saying","Dangerous irregularities in his heart rate and breathing","Loss of memories formed before the injury","Trouble judging when he is full after eating"],"answer":1,"rationale":"The medulla controls vital automatic functions such as heartbeat and breathing, so damage to it can be life-threatening. Understanding speech depends on Wernicke's area, and feeling full depends on the hypothalamus."},{"id":"U1-301","section":"1.5","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Grace, a nurse in Abu Dhabi, has just switched from day shifts to a month of night shifts. During her first week she feels groggy at work around 3 a.m. and struggles to fall asleep when she gets home at 8 a.m., even though she is exhausted. Grace's difficulties are most directly linked to which of the following?","options":["REM rebound","A disruption of her circadian rhythm","Hypnagogic sensations","Sleep apnea"],"answer":1,"rationale":"Shift work forces Grace to be awake and asleep at times that clash with her roughly 24-hour internal clock, which is a circadian rhythm disruption. REM rebound is extra REM after REM deprivation, which does not explain her timing problems."},{"id":"U1-302","section":"1.5","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":1,"stem":"During a family camping trip in Hatta, Omar's cousins notice that his father snores very loudly, then goes silent for several seconds, then suddenly snorts and gasps before the snoring starts again. This pattern repeats throughout the night. Omar's father most likely has which of the following?","options":["Narcolepsy","Somnambulism","Sleep apnea","REM sleep behavior disorder"],"answer":2,"rationale":"Loud snoring broken by pauses in breathing and gasping awake is the classic pattern of sleep apnea. Somnambulism (sleepwalking) involves walking or moving during deep NREM sleep, not breathing pauses."},{"id":"U1-303","section":"1.5","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Mr. Villanueva tells his doctor that he feels drowsy all day, even though he spends eight hours in bed each night. An overnight sleep study shows that the oxygen level in his blood drops dozens of times per hour, and each drop is followed by a brief arousal that he does not remember. Which diagnosis best fits these findings?","options":["Insomnia","Narcolepsy","Somnambulism","Sleep apnea"],"answer":3,"rationale":"Repeated drops in blood oxygen followed by brief arousals point to breathing that stops during sleep, which is sleep apnea. Narcolepsy also causes daytime sleepiness, but it involves sudden sleep attacks, not interrupted breathing at night."},{"id":"U1-304","section":"1.5","skill":"Concept Recall","difficulty":1,"stem":"The internal biological cycle of about 24 hours that regulates sleepiness, alertness and body temperature across the day and night is called","options":["a circadian rhythm","REM rebound","sensory adaptation","the refractory period"],"answer":0,"rationale":"A circadian rhythm is the roughly 24-hour biological clock that times the sleep/wake cycle and other body functions. REM rebound is the increase in REM sleep that follows REM deprivation."},{"id":"U1-305","section":"0.5","skill":"Data Interpretation","difficulty":2,"stimulus":{"type":"table","caption":"Hours of Sleep and Driving-Simulator Errors","headers":["Driver","Hours of sleep","Number of errors"],"rows":[["1","4.5","14"],["2","5","12"],["3","5.5","13"],["4","6","9"],["5","6.5","8"],["6","7","8"],["7","8","5"],["8","9","3"]]},"stem":"A research team recorded how many hours each of eight teenage drivers slept the night before a driving-simulator test and how many errors each driver made during the test. Based on the data in the table, which of the following best describes the relationship between sleep and driving errors?","options":["A positive correlation","No relationship","A negative correlation","A perfect positive correlation"],"answer":2,"rationale":"As hours of sleep go up, the number of errors generally goes down, which is a negative correlation. It is not perfect because the points do not fall in an exact straight line (for example, the drivers who slept 6.5 and 7 hours made the same number of errors)."},{"id":"U1-306","section":"1.5","skill":"Concept Recall","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which of the following statements about REM sleep in healthy adults is most accurate?","options":["It is the stage in which sleepwalking most often occurs.","It makes up most of the first 90-minute sleep cycle of the night.","Heart rate and breathing become slow and highly regular during it.","Its EEG pattern resembles wakefulness, and it is the stage most associated with vivid dreaming."],"answer":3,"rationale":"REM sleep shows fast, wake-like brain waves and is when most vivid dreaming occurs. Sleepwalking happens mainly in deep NREM Stage 3, and REM periods are short early in the night and grow longer toward morning."},{"id":"U1-307","section":"1.5","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"A youth football team flies from Dubai to São Paulo, crossing seven time zones. Several players slept on the plane, yet for the next few days they feel wide awake at 2 a.m. local time and sluggish during afternoon training. Assuming no one is ill, which of the following best explains the players' experience?","options":["Their internal sleep/wake clocks are still set to Dubai time and have not yet shifted to the local day-night cycle.","Sleeping on the plane caused them to skip Stage 2 sleep, so their sleep stages now occur in reverse order.","They are experiencing REM rebound because the flight gave them extra REM sleep.","Their Stage 3 sleep has been permanently replaced by REM sleep."],"answer":0,"rationale":"Jet lag happens because the circadian rhythm stays tied to the home time zone for several days after travel. Sleep stages do not reverse, and REM rebound follows a loss of REM sleep, not a gain."},{"id":"U1-308","section":"1.5","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Yusuf is 8 years old, and his grandfather Hamdan is 72. Which of the following is most likely true when their typical sleep is compared?","options":["Hamdan is more likely than Yusuf to sleepwalk.","Hamdan spends a larger share of his sleep in REM than Yusuf does.","Hamdan spends less time in deep Stage 3 sleep and wakes more often during the night than Yusuf.","Hamdan and Yusuf have nearly identical sleep patterns because sleep stages do not change with age."],"answer":2,"rationale":"As people age, deep Stage 3 sleep shrinks and sleep becomes lighter and more broken, so older adults wake more often. Sleepwalking is more common in children, who also have more deep sleep."},{"id":"U1-309","section":"2.5","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Hiroshi spent a long Saturday learning a new dance routine for his school's cultural show. That evening he kept mixing up the order of the steps. After a full night's sleep, he performed the whole routine smoothly on his first try Sunday morning, without any extra practice. Which of the following best explains Hiroshi's improvement?","options":["Proactive interference from older routines disappeared because he forgot those routines overnight.","Sleep helped consolidate the newly learned steps into more stable long-term memory.","The serial position effect made the middle steps easier to remember after sleep.","State-dependent memory improved because he practiced and performed in the same mood."],"answer":1,"rationale":"Consolidation theory holds that sleep helps organize and strengthen new memories, which explains the overnight improvement without extra practice. Nothing in the scenario suggests he forgot older routines or matched his mood."},{"id":"U1-310","section":"1.5","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"set":"u1c_sleep_motor","stimulus":{"type":"passage","text":"Dr. Adeyemi, a sleep researcher, studies whether losing sleep affects learning a new motor skill. In the evening, 40 university students practice typing a five-key sequence (such as 4-1-3-2-4) as quickly and accurately as possible. Dr. Adeyemi then randomly assigns half of the students to sleep in the lab from 11 p.m. to 7 a.m. (8 hours). The other half are kept awake in the lab, playing quiet board games, until 3 a.m. and then sleep from 3 a.m. to 7 a.m. (4 hours). At 9 a.m., every student types the sequence again for 30 seconds. The 8-hour group types a mean of 22 correct sequences, and the 4-hour group types a mean of 15 correct sequences. The difference is statistically significant."},"stem":"Which of the following explanations of why we sleep is most directly supported by Dr. Adeyemi's results?","options":["Sleep exists mainly to keep animals safe and still during dangerous nighttime hours.","Sleep restores the body's physical energy so that muscles can recover from exercise.","Sleep helps consolidate newly learned information and skills into memory.","Dreams are the brain's way of making sense of random signals from the brainstem."],"answer":2,"rationale":"Students who slept a full night performed the newly learned sequence better, which supports the consolidation theory of sleep. Restoration theory is about replenishing depleted resources, and the study measured learning, not physical recovery."},{"id":"U1-311","section":"0.2","skill":"Research Methods and Design","difficulty":1,"set":"u1c_sleep_motor","stimulus":{"type":"passage","text":"Dr. Adeyemi, a sleep researcher, studies whether losing sleep affects learning a new motor skill. In the evening, 40 university students practice typing a five-key sequence (such as 4-1-3-2-4) as quickly and accurately as possible. Dr. Adeyemi then randomly assigns half of the students to sleep in the lab from 11 p.m. to 7 a.m. (8 hours). The other half are kept awake in the lab, playing quiet board games, until 3 a.m. and then sleep from 3 a.m. to 7 a.m. (4 hours). At 9 a.m., every student types the sequence again for 30 seconds. The 8-hour group types a mean of 22 correct sequences, and the 4-hour group types a mean of 15 correct sequences. The difference is statistically significant."},"stem":"Which research method is Dr. Adeyemi using?","options":["A correlational study","A case study","A longitudinal study","An experiment"],"answer":3,"rationale":"Dr. Adeyemi manipulates how long students sleep and randomly assigns them to conditions, which makes this an experiment. A correlational study would only measure sleep that people chose for themselves."},{"id":"U1-312","section":"0.3","skill":"Research Methods and Design","difficulty":2,"set":"u1c_sleep_motor","stimulus":{"type":"passage","text":"Dr. Adeyemi, a sleep researcher, studies whether losing sleep affects learning a new motor skill. In the evening, 40 university students practice typing a five-key sequence (such as 4-1-3-2-4) as quickly and accurately as possible. Dr. Adeyemi then randomly assigns half of the students to sleep in the lab from 11 p.m. to 7 a.m. (8 hours). The other half are kept awake in the lab, playing quiet board games, until 3 a.m. and then sleep from 3 a.m. to 7 a.m. (4 hours). At 9 a.m., every student types the sequence again for 30 seconds. The 8-hour group types a mean of 22 correct sequences, and the 4-hour group types a mean of 15 correct sequences. The difference is statistically significant."},"stem":"How has Dr. Adeyemi operationally defined sleep deprivation in this study?","options":["Being kept awake until 3 a.m. and allowed to sleep only from 3 a.m. to 7 a.m.","The number of correct sequences typed at 9 a.m.","Sleeping in the lab from 11 p.m. to 7 a.m.","Feeling tired while practicing the typing sequence in the evening"],"answer":0,"rationale":"An operational definition states exactly how a variable is produced or measured; here sleep deprivation means sleeping only 4 hours, from 3 a.m. to 7 a.m. The number of correct sequences is the measure of the dependent variable, not the definition of deprivation."},{"id":"U1-313","section":"0.6","skill":"Research Methods and Design","difficulty":2,"stem":"A survey of 900 secondary students in Dubai finds a significant negative correlation between the number of minutes students spend on their phones after 10 p.m. and the number of hours they sleep on school nights. Which of the following conclusions is justified by this finding?","options":["Using a phone late at night causes students to sleep less.","Students who use their phones more after 10 p.m. tend to sleep fewer hours on school nights.","Sleeping less causes students to spend more time on their phones.","Reducing phone use after 10 p.m. would increase students' sleep by a predictable amount."],"answer":1,"rationale":"A correlation only shows that two variables move together; here, more late-night phone use goes with less sleep. Claims that one variable causes the other, in either direction, require an experiment."},{"id":"U1-314","section":"0.4","skill":"Research Methods and Design","difficulty":2,"stimulus":{"type":"table","caption":"Summary of Dr. Nkemelu's Study Protocol","headers":["Protocol element","What the research team did"],"rows":[["Participants","48 football academy players, aged 12 to 14, who report sleeping less than 7 hours a night"],["Agreement to take part","Each player signed a form agreeing to take part"],["Right to withdraw","Players were told they could quit at any time without penalty"],["Assignment to conditions","A computer randomly assigned players to use a blue-light phone filter every evening for a month or to use their phones as usual"],["Risk review","Procedures judged to involve no more than minimal risk"],["After the study","Every player was told the full purpose of the study"]]},"stem":"Dr. Nkemelu tested whether a blue-light phone filter helps young athletes sleep longer. The table summarizes her study protocol. Based on the table, which of the following is the most serious ethical problem with this study?","options":["The players were not debriefed after the study.","The players were not told they could withdraw.","Random assignment should not be used with participants younger than 18.","No parent or guardian gave informed consent for these minors to take part."],"answer":3,"rationale":"Because the players are 12 to 14 years old, a parent or guardian must give informed consent; a minor's own signed agreement counts only as assent. The table shows that the players were told they could withdraw and were debriefed at the end."},{"id":"U1-315","section":"1.5","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Daniela's neurologist prescribes her a stimulant medication to take each morning so that she can stay alert through her workday at a bank. Daniela's medication is most likely treating which sleep disorder?","options":["Insomnia","Somnambulism","Narcolepsy","REM sleep behavior disorder"],"answer":2,"rationale":"Narcolepsy causes overwhelming daytime sleepiness and sudden sleep attacks, so doctors often prescribe stimulants to keep the person awake. Insomnia is difficulty falling or staying asleep, so a stimulant would make it worse."},{"id":"U1-316","section":"1.5","skill":"Concept Recall","difficulty":2,"stem":"During REM sleep, an EEG shows fast, irregular brain waves much like those of an awake person. Which of the following observations explains why REM sleep is called \"paradoxical\"?","options":["Brain waves become slow, large delta waves.","The eyes remain still beneath the eyelids.","Heart rate drops to the lowest level of the night.","The major voluntary muscles are almost completely relaxed, as if paralyzed."],"answer":3,"rationale":"REM is paradoxical because the brain is highly active while the body's voluntary muscles are at their most relaxed. Slow delta waves describe Stage 3 NREM sleep, and the eyes move rapidly during REM."},{"id":"U1-317","section":"0.3","skill":"Research Methods and Design","difficulty":2,"set":"u1c_blanket","stimulus":{"type":"bar","title":"Mean Total Sleep per Night by Blanket Type","xLabel":"Group","yLabel":"Mean total sleep (minutes)","categories":["Group 1: light blanket","Group 2: weighted blanket"],"series":[{"name":"Mean minutes of sleep","values":[362,409]}],"yMax":480},"stem":"Sleep researchers tested whether sleeping under a weighted blanket increases total sleep time in adults with insomnia. Thirty adults with insomnia who had never used a weighted blanket were randomly split into two groups. For two weeks, Group 1 slept under an ordinary light blanket and Group 2 slept under a 7-kilogram weighted blanket. A wrist sleep tracker recorded each participant's total minutes of sleep every night, and the researchers calculated each group's mean. The results are shown in the graph. Why is it necessary for this study to include the group that slept under an ordinary light blanket?","options":["It provides a comparison that shows what total sleep looks like without the weighted blanket.","It doubles the sample size so that the results can be generalized to everyone.","It makes random assignment unnecessary.","It guarantees that no confounding variables can affect the results."],"answer":0,"rationale":"The control group gives a baseline, so researchers can judge whether the weighted blanket (the independent variable) changed total sleep. A control group does not remove every possible confound; random assignment and careful procedures help with that."},{"id":"U1-318","section":"0.3","skill":"Research Methods and Design","difficulty":1,"set":"u1c_blanket","stimulus":{"type":"bar","title":"Mean Total Sleep per Night by Blanket Type","xLabel":"Group","yLabel":"Mean total sleep (minutes)","categories":["Group 1: light blanket","Group 2: weighted blanket"],"series":[{"name":"Mean minutes of sleep","values":[362,409]}],"yMax":480},"stem":"Sleep researchers tested whether sleeping under a weighted blanket increases total sleep time in adults with insomnia. Thirty adults with insomnia who had never used a weighted blanket were randomly split into two groups. For two weeks, Group 1 slept under an ordinary light blanket and Group 2 slept under a 7-kilogram weighted blanket. A wrist sleep tracker recorded each participant's total minutes of sleep every night, and the researchers calculated each group's mean. The results are shown in the graph. What did the researchers manipulate as the independent variable?","options":["The total minutes of sleep recorded by the wrist tracker","Whether participants slept under a light blanket or a weighted blanket","How severe each participant's insomnia was before the study","The two-week length of the study"],"answer":1,"rationale":"The researchers manipulated the type of blanket, so that is the independent variable. Total minutes of sleep is what they measured, which makes it the dependent variable."},{"id":"U1-319","section":"0.1","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Dr. Tanaka explains dreaming by measuring how activity in the pons and the release of the neurotransmitter acetylcholine change when a sleeper enters REM sleep. Which psychological perspective best matches Dr. Tanaka's approach?","options":["Biological","Humanistic","Sociocultural","Behavioral"],"answer":0,"rationale":"The biological perspective explains behavior and mental processes through brain structures, neurotransmitters and other body systems. A sociocultural approach would focus on how culture and social context shape dreams."},{"id":"U1-320","section":"1.5","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Ms. Reyes tells her class that dreams often jump from scene to scene, such as from a desert safari straight into a crowded classroom, because the cortex is trying to weave a story out of random bursts of activity coming up from the brainstem during REM sleep. Ms. Reyes is describing which explanation of dreaming?","options":["Restoration theory","Activation-synthesis theory","Consolidation theory","REM rebound"],"answer":1,"rationale":"Activation-synthesis theory proposes that the brainstem sends random signals during REM and the cortex synthesizes them into a dream story. Consolidation theory instead links sleep and dreaming to organizing memories from the day."},{"id":"U1-321","section":"0.2","skill":"Research Methods and Design","difficulty":1,"stem":"A school counselor wants to find out whether taking a 20-minute nap after lunch causes better performance on a reaction-time test later in the afternoon. Which of the following research methods would best answer this question?","options":["A survey asking students how often they nap","A correlational study comparing students' usual napping habits with their test scores","An experiment that randomly assigns students to nap or to rest quietly without sleeping before the test","A case study of one student who naps every day"],"answer":2,"rationale":"Only an experiment with random assignment can show that napping causes a change in performance. A correlational study could show that nappers score differently, but other variables might explain the link."},{"id":"U1-322","section":"1.5","skill":"Research Methods and Design","difficulty":1,"set":"u1c_hypnogram","stimulus":{"type":"line","title":"One Participant's Sleep Stages Across the Night","xLabel":"Hours since lights out","yLabel":"Sleep stage","series":[{"name":"Sleep stage","points":[[0,0],[0.1,1],[0.25,2],[0.45,3],[0.95,3],[1.1,2],[1.3,1],[1.5,1],[1.6,2],[1.85,3],[2.2,3],[2.4,2],[2.75,1],[3.05,1],[3.2,2],[3.5,3],[3.65,3],[3.85,2],[4.3,1],[4.75,1],[4.9,2],[5.6,2],[5.8,1],[6.4,1],[6.5,0],[6.6,2],[7.1,2],[7.25,1],[7.95,1],[8.0,0]]}],"bands":[{"from":1.3,"to":1.5,"label":"REM"},{"from":2.75,"to":3.05,"label":"REM"},{"from":4.3,"to":4.75,"label":"REM"},{"from":5.8,"to":6.4,"label":"REM"},{"from":7.25,"to":7.95,"label":"REM"}],"yTicks":[0,1,2,3],"yTickLabels":{"0":"Awake","1":"Stage 1 / REM","2":"Stage 2","3":"Stage 3"}},"stem":"The graph shows how one participant moved through the stages of sleep during a night in a sleep lab. Shaded intervals mark REM periods. The stage information in this graph was most likely collected using which of the following tools?","options":["A PET scan","An electroencephalogram (EEG)","A structural MRI","A brain lesion study"],"answer":1,"rationale":"Sleep stages are identified by their distinct brain-wave patterns, which an EEG records through electrodes on the scalp. A structural MRI shows brain anatomy but not the moment-to-moment electrical activity that defines each stage."},{"id":"U1-323","section":"1.5","skill":"Data Interpretation","difficulty":2,"set":"u1c_hypnogram","stimulus":{"type":"line","title":"One Participant's Sleep Stages Across the Night","xLabel":"Hours since lights out","yLabel":"Sleep stage","series":[{"name":"Sleep stage","points":[[0,0],[0.1,1],[0.25,2],[0.45,3],[0.95,3],[1.1,2],[1.3,1],[1.5,1],[1.6,2],[1.85,3],[2.2,3],[2.4,2],[2.75,1],[3.05,1],[3.2,2],[3.5,3],[3.65,3],[3.85,2],[4.3,1],[4.75,1],[4.9,2],[5.6,2],[5.8,1],[6.4,1],[6.5,0],[6.6,2],[7.1,2],[7.25,1],[7.95,1],[8.0,0]]}],"bands":[{"from":1.3,"to":1.5,"label":"REM"},{"from":2.75,"to":3.05,"label":"REM"},{"from":4.3,"to":4.75,"label":"REM"},{"from":5.8,"to":6.4,"label":"REM"},{"from":7.25,"to":7.95,"label":"REM"}],"yTicks":[0,1,2,3],"yTickLabels":{"0":"Awake","1":"Stage 1 / REM","2":"Stage 2","3":"Stage 3"}},"stem":"The graph shows how one participant moved through the stages of sleep during a night in a sleep lab. Shaded intervals mark REM periods. If the researcher woke this participant at 7.5 hours after lights out rather than at 0.75 hours, the participant would most likely","options":["be in deep Stage 3 sleep and very hard to wake","be sleepwalking, because sleepwalking peaks near morning","report hypnagogic sensations such as the feeling of falling","report a vivid, story-like dream"],"answer":3,"rationale":"At 7.5 hours the participant is in the longest REM period of the night, and REM is when vivid dreaming usually occurs. At 0.75 hours the participant is in Stage 3, which is concentrated early in the night and is when sleepwalking is most likely."},{"id":"U1-324","section":"0.5","skill":"Data Interpretation","difficulty":2,"stimulus":{"type":"table","caption":"Errors on the Attention Task","headers":["Participant","7.5-hour group","5-hour group"],"rows":[["1","6","9"],["2","3","11"],["3","5","8"],["4","4","12"],["5","7","10"],["6","5","7"],["7","5","13"]]},"stem":"Dr. Mensah studied how sleep restriction affects attention. Fourteen adults who usually sleep about 7.5 hours a night gave informed consent and were randomly assigned to sleep either 7.5 hours or 5 hours for one night. The next morning, each participant completed a computer attention task, and the number of errors was recorded, as shown in the table. What is the mean number of errors for the 5-hour group?","options":["5 errors","11 errors","13 errors","10 errors"],"answer":3,"rationale":"The 5-hour group's errors add up to 70 (9 + 11 + 8 + 12 + 10 + 7 + 13), and 70 divided by 7 participants is 10. Five errors is the mean of the 7.5-hour group, and 13 is simply the highest score."},{"id":"U1-325","section":"0.5","skill":"Data Interpretation","difficulty":2,"stimulus":{"type":"table","caption":"Minutes Spent in Stage 3 and REM Sleep","headers":["Volunteer","Minutes in Stage 3","Minutes in REM"],"rows":[["Haruka","72","104"],["Kwame","95","96"],["Lucia","80","121"],["Rohan","88","110"],["Mariam","80","119"]]},"stem":"A sleep clinic recorded how many minutes five volunteers spent in Stage 3 sleep and in REM sleep during one night. Which statement about the data in the table is correct?","options":["The median time in Stage 3 sleep is 88 minutes.","The mean time in REM sleep is 110 minutes.","The range of time in Stage 3 sleep is 15 minutes.","The mode of time in REM sleep is 121 minutes."],"answer":1,"rationale":"The REM times add up to 550 minutes, and 550 divided by 5 is 110. The Stage 3 median is 80 (not 88), the Stage 3 range is 95 minus 72, or 23 minutes, and no REM value repeats, so there is no mode."},{"id":"U1-326","section":"1.5","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":1,"stem":"Ahmed's family flew from Dubai to Los Angeles, a city about 12 hours behind Dubai time. For the first few days, Ahmed could barely keep his eyes open at 3 p.m. but was wide awake at 3 a.m. The trip most likely disrupted which of the following?","options":["Neural plasticity","Ahmed's circadian rhythm","The all-or-none principle","Sensory adaptation"],"answer":1,"rationale":"Crossing many time zones leaves the roughly 24-hour sleep/wake cycle out of step with local time, which is jet lag. Sensory adaptation is a drop in sensitivity to an unchanging stimulus and has nothing to do with sleep timing."},{"id":"U1-327","section":"1.5","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":3,"stem":"Thirty-year-old Ngozi insists that she sleeps straight through each night, yet she wakes with headaches and feels exhausted by mid-morning. Her roommate reports hearing her stop breathing and then gasp many times a night. Which of the following best explains why Ngozi feels so tired?","options":["She has insomnia, so she lies awake for hours each night without realizing it.","She has narcolepsy, so she falls directly into REM sleep during the day.","She has sleep apnea, so pauses in breathing repeatedly pull her out of deeper sleep, even though she does not remember waking.","She has REM sleep behavior disorder, so she physically acts out her dreams."],"answer":2,"rationale":"In sleep apnea, breathing stops repeatedly, and each pause causes a brief arousal that breaks up deeper sleep; people rarely remember these awakenings, so they feel tired without knowing why. People with insomnia are usually aware that they cannot fall or stay asleep."},{"id":"U1-328","section":"1.5","skill":"Concept Recall","difficulty":1,"stem":"Which of the following is the most accurate explanation of jet lag?","options":["The body's roughly 24-hour internal clock is out of step with the day-night cycle of the new location.","Air travel causes REM rebound, which keeps travelers awake at night.","Changes in cabin pressure permanently shorten Stage 3 sleep.","Travelers experience their NREM stages in reverse order until they return home."],"answer":0,"rationale":"Jet lag is a mismatch between the circadian rhythm, which is still set to the home time zone, and the new local day and night. REM rebound only follows a loss of REM sleep and does not explain time-zone effects."},{"id":"U1-329","section":"1.5","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which of the following is the best example of a circadian rhythm?","options":["The roughly 90-minute cycle through NREM and REM stages during a night of sleep","A bear becoming much less active during the winter months each year","A person's body temperature rising during the day, peaking in the late afternoon, and falling to its lowest point in the early morning","Feeling tired after a long, difficult exam"],"answer":2,"rationale":"Body temperature follows a daily cycle of about 24 hours, which is what defines a circadian rhythm. The 90-minute sleep cycle repeats several times a night, and a seasonal change in activity follows a yearly cycle."},{"id":"U1-401","section":"1.6","skill":"Research Methods and Design","difficulty":2,"stem":"Dr. Rahman is running a cross-sectional study comparing the hearing of 15-year-olds with that of 65-year-olds. She wants to measure each participant's absolute threshold for a 1,000 Hz tone. Which operational definition would serve her best?","options":["The smallest increase in volume a participant can notice between two tones played one after the other","Whether the participant says that he or she generally hears well","The softest volume, in decibels, at which a participant reports hearing the tone on at least 50% of trials","The number of different tones a participant can correctly name as high or low"],"answer":2,"rationale":"The absolute threshold is the minimum stimulus intensity detected at least 50% of the time, so it must be measured as a specific volume detected on half of the trials. The smallest noticeable increase between two tones is the just-noticeable difference, not the absolute threshold."},{"id":"U1-402","section":"1.6","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"While waiting at a bus stop, Thandiwe reads a text message on her phone, then looks up to read the route number on a bus at the far end of the street. Both the message and the route number look sharp. Which of the following best explains how her eyes keep both images in focus?","options":["Dark adaptation of the rods in her peripheral retina","Sensory interaction between her vision and hearing","Transduction of light by the cones in her fovea","Accommodation, in which the lens changes its shape to focus light on the retina"],"answer":3,"rationale":"Accommodation is the lens changing curvature so that near and far objects are focused on the retina. Transduction converts light into neural signals but does not adjust focus."},{"id":"U1-403","section":"1.6","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":1,"stem":"A mural at Leila's school shows a large, bright yellow sun. After staring at the center of the sun for about 45 seconds, Leila looks at a plain white wall and sees a bluish circle. This experience is best explained by","options":["trichromatic theory","opponent-process theory","color constancy","dark adaptation"],"answer":1,"rationale":"Opponent-process theory holds that color is processed in opposing pairs (red/green, blue/yellow, black/white), so after the yellow-sensing side is overstimulated, the opposing blue side dominates and produces a blue afterimage. Trichromatic theory explains the three cone types but not afterimages."},{"id":"U1-404","section":"1.6","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":1,"stem":"When Mei Ling starts her shift at a perfume counter in a mall, the mix of scents is very strong. By midday she no longer notices the smell at all, until a customer walks in and says how strong it is. Mei Ling's failure to notice the smell is an example of","options":["anosmia","sensory adaptation","synesthesia","the absolute threshold"],"answer":1,"rationale":"Sensory adaptation is reduced sensitivity to a stimulus that stays constant, so the unchanging scents fade from Mei Ling's awareness. Anosmia is a loss of the sense of smell, but Mei Ling's smell still works, as she would notice a new scent."},{"id":"U1-405","section":"1.6","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":3,"stem":"A cafe's logo shows a green leaf with a black outline on a blue background. After staring at a poster of the logo for 40 seconds, Farah looks at a white napkin and briefly sees a red leaf with a white outline on a yellow background. Which of the following statements best explains what Farah sees?","options":["Her red, green and blue cones each fire more strongly after rest, so she sees all three colors brighter.","Her brain keeps the colors of familiar objects constant, so it recolors the logo to match real leaves.","Cells in her visual system that work in opposing pairs (red/green, blue/yellow, black/white) rebound after one member of each pair has been overstimulated.","Signals from her sense of smell interact with her vision because she associates the cafe with food."],"answer":2,"rationale":"Each color in the afterimage is the opponent of the original (green to red, black to white, blue to yellow), which is the pattern predicted by opponent-process theory. Color constancy keeps an object's color stable under different lighting and would not swap every color for its opposite."},{"id":"U1-406","section":"1.6","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Priya and her cousin Arjun walk into their grandmother's kitchen together. Within seconds, Priya says she smells a faint trace of cooking gas, but Arjun cannot smell anything until he leans right next to the stove. Compared with Arjun, Priya most likely has","options":["a lower absolute threshold for smell","a higher absolute threshold for smell","a larger just-noticeable difference for smell","greater sensory adaptation to the smell of gas"],"answer":0,"rationale":"Detecting a fainter stimulus means Priya needs less of the odor to notice it, which is a lower absolute threshold. Sensory adaptation does not fit because both cousins have just walked in, so neither has been exposed long enough to adapt."},{"id":"U1-407","section":"1.6","skill":"Research Methods and Design","difficulty":2,"stem":"In a lab study, blindfolded participants lift two small boxes, one after the other, and say whether the second box is heavier, lighter or the same weight as the first. The researcher gradually changes the weight of the second box across trials. What is the researcher mainly assessing?","options":["The absolute threshold for touch","Sensory adaptation to pressure","The just-noticeable difference","Sensory interaction"],"answer":2,"rationale":"Comparing two stimuli to see the smallest change a person can detect measures the just-noticeable difference (difference threshold). The absolute threshold is the faintest single stimulus a person can detect, not a comparison between two."},{"id":"U1-408","section":"0.2","skill":"Research Methods and Design","difficulty":1,"stem":"A researcher wants to test the hypothesis that the scent of lemon in a classroom causes students to solve more puzzles in 10 minutes. Which research approach would best test this hypothesis?","options":["A correlational study of how often students' homes smell of lemon","An experiment that randomly assigns students to a lemon-scented room or an unscented room","Naturalistic observation of students solving puzzles at a mall","A survey asking students whether scents help them think"],"answer":1,"rationale":"Testing whether something causes a change requires manipulating it and randomly assigning participants, which is an experiment. A correlational study could only show whether lemon scent and puzzle-solving tend to go together."},{"id":"U1-409","section":"1.6","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Zainab closes one eye while reading a page of her textbook. Even though each eye has a blind spot, she does not see a hole or gap anywhere on the page. Which of the following best explains why?","options":["The fovea contains extra rods that cover the blind spot.","The lens shifts the image so that no light ever lands on the blind spot.","The optic nerve contains photoreceptors that take over at the blind spot.","The brain fills in the missing area using information from the surrounding image."],"answer":3,"rationale":"The blind spot has no photoreceptors, but the brain fills in the gap using the surrounding visual information, so we perceive a complete scene. The optic nerve carries signals to the brain and does not contain photoreceptors."},{"id":"U1-410","section":"1.6","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Walking through a busy souq, Mateo hears a cart's bell. The sound reaches his right ear slightly sooner and slightly louder than his left ear, and he immediately turns to his right. Mateo's quick reaction depends mainly on which of the following?","options":["Sound localization","Place theory of pitch perception","Sensory adaptation in the cochlea","His absolute threshold for loudness"],"answer":0,"rationale":"Sound localization uses differences in the timing and loudness of a sound at the two ears to judge where it is coming from. Place theory explains how we perceive pitch, not where a sound is."},{"id":"U1-411","section":"1.6","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Compared with a medium taster or a nontaster, a person classified as a supertaster is most likely to","options":["have fewer taste receptors on the tongue than average","find bitter foods such as dark leafy greens or grapefruit unpleasantly strong","be unable to detect umami flavors","depend less on smell when tasting food"],"answer":1,"rationale":"Supertasters have an unusually large number of taste receptors, so tastes, especially bitter ones, feel more intense to them. Having fewer receptors would make a person less sensitive, which describes a nontaster."},{"id":"U1-412","section":"1.6","skill":"Concept Recall","difficulty":1,"stem":"Which structures in the inner ear detect rotation of the head and help a person keep his or her balance?","options":["The ossicles (hammer, anvil and stirrup)","The semicircular canals","The cochlea","The retina"],"answer":1,"rationale":"The fluid-filled semicircular canals detect head rotation and support the vestibular sense of balance. The cochlea is also in the inner ear, but it transduces sound for hearing."},{"id":"U1-413","section":"1.6","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"To cut costs, the new owner of a falafel shop slightly reduces the amount of salt in the recipe. Before opening, he tastes one falafel made with the old recipe and one made with the new recipe and cannot tell any difference, but several regular customers immediately say the falafel tastes less salty. Which concept best explains why the owner and the regular customers perceive the change differently?","options":["Absolute threshold","Sensory adaptation","Sensory interaction","Difference threshold"],"answer":3,"rationale":"The difference threshold (just-noticeable difference) is the smallest change in a stimulus a person can detect; the change was large enough for the regular customers to notice but too small for the owner. The absolute threshold concerns detecting salt at all, not detecting a change in an existing amount."},{"id":"U1-414","section":"1.6","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":1,"stem":"Diego stares at a bright blue square on his laptop screen for one minute, then quickly looks at a blank white document. According to opponent-process theory, the afterimage he sees will most likely be","options":["yellow","blue","red","green"],"answer":0,"rationale":"Blue and yellow are an opponent pair, so after the blue response is overstimulated, the yellow response dominates and produces a yellow afterimage. Red and green form a separate opponent pair."},{"id":"U1-415","section":"1.6","skill":"Concept Recall","difficulty":1,"stem":"The process in which the lens of the eye changes its curvature to focus light from near or distant objects onto the retina is called","options":["transduction","dark adaptation","accommodation","sensory interaction"],"answer":2,"rationale":"Accommodation is the lens changing shape to focus images on the retina. Transduction is the conversion of light into neural signals, which happens after the image is focused."},{"id":"U1-416","section":"1.6","skill":"Concept Recall","difficulty":1,"stem":"A person with dichromatism, a form of color vision deficiency, most likely has an irregularity in which of the following?","options":["The rods in the periphery of the retina","The lens","One of the three types of cones","The blind spot"],"answer":2,"rationale":"Color vision deficiency involves damage or irregularities in cones (or the ganglion cells that process their signals), and a dichromat has only two working cone types. Rods do not detect color, so problems with rods would affect night vision instead."},{"id":"U1-417","section":"1.6","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":1,"stem":"Kofi wakes up at night and switches off his bedside lamp to go back to sleep. At first the room looks completely black, but after about 15 minutes he can make out the outline of his wardrobe and door. Which process does this describe?","options":["Dark adaptation","Accommodation","Perceptual adaptation","Sensory interaction"],"answer":0,"rationale":"Dark adaptation is the eye's gradual increase in sensitivity after moving into darkness, mainly due to the rods. Accommodation is focusing by the lens and does not change sensitivity to light."},{"id":"U1-418","section":"1.6","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Sana easily notices when her younger brother slips a 200-gram paperback book into her small, nearly empty school bag. Later, he slips the same book into her fully packed travel suitcase, and she does not notice any change in its weight. Which principle best explains why Sana cannot detect the book in the suitcase?","options":["The absolute threshold","Sensory adaptation","Gate control theory","Weber's law"],"answer":3,"rationale":"Weber's law states that the change needed to notice a difference is a constant proportion of the original stimulus, so the same book is a large change for a light bag but a tiny one for a heavy suitcase. The absolute threshold is about detecting a stimulus at all, and Sana can clearly feel both bags."},{"id":"U1-419","section":"1.6","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Rashid has an inner-ear infection that inflames the nerve carrying signals from his semicircular canals to the brain. Which of the following is Rashid most likely to experience?","options":["Trouble telling high-pitched sounds from low-pitched sounds","Loss of feeling in his fingertips","A spinning sensation and difficulty walking in a straight line","Inability to tell where his arms are when his eyes are closed"],"answer":2,"rationale":"The semicircular canals support the vestibular sense, so disrupted signals cause dizziness and poor balance. Knowing where his limbs are without looking is kinesthesis, which depends on receptors in the muscles and joints, not the inner ear."},{"id":"U1-420","section":"1.6","skill":"Concept Recall","difficulty":1,"stem":"Inside the cochlea, hair cells convert the mechanical vibrations of sound into electrochemical signals that travel to the brain. This conversion is called","options":["accommodation","transduction","sensory adaptation","sound localization"],"answer":1,"rationale":"Transduction is the conversion of one form of energy (here, vibration) into neural signals. Sound localization is judging where a sound comes from, which happens after the signals reach the brain."},{"id":"U1-421","section":"1.4","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":1,"stem":"A tumor pressing on the occipital lobes would most likely interfere with a person's ability to","options":["keep his or her balance on a moving bus","process visual information","detect odors","understand spoken language"],"answer":1,"rationale":"The occipital lobes contain the primary visual cortex, so damage there disrupts vision. Understanding spoken language depends mainly on Wernicke's area in the temporal lobe."},{"id":"U1-422","section":"1.6","skill":"Data Interpretation","difficulty":2,"stimulus":{"type":"line","title":"Smallest Detectable Increase in Weight","xLabel":"Weight of the original object (grams)","yLabel":"Extra weight needed to notice a difference (grams)","series":[{"name":"Just-noticeable difference","points":[[100,2],[200,4],[400,8],[600,12],[800,16],[1000,20]]}]},"stem":"Participants in a study compared a series of objects with slightly heavier versions of the same objects. The graph shows how much weight had to be added before participants noticed a difference. Which concept is best illustrated by the graph?","options":["Gate control theory","The absolute threshold","Sensory adaptation","Weber's law"],"answer":3,"rationale":"The extra weight needed is always 2% of the original weight, so the difference threshold grows in proportion to the starting stimulus, which is Weber's law. The absolute threshold would be a single minimum weight that can be detected at all, not a series of comparisons."},{"id":"U1-423","section":"1.6","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":1,"stem":"After rolling down a tall sand dune several times during a desert trip, Musa stands up and feels as if the whole desert is tilting and spinning around him. Which sense is most directly responsible for this feeling?","options":["Kinesthetic","Olfactory","Vestibular","Auditory"],"answer":2,"rationale":"The vestibular sense, based in the semicircular canals, monitors head movement and balance, and repeated rolling leaves it signaling motion after Musa has stopped. Kinesthesis tracks the position and movement of body parts rather than balance."},{"id":"U1-424","section":"1.6","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":1,"set":"u1c_earbuds","stimulus":{"type":"passage","text":"At a youth music festival, audiologist Dr. Okafor recruits 40 teenage volunteers whose hearing tests come back normal. She flips a coin for each volunteer: heads means 30 minutes of music through earbuds at a high volume, and tails means 30 minutes of reading in a quiet tent. Right after the 30 minutes, each volunteer puts on headphones and hears very soft beeps, one at a time, at many different volumes. For each volunteer, Dr. Okafor records the quietest beep volume (in decibels) that the volunteer reports hearing on at least half of the trials. On average, the earbud group needs significantly louder beeps before reporting them."},"stem":"In this study, the quietest beep volume that each volunteer reports hearing on at least half of the trials is best described as the volunteer's","options":["just-noticeable difference","absolute threshold for hearing","volley rate","sensory interaction level"],"answer":1,"rationale":"The absolute threshold is the minimum stimulus intensity that can be detected at least 50% of the time. The just-noticeable difference would require comparing two tones to find the smallest detectable change."},{"id":"U1-425","section":"0.3","skill":"Research Methods and Design","difficulty":1,"set":"u1c_earbuds","stimulus":{"type":"passage","text":"At a youth music festival, audiologist Dr. Okafor recruits 40 teenage volunteers whose hearing tests come back normal. She flips a coin for each volunteer: heads means 30 minutes of music through earbuds at a high volume, and tails means 30 minutes of reading in a quiet tent. Right after the 30 minutes, each volunteer puts on headphones and hears very soft beeps, one at a time, at many different volumes. For each volunteer, Dr. Okafor records the quietest beep volume (in decibels) that the volunteer reports hearing on at least half of the trials. On average, the earbud group needs significantly louder beeps before reporting them."},"stem":"What did the researchers measure as the dependent variable?","options":["Whether volunteers listened to loud music or read in a quiet tent","The hearing test completed before the study","The 30-minute length of each session","The quietest beep volume each volunteer reported hearing"],"answer":3,"rationale":"The dependent variable is what Dr. Okafor measured: the quietest beep each volunteer could hear. Whether volunteers heard loud music or read in a quiet tent was manipulated, so that is the independent variable."},{"id":"U1-426","section":"0.2","skill":"Research Methods and Design","difficulty":1,"set":"u1c_earbuds","stimulus":{"type":"passage","text":"At a youth music festival, audiologist Dr. Okafor recruits 40 teenage volunteers whose hearing tests come back normal. She flips a coin for each volunteer: heads means 30 minutes of music through earbuds at a high volume, and tails means 30 minutes of reading in a quiet tent. Right after the 30 minutes, each volunteer puts on headphones and hears very soft beeps, one at a time, at many different volumes. For each volunteer, Dr. Okafor records the quietest beep volume (in decibels) that the volunteer reports hearing on at least half of the trials. On average, the earbud group needs significantly louder beeps before reporting them."},"stem":"Which research method did the researchers use?","options":["A case study","A correlational study","An experiment","Naturalistic observation"],"answer":2,"rationale":"Dr. Okafor manipulated music exposure and used a coin flip to randomly assign volunteers to conditions, which defines an experiment. A correlational study would measure how much loud music teens already listen to, without assigning it."},{"id":"U1-427","section":"1.6","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"During a school football final, Kenji twists his ankle in the first half but keeps playing hard and barely notices it. After the final whistle, as he sits on the bench and his teammates ask about his ankle, the pain suddenly feels intense. Which of the following best explains the change in Kenji's experience of pain?","options":["Pain is purely physical, so the injury must have become more severe after the final whistle.","Pain depends partly on psychological factors such as attention, so focusing on the match reduced how much pain he felt.","Pain receptors cannot send signals while the leg muscles are moving, so the signals were delayed until he sat down.","Phantom limb sensations from the injury began only after the game ended."],"answer":1,"rationale":"Pain is shaped by the brain as well as the body; gate control theory suggests that attention and context can open or close the \"gate\" for pain signals, so while Kenji was absorbed in the match, the same injury felt less painful. Nothing suggests the injury worsened; the change came from where his attention was."},{"id":"U1-428","section":"1.4","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Luca hit the back of his head on the pavement in a cycling accident. An eye doctor finds that his eyes, retinas and optic nerves are all healthy, yet Luca cannot see objects on the left side of his visual field. Which brain area was most likely damaged?","options":["The occipital lobe","The temporal lobe","The cerebellum","Broca's area"],"answer":0,"rationale":"The occipital lobe at the back of the brain contains the visual cortex, so damage there can cause vision loss even when the eyes work normally. The cerebellum is also at the back of the brain, but it coordinates movement and balance rather than vision."},{"id":"U1-429","section":"1.6","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":1,"stem":"Karim is standing on a paddleboard in the sea when a wave tips the board. Which sense most directly detects the change in the position of his head so that he can try to stay upright?","options":["Gustation","Olfaction","Audition","The vestibular sense"],"answer":3,"rationale":"The vestibular sense detects head position and movement through the semicircular canals and helps maintain balance. Gustation, olfaction and audition do not signal the tilt of the head."},{"id":"U1-430","section":"1.6","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Dimitri's eyes have only two working types of cones, and he has the most common form of this condition. Which of the following tasks in his job as an electrician apprentice would most likely be hardest for him?","options":["Reading small numbers on a meter held at arm's length in good light","Noticing a dim flashlight beam in a dark crawl space","Keeping his balance while standing on a ladder","Telling apart a red wire and a green wire"],"answer":3,"rationale":"With only two working cone types, Dimitri has dichromatism, and the most common form makes red and green hard to tell apart. His sharp vision in good light and his rod-based dim-light vision are unaffected, and balance depends on the vestibular sense."},{"id":"U1-431","section":"2.1","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"In a science-fair demonstration, students wear prism goggles that shift everything they see about 10 centimeters to the right. At first, their throws at a target land off to the side, but after 20 practice throws they hit the target accurately while still wearing the goggles. Which concept best explains this improvement?","options":["Sensory adaptation","Perceptual adaptation","Perceptual constancy","Perceptual set"],"answer":1,"rationale":"Perceptual adaptation is the ability to adjust to a changed or displaced visual field. Sensory adaptation is a drop in sensitivity to an unchanging stimulus, which would not improve aim."},{"id":"U1-432","section":"1.6","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":1,"stem":"At a vegetable market, Aisha picks out the ripe red tomatoes and leaves the unripe green ones. Which cells in her eyes make it possible for her to tell the two colors apart?","options":["Cones, which respond differently to different wavelengths of light","Rods, which are most active in dim light","Hair cells, which bend in response to vibration","Muscles that change the size of the pupil"],"answer":0,"rationale":"Cones contain pigments sensitive to short, medium or long wavelengths, so they allow color vision. Rods do not detect color and mainly help us see in low light."},{"id":"U1-433","section":"1.4","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":3,"stem":"After a stroke, Mrs. Okonkwo has difficulty with vision, hearing, taste and touch, but her sense of smell is completely unaffected. Damage to which brain structure best explains this pattern?","options":["The amygdala","The cerebellum","The hippocampus","The thalamus"],"answer":3,"rationale":"The thalamus relays information from every sense except smell, which is the only sense not processed first in the thalamus. Damage there could disrupt several senses while leaving smell intact."},{"id":"U1-434","section":"1.6","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Stargazing in the desert, Hessa notices that a very faint star is easier to see when she looks slightly to the side of it than when she looks straight at it. Which of the following best explains this?","options":["Cones are more sensitive in dim light and are concentrated in the periphery of the retina.","Rods are more sensitive in dim light and are concentrated outside the fovea.","The fovea contains the blind spot, so stars disappear when viewed directly.","Rods detect color in dim light, which makes the star stand out."],"answer":1,"rationale":"Rods work best in low light and are found mostly in the periphery, so a faint star shows up better when its image falls outside the fovea. The fovea is packed with cones, which need more light, and the blind spot is not in the fovea."},{"id":"U1-435","section":"1.6","skill":"Concept Recall","difficulty":1,"stem":"In vision, light energy is converted into neural signals within the","options":["lens","pupil","occipital lobe","retina"],"answer":3,"rationale":"The rods and cones of the retina transduce light into neural messages. The occipital lobe processes those messages after they arrive, but the conversion itself happens in the eye."},{"id":"U1-436","section":"1.6","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"A physical therapist is checking a patient after a spinal injury. She asks him to close his eyes and bring the tips of his two index fingers together in front of his face. Next, with his eyes still closed, she moves his arm into a position and asks him to copy it with his other arm. Which sense is the therapist most likely testing?","options":["Vestibular sense","Kinesthesis","Olfaction","Vision"],"answer":1,"rationale":"Kinesthesis senses the position and movement of body parts, so a person can coordinate movements without looking. The vestibular sense handles balance and head position rather than the position of the arms and fingers."},{"id":"U1-437","section":"1.6","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Rania sells colorful scarves at an evening market. As the sun sets and the stalls are lit only by a few dim lamps, she finds it hard to tell the blue scarves from the purple ones, although she can still see their shapes. What best accounts for her difficulty?","options":["Cones need more light to work well, so in dim light the rods, which do not detect color, do most of the seeing.","Her rods detect color during the day but become tired by the evening.","Her lenses cannot accommodate in dim light, so colors blur together.","She has adapted to the scarves' colors after looking at them all day."],"answer":0,"rationale":"Cones handle color but need fairly bright light; in dim light vision relies on rods, which detect shape and movement but not color. Rods never detect color, so they cannot \"tire\" of doing so."},{"id":"U1-438","section":"1.6","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":1,"stem":"When Jun first puts on his new glasses in the morning, he clearly feels them resting on his nose and ears. By lunchtime he no longer feels them at all and even searches for them while they are on his face. Which of the following best explains this?","options":["Transduction","Kinesthesis","Sensory adaptation","Perceptual constancy"],"answer":2,"rationale":"Sensory adaptation is the decrease in sensitivity to a constant, unchanging stimulus, such as steady pressure from glasses. Kinesthesis senses the position and movement of body parts, not the steady touch of the frames."},{"id":"U1-439","section":"1.6","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Aaliyah orders a very sweet cup of karak tea. The first sip tastes intensely sweet, but by the time she finishes the cup, the same tea tastes only mildly sweet, even though the sugar is evenly mixed. Which of the following is operating in this situation?","options":["Sensory interaction","The difference threshold","Transduction","Sensory adaptation"],"answer":3,"rationale":"Continuous exposure to the same sweetness lowers the response of the taste receptors, so the tea seems less sweet: sensory adaptation. The difference threshold would involve noticing a change between two different amounts of sugar, but the tea's sweetness did not actually change."},{"id":"U1-440","section":"1.6","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":1,"stem":"Ravi bangs his shin on the corner of a coffee table and immediately grabs and firmly rubs the area around the bump. The pain eases. Which of the following best explains why rubbing reduced the pain?","options":["Phantom limb sensation","Gate control theory","Signal detection theory","Weber's law"],"answer":1,"rationale":"Gate control theory proposes that signals from touch and pressure can close a neural \"gate\" in the spinal cord, reducing the pain signals that reach the brain. Weber's law describes how much a stimulus must change to be noticed, not how rubbing eases pain."},{"id":"U1-441","section":"0.5","skill":"Data Interpretation","difficulty":2,"stem":"Dr. Al Mansoori is studying whether listening to music changes pain perception. Ten volunteers held one hand in cold (but safe) water while music played and then rated their pain from 1 (no pain) to 10 (worst pain). The ratings were 2, 8, 5, 7, 3, 9, 5, 7, 4 and 6. What is the median pain rating?","options":["5","5.5","5.6","7"],"answer":1,"rationale":"In order, the ratings are 2, 3, 4, 5, 5, 6, 7, 7, 8, 9; with ten scores, the median is the average of the two middle scores (5 and 6), which is 5.5. The value 5.6 is the mean (56 divided by 10), not the median."},{"id":"U1-442","section":"1.6","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"A tone of 3,000 Hz vibrates faster than any single auditory neuron can fire. One theory proposes that groups of neurons take turns, with one firing while another rests, so that together their combined firing keeps pace with the sound. Which theory of pitch perception is this?","options":["Place theory","Frequency theory","Volley theory","Gate control theory"],"answer":2,"rationale":"Volley theory explains how neurons firing in alternating, staggered patterns can together match high frequencies. Simple frequency theory requires a single neuron's firing rate to match the sound wave, which is impossible at 3,000 Hz."},{"id":"U1-443","section":"1.6","skill":"Concept Recall","difficulty":1,"stem":"A school vision screening shows that Tomas has the most common form of color vision deficiency. He is most likely to have trouble telling apart which of the following?","options":["Red and green","Blue and yellow","Black and white","Every color, so he sees only shades of gray"],"answer":0,"rationale":"The most common color vision deficiency involves the red-green system. Seeing only shades of gray (monochromatism) is very rare, and blue-yellow deficiency is much less common."},{"id":"U1-444","section":"1.6","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Adel, a retired tailor, has macular degeneration, an eye disease that destroys photoreceptors in and around the fovea while leaving much of the outer retina working. He can still walk around his home easily using his side vision, but he now often confuses navy thread with dark green thread. Which theory best accounts for his new difficulty with colors?","options":["Place theory","Signal detection theory","Trichromatic theory","Gate control theory"],"answer":2,"rationale":"Trichromatic theory explains color vision through three types of cones, and cones are concentrated in the fovea, so losing them leaves Adel with poor color discrimination. The rods in his outer retina still let him get around, but rods do not detect color."},{"id":"U1-445","section":"1.6","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":3,"stem":"An art teacher wants her students to experience an afterimage of a yellow sun with a black outline when they stare at her painting and then look at a white wall. According to opponent-process theory, which painting should she make?","options":["A yellow sun with a black outline","A red sun with a white outline","A green sun with a black outline","A blue sun with a white outline"],"answer":3,"rationale":"Afterimages appear in the opponent color of the original, so a blue sun produces a yellow afterimage and a white outline produces a black one. Painting the sun yellow would produce a blue afterimage, the opposite of what she wants."},{"id":"U1-446","section":"1.6","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":3,"stem":"Mohammed is starting a summer job at a fish market. On his first morning, the smell is overpowering. Which of the following best predicts what will happen over the next few hours, and why?","options":["The smell will seem stronger, because his absolute threshold for smell will drop with exposure.","He will notice the smell much less, because his smell receptors respond less to a strong odor that stays constant.","He will permanently lose his sense of smell, because strong odors destroy olfactory receptors.","The smell will fade, because the thalamus will filter it out before it reaches his olfactory cortex."],"answer":1,"rationale":"Sensory adaptation means receptors reduce their response to an unchanging stimulus, so the smell fades from awareness. The thalamus cannot explain this, because smell is the one sense that is not processed first in the thalamus."},{"id":"U1-447","section":"1.6","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which experience most clearly demonstrates opponent-process theory at work?","options":["After staring at a bright red bus for a minute, Thandeka looks at a white wall and briefly sees a green shape.","Lucas's pupils become smaller when he walks out into bright sunlight.","A white shirt still looks white to Yara under the orange light of a streetlamp.","Kaito can see well in a dark cinema after sitting there for 10 minutes."],"answer":0,"rationale":"A green afterimage after staring at red shows the red/green opponent pair at work. A white shirt looking white under orange light is color constancy, and seeing better in the dark is dark adaptation."},{"id":"U1-448","section":"1.6","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Amara stares at a black silhouette of a falcon printed on a white poster for 30 seconds. When she then looks at a plain gray wall, she sees a pale, whitish falcon shape. Which of the following best explains Amara's experience?","options":["Place theory","Opponent-process theory","Volley theory","Trichromatic theory"],"answer":1,"rationale":"Opponent-process theory includes a black/white pair, so overstimulating the black response leaves the white response dominant, producing a light afterimage. Trichromatic theory describes the three cone types and does not predict afterimages."},{"id":"U1-449","section":"1.6","skill":"Concept Recall","difficulty":3,"stem":"Which of the following research findings offers the most direct physiological evidence that color is processed in opposing pairs, as opponent-process theory proposes?","options":["The retina contains three types of cones that respond best to short, medium and long wavelengths.","Some ganglion cells in the retina are excited by red light and inhibited by green light.","Rods are more sensitive than cones in dim light.","The fovea contains a high concentration of cones."],"answer":1,"rationale":"Ganglion cells that are excited by one color and inhibited by its opponent show color being processed in opposing pairs, as opponent-process theory predicts. The three cone types are the main evidence for trichromatic theory instead."},{"id":"U1-450","section":"1.6","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":3,"stem":"Trichromatic theory, on its own, has difficulty explaining which of the following experiences?","options":["Lina's phone screen produces every color she sees using only tiny red, green and blue lights.","Ali, who lacks working red-sensitive cones, confuses red and green.","Mia can see thousands of different shades of color in a painting.","After staring at a green square, Noor sees a red square on a white page."],"answer":3,"rationale":"Trichromatic theory explains color through three cone types, but it does not predict that staring at green produces a red afterimage; opponent-process theory does. Mixing red, green and blue light to make other colors, and red-green confusion from a missing cone type, are both explained by trichromatic theory."},{"id":"U1-451","section":"1.6","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":1,"stem":"During a school concert, Sofia moves her finger on the violin string so that the next note sounds higher. Which physical property of the sound wave has changed the most?","options":["Its amplitude","Its frequency","Its loudness","Its timbre"],"answer":1,"rationale":"Pitch depends mainly on frequency, the number of sound waves per second, so a higher note has a higher frequency. Amplitude affects loudness, not pitch."},{"id":"U1-452","section":"1.6","skill":"Concept Recall","difficulty":1,"stem":"The area of the retina that contains no rods or cones because the optic nerve leaves the eye there is called the","options":["fovea","cornea","blind spot","pupil"],"answer":2,"rationale":"The blind spot is where the optic nerve exits the eye, so it has no photoreceptors. The fovea is the opposite: the area most densely packed with cones."},{"id":"U1-453","section":"1.6","skill":"Concept Recall","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which of the following correctly matches a physical property of sound with the psychological experience it mainly produces?","options":["Amplitude is experienced as pitch.","Frequency is experienced as loudness.","Wavelength is experienced as loudness.","Frequency is experienced as pitch."],"answer":3,"rationale":"The frequency of a sound wave (how many cycles occur per second) determines its perceived pitch. Amplitude, the height of the wave, determines loudness."},{"id":"U1-454","section":"0.2","skill":"Research Methods and Design","difficulty":2,"stem":"A neurologist meets a 16-year-old who reports tasting specific flavors whenever she hears certain words, such as tasting lemon whenever she hears the word \"Tuesday.\" This form of synesthesia is extremely rare. Which research method would researchers most likely use to study her experience in depth?","options":["An experiment with random assignment to conditions","A cross-sectional survey of a large random sample","A case study","Naturalistic observation in a school cafeteria"],"answer":2,"rationale":"A case study examines one person in great depth, which suits a condition too rare to gather large groups for. A large random survey would likely find almost no one with this kind of synesthesia."},{"id":"U1-455","section":"1.6","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Ngozi steps from the bright evening sky into a dark observatory dome. After about 20 to 30 minutes, she can see much fainter stars through the open roof than she could at first. Which cells account for most of this improvement?","options":["Rods, which gradually become far more sensitive to dim light","Cones, which are concentrated in the fovea and detect color","Ganglion cells, which form the optic nerve","Hair cells, which transduce vibrations"],"answer":0,"rationale":"Rods are mainly activated in low light and become much more sensitive during dark adaptation, which accounts for the large improvement after 20 to 30 minutes. Cones adapt partly in the first few minutes but remain far less sensitive than rods."},{"id":"U1-456","section":"0.2","skill":"Research Methods and Design","difficulty":2,"set":"u1c_vanilla","stimulus":{"type":"passage","text":"Dr. Bautista studied whether smell changes how sweet food tastes. Sixty adult volunteers each drew a numbered card from a bowl: those with odd numbers tasted plain, unsweetened yogurt in a room where a vanilla scent was gently released into the air, and those with even numbers tasted the same yogurt in an identical unscented room. Each volunteer rated the sweetness of the yogurt from 1 (not sweet at all) to 10 (extremely sweet). Volunteers in the vanilla-scented room gave significantly higher sweetness ratings."},"stem":"Which feature of Dr. Bautista's study shows that it is an experiment?","options":["Volunteers rated sweetness on a scale from 1 to 10.","Volunteers were assigned to the scented or unscented room by drawing numbered cards.","All the volunteers were adults.","Dr. Bautista compared the sweetness ratings of the two groups."],"answer":1,"rationale":"Drawing numbered cards is a form of random assignment, and random assignment to conditions that the researcher controls is what makes a study an experiment. A rating scale is an operational definition and can be used in non-experimental studies too."},{"id":"U1-457","section":"0.3","skill":"Research Methods and Design","difficulty":1,"set":"u1c_vanilla","stimulus":{"type":"passage","text":"Dr. Bautista studied whether smell changes how sweet food tastes. Sixty adult volunteers each drew a numbered card from a bowl: those with odd numbers tasted plain, unsweetened yogurt in a room where a vanilla scent was gently released into the air, and those with even numbers tasted the same yogurt in an identical unscented room. Each volunteer rated the sweetness of the yogurt from 1 (not sweet at all) to 10 (extremely sweet). Volunteers in the vanilla-scented room gave significantly higher sweetness ratings."},"stem":"What is the independent variable in Dr. Bautista's study?","options":["The sweetness rating each volunteer gave","The type of yogurt that was tasted","Whether the tasting room had a vanilla scent","The number on each volunteer's card"],"answer":2,"rationale":"Dr. Bautista manipulated whether the room had a vanilla scent, so that is the independent variable. The sweetness rating is the dependent variable, and the yogurt was the same for everyone."},{"id":"U1-458","section":"1.6","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":1,"stem":"While keeping his eyes on the sheet music, Lorenzo plays a fast piece on the piano, moving his fingers to the correct keys without looking down at his hands. Which sense makes this possible?","options":["Kinesthesis","The vestibular sense","Sensory adaptation","Gustation"],"answer":0,"rationale":"Kinesthesis senses the position and movement of body parts, allowing coordinated movement without watching the hands. The vestibular sense deals with balance and head movement, not finger position."},{"id":"U1-459","section":"1.6","skill":"Concept Recall","difficulty":2,"stem":"After spinning around quickly in a desk chair, Zara stops suddenly but still feels as if the room is turning, because fluid in part of her inner ear keeps moving for a few seconds. In which structures is this fluid located?","options":["The cochlea","The semicircular canals","The eardrum","The thalamus"],"answer":1,"rationale":"The semicircular canals contain fluid that shifts as the head rotates and keeps moving briefly after spinning stops, producing dizziness. The cochlea is also fluid-filled, but it transduces sound rather than motion."},{"id":"U1-460","section":"1.6","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which situation shows sensory adaptation most clearly?","options":["After a few days of wearing prism goggles that shift his view to the left, Felipe reaches for objects accurately again.","In a hearing test, Ana detects a soft tone on exactly half of the trials.","When Ibrahim first steps into a warm bath it feels very hot, but after a few minutes it feels only pleasantly warm.","With a blocked nose from a cold, Oksana finds that her favorite soup tastes bland."],"answer":2,"rationale":"The bath's temperature does not change, but Ibrahim's temperature receptors respond less over time, which is sensory adaptation. Adjusting to prism goggles is perceptual adaptation, a change in how the brain interprets a shifted view rather than a drop in receptor sensitivity."},{"id":"U1-461","section":"1.6","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"In a classroom demonstration, blindfolded students pinch their noses closed and taste small cubes of apple and raw potato. Which result is most likely, and which concept explains it?","options":["Students will easily identify each food, because taste works independently of smell.","Students will taste nothing at all, because synesthesia blocks taste when smell is blocked.","Students will name each food more accurately, because their attention shifts entirely to taste.","Students will have trouble telling the foods apart, because flavor depends on sensory interaction between taste and smell."],"answer":3,"rationale":"Flavor comes from taste and smell working together, so blocking smell makes many foods hard to identify even though basic tastes such as sweet can still be detected. Synesthesia is experiencing one sense through another, not a loss of taste."},{"id":"U1-462","section":"1.6","skill":"Data Interpretation","difficulty":2,"stimulus":{"type":"line","title":"Detection of a Faint Light by One Participant","xLabel":"Light intensity (arbitrary units)","yLabel":"Percentage of trials on which the light was detected","series":[{"name":"Detection rate","points":[[1,2],[2,6],[3,18],[4,50],[5,82],[6,95],[7,99]]}],"hlines":[{"y":50,"label":"50% detection"}]},"stem":"In a dark room, a participant was shown flashes of light at different intensities and reported whether she saw each one. Based on the graph, which of the following is the best interpretation?","options":["Her absolute threshold is about 4 units of intensity.","Her absolute threshold is about 7 units, because that is where she detects nearly every flash.","Her just-noticeable difference is about 4 units of intensity.","The graph shows sensory adaptation, because detection increases as intensity increases."],"answer":0,"rationale":"The absolute threshold is the intensity detected on at least 50% of trials, and the curve crosses 50% at about 4 units. A just-noticeable difference would require comparing two stimuli, which this task did not do."},{"id":"U1-463","section":"1.6","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"To read the tiny ingredient list on a snack wrapper, Ayesha looks directly at the words instead of glancing at them from the side. Looking straight at the print gives the sharpest image mainly because the image then falls on the","options":["fovea, which is densely packed with cones","blind spot, where the optic nerve collects visual signals","periphery of the retina, where rods detect fine detail","lens, which contains the most photoreceptors"],"answer":0,"rationale":"The fovea, at the center of the retina, is packed with cones, which provide the sharpest detail in good light. Rods are concentrated in the periphery and detect shape and motion, not fine detail."},{"id":"U1-464","section":"1.6","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":1,"stem":"During a hearing test, an audiologist keeps lowering the volume of a tone until Rania hears it on only half of the trials. That volume is Rania's","options":["absolute threshold","difference threshold","frequency","sensory adaptation level"],"answer":0,"rationale":"The absolute threshold is the minimum intensity of a stimulus detected at least 50% of the time. The difference threshold would involve detecting a change between two tones rather than detecting one tone at all."},{"id":"U1-465","section":"1.4","skill":"Concept Recall","difficulty":1,"stem":"The primary visual cortex, which first processes signals from the eyes, is located in which lobe of the brain?","options":["Temporal lobe","Parietal lobe","Frontal lobe","Occipital lobe"],"answer":3,"rationale":"The occipital lobe, at the back of the brain, contains the primary visual cortex. The temporal lobe contains the auditory cortex."},{"id":"U1-466","section":"1.6","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which situation is the clearest example of transduction?","options":["The lens of the eye thickens to focus on a nearby page.","Receptor cells in the taste buds convert chemicals dissolved in saliva into neural signals.","A person stops noticing the smell of a cafe after sitting there for an hour.","The brain recognizes a familiar song after hearing its first few notes."],"answer":1,"rationale":"Transduction is converting a physical stimulus, here chemical molecules, into neural signals the brain can use. Recognizing a song is perception, which happens after transduction."},{"id":"U1-467","section":"1.6","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"An eye disease gradually destroys the fovea in both of Mr. Chen's eyes. Which of the following would most likely become difficult for him?","options":["Seeing objects in dim light","Noticing movement at the edges of his vision","Recognizing a friend's face when looking straight at it","Keeping his balance on a moving train"],"answer":2,"rationale":"The fovea provides the sharpest, most detailed central vision, so tasks such as recognizing faces straight ahead suffer. Dim-light vision and detecting motion at the edges depend mostly on rods in the periphery."},{"id":"U1-468","section":"1.6","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Ms. Ortega gives each student a card with a small dot on the right and a small star about 8 centimeters to its left. She tells them to close their right eye, stare at the dot with their left eye, and gradually bring the card closer to their eyes. At a certain distance, Kwame notices that the star vanishes, even though he can still see the dot. What is Ms. Ortega demonstrating?","options":["Dark adaptation","The blind spot","Sensory adaptation","Accommodation"],"answer":1,"rationale":"At a certain distance the star's image falls on the blind spot, where the optic nerve leaves the eye and there are no rods or cones, so it disappears. Sensory adaptation would make a stimulus fade slowly over time, not vanish at one particular distance."},{"id":"U1-469","section":"1.6","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":1,"stem":"Hiroshi has a stuffy nose from allergies, and his grandmother's spiced chicken and rice tastes bland to him, although his family says it is full of flavor. Which concept best describes why Hiroshi cannot enjoy the flavor?","options":["Synesthesia","Perceptual adaptation","Sensory interaction","Kinesthesis"],"answer":2,"rationale":"Taste and smell work together to create flavor, so a blocked nose mutes taste; this is sensory interaction. Synesthesia is experiencing one sense through another, such as seeing colors when hearing music."},{"id":"U1-470","section":"1.6","skill":"Concept Recall","difficulty":2,"stem":"Why does an object seem to disappear when its image falls on the blind spot?","options":["The blind spot contains only rods, so the object loses its color.","The lens cannot focus light onto that part of the retina.","The blind spot contains so many cones that they become overloaded.","There are no rods or cones where the optic nerve leaves the eye, so no light is transduced there."],"answer":3,"rationale":"The blind spot is where the optic nerve exits the eye, and it has no photoreceptors, so light falling there is not converted into neural signals. The lens focuses light across the whole retina, including the blind spot."},{"id":"U1-471","section":"1.6","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":3,"stem":"People can describe a color as a bluish red or a yellowish green, but almost no one reports seeing a color that is both reddish and greenish at the same time. This observation provides support for which of the following?","options":["Opponent-process theory","Trichromatic theory","Feature detection","Color constancy"],"answer":0,"rationale":"Opponent-process theory holds that red and green are processed as an opposing pair, so the same cells cannot signal both at once, which is why a \"reddish green\" is not seen. Trichromatic theory, based on three cone types, does not predict which color combinations are impossible."},{"id":"U1-472","section":"1.6","skill":"Concept Recall","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which of the following sets of theories is used to explain how people perceive pitch?","options":["Place theory, frequency theory and volley theory","Opponent-process theory, place theory and frequency theory","Trichromatic theory and opponent-process theory","Gate control theory and volley theory"],"answer":0,"rationale":"Place, frequency and volley theories together explain pitch perception: place theory for higher pitches and frequency and volley theories for lower and middle pitches. Opponent-process and trichromatic theories explain color vision, and gate control theory explains pain."},{"id":"U2-001","section":"2.1","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":1,"stem":"In a crowded mall food court, Haruto follows everything his cousin is saying across the table while tuning out dozens of nearby conversations, music and announcements. Haruto's ability to do this is best described as","options":["sensory adaptation","change blindness","selective attention","bottom-up processing"],"answer":2,"rationale":"Selective attention is focusing awareness on one stimulus while filtering out others. Sensory adaptation is a drop in sensitivity to an unchanging stimulus, which does not explain choosing one voice over many."},{"id":"U2-002","section":"2.1","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"From the observation deck of a very tall tower, Mariam looks down at buses moving along the road. The images of the buses on her retinas are tiny, yet she perceives them as full-sized buses that are far away. Which perceptual principle best explains her experience?","options":["Proximity","Perceptual constancy","Retinal disparity","Apparent movement"],"answer":1,"rationale":"Perceptual constancy (here, size constancy) lets us perceive objects as keeping their real size even when their retinal images shrink. Retinal disparity is a depth cue, not a principle that keeps size stable."},{"id":"U2-003","section":"2.1","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Chidinma is waiting at a busy clinic and is listening closely for her name to be called. When the receptionist calls out \"Chiara,\" Chidinma stands up and walks to the desk before realizing the name was not hers. Her mistaken perception is best explained by","options":["perceptual set","sensory adaptation","the difference threshold","divided attention"],"answer":0,"rationale":"A perceptual set is a mental predisposition to perceive what we expect. Because Chidinma was expecting her name, she heard a similar name as her own."},{"id":"U2-004","section":"2.1","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"After eye surgery, Rafael must keep one eye covered for two weeks. While walking down a long, straight hospital corridor, which of the following cues could he still use to judge that the far end of the corridor is a long way away?","options":["Convergence","Retinal disparity","Figure-ground","Linear perspective"],"answer":3,"rationale":"Linear perspective is a monocular cue: parallel lines, like the edges of a corridor, appear to meet in the distance, and it works with one eye. Convergence and retinal disparity are binocular cues that require both eyes."},{"id":"U2-005","section":"2.1","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Layla, a goalkeeper on her school football team, scratches her cornea and must wear a patch over one eye for a week. While wearing the patch, which depth cue will Layla be unable to use?","options":["Texture gradient","Relative clarity","Retinal disparity","Interposition"],"answer":2,"rationale":"Retinal disparity depends on comparing the slightly different images from the two eyes, so it is lost when one eye is covered. Texture gradient, relative clarity and interposition are monocular cues that still work with one eye."},{"id":"U2-006","section":"2.1","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"At a school talent show, Priya can clearly pick out her younger brother's voice in the choir as a distinct sound standing out from the piano and the other singers, even though several other voices are louder. Gestalt psychologists would explain Priya's experience using the principle of","options":["similarity","figure-ground","closure","proximity"],"answer":1,"rationale":"Figure-ground is the tendency to organize a stimulus into a focal figure and a surrounding background. Here her brother's voice is the figure and the rest of the music is the ground."},{"id":"U2-007","section":"2.1","skill":"Concept Recall","difficulty":1,"stem":"A photographer has had vision in only one eye since childhood. Which of the following depth cues is she unable to use?","options":["Convergence","Relative size","Linear perspective","Interposition"],"answer":0,"rationale":"Convergence is a binocular cue based on how much the two eyes turn inward to focus on a near object, so it requires two working eyes. The other cues are monocular."},{"id":"U2-008","section":"0.3","skill":"Research Methods and Design","difficulty":1,"set":"u2a_earbuds","stimulus":{"type":"passage","text":"Dr. Okafor wants to know whether wearing noise-cancelling headphones affects how accurately students proofread. Volunteers are randomly assigned to proofread the same three-page essay either while wearing noise-cancelling headphones in a busy cafeteria or without headphones in the same cafeteria. Dr. Okafor records the number of planted spelling errors each volunteer misses."},"stem":"In Dr. Okafor's study, wearing noise-cancelling headphones is best described as","options":["the dependent variable","a confounding variable","the operational definition of proofreading accuracy","the independent variable"],"answer":3,"rationale":"The independent variable is the factor the researcher manipulates, here whether volunteers wear noise-cancelling headphones. The number of missed errors is the dependent variable that operationally defines proofreading accuracy."},{"id":"U2-009","section":"0.2","skill":"Research Methods and Design","difficulty":1,"set":"u2a_earbuds","stimulus":{"type":"passage","text":"Dr. Okafor wants to know whether wearing noise-cancelling headphones affects how accurately students proofread. Volunteers are randomly assigned to proofread the same three-page essay either while wearing noise-cancelling headphones in a busy cafeteria or without headphones in the same cafeteria. Dr. Okafor records the number of planted spelling errors each volunteer misses."},"stem":"Which type of research does Dr. Okafor's study represent?","options":["Correlational study","Case study","Experiment","Naturalistic observation"],"answer":2,"rationale":"Random assignment of volunteers to conditions that the researcher manipulates makes this an experiment. A correlational study would only measure existing variables without manipulating them."},{"id":"U2-010","section":"2.1","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"A well-known drawing can be seen either as a white vase in the center of the page or as two dark faces in profile looking at each other. As viewers stare at it, their perception flips back and forth between the two images. This reversible drawing illustrates the Gestalt principle of","options":["closure","figure-ground","good continuation","similarity"],"answer":1,"rationale":"The drawing is reversible because the same areas can serve as either figure or background. Whichever part is seen as the figure (vase or faces) determines what is perceived."},{"id":"U2-011","section":"2.1","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"As Zhang Wei's father walks toward him across a large car park, the image of his father on Zhang Wei's retinas grows much larger. Zhang Wei does not perceive his father as growing taller. This is an example of","options":["size constancy","relative size","convergence","closure"],"answer":0,"rationale":"Size constancy keeps an object's perceived size stable despite changes in its retinal image. Relative size is a depth cue for judging distance, not the stability of perceived size."},{"id":"U2-012","section":"2.1","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Tomasz is wearing a patch over one eye after a minor injury. During a table-tennis match, he keeps misjudging exactly how far away the ball is as it comes toward him. The most likely reason is that","options":["monocular cues such as relative size and texture gradient stop working when one eye is covered","covering one eye removes binocular cues such as retinal disparity, so he must judge distance from monocular cues alone","covering one eye causes sensory adaptation in the uncovered eye, so it stops responding to the moving ball","size constancy is lost, so the ball seems to grow as it approaches and he cannot tell its distance"],"answer":1,"rationale":"Retinal disparity and convergence need both eyes, so covering one eye removes this precise depth information. Monocular cues still work with one eye, but they are less precise for judging the distance of a fast-moving nearby object."},{"id":"U2-013","section":"0.1","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Lucia panics before every math test. A psychologist who takes a cognitive perspective is asked to explain her anxiety. Which explanation would this psychologist most likely give?","options":["Lucia's anxiety has been strengthened because avoiding studying brings her immediate relief.","Lucia has formed a mental framework (schema) in which tests are threats that reveal her worth, and she interprets every test through that framework.","Lucia has inherited a nervous system that reacts unusually strongly to stress.","Lucia's anxiety reflects the high value placed on academic success in her community."],"answer":1,"rationale":"The cognitive perspective explains behavior in terms of mental processes such as beliefs, interpretations and schemas. Anxiety maintained by relief from avoidance is a behavioral explanation, and an inherited stress response is a biological one."},{"id":"U2-014","section":"2.1","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":3,"stem":"As a friend walks toward you, the image of your friend on your retinas becomes larger, yet you perceive your friend as staying the same size. Size constancy is maintained in this situation because","options":["the retinal image actually stays the same size as the friend approaches","the brain takes into account that the friend's distance is decreasing as the retinal image grows","the brain perceives the friend's distance as staying the same","binocular cues switch off when objects come closer"],"answer":1,"rationale":"Size constancy depends on the brain combining retinal image size with perceived distance: a growing image paired with decreasing distance is interpreted as an object of unchanging size. The retinal image itself does change."},{"id":"U2-015","section":"2.1","skill":"Concept Recall","difficulty":1,"stem":"As Ji-woo brings a small bead closer to her nose to examine it, her brain uses the convergence cue to judge how near the bead is. Convergence is based on which of the following?","options":["The muscle tension created as the eyes turn inward toward a close object","How much of the bead is hidden behind her fingers","The lens changing shape to focus on the object","The difference between the images on the two retinas"],"answer":0,"rationale":"Convergence is the binocular cue based on how much the eyes rotate inward; the greater the inward turn, the closer the object. The difference between the two retinal images is retinal disparity, and lens shape change is accommodation."},{"id":"U2-016","section":"2.1","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Kenji, a visitor from Japan, slurps his noodles loudly at a restaurant in London. Some nearby diners think he is being rude, but in Kenji's home culture, slurping noodles is a way of showing that the food is delicious. Which of the following best explains the different interpretations of Kenji's behavior?","options":["The framing effect changes how choices are presented.","Change blindness prevents people from noticing behavior.","A mental set makes people repeat successful strategies.","Cultural norms and expectations shape how people perceive behavior."],"answer":3,"rationale":"Cultural experiences and expectations are external factors that filter perception, so the same behavior is interpreted differently by people from different cultures."},{"id":"U2-017","section":"2.1","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"A drawing shows three black circles, each with a wedge-shaped slice cut out. The slices are lined up so that most viewers see a bright white triangle lying on top of the circles, even though no triangle edges are actually drawn. The perception of the triangle is best explained by","options":["retinal disparity between the two eyes","the opponent-process theory of color vision","the Gestalt principle of closure","sensory adaptation to the black circles"],"answer":2,"rationale":"Closure is the tendency to fill in gaps to perceive complete figures. Viewers fill in the missing edges and see a whole triangle that is not actually drawn."},{"id":"U2-018","section":"2.1","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Nadia, a chemistry student, and her friend Ahmed, a tile designer, look at the same drawing of six-sided shapes joined together. Nadia sees a diagram of a molecule, while Ahmed sees a pattern for a mosaic floor. The difference in their perceptions is an example of","options":["bottom-up processing","top-down processing","sensory adaptation","change blindness"],"answer":1,"rationale":"Top-down processing occurs when prior knowledge and expectations guide interpretation. Because they receive the same sensory input, a purely bottom-up account cannot explain why they see different things."},{"id":"U2-019","section":"0.2","skill":"Research Methods and Design","difficulty":1,"stem":"Dr. Mensah wants to know whether background noise affects how quickly people solve puzzles. He randomly assigns volunteers to complete the same set of puzzles in either a quiet room or a room playing recorded traffic noise, and he records how long each volunteer takes. Which research method is Dr. Mensah using?","options":["An experiment","A correlational study","A longitudinal study","A survey"],"answer":0,"rationale":"Dr. Mensah manipulates the noise level and randomly assigns participants to conditions, which defines an experiment. A correlational study would measure existing variables without manipulating them."},{"id":"U2-020","section":"2.1","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Before Omar tastes a new brand of lemonade, his sister tells him it is \"unbelievably sour.\" Although the lemonade has the same sugar content as his usual brand, Omar grimaces after the first sip and insists it is far more sour than anything he has tried. Which of the following best explains Omar's reaction?","options":["Sensory interaction","Feature detection","The difference threshold","Top-down processing"],"answer":3,"rationale":"Top-down processing means expectations shape perception. His sister's warning led Omar to perceive the drink as more sour than its actual content would suggest, and the difference threshold concerns detecting real physical differences."},{"id":"U2-021","section":"2.1","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"A poster shows 30 dots arranged in a grid. The dots within each horizontal row are placed very close together, but the rows are spaced far apart from each other. As a result, viewers see five rows of dots rather than six columns. Which Gestalt principle is operating most strongly?","options":["Similarity","Closure","Proximity","Figure-ground"],"answer":2,"rationale":"Proximity is the tendency to group elements that are near each other. Because all the dots look alike, similarity is not what creates the rows; their spacing is."},{"id":"U2-022","section":"2.1","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which of the following is the best example of the Gestalt principle of closure?","options":["Spectators see a group of runners in matching red shirts as one team.","People recognize a panda in a logo that shows only a few black patches and has no outline.","Shoppers see four chairs placed tightly around a small table as one seating area.","A door is still perceived as rectangular when it swings open and its retinal image becomes a trapezoid."],"answer":1,"rationale":"Closure is the tendency to fill in gaps and perceive incomplete forms as complete, as when viewers see a whole panda from a few patches. Matching shirts illustrate similarity, and the chairs illustrate proximity."},{"id":"U2-023","section":"2.1","skill":"Concept Recall","difficulty":1,"stem":"A web designer arranges related buttons close together and gives them the same color so users see them as one group. In doing this, the designer is applying Gestalt principles, which psychologists mainly use to explain","options":["how people organize sensory information into meaningful wholes","how the eye converts light into neural impulses","the minimum amount of stimulation needed to detect a stimulus","how rewards strengthen behaviors"],"answer":0,"rationale":"Gestalt principles describe perceptual organization, the way the brain groups sensations into whole forms. Converting light into neural impulses is transduction, and detection limits are thresholds, both part of sensation."},{"id":"U2-024","section":"2.1","skill":"Concept Recall","difficulty":1,"stem":"Which of the following describes a phenomenon studied by Gestalt psychologists?","options":["A student remembers the first and last words on a list better than the words in the middle.","A child who watches an adult hit an inflatable doll later hits the doll in the same way.","A person's heart rate rises sharply when a loud bang is heard in the dark.","Spectators see fans wearing the same color shirts as one solid block of supporters, separate from fans in other colors."],"answer":3,"rationale":"Grouping fans by shirt color illustrates similarity, a Gestalt principle of perceptual organization. The other options describe the serial position effect, observational learning and a physiological stress response."},{"id":"U2-025","section":"2.1","skill":"Concept Recall","difficulty":1,"stem":"A psychologist working in the Gestalt tradition would be most likely to study which of the following topics?","options":["Classical conditioning","Language development","Perception","Memory storage"],"answer":2,"rationale":"Gestalt psychology focuses on perception, especially how the mind organizes sensations into meaningful wholes."},{"id":"U2-026","section":"2.1","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":1,"stem":"At a crowded school sports day, Fatima tries to keep track of her younger brother as he runs in a relay race among dozens of other children wearing identical uniforms. Which of the following would best allow Fatima to follow his progress through the race?","options":["Sensory adaptation","Selective attention","Divided attention","Retinal disparity"],"answer":1,"rationale":"Selective attention allows a person to focus on one stimulus, such as one runner, while filtering out competing stimuli. Dividing attention among many runners would make it harder, not easier, to follow her brother."},{"id":"U2-027","section":"2.1","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":1,"stem":"On a hand-drawn map for a desert camping trip, the campsite is outlined as a rectangle, but the pen ran dry so two of its corners are missing. Everyone reading the map still sees the campsite as a complete rectangle. This is the result of the perceptual process known as","options":["closure","similarity","figure-ground","perceptual set"],"answer":0,"rationale":"Closure is the tendency to perceive incomplete figures as complete. Map readers fill in the missing corners and see a whole rectangle."},{"id":"U2-028","section":"2.1","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Before playing a recording of crackly radio static to her class, a teacher tells half of the students that the phrase \"open the window\" is hidden in the noise. Most of these students report clearly hearing the phrase, while the students who were not told hear only static. This difference best illustrates","options":["bottom-up processing","sensory adaptation","the cocktail party effect","top-down processing"],"answer":3,"rationale":"Top-down processing occurs when expectations guide perception. Both groups heard the same sound, so the difference comes from what the students were told to expect, not from the sensory input itself."},{"id":"U2-029","section":"2.1","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"A photograph of a pebble beach shows large, clearly separated stones at the bottom of the image. Toward the top of the image, the stones look smaller, more crowded together and less detailed, which makes the beach appear to stretch into the distance. Which monocular depth cue does the photograph illustrate?","options":["Linear perspective","Interposition","Texture gradient","Convergence"],"answer":2,"rationale":"Texture gradient is the cue in which a surface's texture becomes finer, denser and less detailed with distance. Convergence is binocular and cannot operate in a flat photograph."},{"id":"U2-030","section":"2.1","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"While searching a crowded school car park for her father's white car, Mei-Ling walks right past her aunt, who is waving at her from a few meters away in a bright yellow dress. Later, Mei-Ling insists she never saw her aunt at all. Which of the following best explains why she did not notice her aunt?","options":["Change blindness","Inattentional blindness","Bottom-up processing","Sensory adaptation"],"answer":1,"rationale":"Inattentional blindness is failing to see a visible but unexpected stimulus because attention is focused on something else, here the search for the car. Change blindness is failing to notice that something in a scene has changed, which is not what happened."},{"id":"U2-031","section":"2.1","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":3,"stem":"Researchers find that people raised in cities full of rectangular buildings and straight corridors are more likely than people raised in villages of round huts to judge one of two equal lines, each with arrow-like fins at its ends, as longer than the other. This difference best supports which conclusion?","options":["Life experiences shape the assumptions people use to interpret what they see.","People raised among rectangular buildings have sharper visual acuity than people raised among round huts.","Sensory adaptation to straight lines makes city residents less sensitive to line length.","Perception is determined only by the physical features of the stimulus."],"answer":0,"rationale":"The groups saw identical lines but perceived them differently, which shows that experience and culture shape perceptual interpretation. Visual acuity and sensory adaptation concern sensation, not the learned assumptions that produce the illusion."},{"id":"U2-032","section":"2.1","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Yousef's football match starts in bright afternoon sunlight and ends under yellowish stadium floodlights. Although the light reflected from his team's white shirts changes considerably, Yousef perceives the shirts as the same white throughout the match. Which perceptual process allows him to see the shirts as unchanged?","options":["Perceptual set","Change blindness","Motion parallax","Perceptual constancy"],"answer":3,"rationale":"Perceptual constancy (here, color and lightness constancy) keeps the perceived color of an object stable even when lighting conditions change the light it reflects."},{"id":"U2-033","section":"2.1","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":3,"stem":"For a school art exhibition, Omar paints two identical medium-gray circles. One circle sits on a black background and the other sits on a pale gray background. Visitors insist that the circle on the black background is the lighter of the two. Which concept did Omar use to create this illusion?","options":["Apparent movement","Retinal disparity","Perceptual constancy","Closure"],"answer":2,"rationale":"To keep perception stable, the visual system judges lightness relative to the surroundings, the same mechanism behind lightness constancy. Against a dark background the gray looks lighter, so the illusion results from the brain's constancy processes."},{"id":"U2-034","section":"2.1","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Kwame's sister texts him that she has left her jacket on his seat to save it for him. When Kwame walks into the dark cinema, he sees only a vague shadowy shape on a seat in the middle row, yet he immediately recognizes it as his sister's jacket and heads straight for it. Which of the following best explains Kwame's perception?","options":["Bottom-up processing","Top-down processing","Inattentional blindness","Sensory interaction"],"answer":1,"rationale":"Top-down processing uses prior knowledge and expectations, here the text from his sister, to interpret weak or ambiguous sensory input. The faint shape alone (bottom-up input) would not be enough to identify the jacket."},{"id":"U2-035","section":"2.1","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"At dusk on a desert camping trip, Joao and Lina both see a twisted dark stick lying on the sand a few meters ahead. Joao, who was bitten by a snake on an earlier trip, freezes and shouts that there is a snake. Lina, who has never encountered a snake outdoors, sees only a stick and keeps walking. Which concept best explains their different perceptions?","options":["Perceptual set","Retinal disparity","Change blindness","Sensory adaptation"],"answer":0,"rationale":"A perceptual set is a readiness to perceive in a certain way based on past experiences and expectations. Joao's frightening experience primed him to see the ambiguous stick as a snake."},{"id":"U2-036","section":"2.3","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Ravi glances at a street sign for a split second as he cycles past. According to the multi-store model of memory, which process determines whether the information on the sign moves from sensory memory into short-term memory?","options":["Maintenance rehearsal","Retrieval","Sensation","Attention"],"answer":3,"rationale":"In the multi-store model, information in sensory memory that receives attention is passed into short-term memory. Rehearsal keeps information active in short-term memory and helps move it into long-term memory, which is a later step."},{"id":"U2-037","section":"2.1","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":1,"set":"u2a_billboard","stimulus":{"type":"passage","text":"A new bakery chain in Abu Dhabi wants more people to order from its delivery app. It paints its new logo on the side of every delivery van. The logo is a croissant drawn with only a few curved, broken strokes, leaving large gaps in its outline, yet people glancing at a passing van easily recognize the shape as a croissant."},"stem":"Which principle of perception is the van logo designed to use?","options":["Size constancy","Motion parallax","Closure","Proximity"],"answer":2,"rationale":"Closure is the tendency to see incomplete forms as complete. Viewers fill in the gaps in the broken outline and recognize a whole croissant."},{"id":"U2-038","section":"2.1","skill":"Concept Recall","difficulty":1,"set":"u2a_billboard","stimulus":{"type":"passage","text":"A new bakery chain in Abu Dhabi wants more people to order from its delivery app. It paints its new logo on the side of every delivery van. The logo is a croissant drawn with only a few curved, broken strokes, leaving large gaps in its outline, yet people glancing at a passing van easily recognize the shape as a croissant."},"stem":"The van logo relies on which of the following sets of perceptual concepts?","options":["Monocular depth cues","Gestalt principles of perceptual organization","Theories of color vision","Perceptual constancies"],"answer":1,"rationale":"Seeing a complete croissant in a broken outline relies on closure, one of the Gestalt principles of perceptual organization. Depth cues concern distance and color theories concern hue, neither of which the logo depends on."},{"id":"U2-039","section":"0.3","skill":"Research Methods and Design","difficulty":2,"set":"u2a_billboard","stimulus":{"type":"passage","text":"A new bakery chain in Abu Dhabi wants more people to order from its delivery app. It paints its new logo on the side of every delivery van. The logo is a croissant drawn with only a few curved, broken strokes, leaving large gaps in its outline, yet people glancing at a passing van easily recognize the shape as a croissant."},"stem":"Which of the following would be the most appropriate operational definition of the effectiveness of the van logo campaign?","options":["The change in the number of app orders in the month after the logo appears on the vans","The number of colors used in the logo","The marketing team's rating of how creative the logo is","The cost of painting the logo on each van"],"answer":0,"rationale":"An operational definition states how a variable will be measured in observable terms. The campaign aims to increase app orders, so a change in orders after the logo appears measures its effectiveness directly."},{"id":"U2-040","section":"2.1","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Hessa does her chemistry homework while texting friends and watching a video. Her homework has many more errors than when she works on it alone. Research on multitasking suggests that the main reason for her poor performance is that","options":["multitasking speeds up her mental processing so much that she rushes through the problems","her sensory memory fills up and stops taking in new stimuli","doing several tasks at once raises her absolute threshold for each task","attention is limited, so each time she shifts it to her phone or the video, some of the chemistry content is not processed"],"answer":3,"rationale":"Attention is limited, so multitasking really involves rapidly switching attention, and information from the unattended task is missed. Sensory memory does not fill up and stop working, and absolute thresholds are not raised by multitasking."},{"id":"U2-041","section":"2.1","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which of the following scenarios best illustrates the monocular depth cue of relative size?","options":["Adebayo judges the distance of a ball by comparing the slightly different images it casts on each of his retinas.","Ingrid judges that a tree is closer than a fence because the tree partly blocks her view of the fence.","Seo-yeon sees two identical buses and judges that the one with the smaller image is farther away.","Malik judges that the far end of a railway track is distant because the two rails appear to meet."],"answer":2,"rationale":"Relative size is the cue that, of two objects assumed to be the same size, the one casting the smaller retinal image is farther away. The other scenarios show retinal disparity, interposition and linear perspective."},{"id":"U2-042","section":"0.3","skill":"Research Methods and Design","difficulty":2,"set":"u2a_gum","stimulus":{"type":"passage","text":"Dr. Nakamura, a sport psychologist, wanted to know whether a short breathing exercise improves concentration in young athletes. She recruited 60 members of a national youth archery program and randomly assigned half to complete a 10-minute guided breathing exercise and the other half to sit quietly for 10 minutes. Each archer then shot 30 arrows, and Dr. Nakamura recorded how many arrows hit the inner ring of the target."},"stem":"Which of the following is the operational definition of the dependent variable in Dr. Nakamura's study?","options":["Completing the 10-minute breathing exercise","The number of arrows each archer landed in the inner ring","The 60 archers recruited from the youth program","Concentration"],"answer":1,"rationale":"The operational definition states exactly how the dependent variable is measured. Concentration is the variable of interest, but it is measured by the number of arrows in the inner ring. The breathing exercise is the independent variable."},{"id":"U2-043","section":"0.1","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"set":"u2a_gum","stimulus":{"type":"passage","text":"Dr. Nakamura, a sport psychologist, wanted to know whether a short breathing exercise improves concentration in young athletes. She recruited 60 members of a national youth archery program and randomly assigned half to complete a 10-minute guided breathing exercise and the other half to sit quietly for 10 minutes. Each archer then shot 30 arrows, and Dr. Nakamura recorded how many arrows hit the inner ring of the target. Archers who completed the breathing exercise hit the inner ring more often than archers who sat quietly. Before the study, her research assistant had predicted that the breathing exercise would make no difference, but after seeing the results he told the team, \"I knew all along that breathing would help.\""},"stem":"Based on this description, which of the following is the research assistant displaying?","options":["Hindsight bias","Confirmation bias","Belief perseverance","The framing effect"],"answer":0,"rationale":"Hindsight bias is the tendency, after learning an outcome, to believe one would have predicted it. The assistant claims he knew the result all along even though he had predicted the opposite; belief perseverance would mean clinging to his original prediction."},{"id":"U2-044","section":"0.3","skill":"Research Methods and Design","difficulty":2,"set":"u2a_gum","stimulus":{"type":"passage","text":"Dr. Nakamura, a sport psychologist, wanted to know whether a short breathing exercise improves concentration in young athletes. She recruited 60 members of a national youth archery program and randomly assigned half to complete a 10-minute guided breathing exercise and the other half to sit quietly for 10 minutes. Each archer then shot 30 arrows, and Dr. Nakamura recorded how many arrows hit the inner ring of the target."},"stem":"Which of the following elements of Dr. Nakamura's study would most strengthen her ability to infer that the breathing exercise caused any difference in accuracy?","options":["Obtaining informed consent from every archer and a parent","Having both groups shoot at the same range, at the same time of day, with the same bows and instructions","Asking each archer afterward how focused they felt","Publishing the results in a peer-reviewed journal"],"answer":1,"rationale":"Holding all conditions constant except the independent variable rules out confounding variables, which supports a cause-and-effect conclusion. Informed consent and publication are ethical and reporting steps, and a self-report of focus does not rule out other causes."},{"id":"U2-045","section":"2.1","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which of the following best demonstrates retinal disparity?","options":["Kofi holds a pencil at arm's length and looks at it first with only his left eye and then with only his right eye; the pencil seems to jump sideways because each eye receives a slightly different view.","Grace sees the two edges of a long, straight highway appear to meet at the horizon.","Malak judges that a car is closer than a lamppost because the car blocks part of the lamppost.","Distant mountains look hazy to Joaquin, so he judges that they are far away."],"answer":0,"rationale":"Retinal disparity is the difference between the images the two eyes receive, which the brain uses to judge depth; the pencil jumps because each eye's view differs. The other examples show the monocular cues of linear perspective, interposition and relative clarity."},{"id":"U2-046","section":"2.1","skill":"Concept Recall","difficulty":1,"stem":"A psychologist argues that a melody is more than a list of individual notes, because people recognize the same tune even when every note is shifted higher or lower. The claim that people perceive organized wholes, which have properties their separate parts lack, is the core idea of which school of thought?","options":["Structuralism","Gestalt psychology","Psychodynamic theory","Behaviorism"],"answer":1,"rationale":"Gestalt psychology holds that people perceive organized wholes rather than separate parts. Structuralism, by contrast, tried to break experience down into basic elements."},{"id":"U2-047","section":"2.1","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":1,"stem":"Ayesha receives a text from her friend that reads \"Mete me at the mian gaet aftre scohol.\" She instantly reads it as \"Meet me at the main gate after school\" and does not notice the misspellings until her brother points them out. Ayesha's quick reading is best explained by","options":["top-down processing","feature detection","sensory adaptation","divided attention"],"answer":0,"rationale":"Top-down processing uses knowledge and expectations, such as familiar words and the context of the message, to interpret incomplete or flawed input. Feature detection is the bottom-up detection of basic visual features like lines and edges."},{"id":"U2-048","section":"2.1","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Daniyal has just watched a documentary about animals that are active at night. A few minutes later, his cousin says she needs to buy a new bat before Saturday, and Daniyal pictures the flying animal rather than the cricket bat she means. Which of the following concepts best explains Daniyal's interpretation?","options":["Sensory adaptation","Feature detection","Inattentional blindness","Top-down processing"],"answer":3,"rationale":"The documentary created expectations that shaped how Daniyal interpreted an ambiguous word, an example of top-down processing."},{"id":"U2-049","section":"2.1","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":1,"stem":"Fernando is stuck on a word search puzzle. As soon as his friend tells him that every hidden word is a desert animal, he quickly spots CAMEL, ORYX and FENNEC among the jumbled letters. Which of the following concepts does this illustrate?","options":["Bottom-up processing","Perceptual constancy","Top-down processing","Divided attention"],"answer":2,"rationale":"Using knowledge of the theme to guide the search for words is top-down processing. Bottom-up processing would mean working only from the individual letters without any expectation of what to find."},{"id":"U2-050","section":"2.1","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Research finds that drivers who talk on hands-free phones miss nearly as many road hazards as drivers who hold the phone in their hand. This finding is best explained by the fact that","options":["drivers undergo sensory adaptation to the sound of the phone","attention is selective and limited, so focusing on the conversation reduces attention to the road","the conversation interferes with perceptual constancy","phone use switches the brain from top-down to bottom-up processing"],"answer":1,"rationale":"Selective attention means people can fully attend to only a limited amount of information at once. The conversation itself, not holding the phone, draws attention away from processing the road."},{"id":"U2-051","section":"2.1","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"In a laboratory study, Yusuf wears headphones that play a different story into each ear. He is told to repeat aloud only the story in his right ear. Yusuf is most likely to notice something in the story playing in his left ear if that story","options":["suddenly mentions his own name","switches to a different topic","is read in a language he does not speak","repeats the same sentence twice"],"answer":0,"rationale":"Personally meaningful information, especially one's own name, tends to break through the attentional filter even on an unattended channel. Changes in meaning or language on the unattended channel usually go unnoticed."},{"id":"U2-052","section":"2.1","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":1,"stem":"A 3D film uses special glasses to send a slightly different image to each of the viewer's eyes, so that objects seem to leap off the screen. The film creates depth mainly by imitating which binocular depth cue?","options":["Texture gradient","Interposition","Relative clarity","Retinal disparity"],"answer":3,"rationale":"Retinal disparity is the binocular cue based on the difference between the images on the two retinas. Texture gradient, interposition and relative clarity are monocular cues."},{"id":"U2-053","section":"2.1","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":1,"stem":"At a crowded school fair, Nour is chatting with a teacher when her attention is suddenly pulled away because someone several meters behind her mentions the robotics club, which Nour leads. Her response is an example of","options":["sensory adaptation","change blindness","the cocktail party effect","inattentional blindness"],"answer":2,"rationale":"The cocktail party effect is the ability to notice personally relevant information, such as one's name or a topic of special interest, in a noisy environment while attending to something else."},{"id":"U2-054","section":"2.2","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Three-year-old Zayed believes that any large animal with a hump on its back is a camel. At a wildlife park, he points at a bison and shouts, \"Camel!\" His mother explains that a bison is a different kind of animal, and Zayed now forms a separate category for bison. According to Jean Piaget, Zayed's change in thinking is an example of","options":["assimilation","accommodation","an algorithm","functional fixedness"],"answer":1,"rationale":"Accommodation is changing an existing schema, or creating a new one, to fit new information. Calling the bison a camel would have been assimilation; forming a new category is accommodation."},{"id":"U2-055","section":"2.2","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Six-year-old Kai has learned that the family tablet turns on when he presses the round button at the bottom of the screen. Now, whenever he picks up any electronic device, he presses the bottom of the screen and becomes frustrated when nothing happens. Kai's behavior is best explained by his having developed which of the following?","options":["A mental set","Accommodation","Confirmation bias","A prototype"],"answer":0,"rationale":"A mental set is a tendency to approach problems in a way that worked before, even when it no longer works. Accommodation would mean changing his approach in response to the new devices."},{"id":"U2-056","section":"2.4","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"According to the levels of processing model, which of the following study tasks would most improve a student's later recall of the word \"lantern\"?","options":["Counting the number of letters in the word \"lantern\"","Saying the word \"lantern\" aloud several times","Noting whether the word \"lantern\" is printed in capital letters","Explaining to a classmate how a lantern differs from a candle"],"answer":3,"rationale":"The levels of processing model holds that semantic (meaning-based) processing produces the deepest encoding and the best recall. Counting letters and checking print style are structural, and repeating the sound of the word is shallow phonemic processing."},{"id":"U2-057","section":"2.2","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":3,"stem":"Meera learns that Daniel is a quiet student who loves reading poetry. She concludes that he is more likely to be planning to study literature than business, even though ten times as many students at their school plan to study business. Which problem-solving strategy did Meera most likely use?","options":["The availability heuristic","An algorithm","The representativeness heuristic","The framing effect"],"answer":2,"rationale":"The representativeness heuristic is judging likelihood by how well something matches a prototype, while ignoring base rates. Daniel matches Meera's image of a literature student, so she overlooks how many more students choose business."},{"id":"U2-058","section":"2.2","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":1,"stem":"A pharmacy trainer teaches new staff to calculate a child's medicine dose with a fixed checklist: weigh the child, look up the dose per kilogram, multiply the two numbers, and then compare the result with the maximum safe dose. Staff who complete every step in order get the correct dose every time. Which problem-solving strategy is the trainer teaching?","options":["The availability heuristic","An algorithm","The representativeness heuristic","Insight"],"answer":1,"rationale":"An algorithm is a methodical, step-by-step procedure that guarantees a correct solution when every step is followed. A heuristic is a faster shortcut that does not guarantee success."},{"id":"U2-059","section":"2.6","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Li Na spends the morning practicing her multiplication tables for a math quiz. That afternoon in psychology class, she is shown a hand-drawn symbol that can be read either as the letter B or as the number 13, and she immediately sees it as 13. Which of the following best explains this?","options":["Priming","Habituation","Sensory adaptation","Functional fixedness"],"answer":0,"rationale":"Priming is the activation of certain associations in memory, often without awareness, which influences later perception and thinking. Her morning of working with numbers made the number interpretation more readily available."},{"id":"U2-060","section":"2.2","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Rahul needs to draw a long, straight line across his science fair poster but cannot find a ruler anywhere in the house. He runs his pen along the edge of a hardcover textbook instead. In solving the problem this way, Rahul has avoided","options":["confirmation bias","the gambler's fallacy","the availability heuristic","functional fixedness"],"answer":3,"rationale":"Functional fixedness is the tendency to think of objects only in terms of their usual uses. Rahul used a textbook as a straightedge, so he avoided it."},{"id":"U2-061","section":"2.2","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":1,"stem":"When asked to picture a \"fruit,\" most students in Ms. Reyes's class imagine an apple rather than a coconut or an olive. For these students, an apple is best described as","options":["a mental set","an algorithm","a prototype","a heuristic"],"answer":2,"rationale":"A prototype is the most typical or best example of a concept. For most of the class, an apple is the typical fruit."},{"id":"U2-062","section":"2.6","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Participants first read a list of words related to the ocean. Later, when asked to complete the word fragment \"S _ _ L L,\" most of them write \"SHELL\" rather than \"SMELL\" or \"SKILL.\" This result illustrates","options":["framing","priming","assimilation","proactive interference"],"answer":1,"rationale":"Priming occurs when exposure to one stimulus activates related associations that influence a later response. The ocean words made ocean-related completions more likely."},{"id":"U2-063","section":"2.7","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":3,"stem":"Dr. Salem shows 80 volunteers the same 30-second clip of a cyclist riding through a park. Afterward, half the volunteers answer the question \"What color was the cyclist's helmet?\" The other half answer \"What color was the helmet that fell off the cyclist?\" When tested a week later, 32 percent of the second half, but only 6 percent of the first half, report having seen the helmet fall off, although this never happens in the clip. Dr. Salem is investigating which of the following?","options":["The misinformation effect","Proactive interference","The primacy effect","The availability heuristic"],"answer":0,"rationale":"The misinformation effect occurs when misleading information presented after an event, here built into the wording of a question, becomes part of the memory of that event. Proactive interference involves older learning disrupting new learning, which is not what happened here."},{"id":"U2-064","section":"2.2","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Two-year-old Hamad has a pet cat at home and has learned to call small, furry, four-legged animals with tails \"kitty.\" On a visit to his grandfather's farm, he sees a young goat for the first time, points at it and happily shouts, \"Kitty!\" He keeps calling the goat \"kitty\" for the rest of the afternoon. Which concept describes what is happening to Hamad's schema?","options":["Accommodation","Object permanence","A mental set","Assimilation"],"answer":3,"rationale":"Assimilation is fitting new information into an existing schema without changing the schema. Hamad fits the goat into his cat schema instead of forming a new category, which would be accommodation."},{"id":"U2-065","section":"0.5","skill":"Data Interpretation","difficulty":2,"set":"u2a_sunk","stimulus":{"type":"table","caption":"In a 10-round business decision game, researchers counted how many times each of nine participants kept investing in a failing project only because of the money they had already put into it (the sunk-cost fallacy). The results are shown in the table, listed in the order the participants were tested.","headers":["Participant","Number of sunk-cost decisions"],"rows":[["1","4"],["2","9"],["3","2"],["4","6"],["5","3"],["6","9"],["7","5"],["8","9"],["9","10"]]},"stem":"A participant whose score was at the mode of this data set would have made how many sunk-cost decisions?","options":["6","10","9","3"],"answer":2,"rationale":"The mode is the most frequent score. The value 9 appears three times, more often than any other value. The median of the data set is 6, a common mix-up."},{"id":"U2-066","section":"0.5","skill":"Data Interpretation","difficulty":2,"set":"u2a_sunk","stimulus":{"type":"table","caption":"In a 10-round business decision game, researchers counted how many times each of nine participants kept investing in a failing project only because of the money they had already put into it (the sunk-cost fallacy). The results are shown in the table, listed in the order the participants were tested.","headers":["Participant","Number of sunk-cost decisions"],"rows":[["1","4"],["2","9"],["3","2"],["4","6"],["5","3"],["6","9"],["7","5"],["8","9"],["9","10"]]},"stem":"What is the median number of sunk-cost decisions in this data set?","options":["3","6","9","8"],"answer":1,"rationale":"Ordered from lowest to highest, the scores are 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 9, 9, 9, 10, so the middle (fifth) score is 6. The value 3 is the middle score only in the unsorted list, and 9 is the mode."},{"id":"U2-067","section":"2.2","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":1,"stem":"In a creative writing club, students are given ten minutes to write as many different endings as they can for the same unfinished story. Rashid writes 18 endings, many of which his classmates find surprising and original. Rashid is displaying","options":["divergent thinking","convergent thinking","functional fixedness","the representativeness heuristic"],"answer":0,"rationale":"Divergent thinking is generating many different, novel possibilities from a single starting point. Convergent thinking narrows options down to one best answer."},{"id":"U2-068","section":"2.2","skill":"Concept Recall","difficulty":1,"stem":"A person who relies on the availability heuristic is most likely to","options":["judge how likely an event is by how closely it matches a typical example","work through every possible solution until finding the correct one","keep investing in a plan because of what has already been spent on it","judge how likely an event is by how easily examples of it come to mind"],"answer":3,"rationale":"The availability heuristic estimates likelihood based on how readily examples come to mind. Matching a typical example describes the representativeness heuristic."},{"id":"U2-069","section":"2.2","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":1,"stem":"When the school announces that Leila has won the national science competition, several of her classmates say, \"It was obvious she would win.\" Before the announcement, however, none of them had named Leila as a likely winner. The classmates' thinking illustrates","options":["belief perseverance","the representativeness heuristic","hindsight bias","the gambler's fallacy"],"answer":2,"rationale":"Hindsight bias is the tendency to believe, after an outcome is known, that one would have predicted it. The classmates claim the result was obvious only after hearing it; belief perseverance would mean holding on to an earlier belief despite contrary evidence."},{"id":"U2-070","section":"2.2","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"The student council has run out of ideas for raising money for a class trip, and members complain that nothing they try works. Their advisor asks everyone to call out as many fundraising ideas as possible, however unusual, without judging or rejecting any of them yet. The advisor's instruction best reflects which of the following concepts?","options":["Convergent thinking","Divergent thinking","Assimilation","A mental set"],"answer":1,"rationale":"Divergent thinking involves generating many possible solutions to a problem. The advisor is asking for a wide range of ideas rather than for one correct answer, which would be convergent thinking."},{"id":"U2-071","section":"2.2","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":1,"stem":"Whenever Hiro needs the area of a triangle, he multiplies one-half by the base and the height, a formula that always gives the correct answer when applied correctly. Hiro is using","options":["an algorithm","a heuristic","a prototype","insight"],"answer":0,"rationale":"An algorithm is a logical, step-by-step procedure, such as a formula, that guarantees a correct solution. A heuristic is a shortcut that is faster but can lead to errors."},{"id":"U2-072","section":"0.1","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which of the following research questions would a cognitive psychologist be most likely to investigate?","options":["Do infants who receive consistent care form more secure bonds with their parents?","Why do students often overestimate how much they will remember from a lecture that seemed easy to follow?","Does a person's level of the hormone ghrelin predict how much food that person eats?","Do people put in less effort on a group task when their individual contributions cannot be identified?"],"answer":1,"rationale":"Cognitive psychology studies mental processes such as memory, judgment and decision making, so a question about predicting one's own memory fits best. The other questions are developmental, biological and social questions."},{"id":"U2-073","section":"2.2","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":1,"stem":"Searching for his lost earbuds, Musa checks every drawer, shelf and bag in his apartment, one at a time and in order, until he finds them. Musa's strategy is to use","options":["a heuristic","functional fixedness","an algorithm","a mental set"],"answer":2,"rationale":"An algorithm systematically tries every possibility until the solution is found. A heuristic would be a shortcut, such as checking only the places he usually leaves them."},{"id":"U2-074","section":"0.5","skill":"Data Interpretation","difficulty":2,"stimulus":{"type":"bar","title":"Age at which 200 award-winning engineers produced their most influential invention","xLabel":"Age (years)","yLabel":"Number of engineers","categories":["20-24","25-29","30-34","35-39","40-44","45-49","50-54","55-59","60+"],"series":[{"name":"Engineers","values":[8,30,54,44,30,16,10,5,3]}]},"stem":"The graph shows the ages at which 200 award-winning engineers produced what is generally agreed to be their most influential invention. Which of the following conclusions is supported by the graph?","options":["More than half of the engineers produced their most influential invention after age 40.","The most common age group for engineers' most influential inventions is 30 to 34.","More engineers produced their most influential invention at ages 25 to 29 than at ages 40 to 44.","The data show that growing older causes engineers to become less inventive."],"answer":1,"rationale":"The tallest bar is at ages 30 to 34 (54 engineers), so that is the modal age group. Only 64 of the 200 engineers (32 percent) were over 40, the 25 to 29 and 40 to 44 groups are equal at 30, and descriptive data like these cannot show that age causes a change in inventiveness."},{"id":"U2-075","section":"0.3","skill":"Research Methods and Design","difficulty":1,"stem":"Dr. Iwu wants to know whether the color of a room affects creativity. Students sitting in a blue room and students sitting in a white room are each given five minutes to turn a page of identical printed circles into as many different drawings as they can. Which of the following is the dependent variable?","options":["Creativity","Room color","The five-minute time limit","The number of students in each room"],"answer":0,"rationale":"The dependent variable is what is measured, here creativity (operationalized as the number of different drawings produced). Room color is the independent variable, and the time limit is a controlled variable."},{"id":"U2-076","section":"0.6","skill":"Data Interpretation","difficulty":3,"stimulus":{"type":"table","caption":"A psychologist studied the availability heuristic. In a small first trial, 5 volunteers were randomly assigned to watch a documentary about shark attacks and 5 to watch a documentary about coral reefs. Each volunteer then rated the chance of being attacked by a shark on their next beach trip. The mean ratings were unusually extreme.","headers":["Group","Mean rated chance of a shark attack"],"rows":[["Watched shark attack documentary (n = 5)","85%"],["Watched coral reef documentary (n = 5)","2%"]]},"stem":"The psychologist now plans to randomly assign many more volunteers to each group. As more volunteers provide ratings, which of the following results should the psychologist most reasonably expect?","options":["Both group means will stay exactly the same, because the independent variable has not changed.","The shark-documentary group's mean will probably fall and the coral-documentary group's mean will probably rise, so both become less extreme.","The difference between the groups will grow, because larger samples always produce larger effects.","The results will become less trustworthy, because adding participants increases random error."],"answer":1,"rationale":"Extreme means from very small samples tend to move toward more typical values as more data are added, a pattern called regression toward the mean. Larger samples reduce, not increase, the influence of random error, and they do not automatically enlarge an effect."},{"id":"U2-077","section":"2.2","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"In a spelling game, whenever Nkechi hears an unfamiliar word that ends with a \"shun\" sound, she writes the ending as \"-tion,\" because that spelling has usually been correct in the past, even though the correct ending is sometimes \"-sion.\" This illustrates the use of","options":["an algorithm","insight","a heuristic","elaborative rehearsal"],"answer":2,"rationale":"A heuristic is a mental shortcut based on what has usually worked, which is fast but can produce errors. An algorithm would guarantee the correct spelling, for example by checking every word in a dictionary."},{"id":"U2-078","section":"2.2","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":1,"stem":"Mai buys flowers for her grandmother but has no vase, so she rinses out a large glass pasta-sauce jar and uses it as a vase. Mai's solution shows that she has overcome","options":["the sunk-cost fallacy","functional fixedness","hindsight bias","egocentrism"],"answer":1,"rationale":"Functional fixedness is the tendency to see objects only in terms of their usual function. Mai saw a new use for a food jar, so she overcame it."},{"id":"U2-079","section":"2.2","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Coach Adeyemi is known for her creativity. In any game situation, she can quickly sketch a dozen very different set plays for her basketball team, and other coaches often copy her original ideas. Which of the following concepts best explains Coach Adeyemi's talent for generating many possible plays?","options":["Divergent thinking","Convergent thinking","A mental set","The representativeness heuristic"],"answer":0,"rationale":"Divergent thinking is producing many novel possibilities for a single problem, a key part of creativity. Convergent thinking narrows down to one best answer."},{"id":"U2-080","section":"2.2","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":3,"stem":"A quiz asks students whether, in a long English novel, there are more seven-letter words that end in \"ing\" or more seven-letter words that have \"n\" as their sixth letter. Logically, the second group must be larger, because every seven-letter word ending in \"ing\" also has \"n\" as its sixth letter. If a student relies on the availability heuristic, he would most likely","options":["say that words with \"n\" as the sixth letter are more common, because he reasons from the definitions","list every seven-letter word he knows and count both groups before answering","say that both groups are equally common, because both describe seven-letter words","say that words ending in \"ing\" are more common, because examples of them come to mind more easily"],"answer":3,"rationale":"The availability heuristic judges frequency by how easily examples come to mind. Words ending in \"ing\" are easy to think of, so they seem more common even though they are only a subset of the other group. Counting every word would be an algorithm."},{"id":"U2-081","section":"2.2","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"On a family camping trip in the desert, Saeed realizes he has forgotten the tent pegs. Although he has packed several long metal grilling skewers, he decides the tent cannot be put up, and the family sleeps in the car. Saeed's failure to realize that the skewers could hold the tent down is an example of","options":["confirmation bias","the sunk-cost fallacy","functional fixedness","the gambler's fallacy"],"answer":2,"rationale":"Functional fixedness is the inability to see an object as useful for anything other than its usual purpose. Saeed saw the skewers only as tools for grilling."},{"id":"U2-082","section":"0.3","skill":"Research Methods and Design","difficulty":2,"stem":"Dr. Haddad investigated whether time pressure affects problem-solving. She randomly assigned 80 participants to two groups of 40. Participants in one group were repeatedly reminded that time was running out, while participants in the other group received no reminders. Each participant had 10 minutes to work on the same set of 12 matchstick puzzles, and Dr. Haddad recorded how many puzzles each participant solved correctly. How did Dr. Haddad operationally define the dependent variable?","options":["As the repeated reminders that time was running out","As the 10-minute time limit given to every participant","As problem-solving ability","As the number of matchstick puzzles each participant solved correctly"],"answer":3,"rationale":"An operational definition describes exactly how a variable is measured. Problem-solving ability is the dependent variable, but it is measured as the number of puzzles solved correctly; the reminders are the independent variable and the time limit is held constant."},{"id":"U2-083","section":"2.2","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"After seeing several widely shared videos of people stuck in broken elevators, Marisol climbs the stairs to her fifteenth-floor office every day. Yet she regularly looks at her phone while crossing busy streets, which is far more dangerous. Marisol's pattern of fears is best explained by","options":["the availability heuristic","the representativeness heuristic","the gambler's fallacy","a mental set"],"answer":0,"rationale":"The availability heuristic leads people to judge risk by how easily vivid examples come to mind. The memorable elevator videos make that rare danger feel more likely than the common risk of distracted walking."},{"id":"U2-084","section":"2.2","skill":"Concept Recall","difficulty":1,"stem":"Psychologists use which term for a mental block in which a person sees an object only in terms of the job it was designed to do?","options":["a mental set","a prototype","insight","functional fixedness"],"answer":3,"rationale":"Functional fixedness is thinking of objects only in terms of their usual functions. A mental set is a broader tendency to repeat problem-solving approaches that worked before."},{"id":"U2-085","section":"2.2","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":1,"stem":"Before booking a flight home for the holidays, Pablo checks the price of every flight offered by every airline on every date in December, records them all in a spreadsheet and compares them before choosing. The process takes him an entire evening, but he is certain to find the cheapest option. Which of the following concepts did Pablo use to solve this problem?","options":["A heuristic","Divergent thinking","An algorithm","Insight"],"answer":2,"rationale":"An algorithm works through all possible solutions systematically and guarantees an answer. Pablo's method is slow but certain to find the cheapest flight, unlike a heuristic such as checking only his usual airline."},{"id":"U2-086","section":"2.2","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which of the following scenarios best shows a person overcoming functional fixedness?","options":["Ibrahim tries every key on a large key ring, one at a time, until the door opens.","Needing to prop open a heavy door, Sana wedges a folded rubber sandal underneath it.","Lena keeps using the shortcut her tutor taught her, even on problems where it gives wrong answers.","Pierre assumes the quiet student in the library must be the top scorer in the class."],"answer":1,"rationale":"Using a sandal as a doorstop is seeing a new function for an ordinary object, which overcomes functional fixedness. The other scenarios show an algorithm, a mental set and the representativeness heuristic."},{"id":"U2-087","section":"2.2","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which of the following scenarios is an example of the gambler's fallacy?","options":["After rolling no sixes in fifteen turns of a board game, Kalani insists that her next roll is almost certain to be a six.","Oliver sits through a boring film to the end because he has already paid for the ticket.","Mira does not notice that the waiter who took her order has been replaced by a different waiter.","Shoppers choose meat labeled \"90% lean\" over identical meat labeled \"10% fat.\""],"answer":0,"rationale":"The gambler's fallacy is the belief that past random outcomes change the odds of future independent outcomes. Each die roll is independent, so a six is no more likely after a run of misses. The other scenarios show the sunk-cost fallacy, change blindness and framing."},{"id":"U2-088","section":"2.2","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Jae-won has lost the coin toss to choose ends at the start of his last four football matches. Before the next match, he tells his teammates not to bother planning for the less favorable end, because he is sure that he is \"due\" to win the toss this time. The error in his thinking is most likely attributable to","options":["the availability heuristic","hindsight bias","a mental set","the gambler's fallacy"],"answer":3,"rationale":"The gambler's fallacy is the mistaken belief that a run of one outcome makes the opposite outcome more likely in independent random events. Each coin toss still has a 50 percent chance."},{"id":"U2-089","section":"2.2","skill":"Data Interpretation","difficulty":2,"stimulus":{"type":"line","title":"Zainab's estimated chance of landing on gold before each spin","xLabel":"Spin number","yLabel":"Estimated chance of gold (%)","series":[{"name":"Zainab's estimate","points":[[1,10],[2,12],[3,15],[4,20],[5,25],[6,30],[7,40],[8,50]]}],"hlines":[{"y":10,"label":"Actual chance (10%)"}]},"stem":"A game spinner is divided into 10 equal sections, and only 1 of them is gold. Before each spin, Zainab estimates her chance of landing on gold. She does not land on gold on any of the first eight spins. The graph shows her estimates. The pattern in Zainab's thinking reflects which psychological phenomenon?","options":["Hindsight bias","The availability heuristic","The gambler's fallacy","The sunk-cost fallacy"],"answer":2,"rationale":"Each spin is independent, so the true chance stays at 10 percent, yet Zainab's estimates climb toward 50 percent after each miss. Believing that past misses make a win more likely is the gambler's fallacy."},{"id":"U2-090","section":"2.2","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Two students race to unscramble the letters LPAEP. Kiran writes out every possible ordering of the five letters, one after another, until he reaches APPLE. Ines remembers that many English words end in \"-PLE\" and finds the answer within seconds. Kiran's approach involved which of the following?","options":["A heuristic","An algorithm","Insight","Priming"],"answer":1,"rationale":"An algorithm checks every possible solution systematically, which guarantees success but is slow. Ines used a heuristic, a faster shortcut based on common letter patterns that does not guarantee success."},{"id":"U2-091","section":"0.3","skill":"Research Methods and Design","difficulty":2,"stem":"Dr. Mwangi publishes a study concluding that a puzzle app improves \"problem-solving skill,\" but her report never explains how problem-solving skill was measured. The most serious problem this creates is that","options":["other researchers cannot replicate the study to check whether the finding holds up","the participants could not have been randomly assigned to conditions","the study must be reclassified as a case study","the results cannot be reported as a correlation coefficient"],"answer":0,"rationale":"Without a precise operational definition, other researchers do not know how to measure the variable in the same way, so they cannot repeat the study and test the result. Operational definitions do not determine whether random assignment was used or what kind of statistic is reported."},{"id":"U2-092","section":"2.2","skill":"Concept Recall","difficulty":1,"stem":"A problem-solving strategy that guarantees a correct solution but can be very slow, because it may require checking every possibility, is called","options":["a heuristic","a prototype","a mental set","an algorithm"],"answer":3,"rationale":"An algorithm is thorough and certain to find a solution, but it can be time-consuming. A heuristic is faster but can lead to errors."},{"id":"U2-093","section":"2.2","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":1,"stem":"When choosing a checkout line at a busy supermarket, Esperanza always joins the line with the fewest people in it, without counting how many items each shopper has or how fast each cashier works. Her choice is usually quick and often works, though sometimes she picks a slow line. Esperanza is using","options":["an algorithm","functional fixedness","a heuristic","insight"],"answer":2,"rationale":"A heuristic is a mental shortcut that usually works and saves time but does not guarantee the best outcome. Counting every shopper's items and timing every cashier would be closer to an algorithm."},{"id":"U2-094","section":"2.2","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"In a coding class, Yohannes discovers that restarting the program fixes one particular error. He now restarts the program whenever any error appears, even errors that restarting cannot fix, and he keeps getting stuck. Yohannes's approach is an example of","options":["insight","a mental set","functional fixedness","accommodation"],"answer":1,"rationale":"A mental set is the tendency to keep using a strategy that worked in the past, even when it no longer fits the problem. Accommodation would mean adjusting his approach to the new kinds of errors."},{"id":"U2-095","section":"2.2","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":1,"stem":"To solve a Sudoku puzzle, Bongani follows a fixed set of rules from a puzzle book, which promises that following the rules in order will always solve its puzzles: for each empty square, he lists every number from 1 to 9 that is not already in its row, column and box, fills in any square with only one possible number, and repeats until the grid is complete. Which problem-solving approach is Bongani using?","options":["An algorithm","The representativeness heuristic","Insight","Divergent thinking"],"answer":0,"rationale":"An algorithm is a step-by-step procedure that leads to the correct result when followed exactly, as Bongani does with the puzzle book's rules. Insight would be a sudden realization of the solution without working through steps."},{"id":"U2-201","section":"2.2","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Kofi wants to hang a framed photo on his bedroom wall, but he cannot find a hammer anywhere in the apartment. Which of the following cognitive processes would most help him complete the task?","options":["Convergent thinking","Functional fixedness","Divergent thinking","Belief perseverance"],"answer":2,"rationale":"Divergent thinking generates many possible solutions, such as using the heel of a sturdy shoe or a heavy book to tap in the nail. Functional fixedness is the barrier he needs to overcome, not the process that helps him."},{"id":"U2-202","section":"2.2","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Leila's mother, father and two uncles all work as pharmacists, and most of her neighbors' parents do too. When her teacher asks the class to estimate what share of adults in the country work as pharmacists, Leila gives a much higher estimate than her classmates. Leila's reasoning is best explained by","options":["the availability heuristic","the representativeness heuristic","the gambler's fallacy","framing"],"answer":0,"rationale":"Because pharmacists are the examples that come to Leila's mind most easily, she judges them to be more common than they are. The representativeness heuristic involves judging by how well something matches a prototype, not by how easily examples come to mind."},{"id":"U2-203","section":"2.2","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Two-year-old Amal learns that the big yellow vehicle that stops outside her house each morning is called a bus. One afternoon she sees a large yellow delivery truck drive past and excitedly shouts, \"Bus!\" Which concept is Amal demonstrating?","options":["Accommodation","Conservation","Mental set","Assimilation"],"answer":3,"rationale":"Amal fits the new vehicle into her existing schema for bus without changing the schema, which is assimilation. Accommodation would occur if she adjusted her schema to create a separate category for trucks."},{"id":"U2-204","section":"2.2","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"On the first night of a desert camping trip, Omar watches a documentary on his tablet about venomous scorpions. Later, while sitting on the sand by the campfire, he feels something brush against his ankle. He leaps up and shouts to everyone that there is a scorpion, even though it was only a dry leaf blown by the wind. Which cognitive process best explains his reaction?","options":["The availability heuristic","The sunk-cost fallacy","Convergent thinking","The gambler's fallacy"],"answer":0,"rationale":"The vivid images from the documentary are easy to recall, so Omar quickly judges the touch to be a scorpion. The sunk-cost fallacy involves continuing an action because of what has already been invested, which does not fit this situation."},{"id":"U2-205","section":"2.2","skill":"Concept Recall","difficulty":1,"stem":"A quick problem-solving strategy that reduces mental effort and often, but not always, leads to a correct answer is called","options":["an algorithm","a prototype","a heuristic","a mental set"],"answer":2,"rationale":"Heuristics are mental shortcuts that save time but can produce errors. An algorithm, by contrast, is a step-by-step procedure that guarantees a solution but is usually slower."},{"id":"U2-206","section":"2.2","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Researchers ask participants which of two cities receives more international tourists each year. Participants usually choose the city whose name they have heard more often in news reports and travel advertisements, even when the other city actually receives more visitors. Which of the following best explains this finding?","options":["The representativeness heuristic","An algorithm","Framing","The availability heuristic"],"answer":3,"rationale":"The availability heuristic leads people to judge frequency by how easily examples come to mind, so the more familiar city seems bigger. An algorithm would involve systematically checking real tourism figures."},{"id":"U2-207","section":"2.2","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Before her first day as a hospital volunteer, Rosa already expects to see nurses' stations, waiting rooms, beeping monitors and staff in white coats, and she knows she should speak quietly in the corridors. This organized framework of knowledge and expectations about hospitals is best described as a","options":["schema","heuristic","mental set","process of assimilation"],"answer":0,"rationale":"A schema is a mental framework that organizes related knowledge and expectations about a concept or situation. Assimilation is a process of fitting new information into a schema, not the framework itself."},{"id":"U2-208","section":"2.2","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":1,"stem":"A fitness center advertises that \"90 percent of our members reach their fitness goals.\" Another fitness center with identical results advertises that \"10 percent of our members do not reach their fitness goals.\" Most people prefer the first fitness center. This preference is best explained by","options":["framing","priming","the sunk-cost fallacy","the gambler's fallacy"],"answer":0,"rationale":"Framing is the way information is presented, which can change decisions even when the facts are the same. Priming involves earlier exposure to a stimulus influencing later responses, which is not what differs here."},{"id":"U2-209","section":"2.2","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"A preschool teacher writes brief notes about four children during free play. Which note best illustrates the process Jean Piaget called assimilation?","options":["During a video call, Noor holds her drawing up so that it faces her own eyes, not the camera, and asks her grandmother whether she likes it.","Having learned the word \"plane,\" Arjun points at a large bird gliding overhead and shouts, \"Plane!\"","After being told that the striped animal he called a \"horse\" is a zebra, Mei starts using a separate word for striped animals.","Kai says a sparrow is a \"better\" bird than a penguin because sparrows look more like the birds in his picture books."],"answer":1,"rationale":"Arjun fits the bird into his existing schema for plane without changing the schema, which is assimilation. Mei changes her schema to make a new category, which is accommodation, and Noor's behavior shows egocentrism."},{"id":"U2-210","section":"2.2","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"For several days, Rafael has been watching repeated news clips and social media videos about an elevator that broke down in a shopping mall. Although elevator accidents are extremely rare, he now takes the stairs in every building because he believes elevators are very dangerous. Rafael's thinking demonstrates which of the following?","options":["Representativeness heuristic","Mental set","Availability heuristic","Belief perseverance"],"answer":2,"rationale":"Rafael judges the risk of elevators by how easily vivid examples come to mind, which is the availability heuristic. Belief perseverance is clinging to a belief after it has been discredited, and nothing has challenged his belief yet."},{"id":"U2-211","section":"2.6","skill":"Data Interpretation","difficulty":2,"stimulus":{"type":"bar","title":"Interpretations of Ambiguous Shapes by Group","xLabel":"Category of interpretation","yLabel":"Percent of responses","categories":["Sea creatures","Vehicles","Faces","Plants"],"series":[{"name":"Group 1 (ocean podcast)","values":[62,12,14,12]},{"name":"Group 2 (no podcast)","values":[18,28,30,24]}],"yMax":70},"stem":"Dr. Kamau studies perception and judgment. Participants in Group 1 listened to a ten-minute podcast about life on a coral reef. Participants in Group 2 did not listen to a podcast. All participants then viewed a series of ambiguous shapes and wrote down what each shape looked like. Their responses were sorted into categories, as shown in the graph. Which of the following concepts best explains the results for Group 1?","options":["Inattentional blindness","Priming","Framing","Change blindness"],"answer":1,"rationale":"Hearing about reef life activated ocean-related ideas, which made Group 1 more likely to interpret the ambiguous shapes as sea creatures. Framing refers to how a choice is worded, and the shapes were presented in the same way to both groups."},{"id":"U2-212","section":"2.2","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Ayesha has forgotten the three-digit code for her luggage lock. She starts at 000 and works through 001, 002, 003 and so on, testing every possible combination until the lock opens. Which problem-solving approach is Ayesha using?","options":["A heuristic","Functional fixedness","Insight","An algorithm"],"answer":3,"rationale":"An algorithm is a step-by-step procedure that tries all possible solutions and guarantees an answer, even if it is slow. A heuristic would be a shortcut, such as first trying her birthday digits."},{"id":"U2-213","section":"2.2","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"To find a classmate's name on an unsorted list of 300 students, Nadia reads every name one at a time from the top of the list to the bottom. This method guarantees that she will find the name, but it takes much longer than guessing where the name might be. Nadia's method is an example of","options":["an algorithm","a heuristic","a prototype","insight"],"answer":0,"rationale":"Checking every possibility in order is an algorithm, which guarantees a solution at the cost of time. A heuristic would trade that guarantee for speed, for example by scanning only the part of the list she thinks is most likely."},{"id":"U2-214","section":"2.2","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"set":"u2b-burglary","stimulus":{"type":"line","title":"Perceived Versus Actual Burglary Rate, 2010 to 2022","xLabel":"Year","yLabel":"Burglaries per 1,000 homes","series":[{"name":"Actual rate","points":[[2010,30],[2012,28],[2014,26],[2016,24],[2018,22],[2020,19],[2022,18]]},{"name":"Average perceived rate","points":[[2010,41],[2012,43],[2014,42],[2016,44],[2018,45],[2020,44],[2022,46]]}]},"stem":"Researchers randomly selected 400 adults in a large city and asked them to estimate the burglary rate for each year from 2010 to 2022. The researchers compared these estimates with the official burglary rate, as shown in the graph. Burglaries are often featured in local news reports and shared widely on social media. Which psychological concept best explains why participants overestimated the burglary rate in every year of the study?","options":["Gambler's fallacy","Functional fixedness","Availability heuristic","Mental set"],"answer":2,"rationale":"Vivid, frequently shared reports make burglaries easy to recall, so people judge them to be more common than they really are. The gambler's fallacy involves expecting random events to even out, which does not explain a steady overestimate."},{"id":"U2-215","section":"0.5","skill":"Data Interpretation","difficulty":2,"set":"u2b-burglary","stimulus":{"type":"line","title":"Perceived Versus Actual Burglary Rate, 2010 to 2022","xLabel":"Year","yLabel":"Burglaries per 1,000 homes","series":[{"name":"Actual rate","points":[[2010,30],[2012,28],[2014,26],[2016,24],[2018,22],[2020,19],[2022,18]]},{"name":"Average perceived rate","points":[[2010,41],[2012,43],[2014,42],[2016,44],[2018,45],[2020,44],[2022,46]]}]},"stem":"Researchers randomly selected 400 adults in a large city and asked them to estimate the burglary rate for each year from 2010 to 2022. The researchers compared these estimates with the official burglary rate, as shown in the graph. Which of the following conclusions is best supported by the overall results?","options":["Participants' estimates closely followed the downward trend in the actual burglary rate.","The gap between perceived and actual burglary rates grew wider over the period studied.","The actual burglary rate rose and fell unpredictably from 2010 to 2022.","In at least one year, participants underestimated the burglary rate."],"answer":1,"rationale":"The actual rate fell from 30 to 18 while the perceived rate rose from 41 to 46, so the gap widened from 11 to 28 burglaries per 1,000 homes. Participants overestimated the rate in every year, so they never underestimated it."},{"id":"U2-216","section":"0.3","skill":"Research Methods and Design","difficulty":2,"set":"u2b-burglary","stimulus":{"type":"line","title":"Perceived Versus Actual Burglary Rate, 2010 to 2022","xLabel":"Year","yLabel":"Burglaries per 1,000 homes","series":[{"name":"Actual rate","points":[[2010,30],[2012,28],[2014,26],[2016,24],[2018,22],[2020,19],[2022,18]]},{"name":"Average perceived rate","points":[[2010,41],[2012,43],[2014,42],[2016,44],[2018,45],[2020,44],[2022,46]]}]},"stem":"Researchers randomly selected 400 adults in a large city and asked them to estimate the burglary rate for each year from 2010 to 2022. The researchers compared these estimates with the official burglary rate, as shown in the graph. In a follow-up experiment, 120 new volunteers were each randomly placed in one of two conditions. Volunteers in one condition read an infographic showing the official burglary statistics, and volunteers in the other condition read an infographic about recycling. Afterward, every volunteer estimated the current burglary rate. What is the independent variable in the follow-up experiment?","options":["How accurate the volunteers' burglary estimates were","The random placement of volunteers into conditions","Which infographic the volunteers read","The 400 adults who took part in the original survey"],"answer":2,"rationale":"The independent variable is the factor the researchers manipulate, which here is whether volunteers read the burglary statistics or the recycling infographic. The accuracy of the estimates is the dependent variable, and random placement is a procedure for creating equivalent groups, not a variable."},{"id":"U2-217","section":"2.2","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"During a board game, Tariq has rolled a fair six-sided die ten times without getting a six. He tells his cousins, \"My next roll is almost certain to be a six because I'm due for one.\" Tariq's reasoning is an example of which of the following?","options":["Availability heuristic","Sunk-cost fallacy","Belief perseverance","Gambler's fallacy"],"answer":3,"rationale":"The gambler's fallacy is the belief that past random outcomes change the odds of future independent outcomes. Each roll still has a one-in-six chance of being a six."},{"id":"U2-218","section":"2.2","skill":"Research Methods and Design","difficulty":2,"set":"u2b-alt-uses","stimulus":{"type":"table","caption":"Number of alternative uses generated in five minutes","headers":["Volunteer","Brick","Spoon","Shoelace"],"rows":[["Volunteer 1","2","4","1"],["Volunteer 2","6","9","5"],["Volunteer 3","10","7","8"],["Volunteer 4","1","5","0"],["Volunteer 5","8","11","6"],["Volunteer 6","5","3","7"],["Volunteer 7","7","6","9"]]},"stem":"Dr. Okafor studied creativity. Seven volunteers were shown three everyday objects, one at a time, and were asked to list as many different uses for each object as they could in five minutes. The table shows the number of uses each volunteer generated. Which of the following concepts most directly relates to Dr. Okafor's research question?","options":["Convergent thinking","Divergent thinking","Algorithm","Framing"],"answer":1,"rationale":"Producing many varied uses for an object measures divergent thinking, the ability to generate multiple novel possibilities. Convergent thinking narrows down to one correct answer, which this task does not require."},{"id":"U2-219","section":"2.2","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"set":"u2b-alt-uses","stimulus":{"type":"table","caption":"Number of alternative uses generated in five minutes","headers":["Volunteer","Brick","Spoon","Shoelace"],"rows":[["Volunteer 1","2","4","1"],["Volunteer 2","6","9","5"],["Volunteer 3","10","7","8"],["Volunteer 4","1","5","0"],["Volunteer 5","8","11","6"],["Volunteer 6","5","3","7"],["Volunteer 7","7","6","9"]]},"stem":"Dr. Okafor studied creativity. Seven volunteers were shown three everyday objects, one at a time, and were asked to list as many different uses for each object as they could in five minutes. The table shows the number of uses each volunteer generated. The volunteer who listed the fewest uses for the shoelace said afterward, \"I just kept picturing it laced through a shoe.\" Cognitive psychologists would describe this volunteer's difficulty as an example of","options":["the sunk-cost fallacy","priming","functional fixedness","the gambler's fallacy"],"answer":2,"rationale":"Functional fixedness is the tendency to think of objects only in terms of their usual function, which kept Volunteer 4 from imagining other uses for the shoelace."},{"id":"U2-220","section":"0.5","skill":"Data Interpretation","difficulty":2,"set":"u2b-alt-uses","stimulus":{"type":"table","caption":"Number of alternative uses generated in five minutes","headers":["Volunteer","Brick","Spoon","Shoelace"],"rows":[["Volunteer 1","2","4","1"],["Volunteer 2","6","9","5"],["Volunteer 3","10","7","8"],["Volunteer 4","1","5","0"],["Volunteer 5","8","11","6"],["Volunteer 6","5","3","7"],["Volunteer 7","7","6","9"]]},"stem":"Dr. Okafor studied creativity. Seven volunteers were shown three everyday objects, one at a time, and were asked to list as many different uses for each object as they could in five minutes. The table shows the number of uses each volunteer generated. What is the median number of alternative uses generated for the spoon?","options":["5","6","6.4","8"],"answer":1,"rationale":"Ordered from lowest to highest, the spoon values are 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 9 and 11, so the middle value is 6. The mean is about 6.4 and the range is 8, which are different measures."},{"id":"U2-221","section":"2.2","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"While hiking, Jun-ho and his friends realize they have packed a can of beans but no can opener. Jun-ho rubs the top rim of the can firmly against a flat, rough rock until the seal wears thin, then presses the lid open with his thumb. Jun-ho has overcome which barrier to problem solving?","options":["Functional fixedness","Mental set","Confirmation bias","Representativeness heuristic"],"answer":0,"rationale":"Jun-ho saw a new function for an ordinary rock instead of thinking only of a can opener, which overcomes functional fixedness. Mental set is sticking with a strategy that worked before, which is a related but different barrier."},{"id":"U2-222","section":"0.6","skill":"Research Methods and Design","difficulty":2,"stem":"Dr. Al Mansoori compares decisions made by participants who read a positively framed health message with decisions made by participants who read a negatively framed version. She reports that the difference between the two groups is statistically significant. What does this finding mean?","options":["The results prove that the hypothesis is correct for all people.","The difference between the groups is large enough to be important in everyday life.","The independent variable most likely had no effect on the dependent variable.","A difference this large would be unlikely to occur by chance if the framing had no real effect."],"answer":3,"rationale":"Statistical significance means the observed difference is unlikely to be due to chance alone if there were truly no effect. It does not prove the hypothesis or show that the effect is large or practically important."},{"id":"U2-223","section":"2.2","skill":"Research Methods and Design","difficulty":2,"stimulus":{"type":"table","caption":"Responses to the survey question about the proposed metro line","headers":["Response","Percent of commuters"],"rows":[["A great deal","48"],["Somewhat","31"],["A little","14"],["Not at all","7"]]},"stem":"A city transport department hires Dr. Lindqvist, a cognitive psychologist, to measure public opinion about a proposed new metro line before the city decides whether to fund it. She emails a one-question survey to 2,000 commuters. The question reads: \"How much would the new metro line relieve the stressful traffic delays you face every day?\" The department reports the results, shown in the table, as evidence of strong public support. Which of the following is a potential source of bias in these results?","options":["Chunking","Sunk-cost fallacy","Framing","Prospective memory"],"answer":2,"rationale":"The survey question is worded to emphasize stressful delays and assumes the metro would help, so the way it is framed could push commuters toward positive answers. Chunking is a memory strategy for grouping information and would not bias how commuters answered."},{"id":"U2-224","section":"2.2","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":1,"stem":"At a restaurant, Hessa works out the exact tip by multiplying the total bill by 0.15 instead of estimating a rough amount. Using a formula in this way is an example of","options":["a heuristic","divergent thinking","functional fixedness","an algorithm"],"answer":3,"rationale":"A formula is a step-by-step procedure that guarantees the correct answer, which makes it an algorithm. Estimating a rough amount would be a heuristic."},{"id":"U2-225","section":"2.4","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"To prepare for a biology test, Kwame spends an hour building a concept map that links each new term to related terms according to what they mean, such as connecting \"mitochondria\" to \"cellular energy.\" Kwame's study method is an example of which of the following?","options":["Automatic processing","Effortful processing","Procedural memory","Iconic memory"],"answer":1,"rationale":"Deliberately organizing information by meaning takes attention and work, which is effortful processing. Automatic processing happens without conscious effort, such as remembering what you ate for breakfast."},{"id":"U2-226","section":"2.4","skill":"Concept Recall","difficulty":1,"stem":"Pedro is about to learn the names of the bones of the human skull in his anatomy class. According to the multi-store model of memory, which process must occur first before he can later remember the names?","options":["Storage","Retrieval","Encoding","Relearning"],"answer":2,"rationale":"Information must first be encoded, or taken into the memory system, before it can be stored and later retrieved. Retrieval is the final step of getting stored information back out."},{"id":"U2-227","section":"2.3","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Dina took piano lessons when she was five and six years old but stopped and does not remember the lessons at all. At age seventeen she starts piano again and learns new pieces far faster than the other beginners in her class. Dina's rapid learning is most likely due to","options":["episodic memory","prospective memory","implicit memory","echoic memory"],"answer":2,"rationale":"Implicit memory retains skills and learning without conscious recollection, which is why Dina relearns faster even though she cannot recall the lessons. Episodic memory would require her to consciously remember the lesson experiences."},{"id":"U2-228","section":"2.3","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":1,"stem":"Fatima tells her younger brother a story about the afternoon she first rode a camel at their grandfather's farm: the hot sun, the camel kneeling down to let her climb on, and her brother laughing from the shade. Which type of memory is Fatima drawing on to tell this story?","options":["Semantic","Episodic","Procedural","Iconic"],"answer":1,"rationale":"Episodic memory stores personal experiences tied to a particular time and place. Semantic memory holds general facts, such as knowing that camels can store fat in their humps."},{"id":"U2-229","section":"2.3","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"After a minor stroke, a tailor who has used a sewing machine for thirty years can no longer remember how to thread and operate it, although he can still explain what a sewing machine is and recall the day he bought his first one. His difficulty indicates a deficit in which kind of memory?","options":["Semantic","Procedural","Episodic","Iconic"],"answer":1,"rationale":"Procedural memory stores how to perform skills and actions, and that is what the tailor has lost. His knowledge of what a sewing machine is (semantic) and his memory of buying one (episodic) are intact."},{"id":"U2-230","section":"2.3","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":1,"stem":"Kaito, now 17, is asked by his grandmother to describe the day his family moved from Osaka to Dubai when he was twelve. He recalls the long flight, the heat when they first stepped outside the airport and the first meal in their new apartment. Which kind of memory is Kaito mainly using?","options":["Semantic memory","Prospective memory","Iconic memory","Episodic memory"],"answer":3,"rationale":"Recalling a specific personal event from a particular time and place uses episodic memory. Iconic memory is a brief sensory store for visual images that lasts less than a second."},{"id":"U2-231","section":"2.3","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":1,"stem":"Which of the following is the best example of episodic memory?","options":["Lucas remembers the moment his team scored the winning goal in last year's school football final.","Lucas knows that Nairobi is the capital city of Kenya.","Lucas can type on a keyboard without looking at the keys.","Lucas reminds himself to call his grandmother after dinner tonight."],"answer":0,"rationale":"Episodic memory is memory for specific personal events, like a particular goal in a particular match. Knowing a capital city is semantic memory, typing is procedural memory and remembering a future task is prospective memory."},{"id":"U2-232","section":"0.5","skill":"Data Interpretation","difficulty":2,"stimulus":{"type":"table","caption":"Scores on a typing-skill test (out of 20)","headers":["Student","Sunday (Test 1)","Thursday (Test 2)"],"rows":[["1","4","5"],["2","6","6"],["3","7","9"],["4","9","8"],["5","10","11"],["6","12","12"],["7","13","15"],["8","15","14"]]},"stem":"Eight students took a typing-skill test that measures procedural memory on Sunday (Test 1), and the same students took the same test again on Thursday (Test 2). Their scores are shown in the table. Which of the following correlation coefficients was most likely obtained from the two sets of scores?","options":["-.95","+.10","+1.00","+.95"],"answer":3,"rationale":"Students who scored high on Test 1 generally scored high on Test 2, which shows a strong positive correlation. It is not a perfect +1.00 because some students' ranks switched slightly, such as Students 3 and 4."},{"id":"U2-233","section":"2.4","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Four students are learning the same list of new vocabulary words for an English test. According to the levels of processing model, which student is likely to have the most accurate recall because of deep processing?","options":["Ji-woo, who checks whether each word is printed in bold or italic type in the textbook","Tomas, who says each word aloud and claps once for every syllable","Aaliyah, who traces the shape of each word's letters with her finger","Rania, who decides whether each word could describe a character in the novel her class is reading"],"answer":3,"rationale":"Deciding whether a word fits a character requires thinking about its meaning, which is semantic encoding, the deepest level of processing. Attending to print style or letter shapes is structural processing and counting syllables is phonemic processing, both shallower levels."},{"id":"U2-234","section":"0.2","skill":"Research Methods and Design","difficulty":2,"stem":"Dr. Haddad wants to compare two study methods. Participants either quiz themselves with flashcards or reread their notes for 20 minutes, and then all participants take the same test. To reduce the likelihood that a confounding variable affects the results, Dr. Haddad should do which of the following?","options":["Randomly assign participants to the flashcard condition or the rereading condition","Have the flashcard group study in the morning and the rereading group study in the evening","Debrief all participants about the purpose of the study after the test","Report results only for participants who scored above the average on the test"],"answer":0,"rationale":"Random assignment spreads participant differences, such as prior knowledge or motivation, evenly across conditions so they do not vary along with the study method. Studying at different times of day would add a confound, and debriefing is an ethical step that does not control confounds."},{"id":"U2-235","section":"2.4","skill":"Research Methods and Design","difficulty":2,"stem":"Dr. Mbeki shows two groups the same list of 25 words. Participants in one group decide whether each word contains the letter e. Participants in the other group decide whether each word names something that would be useful on a camping trip. In a surprise recall test, the second group remembers far more words. This study shows the importance of","options":["structural encoding","semantic encoding","maintenance rehearsal","procedural memory"],"answer":1,"rationale":"Judging usefulness for camping requires thinking about each word's meaning, which is semantic encoding and leads to better recall. Checking for a letter is structural encoding, a shallow level of processing."},{"id":"U2-236","section":"2.4","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Ms. Petrova wants her students to remember a set of new psychology vocabulary terms. According to the levels of processing model, which worksheet task would most likely produce the best recall?","options":["Write a sentence using each term to describe something that happened last weekend","Count the number of vowels in each term","Write each term in capital letters three times","Find a word that rhymes with each term"],"answer":0,"rationale":"Using each term to describe a real experience requires processing its meaning, the deepest (semantic) level. Rhyming is phonemic processing, and counting vowels or writing in capitals is structural, both shallower levels."},{"id":"U2-237","section":"2.3","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Before loading the car for a family road trip, Sara pictures several different ways to fit five suitcases, a cooler and a stroller into the trunk, mentally rotating and moving each item until she finds an arrangement that works. Which component of memory allows Sara to manipulate these mental images?","options":["Phonological loop","Iconic memory","Visuospatial sketchpad","Procedural memory"],"answer":2,"rationale":"The visuospatial sketchpad is the part of working memory that holds and manipulates visual and spatial information. The phonological loop handles verbal and auditory information instead."},{"id":"U2-238","section":"2.5","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":1,"stem":"During a power cut at night, a flash of lightning lights up Zayed's living room for an instant. For a fraction of a second after the flash, he can still \"see\" the outline of the sofa and table. Which type of memory is Zayed using?","options":["Iconic","Echoic","Semantic","Prospective"],"answer":0,"rationale":"Iconic memory is the very brief sensory memory for visual images, lasting less than a second. Echoic memory is the brief sensory memory for sounds."},{"id":"U2-239","section":"2.3","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":1,"stem":"Before falling asleep, Mariam tells herself that she must hand her signed field-trip form to her teacher as soon as she arrives at school. The next morning, the sight of her teacher at the classroom door reminds her, and she hands in the form. Which type of memory allowed Mariam to carry out this planned action?","options":["Episodic","Procedural","Semantic","Prospective"],"answer":3,"rationale":"Prospective memory is remembering to carry out an intended action in the future, often triggered by a cue such as seeing the teacher. Episodic memory is for past personal events, not planned future actions."},{"id":"U2-240","section":"2.5","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":3,"stem":"Researchers give a group of rats a chemical that blocks the strengthening of synaptic connections involved in long-term potentiation in the hippocampus. Which of the following is the most likely consequence?","options":["The rats will have difficulty learning and remembering the location of a hidden platform in a water maze.","The rats will lose reflexes that they were born with.","The rats' sensory memory will last much longer than normal.","The rats will learn new tasks faster because of reduced interference."],"answer":0,"rationale":"Long-term potentiation is a biological process for memory in which synapses strengthen with repeated activation, so blocking it should impair forming new long-lasting memories. Inborn reflexes do not depend on learning, so they would not be lost."},{"id":"U2-241","section":"2.4","skill":"Research Methods and Design","difficulty":2,"stem":"A history museum compares two ways of training new tour guides to learn the names of 30 artifacts. Each trainee attends a single one-hour session. Trainees in the first session hear each artifact's name and write down a word that sounds similar to it. Trainees in the second session hear each name and write a sentence explaining what the artifact was used for. A week later, trainees from the second session recall nearly twice as many names. Which of the following best explains this difference?","options":["Differences in levels of processing","The spacing effect","The serial position effect","Proactive interference"],"answer":0,"rationale":"Explaining what each artifact was used for requires thinking about meaning, a deeper (semantic) level of processing than attending to sound, so those names are remembered better. Both groups had one session with the same list, so neither the spacing effect nor the serial position effect explains the difference."},{"id":"U2-242","section":"0.5","skill":"Data Interpretation","difficulty":3,"set":"u2b-lop-data","stimulus":{"type":"table","caption":"Number of words recalled (out of 25) by each participant","headers":["Structural group","Semantic group"],"rows":[["2","15"],["5","16"],["7","17"],["9","17"],["14","17"],["15","18"],["18","19"]]},"stem":"In a memory study, participants in the structural group studied a list of 25 words by deciding whether each word was printed in bold type. Participants in the semantic group studied the same list by deciding whether each word fit into a given sentence. The table shows the number of words each participant later recalled. Based on the data, which of the following statements is true?","options":["Scores in the structural group are more spread out than scores in the semantic group.","The structural group's median is higher than its mean.","The semantic group has a larger range than the structural group.","The two groups have the same median."],"answer":0,"rationale":"Structural scores spread from 2 to 18, while semantic scores cluster between 15 and 19, so the structural group shows much more variability. The structural group's median (9) is lower than its mean (10), and the semantic group's range (4) is smaller than the structural range (16)."},{"id":"U2-243","section":"0.5","skill":"Data Interpretation","difficulty":2,"set":"u2b-lop-data","stimulus":{"type":"table","caption":"Number of words recalled (out of 25) by each participant","headers":["Structural group","Semantic group"],"rows":[["2","15"],["5","16"],["7","17"],["9","17"],["14","17"],["15","18"],["18","19"]]},"stem":"In a memory study, participants in the structural group studied a list of 25 words by deciding whether each word was printed in bold type. Participants in the semantic group studied the same list by deciding whether each word fit into a given sentence. The table shows the number of words each participant later recalled. What is the mean number of words recalled by the structural group?","options":["9","14","16","10"],"answer":3,"rationale":"The structural scores add up to 70, and 70 divided by 7 participants equals 10. The value 9 is the median, and 16 is the range."},{"id":"U2-244","section":"0.5","skill":"Data Interpretation","difficulty":1,"set":"u2b-lop-data","stimulus":{"type":"table","caption":"Number of words recalled (out of 25) by each participant","headers":["Structural group","Semantic group"],"rows":[["2","15"],["5","16"],["7","17"],["9","17"],["14","17"],["15","18"],["18","19"]]},"stem":"In a memory study, participants in the structural group studied a list of 25 words by deciding whether each word was printed in bold type. Participants in the semantic group studied the same list by deciding whether each word fit into a given sentence. The table shows the number of words each participant later recalled. What is the range of the semantic group's scores?","options":["2","17","4","19"],"answer":2,"rationale":"The range is the highest score minus the lowest score: 19 minus 15 equals 4. The value 17 is the mode and median, not the range."},{"id":"U2-245","section":"2.4","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"When Luis is introduced to a new coworker named Mbali, he repeats her name quietly to himself several times, paying close attention to how its two syllables sound and to the unusual \"mb\" sound at the start. Which type of encoding is Luis mainly using?","options":["Structural","Semantic","Phonemic","Automatic processing"],"answer":2,"rationale":"Phonemic encoding focuses on how a word sounds, such as its syllables and starting sound. Semantic encoding would focus on meaning, and structural encoding on how the word looks."},{"id":"U2-246","section":"2.5","skill":"Concept Recall","difficulty":1,"stem":"Which of the following best describes long-term potentiation?","options":["Organizing information into meaningful groups so that it is easier to encode","Sensory receptors converting physical energy into neural signals","New information pushing older information out of short-term memory","Repeated stimulation increasing the strength of signal transmission across a synapse"],"answer":3,"rationale":"Long-term potentiation is the strengthening of synaptic connections between neurons through frequent activation, a biological basis of memory. Converting physical energy into neural signals is transduction, which belongs to sensation."},{"id":"U2-247","section":"2.5","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"At the start of the school year, Chen struggled to recall the symbols of the chemical elements. After using them in lab reports, quizzes and homework for many months, he can now retrieve them instantly, even after the summer break. Which of the following concepts most likely accounts for this change at the level of his neurons?","options":["Retroactive interference","Long-term potentiation","Iconic memory","Mood-congruent memory"],"answer":1,"rationale":"Repeatedly activating the same neural pathways strengthens the synapses involved, which is long-term potentiation and makes retrieval faster and more lasting. Retroactive interference would make old learning harder to recall, not easier."},{"id":"U2-248","section":"2.3","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"After a concussion from a cycling accident, Ingrid can still drive her car with a manual gearbox smoothly, but she cannot recall the name of her country's capital city, a fact she has known since childhood. Which type of memory is intact and which is impaired, respectively?","options":["Semantic intact, procedural impaired","Procedural intact, episodic impaired","Episodic intact, prospective impaired","Procedural intact, semantic impaired"],"answer":3,"rationale":"Driving with a manual gearbox is a skill stored in procedural memory, while the name of a capital city is a general fact stored in semantic memory. Episodic memory would involve a specific personal event, which is not what she lost."},{"id":"U2-249","section":"2.3","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Rashid is a strong swimmer and can do several different strokes without thinking about them, yet he has no memory of the swimming lessons he took as a toddler. Rashid's ability to swim depends on which type of memory?","options":["Procedural","Episodic","Semantic","Echoic"],"answer":0,"rationale":"Procedural memory holds skills and actions that can be performed without conscious recall of learning them. His missing memory of the lessons themselves is an episodic memory gap."},{"id":"U2-250","section":"2.3","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":1,"stem":"Years after his last professional match, a retired cricketer can still bowl with nearly perfect technique without thinking through each movement of his arm and feet. This kind of retained skill is classified as which type of memory?","options":["procedural","semantic","iconic","prospective"],"answer":0,"rationale":"Procedural memory is implicit memory for how to perform actions and skills. Semantic memory is for facts and general knowledge."},{"id":"U2-251","section":"2.3","skill":"Concept Recall","difficulty":1,"stem":"A type of implicit memory for how to carry out actions and processes, such as braiding hair or shuffling a deck of cards, is called","options":["episodic memory","echoic memory","semantic memory","procedural memory"],"answer":3,"rationale":"Procedural memory is the implicit memory for skills and procedures. Episodic and semantic memory are both explicit types of memory."},{"id":"U2-252","section":"2.3","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Joaquin can dribble a basketball down the court while watching his teammates, without thinking about how to move his hand or how hard to bounce the ball. His dribbling ability is best classified as","options":["explicit semantic memory","explicit episodic memory","prospective memory","implicit procedural memory"],"answer":3,"rationale":"Dribbling is a skill performed without conscious recall, which makes it procedural memory, a type of implicit memory. Episodic and semantic memories are explicit and can be described in words."},{"id":"U2-253","section":"2.3","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":1,"stem":"From the side of the field, Kenji's coach shouts a sequence of five play calls: \"Left, left, switch, center, right.\" Kenji mutters the sequence under his breath again and again as he jogs over to his teammates, then repeats it to them. Which component of memory was Kenji mainly using to hold the sequence?","options":["Phonological loop","Visuospatial sketchpad","Iconic memory","Episodic memory"],"answer":0,"rationale":"The phonological loop is the part of working memory that holds and rehearses verbal and auditory information, such as a spoken sequence of words. The visuospatial sketchpad handles images and spatial layouts instead."},{"id":"U2-254","section":"2.2","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Mateo tells his project group that they do not need to set phone reminders for their presentation rehearsals, because he is certain he will remember every time and place. When a teammate asks, he rates himself \"completely sure\" that he can recall all six rehearsal times without help. He later misses three of the rehearsals. Which concept best explains Mateo's decision not to set reminders?","options":["Hindsight bias","Overconfidence","Mental set","Framing"],"answer":1,"rationale":"Overconfidence is being more certain of one's judgments or abilities than is justified, which led Mateo to rely on memory alone. Hindsight bias is the feeling after an event that one knew it all along, which does not explain a decision made beforehand."},{"id":"U2-255","section":"2.3","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":1,"stem":"As part of a study on childhood experiences, Dr. Rahman asks adult participants to describe, in as much detail as possible, the first time they visited a zoo, including who they went with and which animals they saw. Participants' descriptions draw mainly on which type of memory?","options":["Procedural memory","Prospective memory","Episodic memory","Echoic memory"],"answer":2,"rationale":"Memories of specific personal experiences tied to a time and place, like a first zoo visit, are episodic memories. Prospective memory is for remembering future intentions, not past events."},{"id":"U2-256","section":"2.3","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":1,"stem":"Which of the following situations relies most directly on semantic memory?","options":["Remembering on Thursday morning to return a library book that is due that day","Briefly \"hearing\" the last few words a teacher said just after you stopped paying attention","Recalling how the waves felt during your family's beach trip last summer","Knowing that water boils at 100 degrees Celsius at sea level"],"answer":3,"rationale":"Semantic memory is knowledge of general facts and concepts, such as the boiling point of water. Recalling the beach trip is episodic memory, and remembering to return the book is prospective memory."},{"id":"U2-257","section":"2.3","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":1,"stem":"Sipho is preparing for a national vocabulary competition. Over many months he has learned the meanings of thousands of rare English words, although he cannot remember where or when he learned most of them. His stored knowledge of these word meanings is best described as which type of memory?","options":["Episodic memory","Procedural memory","Iconic memory","Semantic memory"],"answer":3,"rationale":"Semantic memory stores general knowledge, such as facts and word meanings, even when the learning episode itself is forgotten. Episodic memory would be his memory of the specific times and places he studied."},{"id":"U2-258","section":"2.3","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"During one school day, Farah does each of the following. Which one depends most directly on semantic memory?","options":["At lunchtime, she remembers that she promised to call her aunt after school.","She types her English essay quickly without looking at the keyboard.","She explains to a classmate that photosynthesis converts light energy into chemical energy.","For a split second after the teacher switches off the projector, she can still \"see\" the slide."],"answer":2,"rationale":"Explaining how photosynthesis works draws on general factual knowledge, which is semantic memory. Remembering to call later is prospective memory, typing is procedural memory, and the brief image of the slide is iconic memory."},{"id":"U2-259","section":"0.5","skill":"Data Interpretation","difficulty":2,"stimulus":{"type":"table","caption":"Digit span (number of digits held in short-term memory)","headers":["Participant","Digits recalled"],"rows":[["1","5"],["2","9"],["3","6"],["4","7"],["5","4"],["6","8"],["7","7"],["8","10"],["9","6"],["10","5"]]},"stem":"Researchers measured the short-term memory capacity of 10 participants by reading strings of digits aloud and recording the longest string each participant could repeat back correctly. The results are shown in the table. What is the median number of digits held in participants' short-term memory?","options":["6","6.5","6.7","7"],"answer":1,"rationale":"Arranged in order, the scores are 4, 5, 5, 6, 6, 7, 7, 8, 9 and 10. With an even number of scores, the median is the mean of the two middle values, 6 and 7, which is 6.5. The value 6.7 is the mean, not the median."},{"id":"U2-260","section":"2.4","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Two students prepare for the same history test on ancient trade routes. Bilal reads the chapter aloud three times, trying to memorize each sentence exactly as written. Hana writes her own questions asking why each route mattered to the people who used it, then answers them in her own words. Whose test performance will probably be better, and why?","options":["Bilal's, because repeating material word for word creates the deepest encoding","Hana's, because she relies on structural encoding of the material","Bilal's, because rereading produces chunking of the material","Hana's, because thinking about why the material matters is a deeper level of processing"],"answer":3,"rationale":"Hana's questions force her to think about the meaning of the material, which is semantic encoding and a deeper level of processing that produces better recall. Bilal's rote rereading is shallow maintenance rehearsal, not deep encoding."},{"id":"U2-261","section":"2.4","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Layla can fold a basket of laundry while following the plot of a podcast with no trouble. However, when she tries to work out a math problem in her head at the same time, she loses track of the podcast and has to replay it. Which of the following best describes how Layla processes the task of folding laundry?","options":["Automatic processing","Effortful processing","Bottom-up processing","Divergent thinking"],"answer":0,"rationale":"Folding laundry is so well practiced that it needs little attention, so it is processed automatically and leaves room to follow the podcast. Mental math requires effortful processing, which competes with the podcast for attention."},{"id":"U2-262","section":"2.4","skill":"Concept Recall","difficulty":1,"stem":"Memorizing how a new term is spelled and what it sounds like, without thinking about what it means, involves","options":["shallow processing","deep processing","procedural memory","semantic encoding"],"answer":0,"rationale":"Focusing on appearance and sound rather than meaning is shallow processing (structural and phonemic levels). Semantic encoding and deep processing would involve thinking about meaning."},{"id":"U2-263","section":"2.4","skill":"Data Interpretation","difficulty":2,"stimulus":{"type":"bar","title":"Percent of Words Recalled by Encoding Task","xLabel":"Encoding task","yLabel":"Percent of words recalled","categories":["Bold type? (structural)","Rhymes with \"train\"? (phonemic)","Fits the sentence? (semantic)"],"series":[{"name":"Recall","values":[18,42,71]}],"yMax":100},"stem":"In a memory study, participants answered a different question about each word on a list. For some words they judged whether the word was printed in bold type, for others whether it rhymed with \"train,\" and for others whether it fit into a given sentence. A surprise recall test followed. Which of the following conclusions is best supported by the graph?","options":["Recall rose with each deeper level of processing, from structural to phonemic to semantic.","Phonemic encoding produced the highest recall because it is the deepest level of processing.","The gain in recall from phonemic to semantic encoding was smaller than the gain from structural to phonemic encoding.","More than half of the words were recalled in each of the three conditions."],"answer":0,"rationale":"Recall climbed from 18 percent (structural) to 42 percent (phonemic) to 71 percent (semantic), matching the levels of processing model. The phonemic to semantic gain (29 points) was actually larger than the structural to phonemic gain (24 points)."},{"id":"U2-264","section":"2.5","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":1,"stem":"Mateo sees a car drive away after scraping a parked bicycle. He silently repeats the car's plate number to himself until he can write it down on his phone a minute later. Which memory system is mainly holding the plate number during that minute?","options":["Sensory memory","Working memory","Long-term memory","Prospective memory"],"answer":1,"rationale":"Working memory actively holds and rehearses information for a short time, such as repeating a number until it is written down. Sensory memory would fade within seconds without attention."},{"id":"U2-265","section":"0.6","skill":"Research Methods and Design","difficulty":2,"stem":"Dr. Yilmaz randomly assigns 40 volunteers to learn a 20-item shopping list using either the method of loci or simple rote repetition. On the recall test, the method-of-loci group remembers an average of 14.2 items and the repetition group remembers an average of 13.1 items. A statistical test gives p = .27. Based on these results, what should Dr. Yilmaz conclude?","options":["The method of loci caused the higher recall scores.","The two methods have been proven to be exactly equally effective.","She cannot rule out chance as the explanation for the 1.1-item difference.","The repetition group must have had less prior experience with shopping lists."],"answer":2,"rationale":"A p value of .27 is above the usual .05 cutoff, so the difference is not statistically significant and could plausibly be due to chance. A nonsignificant result also does not prove that the two methods are identical in effectiveness."},{"id":"U2-266","section":"2.6","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"On his first day as a tour guide, Ravi learns a walking route with 12 stops through an old market district. The next morning, when his manager asks him to list the stops in order, Ravi names the opening stops and the final stops easily but leaves several gaps among the stops in between. Ravi's pattern of recall best illustrates which of the following?","options":["Retrograde amnesia","Proactive interference","The spacing effect","The serial position effect"],"answer":3,"rationale":"The serial position effect predicts better memory for the beginning (primacy) and end (recency) of a sequence than for the middle. Retrograde amnesia is loss of memories from before a brain injury, which does not fit Ravi's situation."},{"id":"U2-267","section":"0.5","skill":"Data Interpretation","difficulty":2,"stimulus":{"type":"table","caption":"Weekly minutes using a retrieval-practice app and math quiz scores","headers":["Student","Minutes per week","Quiz score (%)"],"rows":[["1","10","52"],["2","20","55"],["3","30","61"],["4","40","60"],["5","50","68"],["6","60","72"],["7","70","75"],["8","80","81"]]},"stem":"A cognitive psychologist is interested in whether the amount of time students spend using a new retrieval-practice app is related to their scores on a math quiz. The data are shown in the table. Which description of a scatterplot best represents these data?","options":["Points that rise from the lower left to the upper right, clustered closely around a straight line","Points that fall from the upper left to the lower right, clustered closely around a straight line","Points scattered widely with no visible pattern","Points that rise and then fall, forming an upside-down U shape"],"answer":0,"rationale":"Quiz scores generally increase as app minutes increase, with only one small dip, so the data show a strong positive correlation. A pattern falling from upper left to lower right would indicate a negative correlation."},{"id":"U2-268","section":"2.4","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":1,"stem":"Ms. Ito reads aloud a list of twelve supplies the class needs for a science project. When Hamid gets home, he can remember only the first few items on the list. Which of the following concepts best explains Hamid's experience?","options":["The recency effect","The misinformation effect","The primacy effect","The spacing effect"],"answer":2,"rationale":"The primacy effect is better recall of items at the beginning of a list, often because they received more rehearsal. The recency effect would involve remembering the last few items."},{"id":"U2-269","section":"2.4","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Just before boarding, Thandiwe's mother reads out the 12-character booking reference for their flight, which Thandiwe must type into the airline app about a minute later. Thandiwe mentally groups the characters into three sets of four and repeats each set. Which strategy is Thandiwe using, and why does it help?","options":["Distributed practice, because it spreads rehearsal across several study sessions","Chunking, because it reduces the number of separate units held in short-term memory","The method of loci, because it links each character to a familiar location","Structural encoding, because it focuses attention on how the characters look"],"answer":1,"rationale":"Chunking combines the 12 characters into three larger units, which fits more easily within the limited capacity of short-term memory. Distributed practice requires spreading study over time, which is impossible within one minute."},{"id":"U2-270","section":"0.3","skill":"Research Methods and Design","difficulty":2,"set":"u2b-inventions","stimulus":{"type":"line","title":"Percent of Students Recalling Each Invention by List Position","xLabel":"Position in list","yLabel":"Percent of students recalling","series":[{"name":"Recall","points":[[1,88],[2,80],[3,72],[4,60],[5,38],[6,30],[7,26],[8,24],[9,22],[10,85],[11,24],[12,20],[13,22],[14,25],[15,28],[16,35],[17,50],[18,62],[19,74],[20,84]]}],"bands":[{"from":1,"to":4,"label":"Segment I"},{"from":5,"to":9,"label":"Segment II"},{"from":9.5,"to":10.5,"label":"Segment III"},{"from":11,"to":16,"label":"Segment IV"},{"from":17,"to":20,"label":"Segment V"}]},"stem":"Researchers at a science museum play the same recorded audio tour to two visiting school groups. The tour names 20 inventions in the order they were invented, from the wheel to the smartphone, and the 10th invention named is the airplane. A group of 12-year-olds hears the tour at 9 a.m., and a group of 16-year-olds hears it at 3 p.m. Each student then writes down as many of the inventions as possible. The graph shows recall for both groups combined. The researchers find that the 3 p.m. group recalled more inventions than the 9 a.m. group and conclude that hearing the tour later in the day improves recall. Which of the following is a confounding variable?","options":["The ages of the students in the two groups","The order in which the inventions were named","The number of inventions in the tour","The recording used for the tour"],"answer":0,"rationale":"The two groups differed in age as well as time of day, so the difference in recall could be due to age rather than time of day. The order, number of inventions and recording were the same for both groups, so they cannot explain the difference."},{"id":"U2-271","section":"2.6","skill":"Data Interpretation","difficulty":2,"set":"u2b-inventions","stimulus":{"type":"line","title":"Percent of Students Recalling Each Invention by List Position","xLabel":"Position in list","yLabel":"Percent of students recalling","series":[{"name":"Recall","points":[[1,88],[2,80],[3,72],[4,60],[5,38],[6,30],[7,26],[8,24],[9,22],[10,85],[11,24],[12,20],[13,22],[14,25],[15,28],[16,35],[17,50],[18,62],[19,74],[20,84]]}],"bands":[{"from":1,"to":4,"label":"Segment I"},{"from":5,"to":9,"label":"Segment II"},{"from":9.5,"to":10.5,"label":"Segment III"},{"from":11,"to":16,"label":"Segment IV"},{"from":17,"to":20,"label":"Segment V"}]},"stem":"Researchers at a science museum play the same recorded audio tour to two visiting school groups. The tour names 20 inventions in the order they were invented, from the wheel to the smartphone, and the 10th invention named is the airplane. A group of 12-year-olds hears the tour at 9 a.m., and a group of 16-year-olds hears it at 3 p.m. Each student then writes down as many of the inventions as possible. The graph shows recall for both groups combined. The results in Segment I of the graph are consistent with which of the following?","options":["The recency effect","The spacing effect","The primacy effect","The misinformation effect"],"answer":2,"rationale":"Segment I covers the first items on the list, which were recalled well, showing the primacy effect. The recency effect is the high recall at the end of the list in Segment V."},{"id":"U2-272","section":"2.6","skill":"Data Interpretation","difficulty":3,"set":"u2b-inventions","stimulus":{"type":"line","title":"Percent of Students Recalling Each Invention by List Position","xLabel":"Position in list","yLabel":"Percent of students recalling","series":[{"name":"Recall","points":[[1,88],[2,80],[3,72],[4,60],[5,38],[6,30],[7,26],[8,24],[9,22],[10,85],[11,24],[12,20],[13,22],[14,25],[15,28],[16,35],[17,50],[18,62],[19,74],[20,84]]}],"bands":[{"from":1,"to":4,"label":"Segment I"},{"from":5,"to":9,"label":"Segment II"},{"from":9.5,"to":10.5,"label":"Segment III"},{"from":11,"to":16,"label":"Segment IV"},{"from":17,"to":20,"label":"Segment V"}]},"stem":"Researchers at a science museum play the same recorded audio tour to two visiting school groups. The tour names 20 inventions in the order they were invented, from the wheel to the smartphone, and the 10th invention named is the airplane. A group of 12-year-olds hears the tour at 9 a.m., and a group of 16-year-olds hears it at 3 p.m. Each student then writes down as many of the inventions as possible. The graph shows recall for both groups combined. The results in which segment of the graph are inconsistent with the serial position effect?","options":["Segment I","Segment II","Segment III","Segment IV"],"answer":2,"rationale":"The serial position effect predicts poor recall in the middle of a list, but the airplane in Segment III was recalled by most students, likely because it is especially familiar and distinctive. Segment I shows the expected primacy effect, and Segments II and IV show the expected low recall for middle items."},{"id":"U2-273","section":"3.4","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"At his community center, 85-year-old Mr. Delgado prefers activities that draw on his crystallized intelligence, which has remained strong as he has aged. Which of the following activities will most likely appeal to him?","options":["A crossword puzzle using vocabulary he has known for decades","A fast-paced video game that requires split-second reactions","A newly invented strategy game with unfamiliar rules and timed rounds","A puzzle that requires quickly spotting new patterns in abstract shapes"],"answer":0,"rationale":"Crystallized intelligence is accumulated knowledge and vocabulary, which tends to stay stable or grow with age. Rapidly solving novel problems relies on fluid intelligence, which tends to decline in later adulthood."},{"id":"U2-274","section":"2.5","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":3,"stem":"Dr. Novak reads a list of 15 unrelated words to participants. Instead of recalling the words right away, participants must count backward by threes for 30 seconds before writing down as many words as they can. Compared with participants who recall the list immediately, which pattern of recall is most likely for this group?","options":["Words from the beginning of the list are still recalled well, but the advantage for words at the end largely disappears.","Words at the end of the list are recalled best, and words at the beginning are mostly forgotten.","Words in the middle of the list are recalled better than words at the beginning.","Words in every position of the list are recalled equally well."],"answer":0,"rationale":"The counting task blocks rehearsal and pushes the last words out of short-term memory, which removes the recency effect. The first words were rehearsed more and reached long-term memory, so the primacy effect remains."},{"id":"U2-275","section":"2.6","skill":"Data Interpretation","difficulty":1,"stimulus":{"type":"bar","title":"Average Recall by Position in a 21-Word List","xLabel":"Position of words in list","yLabel":"Percent recalled","categories":["Words 1 to 3","Words 4 to 6","Words 7 to 9","Words 10 to 12","Words 13 to 15","Words 16 to 18","Words 19 to 21"],"series":[{"name":"Percent recalled","values":[76,48,31,26,30,52,81]}],"yMax":100},"stem":"Participants heard a list of 21 unrelated words and immediately wrote down as many as they could remember. The graph shows the average percent recalled for words in each part of the list. The graph illustrates which of the following psychological concepts?","options":["Proactive interference","Chunking","Forgetting curve","Serial position effect"],"answer":3,"rationale":"Recall is highest at the beginning and end of the list and lowest in the middle, the U-shaped pattern of the serial position effect. The forgetting curve shows memory declining over time, not across list positions."},{"id":"U2-276","section":"2.4","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Ms. Haddad will read a list of 12 lab safety rules aloud to her class only once. She wants students to remember three of the rules above all others. Based on the serial position effect, where in the list should she place those three rules?","options":["In positions 5, 6 and 7, so they are surrounded by other rules","Spread evenly through the middle of the list, in positions 4, 6 and 8","At the beginning and end of the list, such as positions 1, 2 and 12","In positions 7, 8 and 9, after students have had time to start paying attention"],"answer":2,"rationale":"Items at the beginning of a list benefit from the primacy effect and items at the end benefit from the recency effect, so those positions are recalled best. Items placed in the middle get neither advantage and are the most likely to be forgotten."},{"id":"U2-277","section":"2.6","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":1,"stem":"On her first day at a part-time job in a bookstore, Marisol is introduced to ten coworkers one after another. Immediately after the introductions, the manager asks her to name everyone, and she can remember only the last two people she met. Which concept best describes Marisol's memory for the names?","options":["Recency effect","Primacy effect","Proactive interference","Retrograde amnesia"],"answer":0,"rationale":"The recency effect is better immediate recall of the last items in a series, which are still in short-term memory. Proactive interference and the primacy effect would both predict better memory for the people she met first, not the last ones."},{"id":"U2-278","section":"2.7","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"While Thabo waits for his turn at a chess tournament, the organizer reads out the names of the four judges over the loudspeaker. Thabo is busy reviewing his opening moves on his phone and is not listening. An hour later a friend asks who the judges are, and Thabo cannot name any of them, although he can describe every move of the game he just played. Which explanation best accounts for Thabo's inability to name the judges?","options":["Encoding failure","Retroactive interference","Tip-of-the-tongue phenomenon","Anterograde amnesia"],"answer":0,"rationale":"Thabo was not paying attention when the names were read, so they were never encoded into long-term memory in the first place. He forms new memories normally, as his detailed memory of the game shows, so anterograde amnesia does not fit."},{"id":"U2-279","section":"2.4","skill":"Data Interpretation","difficulty":2,"stimulus":{"type":"bar","title":"Percent of Material Recalled by Practice Type and Test Delay","xLabel":"Time of test","yLabel":"Percent recalled","categories":["Immediately after studying","One week later"],"series":[{"name":"Massed practice","values":[78,28]},{"name":"Distributed practice","values":[74,55]}],"yMax":100},"stem":"Students learned the same material either in one long session (massed practice) or in several shorter sessions over a week (distributed practice). They were tested immediately after their final session and again one week later. Based on the data in the graph, what is the most appropriate conclusion?","options":["Massed practice produced better recall than distributed practice on both tests.","Distributed practice produced much better recall than massed practice on the immediate test.","Both groups forgot about the same amount of material over the week.","Distributed practice led to much less forgetting over the week than massed practice did."],"answer":3,"rationale":"The massed group dropped from 78 to 28 percent (a 50-point loss), while the distributed group dropped only from 74 to 55 percent (a 19-point loss), which is the spacing effect. On the immediate test the groups were similar, with massed practice slightly ahead."},{"id":"U2-280","section":"0.3","skill":"Research Methods and Design","difficulty":2,"stem":"A language-learning company tests its app with 90 adult users who have never studied Mandarin. Each user is randomly given one of two schedules for learning the same 60 Chinese characters: a single three-hour session, or 30 minutes a day for six days. One week after the final session, every user is asked to translate all 60 characters. What is the dependent variable in this study?","options":["The number of characters each user correctly translates one week later","Whether users receive the single-session or the daily schedule","The three hours of total study time each user receives","Users' prior experience with Mandarin"],"answer":0,"rationale":"The dependent variable is the outcome that is measured, which is the number of characters correctly translated. The schedule is the independent variable, and total study time and prior experience were held constant across groups."},{"id":"U2-281","section":"2.4","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":1,"stem":"To remember the main points of her speech for a class presentation, Fernanda imagines placing each point at a different landmark along her walk from home to school: the front gate, the bakery, the bus stop and the school entrance. The technique Fernanda is using is called","options":["chunking","distributed practice","the method of loci","iconic memory"],"answer":2,"rationale":"The method of loci is a mnemonic device that links items to a sequence of familiar locations. Chunking groups items into larger units but does not use imagined places."},{"id":"U2-282","section":"0.2","skill":"Research Methods and Design","difficulty":2,"stem":"Dr. Abubakar notices that older adults at a community center who say they use the method of loci tend to remember their shopping lists better than those who do not. He wants to know whether the method of loci actually causes better recall. Which approach would allow him to draw that conclusion?","options":["Measuring how strongly method-of-loci use and recall are correlated in a much larger sample","Observing community center members without interfering while they shop at a market","Randomly assigning adults to learn either the method of loci or a control task, then testing their recall","Interviewing one adult who uses the method of loci in depth about her memory habits"],"answer":2,"rationale":"Only an experiment, in which the researcher manipulates the independent variable and randomly assigns participants to conditions, can establish cause and effect. A larger correlational sample can show a stronger relationship but still cannot rule out other explanations."},{"id":"U2-283","section":"2.5","skill":"Data Interpretation","difficulty":2,"stimulus":{"type":"line","title":"Probability of Recall by Position in a 15-Item List","xLabel":"Position in list","yLabel":"Probability of recall","series":[{"name":"Recall","points":[[1,0.82],[2,0.7],[3,0.58],[4,0.45],[5,0.36],[6,0.31],[7,0.28],[8,0.27],[9,0.29],[10,0.32],[11,0.38],[12,0.48],[13,0.6],[14,0.72],[15,0.86]]}]},"stem":"Participants studied a 15-item list of grocery items and then tried to recall them immediately. The graph shows the probability of recall for each position in the list. The high recall for items 13 to 15 is best explained by which of the following?","options":["These items were still held in short-term memory when recall began.","These items received more rehearsal than any other items on the list.","These items were encoded semantically, while earlier items were not.","These items were retrieved using cues from the testing room."],"answer":0,"rationale":"The last items on a list are still in short-term memory at the moment of immediate recall, which produces the recency effect. Extra rehearsal explains the high recall of the first items (primacy), not the last ones."},{"id":"U2-284","section":"0.3","skill":"Research Methods and Design","difficulty":1,"stem":"Mr. Osei, a school psychologist, wants to know whether students who take a short practice quiz after each biology lesson remember more than students who write a summary of each lesson. He randomly assigns 60 Grade 11 students to one of the two conditions, and three weeks later all students take the same end-of-unit test. What is the independent variable?","options":["Students' scores on the end-of-unit test","Whether students took practice quizzes or wrote summaries","The three-week delay before the test","The biology content covered in the unit"],"answer":1,"rationale":"The independent variable is what the researcher manipulates, which is whether students took practice quizzes or wrote summaries. The test scores are the dependent variable, and the delay and content were the same for everyone."},{"id":"U2-285","section":"2.6","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"A week after her football team's final match, a classmate asks Chiamaka what color boots each of her teammates wore that day. She closes her eyes, brings up a picture of the team photo taken before kickoff, and scans across it from left to right, naming each color. This retrieval strategy relies most heavily on","options":["the phonological loop","procedural memory","mental imagery","mood-congruent memory"],"answer":2,"rationale":"Chiamaka retrieves the information by forming and scanning a mental picture, which is mental imagery. The phonological loop holds verbal and auditory information, not visual scenes."},{"id":"U2-286","section":"2.6","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Sofia's grandmother quickly tells her eight things to buy for dinner, in this order: rice, tomatoes, onions, yogurt, lemons, bread, eggs and mint. Sofia does not write anything down. Based on the serial position effect, which item is Sofia most likely to forget by the time she reaches the shop?","options":["Rice","Tomatoes","Mint","Lemons"],"answer":3,"rationale":"The serial position effect predicts that items in the middle of a list are recalled worst, and lemons is fifth of eight. Rice and tomatoes benefit from the primacy effect, and mint, the last item, benefits from the recency effect."},{"id":"U2-287","section":"0.5","skill":"Data Interpretation","difficulty":2,"stimulus":{"type":"table","caption":"Average number of participants (out of 40) who recalled each word","headers":["Words read","Average number recalling each word"],"rows":[["Words 1 to 4","31"],["Words 5 to 8","19"],["Words 9 to 12","12"],["Words 13 to 16","13"],["Words 17 to 20","29"]]},"stem":"To study whether a word's place in a sequence affects how well it is remembered, a researcher played a recording of 20 unrelated words to 40 participants, with one new word every three seconds. Participants then wrote down every word they could remember. The table shows the average number of participants who recalled each word in each part of the list. Which conclusion is best supported by the data?","options":["Words at the start and the end of the list were each recalled by more than twice as many participants as words 9 to 12.","Recall declined steadily from the first words to the last words.","The recency effect was stronger than the primacy effect in this study.","Words in the middle of the list were recalled by more than half of the participants."],"answer":0,"rationale":"Words 1 to 4 (31) and words 17 to 20 (29) were each recalled by more than twice as many participants as words 9 to 12 (12), the U-shaped pattern of the serial position effect. Recall for the first words was slightly higher than for the last, so recency was not stronger than primacy."},{"id":"U2-288","section":"0.3","skill":"Research Methods and Design","difficulty":1,"stem":"An education ministry wants to estimate what percentage of Grade 12 students in the country use flashcard apps to study. The research team has a complete list of all 48,000 Grade 12 students but only enough funding to survey 1,200 of them. Which procedure would best allow the team to generalize its results to all Grade 12 students?","options":["Surveying the first 1,200 students who respond to a social media post about the study","Using a computer to select 1,200 names at random from the complete list","Randomly assigning 1,200 students to use or not use a flashcard app for a month","Surveying every Grade 12 student at the ten largest schools in the capital"],"answer":1,"rationale":"Random sampling from the full population gives every student an equal chance of being chosen, so the sample is likely to be representative. Random assignment places participants into experimental conditions, which does not help describe the population."},{"id":"U2-289","section":"0.4","skill":"Research Methods and Design","difficulty":2,"stem":"A sports psychologist plans a study of memory for tactical plays with players aged 14 to 16 at a youth football academy. Her procedure is to hand each player a consent form in the locker room, have the player read and sign it, and then begin testing. An Institutional Review Board reviews her plan. Which change would the board most likely require?","options":["Removing the debriefing at the end of the study to save time","Getting permission from each player's parent or guardian as well as the player's own agreement","Using deception so that players cannot guess the hypothesis","Recruiting only players who have the highest school grades"],"answer":1,"rationale":"Minors cannot legally give informed consent on their own, so a parent or guardian must consent while the young person gives assent. Removing debriefing or adding unnecessary deception would make the study less ethical, not more."},{"id":"U2-290","section":"2.4","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"According to the serial position effect, why are words in the middle of a long list usually the ones most likely to be forgotten?","options":["They are processed only at the structural level.","They receive less rehearsal than the first words and are no longer in short-term memory like the last words.","They are stored only in sensory memory.","They are transferred to long-term memory too quickly to be retrieved."],"answer":1,"rationale":"Early words get extra rehearsal (primacy effect) and the last words are still in short-term memory at recall (recency effect), but middle words get neither advantage. Sensory memory lasts only seconds and is not the reason middle items are lost."},{"id":"U2-701","section":"2.7","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"For a vocabulary competition, Mateo memorizes a list of 40 rare English words until he can define every one of them perfectly. He does not review the list afterward. If his memory follows the pattern described by the forgetting curve, during which period will Mateo most likely lose the largest share of the definitions?","options":["Between the second and third week after learning","Evenly across the first month, losing the same amount each day","Within the first day or two after learning","Only after several months, once the memories begin to decay"],"answer":2,"rationale":"The forgetting curve shows that forgetting is fastest soon after learning and then levels off. Mateo will lose most of what he forgets within the first day or two, with much slower loss after that."},{"id":"U2-702","section":"2.7","skill":"Data Interpretation","difficulty":1,"stimulus":{"type":"line","title":"Percent of Studied Facts Retained Over 30 Days","xLabel":"Days since learning","yLabel":"Percent retained","series":[{"name":"Retention","points":[[0,100],[1,46],[2,39],[4,34],[7,30],[14,27],[30,24]]}]},"stem":"Volunteers at a science museum learned 50 facts about desert wildlife. Each volunteer was tested once, after one of several delays. The graph shows the average percent of facts retained at each delay. Which conclusion is best supported by the graph?","options":["Retention decreased by about the same amount each day across the 30 days","Most of the forgetting occurred within the first day, after which the rate of forgetting slowed","Forgetting did not begin until after the first week","Participants had forgotten nearly all of the facts by day 30"],"answer":1,"rationale":"Retention fell from 100 to 46 percent in the first day but only from 30 to 24 percent between day 7 and day 30. This rapid early drop that then levels off is the forgetting curve, and about a quarter of the facts were still retained at day 30."},{"id":"U2-703","section":"2.7","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":1,"stem":"Yasmin has ridden the same elevator in her apartment building twice a day for three years. When a visiting cousin asks whether the emergency alarm button is on the left or the right side of the panel, Yasmin realizes she has no idea, even after thinking hard about it. This is best explained by","options":["retroactive interference","repression","source amnesia","encoding failure"],"answer":3,"rationale":"Yasmin never paid attention to where the alarm button is, so the detail was never encoded into long-term memory. Retroactive interference would require newer learning to disrupt an older memory, which is not the case here."},{"id":"U2-704","section":"2.7","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"After six years of coaching one school basketball team, Lerato moves to a new school and learns the new team's names for each of its plays. During the first weeks of practice, she keeps calling out the names her old team used for the same plays, which confuses her new players. Lerato's errors are best explained by","options":["proactive interference","retroactive interference","the misinformation effect","encoding failure"],"answer":0,"rationale":"Proactive interference occurs when older learning disrupts the recall of newer information. Lerato's well-learned old play names are getting in the way of the new ones; retroactive interference would be the reverse, with the new names disrupting recall of the old ones."},{"id":"U2-705","section":"2.7","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Ines studied Spanish for three years in middle school. This year her family moved to Lisbon, and she has been learning Portuguese every day. When she tries to write a letter in Spanish to her old pen pal, she keeps using Portuguese words without noticing. Which concept best explains her difficulty?","options":["Proactive interference","Tip-of-the-tongue phenomenon","Retroactive interference","Imagination inflation"],"answer":2,"rationale":"Retroactive interference occurs when newly learned information disrupts the recall of older information. Her recent Portuguese learning is disrupting her older Spanish; proactive interference would mean the Spanish was interfering with her learning of Portuguese."},{"id":"U2-706","section":"2.7","skill":"Research Methods and Design","difficulty":3,"stimulus":{"type":"passage","text":"Dr. Abubakar randomly assigns 60 participants to two groups. Both groups first study List 1, a list of 20 made-up product names, until they can recall all of them. Group A then spends 20 minutes studying List 2, a different set of 20 made-up product names. Group B spends the same 20 minutes completing a simple coloring task. Both groups are then asked to recall List 1."},"stem":"If Group A recalls significantly fewer List 1 names than Group B, the results would best demonstrate which of the following?","options":["Proactive interference, because List 1 was learned before List 2","Retroactive interference, because learning List 2 disrupted the recall of List 1","Encoding failure, because Group A did not pay attention to List 1","Source amnesia, because Group A could not remember where they learned the names"],"answer":1,"rationale":"Both groups learned List 1 equally well, and the only difference was what they did afterward. Poorer recall of an earlier list after learning a similar new list is retroactive interference; proactive interference would be shown if the earlier list disrupted recall of the later one."},{"id":"U2-707","section":"2.7","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":1,"stem":"At a family dinner, Rashid tries to recall the name of the actor who starred in a film he loved as a teenager. He is sure the name begins with an M and has three syllables, and he can picture the actor's face, but the full name will not come to him. Rashid is experiencing","options":["encoding failure","proactive interference","the misinformation effect","the tip-of-the-tongue phenomenon"],"answer":3,"rationale":"In the tip-of-the-tongue phenomenon, a person knows a memory is stored and can recall partial cues such as the first letter, but cannot fully retrieve it. This is a retrieval problem rather than encoding failure, because the name clearly was stored."},{"id":"U2-708","section":"2.7","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"While shopping at a supermarket, Carmen is greeted warmly by a man. He looks familiar, but she cannot recall who he is. When he mentions that he taught her chemistry two years ago, she immediately remembers his name and several of his lessons. Carmen's initial failure to recall the man is best explained as","options":["inadequate retrieval, because the memory was stored but the supermarket lacked cues to bring it back","encoding failure, because she never stored any information about her chemistry teacher","retroactive interference, because newer memories had permanently erased her memory of the man","repression, because memories of chemistry class caused her anxiety"],"answer":0,"rationale":"Carmen recalled the teacher once she was given a cue, which shows the memory was encoded and stored all along. The problem was retrieval in a setting without familiar cues; with encoding failure, the cue would not have helped because the information was never stored."},{"id":"U2-709","section":"2.7","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Tomás was in a frightening boating accident when he was eight. Now 17, he can describe the days before and after the trip, but he says he cannot remember anything about the accident itself. A psychodynamic therapist would most likely explain his missing memory by suggesting that","options":["the accident happened too quickly for the memory to be encoded","the memory was pushed out of conscious awareness to protect him from distress","later memories of other boat trips have interfered with the memory of the accident","he has confused the memory with a story he heard from his parents"],"answer":1,"rationale":"Psychodynamic theorists propose repression, in which distressing memories are kept out of conscious awareness to defend the ego from anxiety. Many memory researchers question how often repression actually happens, but it is the explanation a psychodynamic therapist would offer."},{"id":"U2-710","section":"2.7","skill":"Concept Recall","difficulty":1,"stem":"According to psychodynamic theorists, repression is best described as","options":["the gradual fading of memories that are not rehearsed","the disruption of new learning by information learned earlier","the blocking of anxiety-provoking memories from conscious awareness to protect the ego","the confusion of where or when a piece of information was learned"],"answer":2,"rationale":"Repression is a psychodynamic idea in which the mind keeps distressing memories out of awareness to defend the ego. Confusing where information was learned is source amnesia, and earlier learning disrupting new learning is proactive interference."},{"id":"U2-711","section":"2.7","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Priya watched a football match in which two players bumped shoulders while chasing the ball. The next day, a news article described the moment as a 'violent shove.' A week later, when friends ask about the match, Priya insists she saw one player push the other hard with both hands. Priya's recollection best illustrates","options":["proactive interference","encoding failure","imagination inflation","the misinformation effect"],"answer":3,"rationale":"The misleading description in the article, received after the event, was incorporated into Priya's memory of what she saw. Imagination inflation involves repeatedly imagining an event, which is not described here."},{"id":"U2-712","section":"2.7","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Hamad tells his classmates a surprising fact about how long camels can go without drinking water. When they ask where he learned it, he says it was from a science documentary. In fact, he heard it from his older cousin during a desert camping trip, and he has never watched such a documentary. Hamad's error is an example of","options":["source amnesia","the tip-of-the-tongue phenomenon","retroactive interference","repression"],"answer":0,"rationale":"Source amnesia is remembering a piece of information but forgetting or misattributing where it came from. Hamad recalls the fact itself but credits the wrong source."},{"id":"U2-713","section":"2.7","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Dr. Ferreira asks participants to vividly picture, on three separate days, a childhood scene in which they won first prize at a school science fair. Participants who had earlier rated this event as unlikely to have happened to them later rate it as more likely to have really occurred. This change in their ratings best demonstrates","options":["the misinformation effect","imagination inflation","proactive interference","the forgetting curve"],"answer":1,"rationale":"Imagination inflation occurs when repeatedly imagining an event increases a person's confidence that it really happened. The misinformation effect involves misleading information supplied by someone else after an actual event, not self-generated imagery."},{"id":"U2-714","section":"2.7","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Gabriel has told the story of his family's first desert camping trip many times. His sister points out that his latest version includes a sandstorm and a flat tire, both of which actually happened on a different trip two years later. Gabriel is surprised, because those details feel like a genuine part of his memory. Which explanation best accounts for this?","options":["The original trip was never encoded, so Gabriel invented the entire story","Gabriel is repressing the memory of the first trip because it was distressing","Memories are reconstructed each time they are recalled and can absorb details from other experiences before being stored again","A tip-of-the-tongue state has prevented Gabriel from retrieving the real details"],"answer":2,"rationale":"Constructive memory means we rebuild memories when we recall them, and each retrieval allows the memory to be altered before it is consolidated again. Details from the later trip have blended into the earlier memory, even though the first trip itself was clearly encoded."},{"id":"U2-715","section":"2.7","skill":"Research Methods and Design","difficulty":3,"stimulus":{"type":"passage","text":"Dr. Okafor shows 90 participants a two-minute video of a person cooking in a kitchen. The kettle in the video is blue. Participants are then randomly assigned to read one of two written summaries of the video. The summaries are identical except that one mentions 'the blue kettle' and the other mentions 'the red kettle.' One day later, all participants are asked, 'What color was the kettle?'"},"stem":"Which result would provide the strongest evidence of the misinformation effect?","options":["Participants in both groups correctly report that the kettle was blue at the same rate","Participants who read the accurate summary report more details about the video overall","Participants in both groups are equally likely to report a red kettle","Participants who read the summary mentioning a red kettle are more likely than the other group to report a red kettle"],"answer":3,"rationale":"The misinformation effect is shown when misleading information received after an event changes what people report remembering. Comparing the misled group with the accurately informed comparison group shows that the false detail, not chance, produced the red kettle reports."},{"id":"U2-716","section":"2.7","skill":"Data Interpretation","difficulty":3,"stimulus":{"type":"table","caption":"Mean number of French words recalled (out of 25) after each 30-minute activity","headers":["Group","Activity after studying the French words","Mean French words recalled"],"rows":[["1","Rested quietly","19"],["2","Solved math problems","17"],["3","Studied Italian vocabulary words","10"]]},"stem":"Dr. Lim randomly assigned students to three groups. All groups studied the same 25 French vocabulary words in the same way, spent 30 minutes on a different activity, and were then tested on the French words. Which conclusion is best supported by the results in the table?","options":["Learning similar new material after studying caused the most forgetting of the original material, consistent with retroactive interference","Earlier learning of French interfered with later learning of Italian, consistent with proactive interference","Any mental activity after studying produces the same amount of forgetting","Group 3 recalled fewer words because they failed to encode the French words in the first place"],"answer":0,"rationale":"All groups studied the French words the same way, so the differences reflect what happened afterward. Group 3, who learned similar new words, recalled far fewer French words than the other groups, showing that new learning disrupts recall of older learning, especially when the material is similar."},{"id":"U2-717","section":"2.7","skill":"Data Interpretation","difficulty":2,"stimulus":{"type":"bar","title":"Mean Recall of the Bird-Name List","xLabel":"Group","yLabel":"Bird names recalled (out of 20)","categories":["Studied fish names, then bird names","Studied bird names only"],"series":[{"name":"Mean recall","values":[9,15]}],"yMax":20},"stem":"In a memory study, one group of participants studied a list of 20 fish names and then a list of 20 bird names. A second group studied only the bird names. Both groups were then tested on the bird names. The graph shows the results. The difference between the groups best illustrates","options":["retroactive interference","encoding failure","proactive interference","source amnesia"],"answer":2,"rationale":"The group that learned fish names first recalled fewer of the later bird names, so earlier learning disrupted recall of newer learning. That is proactive interference; retroactive interference would require the later list to disrupt recall of the earlier one."},{"id":"U2-718","section":"2.7","skill":"Concept Recall","difficulty":1,"stem":"Hermann Ebbinghaus's research on how well people retain newly learned lists of nonsense syllables is best summarized by which statement?","options":["Forgetting happens at a steady rate until nothing is left","Forgetting is rapid soon after learning and then levels off over time","Little forgetting occurs until several weeks after learning","Information that has been learned well is never forgotten"],"answer":1,"rationale":"Ebbinghaus's forgetting curve shows a steep drop in retention shortly after learning, followed by a much slower decline. Some information is still retained long after learning, so retention does not fall to zero."},{"id":"U2-719","section":"2.7","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":3,"stem":"During a match, Arjun's coach asks him what jersey number his new teammate wears. Arjun has played beside the teammate for a week but cannot recall it. The coach then shows him four possible numbers, and Arjun still cannot pick the right one; every option seems equally familiar. Which explanation is best supported by this evidence?","options":["Tip-of-the-tongue phenomenon, because the number is stored but temporarily blocked","Proactive interference, because the numbers of his former teammates are blocking the new one","Repression, because forgetting the number protects Arjun from anxiety","Encoding failure, because even recognition, an easier memory task, did not help"],"answer":3,"rationale":"Recognition provides strong retrieval cues, so if the number had been stored, choosing it from a few options would usually succeed. Failing both recall and recognition suggests it was never encoded, whereas a tip-of-the-tongue state usually ends once the correct answer is shown."},{"id":"U2-720","section":"2.7","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"A security officer at a shopping mall needs to interview a shopper who saw a bag being stolen from a cafe table. The officer wants the shopper's account to be as accurate as possible. Based on research on memory errors, which interviewing approach is best?","options":["Ask open-ended questions such as 'What did you see?' and avoid mentioning details the shopper has not reported","Show the shopper a photo of the most likely suspect and ask whether this was the person","Ask the shopper to imagine the theft several times so that more details come to mind","Ask questions such as 'How fast was the thief running when he knocked over the display?'"],"answer":0,"rationale":"Leading questions and suggested details can create the misinformation effect, and repeatedly imagining an event can produce imagination inflation. Open-ended questions let the witness report only what they actually remember."},{"id":"U2-801","section":"2.8","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":1,"stem":"Leila is 8 years old. On an early intelligence test, she answers questions at the level of a typical 10-year-old. Using the original ratio formula for the intelligence quotient, what is Leila's IQ?","options":["80","100","125","180"],"answer":2,"rationale":"The ratio IQ divides mental age by chronological age and multiplies by 100: 10 divided by 8 is 1.25, which gives an IQ of 125. A score of 80 comes from reversing the formula and dividing 8 by 10."},{"id":"U2-802","section":"2.8","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":3,"stem":"Dr. Ito tries to use the original ratio formula for IQ (mental age divided by chronological age, times 100) with adults. Under this formula, a 40-year-old whose test performance matches that of an average 20-year-old would receive an IQ of 50. What is the main reason this formula does not work well for adults?","options":["Mental age stops increasing in early adulthood while chronological age keeps rising, so scores would fall misleadingly with age","Adults cannot take tests under standardized conditions","The formula measures achievement rather than aptitude when used with adults","Adults' scores are less reliable because adults have already taken many tests"],"answer":0,"rationale":"Performance on mental age tasks levels off in early adulthood, so dividing by an ever-increasing chronological age would make typical adults look less intelligent as they get older. This is why modern tests compare a person's score with the scores of others the same age."},{"id":"U2-803","section":"2.8","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Dr. Hosseini gives 400 high school students tests of vocabulary, spatial puzzles, arithmetic reasoning and memory span. She finds that students who score high on any one of the tests tend to score high on the others as well. These findings would be used most directly to support the idea that","options":["intelligence consists of several fully independent abilities","a general intelligence factor (g) underlies performance on many different mental tasks","intelligence test scores rise across generations","aptitude tests predict future performance better than achievement tests"],"answer":1,"rationale":"Positive correlations among scores on very different tests suggest a single general ability, g, contributing to all of them. If intelligence were made of fully independent abilities, a score on one test would tell us little about scores on the others."},{"id":"U2-804","section":"2.8","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Lucas earns average scores on his school's reading and math tests. However, he composes music that wins regional competitions, and classmates often seek him out because he understands how others are feeling. A psychologist argues that a single IQ score would miss many of Lucas's strengths. This psychologist most likely supports the view that","options":["intelligence is mainly a single general ability called g","intelligence is fixed at birth and cannot change","IQ is best calculated by dividing mental age by chronological age","intelligence is made up of multiple, relatively separate abilities"],"answer":3,"rationale":"Multiple-abilities theories, such as Howard Gardner's theory of multiple intelligences, argue that intelligence includes distinct abilities such as musical and interpersonal skill. The view that intelligence is a single general ability, g, is the position these theorists challenge."},{"id":"U2-805","section":"2.8","skill":"Concept Recall","difficulty":1,"stem":"Which statement best reflects how psychologists currently view the definition and measurement of intelligence?","options":["There is still no full agreement on how to define or measure intelligence, and tests can be influenced by bias","Modern tests have shown that intelligence is a single general ability that can be measured without bias","Most researchers now reject g and treat intelligence only as a set of separate abilities","Intelligence is now defined as the knowledge a person shows on achievement tests"],"answer":0,"rationale":"Psychologists still disagree about how to define and measure intelligence, including whether it is one general ability (g) or several abilities, and tests can reflect bias. Claims that the question is settled, whether in favor of g alone, separate abilities alone or achievement scores, go beyond the evidence."},{"id":"U2-806","section":"2.8","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"A school psychologist uses an intelligence test to help decide which students should receive extra learning support. In one room, students take the test in silence with a strict 40-minute limit. In another room, the examiner allows 60 minutes and reads some questions aloud, while construction noise comes through the windows. Which principle has most clearly been violated?","options":["Predictive validity","Split-half reliability","Standardization","Construct validity"],"answer":2,"rationale":"A standardized test is given with the same procedures and conditions for everyone, so scores can be fairly compared. Different time limits, instructions and noise levels mean score differences may reflect testing conditions rather than ability."},{"id":"U2-807","section":"2.8","skill":"Research Methods and Design","difficulty":2,"stem":"Dr. Achebe has written a 60-item test of verbal reasoning and gives it once to 300 students. She then scores each student's answers to the odd-numbered items separately from the even-numbered items and finds that the two sets of scores are strongly positively correlated. Dr. Achebe has provided evidence of the test's","options":["test-retest reliability","split-half reliability","predictive validity","standardization"],"answer":1,"rationale":"Comparing scores on two halves of a single test given once checks split-half reliability, showing that the items measure consistently. Test-retest reliability would require giving the test twice on different occasions."},{"id":"U2-808","section":"2.8","skill":"Data Interpretation","difficulty":3,"stimulus":{"type":"table","caption":"Correlations for the New Reasoning Test (NRT)","headers":["Comparison","Correlation (r)"],"rows":[["NRT scores and NRT scores from the same students three weeks later","+0.89"],["Scores on odd-numbered items and scores on even-numbered items","+0.86"],["NRT scores and first-year university grades","+0.08"]]},"stem":"A university is deciding whether to use the New Reasoning Test (NRT) to select applicants who will earn high grades in their first year. A psychologist collects the data shown in the table. Which conclusion is best supported?","options":["The test has strong predictive validity but weak reliability","The test has weak reliability and weak predictive validity","The test is not standardized, because its correlations differ from one another","The test is reliable but has weak predictive validity for first-year grades"],"answer":3,"rationale":"The high test-retest and split-half correlations show the test gives consistent results, so it is reliable. The near-zero correlation with first-year grades shows it does not predict university success, so it lacks predictive validity for this purpose; a test can be reliable without being valid."},{"id":"U2-809","section":"2.8","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"A company advertises a 'problem-solving ability test' for job applicants. A psychologist reviews it and finds that almost every item asks applicants to define rare English vocabulary words. She argues that applicants' scores mostly reflect their reading background rather than their problem-solving ability. Her criticism is mainly about the test's","options":["construct validity","test-retest reliability","split-half reliability","standardization"],"answer":0,"rationale":"Construct validity is the extent to which a test actually measures the trait it claims to measure. A vocabulary test labeled as a problem-solving test may give consistent scores, but it is not measuring the intended construct."},{"id":"U2-810","section":"2.8","skill":"Research Methods and Design","difficulty":1,"stem":"An aviation academy gives all applicants a spatial aptitude test. Four years later, researchers find that applicants' test scores are strongly correlated with their performance ratings as trainee pilots. This finding is evidence of the test's","options":["split-half reliability","standardization","predictive validity","test-retest reliability"],"answer":2,"rationale":"Predictive validity is shown when test scores accurately forecast a later outcome the test is meant to predict. Here, aptitude scores predict pilot performance years later."},{"id":"U2-811","section":"2.8","skill":"Data Interpretation","difficulty":2,"stimulus":{"type":"bar","title":"Mean Puzzle Score by Instructions Given","xLabel":"Instructions before the puzzles","yLabel":"Mean score (out of 30)","categories":["Practice activity instructions","Natural talent instructions"],"series":[{"name":"Mean score","values":[21,15]}],"yMax":30},"stem":"Dr. Mbeki randomly assigns students from the same school to two conditions before they complete identical logic puzzles. One group is told the puzzles are a practice activity. The other group is told the puzzles measure natural talent and that students from their school usually score poorly. The graph shows the results. Which concept best explains the difference between the groups?","options":["Stereotype lift","Stereotype threat","The Flynn effect","Growth mindset"],"answer":1,"rationale":"Stereotype threat occurs when worry about confirming a negative stereotype about one's own group lowers performance. The students warned that their school usually scores poorly did worse on identical puzzles; stereotype lift is a boost from hearing that a different group is expected to do poorly."},{"id":"U2-812","section":"2.8","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":3,"stem":"Before a strategy-game test, members of a school's chess club are told by the organizer that 'students from the drama club usually score much lower than chess club members on this test.' The chess club members then score higher than an equally skilled group of chess players who were given no comparison. This boost in performance best illustrates","options":["stereotype lift","stereotype threat","construct validity","the Flynn effect"],"answer":0,"rationale":"Stereotype lift is a performance boost that occurs when people are aware that another group is negatively stereotyped and compare themselves favorably. Stereotype threat would instead lower the performance of the group targeted by the negative stereotype, which here is the drama club."},{"id":"U2-813","section":"2.8","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"A test developer in Abu Dhabi reviews an intelligence test that was written elsewhere. One reasoning item asks what a person should do when snow blocks the driveway, and many students who have never seen snow get it wrong even though they reason well on similar items. Which revision best fits the goal of making the test socio-culturally responsive?","options":["Keep the item, because the test has already been standardized on other students","Give students who miss the item extra time on the rest of the test","Keep the item but score it more leniently for students who have never seen snow","Replace the item with one that requires the same reasoning but uses situations familiar to all test takers"],"answer":3,"rationale":"Socio-culturally responsive tests aim to measure reasoning without depending on experiences that only some test takers have had. Rewriting the item with familiar content keeps what the item measures while removing a source of bias; scoring some students more leniently would break standardized scoring and leave the biased item in place."},{"id":"U2-814","section":"2.8","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Dr. Mensah finds an intelligence test that was standardized on a large sample of teenagers in 1970, when the average score was set at 100. She gives the same test to a large sample of teenagers today and scores them using the 1970 norms. Today's teenagers average about 112. This finding is best explained by","options":["stereotype threat","poor split-half reliability","the Flynn effect","the ratio IQ formula"],"answer":2,"rationale":"The Flynn effect is the rise in average intelligence test scores across generations in much of the world. It has been linked to societal changes such as better nutrition, health care, education and living standards."},{"id":"U2-815","section":"2.8","skill":"Concept Recall","difficulty":1,"stem":"Which factor is most often used to explain the Flynn effect, the rise in IQ scores across much of the world over the past century?","options":["Genetic changes in the population over a few generations","Improvements in nutrition, health care and living conditions","Increased stereotype threat among test takers","A shift from aptitude tests to achievement tests"],"answer":1,"rationale":"The rise happened too quickly to be caused by genetic change, so it is attributed to environmental factors such as better nutrition, health care, schooling and socioeconomic conditions."},{"id":"U2-816","section":"2.8","skill":"Data Interpretation","difficulty":2,"stimulus":{"type":"table","caption":"Scores on the same standardized intelligence test, 500 students per town","headers":["Town","Mean score","Lowest score","Highest score"],"rows":[["Town P","101","68","134"],["Town Q","97","70","131"]]},"stem":"A researcher gives the same standardized intelligence test to 500 students in each of two towns. Town P has well-funded schools, while Town Q has fewer school resources and more families living in poverty. Which conclusion is best supported by the results in the table?","options":["The variation in scores within each town is much larger than the difference between the two town averages","The difference between the towns proves that students in Town Q have less inborn ability","Most students in Town P scored higher than most students in Town Q","The test cannot be reliable, because scores in both towns covered a wide range"],"answer":0,"rationale":"Scores within each town span more than 60 points, while the two means differ by only 4 points, so scores vary more within groups than between them. Because the towns also differ in poverty and school resources, the small gap cannot be attributed to inborn ability."},{"id":"U2-817","section":"2.8","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":3,"stimulus":{"type":"passage","text":"A historian studies records from the early 1900s, when intelligence tests written in English were given to newly arrived immigrants at a US port of entry. Many of the test takers spoke little English and had little formal schooling. Officials and supporters of eugenics, a now-discredited movement that tried to control which people could have children or enter the country, used the low average scores to argue for restricting immigration."},"stem":"Which statement is the best scientific criticism of how these test scores were used?","options":["The tests measured aptitude rather than achievement, so they could not be used for any decision","The scores proved that intelligence is a single general ability","The scores likely reflected unfamiliarity with the English language and with schooling rather than intelligence, so the tests lacked validity for these test takers","The tests should have used the ratio IQ formula instead of comparing scores across groups"],"answer":2,"rationale":"A test is valid only if it measures what it claims to measure for the people taking it. Scores of people tested in an unfamiliar language mainly reflected language and schooling, so using them to restrict immigration was a misuse; test scores have also been used historically to limit access to jobs, military ranks and schools."},{"id":"U2-818","section":"2.8","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":1,"stem":"Before choosing her university courses, Salma takes a test designed to predict how well she would learn computer programming if she studied it in the future. She has never written any code before. This test is best described as","options":["an achievement test","a personality inventory","a check of split-half reliability","an aptitude test"],"answer":3,"rationale":"Aptitude tests are designed to predict a person's future performance or capacity to learn. An achievement test would measure what Salma has already learned, and she has not yet studied programming."},{"id":"U2-819","section":"2.8","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Mr. Santos, a school counselor, is explaining the difference between achievement tests and aptitude tests to a group of parents. Which of the following is the best example of an achievement test?","options":["A test given to new employees to predict how quickly they will learn a new accounting program","An end-of-unit exam measuring how much students learned about the cell cycle in biology class","A test used to forecast which applicants will succeed in a future nursing program","A spatial reasoning test used to predict success in a first-year architecture course"],"answer":1,"rationale":"Achievement tests measure what a person has already learned, such as the content of a biology unit. The other tests are designed to predict future learning or performance, which makes them aptitude tests."},{"id":"U2-820","section":"2.8","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"After scoring poorly on his first physics test, Kofi tells his teacher, 'I'm just not a physics person, so studying won't help.' Which response from the teacher is most likely to encourage a growth mindset and improve Kofi's later achievement?","options":["'Don't worry, some people are naturally talented at physics and others are not.'","'You're so smart that you'll do better next time without needing to study.'","'Let's move you to an easier class so you don't have to struggle.'","'Your abilities can grow with effort and better strategies. Let's look at which study methods to change for the next test.'"],"answer":3,"rationale":"A growth mindset is the belief that abilities can be developed through effort and learning, and it is linked to greater persistence and achievement. Telling Kofi that talent is natural or praising him for being smart reinforces a fixed mindset."},{"id":"U3-001","section":"3.1","skill":"Research Methods and Design","difficulty":2,"stem":"A research team follows the same 300 children from kindergarten until the end of high school to see how early friendships relate to later well-being. Which of the following is a major weakness of this kind of design?","options":["Families who drop out over the years may differ in important ways from those who stay, so the final sample may be biased.","Differences between age groups may reflect the different time periods in which each group grew up.","The design cannot show how individual children change as they get older.","All participants must be tested on a single day, so change over time cannot be observed."],"answer":0,"rationale":"This is a longitudinal study, and its main weakness is attrition: the people who leave may differ from those who remain. Differences caused by growing up in different eras (cohort effects) are a problem for cross-sectional designs, not longitudinal ones."},{"id":"U3-002","section":"3.1","skill":"Research Methods and Design","difficulty":2,"stem":"A university team wants to know whether teenagers who play competitive online games sleep fewer hours than teenagers who do not, across the ages of 12 to 18. Compared with a longitudinal approach, one advantage of using a cross-sectional design for this question is that it","options":["eliminates cohort effects between the different age groups","can be completed in a much shorter period of time","shows how each individual teenager's sleep changes with age","allows the researchers to conclude that gaming causes poor sleep"],"answer":1,"rationale":"A cross-sectional study compares different age groups at one point in time, so it is quick and relatively inexpensive. It is actually more vulnerable to cohort effects, and it cannot track individual change the way a longitudinal study can."},{"id":"U3-003","section":"0.2","skill":"Research Methods and Design","difficulty":2,"set":"u3a_hand","stimulus":{"type":"table","caption":"Dr. Okafor's data: hand length and reading speed of children aged 5 to 11","headers":["Child","Hand length (cm)","Reading speed (words per minute)"],"rows":[["1","11.0","20"],["2","11.8","34"],["3","12.5","41"],["4","13.1","55"],["5","13.9","62"],["6","14.6","78"],["7","15.2","85"],["8","16.0","97"]]},"stem":"Dr. Okafor measured the hand length and the reading speed of 8 children between the ages of 5 and 11 during a single school visit. She did not change anything about the children's lives or classes. Her data are shown in the table. Which research method did Dr. Okafor use?","options":["A longitudinal study","An experiment","A correlational study","A case study"],"answer":2,"rationale":"Dr. Okafor measured two existing variables (hand length and reading speed) without manipulating anything, which makes this a correlational study. It is not an experiment because there is no independent variable and no random assignment."},{"id":"U3-004","section":"0.2","skill":"Research Methods and Design","difficulty":3,"set":"u3a_hand","stimulus":{"type":"table","caption":"Dr. Okafor's data: hand length and reading speed of children aged 5 to 11","headers":["Child","Hand length (cm)","Reading speed (words per minute)"],"rows":[["1","11.0","20"],["2","11.8","34"],["3","12.5","41"],["4","13.1","55"],["5","13.9","62"],["6","14.6","78"],["7","15.2","85"],["8","16.0","97"]]},"stem":"Dr. Okafor measured the hand length and the reading speed of 8 children between the ages of 5 and 11 during a single school visit. She did not change anything about the children's lives or classes. Her data are shown in the table. A colleague points out that the children's age could be responsible for both their larger hands and their faster reading. Which concept does the colleague's point illustrate?","options":["The directionality problem","Confirmation bias","The placebo effect","The third variable problem"],"answer":3,"rationale":"The third variable problem occurs when an outside factor, here age, influences both measured variables and creates a correlation between them. The directionality problem would be a question of which variable causes the other, not an outside cause of both."},{"id":"U3-005","section":"0.5","skill":"Data Interpretation","difficulty":2,"set":"u3a_hand","stimulus":{"type":"table","caption":"Dr. Okafor's data: hand length and reading speed of children aged 5 to 11","headers":["Child","Hand length (cm)","Reading speed (words per minute)"],"rows":[["1","11.0","20"],["2","11.8","34"],["3","12.5","41"],["4","13.1","55"],["5","13.9","62"],["6","14.6","78"],["7","15.2","85"],["8","16.0","97"]]},"stem":"Dr. Okafor measured the hand length and the reading speed of 8 children between the ages of 5 and 11 during a single school visit. She did not change anything about the children's lives or classes. Her data are shown in the table. Which of the following best describes the relationship shown in the data?","options":["A positive correlation","A negative correlation","No correlation","A bimodal distribution"],"answer":0,"rationale":"As hand length increases, reading speed also increases, so the two variables move in the same direction, which is a positive correlation. A negative correlation would require one variable to fall as the other rises."},{"id":"U3-006","section":"3.1","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Two developmental psychologists are debating. One argues that a child's reasoning improves a tiny bit every day, like walking up a ramp. The other argues that reasoning changes in sudden, qualitatively different steps, like climbing a staircase. Their debate reflects which major issue in developmental psychology?","options":["Stability versus change","Nature versus nurture","Continuity versus discontinuity","Cross-sectional versus longitudinal design"],"answer":2,"rationale":"The continuity versus discontinuity issue asks whether development is gradual and smooth or occurs in distinct stages. Stability versus change instead asks whether traits stay the same across the lifespan."},{"id":"U3-007","section":"3.1","skill":"Research Methods and Design","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which of the following studies uses a cross-sectional design?","options":["Measuring the reading speed of a group of 8-year-olds, and then measuring the same children again at ages 12 and 16","Comparing the reading speed of 8-year-olds, 12-year-olds, and 16-year-olds who are all tested during the same week","Randomly assigning 8-year-olds to read on tablets or on paper and comparing their reading speed","Studying in great depth the reading habits of one 12-year-old with an unusual memory"],"answer":1,"rationale":"A cross-sectional study compares people of different ages at the same point in time. Retesting the same children at later ages is longitudinal, random assignment makes a study an experiment, and an in-depth study of one person is a case study."},{"id":"U3-008","section":"3.1","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Dr. Haddad claims that children all over the world move through the same series of separate stages, and that thinking in each stage is different in kind from thinking in the stage before it. Dr. Haddad's view is most consistent with which position?","options":["The continuity view of development","The view that development is shaped mainly by nurture","The view that personality is stable across the lifespan","The discontinuity (stage) view of development"],"answer":3,"rationale":"Proposing distinct, universal stages that differ in kind is the discontinuity view, the position taken by stage theorists such as Piaget. The continuity view sees development as gradual and cumulative."},{"id":"U3-009","section":"3.1","skill":"Research Methods and Design","difficulty":1,"stem":"Dr. Mensah wants to know whether reaction time changes with age. On the same afternoon, she tests one group of 20-year-olds, one group of 45-year-olds, and one group of 70-year-olds on an identical reaction-time task and compares the group averages. Which research design is she using?","options":["Cross-sectional","Longitudinal","Case study","Naturalistic observation"],"answer":0,"rationale":"Comparing different age groups at a single point in time is a cross-sectional design. A longitudinal design would follow the same people as they aged."},{"id":"U3-010","section":"0.4","skill":"Research Methods and Design","difficulty":2,"stem":"A psychologist wonders whether growing up in a home with no books slows children's language development. Which of the following best explains why she would avoid testing this question with an experiment?","options":["Experiments cannot measure language skills in a reliable way.","Experiments can never be conducted with child participants.","Randomly assigning children to be raised for years in homes without books could harm them, which would be unethical.","Experiments can include only one group, so there would be nothing to compare."],"answer":2,"rationale":"An experiment would require the researcher to control a child's home environment over a long period, which risks harm and is unethical. Children can take part in many experiments; the problem is this particular long-term manipulation."},{"id":"U3-011","section":"3.1","skill":"Concept Recall","difficulty":1,"stem":"A research design in which the same group of participants is assessed repeatedly over a period of many years is called a","options":["cross-sectional study","case study","naturalistic observation","longitudinal study"],"answer":3,"rationale":"A longitudinal study follows the same individuals over time. A cross-sectional study compares different age groups at one time."},{"id":"U3-012","section":"3.1","skill":"Research Methods and Design","difficulty":1,"stem":"Since 1995, a research team has surveyed the same 1,200 adults every three years about their friendships, exercise habits, and life satisfaction to learn what predicts healthy aging. Which research method are the researchers using?","options":["An experiment","A longitudinal study","A cross-sectional study","A case study"],"answer":1,"rationale":"The same participants are measured repeatedly over many years, which defines a longitudinal study. Nothing is manipulated, so it is not an experiment."},{"id":"U3-013","section":"3.2","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Fatima's 13-month-old son Omar is not yet walking on his own, but her neighbor's 10-month-old daughter is already taking steps. Fatima is worried. Which response would a developmental psychologist most likely give her?","options":["Infants reach motor milestones in roughly the same order, but the age at which each child reaches them varies within a normal range.","Omar should receive a sweet each time he stands so that operant conditioning speeds up his walking.","Omar most likely has a genetic mutation and should be tested immediately.","Walking is learned mainly by watching other children, so Omar needs to spend more time with older toddlers."],"answer":0,"rationale":"Physical development happens in generally the same order, but its timing varies from child to child, and walking anywhere in a broad range of ages is typical. Walking depends mainly on maturation, not on rewards or imitation."},{"id":"U3-014","section":"3.1","skill":"Research Methods and Design","difficulty":1,"stem":"A team measures the vocabulary of a group of 40 toddlers at 18 months, measures the same toddlers again at 30 months, and once more at 42 months. Which research method is being used?","options":["Cross-sectional","Experimental","Longitudinal","Case study"],"answer":2,"rationale":"Testing the same participants at several ages is a longitudinal design. A cross-sectional design would test different children of different ages at once."},{"id":"U3-015","section":"3.2","skill":"Data Interpretation","difficulty":2,"stimulus":{"type":"line","title":"Average hearing threshold by sound frequency and age group","xLabel":"Sound frequency (Hz)","yLabel":"Hearing threshold (decibels needed to detect the tone)","series":[{"name":"Ages 20 to 29","points":[[250,5],[500,5],[1000,5],[2000,6],[4000,8],[8000,10]]},{"name":"Ages 50 to 59","points":[[250,8],[500,9],[1000,10],[2000,15],[4000,25],[8000,35]]},{"name":"Ages 75 to 84","points":[[250,15],[500,18],[1000,22],[2000,30],[4000,45],[8000,60]]}]},"stem":"Audiologists measured hearing thresholds, the softest level in decibels at which a tone can be detected, for adults in three age groups. A higher threshold means a louder sound is needed before the person can hear it. Which conclusion is best supported by the graph?","options":["Adults aged 20 to 29 need louder sounds than adults aged 75 to 84 to detect an 8,000 Hz tone.","Hearing at 250 Hz is completely unaffected by age.","Adults aged 75 to 84 are unable to hear any 8,000 Hz tone, no matter how loud it is.","The difference between the age groups is largest at the highest frequencies."],"answer":3,"rationale":"At 250 Hz the groups differ by only 10 decibels, but at 8,000 Hz the oldest group needs 50 more decibels than the youngest, showing that age-related hearing loss mainly affects high-pitched sounds. The oldest group can still hear an 8,000 Hz tone if it reaches about 60 decibels."},{"id":"U3-016","section":"3.2","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":1,"stem":"A poster at a pediatric clinic explains that most infants first roll over between 4 and 6 months of age, with an average of about 5 months. The poster is describing","options":["developmental norms","a critical period","test reliability","imprinting"],"answer":0,"rationale":"Developmental norms describe the typical ages and ranges at which milestones are reached. A critical period is a window when certain experiences must occur for normal development."},{"id":"U3-017","section":"3.2","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"A pediatrician wants to check whether a newborn's rooting reflex is developing normally. Which of the following should she do?","options":["Place a finger in the baby's palm and watch whether the baby's fingers close around it","Gently stroke the baby's cheek near the mouth and watch whether the baby turns toward the touch","Make a sudden loud noise and watch whether the baby flings its arms outward","Hold the baby upright with its feet touching a table and watch for stepping movements"],"answer":1,"rationale":"The rooting reflex is triggered by touching the cheek or the corner of the mouth, and the baby turns toward the touch and opens its mouth, which helps with feeding. Closing the fingers on a palm touch is the grasping reflex, and flinging the arms after a loud noise is the startle (Moro) reflex."},{"id":"U3-018","section":"0.5","skill":"Data Interpretation","difficulty":1,"stem":"For 500 newborns, Dr. Lim records how many cigarettes each mother smoked per day during pregnancy and the baby's birth weight. To display the relationship between these two measures, Dr. Lim should use a","options":["histogram","pie chart","scatterplot","frequency polygon"],"answer":2,"rationale":"A scatterplot shows each participant as a point on two variables, which displays a correlation. Histograms and frequency polygons show how often scores occur on a single variable."},{"id":"U3-019","section":"3.2","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which of the following statements about learning a second language is best supported by research on critical or sensitive periods?","options":["Adults usually achieve more native-like pronunciation in a new language than children do.","The age at which a person first learns a second language has no relationship to how fluent they become.","Children have more difficulty than adults producing sounds that are not found in their first language.","People who begin learning a second language in early childhood are more likely to speak it without a noticeable accent."],"answer":3,"rationale":"Sensitive period research shows that the earlier a person is exposed to a second language, the more likely they are to master its sounds and grammar with native-like skill. Adults who start later usually keep a stronger accent."},{"id":"U3-020","section":"3.2","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"A school board in Abu Dhabi decides to begin teaching French in kindergarten rather than in grade 7, arguing that children who start younger are much more likely to become fully fluent. Which concept most directly supports the board's decision?","options":["Critical or sensitive periods for language","The zone of proximal development","Crystallized intelligence","Conservation"],"answer":0,"rationale":"Research on critical or sensitive periods suggests that language is acquired most easily, and most completely, when exposure begins early in childhood. The zone of proximal development concerns guided learning at any age, not a time window."},{"id":"U3-021","section":"3.2","skill":"Concept Recall","difficulty":1,"stem":"The visual cliff apparatus developed by Eleanor Gibson and Richard Walk was designed to test whether infants can perceive","options":["object permanence","depth","color","changes in pitch"],"answer":1,"rationale":"The visual cliff uses a glass surface over an apparent drop to see whether crawling infants avoid the deep side, which shows depth perception."},{"id":"U3-022","section":"3.2","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":1,"stem":"In a lab, 10-month-old Leila crawls happily across a glass tabletop until she reaches a section where a checkered floor appears to be far below the glass. Even when her father calls to her from the other side, she stops at the edge and begins to cry. Leila's behavior suggests that she has developed","options":["object permanence","sensory adaptation","depth perception","conservation"],"answer":2,"rationale":"Refusing to crawl over what looks like a drop shows that Leila perceives depth, which is the point of the visual cliff. Object permanence concerns knowing that hidden objects still exist."},{"id":"U3-023","section":"3.2","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Suppose a child grows up almost completely isolated from human speech until age 12. After rescue, intensive teaching helps the child learn many individual words, but the child never masters grammar or speaks fluently. A case like this is most often used to illustrate","options":["overgeneralization of grammatical rules","assimilation","theory of mind","a critical or sensitive period for language"],"answer":3,"rationale":"When language exposure is missed during early childhood, full language ability is very difficult to acquire later, which is evidence for a critical or sensitive period. Overgeneralization is a normal error children make while learning grammar, not a lasting deficit."},{"id":"U3-024","section":"3.2","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Ducklings that see a moving object within roughly the first day or two after hatching will follow it as if it were their parent, but ducklings first shown the object several days later rarely do. This pattern is best explained by","options":["a critical period","a reflex","habituation","a schema"],"answer":0,"rationale":"Imprinting happens reliably only during a narrow window early in life, which is the definition of a critical period. A reflex is an automatic response that does not depend on such a time window."},{"id":"U3-025","section":"0.4","skill":"Research Methods and Design","difficulty":2,"stem":"Some studies suggest that heavy air pollution may act as a teratogen. A research team wants to know whether breathing heavily polluted air during pregnancy delays children's language milestones, such as first words and two-word phrases. Which research plan would let the team study this question ethically?","options":["Randomly assign pregnant volunteers to spend their pregnancies in either high-pollution or low-pollution neighborhoods","Expose a randomly chosen half of pregnant volunteers to polluted air in a laboratory for one hour each day","Compare the language milestones of children whose mothers already lived in high-pollution or low-pollution neighborhoods during pregnancy","Study only children born in clean-air areas, so that pollution cannot affect the results"],"answer":2,"rationale":"Deliberately exposing pregnant participants to a suspected teratogen would risk harm, so researchers must compare people who were already exposed, which is a correlational approach. Studying only clean-air births would leave no differences in pollution to compare."},{"id":"U3-026","section":"3.2","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"At 3 months, Mei could hold her head steady while lying on her stomach. At 6 months she sat with support, and at 8 months she sat without any support. Her cousin reached each of these milestones about a month later than Mei did. Which statement best reflects the developmental perspective on these milestones?","options":["Each milestone appears only after a parent has modeled it many times.","Mei's cousin will probably have delayed motor skills throughout life.","The order of these motor milestones is generally the same for all infants, although the exact timing varies.","Most infants sit without support before they gain head control, so Mei's pattern is unusual."],"answer":2,"rationale":"Motor development follows a largely universal sequence driven by maturation, while the timing differs from infant to infant. A one-month difference is well within the normal range and does not predict later problems."},{"id":"U3-027","section":"3.2","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":1,"stem":"Across cultures with very different parenting practices, nearly all infants lift their heads before they sit, and sit before they stand. This shared sequence is best explained by","options":["imprinting","parenting style","observational learning","biological maturation"],"answer":3,"rationale":"Maturation is the biological growth process that produces orderly changes in behavior largely independent of experience, which is why the same sequence appears across cultures. Parenting style and observational learning would be expected to vary across cultures."},{"id":"U3-028","section":"3.2","skill":"Concept Recall","difficulty":1,"stem":"When a nurse brushes the side of a newborn's face, the baby turns its head toward the touch and opens its mouth as if searching for food. This response is called the","options":["rooting reflex","grasping reflex","startle (Moro) reflex","imprinting response"],"answer":0,"rationale":"The rooting reflex is the turn toward a touch on the cheek, which helps the infant find a source of food. The grasping reflex involves closing the hand when the palm is touched."},{"id":"U3-029","section":"3.2","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":1,"stem":"On a farm, a group of newly hatched chicks begins following the farmer's dog, which was the first large moving thing they saw after hatching, and they keep following it for weeks. This is an example of","options":["object permanence","imprinting","secure attachment","classical conditioning"],"answer":1,"rationale":"Imprinting is the tendency of some young animals to form a strong bond with the first moving object they encounter during a critical period, as Konrad Lorenz showed with geese. Secure attachment describes a quality of the human infant and caregiver bond, not this automatic early following."},{"id":"U3-030","section":"3.2","skill":"Concept Recall","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which of the following correctly matches a physical change with the stage of life in which it typically occurs?","options":["The appearance of the rooting reflex in adolescence","The adolescent growth spurt in early childhood","Slower reaction time and reduced hearing sensitivity in late adulthood","A large increase in flexibility in late adulthood"],"answer":2,"rationale":"Late adulthood is marked by gradual declines in reaction time, mobility, flexibility, and sensory acuity. The rooting reflex is present at birth and the growth spurt occurs in adolescence."},{"id":"U3-031","section":"0.4","skill":"Research Methods and Design","difficulty":2,"stem":"Researchers want to know whether high levels of stress during pregnancy are linked to lower birth weight. Which of the following best explains why a correlational design is the most appropriate choice for this question?","options":["A correlational design shows which of the two variables causes the other.","It would be unethical to randomly assign pregnant women to experience high stress.","A correlational design gives researchers more control over extraneous variables than an experiment does.","A correlational design does not require informed consent from participants."],"answer":1,"rationale":"Because deliberately stressing pregnant participants could harm them and their babies, researchers must measure stress that already exists rather than manipulate it. Correlational designs actually offer less control than experiments and cannot establish cause and effect."},{"id":"U3-032","section":"3.2","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":1,"stem":"Four-year-old Sanjay can run, jump, and climb the playground ladder with ease, but he still struggles to cut along a line with scissors and to fasten small buttons. Sanjay's difficulties are mainly with","options":["gross motor skills","depth perception","fine motor skills","the grasping reflex"],"answer":2,"rationale":"Fine motor skills involve small, precise movements of the hands and fingers, such as cutting and buttoning. Running and climbing are gross motor skills, which Sanjay already performs well."},{"id":"U3-033","section":"3.2","skill":"Concept Recall","difficulty":1,"stem":"According to developmental psychologists, which of the following is one of the main physical milestones of adolescence?","options":["The disappearance of the rooting reflex","A gradual decline in reaction time and sensory acuity","The first appearance of the pincer grasp","The adolescent growth spurt"],"answer":3,"rationale":"Adolescence is marked by puberty and the adolescent growth spurt, a period of rapid increase in height and weight. The rooting reflex fades in infancy, and declines in reaction time belong to later adulthood."},{"id":"U3-034","section":"3.2","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":1,"stem":"Over the past year, seventh grader Khalid has grown nine centimeters, outgrown two pairs of football boots, and feels a little clumsy on the field because his arms and legs suddenly seem longer. Which of the following best explains these changes?","options":["The adolescent growth spurt","Egocentrism","Animism","A critical period for motor learning"],"answer":0,"rationale":"A rapid increase in height and limb length in early adolescence is the adolescent growth spurt, which comes with puberty. Egocentrism and animism are features of preoperational thinking, not physical changes."},{"id":"U3-035","section":"3.2","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Two healthy 12-year-old classmates, Ines and Haruka, are very different in size: Ines has already begun puberty and her growth spurt, while Haruka has not. Which statement would a developmental psychologist most likely make about this difference?","options":["Haruka's development is abnormal, because puberty always begins at exactly the same age.","The sequence of physical changes in puberty is similar for most adolescents, but the age at which it begins varies with factors such as genetics and nutrition.","Ines began puberty early because she has entered Piaget's formal operational stage.","Puberty is controlled entirely by learning, so Haruka will begin once she observes her classmates."],"answer":1,"rationale":"As with earlier physical milestones, the order of changes in puberty is fairly consistent, while timing varies widely and is influenced by heredity and environment, including nutrition. Cognitive stages do not trigger puberty."},{"id":"U3-036","section":"3.2","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":1,"stem":"During her pregnancy, Ana regularly worked with a pesticide that was later found to cause heart abnormalities in developing babies. In developmental terms, the pesticide is best described as","options":["a genetic mutation","a neurotransmitter","a maturational factor","a teratogen"],"answer":3,"rationale":"A teratogen is any environmental agent, such as a chemical, drug, or virus, that can harm the developing baby before birth. A genetic mutation is a change in DNA, not an outside substance."},{"id":"U3-037","section":"3.2","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":3,"stem":"Researchers found that infants who had been crawling for several weeks were more likely to avoid the deep side of a visual cliff than infants of the same age who had only just begun to crawl. Which conclusion does this finding best support?","options":["Experience moving around the environment contributes to the development of depth perception.","Depth perception is fully present at birth and does not change with experience.","Infants avoid the deep side mainly because they have not yet developed object permanence.","The visual cliff measures attachment security rather than perception."],"answer":0,"rationale":"Because age was the same but crawling experience differed, the result suggests that self-produced movement helps depth perception develop. If depth perception were fully present at birth, crawling experience should not matter."},{"id":"U3-038","section":"3.2","skill":"Research Methods and Design","difficulty":1,"set":"u3a_cliff","stimulus":{"type":"bar","title":"Percentage of infants who crawled onto the deep side, by surface condition","xLabel":"Surface condition under the glass on the deep side","yLabel":"Infants who crawled onto the deep side (%)","categories":["Checkerboard pattern","Plain gray surface"],"series":[{"name":"Percentage of infants","values":[10,60]}],"yMax":100},"stem":"Dr. Rahman's team built a visual cliff: a sturdy glass platform with a shallow side and a side that appeared to drop about one meter. Sixty crawling infants were randomly assigned to one of two conditions. In one condition, a high-contrast checkerboard pattern was placed on the floor beneath the deep side. In the other, a plain gray surface with no pattern was placed there. Each infant's caregiver called to the infant from across the deep side, and the researchers recorded whether the infant crawled onto the deep side. This study is most directly related to which area of research?","options":["Attachment styles","Object permanence","Depth perception","Sensory adaptation"],"answer":2,"rationale":"The study uses a visual cliff to see whether visual cues change how infants respond to an apparent drop, which is research on depth perception. Although caregivers call to the infants, the variable being studied is the visual surface, not the caregiver relationship."},{"id":"U3-039","section":"0.3","skill":"Research Methods and Design","difficulty":2,"set":"u3a_cliff","stimulus":{"type":"bar","title":"Percentage of infants who crawled onto the deep side, by surface condition","xLabel":"Surface condition under the glass on the deep side","yLabel":"Infants who crawled onto the deep side (%)","categories":["Checkerboard pattern","Plain gray surface"],"series":[{"name":"Percentage of infants","values":[10,60]}],"yMax":100},"stem":"Dr. Rahman's team built a visual cliff: a sturdy glass platform with a shallow side and a side that appeared to drop about one meter. Sixty crawling infants were randomly assigned to one of two conditions. In one condition, a high-contrast checkerboard pattern was placed on the floor beneath the deep side. In the other, a plain gray surface with no pattern was placed there. Each infant's caregiver called to the infant from across the deep side, and the researchers recorded whether the infant crawled onto the deep side. Which of the following is the dependent variable in this study?","options":["The type of surface placed beneath the deep side","The caregiver calling to the infant from across the deep side","The age of the infants","Whether each infant crawled onto the deep side"],"answer":3,"rationale":"The dependent variable is what the researchers measure, here whether each infant crawled onto the deep side, summarized as a percentage. The type of surface is the independent variable, and the caregiver's call was kept the same in both conditions."},{"id":"U3-040","section":"0.2","skill":"Research Methods and Design","difficulty":2,"set":"u3a_cliff","stimulus":{"type":"bar","title":"Percentage of infants who crawled onto the deep side, by surface condition","xLabel":"Surface condition under the glass on the deep side","yLabel":"Infants who crawled onto the deep side (%)","categories":["Checkerboard pattern","Plain gray surface"],"series":[{"name":"Percentage of infants","values":[10,60]}],"yMax":100},"stem":"Dr. Rahman's team built a visual cliff: a sturdy glass platform with a shallow side and a side that appeared to drop about one meter. Sixty crawling infants were randomly assigned to one of two conditions. In one condition, a high-contrast checkerboard pattern was placed on the floor beneath the deep side. In the other, a plain gray surface with no pattern was placed there. Each infant's caregiver called to the infant from across the deep side, and the researchers recorded whether the infant crawled onto the deep side. Which of the following best explains why this study is an experiment?","options":["The researchers manipulated the surface beneath the deep side and randomly assigned infants to the two conditions.","The researchers recorded a behavior that could be easily counted.","The infants' caregivers were present in both conditions.","The researchers compared infants who differed in age at a single point in time."],"answer":0,"rationale":"A study is an experiment when the researcher manipulates an independent variable and randomly assigns participants to conditions, and both happened here. Measuring a countable behavior and keeping caregivers present are useful controls, but they do not by themselves make a study an experiment."},{"id":"U3-041","section":"0.4","skill":"Research Methods and Design","difficulty":2,"stem":"A university ethics board reviews a proposed visual cliff study in which crawling infants are placed on a glass platform beside an apparent drop while a caregiver calls them across. The board approves the study only if researchers end any trial as soon as an infant shows signs of fear or begins to cry. This condition mainly protects which ethical principle?","options":["Keeping participants' identities confidential","Protecting participants from harm, including emotional distress","Avoiding unnecessary deception of participants","Debriefing participants at the end of the study"],"answer":1,"rationale":"Stopping a trial when an infant becomes frightened limits emotional distress, which applies the principle of protecting participants from harm. Confidentiality, avoiding deception, and debriefing are real ethical requirements, but none of them addresses an infant's fear during the procedure."},{"id":"U3-042","section":"0.5","skill":"Data Interpretation","difficulty":2,"stem":"Dr. Kapoor studies adults between the ages of 30 and 80. She predicts that as age increases, scores on a balance-and-agility test will decrease, and that this pattern will be very consistent across participants. Which correlation coefficient would best match her prediction?","options":["+0.85","-0.10","+0.05","-0.85"],"answer":3,"rationale":"A strong negative correlation has a coefficient close to -1.00, showing that as one variable rises the other falls consistently. A coefficient of +0.85 is equally strong but in the wrong direction, and values near zero show almost no relationship."},{"id":"U3-043","section":"3.2","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Seventy-eight-year-old Mr. Farouk complains that his favorite lentil soup tastes bland lately, even though his daughter has followed the same family recipe for years. His doctor finds that he is healthy for his age. Which explanation is most consistent with research on physical development in late adulthood?","options":["An increase in the number of his taste buds, which makes it harder to tell flavors apart","A decline in crystallized intelligence, so he no longer remembers how the soup should taste","A gradual decline in his sense of smell, which supplies much of what people experience as flavor","Sensory adaptation caused by eating the same soup for many years"],"answer":2,"rationale":"Smell and taste sensitivity decline gradually in late adulthood, and because smell supplies much of a food's flavor, familiar dishes can seem bland. The number of taste buds decreases rather than increases with age, and sensory adaptation is a short-term response to an unchanging stimulus."},{"id":"U3-044","section":"3.6","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Nine-year-old Amira spends hours on her science fair model and glows with pride when her teacher praises it. Her classmate Rashid, whose projects have been criticized repeatedly, has begun to say that he is just not good at school. According to Erik Erikson, both children are dealing with which psychosocial stage?","options":["Trust versus mistrust","Industry versus inferiority","Identity versus role confusion","Integrity versus despair"],"answer":1,"rationale":"In the elementary school years, Erikson's stage of industry versus inferiority centers on developing a sense of competence through effort and accomplishment. Identity versus role confusion is the adolescent stage of figuring out who one is."},{"id":"U3-045","section":"3.1","skill":"Concept Recall","difficulty":1,"stem":"The long-standing question of how much human development is shaped by genes and how much is shaped by experience is known as the","options":["nature versus nurture issue","continuity versus discontinuity issue","stability versus change issue","cross-sectional versus longitudinal debate"],"answer":0,"rationale":"The nature versus nurture issue concerns the relative contributions of heredity and environment. Continuity versus discontinuity is about whether development is gradual or stage-like."},{"id":"U3-046","section":"0.5","skill":"Data Interpretation","difficulty":2,"stem":"A researcher examines whether the month in which children were born is related to their scores on a puzzle-solving task at age 10. Which of the following descriptions of a scatterplot would represent the weakest possible correlation between these variables?","options":["The points cluster tightly around a line that rises from left to right.","The points cluster tightly around a line that falls from left to right.","The points cluster loosely around a line that falls gently from left to right.","The points are spread evenly across the graph, and the line of best fit is flat."],"answer":3,"rationale":"When points are scattered with no pattern and the best-fit line is flat, the correlation coefficient is near zero, the weakest possible relationship. Tight clustering around any sloped line, rising or falling, indicates a strong correlation."},{"id":"U3-047","section":"0.2","skill":"Research Methods and Design","difficulty":2,"stem":"As part of a school campaign to reduce air pollution, Chiamaka wants to compare how often parents waiting in the school pick-up zone leave their car engines running in the morning versus in the afternoon. Which method would give her the most accurate measure of this behavior?","options":["A questionnaire sent home asking parents how often they leave their engines running","A case study of one family's driving habits","Naturalistic observation of the pick-up zone at both times of day","A laboratory experiment in which parents use a driving simulator"],"answer":2,"rationale":"Watching the behavior as it naturally happens avoids the problem of parents reporting what sounds acceptable rather than what they actually do. A case study of one family could not represent all parents, and a simulator would not show real behavior in the pick-up zone."},{"id":"U3-048","section":"0.5","skill":"Data Interpretation","difficulty":2,"stem":"On a quiz about Piaget's stages of cognitive development, most students in a class earned high scores, while only a few students earned low scores. The distribution of quiz scores is best described as","options":["normally distributed","positively skewed","negatively skewed","bimodal"],"answer":2,"rationale":"In a negatively skewed distribution, most scores are bunched at the high end and the tail stretches toward the few low scores. A positive skew would have most scores at the low end."},{"id":"U3-049","section":"1.1","skill":"Concept Recall","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which of the following statements is best supported by twin and adoption research on human development?","options":["Adopted children resemble their adoptive parents more than their biological parents on temperament.","Identical twins raised in different homes are often more alike on many traits than unrelated children raised in the same home.","Fraternal twins share more of their genes than identical twins do.","Environment has no measurable influence on traits once genes are taken into account."],"answer":1,"rationale":"The similarity of identical twins reared apart points to a genetic contribution to many traits, while differences between them show that environment also matters. Identical twins share essentially all their genes, while fraternal twins share about half."},{"id":"U3-050","section":"3.4","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":1,"stem":"Nine-month-old Yusuf watches his ball roll behind a curtain. He immediately crawls to the curtain and pulls it aside to find the ball. Developmental psychologists would say that Yusuf's behavior shows he has developed","options":["conservation","object permanence","reversibility","egocentrism"],"answer":1,"rationale":"Searching for a ball that has disappeared from view shows that Yusuf understands it still exists, which is object permanence. Conservation and reversibility develop years later."},{"id":"U3-051","section":"3.4","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Eleven-month-old Noor has recently acquired object permanence. Which of the following behaviors would Noor be most likely to show?","options":["She insists that a tall, narrow glass holds more juice than a short, wide one with the same amount.","She loses interest in her toy once her father drapes a cloth over it.","She explains that the moon follows the car because it likes her.","She lifts a blanket to find her rattle after watching her father hide it there."],"answer":3,"rationale":"With object permanence, Noor understands that the hidden rattle still exists and searches for it. Losing interest in a covered toy is what an infant without object permanence would do."},{"id":"U3-052","section":"3.4","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Twelve-month-old Rafael begins crying and calling for his grandmother as soon as she leaves the room, even though he can no longer see or hear her. Piaget would argue that Rafael's distress depends on his having developed","options":["object permanence","conservation","abstract reasoning","reversibility"],"answer":0,"rationale":"Rafael can only miss his grandmother if he understands that she continues to exist when she is out of sight, which is object permanence. Conservation, reversibility, and abstract reasoning all develop much later."},{"id":"U3-053","section":"3.4","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":1,"stem":"A teacher shows five-year-old Hana two identical balls of clay, and Hana agrees they contain the same amount. The teacher then rolls one ball into a long, thin snake while Hana watches, and Hana says the snake has more clay. According to Jean Piaget, Hana has not yet mastered","options":["object permanence","animism","conservation","egocentrism"],"answer":2,"rationale":"Conservation is the understanding that a quantity stays the same when only its shape or appearance changes. Preoperational children like Hana focus on the snake's length and conclude it has more."},{"id":"U3-054","section":"3.4","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":1,"stem":"An infant watches her mother place a set of shiny keys inside a closed box. The infant then reaches for the box and tries to open it to get the keys. According to Jean Piaget, the infant's action shows the development of","options":["conservation of number","hypothetical reasoning","metacognition","object permanence"],"answer":3,"rationale":"Trying to retrieve keys that are hidden inside a box shows an understanding that objects continue to exist when out of sight. Hypothetical reasoning and metacognition belong to much later stages of development."},{"id":"U3-055","section":"3.4","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"In a classic task, children sit at a table with a model of three mountains and are asked to describe what a doll sitting on the opposite side would see. According to Jean Piaget, a child who can correctly describe the doll's view, rather than her own, has most likely entered which stage?","options":["Sensorimotor","Preoperational","Concrete operational","Formal operational"],"answer":2,"rationale":"Piaget held that children overcome egocentrism and can take another's visual perspective by the concrete operational stage. Preoperational children typically describe their own view of the mountains."},{"id":"U3-056","section":"3.4","skill":"Concept Recall","difficulty":1,"stem":"In Piaget's theory, the first stage in which children can reason logically about concrete, tangible objects and events, such as sorting items by more than one feature at once, is the","options":["sensorimotor stage","preoperational stage","formal operational stage","concrete operational stage"],"answer":3,"rationale":"The concrete operational stage brings logical reasoning about concrete objects, including conservation and classification. Formal operational thought adds abstract and hypothetical reasoning later."},{"id":"U3-057","section":"3.4","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Healthy 74-year-old Amina joins her grandchildren for a family game night. Compared with how she would have performed at age 30, on which game is she most likely to show the greatest decline?","options":["A fast-paced new video game whose controls and rules she must work out during play","A vocabulary game in which players define unusual words","A quiz about the history of the city where she grew up","A game of telling the stories behind old family photographs"],"answer":0,"rationale":"Working out unfamiliar rules quickly depends on fluid intelligence and processing speed, which tend to decline in later adulthood. Defining words and recalling local history draw on crystallized intelligence, which stays relatively stable."},{"id":"U3-058","section":"3.4","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":1,"stem":"Two-year-old Kofi knows the word \"dog\" for his family's pet. At a farm, he points at a goat and excitedly shouts, \"Dog!\" According to Jean Piaget, Kofi is showing the cognitive process of","options":["accommodation","assimilation","reversibility","theory of mind"],"answer":1,"rationale":"Assimilation is fitting a new experience into an existing schema, so Kofi treats the goat as a dog. If he later created a new category for goats, that would be accommodation."},{"id":"U3-059","section":"3.4","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Sixty-eight-year-old Mr. Chen has driven a taxi in the same city for 40 years and still knows every street in the old neighborhoods. However, he now finds it much harder than before to work out quick routes through a newly built district with an unfamiliar road layout. A medical checkup shows no signs of disease. His difficulty most likely reflects","options":["a decline in crystallized intelligence","the end of a critical period for spatial learning","the onset of dementia","a decline in fluid intelligence"],"answer":3,"rationale":"Solving new problems quickly, such as planning routes through an unfamiliar layout, depends on fluid intelligence, which tends to decline with age. His intact knowledge of the old streets shows stable crystallized intelligence, and a healthy checkup makes dementia unlikely."},{"id":"U3-060","section":"2.8","skill":"Research Methods and Design","difficulty":1,"stem":"A researcher gives a new test of number sense to 600 second graders, gives the identical test to the same children two weeks later, and correlates the two sets of scores. She is evaluating the test's","options":["test-retest reliability","predictive validity","standardization","cohort effects"],"answer":0,"rationale":"Giving the same test twice and checking whether scores are consistent measures test-retest reliability. Validity concerns whether the test measures what it is supposed to measure, which this procedure does not show."},{"id":"U3-061","section":"3.4","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Four-year-old Aisha watches her brother put his chocolate bar in a drawer and leave the room. While he is gone, their mother moves the chocolate to a cupboard. When asked where her brother will look for his chocolate first, Aisha answers, \"In the drawer, because he didn't see Mama move it.\" Aisha's answer shows that she has","options":["egocentric thinking","conservation","formal operational thought","a theory of mind"],"answer":3,"rationale":"Theory of mind is the understanding that other people have their own beliefs and knowledge, which may differ from reality and from one's own. An egocentric child would say the brother will look in the cupboard, because that is where Aisha knows it is."},{"id":"U3-062","section":"3.4","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":1,"stem":"A child watches as two equal scoops of rice are poured out: one onto a small plate, where it forms a high pile, and one onto a large tray, where it spreads thin. The child insists that the small plate has more rice. This child has not yet acquired what Jean Piaget called","options":["conservation","object permanence","theory of mind","habituation"],"answer":0,"rationale":"The child is fooled by the height of the pile and does not understand that the amount stays the same when its arrangement changes, which shows a lack of conservation. Object permanence develops much earlier, in infancy."},{"id":"U3-063","section":"3.4","skill":"Research Methods and Design","difficulty":1,"stem":"During a lab session, a researcher shows seven-month-old Arjun a stuffed giraffe. While Arjun watches, she covers the giraffe completely with a cloth and records whether Arjun pulls the cloth away to find it. Which ability is the researcher assessing?","options":["Conservation","Object permanence","Animism","Theory of mind"],"answer":1,"rationale":"Hiding a toy while the infant watches and seeing whether he searches for it is the classic test of object permanence, the understanding that objects still exist when out of sight."},{"id":"U3-064","section":"3.4","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":3,"stem":"Studies using looking-time methods have found that infants only a few months old stare longer at \"impossible\" events, such as a toy car that seems to pass straight through a solid block, than at possible events. These findings most directly challenge which idea?","options":["Vygotsky's idea that children learn best within their zone of proximal development","Erikson's idea that infants must resolve the crisis of trust versus mistrust","Piaget's idea that infants come to understand how physical objects behave only gradually, through many months of sensorimotor activity","Ainsworth's idea that attachment quality can be measured in the Strange Situation"],"answer":2,"rationale":"Piaget believed that understanding of objects, including object permanence, emerged gradually through the sensorimotor stage. Looking-time research suggests young infants grasp basic physical rules earlier than Piaget proposed."},{"id":"U3-065","section":"3.4","skill":"Concept Recall","difficulty":1,"stem":"The ability to reason quickly and abstractly in order to solve problems one has never encountered before, which tends to decline in later adulthood, is known as","options":["crystallized intelligence","emotional intelligence","practical intelligence","fluid intelligence"],"answer":3,"rationale":"Fluid intelligence is the capacity for quick, abstract reasoning on new problems, and it tends to decline with age. Crystallized intelligence is accumulated knowledge and skills, which remain relatively stable."},{"id":"U3-066","section":"3.4","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"A researcher places two rows of seven coins in front of four-year-old Tomas, spaced identically, and Tomas agrees the rows have the same number of coins. While Tomas watches, the researcher spreads out the coins in the second row so it becomes much longer. Tomas now says the longer row has more coins. Tomas's answer reflects a lack of","options":["assimilation","conservation","animism","object permanence"],"answer":1,"rationale":"Conservation of number is understanding that the number of objects stays the same when they are rearranged. Tomas judges by the length of the row, a typical preoperational error."},{"id":"U3-067","section":"3.4","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":1,"stem":"Three-year-old Omar is talking with his aunt on the telephone. When she asks whether he enjoyed his birthday party, Omar silently nods his head, expecting her to understand his answer. Which concept does this example illustrate?","options":["Egocentrism","Animism","Assimilation","Conservation"],"answer":0,"rationale":"Egocentrism is a young child's difficulty taking another person's point of view, so Omar assumes his aunt can see his nod. Animism is giving lifelike qualities to objects."},{"id":"U3-068","section":"3.4","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":1,"stem":"Five-year-old Kai and his sister are each given identical skipping ropes. His sister lays hers out straight across the floor, while Kai's stays coiled in a loose circle. Kai complains that his sister got the longer rope. Kai's complaint shows that he lacks","options":["object permanence","animism","conservation","accommodation"],"answer":2,"rationale":"Kai does not understand that coiling or straightening a rope does not change its length, which is a failure of conservation typical of the preoperational stage."},{"id":"U3-069","section":"0.1","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"A psychologist with a cognitive perspective watches seven-year-olds building towers with blocks during free play. She would be most interested in","options":["which children take turns and share the blocks with others","which colors of blocks the children choose most often","how much the children's heart rates rise while they build","the strategies the children use to figure out how to keep their towers from falling"],"answer":3,"rationale":"The cognitive perspective focuses on mental processes such as thinking, reasoning, and problem solving. Sharing and turn-taking would interest a social psychologist, and heart rate would interest a biological psychologist."},{"id":"U3-070","section":"3.4","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which of the following best illustrates Piaget's concept of conservation?","options":["Eight-year-old Rania explains that a row of ten buttons still has ten buttons after her brother pushes them closer together.","Two-year-old Ji-woo calls every four-legged animal at the zoo a \"cat.\"","Four-year-old Lucia says that the moon follows her family's car because it wants to come home with her.","Fifteen-year-old Sami argues about how history would have changed if the printing press had never been invented."],"answer":0,"rationale":"Recognizing that the number of buttons stays the same despite a change in arrangement is conservation. Lucia's belief is animism, Ji-woo is fitting new animals into an existing schema, and Sami's hypothetical reasoning reflects formal operational thought."},{"id":"U3-071","section":"3.4","skill":"Data Interpretation","difficulty":2,"stimulus":{"type":"line","title":"Average fluid and crystallized intelligence scores by age","xLabel":"Age (years)","yLabel":"Average score (0 to 100)","series":[{"name":"Fluid intelligence","points":[[10,40],[20,70],[30,75],[40,68],[50,60],[60,52],[70,44],[80,36]]},{"name":"Crystallized intelligence","points":[[10,30],[20,55],[30,68],[40,74],[50,78],[60,80],[70,79],[80,76]]}]},"stem":"Researchers compared average fluid and crystallized intelligence scores of people at different ages, as shown in the graph. Which conclusion is best supported by the data?","options":["Fluid intelligence reaches its highest level after age 60.","Both types of intelligence decline at the same rate after age 40.","Crystallized intelligence reaches its highest level at age 20.","From age 40 onward, crystallized intelligence scores are higher than fluid intelligence scores."],"answer":3,"rationale":"Fluid scores peak around age 30 and then fall, while crystallized scores keep rising until about 60 and stay high, so crystallized scores exceed fluid scores at every age from 40 to 80. The two lines clearly decline at different rates after 40."},{"id":"U3-072","section":"3.4","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"A heritage museum recruits healthy retired volunteers aged 75 to 85. The coordinator wants to give them roles that draw on their crystallized intelligence, which tends to stay strong in late adulthood. Which role best fits this goal?","options":["Learning to run a newly installed ticketing system during the busiest hours","Giving guided talks about the traditional trades they practiced when they were young","Memorizing a new set of unfamiliar exhibit codes each morning","Quickly solving the timed logic puzzles in a new interactive exhibit"],"answer":1,"rationale":"Crystallized intelligence is knowledge built up over a lifetime, such as experience with traditional trades, and it remains relatively stable in old age. The other roles require fast learning of new, unfamiliar material, which relies on fluid intelligence."},{"id":"U3-073","section":"3.4","skill":"Concept Recall","difficulty":1,"stem":"Pretend play, rapidly growing use of symbols such as words and images, and an inability to conserve are hallmarks of which of Jean Piaget's stages of cognitive development?","options":["Sensorimotor","Preoperational","Concrete operational","Formal operational"],"answer":1,"rationale":"The preoperational stage, from roughly ages 2 to 7, is marked by symbolic thinking and pretend play, as well as errors such as failing to conserve, animism, and egocentrism."},{"id":"U3-074","section":"3.4","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":1,"stem":"Three-year-old Ravi plays hide-and-seek by standing in the middle of the living room with his hands over his eyes. He is certain that because he cannot see his cousin, his cousin cannot see him. Which concept does Ravi's behavior illustrate?","options":["Animism","Object permanence","Egocentrism","Conservation"],"answer":2,"rationale":"Ravi assumes that others experience the world exactly as he does, which is preoperational egocentrism. Object permanence is about understanding that hidden objects still exist."},{"id":"U3-075","section":"3.4","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"When her classmate Sofia drops her ice cream and starts to cry, four-year-old Mariam offers Sofia a cat sticker. Mariam explains that Sofia loves cats, even though Mariam herself prefers dinosaurs. Mariam's actions show evidence of","options":["theory of mind","egocentrism","object permanence","conservation"],"answer":0,"rationale":"Mariam understands that Sofia has her own feelings and preferences that differ from Mariam's, which is theory of mind. An egocentric child would offer what she herself likes, such as a dinosaur sticker."},{"id":"U3-076","section":"3.4","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":1,"stem":"A few months ago, when her grandmother's cat walked behind the sofa, baby Hessa acted as though it had simply vanished. Now, at 10 months, Hessa crawls to the end of the sofa to peek behind it. Which statement best describes the change in Hessa's thinking?","options":["She now understands that a quantity stays the same when its shape changes.","She now understands that other people have thoughts different from her own.","She now believes the cat has feelings just like hers.","She now understands that objects and animals continue to exist when they are out of sight."],"answer":3,"rationale":"Searching for the hidden cat shows that Hessa has achieved object permanence. Understanding that quantity stays the same despite changes in shape is conservation, which develops years later."},{"id":"U3-077","section":"2.8","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":1,"stem":"Bilal can name the capital city of almost every country and can explain the rules of a dozen different sports. These abilities are best described as reflecting his","options":["fluid intelligence","crystallized intelligence","emotional intelligence","formal operational thought"],"answer":1,"rationale":"Crystallized intelligence is the store of facts and knowledge a person accumulates through experience. Fluid intelligence is the ability to reason through new problems."},{"id":"U3-078","section":"3.4","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":3,"stem":"At age 58, Dr. Rossi finds that she is slower than her young lab assistants at mastering each new version of the lab's statistics software, yet her ability to explain the history of her field and define its technical terms keeps getting better. If Dr. Rossi stays healthy, which prediction about the next 15 years is most consistent with research on intelligence and aging?","options":["Both abilities will improve steadily, because she has reached Piaget's formal operational stage.","Her knowledge of technical terms will decline faster than her speed at learning new software.","Her speed at learning unfamiliar software will likely keep declining, while her knowledge of her field will likely remain strong.","Both abilities will decline at exactly the same rate, because intelligence is a single general ability."],"answer":2,"rationale":"Learning new systems quickly depends on fluid intelligence, which tends to decline across adulthood, while accumulated knowledge reflects crystallized intelligence, which stays stable or grows. The prediction that knowledge would fall faster than learning speed reverses this pattern."},{"id":"U3-079","section":"3.4","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":3,"stem":"At a community library, Dr. Osei recruited healthy volunteers aged 25 and healthy volunteers aged 70 and asked them to define a list of uncommon words, such as \"candid\" and \"meticulous.\" The average scores of the two age groups were almost identical. Which explanation best accounts for this result?","options":["Defining words draws on crystallized intelligence, which remains relatively stable through healthy aging.","Defining words draws on fluid intelligence, which typically improves in later adulthood.","Vocabulary can be learned only during a critical period in childhood, so neither group could have added new words as adults.","The older group must have been unusually gifted, because vocabulary normally declines sharply after age 60."],"answer":0,"rationale":"Vocabulary is accumulated knowledge, or crystallized intelligence, which does not typically decline in healthy aging. Fluid intelligence, the ability to reason through new problems, tends to decline rather than improve with age."},{"id":"U3-080","section":"3.4","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Four-year-old Kwame spends the morning pretending that a wooden block is a telephone and holding long conversations with it. Later, his father lays two pencils of equal length side by side, then slides one pencil slightly ahead of the other, and Kwame insists that the pencil sticking out is now longer. According to Jean Piaget, Kwame is in which stage?","options":["Formal operational","Concrete operational","Preoperational","Sensorimotor"],"answer":2,"rationale":"Pretend play with symbols and failing to conserve length are both typical of the preoperational stage, which runs from about ages 2 to 7. A concrete operational child would recognize that sliding a pencil does not change its length."},{"id":"U3-081","section":"3.4","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":1,"stem":"Five-month-old Hamdan is batting happily at a rattle when his cousin drops a scarf over it. Hamdan makes no attempt to pull the scarf away and turns to watch the ceiling fan instead. A few months later, he yanks scarves off hidden toys right away. At five months, Hamdan was in which of Jean Piaget's stages of cognitive development?","options":["Sensorimotor","Preoperational","Concrete operational","Formal operational"],"answer":0,"rationale":"At five months Hamdan did not yet have object permanence, which develops during the sensorimotor stage, from birth to about age 2. His later searching shows object permanence emerging while he is still in that same stage."},{"id":"U3-082","section":"3.4","skill":"Concept Recall","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which of the following statements about intelligence across adulthood is best supported by research?","options":["Fluid intelligence increases steadily across adulthood.","Both fluid and crystallized intelligence peak in early adolescence and then decline.","Fluid intelligence remains stable while crystallized intelligence declines sharply after age 30.","Crystallized intelligence tends to remain stable or increase through much of adulthood."],"answer":3,"rationale":"Crystallized intelligence, built from knowledge and experience, remains relatively stable or grows through adulthood. It is fluid intelligence, not crystallized, that tends to decline as people age."},{"id":"U3-083","section":"3.4","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Sixteen-year-old Amal enjoys debating how society would change if humans no longer needed sleep. In science class, she tests possible explanations for an experiment's results by changing one factor at a time and reasoning about what each outcome would mean. According to Jean Piaget, Amal's thinking is characteristic of which stage?","options":["Preoperational","Concrete operational","Formal operational","Sensorimotor"],"answer":2,"rationale":"Reasoning about hypothetical situations and testing ideas systematically are signs of formal operational thought. Concrete operational thinkers reason logically about concrete situations but struggle to think systematically about abstract possibilities."},{"id":"U3-084","section":"3.4","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"In Jean Piaget's theory of cognitive development, which of the following best illustrates accommodation?","options":["A toddler who knows the word \"cat\" calls a small dog a \"cat.\"","A toddler used to drinking from a bottle tips a cup the same way and spills, then changes her grip and sips more slowly.","A four-year-old insists that a tall, thin glass holds more juice than a short, wide glass with the same amount.","A six-month-old repeats \"ba-ba-ba\" while playing with her blocks."],"answer":1,"rationale":"Accommodation is changing an existing schema to fit a new experience, so adjusting her drinking method for a cup is accommodation. Calling a dog a cat is assimilation, because the toddler fits the dog into her existing schema without changing it."},{"id":"U3-085","section":"3.4","skill":"Concept Recall","difficulty":1,"stem":"Which of the following milestones typically appears latest in development?","options":["Reasoning systematically about hypothetical situations","Babbling","Object permanence","Pretend play using symbols"],"answer":0,"rationale":"Systematic hypothetical reasoning belongs to Piaget's formal operational stage, which begins around adolescence. Babbling and object permanence appear in infancy, and pretend play appears in the preschool years."},{"id":"U3-086","section":"3.4","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":1,"stem":"Seven-month-old Priya explores each new toy by shaking it, banging it on the floor, and putting it in her mouth. According to Jean Piaget, Priya is most likely in which stage of cognitive development?","options":["Concrete operational","Sensorimotor","Formal operational","Preoperational"],"answer":1,"rationale":"In the sensorimotor stage, infants learn about the world mainly through their senses and physical actions, such as mouthing and shaking objects."},{"id":"U3-087","section":"3.4","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":1,"stem":"Four-year-old Tariq warns his mother not to step on the flowers because \"it will hurt them and they will cry.\" Tariq's belief is an example of which characteristic of Piaget's preoperational stage?","options":["Conservation","Object permanence","Animism","Reversibility"],"answer":2,"rationale":"Animism is the belief that nonliving or nonhuman things have thoughts and feelings, a typical feature of preoperational thinking. Conservation and reversibility are abilities that preoperational children lack."},{"id":"U3-088","section":"3.4","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":1,"stem":"A swimming coach first holds a beginner's waist while she kicks, then supports only her hands, and finally lets go once she can float on her own. The coach's gradually reduced support is an example of","options":["accommodation","egocentrism","imprinting","scaffolding"],"answer":3,"rationale":"Scaffolding, from Vygotsky's sociocultural approach, is temporary support from a more skilled person that is reduced as the learner becomes more capable. Accommodation is Piaget's term for changing a schema, not a teaching technique."},{"id":"U3-089","section":"3.4","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which of the following scenarios best illustrates assimilation in Jean Piaget's theory?","options":["A child who knows how to open twist-off bottle caps tries to open a new jar of jam by twisting the lid in exactly the same way.","A child who has called every man \"Baba\" learns to use the word only for her own father.","A child who is praised each time he shares his toys begins sharing more often.","A child becomes afraid of the vacuum cleaner after it once roared loudly beside her."],"answer":0,"rationale":"Assimilation is handling a new experience with an existing schema without changing it, as when the child applies a bottle-opening schema to the jar. Narrowing the meaning of a word is accommodation, and the other two scenarios describe operant and classical conditioning."},{"id":"U3-090","section":"3.4","skill":"Research Methods and Design","difficulty":2,"stem":"A researcher wants to find out whether 6-month-old and 10-month-old infants differ in object permanence. Which procedure would best measure this ability?","options":["Gently stroking each infant's cheek and recording whether the infant turns toward the touch","Placing each infant on a visual cliff and recording whether the infant crawls onto the deep side","Showing each infant two equal rows of blocks, spreading one row out, and recording where the infant looks","Hiding a favorite toy behind a cushion while the infant watches and recording whether the infant searches behind the cushion"],"answer":3,"rationale":"Searching for a toy that was hidden while the infant watched shows an understanding that the toy still exists, which is the classic test of object permanence. Stroking the cheek tests the rooting reflex, the visual cliff tests depth perception, and the rows of blocks test conservation."},{"id":"U3-091","section":"3.4","skill":"Concept Recall","difficulty":1,"stem":"In Piaget's theory, the understanding that people and objects still exist even when they cannot be seen, heard, or touched is called","options":["conservation","assimilation","object permanence","a schema"],"answer":2,"rationale":"Object permanence is the awareness that things continue to exist when they are out of sight, and it develops during the sensorimotor stage."},{"id":"U3-092","section":"3.4","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Seven-year-old Lucas cannot solve a two-step math word problem on his own, but he can solve it when his older sister asks him a few guiding questions. According to Lev Vygotsky, this kind of problem falls within Lucas's","options":["concrete operational stage","zone of proximal development","theory of mind","crystallized intelligence"],"answer":1,"rationale":"The zone of proximal development is the range between what a learner can do alone and what the learner can do with help from someone more skilled. Vygotsky argued that learning happens best within this zone."},{"id":"U3-093","section":"3.4","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"A school counselor visits a nursery and several classrooms to observe children of different ages. Which observation is most clearly associated with Jean Piaget's concrete operational stage?","options":["Two-year-old Jamal pretends that a banana is a telephone and talks into it.","Eight-month-old Nadia learns about a new set of keys by shaking them and putting them in her mouth.","Fifteen-year-old Elena designs a plan to test, one factor at a time, which of several variables affects how fast a pendulum swings.","Eight-year-old Hiba lines up sticks from shortest to longest and reasons that if one stick is longer than a second, and the second is longer than a third, the first is longer than the third."],"answer":3,"rationale":"Ordering objects and reasoning logically about concrete relationships, such as which stick is longest, are concrete operational skills. Elena's systematic testing of variables reflects formal operational thought, Jamal's pretend play is preoperational, and Nadia's shaking and mouthing is sensorimotor."},{"id":"U3-094","section":"2.8","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":1,"stem":"Which type of intelligence would contribute most to a contestant's success on a quiz show about world history?","options":["Crystallized","Fluid","Emotional","Creative"],"answer":0,"rationale":"Answering history questions requires recalling stored facts, which is crystallized intelligence. Fluid intelligence is used for reasoning through new problems rather than retrieving learned knowledge."},{"id":"U3-095","section":"2.8","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"At a robotics competition, each team member is given a different task. Which task depends most heavily on fluid intelligence?","options":["Listing the safety rules the team memorized during training","Explaining the meaning of technical terms used in the rulebook","Working out on the spot how to build a gripping arm from an unfamiliar set of parts, with no instructions","Recalling which schools won the competition in past years"],"answer":2,"rationale":"Fluid intelligence is the ability to reason through new, unfamiliar problems, such as building with parts one has never used before. The other tasks rely on knowledge already stored in memory, which is crystallized intelligence."},{"id":"U3-096","section":"3.4","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Ms. Al Suwaidi designs her lessons so that each student works on tasks that are slightly too hard to complete alone but that the student can manage with guidance from a classmate or teacher. Her approach most closely reflects which of the following?","options":["Erikson's stage of industry versus inferiority","Piaget's concept of accommodation","The critical period hypothesis","Vygotsky's zone of proximal development"],"answer":3,"rationale":"Targeting tasks just beyond what a student can do independently, with support from a more skilled person, applies Vygotsky's zone of proximal development."},{"id":"U3-097","section":"3.4","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Researchers find that preschoolers learn to tie their shoes fastest when an adult first demonstrates, then guides the child's fingers, and then gradually steps back as the child improves. Which theorist's ideas best explain this finding?","options":["Jean Piaget","Lev Vygotsky","Erik Erikson","Konrad Lorenz"],"answer":1,"rationale":"Vygotsky emphasized that children learn through social interaction and support from more experienced people, which is reduced as skill grows. Piaget focused more on children independently constructing knowledge through stages."},{"id":"U3-098","section":"3.5","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Three-year-old Joaquin runs to his grandmother and says, \"I seed two big trucks, and they goed really fast!\" Which conclusion about language development does Joaquin's statement best support?","options":["Children learn grammar mainly because adults reinforce their correct sentences.","Joaquin is still in the telegraphic speech stage, so he leaves out small function words.","Children actively work out grammatical rules from the speech they hear, rather than only copying it.","Joaquin has passed the critical period for learning irregular verbs, so he will keep making this error."],"answer":2,"rationale":"Saying \"seed\" and \"goed\" is overgeneralization: Joaquin applies the regular past-tense rule to irregular verbs. Adults do not say these words, so he is not copying them, and his sentence includes small words such as \"and\" and \"they,\" so it is not telegraphic."},{"id":"U3-099","section":"3.2","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":3,"stem":"Researchers tested the grammar skills of adults who had moved to a new country at different ages and had all lived there for at least ten years. Which pattern of results would best fit research on sensitive periods for language?","options":["Adults who arrived after age 20 scored highest, because their cognitive abilities were more mature when they began learning.","Grammar scores were the same regardless of age of arrival, because all participants had lived in the country for at least ten years.","Adults who arrived as teenagers scored lower than everyone else, including those who arrived after age 30.","Those who arrived in early childhood scored close to native speakers, and scores tended to fall the later in life a person arrived."],"answer":3,"rationale":"Sensitive period research shows that the earlier a person is immersed in a language, the more likely they are to master its grammar with native-like skill, and ability tends to decline with later age of arrival. Years of exposure alone do not erase the advantage of an early start."},{"id":"U3-100","section":"3.5","skill":"Concept Recall","difficulty":1,"stem":"The smallest distinctive sound units of a language, such as the \"sh\" sound at the start of the word \"ship,\" are called","options":["phonemes","morphemes","syntax","semantics"],"answer":0,"rationale":"Phonemes are the basic sound units of language. Morphemes are the smallest units that carry meaning, such as \"ship\" or the plural \"-s.\""},{"id":"U3-101","section":"3.5","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":1,"stem":"In the word \"unhappiness,\" the parts \"un,\" \"happy,\" and \"ness\" each carry meaning, and none of them can be broken into smaller meaningful parts. Each of these parts is a","options":["phoneme","morpheme","syntax rule","telegraphic phrase"],"answer":1,"rationale":"A morpheme is the smallest unit of language that carries meaning, and it can be a whole word or part of a word. Phonemes are units of sound with no meaning of their own."},{"id":"U3-102","section":"3.5","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Infants around the world begin babbling at about the same age, and their early babbling includes many speech sounds from languages they have never heard. By about 10 months, however, infants raised in Arabic-speaking homes in Al Ain and infants raised in Spanish-speaking homes in Lima begin to babble differently. Which explanation best accounts for this change?","options":["The two groups of infants are passing through entirely different stages of language development.","Infants' babbling gradually narrows toward the sounds of the language spoken around them.","Babbling is controlled entirely by genes, so the difference must be inherited.","The infants have moved into the telegraphic speech stage, which differs across languages."],"answer":1,"rationale":"Babbling begins as a universal milestone, but experience with the home language gradually shapes it so that infants produce more of the sounds they hear. The stages of language development are similar across cultures, and telegraphic speech does not appear until about age 2."},{"id":"U3-103","section":"3.5","skill":"Concept Recall","difficulty":1,"stem":"Which of the following stages of language development occurs before a child produces any recognizable words?","options":["Telegraphic speech","The one-word stage","Overgeneralization","Babbling"],"answer":3,"rationale":"Babbling, which begins at around 4 to 6 months, involves repeating speech sounds such as \"ba-ba\" before real words appear. The one-word stage and telegraphic speech both involve actual words."},{"id":"U3-104","section":"3.5","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":1,"stem":"A childcare center that serves both infants and toddlers records four things the children said during one morning. Which utterance is the clearest example of overgeneralization?","options":["\"Look, I have two foots!\"","\"Mommy go car.\"","\"Ba-ba-ba-ba.\"","\"Doggie!\" (while pointing at the family dog)"],"answer":0,"rationale":"Saying \"foots\" applies the regular plural rule to an irregular noun, which is overgeneralization. \"Mommy go car\" is telegraphic speech, \"ba-ba-ba-ba\" is babbling, and \"Doggie!\" is a one-word utterance."},{"id":"U3-105","section":"3.5","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":1,"stem":"The sentence \"Ball the kicked quickly boy the\" sounds wrong to an English speaker mainly because it breaks the rules of","options":["semantics","phonemes","syntax","morphemes"],"answer":2,"rationale":"Syntax is the set of rules for ordering words into sentences. Each word is a real, meaningful English word, so the problem is the word order, not the sounds or the meanings of the words."},{"id":"U3-106","section":"0.3","skill":"Research Methods and Design","difficulty":2,"set":"u3a_rala","stimulus":{"type":"bar","title":"Percentage of infants who distinguished /ra/ from /la/, by age and home language","xLabel":"Age of infants","yLabel":"Infants who turned toward the speaker at the sound change (%)","categories":["6 to 8 months","8 to 10 months","10 to 12 months"],"series":[{"name":"Japanese-learning infants","values":[80,58,22]},{"name":"English-learning infants","values":[78,83,88]}],"yMax":100},"stem":"Researchers randomly selected infants from city birth records whose families spoke either Japanese or English at home. Japanese does not distinguish between the sounds /ra/ and /la/. Each infant heard /ra/ repeated through a speaker, which then switched to /la/. Infants who had learned to turn toward the speaker when they detected a change were counted as able to distinguish the sounds. The results are shown in the graph. Which of the following is the best operational definition of the dependent variable?","options":["Whether each infant's family spoke Japanese or English at home","The number of times /ra/ was repeated before the sound switched","Each infant's general ability to perceive speech sounds","The percentage of infants who turned toward the speaker when the sound switched from /ra/ to /la/"],"answer":3,"rationale":"An operational definition states exactly how a variable is measured, here the percentage of infants who turned toward the speaker when the sound changed. A general ability to perceive speech sounds names the concept without saying how it is measured, and home language is a variable being compared, not the outcome."},{"id":"U3-107","section":"0.3","skill":"Research Methods and Design","difficulty":2,"set":"u3a_rala","stimulus":{"type":"bar","title":"Percentage of infants who distinguished /ra/ from /la/, by age and home language","xLabel":"Age of infants","yLabel":"Infants who turned toward the speaker at the sound change (%)","categories":["6 to 8 months","8 to 10 months","10 to 12 months"],"series":[{"name":"Japanese-learning infants","values":[80,58,22]},{"name":"English-learning infants","values":[78,83,88]}],"yMax":100},"stem":"Researchers randomly selected infants from city birth records whose families spoke either Japanese or English at home. Japanese does not distinguish between the sounds /ra/ and /la/. Each infant heard /ra/ repeated through a speaker, which then switched to /la/. Infants who had learned to turn toward the speaker when they detected a change were counted as able to distinguish the sounds. The results are shown in the graph. Which of the following is made possible because the researchers used random selection?","options":["Generalizing the results to the wider population of infants from that city","Drawing cause and effect conclusions about home language","Eliminating the need for a comparison group","Preventing any infants from dropping out of the study"],"answer":0,"rationale":"Random selection makes the sample more representative, which allows findings to be generalized to the population it was drawn from. Cause and effect conclusions require random assignment to conditions, and home language cannot be randomly assigned."},{"id":"U3-108","section":"3.5","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":1,"set":"u3a_rala","stimulus":{"type":"bar","title":"Percentage of infants who distinguished /ra/ from /la/, by age and home language","xLabel":"Age of infants","yLabel":"Infants who turned toward the speaker at the sound change (%)","categories":["6 to 8 months","8 to 10 months","10 to 12 months"],"series":[{"name":"Japanese-learning infants","values":[80,58,22]},{"name":"English-learning infants","values":[78,83,88]}],"yMax":100},"stem":"Researchers randomly selected infants from city birth records whose families spoke either Japanese or English at home. Japanese does not distinguish between the sounds /ra/ and /la/. Each infant heard /ra/ repeated through a speaker, which then switched to /la/. Infants who had learned to turn toward the speaker when they detected a change were counted as able to distinguish the sounds. The results are shown in the graph. The 6- to 8-month-old infants in this study are most likely in which stage of language development?","options":["Telegraphic speech","Babbling","The one-word stage","Overgeneralization"],"answer":1,"rationale":"Infants around 6 to 8 months are typically babbling, producing repeated speech sounds without real words. The one-word stage usually begins around the first birthday, and telegraphic speech appears around age 2."},{"id":"U3-201","section":"3.5","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":1,"stem":"Layla adds the prefix 're' to the word 'write' to form 'rewrite,' which changes the meaning to 'write again.' In the study of language, the prefix 're' is best classified as a","options":["morpheme","phoneme","rule of syntax","telegraphic utterance"],"answer":0,"rationale":"A morpheme is the smallest unit of language that carries meaning, and 're' adds the meaning 'again.' A phoneme is only a unit of sound and carries no meaning by itself."},{"id":"U3-202","section":"3.5","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"The sentences 'The goalkeeper blocked the striker' and 'The striker blocked the goalkeeper' contain exactly the same words but describe different events. Which component of language accounts for this difference in meaning?","options":["Phonemes","Morphemes","Syntax","Babbling"],"answer":2,"rationale":"Syntax is the set of rules for arranging words into sentences, so changing word order changes who did what. The words and their sounds (phonemes) and meaningful parts (morphemes) are identical in both sentences."},{"id":"U3-203","section":"3.5","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Omar grew up speaking Arabic and began learning English at age 30. He has great difficulty hearing and producing the difference between the English words 'park' and 'bark,' because the 'p' sound does not exist in his first language. Omar's difficulty most directly involves","options":["morphemes","semantics","phonemes","rules for word order"],"answer":2,"rationale":"Phonemes are the smallest distinctive sound units of a language, and adults often struggle with sounds that were not part of the language they learned first. The words' meanings (semantics) are not the problem; telling the sounds apart is."},{"id":"U3-204","section":"3.6","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Kwame is 26 and has just settled into his first full-time job in a new city. He spends much of his energy trying to build deep, trusting friendships in which he can share his private hopes and worries, and he fears ending up lonely. According to Erikson's psychosocial theory, Kwame is working through which stage?","options":["Identity versus role confusion","Generativity versus stagnation","Autonomy versus shame and doubt","Intimacy versus isolation"],"answer":3,"rationale":"In early adulthood, Erikson's intimacy versus isolation stage centers on forming close, committed relationships, and failure brings loneliness. Identity versus role confusion comes earlier, in adolescence, and focuses on working out who one is."},{"id":"U3-205","section":"3.6","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":1,"stem":"At 79, Mr. Haddad spends his evenings looking through old photo albums with his grandchildren, telling stories about his life and asking himself whether the choices he made were worthwhile. Erikson would say he is in which final stage of psychosocial development?","options":["Integrity versus despair","Generativity versus stagnation","Intimacy versus isolation","Industry versus inferiority"],"answer":0,"rationale":"Erikson's eighth and last stage, integrity versus despair, involves reviewing one's life and either feeling satisfied (integrity) or regretful (despair). Generativity versus stagnation belongs to middle adulthood and focuses on contributing to the next generation."},{"id":"U3-206","section":"3.6","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"During Ainsworth's Strange Situation procedure, which behavior while the caregiver is present in the room would most strongly suggest that a toddler is securely attached?","options":["Ignoring the caregiver and treating the stranger exactly the same way","Exploring the toys freely while periodically checking back with the caregiver","Clinging to the caregiver and refusing to explore even before any separation","Moving toward the caregiver and then freezing or turning away in a confused manner"],"answer":1,"rationale":"Securely attached children use the caregiver as a secure base from which to explore. Clinging and refusing to explore fits anxious attachment, and confused approach-then-freeze behavior fits disorganized attachment."},{"id":"U3-207","section":"3.6","skill":"Concept Recall","difficulty":1,"stem":"A researcher records how a 14-month-old reacts as a parent leaves a playroom, a stranger enters, and the parent later returns. This procedure, developed by Mary Ainsworth, is designed to assess the child's","options":["temperament","object permanence","attachment style","conservation skills"],"answer":2,"rationale":"The Strange Situation measures attachment by observing reactions to separation from and reunion with a caregiver. Temperament is related to attachment, but the procedure was built to classify attachment styles."},{"id":"U3-208","section":"3.6","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Two-year-old Sofia is dropped off at a new nursery for the first time by her father. If Sofia is securely attached, research suggests she is most likely to","options":["show no reaction when her father leaves and not look up when he returns","cry for the whole morning and push her father away angrily when he returns","seem dazed, approach her father when he returns, and then suddenly back away","cry briefly when he leaves, settle and play, and greet him happily for comfort when he returns"],"answer":3,"rationale":"Secure attachment involves some distress at separation, recovery and exploration, and seeking comfort on reunion. Mixing clinging with anger fits anxious attachment, and showing no reaction fits avoidant attachment."},{"id":"U3-209","section":"3.6","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":1,"stem":"Ten-year-old Arjun keeps comparing his math scores and football skills with his classmates' and often worries that he is not as capable as they are. According to Erikson, Arjun is in which psychosocial stage?","options":["Industry versus inferiority","Initiative versus guilt","Identity versus role confusion","Trust versus mistrust"],"answer":0,"rationale":"Industry versus inferiority, during the elementary school years, centers on developing competence in schoolwork and other skills. Identity versus role confusion comes later, in adolescence."},{"id":"U3-210","section":"3.6","skill":"Data Interpretation","difficulty":2,"stimulus":{"type":"bar","title":"Peer Social Competence at Age 7 by Infant Attachment Group","xLabel":"Attachment group","yLabel":"Mean social competence score (1 to 10)","categories":["Group W","Group X","Group Y","Group Z"],"series":[{"name":"Mean score","values":[4.3,7.9,3.6,2.7]}],"yMax":10},"stem":"A researcher rated the social competence of 7-year-olds who had been classified by attachment style as infants. Higher scores mean the child cooperated, shared and resolved conflicts with peers more successfully. The attachment group labels were removed from the graph. Based on research on attachment, which attachment style does Group X most likely represent?","options":["Avoidant","Secure","Anxious","Disorganized"],"answer":1,"rationale":"Group X has by far the highest social competence score, and secure attachment in infancy predicts better social competence later in childhood. The insecure styles (avoidant, anxious and disorganized) are associated with weaker peer relationships."},{"id":"U3-211","section":"3.6","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":1,"stem":"Ms. Okafor explains the reasons behind her household rules, listens when her son disagrees, and expects him to finish his homework before gaming. She is also warm, affectionate and supportive. Which parenting style does Ms. Okafor show?","options":["Authoritarian","Permissive","Uninvolved","Authoritative"],"answer":3,"rationale":"Authoritative parents combine reasonable demands and clear rules with warmth and two-way communication. Authoritarian parents also set rules but expect obedience without discussion and show less warmth."},{"id":"U3-212","section":"3.6","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"A study of teenagers in the United States finds that those whose parents combine warmth with reasonable, clearly explained rules score highest on measures of self-reliance and confidence. This finding most directly supports the benefits of which parenting style in that cultural context?","options":["Authoritative","Authoritarian","Permissive","Disorganized"],"answer":0,"rationale":"Warmth plus reasonable, explained rules defines authoritative parenting, which in many Western samples is linked to self-reliant, confident children. Research also shows cultural differences in how parenting styles relate to outcomes. Disorganized is an attachment style, not a parenting style."},{"id":"U3-213","section":"3.6","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":1,"stem":"Nurses notice that newborn Mei feeds and sleeps on a regular schedule, adapts quickly to new situations and is usually cheerful. A psychologist describing Mei as an 'easy' baby is referring to her","options":["attachment style","temperament","object permanence","maturation level"],"answer":1,"rationale":"Temperament is a person's characteristic emotional reactivity and intensity, visible from birth; 'easy,' 'difficult' and 'slow-to-warm-up' are temperament categories. Attachment style describes the bond with a caregiver and is measured later in infancy."},{"id":"U3-214","section":"3.6","skill":"Concept Recall","difficulty":1,"stem":"Compared with children of authoritarian parents, children raised by authoritative parents are more likely to be","options":["anxious and withdrawn around peers","impulsive and poor at self-control","self-reliant and socially competent","obedient but low in self-esteem"],"answer":2,"rationale":"Authoritative parenting is associated with independence, self-reliance and social competence. Obedience paired with lower self-esteem is more typical of children of authoritarian parents."},{"id":"U3-215","section":"3.6","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":1,"stem":"Hessa's mother draws up a strict weekly study timetable and a fixed bedtime for her without asking for her opinion. When Hessa asks why a rule exists, her mother replies 'because I said so' and expects her to comply at once. Her mother rarely offers praise or affection. Which parenting style does Hessa experience?","options":["Authoritative","Permissive","Uninvolved","Authoritarian"],"answer":3,"rationale":"Authoritarian parents impose rules, expect obedience and do not explain or discuss their reasons, and they tend to be low in warmth. Authoritative parents also hold high standards but explain their rules, listen to their children and are warm."},{"id":"U3-216","section":"3.6","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"A longitudinal study follows infants who were rated as securely attached at 12 months of age. By the time they reach preschool, these children are most likely to show","options":["more frequent tantrums when separated from caregivers","greater social competence with peers","higher rates of shyness in new groups","less curiosity in unfamiliar settings"],"answer":1,"rationale":"Secure attachment in infancy predicts later social competence, such as cooperating and getting along with peers. Tantrums at separation, shyness and reduced exploration are more often linked with insecure attachment."},{"id":"U3-217","section":"0.3","skill":"Research Methods and Design","difficulty":2,"stem":"Dr. Mensah studies how exosystem factors, such as a parent's work schedule, relate to children's homework habits. She requires her research team to state exactly how 'homework time' will be measured: the number of minutes a child records in a daily diary. What is the main reason for this requirement?","options":["To make sure participants do not know which group they are in","To guarantee that the sample represents the population","So other researchers can repeat the study using the same measures","So the dependent variable cannot influence the independent variable"],"answer":2,"rationale":"An operational definition states precisely how a variable is measured, which allows other researchers to replicate the study. It does not determine how participants are sampled or whether a study is blind."},{"id":"U3-218","section":"3.6","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which of the following questions is most typical of a person in Erikson's stage of industry versus inferiority?","options":["Am I as good at reading and sports as the other kids in my class?","Who am I, and what do I want to become?","Have I contributed something that will outlast me?","Looking back, has my life been worthwhile?"],"answer":0,"rationale":"Industry versus inferiority, in middle childhood, focuses on building competence and skills and comparing oneself with peers. 'Who am I?' belongs to the identity stage of adolescence, and the other two questions belong to the adult stages of generativity and integrity."},{"id":"U3-219","section":"3.6","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which of the following best illustrates the conflict Erikson called intimacy versus isolation?","options":["Lina, 15, tries out different clubs, styles and friend groups to figure out who she really is.","Mr. Castillo, 52, starts coaching young players so the club he loves will continue after he retires.","Aiko, 4, wants to plan her own games but feels bad when her ideas annoy her older cousins.","Tariq, 24, has moved to a new city for work and worries he will never form close, trusting friendships there."],"answer":3,"rationale":"Intimacy versus isolation is the young adult task of forming close, committed relationships; Tariq's worry about loneliness fits this stage. Lina fits identity versus role confusion, Mr. Castillo fits generativity, and Aiko fits initiative versus guilt."},{"id":"U3-220","section":"3.6","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":1,"stem":"Sixteen-year-old Farah keeps changing her mind about future careers, tries out different friend groups, and spends a lot of time thinking about her own values. According to Erikson, Farah is working through which conflict?","options":["Intimacy versus isolation","Identity versus role confusion","Industry versus inferiority","Autonomy versus shame and doubt"],"answer":1,"rationale":"Erikson proposed that the central task of adolescence is identity versus role confusion: forming a coherent sense of self. Intimacy versus isolation follows in early adulthood, once identity is more settled."},{"id":"U3-221","section":"3.6","skill":"Concept Recall","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which statement best captures a core assumption of Erikson's psychosocial theory?","options":["Development is complete once formal operational thinking appears in adolescence.","Personality is fixed by early childhood and changes little afterward.","Each stage of life centers on a distinct social conflict whose resolution affects later development.","People move through the stages by biological maturation alone, regardless of their social experiences."],"answer":2,"rationale":"Erikson proposed eight stages across the whole lifespan, each built around a psychosocial conflict that must be resolved to move on healthily. His theory emphasizes development continuing into old age, so it rejects the idea that personality is fixed in early childhood."},{"id":"U3-222","section":"3.6","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"In the Strange Situation, 18-month-old Nikolai stays glued to his mother's lap and barely explores the toys. When she leaves he becomes extremely upset, and when she returns he clings to her but stays upset and is very hard to soothe. Nikolai's behavior best fits which attachment style?","options":["Secure","Insecure-avoidant","Insecure-anxious","Disorganized"],"answer":2,"rationale":"Clinging, little exploration, intense distress at separation and difficulty being comforted on reunion describe anxious (insecure) attachment. An avoidant child would show little distress and ignore the caregiver on return."},{"id":"U3-223","section":"3.6","skill":"Concept Recall","difficulty":1,"stem":"In Harry Harlow's research, infant monkeys spent most of their time clinging to a soft cloth surrogate mother and visited a wire surrogate only when it was time to feed from its bottle. The main conclusion drawn from these results was that","options":["attachment forms mainly to whoever provides nourishment","monkeys imprint on the first moving object they see after birth","comfort from soft physical contact matters more than food in forming attachment","infants learn attachment behaviors by imitating adult monkeys"],"answer":2,"rationale":"Harlow's monkeys preferred the cloth surrogate even though the wire one provided food, showing that contact comfort is central to attachment. If attachment depended mainly on feeding, they would have preferred the wire surrogate."},{"id":"U3-224","section":"3.6","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":3,"stem":"Before Harry Harlow's surrogate mother studies, many psychologists believed that infants become attached to their mothers for a particular reason. Harlow's findings most directly challenged the idea that","options":["infants attach to caregivers chiefly because caregivers satisfy their hunger","attachment depends on a critical period for imprinting","infants cannot tell one caregiver from another","attachment is learned mainly by watching other infants"],"answer":0,"rationale":"The monkeys clung to the cloth surrogate that gave no milk rather than the wire surrogate that fed them, which contradicted the view that attachment grows out of being fed. The studies said nothing directly about imprinting periods or learning by observation."},{"id":"U3-225","section":"3.6","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"A hospital introduces a program in which parents hold their newborns skin-to-skin against their chests for long periods each day. The idea that this physical closeness supports bonding between parent and infant is most consistent with findings from","options":["Pavlov's research on conditioned salivation in dogs","Harlow's studies of contact comfort in infant monkeys","Piaget's observations of object permanence","Bandura's research on children imitating aggression"],"answer":1,"rationale":"Harlow showed that soft, comforting touch is a key basis of attachment, which supports programs built on physical closeness. Piaget's work on object permanence concerns cognitive development, not the emotional bond."},{"id":"U3-226","section":"3.6","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"In Harlow's research, a noisy mechanical toy was suddenly placed in the cage of an infant monkey raised with a soft cloth surrogate and a wire surrogate that held a feeding bottle. Which response did Harlow observe, and what did it show?","options":["The infant ran to the wire surrogate, showing that the source of food provides security.","The infant froze and avoided both surrogates, showing that artificial mothers cannot provide security.","The infant attacked the toy, showing that fear produces aggression in young primates.","The infant ran to the cloth surrogate and clung to it, showing that contact comfort mattered more than food."],"answer":3,"rationale":"Frightened infants fled to the cloth surrogate and, once calmed, sometimes went on to explore the scary object. This showed that the comforting surrogate, not the one that fed them, served as a secure base."},{"id":"U3-227","section":"3.6","skill":"Research Methods and Design","difficulty":3,"stem":"In a version of Harlow's study, some infant monkeys received milk only from a wire surrogate and others received milk only from a cloth surrogate. Which result would provide the strongest support for the contact comfort explanation of attachment?","options":["Monkeys always preferred whichever surrogate held the bottle.","Monkeys fed by the wire surrogate still spent most of their time on the cloth surrogate and fled to it when frightened.","Monkeys fed by the cloth surrogate ran to the wire surrogate when frightened.","Monkeys showed no preference for either surrogate when frightened."],"answer":1,"rationale":"If the cloth surrogate is preferred even when the wire one provides all the food, food cannot explain the attachment; contact comfort does. This is the pattern Harlow found. A preference that follows the bottle would instead support a feeding explanation."},{"id":"U3-228","section":"3.6","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":1,"stem":"At the park, three-year-old Amara digs in the sandpit while glancing back now and then at her grandmother on a nearby bench. When her grandmother walks away briefly to buy water, Amara cries a little but soon goes back to digging. When her grandmother returns, Amara runs to hug her and then returns to play. Amara's behavior best reflects which attachment style?","options":["Secure","Avoidant","Anxious","Disorganized"],"answer":0,"rationale":"Amara explores from a secure base, is mildly upset at separation, recovers, and seeks comfort on reunion, all signs of secure attachment. An avoidant child would show little interest in the grandmother's leaving or return."},{"id":"U3-229","section":"3.6","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Joaquin, now 13, is moving out of Erikson's industry versus inferiority stage and into identity versus role confusion. Which change is he most likely experiencing?","options":["His focus is shifting from mastering skills toward working out who he is and what he values.","He is becoming more concerned with whether he can trust the people who care for him.","He is starting to review whether his life as a whole has been meaningful.","He mainly wants to do basic tasks like dressing himself without help."],"answer":0,"rationale":"Industry versus inferiority centers on competence, while identity versus role confusion centers on defining the self. Trust, life review and basic independence belong to the trust, integrity and autonomy stages."},{"id":"U3-230","section":"3.6","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Ji-woo's development is shaped by her country's broad cultural values, such as a strong emphasis on academic achievement and respect for elders, and by national policies on education. In ecological systems theory, these influences belong to which system?","options":["Microsystem","Mesosystem","Exosystem","Macrosystem"],"answer":3,"rationale":"The macrosystem is the wider culture: its values, beliefs, customs and laws. The microsystem is the people in direct contact with the child, such as family and classmates, and the exosystem is indirect settings like a parent's workplace."},{"id":"U3-231","section":"3.4","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Eight-month-old Zainab used to show no reaction when her father left the room. Now she cries and searches for him whenever he leaves. Which cognitive development most likely made this new reaction possible?","options":["Object permanence","Conservation","Egocentrism","Theory of mind"],"answer":0,"rationale":"Separation anxiety requires object permanence, the understanding that people and objects continue to exist when out of sight. A baby who lacks it has no reason to search for or miss someone who is not visible."},{"id":"U3-232","section":"0.3","skill":"Research Methods and Design","difficulty":2,"stem":"Dr. Al Suwaidi wants to examine how taking part in team sports relates to life satisfaction among adolescents. She decides to measure 'life satisfaction' as the total score on a specific 10-item questionnaire. Why do researchers use operational definitions like this one?","options":["They decide which statistical test will be used to analyze the data.","They ensure that participants are randomly assigned to conditions.","They prevent the researcher from knowing which participants are in which group.","They state exactly how variables are measured so that other researchers can replicate the study."],"answer":3,"rationale":"An operational definition specifies the precise procedures used to measure a variable, which makes replication possible. Random assignment and blind procedures are separate features of research design."},{"id":"U3-233","section":"3.6","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which of the following statements by a parent best illustrates an authoritarian parenting style?","options":["'You will be home by 8:00 because I said so, and there is nothing to discuss.'","'Curfew is 9:00 on school nights because you need sleep. Tell me if you think that is unfair and we can talk.'","'Come home whenever you like. I trust you to decide for yourself.'","'I do not really mind when you get home. Do what you want.'"],"answer":0,"rationale":"Authoritarian parents demand obedience and do not explain or discuss their rules, which is what 'because I said so' signals. The second statement is authoritative (firm but explained and open to discussion), and the last two show permissive and uninvolved styles."},{"id":"U3-234","section":"3.6","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":1,"stem":"Jun's parents let him decide how many hours to spend on video games, rarely set rules about bedtime, and seldom enforce consequences, though they are warm and affectionate with him. Which parenting style do Jun's parents use?","options":["Authoritative","Authoritarian","Permissive","Uninvolved"],"answer":2,"rationale":"Permissive parents are warm but make few demands and set few limits. Uninvolved parents also set few limits, but they are low in warmth as well, which does not fit Jun's affectionate parents."},{"id":"U3-235","section":"3.6","skill":"Concept Recall","difficulty":1,"stem":"Parents who expect unquestioning obedience from their children and rarely explain the reasons behind their rules are described as","options":["permissive","authoritarian","authoritative","uninvolved"],"answer":1,"rationale":"Authoritarian parenting is high in demands and control but low in explanation and warmth. Authoritative parents are also demanding, but they explain their reasoning and encourage discussion."},{"id":"U3-236","section":"3.6","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"A large study of adolescents in a Western country measured each teen's social competence and self-esteem and classified the parenting style used in each home. Which pattern of results would be most consistent with past research in such samples?","options":["Teens with authoritarian parents scored highest on self-esteem.","Teens with permissive parents scored highest on self-control.","Parenting style was unrelated to either measure.","Teens with authoritative parents scored highest on both measures."],"answer":3,"rationale":"In many Western samples, authoritative parenting is associated with the highest self-esteem and social competence. Permissive parenting tends to be linked with weaker self-control, and research notes that these patterns can vary across cultures."},{"id":"U3-237","section":"0.2","skill":"Research Methods and Design","difficulty":1,"stem":"To estimate how many adolescents across the country use social media after midnight, a research team sends a short questionnaire to 5,000 randomly selected secondary school students. This research most likely uses which method?","options":["Survey","Case study","Controlled experiment","Twin study"],"answer":0,"rationale":"A survey uses questionnaires or interviews to gather self-reported data from many people, and random selection helps make the sample representative. A case study examines one person or group in depth, and nothing is manipulated here, so it is not an experiment."},{"id":"U3-238","section":"3.6","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":1,"stem":"Mr. Delgado, 55, spends his Saturdays teaching neighborhood teenagers how to repair bicycles, and he has started recording his family's recipes and stories so that his grandchildren will have them one day. He says that helping the next generation is what makes this time of his life feel worthwhile. Which stage of psychosocial development is Mr. Delgado most likely in?","options":["Integrity versus despair","Intimacy versus isolation","Generativity versus stagnation","Industry versus inferiority"],"answer":2,"rationale":"Generativity versus stagnation, in middle adulthood, involves contributing to the next generation through work, mentoring, family and community. Integrity versus despair comes later and centers on looking back and reviewing one's whole life."},{"id":"U3-239","section":"0.5","skill":"Data Interpretation","difficulty":2,"stimulus":{"type":"table","caption":"Parental Warmth Scores (1 to 20)","headers":["Parent","Score"],"rows":[["1","14"],["2","9"],["3","17"],["4","6"],["5","12"],["6","15"],["7","11"],["8","18"],["9","8"],["10","13"]]},"stem":"Ten parents completed a questionnaire measuring parental warmth. Scores can range from 1 (very low warmth) to 20 (very high warmth). The table shows each parent's score. What is the range of the scores?","options":["12.5","18","12","6"],"answer":2,"rationale":"The range is the highest score minus the lowest score: 18 minus 6 equals 12. A value of 12.5 is the median (the average of the two middle scores, 12 and 13), and 18 and 6 are just the highest and lowest scores."},{"id":"U3-240","section":"0.2","skill":"Research Methods and Design","difficulty":1,"stem":"To study how often toddlers share toys, a researcher sits quietly in the corner of a shopping mall's indoor play area over several weekends. She records every instance of sharing she sees and never interacts with the children or changes anything about the setting. Which research method is she using?","options":["Naturalistic observation","Laboratory observation","Case study","Survey"],"answer":0,"rationale":"Naturalistic observation means watching and recording behavior in its everyday setting without interfering, and a mall play area is a natural setting for toddlers. Laboratory observation would take place in a controlled research space, and a survey would ask people to report on behavior rather than watching it."},{"id":"U3-241","section":"3.6","skill":"Concept Recall","difficulty":1,"stem":"From birth, some infants are calm and easygoing while others are fussy, intense and highly reactive. These early, biologically influenced differences in emotional reactivity are called","options":["attachment","imprinting","temperament","habituation"],"answer":2,"rationale":"Temperament refers to a person's characteristic emotional reactivity and intensity, which appears very early and is partly inherited. Attachment is the emotional bond with a caregiver, which temperament can influence."},{"id":"U3-242","section":"3.6","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Twin infants raised in the same home differ from their first weeks of life: one is cautious and slow to warm up to new people, while the other eagerly approaches strangers and new toys. Which concept best accounts for this early difference?","options":["Object permanence","Separation anxiety","Authoritative parenting","Temperament"],"answer":3,"rationale":"Temperament is an infant's individual style of reacting to and engaging with the world, such as being slow-to-warm-up or outgoing. Because both twins share the same parents and home, a difference in parenting style is an unlikely explanation."},{"id":"U3-243","section":"0.5","skill":"Data Interpretation","difficulty":2,"stimulus":{"type":"bar","title":"Weekly Hours at Part-Time Jobs (Graduating Seniors)","xLabel":"Hours per week","yLabel":"Number of seniors","categories":["0 to 4","5 to 9","10 to 14","15 to 19","20 to 24","25 or more"],"series":[{"name":"Seniors","values":[48,31,17,9,5,2]}]},"stem":"Students in a psychology class asked graduating seniors how many hours per week they work at part-time jobs. The results, shown in the graph, form a positively skewed distribution. Which statistic can be identified precisely from the graph without doing any calculations?","options":["Mean","Median","Mode","Standard deviation"],"answer":2,"rationale":"The mode is the most frequent score, so it can be read directly as the category with the highest frequency (0 to 4 hours here). The mean, median and standard deviation all require counting or calculating from the full set of scores."},{"id":"U3-244","section":"3.7","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"At a water park, a staff member blows one long whistle a few seconds before a giant overhead bucket tips and dumps icy water on the children below, which makes them gasp and shiver. After several rounds, eight-year-old Tomas gasps as soon as he hears the long whistle, before any water falls. Which of the following correctly identifies the conditioned stimulus (CS) and the unconditioned stimulus (UCS)?","options":["CS: the icy water; UCS: the long whistle","CS: the long whistle; UCS: gasping","CS: the long whistle; UCS: the icy water","CS: gasping; UCS: the icy water"],"answer":2,"rationale":"The whistle was originally neutral but now triggers gasping because it repeatedly came just before the icy water, which causes gasping without any learning. Gasping is a response (unconditioned to the water, conditioned to the whistle), not a stimulus."},{"id":"U3-245","section":"3.7","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"On family drives up a long, winding mountain road, eight-year-old Lucia often gets carsick from the constant twisting motion. After several trips, she starts to feel queasy as soon as she watches her father load the suitcases into the car, before the family has even left home. For Lucia, the sight of the suitcases being loaded is now","options":["a conditioned stimulus","an unconditioned stimulus","a neutral stimulus","a conditioned response"],"answer":0,"rationale":"Loading the suitcases was neutral at first, but it reliably came before the nausea-causing motion of the drive, so it now triggers queasiness by itself as a conditioned stimulus. The twisting motion is the unconditioned stimulus, and the queasiness she now feels at home is the conditioned response."},{"id":"U3-246","section":"0.1","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"A psychologist argues that a student's habit of refusing to go to school persists because staying home has repeatedly brought relief from anxiety and extra attention from family members. This explanation reflects which perspective?","options":["Biological","Cognitive","Behavioral","Psychodynamic"],"answer":2,"rationale":"The behavioral perspective explains behavior, including maladaptive behavior, as the product of its consequences: here relief (negative reinforcement) and attention (positive reinforcement). A cognitive explanation would focus on the student's thoughts and beliefs instead."},{"id":"U3-247","section":"3.8","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Malik's teacher regularly praises him for raising his hand before speaking in class. Malik soon begins raising his hand before speaking at his robotics club and during family discussions at home, where no one has praised him for it. Malik's behavior best demonstrates","options":["discrimination","shaping","spontaneous recovery","generalization"],"answer":3,"rationale":"Generalization occurs when a behavior that was reinforced in one situation also appears in similar situations. Discrimination would mean raising his hand only in the class where it is reinforced."},{"id":"U3-248","section":"3.7","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Toddler Yusuf learned to get excited when he heard the jingle of his grandfather's car keys, because the jingle always came right before a trip to the park. Now he also gets excited whenever anyone jingles keys, and even when someone shakes a bag of coins. This is an example of","options":["stimulus generalization","stimulus discrimination","extinction","higher-order conditioning"],"answer":0,"rationale":"Responding with the conditioned response to stimuli similar to the original conditioned stimulus is stimulus generalization. Discrimination would mean he got excited only at his grandfather's keys."},{"id":"U3-249","section":"3.7","skill":"Research Methods and Design","difficulty":2,"stem":"A researcher repeatedly sounds a buzzer just before squirting a small amount of lemon juice into a volunteer's mouth, which makes the volunteer salivate. After many pairings, which observation would provide the clearest evidence that classical conditioning has occurred?","options":["The volunteer salivates whenever lemon juice is given, whether or not the buzzer sounds.","The volunteer salivates when the buzzer sounds and no lemon juice follows.","The volunteer salivates more to the lemon juice on later trials than on early trials.","The volunteer can accurately report that the buzzer always came before the lemon juice."],"answer":1,"rationale":"Conditioning is shown when the formerly neutral buzzer produces salivation by itself. Salivating to lemon juice is an unlearned reflex, and being able to describe the order of events shows awareness, not a conditioned response."},{"id":"U3-250","section":"3.7","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":3,"stem":"Lab rats learned to freeze whenever a light of medium brightness (level 3 on a 5-level scale) switched on, because the light was repeatedly followed by a mild foot shock. The researcher then presents lights at all five brightness levels in random order without any shock. If stimulus generalization has occurred, which result is most likely?","options":["The rats freeze equally often to all five lights.","The rats freeze most to the level 5 light because it is the brightest.","The rats freeze to the level 3 light only and not at all to the other lights.","The rats freeze most to the level 3 light and progressively less as the lights become less similar to it."],"answer":3,"rationale":"In stimulus generalization, stimuli similar to the conditioned stimulus also trigger the conditioned response, and the response gets weaker the less similar they are. Freezing to the level 3 light only would show discrimination, which usually requires additional training."},{"id":"U3-251","section":"3.7","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Phobias of cars and electrical outlets are rare, even though these objects cause many injuries in modern life. Phobias of deep water, heights and enclosed spaces are far more common. Which concept best explains this pattern?","options":["Stimulus generalization","Observational learning","Biological preparedness","Instinctive drift"],"answer":2,"rationale":"Biological preparedness means organisms are predisposed to learn some associations, such as fear of dangers our ancestors faced, more easily than others. Instinctive drift is related but refers to trained operant behaviors slipping back toward instinctive ones."},{"id":"U3-252","section":"3.8","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"A pigeon in a lab receives grain when it pecks a key that is lit green, but receives nothing when it pecks the same key lit yellow. After many sessions, the pigeon pecks only when the key is green. This is an example of","options":["stimulus discrimination","stimulus generalization","latent learning","habituation"],"answer":0,"rationale":"The pigeon has learned to respond to the stimulus that signals reinforcement (green) and not to a similar one that does not (yellow), which is discrimination. Generalization would mean pecking at both colors."},{"id":"U3-253","section":"3.7","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":1,"stem":"After being stuck in an elevator during a power cut, Sana felt panic whenever she entered an elevator. Her new job required her to ride one every day, and after weeks of uneventful rides her panic faded away. Behaviorists would say that her fear has been","options":["generalized","spontaneously recovered","repressed","extinguished"],"answer":3,"rationale":"Extinction occurs when the conditioned stimulus (the elevator) is repeatedly presented without the unconditioned stimulus (the frightening event), so the conditioned response weakens. Repression is a psychodynamic idea, not a behavioral one."},{"id":"U3-254","section":"3.7","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"On a school trip, Ines tried mint-flavored hot chocolate for the first time. A few hours later, she became sick from motion sickness on a long, bumpy bus ride. Weeks later, the smell of mint hot chocolate alone makes her feel queasy, even though she knows the drink did not cause her illness. In this example, the mint hot chocolate's flavor and smell function as","options":["an unconditioned stimulus","a conditioned stimulus","a conditioned response","a negative reinforcer"],"answer":1,"rationale":"The flavor was neutral until it was followed by the nausea of motion sickness (the unconditioned stimulus), so it now triggers queasiness as a conditioned stimulus. Taste aversions like this can form even with a delay of hours and even when the person knows the food was not to blame."},{"id":"U3-255","section":"0.1","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which of the following research questions would a behavioral psychologist be most likely to investigate?","options":["How do unconscious conflicts shape students' attitudes toward teachers?","Does awarding points for completed homework, which can be exchanged for privileges, increase homework completion?","How do students' beliefs about their own abilities affect how hard they try?","Which genes are associated with differences in academic performance?"],"answer":1,"rationale":"Behavioral psychologists study how observable behavior changes in response to consequences, and a points-for-privileges system is a token economy. Questions about beliefs are cognitive, unconscious conflicts are psychodynamic, and genes are biological."},{"id":"U3-256","section":"3.7","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Grace's puppy now whimpers and hides whenever the garden sprinklers spray against the living-room window. Grace remembers that during the stormy season, the patter of rain on that window was often followed moments later by crashing thunder, and the puppy soon began whimpering at the patter alone. Which concept best explains the puppy's reaction to the sprinklers?","options":["Stimulus discrimination","Spontaneous recovery","Stimulus generalization","Higher-order conditioning"],"answer":2,"rationale":"The spray of the sprinklers sounds like rain, the conditioned stimulus, so the conditioned fear response generalizes to it. Higher-order conditioning would require the sprinklers to be repeatedly paired with the rain sound, and discrimination would mean whimpering at the rain but not the sprinklers."},{"id":"U3-257","section":"3.7","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which of the following best illustrates a learned response produced by classical conditioning?","options":["A newborn turns her head and opens her mouth when her cheek is stroked.","A shop assistant greets more customers after her manager starts praising her for doing so.","Hamad's mouth waters when he hears the sizzle of his mother's frying pan, a sound that has often come just before dinner.","A student stops chatting during lessons after losing break time for it."],"answer":2,"rationale":"The sizzle was neutral until it was repeatedly paired with food, and now it triggers salivation, a conditioned response. Head-turning to a cheek stroke is an unlearned reflex, and the other two examples are behaviors changed by their consequences (operant conditioning)."},{"id":"U3-258","section":"3.7","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":1,"stem":"A dog has been conditioned to salivate when it hears a doorbell. Over the next two weeks, the doorbell rings many times but food never follows it, and the dog's salivation to the doorbell gradually weakens. This weakening is called","options":["acquisition","spontaneous recovery","stimulus discrimination","extinction"],"answer":3,"rationale":"Extinction is the weakening of a conditioned response when the conditioned stimulus is repeatedly presented without the unconditioned stimulus. Spontaneous recovery would be the response reappearing after a rest period."},{"id":"U3-259","section":"3.7","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":3,"stem":"The elevator in Sofia's apartment building makes a sharp grinding screech every time it stops at her floor, and the screech always makes her shoulders tense. Just before each screech, the elevator's display flashes her floor number, 7. Now Sofia's shoulders tense as soon as she sees the 7 light up. Which statement about this example is accurate?","options":["The flashing 7 is the unconditioned stimulus, because it comes first.","The screech is the unconditioned stimulus, and tensing at the screech is the unconditioned response.","Tensing at the flashing 7 is an unconditioned response, because it feels the same as her reaction to the screech.","The screech is the conditioned stimulus, because Sofia has learned to expect it."],"answer":1,"rationale":"The screech makes her tense without any learning, so it is the unconditioned stimulus and tensing at it is the unconditioned response. The flashing 7 became a conditioned stimulus through pairing, so tensing at it is a conditioned response even though it looks like the original reaction."},{"id":"U3-260","section":"0.1","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":1,"set":"u3b_logo_ad","stimulus":{"type":"passage","text":"Dr. Lim, a consumer psychologist, set up a laptop slideshow at a shopping mall and recruited 60 adult shoppers. Each shopper was randomly assigned to one of two conditions. In the paired condition, the logo of an unfamiliar granola bar brand appeared 20 times, each time followed immediately by a cheerful photo, such as a smiling baby or a sunny beach. In the unpaired condition, the same logo appeared 20 times, each time followed by a plain gray screen. Afterward, every shopper was offered a free snack from a tray of four brands, and Dr. Lim recorded whether each shopper picked the granola bar from the slideshow."},"stem":"Which psychological perspective is most closely linked to the research described in the passage?","options":["Humanistic","Behavioral","Evolutionary","Psychodynamic"],"answer":1,"rationale":"The study tests whether repeatedly pairing a logo with pleasant photos changes reactions to the product, which is classical conditioning, a central topic of the behavioral perspective."},{"id":"U3-261","section":"0.3","skill":"Research Methods and Design","difficulty":1,"set":"u3b_logo_ad","stimulus":{"type":"passage","text":"Dr. Lim, a consumer psychologist, set up a laptop slideshow at a shopping mall and recruited 60 adult shoppers. Each shopper was randomly assigned to one of two conditions. In the paired condition, the logo of an unfamiliar granola bar brand appeared 20 times, each time followed immediately by a cheerful photo, such as a smiling baby or a sunny beach. In the unpaired condition, the same logo appeared 20 times, each time followed by a plain gray screen. Afterward, every shopper was offered a free snack from a tray of four brands, and Dr. Lim recorded whether each shopper picked the granola bar from the slideshow."},"stem":"Which of the following best describes the experimental group in the study described in the passage?","options":["The shoppers who saw the logo followed by a plain gray screen","The shoppers who picked the granola bar from the tray","All shoppers at the mall, the population the sample was drawn from","The shoppers who saw the logo followed by cheerful photos"],"answer":3,"rationale":"The experimental group receives the treatment, which here is the logo paired with pleasant photos. The gray-screen group is the control group, and the shoppers who picked the bar are defined by the outcome, not by the condition they were assigned to."},{"id":"U3-262","section":"0.2","skill":"Research Methods and Design","difficulty":2,"set":"u3b_logo_ad","stimulus":{"type":"passage","text":"Dr. Lim, a consumer psychologist, set up a laptop slideshow at a shopping mall and recruited 60 adult shoppers. Each shopper was randomly assigned to one of two conditions. In the paired condition, the logo of an unfamiliar granola bar brand appeared 20 times, each time followed immediately by a cheerful photo, such as a smiling baby or a sunny beach. In the unpaired condition, the same logo appeared 20 times, each time followed by a plain gray screen. Afterward, every shopper was offered a free snack from a tray of four brands, and Dr. Lim recorded whether each shopper picked the granola bar from the slideshow."},"stem":"Which feature of the study described in the passage most clearly makes it an experiment?","options":["Dr. Lim recorded an actual behavior (snack choice) instead of asking for a rating.","Dr. Lim predicted that the pairing would make shoppers more likely to pick the granola bar.","Dr. Lim manipulated what followed the logo and randomly assigned shoppers to conditions.","All of the shoppers were recruited from the same mall."],"answer":2,"rationale":"An experiment requires manipulating an independent variable (what followed the logo) and randomly assigning participants to conditions. Measuring behavior or stating a prediction can happen in nonexperimental studies too."},{"id":"U3-263","section":"3.7","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":1,"stem":"Which of the following is an example of a conditioned response?","options":["Pupils shrinking in bright sunlight","A child's heart racing at the sight of a doctor's white coat after several painful injections","Turning off an alarm clock to stop its noise","Sneezing when dust enters the nose"],"answer":1,"rationale":"The white coat was neutral until it was paired with painful injections, so the racing heart it now triggers is learned. Shrinking pupils and sneezing are reflexes, and turning off an alarm is an operant behavior maintained by removing the noise."},{"id":"U3-264","section":"3.7","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"In a lab study, participants receive a mild shock paired with certain pictures. Fear responses develop faster, and resist extinction longer, for pictures of spiders and snakes than for pictures of flowers or mushrooms. This finding best illustrates","options":["observational learning","higher-order conditioning","habituation","biological preparedness"],"answer":3,"rationale":"Biological preparedness is the inborn tendency to learn some associations, such as fear of creatures that threatened our ancestors, more easily than others. Nothing in the study involves watching others or pairing two conditioned stimuli."},{"id":"U3-265","section":"3.7","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Ama's fear of needles makes it hard for her to get medical care. Her therapist teaches her to relax deeply and then pairs that relaxed state with a series of increasingly needle-related images and situations. Which learning principle is the therapist relying on?","options":["Operant conditioning","Observational learning","Classical conditioning","Latent learning"],"answer":2,"rationale":"This is counterconditioning, which uses classical conditioning to associate the feared stimulus with a new response (relaxation) that is incompatible with fear. Operant conditioning would instead change behavior through rewards or punishments."},{"id":"U3-266","section":"0.1","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"A humanistic psychologist and a behaviorist are debating why a student works hard at school. Which criticism would the humanistic psychologist most likely make of the behaviorist's explanation?","options":["It places too much weight on unconscious conflicts from early childhood.","It ignores free will and the drive for personal growth by reducing behavior to past reinforcement.","It overemphasizes the student's thoughts and expectations.","It relies too heavily on brain chemistry and genetics."],"answer":1,"rationale":"Humanistic psychologists stress free will and growth toward self-actualization, while behaviorists explain behavior through observable conditioning. Unconscious conflict is a psychodynamic idea, and behaviorism actually tends to ignore thoughts rather than overemphasize them."},{"id":"U3-267","section":"3.7","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":1,"stem":"When Mariam started working part-time at a café, the loud hiss of the espresso machine startled her every time it ran. After a few weeks of hearing it all day, she barely noticed the sound at all. Which of the following has occurred?","options":["Sensitization","Habituation","Extinction","Accommodation"],"answer":1,"rationale":"Habituation is a decreasing response to a stimulus that is repeated or constant. Extinction is different: it applies to a learned conditioned response, while the startle to a loud hiss was never conditioned."},{"id":"U3-268","section":"1.6","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"At a school talent show, a pianist and a guitarist take turns playing the same note, at the same loudness, from the same spot behind a closed curtain. Most of the audience can still tell which instrument is playing each time. Which property of sound makes this possible?","options":["Pitch, which depends mainly on the frequency of the sound wave","Loudness, which depends mainly on the amplitude of the sound wave","Timbre, which depends on the complex mix of frequencies in the sound","Sound localization, which depends on differences in when the sound reaches each ear"],"answer":2,"rationale":"Timbre is the distinctive quality of a sound, produced by its mix of overtones, that lets us tell instruments apart when pitch and loudness are the same. Localization cannot help here because both instruments play from the same spot."},{"id":"U3-269","section":"3.8","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Nour's little brother whines for sweets at the supermarket checkout, and their father usually gives in to stop the whining. Using operant conditioning principles, which strategy would most effectively reduce the whining over time?","options":["Give in occasionally so the brother does not become too upset.","Consistently refuse the sweets and give no attention to the whining.","Hand over sweets as soon as the whining starts so it ends quickly.","Buy sweets before entering the store so the brother is not tempted."],"answer":1,"rationale":"Removing the reinforcer (sweets and attention) every time leads to extinction of the whining. Giving in only occasionally creates partial reinforcement, which makes the whining even more persistent."},{"id":"U3-270","section":"3.7","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":1,"stem":"Which of the following is the best example of habituation in a pet parrot?","options":["The parrot learned to say 'hello' after being given seeds each time it did so.","The parrot stopped climbing the curtains after being sprayed with water each time.","The parrot began squawking when it heard the fridge open, because fruit usually followed.","The parrot squawked loudly the first few times a new wind chime rang outside, but after a week it no longer reacted."],"answer":3,"rationale":"Habituation is a reduced response to a repeated stimulus, like the wind chime. The other examples involve learning from consequences or learning that one stimulus predicts another."},{"id":"U3-271","section":"3.7","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":1,"stem":"Every evening, Mr. Tanaka's old printer beeps right before it makes a loud grinding noise that makes his cat jump. After many evenings, the cat jumps as soon as it hears the beep. In this scenario, the beep is the","options":["unconditioned stimulus","unconditioned response","conditioned response","conditioned stimulus"],"answer":3,"rationale":"The beep was originally neutral but now triggers jumping because it reliably came before the loud grinding, the unconditioned stimulus. The jump to the beep is the conditioned response."},{"id":"U3-272","section":"3.7","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"After several painful vaccinations at a clinic, six-year-old Thandiwe became afraid of the nurse who gave them. Which of the following would best illustrate stimulus generalization of her fear?","options":["She later becomes nervous around anyone in a medical uniform, including the pharmacist at the local pharmacy and the nurse at her school.","Her fear of the nurse fades after several visits with no injections.","She is afraid of that nurse but not of the other staff at the clinic.","Her fear returns suddenly after a year with no clinic visits."],"answer":0,"rationale":"Generalization is the conditioned response spreading to stimuli similar to the original conditioned stimulus. Fearing only that nurse is discrimination, fading fear is extinction, and the return after a break is spontaneous recovery."},{"id":"U3-273","section":"0.5","skill":"Data Interpretation","difficulty":2,"stimulus":{"type":"table","caption":"Mean Fear Ratings During an Elevator Ride (1 to 10)","headers":["Point in ride","Control group","Counterconditioning group"],"rows":[["Before entering","5","5"],["Doors closing","8","4"],["Rising","9","3"],["Stopping","8","3"],["Exiting","6","2"]]},"stem":"A therapist tests a relaxation-based counterconditioning program for people who are afraid of elevators. One group completes the program and a control group does not. The table shows each group's mean fear rating (1 to 10, higher numbers mean more fear) at five points during an elevator ride. Which statement is accurate based on the data in the table?","options":["The mode of the control group's ratings is 9.","The median of the control group's ratings is 9.","The range of the counterconditioning group's ratings is 5.","The mean of the counterconditioning group's ratings is 3.4."],"answer":3,"rationale":"The counterconditioning group's ratings sum to 17 (5 + 4 + 3 + 3 + 2), and 17 divided by 5 is 3.4. The control group's mode and median are both 8, and the counterconditioning group's range is 5 minus 2, which is 3."},{"id":"U3-274","section":"3.7","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Kenji's dog gets excited whenever it hears Kenji's car pull into the driveway, because a walk always followed. Kenji's new job now means the dog hears the car every evening, but no walk follows. Which description shows extinction taking place?","options":["The car sound is presented again and again without the walk, and the dog's excitement gradually weakens.","The car sound continues to be followed by walks, and the dog's excitement grows stronger.","The dog's excitement fades, but after Kenji's two-week trip the car sound excites the dog again.","The dog gets excited only at the sound of Kenji's car and not at other cars."],"answer":0,"rationale":"Extinction requires the conditioned stimulus (the car sound) to occur repeatedly without the unconditioned stimulus (the walk), so the conditioned response weakens. Renewed excitement after a break is spontaneous recovery, and responding only to Kenji's car is discrimination."},{"id":"U3-275","section":"3.7","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"When a researcher gently touches a sea slug's siphon, the slug pulls in its gill as a protective reflex. After the same gentle touch is repeated many times with nothing harmful following, the gill withdrawal becomes weaker and weaker. This decline in responding is most likely","options":["sensitization","habituation","stimulus discrimination","spontaneous recovery"],"answer":1,"rationale":"Habituation is a diminished response to a repeated, harmless stimulus, and it occurs even in simple animals like sea slugs. Sensitization is the opposite: an increased response, usually after an intense or harmful stimulus."},{"id":"U3-276","section":"3.7","skill":"Data Interpretation","difficulty":2,"stimulus":{"type":"line","title":"Strength of Conditioned Eye-Blink Response","xLabel":"Trial","yLabel":"Response strength (0 to 10)","series":[{"name":"Conditioned response","points":[[1,1],[2,3],[3,5],[4,7],[5,8],[6,9],[7,9.5],[8,10],[9,9],[10,7],[11,5],[12,3.5],[13,2],[14,1.5],[15,1],[16,0.5],[17,5],[18,3],[19,1.5],[20,0.5]]}],"bands":[{"from":1,"to":8,"label":"Phase 1"},{"from":9,"to":16,"label":"Phase 2"},{"from":17,"to":20,"label":"Phase 3"}]},"stem":"A researcher measured the strength of a conditioned eye-blink response across 20 trials in three phases. In Phase 1 a tone was paired with a puff of air; in Phase 2 the tone was presented alone; Phase 3 began after a 24-hour rest, with the tone again presented alone. Which sequence of concepts does the graph show across the three phases?","options":["Acquisition, extinction, spontaneous recovery","Generalization, extinction, higher-order conditioning","Acquisition, habituation, discrimination","Extinction, acquisition, generalization"],"answer":0,"rationale":"The response grows while the tone is paired with the air puff (acquisition), weakens when the tone is presented alone (extinction), and then partly reappears after a rest before fading again (spontaneous recovery)."},{"id":"U3-277","section":"3.7","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Visitors at an aquarium notice that the goldfish in one tank rush to the surface the moment a green lamp above the tank switches on, even though no food has been dropped. A keeper explains that for the past month, the lamp has been switched on a few seconds before every feeding, and the fish have always swum up to eat when the pellets land on the water. In this example, what is the conditioned stimulus?","options":["The green lamp switching on","The food pellets landing on the water","The water in the tank","Swimming to the surface when the lamp switches on"],"answer":0,"rationale":"The lamp was neutral at first and now triggers swimming to the surface because it was paired with food, so it is the conditioned stimulus. The food is the unconditioned stimulus, and swimming up when the lamp comes on is the conditioned response."},{"id":"U3-278","section":"3.7","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"A researcher conditions a dog to salivate to a tone of 1,000 hertz by pairing the tone with food. Which result would demonstrate stimulus generalization?","options":["The dog salivates to food presented without any tone.","The dog stops salivating to the 1,000-hertz tone after many tone-alone trials.","The dog salivates, somewhat less, to a 1,200-hertz tone it has never heard before.","The dog salivates only to the 1,000-hertz tone and not to any other tone."],"answer":2,"rationale":"Stimulus generalization is responding to stimuli that are similar to the conditioned stimulus, usually less strongly as similarity decreases. Responding only to the original tone is discrimination, and the weakening response is extinction."},{"id":"U3-279","section":"3.7","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"In a lab, a 5-month-old sucks on a pacifier connected to a sensor while a recording plays the syllable 'ba' through a speaker. At first she sucks rapidly each time the syllable plays, but after it has been played many times her sucking slows back to its usual rate. This decline in responding is an example of","options":["dishabituation","extinction","accommodation","habituation"],"answer":3,"rationale":"Habituation is a decreasing response to a stimulus that is presented repeatedly. Dishabituation would be the renewed burst of sucking that appears if a new syllable, such as 'pa,' were played."},{"id":"U3-280","section":"3.7","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":1,"stem":"When a puff of air is directed at a newborn's eye, the baby blinks the very first time, with no prior learning. In classical conditioning terms, the puff of air is","options":["a conditioned stimulus","an unconditioned stimulus","an unconditioned response","a conditioned response"],"answer":1,"rationale":"An unconditioned stimulus naturally triggers a response without learning, just as food did for Pavlov's dogs at the start of his experiments. The blink itself is the unconditioned response."},{"id":"U3-281","section":"3.7","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":3,"stem":"A rabbit has learned to blink when it hears a tone that has repeatedly been followed by a puff of air to its eye. How should the rabbit's blink be classified?","options":["It is always an unconditioned response, because blinking is a reflex.","It is an unconditioned response when caused by the air puff and a conditioned response when caused by the tone.","It is always a conditioned response once learning has taken place.","It is an unconditioned stimulus for the tone and a conditioned stimulus for the air puff."],"answer":1,"rationale":"The same behavior can be both: the blink to the air puff is unlearned (unconditioned response), while the blink to the tone is learned (conditioned response). Blinking is a response, not a stimulus."},{"id":"U3-282","section":"3.7","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"In phase one of a study, a rat learns to freeze when it hears a tone, because the tone has been paired with a mild shock. In phase two, a light is repeatedly switched on just before the tone, and no shock is given. After several light-tone pairings, what is the most likely result?","options":["The rat freezes when the light is switched on by itself.","The rat freezes only when the light and the tone are presented together.","The rat freezes to the light only if the light is later paired directly with a shock.","The rat stops freezing to both the light and the tone because no shock was given in phase two."],"answer":0,"rationale":"This is higher-order conditioning: a well-learned conditioned stimulus (the tone) acts like an unconditioned stimulus, so a new stimulus paired with it (the light) comes to trigger freezing by itself, without ever being paired with the shock."},{"id":"U3-283","section":"3.7","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"As a child, Kareem often visited a neighbor whose large, untrained dog would leap up and knock him to the ground the moment the metal gate creaked open. Kareem is now 17, and the creak of any metal gate makes his heart pound, even though the neighbor moved away years ago. In the development of Kareem's fear, what was the unconditioned stimulus (UCS)?","options":["The creak of the metal gate","Being knocked to the ground by the dog","His pounding heart","Visiting the neighbor's home"],"answer":1,"rationale":"Being knocked down naturally produced fear without any learning, so it is the unconditioned stimulus. The creak of the gate was neutral at first and became the conditioned stimulus after being paired with it, and his pounding heart is a response."},{"id":"U3-284","section":"3.7","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":1,"stem":"A nurse notices that a patient who receives a painful injection every morning right after the nurse's medicine cart squeaks into the room now tenses up whenever he hears the cart squeak. The type of learning this patient shows is","options":["classical conditioning","shaping","latent learning","instinctive drift"],"answer":0,"rationale":"The squeak was paired with an event that naturally causes tensing (the painful injection), so the squeak alone now triggers tensing, which is classical conditioning. Shaping is an operant technique that reinforces successive steps toward a target behavior."},{"id":"U3-285","section":"3.7","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":1,"stem":"At 17, Jae-won tenses up whenever he sees a horse, even in a film. Which account of how his fear began would best fit classical conditioning?","options":["He watched his older brother get thrown from a horse and saw how frightened his brother was.","He was kicked by a horse during a farm visit, and horses became linked with the pain and shock of the kick.","Worried relatives praised and comforted him each time he refused to go near horses.","He saw horses so often as a young child that he stopped paying attention to them."],"answer":1,"rationale":"In classical conditioning, a neutral stimulus (horses) is paired with an event that naturally causes fear (the painful kick), so horses alone come to trigger fear. Watching his brother describes observational learning, being praised for avoidance is operant reinforcement, and seeing horses often would lead to habituation, not fear."},{"id":"U3-286","section":"3.7","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":3,"stem":"In a hypothetical study, volunteers drank a green, strawberry-flavored drink each time they took a medication that lowers immune system activity. Later, when they drank the same drink with a sugar pill instead of the medication, their immune activity still dropped. What was the conditioned stimulus?","options":["The immune-lowering medication","The green, strawberry-flavored drink","The sugar pill","The drop in immune activity after the drink alone"],"answer":1,"rationale":"The drink was neutral until it was paired with the medication, and then it triggered the immune change by itself, so it is the conditioned stimulus. The medication is the unconditioned stimulus, the sugar pill has no effect of its own, and the drop in immune activity after the drink alone is the conditioned response."},{"id":"U3-287","section":"3.7","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"During a long hospital stay as a young child, Daniel was soothed to sleep each night by a nurse who gently rubbed his back while humming the same lullaby. Now an adult, Daniel hears that lullaby playing in a bookstore and immediately feels calm and drowsy. In classical conditioning terms, the lullaby is now","options":["an unconditioned stimulus","a neutral stimulus","a conditioned stimulus","an unconditioned response"],"answer":2,"rationale":"The lullaby was neutral until it was repeatedly paired with the soothing back rub (the unconditioned stimulus), so it now triggers calm by itself as a conditioned stimulus. It is no longer neutral, because it now produces a response on its own."},{"id":"U3-288","section":"0.1","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":1,"stem":"Dr. Ferreira explains a child's tantrums only in terms of what happens immediately before and after them, and she refuses to speculate about the child's thoughts or feelings. Her approach is most consistent with which perspective?","options":["Cognitive","Psychodynamic","Humanistic","Behavioral"],"answer":3,"rationale":"Behaviorists focus on observable behavior and the environmental events around it, traditionally leaving out mental processes. A cognitive psychologist would focus precisely on the child's thoughts."},{"id":"U3-289","section":"3.7","skill":"Research Methods and Design","difficulty":1,"stem":"A researcher replicating Pavlov's work has conditioned a dog to salivate to a tone. Which procedure should she use if she wants to produce extinction of the conditioned response?","options":["Present the tone repeatedly without food.","Present food repeatedly without the tone.","Continue pairing the tone with food.","Pair a new light with the tone."],"answer":0,"rationale":"Extinction occurs when the conditioned stimulus is presented without the unconditioned stimulus, so the conditioned response weakens. Continuing the pairing strengthens learning, and pairing a light with the tone would produce higher-order conditioning."},{"id":"U3-290","section":"3.7","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":3,"stem":"Which finding best illustrates the role of biological predispositions in classical conditioning?","options":["Any stimulus can become a conditioned stimulus equally easily if it is paired often enough with an unconditioned stimulus.","Birds that find food by sight readily learn to avoid food of a certain color after becoming ill, while rats more readily learn to avoid a certain taste.","Conditioning can occur only when the unconditioned stimulus satisfies a biological need such as hunger.","Humans learn conditioned associations more strongly than other species because of their larger cortex."],"answer":1,"rationale":"Different species are prepared to learn the associations that best fit how they survive, so sight-feeding birds link illness with color and rats link it with taste. The claim that any stimulus works equally well is exactly what biological preparedness research contradicts."},{"id":"U3-291","section":"3.7","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"After being stung several times by wasps that nested near his family's garden shed, Kofi panics whenever a wasp flies near him. However, he stays calm while helping his uncle check the honeybee hives on his farm, even though the bees look and sound somewhat similar. Kofi's different reactions to wasps and honeybees illustrate","options":["stimulus generalization","habituation","stimulus discrimination","latent learning"],"answer":2,"rationale":"Kofi gives the conditioned fear response to the stimulus linked with the stings (wasps) but not to a similar stimulus that has not been (honeybees), which is discrimination. Generalization would mean panicking at honeybees too."},{"id":"U3-292","section":"3.8","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Every school day for a year, Ji-ho pressed the button on the hallway vending machine and a drink dropped out. Then the machine broke. For the next two weeks, he pressed the button less and less often whenever nothing came out, and he eventually stopped trying at all. The change in Ji-ho's button pressing is an example of","options":["extinction","stimulus discrimination","shaping","positive punishment"],"answer":0,"rationale":"Pressing the button had been reinforced with a drink; once the reinforcement stopped, the behavior gradually faded, which is operant extinction. Positive punishment would require adding an unpleasant consequence after pressing, which did not happen."},{"id":"U3-293","section":"3.7","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":1,"stem":"Which of the following situations best illustrates habituation?","options":["A toddler picks out her grandmother's face in a crowd of strangers.","A dog learns to sit because it receives a treat each time.","After a month of living near the airport, Musa no longer wakes up when planes take off at night.","A child avoids the stove after touching it once and getting burned."],"answer":2,"rationale":"Habituation is a reduced response to a repeated or constant stimulus, like the nightly plane noise. The dog and stove examples involve learning from consequences, and face recognition is perception, not habituation."},{"id":"U3-294","section":"0.1","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Tomás is outgoing and talkative. Which explanation of his personality would a strict behaviorist most likely give?","options":["He has repeatedly been rewarded with attention and praise for being sociable.","He inherited genes that predispose him to extraversion.","He has an unconscious need for approval that began in early childhood.","He believes he is likable and expects others to respond warmly to him."],"answer":0,"rationale":"Behaviorists such as Skinner explain personality as patterns of behavior shaped by reinforcement and punishment. Expectations and beliefs are cognitive explanations, and unconscious needs are psychodynamic ones."},{"id":"U3-295","section":"3.7","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":1,"stem":"When construction began next door, the Hassan family's pet rabbit hid under the sofa every time the drilling started. Over several weeks, the rabbit began ignoring the drilling and kept eating calmly. Which process has occurred?","options":["Sensitization","Habituation","Higher-order conditioning","Negative reinforcement"],"answer":1,"rationale":"Habituation is a reduced response to a stimulus that is repeated and has no important consequence. Sensitization would be the opposite, with the rabbit reacting more and more strongly to the drilling."},{"id":"U3-296","section":"3.7","skill":"Concept Recall","difficulty":2,"stem":"According to research on taste aversion, a rat is most likely to develop a strong aversion to a new flavor of water after which experience?","options":["Receiving a mild foot shock while drinking it","Hearing a loud tone while drinking it","Becoming nauseated a few hours after drinking it","Seeing a bright light while drinking it"],"answer":2,"rationale":"Animals readily link tastes with later sickness, even after a delay of hours and sometimes after only one pairing. Taste is much less readily associated with external events such as shocks, sounds or lights."},{"id":"U3-297","section":"3.7","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":3,"stem":"In their taste aversion research, John Garcia and Robert Koelling found that rats easily learned to link a flavor with later illness but not with a shock, and easily learned to link lights and sounds with a shock but not with illness. Which conclusion do these findings best support?","options":["Any neutral stimulus can be conditioned equally well to any unconditioned stimulus.","Conditioning requires the conditioned and unconditioned stimuli to occur at exactly the same moment.","Rats are unable to learn associations involving lights or sounds.","Organisms are biologically prepared to learn some associations more easily than others."],"answer":3,"rationale":"The type of stimulus mattered: taste paired naturally with illness, and lights and sounds with pain. This shows biological preparedness. Rats did learn light and sound associations, just with shock rather than illness."},{"id":"U3-298","section":"3.7","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":1,"stem":"Baby Leila's father hangs a new spinning mobile above her crib. On the first day her eyes follow it closely and she kicks excitedly whenever it spins. By the end of the week, the mobile can spin for minutes without her looking up, yet when her father swaps in a different mobile, she stares and kicks again. Her reduced reaction to the first mobile is an example of","options":["Object permanence","Habituation","Accommodation","Extinction"],"answer":1,"rationale":"Habituation is a decreasing response to a stimulus that is presented repeatedly, and her renewed interest in the new mobile shows the change was not simply tiredness. Extinction applies to a conditioned response, and her interest in the mobile was never conditioned."},{"id":"U3-299","section":"3.7","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Ming got food poisoning after eating grilled prawns at a seaside restaurant. Since then, she feels queasy when she smells crab, lobster or any other shellfish, even though prawns were the only food linked to her illness. Ming's reaction to the other shellfish is best explained by","options":["habituation","stimulus discrimination","stimulus generalization","negative reinforcement"],"answer":2,"rationale":"Her conditioned nausea has spread from the original food (prawns) to similar foods, which is stimulus generalization. Discrimination would mean feeling queasy only at prawns."},{"id":"U3-300","section":"3.7","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"During her first week at a bakery, Rosa burned her fingertips several times on trays that had just come out of the oven, and each time she yanked her hand away instantly. Now, whenever the oven timer beeps, she pulls her hands close to her body before anyone opens the oven door. Which part of this example is the unconditioned response?","options":["The oven timer beeping","The heat of the freshly baked tray","Yanking her hand away from the hot tray","Pulling her hands in when the timer beeps"],"answer":2,"rationale":"Yanking her hand away from a burning tray is an unlearned reaction, so it is the unconditioned response. The heat is the unconditioned stimulus, the timer is the conditioned stimulus, and pulling her hands in at the beep is the conditioned response."},{"id":"U3-301","section":"3.7","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which of the following responses was most likely acquired through classical conditioning?","options":["A newborn grasping a finger placed in her palm","A teenager's nausea at the smell of a cologne a relative wore during a long, carsick road trip","A person coughing when smoke enters the lungs","A cat's pupils widening in a dark room"],"answer":1,"rationale":"The cologne smell was neutral until it was paired with carsickness, so the nausea it now triggers is a learned conditioned response. The other three are unlearned reflexes."},{"id":"U3-302","section":"3.2","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which of the following is best explained as an inborn reflex rather than a product of learning?","options":["A toddler copying her older brother's dance moves after watching him","A baby startling and flinging out her arms at a sudden loud noise","A rat that explored a maze without food later running it quickly once food is placed at the end","A new cashier counting change faster after months on the job"],"answer":1,"rationale":"The startle reflex in infants is present from birth and needs no experience. Copying the dance is observational learning, the maze running is latent learning, and the cashier's speed reflects practice and reinforcement."},{"id":"U3-303","section":"0.1","skill":"Concept Recall","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which statement best represents the behaviorist view of development?","options":["Development unfolds through biologically timed stages of thinking.","Children develop mainly through their history of conditioning and the consequences of their behavior.","Early unconscious conflicts shape adult personality.","Children actively construct knowledge by building and revising schemas."],"answer":1,"rationale":"Behaviorism holds that development is largely a product of learning through conditioning. Stages of thinking and schemas come from Piaget's cognitive theory, and unconscious conflicts come from psychodynamic theory."},{"id":"U3-304","section":"3.8","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":1,"stem":"A swimming coach first praises six-year-old Chiamaka for putting her face in the water, then only for floating with her face in the water, then only for kicking while floating, and finally only for swimming the full width of the pool. This process is known as","options":["negative reinforcement","observational learning","shaping","instinctive drift"],"answer":2,"rationale":"Shaping reinforces successive approximations of a target behavior, raising the requirement step by step. Negative reinforcement would involve removing something unpleasant, which is not happening here."},{"id":"U3-305","section":"3.8","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":1,"stem":"Each time a dolphin at an aquarium leaps through a hoop, its trainer rewards it with a fish, and the dolphin now leaps through the hoop frequently. The dolphin's leap is best described as","options":["an unconditioned response","a conditioned stimulus","a classically conditioned response","an operant response"],"answer":3,"rationale":"The leap is a voluntary behavior that increased because of its consequence (the fish), which makes it an operant response. A classically conditioned response is an automatic reaction triggered by a stimulus that comes before it."},{"id":"U3-306","section":"3.8","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"A school library suspends a student's borrowing privileges for one week every time the student returns a book late. Over the term, the number of late returns drops sharply. This policy illustrates","options":["negative punishment","positive punishment","negative reinforcement","positive reinforcement"],"answer":0,"rationale":"Taking away something students value (borrowing privileges) to decrease a behavior (late returns) is negative punishment. Negative reinforcement removes something unpleasant to increase a behavior, which is the opposite effect."},{"id":"U3-307","section":"3.8","skill":"Research Methods and Design","difficulty":2,"stimulus":{"type":"passage","text":"Volunteers were randomly assigned to one of two conditions and heard bursts of loud, unpleasant noise through headphones. In the controllable condition, turning a dial stopped each burst. In the uncontrollable condition, the bursts started and stopped on their own no matter what volunteers did with the dial. Afterward, all volunteers worked on a set of solvable anagram puzzles with no noise. Volunteers from the uncontrollable condition gave up sooner and solved fewer anagrams."},"stem":"The results described in the passage most directly demonstrate which concept?","options":["Superstitious behavior","Latent learning","Learned helplessness","Instinctive drift"],"answer":2,"rationale":"Volunteers who learned that their actions could not control an unpleasant event later stopped trying on a new task they could have mastered, which is learned helplessness. Latent learning is learning that shows up only once there is an incentive, which does not fit the drop in effort."},{"id":"U3-801","section":"3.8","skill":"Concept Recall","difficulty":1,"stem":"Edward Thorndike's law of effect states that","options":["a neutral stimulus repeatedly paired with an unconditioned stimulus will come to trigger a similar response","behaviors followed by satisfying consequences become more likely to recur, while behaviors followed by unpleasant consequences become less likely","learning can take place without reinforcement and appear later when there is a reason to show it","people learn new behaviors mainly by watching and copying the actions of others"],"answer":1,"rationale":"The law of effect links a behavior to its consequences: satisfying outcomes strengthen it and unpleasant outcomes weaken it, which became the basis of operant conditioning. Pairing a neutral stimulus with an unconditioned stimulus describes classical conditioning instead."},{"id":"U3-802","section":"3.8","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"On her morning bus ride to school, Lina is bothered by the loud conversations around her. One day she puts on noise-cancelling headphones, and the noise she dislikes disappears. Over the following weeks, Lina puts on her headphones as soon as she boards the bus. Which operant conditioning process best explains the increase in this behavior?","options":["Positive punishment","Negative punishment","Negative reinforcement","Positive reinforcement"],"answer":2,"rationale":"Putting on the headphones removes an unpleasant stimulus (the noise), and the behavior increases, so this is negative reinforcement. Positive reinforcement would require adding something pleasant, and either type of punishment would make the behavior less likely."},{"id":"U3-803","section":"3.8","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Mr. Petrovic notices that several students chat during his weekly vocabulary tests. He announces that anyone who talks during a test will be given an extra page of vocabulary exercises to complete at home. After two weeks of this policy, talking during tests nearly stops. Mr. Petrovic's policy is an example of","options":["positive punishment","negative punishment","negative reinforcement","positive reinforcement"],"answer":0,"rationale":"An unwanted consequence (extra homework) is added after the behavior, and the behavior decreases, which is positive punishment. Negative punishment would involve taking away something the student likes, such as free time."},{"id":"U3-804","section":"3.8","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":3,"stem":"Ravi dislikes washing the family car, a chore he normally does every Saturday. His parents tell him that any week he scores above 90 percent on his chemistry quiz, he will be excused from washing the car that weekend. Ravi begins studying chemistry much more often. Which concept best explains the increase in Ravi's studying?","options":["Negative punishment, because a chore is taken away","Positive reinforcement, because a reward is added","Positive punishment, because the threat of the chore is unpleasant","Negative reinforcement, because an unpleasant task is removed"],"answer":3,"rationale":"Removing an unpleasant task (washing the car) after the desired behavior makes the behavior more likely, which is negative reinforcement. It is tempting to call this negative punishment because something is taken away, but punishment always decreases a behavior, and here studying increases."},{"id":"U3-805","section":"3.8","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":1,"stem":"Which of the following is the best example of a primary reinforcer?","options":["Receiving a gold star sticker on a spelling test","Earning points that can be exchanged for prizes at a school fair","Drinking cool water after a long hike in the desert heat","Being given a certificate for perfect attendance"],"answer":2,"rationale":"A primary reinforcer satisfies a biological need without any learning, and water for a thirsty hiker does exactly that. Stickers, points and certificates are secondary reinforcers that gain their value through association with other rewards."},{"id":"U3-806","section":"3.8","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"At a marine park, a trainer blows a short whistle every time she gives a sea lion a fish. After weeks of this, the trainer finds she can blow the whistle alone right after the sea lion performs a trick, and the sea lion keeps performing the trick reliably. In this situation, the whistle has become","options":["a primary reinforcer","a secondary reinforcer","an unconditioned stimulus","a negative reinforcer"],"answer":1,"rationale":"The whistle has no natural value to the sea lion; it gained reinforcing power by being paired with fish, so it is a secondary (conditioned) reinforcer. The fish itself is the primary reinforcer because it meets a biological need."},{"id":"U3-807","section":"3.8","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Dr. Okafor is training a lab rat to press a lever. She first gave the rat a food pellet whenever it faced the lever, then only when it moved toward the lever. The rat now reliably touches the lever with its paw. If she continues to use shaping correctly, what should Dr. Okafor do next?","options":["Give a pellet every time the rat faces the lever, to keep the earlier behavior strong","Stop giving pellets entirely so the rat discovers the full behavior on its own","Give a pellet at random times so the rat cannot predict when food will arrive","Give a pellet only when the rat pushes the lever down, and no longer for simply touching it"],"answer":3,"rationale":"Shaping reinforces successive approximations, so each step requires a behavior a little closer to the final target before reinforcement is given. Going back to earlier steps or stopping reinforcement would not move the rat toward pressing the lever."},{"id":"U3-808","section":"3.8","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"A trainer uses sunflower seeds as rewards to teach a hamster to drop a small bead into a cup. The hamster learns the trick quickly. After several weeks, however, the hamster starts stuffing the beads into its cheek pouches and carrying them to its nest instead of dropping them in the cup, even though this earns no seeds. Which concept best describes this change?","options":["Instinctive drift","Spontaneous recovery","Superstitious behavior","Stimulus generalization"],"answer":0,"rationale":"Instinctive drift is the tendency of a trained animal to slide back toward natural, instinctive behaviors (here, hoarding objects in its cheek pouches), showing that reinforcement cannot shape just any behavior. Spontaneous recovery is the return of an extinguished response, which is not what happens here."},{"id":"U3-809","section":"3.8","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"In an online game, a rare treasure chest appears completely at random. The first time Arjun found one, he had just made his character spin in a circle. Now, before entering every new area of the game, Arjun makes his character spin in a circle, even though spinning has no effect on whether a chest appears. Arjun's spinning is best described as","options":["instinctive drift","latent learning","superstitious behavior","a fixed-interval response"],"answer":2,"rationale":"Superstitious behavior develops when a reinforcer happens to follow a behavior that did not actually cause it, so the unrelated behavior is strengthened. Instinctive drift involves an animal reverting to natural behaviors, which does not fit a learned game habit."},{"id":"U3-810","section":"3.8","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"At her old job, Leila's manager harshly criticized every report she wrote, no matter how carefully she researched or checked it. Leila now works for a manager who gives fair and helpful feedback, yet she puts little effort into her reports and says, \"Nothing I do makes a difference anyway.\" Which concept best explains Leila's current behavior?","options":["Extinction","Learned helplessness","Negative reinforcement","Instinctive drift"],"answer":1,"rationale":"Leila learned that her actions had no control over an unpleasant outcome, and she now gives up even in a new situation where effort would pay off, which is learned helplessness. Extinction is the weakening of a response when reinforcement stops, but her problem is a belief that she has no control."},{"id":"U3-811","section":"3.8","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":1,"stem":"Four-year-old Haruto's father wants him to learn to put his toys back in the toy box. At first, his father praises Haruto every single time he puts a toy away. Which type of reinforcement schedule is Haruto's father using?","options":["Continuous reinforcement","Fixed-ratio reinforcement","Variable-interval reinforcement","Variable-ratio reinforcement"],"answer":0,"rationale":"Reinforcing every correct response is a continuous reinforcement schedule, which is the fastest way to establish a new behavior. Ratio and interval schedules are partial schedules that reinforce only some responses."},{"id":"U3-812","section":"3.8","skill":"Data Interpretation","difficulty":1,"stimulus":{"type":"table","caption":"Reward rules for four phone apps","headers":["App","Reward rule"],"rows":[["StepUp","A badge is earned for the first step counted after each full hour has passed"],["LuckySpin","A prize is won after an unpredictable number of spins, about 1 in every 20 on average"],["NewsPing","A new article becomes available at unpredictable times and is seen on the next check"],["SnackStop","A free snack is earned after every 6 purchases"]]},"stem":"The table describes the reward rules for four phone apps. Which app uses a fixed-ratio schedule of reinforcement?","options":["StepUp","LuckySpin","NewsPing","SnackStop"],"answer":3,"rationale":"SnackStop gives a reward after a set number of responses (every 6 purchases), which is a fixed-ratio schedule. LuckySpin is also based on the number of responses, but the number is unpredictable, which makes it variable-ratio."},{"id":"U3-813","section":"3.8","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"A sneaker website restocks a popular shoe at unpredictable times, sometimes after a few hours and sometimes after a few days. Only the first visit after a restock lets Omar buy a pair. Omar ends up checking the site at a slow but steady rate throughout the day. Which schedule of reinforcement is Omar's checking behavior on?","options":["Fixed-interval","Fixed-ratio","Variable-interval","Variable-ratio"],"answer":2,"rationale":"Reinforcement depends on an unpredictable amount of time passing, not on how many times Omar checks, so this is a variable-interval schedule, which produces slow, steady responding. It is not variable-ratio because checking more often does not make a restock happen sooner."},{"id":"U3-814","section":"3.8","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":3,"stem":"Four toddlers each learn to press a button on a toy that plays a song. The toys differ only in when the song plays. Later, the batteries in all four toys die, so pressing never plays a song. Which toddler is likely to keep pressing the button the longest?","options":["Rania, whose toy played the song after every press","Diego, whose toy played the song for the first press after each 30 seconds had passed","Mei, whose toy played the song after exactly every fifth press","Kwesi, whose toy played the song after an unpredictable number of presses, averaging about five"],"answer":3,"rationale":"Kwesi's toy used a variable-ratio schedule, which produces the behavior most resistant to extinction because the learner can never tell when the next reward might come. Rania's continuous schedule leads to the fastest extinction, since the change from every press to no presses being rewarded is easy to notice."},{"id":"U3-815","section":"3.8","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Youssef often asks his older sister Mariam for help with his math homework. When she is reading at the kitchen table, she usually stops and helps him. When she is wearing her headphones, she ignores his questions. After a few weeks, Youssef asks for help only when Mariam is not wearing headphones. Which operant conditioning concept best explains Youssef's behavior?","options":["Stimulus generalization","Stimulus discrimination","Shaping","Learned helplessness"],"answer":1,"rationale":"Asking for help is reinforced when Mariam is not wearing headphones but not when she is, and Youssef now responds only in the presence of the cue that signals reinforcement, which is stimulus discrimination. Generalization would mean he kept asking in both situations because they seem similar."},{"id":"U3-816","section":"3.8","skill":"Data Interpretation","difficulty":2,"stimulus":{"type":"line","title":"Cumulative lever presses during one 15-minute session","xLabel":"Time (minutes)","yLabel":"Cumulative lever presses","series":[{"name":"Cumulative presses","points":[[0,0],[1,2],[2,5],[3,12],[4,25],[5,45],[6,46],[7,49],[8,55],[9,66],[10,83],[11,85],[12,89],[13,98],[14,115],[15,145]]}],"bands":[{"from":4.9,"to":5.1,"label":"R"},{"from":9.9,"to":10.1,"label":"R"},{"from":14.9,"to":15.0,"label":"R"}]},"stem":"A researcher recorded the cumulative number of lever presses made by a rat during a 15-minute session. The shaded bands labeled R mark the moments when a food pellet was delivered. Based on the pattern of responding in the graph, which reinforcement schedule was the rat most likely on?","options":["Fixed-interval","Fixed-ratio","Variable-ratio","Variable-interval"],"answer":0,"rationale":"Pellets came at equal time intervals while the number of presses between them varied, and the line flattens right after each pellet and then curves upward more and more steeply until the next one, producing the scalloped shape typical of a fixed-interval schedule. Variable schedules produce a fairly straight, steady line because the next reward cannot be predicted."},{"id":"U3-817","section":"3.8","skill":"Data Interpretation","difficulty":3,"stimulus":{"type":"bar","title":"Pecking rate by reinforcement schedule","xLabel":"Training schedule","yLabel":"Responses per hour","categories":["Continuous","Fixed-ratio 10","Variable-ratio 10"],"series":[{"name":"Final training session","values":[300,900,1100]},{"name":"First hour of extinction","values":[60,250,700]}],"yMax":1200},"stem":"Dr. Haddad trained three groups of pigeons to peck a key for grain, each on a different schedule. The graph shows each group's response rate in the final training session and in the first hour of extinction, when pecking no longer produced grain. Which conclusion is best supported by the data?","options":["Continuous reinforcement produced the highest response rate during training","The variable-ratio group lost the largest number of responses per hour when extinction began","Partial reinforcement, especially on a variable-ratio schedule, produced pecking that was more resistant to extinction than continuous reinforcement","The fixed-ratio group kept a larger share of its training response rate during extinction than the variable-ratio group"],"answer":2,"rationale":"During extinction the variable-ratio group kept about 64 percent of its training rate (700 of 1,100), compared with about 28 percent for fixed-ratio and 20 percent for continuous, showing the partial reinforcement effect. The fixed-ratio group, not the variable-ratio group, had the largest raw drop (650 responses per hour)."},{"id":"U3-818","section":"3.8","skill":"Data Interpretation","difficulty":2,"stimulus":{"type":"table","caption":"Homework assignments completed (out of 5)","headers":["Student","Week before tokens","Week with tokens"],"rows":[["Aisha","2","4"],["Bilal","1","4"],["Carlos","3","5"],["Dina","2","3"],["Eun-ji","2","4"]]},"stem":"Ms. Bautista introduced a classroom system in which students earn tokens for each homework assignment they complete, and tokens can later be exchanged for extra free-reading time. The table shows the number of assignments (out of 5) that five students completed in a week before and a week during the system. Which statement best describes and interprets the data?","options":["The mean rose from 2.0 to 4.0, which shows that the tokens acted as primary reinforcers","The mean rose from 2.0 to 4.0, which is consistent with the tokens acting as secondary reinforcers that strengthened homework completion","The mean rose from 2.0 to 3.0, which is consistent with the tokens acting as secondary reinforcers","The mean stayed the same, but the range of scores became smaller during the token week"],"answer":1,"rationale":"The before scores total 10 and the during scores total 20, so the means are 2.0 and 4.0. Tokens have no value on their own and gain it through what they can be exchanged for, which makes them secondary reinforcers rather than primary ones."},{"id":"U3-819","section":"3.8","skill":"Research Methods and Design","difficulty":3,"stimulus":{"type":"passage","text":"Dr. Lindqvist wanted to test whether a token system increases on-task behavior. Mr. Santos's morning class received tokens for staying on task, which could be traded for small prizes. Ms. Akhtar's afternoon class did not receive tokens. Observers who did not know the purpose of the study recorded the percentage of time each student spent on task over four weeks. The morning class averaged 82 percent on task and the afternoon class averaged 61 percent. Dr. Lindqvist concluded that the token system caused the higher on-task behavior."},"stem":"Based on the passage, which is the most serious problem with Dr. Lindqvist's conclusion that the token system caused the improvement?","options":["On-task behavior was the independent variable, so it could not have been measured","Tokens are primary reinforcers, so the results cannot apply to other kinds of rewards","The study has no dependent variable, so no conclusion about behavior can be drawn","The classes differed in more than the token system, such as time of day and teacher, so these differences could explain the results"],"answer":3,"rationale":"Because the classes were not randomly assigned and also differed in time of day and teacher, these confounding variables offer other explanations for the higher on-task behavior. On-task behavior was actually the dependent variable, and tokens are secondary reinforcers, not primary ones."},{"id":"U3-820","section":"3.8","skill":"Concept Recall","difficulty":1,"stem":"In operant conditioning, the word \"negative\" in the terms negative reinforcement and negative punishment refers to","options":["removing a stimulus after a behavior","a consequence that feels unpleasant","a decrease in how often a behavior occurs","a behavior that is undesirable"],"answer":0,"rationale":"\"Negative\" means that a stimulus is taken away, and \"positive\" means that a stimulus is added. Whether the behavior increases or decreases is what separates reinforcement from punishment, so \"negative\" does not mean bad or unpleasant."},{"id":"U3-901","section":"3.9","skill":"Concept Recall","difficulty":1,"stem":"Learning that occurs when a person observes the consequences of someone else's behavior, rather than experiencing those consequences directly, is called","options":["latent learning","insight learning","vicarious conditioning","higher-order conditioning"],"answer":2,"rationale":"Vicarious conditioning is learning from the rewards and punishments that other people receive, a key idea in social learning theory. Latent learning is learning that stays hidden until there is a reason to show it."},{"id":"U3-902","section":"3.9","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":1,"stem":"Noor watches closely as her grandmother folds samosas at a family gathering, tucking each corner in a special way. Without being given any instructions or praise, Noor picks up a piece of dough and folds a samosa using the same finger movements her grandmother used. Noor's behavior is best explained by","options":["observational learning","insight learning","classical conditioning","shaping"],"answer":0,"rationale":"Noor learned the behavior by watching a model and copying it, which is observational learning. Shaping would require rewarding small steps toward the behavior, and Noor received no reward at all."},{"id":"U3-903","section":"3.9","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"In Yusuf's history class, the teacher warmly praises a classmate, Daniela, each time she asks a thoughtful question. Yusuf has never been praised for asking questions himself, but over the next few weeks he begins asking far more questions in class. Which concept best explains the change in Yusuf's behavior?","options":["Positive reinforcement","Stimulus generalization","Latent learning","Vicarious reinforcement"],"answer":3,"rationale":"Yusuf's behavior increased because he saw another student rewarded for it, which is vicarious reinforcement. It is not ordinary positive reinforcement because Yusuf himself never received the praise."},{"id":"U3-904","section":"3.9","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"At a busy cafe, the manager assigns Thabo an extra cleaning shift after he arrives late for the third time. The other part-time staff, who saw this happen, all start arriving on time even though none of them has been given an extra shift. The change in the other workers' behavior best illustrates","options":["positive punishment","vicarious punishment","negative reinforcement","learned helplessness"],"answer":1,"rationale":"The other workers reduced their lateness after seeing someone else receive an unpleasant consequence, which is vicarious punishment. Thabo himself experienced positive punishment, but the observers learned without receiving any consequence."},{"id":"U3-905","section":"3.9","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"A school wants to persuade its Grade 10 students to use the new reusable water bottle stations instead of buying plastic bottles. The student council plans a short video showing someone choosing to refill a bottle. According to social learning theory, which person would most likely be the most effective model for this audience?","options":["A famous scientist from another country explaining plastic pollution","The school principal reading out the new recycling policy","A popular Grade 10 student from the same school refilling a bottle between classes","A cartoon animal character singing about water bottles"],"answer":2,"rationale":"Observers are more likely to imitate a model they see as similar to themselves, and a well-liked classmate of the same age and school is the most similar choice. A famous scientist may have expertise, but is much less similar to the students."},{"id":"U3-906","section":"3.9","skill":"Research Methods and Design","difficulty":3,"stimulus":{"type":"passage","text":"In a classic study, Albert Bandura showed children a short film of an adult hitting and kicking a large inflatable doll. In one version the adult was praised afterward, in another the adult was scolded, and in a third there were no consequences. When each child was then left alone with the doll, children who had seen the adult scolded copied the fewest aggressive actions. Later, the experimenter offered every child a small treat for each action from the film they could copy, and the differences between the three groups largely disappeared."},"stem":"Which conclusion is best supported by the study described in the passage?","options":["Seeing the model scolded reduced how often children performed the behaviors, but not whether they learned them","Children who saw the model scolded never learned the aggressive behaviors","Observational learning can only occur when the model is rewarded","The children imitated the model mainly because they were directly punished during the first play session"],"answer":0,"rationale":"When an incentive was offered, children in all three groups could copy the behaviors, so they had all learned them; the model's consequences mainly affected whether they chose to perform them. This shows that observational learning does not require the model to be rewarded."},{"id":"U3-907","section":"3.9","skill":"Research Methods and Design","difficulty":2,"stimulus":{"type":"passage","text":"Dr. Achieng wants to know whether watching a peer share makes young children more generous. She randomly assigns 60 six-year-olds to watch one of two three-minute videos. In one video, a child gives some of her stickers to a classmate. In the other video, the same child plays alone with her stickers. Afterward, each child receives 10 stickers and is told that a classmate is absent today. Each child may place as many stickers as they wish in an envelope for that classmate, and Dr. Achieng counts the stickers in each envelope."},"stem":"Based on the passage, which is the operational definition of the dependent variable in Dr. Achieng's study?","options":["Whether a child watched the sharing video or the playing-alone video","The age of the children taking part in the study","The 10 stickers each child received after the video","The number of stickers, out of 10, each child put in the envelope for the absent classmate"],"answer":3,"rationale":"The dependent variable is the measured outcome, and it is defined here as the number of stickers a child gives away. Which video a child watched is the independent variable, the factor the researcher manipulated."},{"id":"U3-908","section":"3.9","skill":"Data Interpretation","difficulty":2,"stimulus":{"type":"bar","title":"Teens who used the model's shortcut","xLabel":"What happened to the model in the video","yLabel":"Percentage of teens (%)","categories":["Praised","No consequences","Criticized"],"series":[{"name":"Used the shortcut","values":[78,52,18]}],"yMax":100},"stem":"Dr. Ferreira showed three groups of teenagers a video of a peer solving a puzzle using an unusual shortcut. In one version the peer was praised, in another the peer was criticized for not following the standard steps, and in the third there were no consequences. Each teen then solved a similar puzzle. Which conclusion is best supported by the graph?","options":["The teens in the criticized condition were directly punished, which lowered their use of the shortcut","Observing the consequences a model received changed how often the teens copied the model's method","Use of the shortcut was about the same in every group, so the model's consequences had little effect","Imitation was highest when the model received no consequences at all"],"answer":1,"rationale":"Shortcut use was highest after seeing the model praised (78 percent) and lowest after seeing the model criticized (18 percent), which fits vicarious reinforcement and vicarious punishment. The teens themselves received no consequences, so this was not direct punishment."},{"id":"U3-909","section":"3.9","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":1,"stem":"Mariam spends twenty minutes on a difficult logic riddle and gets nowhere. Frustrated, she sets it aside and starts washing the dishes. Suddenly, with no hint from anyone, the answer pops into her head, and she is sure it is correct. Mariam's experience is an example of","options":["latent learning","shaping","insight learning","vicarious conditioning"],"answer":2,"rationale":"Insight learning is the sudden realization of a solution without gradual trial and error, reinforcement or a model. Latent learning involves knowledge picked up earlier without reinforcement and shown later, not a sudden solution to a new problem."},{"id":"U3-910","section":"3.9","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Psychologist Wolfgang Köhler placed bananas high out of reach of a chimpanzee, with several wooden crates in the enclosure. After a period of seeming to do nothing useful, the chimpanzee suddenly stacked the crates, climbed up and grabbed the bananas. Which conclusion did Köhler draw from observations like this?","options":["Some problem solving involves a sudden mental reorganization of the problem rather than gradual strengthening through reinforcement","Chimpanzees solve problems only through slow trial and error guided by rewards","The chimpanzee had been shaped to stack crates through successive approximations","The chimpanzee had formed a cognitive map of the enclosure through latent learning"],"answer":0,"rationale":"Köhler argued that the sudden, complete solution showed insight, a cognitive restructuring of the problem, rather than a response built up gradually by reinforcement. No step-by-step rewards were given, so shaping does not fit."},{"id":"U3-911","section":"3.9","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":3,"stem":"Ikaika and Sara each try to open a wooden puzzle box. Ikaika pushes and pulls different panels, and over many attempts his opening time slowly falls from ten minutes to two minutes. Sara turns the box over in her hands for several minutes without moving anything, then opens it in one smooth sequence and does so quickly on every later try. Which statement best describes their learning?","options":["Both students show latent learning, because neither was given a reward","Ikaika shows insight learning, while Sara shows trial-and-error learning","Sara shows latent learning, because her knowledge appeared only after a delay","Ikaika shows gradual trial-and-error learning shaped by consequences, while Sara shows insight learning"],"answer":3,"rationale":"Ikaika's slow, steady improvement fits trial-and-error learning in which successful moves are strengthened by their consequences, as in Thorndike's law of effect. Sara's sudden, complete solution that she repeats easily afterward is the signature of insight learning."},{"id":"U3-912","section":"3.9","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"For years, Hessa has sat in the back seat on her family's weekend drives to her grandmother's house in Al Ain, usually chatting or reading. No one ever asked her to learn the route. One weekend her uncle drives instead, gets confused, and asks for help. Hessa gives him every turn correctly. Hessa's knowledge of the route best illustrates","options":["insight learning","latent learning","shaping","vicarious reinforcement"],"answer":1,"rationale":"Hessa learned the route without any reinforcement, and her knowledge was not shown until she had a reason to use it, which is latent learning. Insight learning is a sudden solution to a problem, not knowledge that was quietly built up over many trips."},{"id":"U3-913","section":"3.9","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Kwame often wanders around a large shopping mall with his friends. One day the main corridor he usually takes to the food court is closed for repairs. Even though he has never walked any other route to the food court, Kwame immediately heads through a side wing and arrives without getting lost. Kwame's success most likely depends on","options":["a cognitive map formed through latent learning","a response shaped through successive approximations","vicarious reinforcement from watching other shoppers","a superstitious behavior"],"answer":0,"rationale":"Kwame has a mental representation of the mall's layout, a cognitive map, which he built while exploring without any reward and which lets him take a new route. Shaping would require rewards for gradual steps toward the behavior, which he never received."},{"id":"U3-914","section":"3.9","skill":"Data Interpretation","difficulty":2,"stimulus":{"type":"line","title":"Mean maze errors per day","xLabel":"Day","yLabel":"Mean number of errors","series":[{"name":"Food every day","points":[[1,10],[2,8.5],[3,7],[4,6],[5,5],[6,4.2],[7,3.6],[8,3.2],[9,3],[10,2.8],[11,2.7],[12,2.6]]},{"name":"Never food","points":[[1,10],[2,9.5],[3,9],[4,8.6],[5,8.2],[6,7.9],[7,7.6],[8,7.4],[9,7.2],[10,7],[11,6.9],[12,6.8]]},{"name":"Food from day 7","points":[[1,10],[2,9.4],[3,9],[4,8.5],[5,8.1],[6,7.8],[7,7.5],[8,3],[9,2.8],[10,2.7],[11,2.6],[12,2.5]]}]},"stem":"Dr. Nakamura ran three groups of mice through the same maze once a day. One group found food at the end every day, one never found food, and one first found food at the end of the day 7 run. Which explanation best accounts for the sharp drop in errors for the third group on day 8?","options":["The food on day 7 acted as a primary reinforcer that taught the mice the whole maze in a single trial","The mice suddenly gained insight into the maze without having learned anything earlier","The mice had learned the layout of the maze on the unrewarded days, but showed it only once food gave them a reason to","The mice in the third group were copying the mice that had found food every day"],"answer":2,"rationale":"The third group's errors fell almost at once to the level of the always-rewarded group, which is too fast to be new learning from one reward. This shows latent learning: the mice had formed a cognitive map earlier and used it once there was an incentive."},{"id":"U3-915","section":"3.9","skill":"Data Interpretation","difficulty":3,"stimulus":{"type":"table","caption":"Number of the model's 15 building steps copied by each child","headers":["Child","Same-age model group","Adult model group"],"rows":[["1","6","2"],["2","7","3"],["3","5","15"],["4","8","2"],["5","6","3"],["6","7","2"]]},"stem":"Dr. Siddiqui showed children a video of either a same-age child or an adult building a tower using 15 specific steps. She then counted how many of the 15 steps each child copied. Which statement is best supported by the data in the table?","options":["The adult-model group had the higher mean, which suggests adults are more effective models for children","The two groups have the same range, so the variability of the scores does not differ","The adult-model group has a mode of 3, which is higher than its median","The median is a better summary of the adult-model group because of one extreme score, and both the mean and the median show more imitation of the same-age model"],"answer":3,"rationale":"The same-age group has a mean and median of 6.5, while the adult group has a mean of 4.5 and a median of 2.5; the score of 15 pulls the adult mean up, so the median describes that group better. Either way, children copied the more similar model more, as social learning theory predicts."},{"id":"U3-916","section":"3.9","skill":"Concept Recall","difficulty":1,"stem":"Edward Tolman used the term for a mental representation of the layout of an environment, such as a maze or a neighborhood. This term is","options":["prototype","cognitive map","insight","secondary reinforcer"],"answer":1,"rationale":"A cognitive map is a mental picture of a place's layout that can be formed through latent learning, as Tolman showed with rats exploring mazes. A prototype is the best example of a concept, which is a different idea."},{"id":"U3-917","section":"3.9","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Researchers recording the activity of single neurons in the frontal lobe of a monkey find that the same cells fire when the monkey picks up a raisin and when the monkey simply watches a researcher pick up a raisin. Neurons like these have been proposed as a neural basis for which kind of learning?","options":["Observational learning","Latent learning","Insight learning","Classical conditioning"],"answer":0,"rationale":"These are mirror neurons, which fire both when performing an action and when watching another individual perform it, and they are thought to help explain imitation and observational learning. Their role in humans is still being studied, but the link is to learning from models."},{"id":"U3-918","section":"3.9","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Mr. Al Nuaimi often tells his children that checking phones during family dinner is rude. However, he checks his own phone many times during every meal. According to research on social learning, what are his children most likely to do as they get older?","options":["Avoid using phones at dinner, because their father has repeated the rule so often","Develop learned helplessness about family meals","Check their phones during dinner, because children tend to imitate what models do more than what they say","Avoid phones at dinner, because they have experienced vicarious punishment"],"answer":2,"rationale":"Social learning research shows that when a model's words and actions conflict, children tend to copy the actions. No one has been seen being punished for phone use, so vicarious punishment does not apply."},{"id":"U3-919","section":"3.9","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":1,"stem":"Which of the following scenarios best illustrates latent learning?","options":["Bea receives a sticker each time she reads a page of her book, so she reads more pages","Chidi sees his brother praised for washing the dishes, so Chidi starts washing them too","Dana stares at a riddle for ten minutes and then suddenly sees the answer","Arash explores a maze game just for fun with no points; when the game later offers a prize for the fastest finish, he takes the shortest route at once"],"answer":3,"rationale":"Arash learned the maze without any reinforcement, and his knowledge became visible only once a prize gave him a reason to use it, which is latent learning. The other scenarios show positive reinforcement, vicarious reinforcement and insight learning."},{"id":"U3-920","section":"3.9","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":3,"stem":"A classmate claims, \"An animal cannot learn the layout of a place unless it is reinforced while it explores.\" Which research finding most directly refutes this claim?","options":["Rats fed at the end of a maze from the first day made fewer errors than rats that were never fed","Rats that explored a maze for days with no food ran it almost as well as rats fed all along, soon after food was first added at the end","Rats given food at random times developed odd, repeated movements that had no effect on the food","Rats that could not escape shocks in one cage later failed to escape shocks in a cage where escape was possible"],"answer":1,"rationale":"If unrewarded rats perform almost as well as rewarded rats as soon as food appears, they must have learned the layout without reinforcement, which is latent learning and directly contradicts the claim. The finding that fed rats made fewer errors is consistent with the claim, so it cannot refute it."},{"id":"U4-001","section":"4.1","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Layla captains her school's football team and speaks to reporters after every match. When the team wins, she talks about her own goals and her leadership on the pitch. When the team loses, she blames the referee's decisions and the bumpy condition of the pitch. Which of the following best explains Layla's explanations?","options":["Actor-observer bias","Self-serving bias","The just-world phenomenon","Belief perseverance"],"answer":1,"rationale":"Layla credits herself for the team's wins and blames outside factors for its losses, which is the self-serving bias. Actor-observer bias compares how people explain their own behavior with how they explain other people's behavior, which is not what Layla is doing."},{"id":"U4-002","section":"4.1","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Daniyal just won a regional chess tournament that included several highly ranked players. According to research on attributional biases, Daniyal is most likely to explain his victory by pointing to","options":["the fact that his opponents were having an off day","a few lucky mistakes made by the other players","the quiet room where the final was played","his own skill and long hours of preparation"],"answer":3,"rationale":"People tend to credit their successes to internal, dispositional causes such as ability and effort (the self-serving bias). The other explanations are external, situational causes, which people more often use for failures."},{"id":"U4-003","section":"4.1","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Tomas has just started at a new school. Based on research on how liking and friendships form, which of the following students is Tomas most likely to become close friends with by the end of the year?","options":["Ishaan, a former neighbor who now lives in another city and messages Tomas once a month","Haruto, a very popular student in Tomas's grade who has never shared a class with him","Bilal, who attends a different campus and whose hobbies are very different from Tomas's","Kwame, who is assigned the desk next to Tomas in most classes and shares his interest in robotics"],"answer":3,"rationale":"Repeated contact (proximity), which produces the mere-exposure effect, along with similar interests strongly predicts liking and friendship. Kwame is the only student with both frequent exposure and similarity."},{"id":"U4-004","section":"4.1","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"During the morning commute, Andrea sees a car stalled in the middle lane, causing a long traffic jam. According to the fundamental attribution error, Andrea is most likely to think which of the following?","options":["The extreme heat this morning probably drained the car's battery.","The driver may have braked suddenly to avoid debris on the road.","The heavy rain last night may have flooded the car's engine.","The driver is careless and never bothers to maintain the car."],"answer":3,"rationale":"The fundamental attribution error is the tendency to explain another person's behavior with personal traits while underestimating situational causes. Blaming the driver's carelessness is a dispositional explanation; the other choices point to the situation."},{"id":"U4-005","section":"4.1","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":1,"stem":"When Rania started a part-time job at a perfume shop, she disliked the smell of the shop's signature oud fragrance. After two months of smelling it on every shift, she likes it so much that she bought a bottle for herself. Rania's change in feelings is best explained by","options":["the mere-exposure effect","sensory adaptation","the foot-in-the-door technique","belief perseverance"],"answer":0,"rationale":"The mere-exposure effect is the tendency to like a stimulus more after repeated exposure to it. Sensory adaptation would make Rania notice the scent less, not like it more."},{"id":"U4-006","section":"4.1","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Mateo designed a simple phone app that became popular overnight after a famous influencer happened to mention it in a video. Mateo now tells reporters that the app succeeded because of his brilliant design instincts and business sense. Which of the following best describes Mateo's explanation?","options":["Situational attribution","Self-serving bias","Hindsight bias","Just-world phenomenon"],"answer":1,"rationale":"Mateo credits his own abilities for a success that was largely due to luck, which is the self-serving bias. A situational attribution would credit the influencer's mention."},{"id":"U4-007","section":"4.1","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"A basketball player misses a shot in the final second, and her team loses by one point. Which of the following statements is LEAST likely to reflect the self-serving bias?","options":["\"The referee ignored the defender who was pushing me.\"","\"The lights in this arena were glaring right in my eyes.\"","\"My teammate passed me the ball far too late.\"","\"I rushed my shot and need to work on my footwork.\""],"answer":3,"rationale":"The self-serving bias leads people to blame failures on external factors. Admitting that she rushed the shot is an internal explanation for failure, so it is the least self-serving response."},{"id":"U4-008","section":"4.1","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Students hear a rumor that a popular food truck outside their university is about to go out of business. Worried that they will lose their money, many students stop buying the truck's prepaid meal cards. Sales drop so sharply that the owner closes the truck a month later. Which of the following has most likely occurred?","options":["A self-fulfilling prophecy","The halo effect","The fundamental attribution error","Social loafing"],"answer":0,"rationale":"The false belief that the truck would close changed students' behavior, and that behavior caused the very closure they expected. That is a self-fulfilling prophecy."},{"id":"U4-009","section":"4.1","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"At the start of the term, a math tutor is mistakenly told that one of her students, Ahmed, scored in the top 1 percent on an aptitude test. In reality, Ahmed's score was average. The tutor gives Ahmed harder problems, waits longer for his answers and praises his effort often. By the end of the term, Ahmed's math grades have risen sharply. This outcome is an example of","options":["social facilitation","a self-fulfilling prophecy","actor-observer bias","the mere-exposure effect"],"answer":1,"rationale":"The tutor's false expectation led her to treat Ahmed in ways that produced the high performance she expected, which is a self-fulfilling prophecy. Social facilitation refers to performance changes caused by the presence of an audience."},{"id":"U4-010","section":"4.1","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Farah was not keen on Joon when he joined her weekend football club, because he seemed very quiet. After seeing him at practice twice a week for a whole season, Farah now enjoys his company and invites him to join her friends after matches. Farah's growing liking for Joon is most likely due to","options":["group polarization","social loafing","the self-serving bias","the mere-exposure effect"],"answer":3,"rationale":"Repeated contact with Joon increased Farah's liking for him, which is the mere-exposure effect. Group polarization involves opinions becoming more extreme after group discussion."},{"id":"U4-011","section":"4.1","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"set":"U4A-SWIM","stimulus":{"type":"passage","text":"Dr. Okafor, a sport psychologist, studied 10 members of a university swim team. Each swimmer completed an Attribution Questionnaire (scores from 10 to 50) about what they think determines their race results: their own training and effort, or outside factors such as luck, lane assignment and the officials. A low score means a swimmer mostly credits their own training and effort; a high score means the swimmer mostly credits outside factors. Dr. Okafor then compared each swimmer's score with the number of personal-best times the swimmer set during the season. Results (questionnaire score, personal bests): 14, 8; 18, 9; 21, 6; 25, 7; 28, 5; 32, 6; 35, 3; 39, 4; 43, 2; 47, 3."},"stem":"Based on the study described, a swimmer with a questionnaire score of 14 says after a fast race, \"All those early-morning sessions finally paid off.\" This swimmer's explanation is best described as","options":["a dispositional attribution","a situational attribution","the fundamental attribution error","the just-world phenomenon"],"answer":0,"rationale":"Crediting one's own effort and training is an internal, dispositional attribution, which fits the swimmer's low questionnaire score. The fundamental attribution error concerns explaining other people's behavior, not one's own."},{"id":"U4-012","section":"0.5","skill":"Data Interpretation","difficulty":2,"set":"U4A-SWIM","stimulus":{"type":"passage","text":"Dr. Okafor, a sport psychologist, studied 10 members of a university swim team. Each swimmer completed an Attribution Questionnaire (scores from 10 to 50) about what they think determines their race results: their own training and effort, or outside factors such as luck, lane assignment and the officials. A low score means a swimmer mostly credits their own training and effort; a high score means the swimmer mostly credits outside factors. Dr. Okafor then compared each swimmer's score with the number of personal-best times the swimmer set during the season. Results (questionnaire score, personal bests): 14, 8; 18, 9; 21, 6; 25, 7; 28, 5; 32, 6; 35, 3; 39, 4; 43, 2; 47, 3."},"stem":"Based on the study described, which correlation coefficient is most consistent with the relationship between Attribution Questionnaire scores and the number of personal-best times?","options":["+0.88","-0.06","-0.35","-0.90"],"answer":3,"rationale":"As questionnaire scores rise, personal bests fall fairly consistently, which indicates a strong negative correlation, so -0.90 fits best. A value of -0.35 would describe a weak negative trend with much more scatter, and a value near zero would mean no relationship."},{"id":"U4-013","section":"0.6","skill":"Data Interpretation","difficulty":2,"set":"U4A-SWIM","stimulus":{"type":"passage","text":"Dr. Okafor, a sport psychologist, studied 10 members of a university swim team. Each swimmer completed an Attribution Questionnaire (scores from 10 to 50) about what they think determines their race results: their own training and effort, or outside factors such as luck, lane assignment and the officials. A low score means a swimmer mostly credits their own training and effort; a high score means the swimmer mostly credits outside factors. Dr. Okafor then compared each swimmer's score with the number of personal-best times the swimmer set during the season. Results (questionnaire score, personal bests): 14, 8; 18, 9; 21, 6; 25, 7; 28, 5; 32, 6; 35, 3; 39, 4; 43, 2; 47, 3."},"stem":"Based on the study described, which of the following is the most accurate interpretation of the results?","options":["Believing that luck and officials determine race results causes swimmers to set fewer personal bests.","Swimmers who more strongly attributed their results to outside factors tended to set fewer personal bests.","Swimmers who set more personal bests trained more hours than swimmers who set fewer personal bests.","Attribution scores and personal bests were unrelated, because each swimmer trained a different amount."],"answer":1,"rationale":"The data show a negative correlation: higher external attribution scores go with fewer personal bests. Because this is a correlational study, it cannot show that the beliefs cause the poorer results, and training hours were never measured."},{"id":"U4-014","section":"4.1","skill":"Concept Recall","difficulty":1,"stem":"The tendency to give too much weight to personality and too little weight to circumstances when explaining why another person behaved a certain way is known as","options":["the self-serving bias","a situational attribution","the just-world phenomenon","the fundamental attribution error"],"answer":3,"rationale":"The fundamental attribution error is overestimating dispositional causes and underestimating situational causes of other people's behavior. The self-serving bias concerns how people explain their own successes and failures."},{"id":"U4-015","section":"4.1","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Before a group project begins, Elif hears from a friend that her new partner, Tariq, is arrogant. Elif speaks curtly to Tariq and challenges every suggestion he makes. Tariq becomes defensive and starts insisting that his own ideas are always best, which convinces Elif that the rumor was true. This situation best illustrates which of the following?","options":["Belief perseverance","Actor-observer bias","Self-fulfilling prophecy","Normative social influence"],"answer":2,"rationale":"Elif's expectation changed how she treated Tariq, and her treatment drew out the very behavior she expected. Belief perseverance would involve Elif clinging to the belief after clear evidence showed it was false, which is not described."},{"id":"U4-016","section":"4.1","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"At a busy restaurant, Mr. Al Mansoori watches a new waiter drop a tray of drinks and tells his family that the young man is clumsy and careless. He did not notice that another diner's bag strap was stretched across the aisle and caught the waiter's foot. Mr. Al Mansoori's judgment of the waiter is best described by which of the following?","options":["Situational attribution","Self-serving bias","The just-world phenomenon","Fundamental attribution error"],"answer":3,"rationale":"Mr. Al Mansoori explained the accident with personal traits (clumsiness and carelessness) and overlooked the situational cause (the bag strap), which is the fundamental attribution error. A situational attribution would have blamed the strap."},{"id":"U4-017","section":"4.1","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"In its annual report for a year of record profits, the leadership team of a sportswear company credits its own bold strategy and smart decisions. The next year, when profits fall sharply, the leadership team's report blames a weak economy, shipping delays and unusually warm winter weather. The leadership team's reports most clearly demonstrate","options":["the self-serving bias","the actor-observer bias","low self-efficacy","an external locus of control"],"answer":0,"rationale":"Crediting successes to one's own abilities while blaming failures on outside circumstances is the self-serving bias. An external locus of control would mean believing that outside forces control outcomes in general, but the leaders claim full credit for the good year."},{"id":"U4-018","section":"4.1","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Nadia was offered a summer internship after her interview. She tells her friends that she only got it because the interviewer happened to be in a good mood that day. Which concept best applies to Nadia's explanation?","options":["External locus of control","Internal locus of control","Self-serving bias","Optimistic explanatory style"],"answer":0,"rationale":"Nadia credits an outside factor (the interviewer's mood) rather than her own abilities for her outcome, which reflects an external locus of control. The self-serving bias would lead her to credit her own skills for a success."},{"id":"U4-019","section":"4.1","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":1,"stem":"A football coach is trying to understand how a referee decided that a player's foul was intentional rather than accidental. Which of the following theories would be most useful for the coach?","options":["Attribution theory","Cognitive dissonance theory","Drive-reduction theory","The elaboration likelihood model"],"answer":0,"rationale":"Attribution theory describes how people explain the causes of others' behavior, such as deciding whether an act came from the person's intentions or from the situation. Cognitive dissonance theory explains attitude change after inconsistent actions."},{"id":"U4-020","section":"4.1","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":1,"stem":"Which of the following examples best illustrates the mere-exposure effect?","options":["Grace orders a pistachio dessert because the menu describes it as the chef's award-winning recipe.","Ravi agrees to tutor a classmate for a whole term after first agreeing to help with a single homework sheet.","Omar disliked his school's new logo when it was unveiled, but after seeing it on banners every day, he now prefers it to the old one.","Lucia rates a new song more highly after learning that most of her classmates like it."],"answer":2,"rationale":"Omar's liking for the logo increased only because of repeated exposure, which defines the mere-exposure effect. Ravi's case shows the foot-in-the-door technique, and Lucia's shows conformity to her classmates' opinions."},{"id":"U4-021","section":"4.1","skill":"Concept Recall","difficulty":1,"stem":"When one person's expectations about another person lead the first person to act in ways that bring out the very behavior that was expected, the result is called","options":["confirmation bias","a self-fulfilling prophecy","belief perseverance","the halo effect"],"answer":1,"rationale":"A self-fulfilling prophecy occurs when expectations change our behavior toward someone, which in turn leads that person to behave in line with the expectations. Confirmation bias is only about seeking information that supports a belief."},{"id":"U4-022","section":"4.1","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Sami has always been a punctual, hardworking student. This week he arrived late to class three days in a row. A substitute teacher who has never met Sami concludes that he is lazy. Ms. Rahman, who has taught Sami for three years, instead suspects that something has changed at home or on his way to school. Ms. Rahman's explanation is best described as","options":["a dispositional attribution","a situational attribution","the fundamental attribution error","the self-serving bias"],"answer":1,"rationale":"Because Ms. Rahman knows that lateness is unusual for Sami, she looks for an external cause, which is a situational attribution. The substitute teacher's conclusion, not Ms. Rahman's, reflects the fundamental attribution error."},{"id":"U4-023","section":"4.1","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":1,"stem":"Which of the following best reflects an external attribution?","options":["Priya believes her high quiz score shows that she has a real talent for chemistry.","Diego thinks his neighbor shouts at his children because he is an angry person.","Salma says she was late to school because the bus broke down on the highway.","Kenji decides that a classmate who never shares notes must be selfish."],"answer":2,"rationale":"Salma explains her lateness with a cause outside herself (the broken-down bus), which is an external, situational attribution. The other people all point to internal causes such as talent, anger or selfishness."},{"id":"U4-024","section":"4.1","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"During an online class, a student named Yuki always keeps her camera off and rarely speaks. Several classmates decide that she is lazy and does not care about the course. They do not know that Yuki shares a small room and a single laptop with three younger siblings. Which of the following best explains the classmates' conclusion?","options":["Self-serving bias","Hindsight bias","Fundamental attribution error","Group polarization"],"answer":2,"rationale":"The classmates explain Yuki's behavior with personal traits while overlooking the situational cause (her crowded home setting), which is the fundamental attribution error."},{"id":"U4-025","section":"4.1","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"During a holiday sale at a crowded mall, a shopper pushing a stroller bumps hard into Yousef's shopping bags and hurries on without apologizing. Yousef tells his sister that the woman is rude and thinks only of herself. He did not notice that her toddler had just run off toward the escalator and she was rushing to catch him. Yousef's explanation for the shopper's behavior is most consistent with which of the following?","options":["Self-fulfilling prophecy","Fundamental attribution error","Out-group homogeneity bias","Social loafing"],"answer":1,"rationale":"Yousef attributes the shopper's behavior to her character instead of to the urgent situation, overestimating dispositional causes. That is the fundamental attribution error."},{"id":"U4-026","section":"4.1","skill":"Concept Recall","difficulty":1,"stem":"The fundamental attribution error occurs when individuals","options":["credit their own successes to ability while blaming their failures on circumstances","assume that people generally get the outcomes they deserve in life","give too much weight to personal traits and too little to circumstances when explaining the behavior of others","let one positive trait of a person shape their judgments of that person's other traits"],"answer":2,"rationale":"The fundamental attribution error is about explaining others' behavior with dispositions while underestimating the situation. Crediting one's own successes is the self-serving bias, and assuming people get what they deserve is the just-world phenomenon."},{"id":"U4-027","section":"4.1","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":1,"stem":"Ahmed and Paolo were assigned neighboring lockers at the start of the year and see each other several times every school day. By the end of the first term, they have become good friends. Which concept best explains the growth of their friendship?","options":["Mere-exposure effect","Social reciprocity norm","Social loafing","Deindividuation"],"answer":0,"rationale":"Frequent, repeated contact tends to increase liking, which is the mere-exposure effect. Nothing in the scenario describes them repaying favors, so the reciprocity norm does not fit."},{"id":"U4-028","section":"4.1","skill":"Data Interpretation","difficulty":2,"stimulus":{"type":"bar","title":"Explanations Given in Reviews of Late Deliveries","xLabel":"Type of explanation","yLabel":"Percentage of reviews","categories":["Dispositional","Situational"],"series":[{"name":"Reviews","values":[78,22]}],"yMax":100},"stem":"Dr. Mensah analyzed 500 online customer reviews written about late food deliveries in her city. She coded each reviewer's explanation for the lateness as either dispositional (for example, \"the driver is lazy\") or situational (for example, \"traffic was heavy\"). Based on the graph of her findings, which of the following concepts is being demonstrated by the reviewers?","options":["Self-serving bias","Optimistic explanatory style","Fundamental attribution error","Belief perseverance"],"answer":2,"rationale":"Most reviewers explained the drivers' lateness with personal traits rather than circumstances, which is the fundamental attribution error. The self-serving bias would apply to how reviewers explain their own outcomes, not a driver's."},{"id":"U4-029","section":"0.6","skill":"Data Interpretation","difficulty":2,"stimulus":{"type":"table","caption":"Pessimism Scores and Stress Symptoms","headers":["Participant","Pessimism score (1 to 10)","Stress symptoms in one month"],"rows":[["1","2","1"],["2","3","3"],["3","4","2"],["4","5","4"],["5","6","6"],["6","7","5"],["7","8","7"],["8","9","8"]]},"stem":"A researcher measured explanatory style in eight adults. Higher pessimism scores mean the person tends to explain bad events as permanent, personal and affecting all areas of life. She also counted each person's stress-related symptoms over one month. Which conclusion is best supported by the data in the table?","options":["People who explain bad events more pessimistically tend to report fewer stress symptoms.","Pessimism and stress symptoms are unrelated, because two participants do not fit a perfect pattern.","People who explain bad events more pessimistically tend to report more stress symptoms.","Becoming more pessimistic causes people to develop more stress symptoms."],"answer":2,"rationale":"As pessimism scores rise, the number of symptoms generally rises too, which is a strong positive correlation even though a couple of participants break the exact pattern. Correlational data like these cannot show that pessimism causes the symptoms."},{"id":"U4-030","section":"2.2","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"A week before the student council election, Leila told her friends that the race between Yaw and Daniela was impossible to call. After Yaw won by a wide margin, Leila said, \"I always knew Yaw would win. It was obvious from the start.\" Which of the following concepts does Leila's statement demonstrate?","options":["Self-serving bias","Confirmation bias","Hindsight bias","Belief perseverance"],"answer":2,"rationale":"Hindsight bias is the tendency to believe, after learning an outcome, that one would have predicted it. Leila was unsure beforehand but now claims she knew all along."},{"id":"U4-031","section":"4.1","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":3,"stem":"In a classic study by Darley and Latané, participants who believed that several other people could also hear a person having a seizure over an intercom were much slower to seek help than participants who believed they were the only listener. When students read about this study, many conclude that the participants who failed to help were simply selfish and uncaring people, and they overlook how strongly the situation shaped behavior. The students' mistaken judgment is best explained by","options":["diffusion of responsibility","the self-serving bias","groupthink","the fundamental attribution error"],"answer":3,"rationale":"The students blame the participants' character and underestimate situational forces, which is the fundamental attribution error. Diffusion of responsibility explains why the participants did not help, but not why readers judge them harshly."},{"id":"U4-032","section":"4.1","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":1,"stem":"Hessa is trying to work out why her coworker missed an important project deadline. Which of the following explanations would be a dispositional attribution?","options":["She is disorganized and does not plan her time well.","Her laptop crashed the night before the deadline.","Her manager assigned her three projects due the same week.","The office internet connection was down for most of the week."],"answer":0,"rationale":"A dispositional attribution points to a person's internal traits, such as being disorganized. The other explanations point to situational causes outside her control."},{"id":"U4-033","section":"4.1","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"A researcher gives first-year university students a questionnaire measuring locus of control and then follows them through their first semester. Based on research on locus of control, which of the following findings is she most likely to report?","options":["Students with an external locus of control kept working longer on difficult assignments.","Students with an internal locus of control were more likely to blame their professors for low grades.","Students with an internal locus of control used more active coping, such as making study plans, and reported fewer symptoms of depression.","Locus of control was unrelated to how students coped with stress."],"answer":2,"rationale":"Believing that one's own actions shape outcomes (an internal locus of control) is linked to active coping, persistence and lower rates of depression. Blaming professors for grades reflects an external locus of control, not an internal one."},{"id":"U4-034","section":"4.1","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"When Zainab receives her report card, she tells her parents that her top grade in art shows her natural creativity. In the same conversation, she says that her low grade in geography happened only because the geography teacher marks unfairly. Zainab's explanations best illustrate","options":["the fundamental attribution error","the actor-observer bias","the halo effect","the self-serving bias"],"answer":3,"rationale":"Zainab credits her success to a personal quality and blames her poor result on an outside cause. That pattern protects her self-esteem and is the self-serving bias."},{"id":"U4-035","section":"4.1","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":1,"stem":"Ikenna believes that whether he gets into his chosen university depends mainly on how hard he works and the choices he makes. Ikenna's belief best illustrates","options":["an internal locus of control","an external locus of control","self-actualization","a pessimistic explanatory style"],"answer":0,"rationale":"An internal locus of control is the belief that one's own actions control one's outcomes. An external locus of control would credit luck, other people or fate."},{"id":"U4-036","section":"4.1","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":1,"stem":"When her teammate Hana brought snacks for the whole volleyball team after practice, Precious thought, \"She did that because she is such a thoughtful person.\" Precious's explanation is best described as","options":["a situational attribution","a dispositional attribution","the just-world phenomenon","social facilitation"],"answer":1,"rationale":"Precious explains Hana's behavior with a personal trait (thoughtfulness), which is a dispositional attribution. A situational attribution would point to something like the coach asking Hana to bring snacks."},{"id":"U4-037","section":"0.2","skill":"Research Methods and Design","difficulty":2,"stem":"Ms. Delgado, the school counselor, wants to find out whether the proportion of students with an internal versus an external locus of control differs between Grade 10 and Grade 12 at her school. Which of the following research methods is most appropriate for her question?","options":["A case study of one Grade 10 student and one Grade 12 student","An experiment that randomly assigns students to Grade 10 or Grade 12","A questionnaire survey given to a large sample of students in both grades","Naturalistic observation of students during lunch breaks"],"answer":2,"rationale":"Locus of control is a belief that can be measured quickly with self-report questionnaires across many students, so a survey fits best. Grade level cannot be randomly assigned, and beliefs cannot be seen directly through observation."},{"id":"U4-038","section":"4.1","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":1,"stem":"Rafael loses an online racing game and immediately tells his friends that the app lagged at the most important moment. Rafael's explanation illustrates","options":["the fundamental attribution error","belief perseverance","an internal locus of control","the self-serving bias"],"answer":3,"rationale":"Blaming one's own loss on an outside factor protects self-esteem and reflects the self-serving bias. The fundamental attribution error involves explaining other people's behavior, not one's own."},{"id":"U4-039","section":"4.1","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"At first, Sofia found her new neighbor's cheerful greetings in the elevator awkward and a little annoying. After seeing him almost every morning for several months, she now looks forward to their short chats and considers him a friend. Which of the following concepts is most likely responsible for Sofia's change in opinion?","options":["Mere-exposure effect","Belief perseverance","Social facilitation","Self-fulfilling prophecy"],"answer":0,"rationale":"Repeated exposure to the neighbor over many months increased Sofia's liking for him, which is the mere-exposure effect. Belief perseverance would have kept her original negative opinion in place."},{"id":"U4-040","section":"4.1","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Tariq skims his notes for ten minutes and then fails the written theory portion of his driving test. Afterward, he tells his brother, \"Passing that test is just a matter of which questions the computer happens to give you.\" Tariq's judgment of his situation most clearly reflects","options":["an internal locus of control","the fundamental attribution error","hindsight bias","an external locus of control"],"answer":3,"rationale":"Tariq believes his outcome was determined by chance rather than by his own preparation, which reflects an external locus of control. The fundamental attribution error concerns how people explain other people's behavior."},{"id":"U4-041","section":"4.1","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"A bookshop manager notices that her newest cashier, Liam, made several mistakes during his first weekend shift. Which of the following thoughts by the manager is an example of the fundamental attribution error?","options":["\"The new register software crashed twice on Saturday.\"","\"Liam just is not a careful person.\"","\"Weekends are our busiest days, so most new staff make errors.\"","\"He has only had one short training session so far.\""],"answer":1,"rationale":"Blaming Liam's character while ignoring situational factors such as faulty software, heavy crowds or limited training is the fundamental attribution error. The other thoughts all point to the situation."},{"id":"U4-042","section":"4.1","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"After she is not selected for a national youth football squad, Ama thinks about why it happened. Which of the following thoughts best reflects an optimistic explanatory style?","options":["\"I am just not good enough, and I never will be.\"","\"The selectors had very few places this year, and I can work on my speed before next season's trials.\"","\"I fail at everything I try, not only football.\"","\"Players like me never get picked, so there is no point trying again.\""],"answer":1,"rationale":"An optimistic explanatory style explains bad events as caused by temporary, specific and often external factors. The other thoughts treat the setback as permanent, personal or affecting every area of life, which is a pessimistic style."},{"id":"U4-043","section":"4.1","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":3,"stem":"During a relay race, Diego fumbles the baton handoff and says it happened because the track was slippery. The following week, when his teammate Olivia fumbles a handoff on the same track, Diego says it happened because she is clumsy. Diego is demonstrating","options":["the self-serving bias","the halo effect","the just-world phenomenon","the actor-observer bias"],"answer":3,"rationale":"Diego explains his own mistake with the situation but explains the same mistake by someone else with a personal trait, which is the actor-observer bias. The self-serving bias alone would not explain why he judges Olivia's identical error differently."},{"id":"U4-044","section":"4.1","skill":"Concept Recall","difficulty":1,"stem":"The tendency for sports fans to develop a more favorable attitude toward a sports drink brand after seeing its logo on stadium billboards at many games is referred to as","options":["the central route to persuasion","a self-fulfilling prophecy","the social reciprocity norm","the mere-exposure effect"],"answer":3,"rationale":"Liking a stimulus more simply because it has been encountered repeatedly is the mere-exposure effect. The central route to persuasion relies on thinking carefully about arguments, which logos do not provide."},{"id":"U4-045","section":"4.1","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":1,"stem":"Mrs. Kapoor notices that her son's friend Yaw has visited their home three times without once looking up from his phone to greet her. She tells her husband that Yaw is a rude young man. The mental process Mrs. Kapoor used to explain the cause of Yaw's behavior is called","options":["social comparison","obedience","attribution","deindividuation"],"answer":2,"rationale":"An attribution is an explanation of the cause of someone's behavior. Mrs. Kapoor decided that Yaw's behavior was caused by his personality, without considering other possible reasons, such as an urgent message he was dealing with."},{"id":"U4-046","section":"4.1","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Before starting a part-time job at a juice bar, Mariam is convinced that her new coworkers will find her boring. During breaks she stays quiet and eats lunch alone in the storeroom. After two weeks, her coworkers rarely invite her to join them, and Mariam decides she was right about them all along. Mariam's experience is most representative of","options":["social loafing","a self-fulfilling prophecy","out-group homogeneity bias","deindividuation"],"answer":1,"rationale":"Mariam's expectation led her to withdraw, and her withdrawal produced the very response from her coworkers that she expected, which is a self-fulfilling prophecy."},{"id":"U4-047","section":"4.1","skill":"Data Interpretation","difficulty":2,"stimulus":{"type":"bar","title":"Mean Liking Rating by Number of Exposures","xLabel":"Number of times the logo appeared in the slideshow","yLabel":"Mean liking rating (1 to 7)","categories":["0","2","5","10","25"],"series":[{"name":"Mean liking","values":[3.1,3.6,4.2,4.8,5.3]}],"yMax":7},"stem":"In a laboratory study, Dr. Nakamura showed participants a rapid slideshow of made-up company logos that none of them had seen before. Each logo appeared 0, 2, 5, 10 or 25 times. Afterward, participants rated how much they liked each logo on a scale from 1 (dislike very much) to 7 (like very much). The results are shown in the graph. Which of the following best explains the pattern of results?","options":["The door-in-the-face technique","The mere-exposure effect","Sensory adaptation","Social facilitation"],"answer":1,"rationale":"Liking ratings rise steadily as the number of exposures increases, which is the mere-exposure effect. Sensory adaptation would make participants respond less to a repeated stimulus, not like it more."},{"id":"U4-048","section":"4.1","skill":"Data Interpretation","difficulty":3,"stimulus":{"type":"table","caption":"Percentage of Riders Who Agree with Each Statement","headers":["Statement","Company X riders (n = 120)","Company Y riders (n = 120)"],"rows":[["I can choose which shifts I work.","82%","21%"],["How much I earn depends mainly on how hard I work.","76%","30%"],["Whether I get a bonus is mostly a matter of luck.","18%","71%"],["I feel exhausted by my job in most weeks.","24%","68%"]]},"stem":"An industrial-organizational psychologist surveyed food-delivery riders at two companies about their jobs. The results are shown in the table. Which of the following concepts best accounts for the pattern of responses from Company Y riders?","options":["Social loafing","Optimistic explanatory style","External locus of control","Self-serving bias"],"answer":2,"rationale":"Company Y riders believe their shifts, pay and bonuses are controlled by luck or by the company rather than by their own actions, which reflects an external locus of control. Feeling a lack of control is linked to higher stress and burnout, which matches their higher exhaustion."},{"id":"U4-049","section":"4.1","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which of the following situations most clearly illustrates the mere-exposure effect?","options":["Elena feels anxious each time she passes the dentist's office where she once had a painful procedure.","Mei Ling found the smell of her grandmother's fermented tofu off-putting, but after it was served at almost every family dinner for a year, she now asks for second helpings.","Tunde buys a particular phone because a famous footballer appears in its advertisements.","Nour stops noticing the hum of the classroom air conditioner a few minutes after class begins."],"answer":1,"rationale":"Mei Ling's liking increased only because of repeated exposure, which is the mere-exposure effect. Elena's reaction is classical conditioning, Tunde's is the peripheral route to persuasion, and Nour's is sensory adaptation."},{"id":"U4-050","section":"4.1","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":1,"stem":"Which of the following is an example of a person who demonstrates an internal locus of control?","options":["A student signs up for extra coding workshops because she believes her own effort will shape her future career.","A runner believes his slow race time was caused by a strong headwind.","An employee believes that raises depend entirely on whether the boss happens to like you.","A shopper buys lottery tickets only on dates he considers lucky."],"answer":0,"rationale":"An internal locus of control is the belief that outcomes depend on one's own actions. The other people believe that outside forces such as wind, the boss or luck control what happens to them."},{"id":"U4-051","section":"0.5","skill":"Data Interpretation","difficulty":3,"stimulus":{"type":"bar","title":"Implicit Attitude Scores in Three Samples of First-Year Students","xLabel":"Implicit attitude score (1 = strong preference for own university's teams, 7 = strong preference for rival university's teams)","yLabel":"Number of students","categories":["1","2","3","4","5","6","7"],"series":[{"name":"Sample A","values":[0,2,8,20,8,2,0]},{"name":"Sample B","values":[5,6,6,6,6,6,5]},{"name":"Sample C","values":[0,1,6,26,6,1,0]}],"yMax":30},"stem":"A researcher measured implicit attitudes toward the sports teams of a rival university in three samples of 40 first-year students each. The frequency distributions are shown in the graph, and all three samples have a mean of 4. Which sample has the smallest standard deviation?","options":["Sample A","Sample B","Sample C","The standard deviations are equal, because all three samples have the same mean."],"answer":2,"rationale":"Sample C has the most scores packed at the center (26 of 40 students scored 4), so its scores vary least around the mean. Sample B is spread almost evenly across the scale, and having the same mean says nothing about how spread out scores are."},{"id":"U4-052","section":"4.2","skill":"Concept Recall","difficulty":1,"stem":"According to cognitive dissonance theory, people are motivated to","options":["reduce the discomfort caused by holding beliefs and actions that are inconsistent","reduce physiological drive states such as hunger and thirst","earn external rewards and incentives offered by others","keep their level of arousal at an optimal point"],"answer":0,"rationale":"Cognitive dissonance theory states that inconsistency between attitudes and actions creates discomfort, which people are motivated to reduce. The other options describe drive-reduction, incentive and arousal theories of motivation."},{"id":"U4-053","section":"4.1","skill":"Concept Recall","difficulty":1,"stem":"In social psychology, a stereotype is best described as","options":["a negative emotional reaction toward members of a group","unfair behavior directed at members of a group","a generalized belief about the characteristics of members of a group","a tendency to favor members of one's own group over members of other groups"],"answer":2,"rationale":"A stereotype is a generalized belief (a thought) about a group. A negative feeling is prejudice, unfair behavior is discrimination, and favoring one's own group is in-group bias."},{"id":"U4-054","section":"0.2","skill":"Research Methods and Design","difficulty":2,"stem":"Dr. Al Mansoori wants to understand how students feel about a new school rule that bans phones during lunch. She meets with 15 students one at a time, asks open-ended questions such as \"How did lunch change for you in the first week?\" and asks follow-up questions based on each student's answers. She records the conversations and later codes them for common themes. Which of the following measurement tools is Dr. Al Mansoori using?","options":["A Likert scale","A projective test","Naturalistic observation","An interview"],"answer":3,"rationale":"Asking participants open-ended questions face to face and recording their answers is an interview. A Likert scale would ask students to rate agreement on a fixed numbered scale."},{"id":"U4-055","section":"4.2","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Abdullah decided that his football club's new striker was a poor player after the striker missed two penalties in his first match. Over the rest of the season, the striker scores more goals than any other player in the league, yet Abdullah still insists that he is overrated and keeps bringing up the two missed penalties. Abdullah's continued opinion of the striker best illustrates","options":["a self-fulfilling prophecy","the mere-exposure effect","hindsight bias","belief perseverance"],"answer":3,"rationale":"Belief perseverance is holding on to a belief even after strong evidence shows it is wrong. Abdullah keeps his first impression despite a season of contrary evidence and focuses only on the facts that fit it."},{"id":"U4-056","section":"4.2","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Leon hears a sports-science report claiming that a certain morning stretching routine prevents running injuries. He starts the routine and spends weeks explaining to his teammates why it works. A month later, a respected journal announces that the original study was badly flawed and that the routine has no effect on injuries. Based on research on belief perseverance, Leon is most likely to","options":["accept the new report because it comes from a more careful study","forget the original claim, because the new information interferes with his memory of it","keep the belief only if his teammates also keep doing the routine","keep defending the routine and look for reasons the new report might be wrong"],"answer":3,"rationale":"Belief perseverance means people tend to keep a belief even after the evidence for it is discredited, especially after they have explained to others why it is true. The reasons Leon built while explaining the routine remain even when the original evidence is removed."},{"id":"U4-057","section":"4.1","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"When a large company announces layoffs, Martina says, \"People who lose their jobs must have done something to deserve it. Good workers never get let go.\" Martina's belief is best explained by which of the following?","options":["In-group bias","Cognitive dissonance","The just-world phenomenon","The self-serving bias"],"answer":2,"rationale":"The just-world phenomenon is the belief that people get what they deserve, which leads to blaming victims for their misfortune."},{"id":"U4-058","section":"4.2","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"To join her school's robotics team, Lina had to get through a three-week trial of early-morning sessions, difficult coding tests and blunt feedback from older members. Her friend Joy joined the school's photography club simply by signing a form. Months later, Lina rates her team as far more valuable and enjoyable than Joy rates her club, even though both groups meet for the same number of hours and do similar amounts of work. Which of the following best explains Lina's attitude?","options":["Positive reinforcement","Social facilitation","Cognitive dissonance","The mere-exposure effect"],"answer":2,"rationale":"Having endured so much to join, Lina reduces the dissonance between her effort and the group's ordinary value by deciding the team is especially worthwhile (effort justification). Both students spend the same time in their groups, so mere exposure cannot explain the difference."},{"id":"U4-059","section":"4.2","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Rasha used to believe that copying homework answers was dishonest. Over the past term she began copying answers from a group chat a few times a week. She now tells others, \"Homework does not really matter, and everyone does it anyway.\" Which of the following best explains Rasha's current belief?","options":["Group polarization made her original attitude more extreme.","Belief perseverance kept her original attitude unchanged.","Cognitive dissonance led her to change her attitude to match her behavior.","Social loafing reduced the effort she put into her own work."],"answer":2,"rationale":"When Rasha's behavior conflicted with her attitude, she reduced the discomfort by changing her attitude to fit what she was doing. Group polarization would have strengthened her original view rather than reversing it."},{"id":"U4-060","section":"0.3","skill":"Research Methods and Design","difficulty":3,"stem":"A researcher investigating cognitive dissonance asks 300 students to complete a questionnaire. One item reads: \"Responsible students make sure their study habits match their goals. Rate from 1 (not at all) to 7 (completely) how well your study habits match your goals.\" The researcher then correlates these ratings with students' stress scores. Which of the following is the most likely problem with this item?","options":["Because the item uses a numbered scale, it measures behavior directly rather than through self-report.","The wording signals which answer is desirable, so students may inflate their ratings and introduce self-report bias.","The leading wording makes students' self-reports more accurate and valid.","Wording problems can affect the results of experiments but not of correlational studies."],"answer":1,"rationale":"Linking a high rating to being a \"responsible student\" pressures participants to give socially desirable answers, which biases self-reports in any study that uses them. A numbered rating scale is still a self-report measure."},{"id":"U4-061","section":"4.1","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Throughout the school year, Victor often mocked younger students in the hallway. In June, he catches a bad flu and misses an important exam. A classmate who heard about it says, \"He got exactly what was coming to him.\" The classmate's comment best represents which of the following?","options":["In-group bias","Just-world phenomenon","Actor-observer bias","Belief perseverance"],"answer":1,"rationale":"The just-world phenomenon is the belief that good things happen to good people and bad things happen to bad people. The classmate assumes Victor's illness was a deserved punishment."},{"id":"U4-062","section":"0.3","skill":"Research Methods and Design","difficulty":2,"stem":"A researcher plans to interview residents of a coastal city about their attitudes toward a new public transport plan. She randomly selects participants from a complete list of all city residents. Drawing a random sample in this way primarily ensures that","options":["every neighborhood will be represented in exact proportion to its population","the researcher's expectations will not influence how she asks the questions","participants will answer the interview questions honestly","every resident has an equal chance of being selected for the study"],"answer":3,"rationale":"Random sampling means every member of the population has an equal chance of being chosen, which helps make the sample representative. Guaranteeing exact proportions for subgroups requires stratified sampling, and random sampling does not prevent experimenter bias or dishonest answers."},{"id":"U4-063","section":"0.2","skill":"Research Methods and Design","difficulty":2,"stem":"A widely cited study conducted in Brazil found that teenagers who volunteered regularly held more positive attitudes toward older adults than teenagers who did not volunteer. Dr. Nasser, a researcher in Dubai, wonders whether the same finding holds for teenagers in the UAE. Which of the following approaches would best answer his question?","options":["Repeat the original study's procedures with a sample of UAE teenagers","Conduct an in-depth case study of one UAE teenager who volunteers regularly","Reanalyze the original Brazilian data with a different statistical test","Ask UAE teenagers to predict whether they would respond the same way as the Brazilian participants"],"answer":0,"rationale":"Replicating the study with a new population tests whether the original finding generalizes to that population. A single case cannot represent UAE teenagers, and reanalyzing the Brazilian data says nothing new about teenagers in the UAE."},{"id":"U4-064","section":"4.1","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"While on a trip abroad, Marta learns that in the country she is visiting, the official weekend falls on different days from the weekend in her home country. She tells her travel group, \"Their calendar is backwards. They should do it the normal way, like we do.\" Which concept best explains Marta's statement?","options":["Out-group homogeneity bias","Self-serving bias","Belief perseverance","Ethnocentrism"],"answer":3,"rationale":"Ethnocentrism is judging other cultures by the standards of one's own culture and viewing one's own ways as correct. Marta treats her own country's calendar as the normal standard."},{"id":"U4-065","section":"4.1","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Rashid, a member of the school robotics club, describes the robotics members as \"a really varied mix of personalities.\" However, he says that the drama club members \"are all the same: loud and always wanting attention.\" Rashid's view of the drama club members best demonstrates","options":["in-group bias","out-group homogeneity bias","the just-world phenomenon","ethnocentrism"],"answer":1,"rationale":"Out-group homogeneity bias is seeing members of other groups as all alike while seeing one's own group as diverse. Rashid contrasts his varied in-group with an out-group he sees as uniform; he is not mainly claiming his own club is better, which would be in-group bias."},{"id":"U4-066","section":"4.2","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":3,"stem":"In a classic study by Festinger and Carlsmith, participants spent an hour on very dull tasks, such as turning pegs on a board. Each participant was then asked to tell the next waiting person that the tasks had been fun and interesting. Some participants were paid a small amount for saying this, and others were paid a large amount. Later, all participants rated how enjoyable the tasks had really been. Which result does cognitive dissonance theory predict?","options":["Participants paid a large amount rate the tasks as more enjoyable than those paid a small amount.","Participants paid a small amount rate the tasks as more enjoyable than those paid a large amount.","Both groups rate the tasks as equally enjoyable, because they did the same tasks.","Participants paid a large amount feel the most discomfort and change their attitudes the most."],"answer":1,"rationale":"Participants paid only a little had no good external reason for saying the tasks were fun, so they reduced dissonance by deciding the tasks really were enjoyable. A large payment fully justified the statement, so those participants felt little need to change their attitudes."},{"id":"U4-067","section":"4.2","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Mr. Qureshi, a hiring manager, sincerely believes that he judges all job applicants equally. An audit of his decisions over one year shows that he consistently rated applicants from his own former university higher than equally qualified applicants from other universities. When he is shown the pattern, he is genuinely surprised. Which of the following concepts best explains Mr. Qureshi's ratings?","options":["Normative social influence","Implicit attitudes","Social loafing","Belief perseverance"],"answer":1,"rationale":"Implicit attitudes are attitudes people hold without being aware of them, and they can still shape behavior. Mr. Qureshi did not realize he was favoring applicants from his own university."},{"id":"U4-068","section":"4.1","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which of the following is the best example of a stereotype, rather than prejudice or discrimination?","options":["Omar feels irritated whenever students from a rival school walk into the sports hall.","Omar assumes that any classmate who wears glasses must be good at math.","Omar refuses to let students from the grade below join his team.","Omar cheers more loudly for his own house team than for the other teams."],"answer":1,"rationale":"A stereotype is a generalized belief about a group, such as assuming that people who wear glasses are good at math. A negative feeling toward a group is prejudice, and refusing to include group members is discrimination."},{"id":"U4-069","section":"4.2","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":3,"stem":"After Sven spread an unkind rumor about a new classmate, he began noticing more and more faults in her and told his friends she was an unpleasant person anyway. Which of the following theories best explains Sven's change in attitude toward the classmate?","options":["The two-factor theory of emotion","Social facilitation theory","Drive-reduction theory","Cognitive dissonance theory"],"answer":3,"rationale":"Harming someone conflicts with seeing oneself as a decent person. Sven reduces the dissonance by deciding the classmate deserved poor treatment, so his attitude shifts to match his behavior."},{"id":"U4-070","section":"4.1","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Students in the science stream at a high school start a private group chat in which they often describe arts-stream students as \"not smart enough for real subjects.\" In class, however, the science students treat the arts students politely and include them in mixed projects. Which of the following best describes the science students' group chat comments?","options":["Discrimination, unfair treatment of members of an out-group","Prejudice, a negative attitude toward members of an out-group","Stereotype threat, worry about confirming a negative belief about one's own group","Social facilitation, improved performance in the presence of others"],"answer":1,"rationale":"The science students hold and share a negative attitude toward a group they do not belong to, which is prejudice. Because they treat the arts students fairly in class, their behavior does not show discrimination."},{"id":"U4-071","section":"4.1","skill":"Concept Recall","difficulty":1,"stem":"Which of the following tendencies is most directly explained by the just-world phenomenon?","options":["Assuming that people who suffer misfortune must have done something to deserve it","Feeling tense after acting in a way that goes against one's values","Putting in less effort on a task when working as part of a group","Overestimating how many other people share one's own opinions"],"answer":0,"rationale":"The just-world phenomenon is the belief that the world is fair, which leads people to blame victims for their suffering. The other options describe cognitive dissonance, social loafing and the false consensus effect."},{"id":"U4-072","section":"4.1","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"On the first morning of a summer camp, campers are randomly assigned to either the Blue team or the Green team. That afternoon, each camper is asked to divide 20 bonus points between a Blue camper and a Green camper, neither of whom they have met. Most campers give more of the points to the camper from their own team. This is an example of which concept?","options":["Social loafing","Deindividuation","Mere-exposure effect","In-group bias"],"answer":3,"rationale":"In-group bias is the tendency to favor members of one's own group. Even a random, brand-new group membership was enough to make campers favor their own team, and they had never met either camper, so mere exposure cannot explain it."},{"id":"U4-073","section":"4.2","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":1,"stem":"Ayumi strongly values honesty. One morning she oversleeps and tells her teacher she was late because she felt sick. Afterward, Ayumi is most likely to experience","options":["cognitive dissonance","relative deprivation","deindividuation","group polarization"],"answer":0,"rationale":"Acting against one of her core values creates an uncomfortable inconsistency between Ayumi's attitudes and her actions, which is cognitive dissonance."},{"id":"U4-074","section":"4.2","skill":"Data Interpretation","difficulty":2,"set":"U4A-GIFT","stimulus":{"type":"table","caption":"Mean Desirability Ratings (1 = not desirable, 10 = very desirable)","headers":["Water bottle","Rating before the choice","Rating after the choice"],"rows":[["Bottle the participant chose","6.2","7.9"],["Bottle the participant gave up","6.0","4.6"]]},"stem":"Dr. Lindqvist recruited 12 volunteers, all members of one university hiking club. Each volunteer rated eight reusable water bottle designs. The researcher then offered each volunteer a free bottle, but only as a choice between two designs that the volunteer had rated almost equally. Immediately after choosing, volunteers rated those two designs again. The table shows the mean ratings. These results are best explained by which of the following?","options":["Cognitive dissonance","The mere-exposure effect","The halo effect","Belief perseverance"],"answer":0,"rationale":"After choosing between two similar options, volunteers rated the chosen bottle higher and the rejected bottle lower, which reduces the dissonance of giving up an attractive option. Volunteers rerated the bottles immediately, with no extra exposure to the chosen one, so the mere-exposure effect cannot explain the change."},{"id":"U4-075","section":"0.3","skill":"Research Methods and Design","difficulty":2,"set":"U4A-GIFT","stimulus":{"type":"table","caption":"Mean Desirability Ratings (1 = not desirable, 10 = very desirable)","headers":["Water bottle","Rating before the choice","Rating after the choice"],"rows":[["Bottle the participant chose","6.2","7.9"],["Bottle the participant gave up","6.0","4.6"]]},"stem":"Dr. Lindqvist recruited 12 volunteers, all members of one university hiking club. Each volunteer rated eight reusable water bottle designs. The researcher then offered each volunteer a free bottle, but only as a choice between two designs that the volunteer had rated almost equally. Immediately after choosing, volunteers rated those two designs again. The table shows the mean ratings. Which of the following changes to the design would most improve the generalizability of the findings?","options":["Having volunteers rate the bottles a third time a week later","Giving every volunteer the same bottle instead of letting them choose","Telling volunteers the hypothesis before the study begins","Recruiting volunteers of different ages and backgrounds from several places instead of one hiking club"],"answer":3,"rationale":"Generalizability depends on how well the sample represents the wider population. A broader, more varied sample would let the results apply to more people than the members of a single university club."},{"id":"U4-076","section":"4.2","skill":"Research Methods and Design","difficulty":2,"set":"U4A-GIFT","stimulus":{"type":"table","caption":"Mean Desirability Ratings (1 = not desirable, 10 = very desirable)","headers":["Water bottle","Rating before the choice","Rating after the choice"],"rows":[["Bottle the participant chose","6.2","7.9"],["Bottle the participant gave up","6.0","4.6"]]},"stem":"Dr. Lindqvist recruited 12 volunteers, all members of one university hiking club. Each volunteer rated eight reusable water bottle designs. The researcher then offered each volunteer a free bottle, but only as a choice between two designs that the volunteer had rated almost equally. Immediately after choosing, volunteers rated those two designs again. The table shows the mean ratings. Dr. Lindqvist also wants to know whether volunteers hold implicit attitudes toward the brands printed on the bottles. Which of the following methods would best measure an implicit attitude?","options":["A questionnaire asking volunteers to rate how much they trust each brand","An interview asking volunteers to explain why they chose their bottle","A timed computer task measuring how quickly volunteers pair each brand's logo with positive or negative words","Asking volunteers to rank the brands from most to least favorite"],"answer":2,"rationale":"Implicit attitudes operate outside conscious awareness, so they are measured indirectly, for example through reaction times in a timed pairing task. Questionnaires, interviews and rankings all ask for conscious, explicit judgments."},{"id":"U4-077","section":"4.2","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Bella believes that wasting water is irresponsible, yet she regularly takes 25-minute showers. According to cognitive dissonance theory, if Bella keeps taking long showers, she is most likely to","options":["seek out more reports describing local water shortages","become even more convinced that wasting water is irresponsible","tell herself that her showers use very little water compared with what factories use","start taking even longer showers to prove the belief does not matter to her"],"answer":2,"rationale":"To reduce dissonance without changing her behavior, Bella is likely to add a thought that makes the behavior seem acceptable. Reading more about water shortages or strengthening her belief would increase her discomfort rather than reduce it."},{"id":"U4-078","section":"2.2","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Before buying a new phone, Kwabena has already decided that he wants the Model Z. When he searches online, he clicks on reviews that praise Model Z's camera and skips any reviews with titles mentioning its battery problems. He then tells his friends that \"every review agrees\" that Model Z is the best choice. Which of the following concepts best describes Kwabena's approach?","options":["Hindsight bias","Self-serving bias","Confirmation bias","Group polarization"],"answer":2,"rationale":"Confirmation bias is the tendency to seek out information that supports an existing belief and to avoid or discount information that challenges it. Kwabena reads only the reviews that fit his preference."},{"id":"U4-079","section":"0.3","skill":"Research Methods and Design","difficulty":2,"stem":"A phone-tracking app shows that a group of high school students spend an average of 5 hours a day on their phones. When the same students are asked in a face-to-face survey, they report an average of about 2 hours a day. Which of the following best explains the difference?","options":["Sampling bias","Social desirability bias","Confirmation bias","Hindsight bias"],"answer":1,"rationale":"Social desirability bias is the tendency to answer surveys in ways that make oneself look good. Because the same students provided both measures, sampling bias cannot explain the gap."},{"id":"U4-080","section":"4.1","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Before meeting the new members of her school's chess club, Dalia learns that three of them play on the school football team. She assumes they will be impatient and uninterested in careful strategy, saying, \"Athletes care about speed, not thinking.\" Dalia's assumption is an example of which of the following?","options":["A stereotype","Self-serving bias","Social facilitation","Belief perseverance"],"answer":0,"rationale":"A stereotype is a generalized belief about the members of a group, here based only on the fact that the new members play football. Belief perseverance would require Dalia to keep the belief after it was disproven, which has not happened yet."},{"id":"U4-081","section":"4.1","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":1,"stem":"Mr. Okoro believes that all teenagers are glued to their phones and cannot hold a real conversation. Which of the following concepts best represents Mr. Okoro's belief?","options":["Discrimination","Just-world phenomenon","Stereotype","Self-fulfilling prophecy"],"answer":2,"rationale":"A stereotype is a generalized belief about a group, in this case an age group. Discrimination would involve behavior, such as refusing to hire teenagers."},{"id":"U4-082","section":"4.2","skill":"Concept Recall","difficulty":1,"stem":"The uncomfortable tension people feel when they notice that two of their thoughts, or their thoughts and actions, are inconsistent, along with their motivation to bring them back into agreement, is called","options":["an approach-avoidance conflict","belief perseverance","social comparison","cognitive dissonance"],"answer":3,"rationale":"Cognitive dissonance is the discomfort produced by inconsistent cognitions and the drive to reduce it. An approach-avoidance conflict involves a single goal that has both attractive and unattractive features."},{"id":"U4-083","section":"4.2","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":3,"stem":"Bashir thinks of himself as a helpful neighbor. When his elderly neighbor asks him to help carry her groceries up the stairs, he says no, and afterward he feels uncomfortable. Which of the following shows Bashir reducing his dissonance by changing his attitude rather than his behavior?","options":["He goes back downstairs and carries the groceries for her.","He decides that being helpful to neighbors is not very important to him after all.","He tells himself that neighbors should always help one another, no matter how tired they are.","He apologizes to her the next morning and offers to carry her laundry down for her."],"answer":1,"rationale":"Lowering the importance of being helpful changes the attitude so that it no longer conflicts with the refusal. Helping her or apologizing changes behavior, and insisting that neighbors must always help keeps the conflict alive."},{"id":"U4-084","section":"4.3","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Six judges at a youth film festival each privately rate a short film as somewhat weak. After discussing the film together for an hour, during which every judge shares criticisms, the panel agrees that it is one of the weakest films ever submitted to the festival. Which of the following has most likely occurred?","options":["Groupthink","Diffusion of responsibility","Social loafing","Group polarization"],"answer":3,"rationale":"Group polarization is the strengthening of a group's dominant opinion after discussion among members who already lean the same way. Groupthink involves suppressing disagreement to keep harmony, but here the judges already agreed and simply became more extreme."},{"id":"U4-085","section":"4.3","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"A group of parents who are already somewhat worried about how much time their children spend on video games attend a two-day workshop. During the workshop, they discuss the issue only with one another. On a survey afterward, their concern is much stronger than before, and several parents who had favored simple time limits now support a complete ban. Which of the following processes best accounts for this change?","options":["Deindividuation","Social facilitation","Group polarization","Diffusion of responsibility"],"answer":2,"rationale":"Discussing an issue with people who share the same leaning pushes the group's views further in that direction, which is group polarization."},{"id":"U4-086","section":"4.3","skill":"Concept Recall","difficulty":1,"stem":"Which of the following best describes group polarization?","options":["Discussion among like-minded people pushes their shared view further in the direction they already leaned.","Group members hold back their doubts so that the group can reach agreement smoothly.","Individuals put in less effort when their work is pooled with others' work.","People lose self-awareness and self-restraint when they are anonymous in a crowd."],"answer":0,"rationale":"Group polarization is the intensification of a group's existing view through discussion. The other options describe groupthink, social loafing and deindividuation."},{"id":"U4-087","section":"4.3","skill":"Research Methods and Design","difficulty":2,"stem":"Dr. Chua conducted an experiment in which participants joined an online discussion about a controversial school rule. Participants were randomly assigned either to post under random usernames with no profile photos or to post under their real names with photos displayed. Participants in the username-only group posted an average of 4.2 insulting comments, compared with 1.6 for participants who could be identified. Which conclusion about deindividuation do these results best support?","options":["Being unidentifiable can reduce self-restraint and increase hostile behavior.","Discussing a controversial topic always makes group members' opinions more extreme.","People who can be identified feel more pressure to obey authority figures.","Online settings make people put less effort into group tasks."],"answer":0,"rationale":"The only difference between the groups was whether participants could be identified, and anonymity produced more insulting comments. Losing identifiability lowers self-awareness and personal responsibility, which promotes deindividuation."},{"id":"U4-088","section":"4.1","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"At a job fair, recruiter Ms. Bakshi meets an applicant, Andile, who greets her with a warm smile and a firm handshake. Without checking his references or test results, she rates him higher than the other applicants on honesty, reliability and technical skill. Which of the following concepts has most likely shaped Ms. Bakshi's ratings?","options":["Belief perseverance","The halo effect","Social facilitation","The actor-observer bias"],"answer":1,"rationale":"The halo effect occurs when one positive impression leads people to assume a person has other, unrelated positive traits. Andile's friendly first impression colors Ms. Bakshi's judgments of qualities she has no evidence about."},{"id":"U4-089","section":"4.1","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Kiran always sits in the front row and speaks very politely in Ms. Ibrahim's class. When Ms. Ibrahim grades a set of essays with students' names visible, she gives Kiran's essay a noticeably higher score than an almost identical essay written by another student. Which of the following concepts best explains Ms. Ibrahim's grading?","options":["Out-group homogeneity bias","The halo effect","Hindsight bias","Upward social comparison"],"answer":1,"rationale":"Ms. Ibrahim lets one positive impression of Kiran (his polite, attentive behavior) shape her judgment of an unrelated quality (the quality of his essay), which is the halo effect."},{"id":"U4-090","section":"4.1","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":3,"stem":"In an experiment, participants watched one of two videos of the same university lecturer answering student questions. In one video he answered warmly and patiently; in the other he answered coldly and impatiently. His accent, clothing and gestures were identical in both videos. Participants who saw the warm version rated his accent, appearance and gestures as more appealing than participants who saw the cold version. Which of the following best explains these ratings?","options":["The fundamental attribution error","The social reciprocity norm","The halo effect","The mere-exposure effect"],"answer":2,"rationale":"A positive impression of the lecturer's warmth spread to judgments of unrelated, unchanged qualities such as his accent and appearance, which is the halo effect. Both groups saw the lecturer for the same amount of time, so the mere-exposure effect cannot explain the difference."},{"id":"U4-091","section":"4.6","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"The manager of a large call center wants to improve both employee performance and job satisfaction. According to research in industrial-organizational psychology, which of the following approaches is most likely to achieve both goals?","options":["Increasing the number of supervisors who walk the floor checking on employees","Moving employees to a new building with brighter lighting","Letting teams help set their own schedules and giving regular, specific feedback on their progress","Replacing team goals with strict hourly call quotas for each employee"],"answer":2,"rationale":"Jobs that offer autonomy and useful feedback tend to increase motivation, performance and satisfaction. Closer monitoring and strict quotas raise stress and burnout, and a change of surroundings tends to have only short-lived effects."},{"id":"U4-092","section":"4.3","skill":"Concept Recall","difficulty":2,"stem":"In variations of Stanley Milgram's obedience studies, participants were LEAST likely to continue to the highest shock level under which of the following conditions?","options":["When they saw two other \"teachers\" (who were actually confederates) refuse to continue","When the experimenter stayed in the same room giving instructions","When the study took place at a prestigious university","When the learner was in a separate room and could not be seen"],"answer":0,"rationale":"Seeing other people defy the authority figure sharply reduced obedience in Milgram's research. Having the experimenter close by, a prestigious setting and a hidden learner all tended to increase obedience."},{"id":"U4-093","section":"4.3","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":1,"stem":"Based on research on obedience, which of the following requests is most likely to be followed?","options":["A stranger at a bus stop tells people to form a second line.","A uniformed police officer directs drivers to take a detour around a closed road.","A younger sibling tells an older sibling to turn off the television.","A new student tells classmates to switch seats before the teacher arrives."],"answer":1,"rationale":"People are most likely to obey directives that come from a legitimate authority figure, such as a uniformed police officer. The other people lack recognized authority over those they are directing."},{"id":"U4-094","section":"4.3","skill":"Concept Recall","difficulty":1,"stem":"Which of the following research questions would an industrial-organizational psychologist be most likely to investigate?","options":["Whether a new onboarding program reduces burnout among hospital employees","How infants' memory abilities change during the first year of life","Whether a new medication reduces symptoms of panic disorder","How teenagers' moral reasoning develops over time"],"answer":0,"rationale":"Industrial-organizational psychologists study behavior in the workplace, including training, management and burnout. The other questions belong to developmental and clinical psychology."},{"id":"U4-095","section":"4.3","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"A warehouse manager notices that when workers are given a single shared target for the whole shift, each worker packs fewer boxes than when each worker's output is tracked individually. Which of the following best explains the manager's observation?","options":["Social facilitation","Deindividuation","Social loafing","The social reciprocity norm"],"answer":2,"rationale":"Social loafing is the tendency to put in less effort when working toward a group goal in which individual contributions are not identified."},{"id":"U4-096","section":"4.3","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"At a late-night football match, a section of fans all wearing identical masks and face paint chant insults at the referee that most of them say they would never shout as individuals. This is an example of","options":["deindividuation","social loafing","the bystander effect","the foot-in-the-door technique"],"answer":0,"rationale":"Deindividuation is the loss of self-awareness and self-restraint in group situations that offer arousal and anonymity, such as a crowd of masked fans."},{"id":"U4-097","section":"4.3","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":1,"stem":"Musa pulls on a rope attached to a force meter. Pulling alone, he produces about 60 kilograms of force. When he pulls as one of eight team members in a tug-of-war, sensors on his section of the rope show that he produces only about 40 kilograms of force. Musa's drop in effort is best explained by","options":["social loafing","social facilitation","the self-serving bias","out-group homogeneity bias"],"answer":0,"rationale":"Social loafing is the tendency to exert less effort when working in a group than when working alone, especially when individual contributions are hard to identify. Social facilitation would predict stronger performance in the presence of others."},{"id":"U4-098","section":"4.3","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Mrs. Lopes, Fatima's new neighbor, spent an afternoon helping Fatima carry boxes into her apartment. The next day, Fatima feels that she ought to bake something for Mrs. Lopes as a thank-you. Which concept best explains why Fatima feels this way?","options":["The social responsibility norm","The foot-in-the-door technique","The social reciprocity norm","The mere-exposure effect"],"answer":2,"rationale":"The social reciprocity norm is the expectation that people should return help or kindness they have received. The social responsibility norm involves helping those who depend on us, without expecting anything in return."},{"id":"U4-099","section":"4.3","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":1,"stem":"Sofia, an experienced puzzle solver, completes a Rubik's Cube faster when her classmates gather around to watch than when she practices alone in her room. Researchers would explain Sofia's performance using which of the following?","options":["Deindividuation","Social facilitation","Informational social influence","Social loafing"],"answer":1,"rationale":"Social facilitation is improved performance on simple or well-practiced tasks in the presence of others. Social loafing would involve less effort in a group, not better performance in front of an audience."},{"id":"U4-100","section":"4.3","skill":"Data Interpretation","difficulty":3,"stimulus":{"type":"table","caption":"Percentage of Darts Hitting the Target Zone","headers":["Group","Throwing alone","Throwing while four people watch"],"rows":[["Experienced players","68%","77%"],["Beginner players","34%","22%"]]},"stem":"A sport psychologist records how accurately experienced and beginner darts players throw, first while practicing alone and then while four spectators watch closely. The results are shown in the table. Which of the following principles best accounts for these results?","options":["Social facilitation","Obedience","Social loafing","Groupthink"],"answer":0,"rationale":"The presence of others increases arousal, which improves performance on well-learned tasks but worsens performance on tasks that are not yet mastered. That is why experienced players improved while beginners got worse in front of spectators."},{"id":"U4-101","section":"4.2","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"A city council wants residents to support a new recycling program. Its brochure compares the program's costs with those of the current system, presents landfill data from three similar cities that adopted the program, and explains step by step how collection will work. The brochure relies mainly on","options":["the central route to persuasion","the peripheral route to persuasion","the door-in-the-face technique","the foot-in-the-door technique"],"answer":0,"rationale":"The central route to persuasion relies on strong evidence and logical arguments that the audience thinks about carefully. The peripheral route relies on surface cues such as attractiveness or celebrity endorsements."},{"id":"U4-102","section":"4.3","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":3,"stem":"Nikhil began learning a difficult juggling routine two days ago and still drops the balls often during practice. He performs the routine at the school talent show in front of a packed hall. Based on research on social facilitation, how is the audience most likely to affect Nikhil's performance?","options":["He will probably perform better than in practice, because an audience improves performance on every task.","He will probably perform worse than in practice, because the audience strengthens his still error-prone responses.","He will probably put in less effort, because the other performers share responsibility for the show.","The audience will probably have no effect, because the routine was planned in advance."],"answer":1,"rationale":"The presence of others increases arousal, which helps on well-learned tasks but hurts on new or difficult tasks. Because Nikhil has not mastered the routine, the audience is likely to make his mistakes more frequent."},{"id":"U4-103","section":"4.3","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"A children's charity mails potential donors a packet of free personalized address labels together with its request for a donation. Fundraisers find that donations are higher when the labels are included. Which of the following principles best explains why the free labels increase donations?","options":["The door-in-the-face technique","The central route to persuasion","The social reciprocity norm","Group polarization"],"answer":2,"rationale":"Receiving an unrequested gift makes people feel they should give something back, which is the social reciprocity norm. The door-in-the-face technique starts with a large request that is expected to be refused, which does not happen here."},{"id":"U4-104","section":"4.3","skill":"Concept Recall","difficulty":1,"stem":"An airline wants to evaluate how well its pilot selection interviews predict later job performance and whether its leadership training improves communication among flight crews. Which type of psychologist is most appropriate for this work?","options":["Industrial-organizational psychologist","Clinical psychologist","Developmental psychologist","Biological psychologist"],"answer":0,"rationale":"Industrial-organizational psychologists study hiring, training, leadership and performance in the workplace. Clinical psychologists assess and treat psychological disorders."},{"id":"U4-105","section":"4.3","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"A factory supervisor tells line workers to skip a required safety check so that the shift can meet its production target. Although several workers believe skipping the check is wrong, all of them follow the supervisor's instruction. Which phenomenon do the workers' actions best demonstrate?","options":["Conformity","Group polarization","Obedience","Diffusion of responsibility"],"answer":2,"rationale":"Obedience is complying with a direct order from an authority figure, even when it conflicts with one's own judgment. Conformity involves matching the behavior of peers without being directly ordered to."},{"id":"U4-106","section":"4.3","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":1,"stem":"Which of the following individuals best demonstrates social loafing?","options":["Jonah goes along with his teammates' plan even though he privately thinks it will fail.","Camila becomes louder and more impulsive when she is part of a large, cheering crowd at a concert.","During a class beach cleanup, Priya collects far less litter than usual because only the class's total will be reported.","Arjun waits for someone else to call for help after he sees a minor car accident on a busy street."],"answer":2,"rationale":"Social loafing is reduced effort on a group task when individual contributions are not identified. Camila's behavior reflects deindividuation, and Arjun's reflects diffusion of responsibility in the bystander effect."},{"id":"U4-107","section":"4.3","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":1,"stem":"Which of the following is an example of social facilitation?","options":["A student puts in less effort on a group poster than on posters he makes alone.","A worker smiles more at customers after his manager praises him for being friendly.","A shopper buys a gadget because many people around him are buying it.","A practiced pianist plays a familiar piece more smoothly when her family gathers to listen."],"answer":3,"rationale":"Social facilitation is improved performance on a well-learned task when others are present. Less effort in a group is social loafing, smiling more after praise reflects reinforcement, and buying because others do reflects conformity."},{"id":"U4-201","section":"4.3","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Six students are assigned a shared poster project, and the teacher will grade only the finished group poster, not each person's contribution. Compared with working on a solo poster, what is each student most likely to do?","options":["Put in less effort than they would on their own project","Perform a well-practiced skill faster because classmates are watching","Lose self-awareness and act in aggressive, antisocial ways","Become more willing to voice an unpopular opinion to the group"],"answer":0,"rationale":"When individual effort cannot be identified and only the group product is judged, people tend to slack off, which is social loafing. Social facilitation involves being observed while performing, not pooling effort into one shared product."},{"id":"U4-202","section":"4.3","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":1,"stem":"A psychologist hired by a hotel chain studies which supervisor styles reduce burnout among front-desk staff and how relationships within housekeeping teams affect job satisfaction. This psychologist most likely works in which specialty?","options":["Clinical psychology","Developmental psychology","Industrial-organizational psychology","Health psychology"],"answer":2,"rationale":"Industrial-organizational (I/O) psychologists study people at work, including management styles, team relationships, job satisfaction and burnout. Health psychologists focus on how behavior affects physical health rather than on workplace management."},{"id":"U4-203","section":"4.3","skill":"Concept Recall","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which statement best summarizes a key finding from Solomon Asch's line-judgment studies of conformity?","options":["Conformity rose steadily with every additional confederate, with no leveling off.","Conformity dropped sharply when even one other group member disagreed with the majority.","Most participants went along with the majority on every critical trial.","Participants conformed less as the line judgments became harder to make."],"answer":1,"rationale":"When the majority was no longer unanimous, because a single ally gave the correct answer, participants conformed far less. Only a small minority conformed on every critical trial, conformity leveled off after a majority of about three or four, and harder judgments tend to increase conformity rather than reduce it."},{"id":"U4-204","section":"4.3","skill":"Concept Recall","difficulty":1,"stem":"Which concept proposes that the anonymity and arousal of being in a large crowd can reduce self-awareness and self-restraint, leading people to commit antisocial acts they would avoid if they were alone?","options":["Diffusion of responsibility","Group polarization","Social loafing","Deindividuation"],"answer":3,"rationale":"Deindividuation is the loss of self-awareness and personal accountability in anonymous, arousing group settings. Diffusion of responsibility explains why people in groups fail to help, not why they act aggressively."},{"id":"U4-205","section":"4.3","skill":"Concept Recall","difficulty":1,"stem":"In Philip Zimbardo's 1971 study, college students were randomly assigned to play guards or prisoners in a mock prison, and the study was stopped early after some guards began treating prisoners harshly. The conclusion most often drawn from this study is that","options":["people with aggressive personality traits tend to seek positions of authority","competition between groups can be reduced by giving them a shared goal","group discussion pushes members toward more extreme views","social roles and situational pressures can strongly shape behavior"],"answer":3,"rationale":"Because roles were assigned at random, the harsh behavior was interpreted as a product of the situation and the assigned role rather than of the students' personalities. The study has also been criticized on ethical and methodological grounds, which is worth discussing alongside this conclusion."},{"id":"U4-206","section":"4.3","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"In Nadia's home culture, children are praised mainly for how their accomplishments bring honor to the family, and people often introduce themselves by their roles, such as \"the eldest grandchild of the Haddad family.\" Psychologists would describe this cultural pattern as","options":["collectivism","individualism","ethnocentrism","multiculturalism"],"answer":0,"rationale":"Collectivist cultures define the self through group memberships and value group achievement and harmony over personal achievement. Individualism would emphasize personal goals and unique personal traits instead."},{"id":"U4-207","section":"4.3","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Kenji, age 3, has just moved with his parents from a community where young children commonly call adult visitors by their first names to one where this is considered disrespectful and children are expected to use titles such as \"Auntie\" or \"Uncle.\" Kenji keeps greeting adult visitors by their first names. Which prediction is most consistent with research on social norms?","options":["His parents will attribute his behavior to his personality, showing the fundamental attribution error.","His parents will likely begin teaching him to use titles, because the new community's norms define what is expected of children.","His habit will grow stronger through group polarization as he meets more adults.","He will stop on his own because of social facilitation whenever visitors are present."],"answer":1,"rationale":"Social norms define the roles and expectations a community holds for its members, so caregivers usually shape a child's behavior to fit the norms of the community they live in. Group polarization, social facilitation and the fundamental attribution error do not explain adjusting to a new community's expectations."},{"id":"U4-208","section":"4.3","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":1,"stem":"On his first day at a school in Abu Dhabi, Tomas, a new student from Argentina, stays seated when the teacher walks into the classroom, while every one of his classmates stands up. Several classmates glance at him and frown. Which explanation best accounts for the classmates' reaction?","options":["The classmates were displacing frustration from an earlier lesson onto Tomas.","The classmates were showing the self-serving bias.","Tomas broke a social norm shared by his new class.","The classmates were experiencing deindividuation."],"answer":2,"rationale":"Social norms are shared expectations for how group members should behave, and people who break them often receive disapproval. Nothing in the scenario suggests misdirected anger, a bias about the classmates' own successes, or a loss of self-awareness in an anonymous crowd."},{"id":"U4-209","section":"4.3","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which pattern of behavior is best explained by a group norm?","options":["Most teenagers grow several centimeters between the ages of 12 and 15.","People blink more often when the air in a room is very dry.","New employees at a cafe quickly learn to keep their phones out of sight at the counter, even though no written rule requires it.","An experienced runner records faster times on a familiar course when spectators line the route."],"answer":2,"rationale":"A group norm is an unwritten expectation shared by group members, and the cafe staff follow one even without a formal rule. Growth and blinking are biological, and faster running in front of spectators is social facilitation."},{"id":"U4-210","section":"0.2","skill":"Research Methods and Design","difficulty":2,"set":"u4b_homeaway","stimulus":{"type":"bar","title":"Number of Wins at Home and Away, by Team","xLabel":"Team","yLabel":"Number of wins","categories":["Volleyball","Cricket","Handball"],"series":[{"name":"Home matches","values":[15,12,7]},{"name":"Away matches","values":[9,10,9]}]},"stem":"Dr. Mensah, a sports psychologist, attended every match played by three teams at a secondary school during one season. Without interacting with the players, she recorded whether each match was played at home or away and whether the team won. Her results are shown in the graph. Which research design did Dr. Mensah use?","options":["A true experiment","A case study","A cross-sectional study","A naturalistic observation"],"answer":3,"rationale":"Dr. Mensah recorded behavior as it naturally occurred, without manipulating any variable or interacting with the players, which is naturalistic observation. It is not an experiment because she did not randomly assign teams to play at home or away."},{"id":"U4-211","section":"0.3","skill":"Research Methods and Design","difficulty":2,"set":"u4b_homeaway","stimulus":{"type":"bar","title":"Number of Wins at Home and Away, by Team","xLabel":"Team","yLabel":"Number of wins","categories":["Volleyball","Cricket","Handball"],"series":[{"name":"Home matches","values":[15,12,7]},{"name":"Away matches","values":[9,10,9]}]},"stem":"Dr. Mensah, a sports psychologist, attended every match played by three teams at a secondary school during one season. Without interacting with the players, she recorded whether each match was played at home or away and whether the team won. Her results are shown in the graph. Which hypothesis is Dr. Mensah most likely investigating?","options":["Volleyball players have higher self-efficacy than cricket players.","Performing in front of a supportive home crowd improves a team's performance.","Teams that train more hours each week win more matches.","Teams with more experienced coaches win more matches overall."],"answer":1,"rationale":"Comparing home and away wins tests whether a friendly audience improves performance, a form of social facilitation. Her data do not include training hours, coaching experience or self-efficacy."},{"id":"U4-212","section":"0.5","skill":"Data Interpretation","difficulty":3,"set":"u4b_homeaway","stimulus":{"type":"bar","title":"Number of Wins at Home and Away, by Team","xLabel":"Team","yLabel":"Number of wins","categories":["Volleyball","Cricket","Handball"],"series":[{"name":"Home matches","values":[15,12,7]},{"name":"Away matches","values":[9,10,9]}]},"stem":"Dr. Mensah, a sports psychologist, attended every match played by three teams at a secondary school during one season. Without interacting with the players, she recorded whether each match was played at home or away and whether the team won. Her results are shown in the graph. Which statement about the graph is accurate?","options":["Every team won more matches at home than away.","The teams won more matches away in total than at home.","The volleyball team shows the largest gap between its home wins and its away wins.","The median number of away wins across the three teams is 10."],"answer":2,"rationale":"Volleyball's gap is 6 (15 home, 9 away), compared with 2 for cricket and 2 for handball. Handball won more away matches than home matches, the teams won 34 home matches versus 28 away in total, and away wins of 9, 9 and 10 have a median of 9."},{"id":"U4-213","section":"0.2","skill":"Research Methods and Design","difficulty":2,"stem":"Dr. Alvarez wants to find out whether shoppers are more likely to leave trash on tables in crowded food courts than in quiet ones. She plans to watch shoppers discreetly at several malls without interfering. What is the main advantage of this approach?","options":["It allows her to conclude that crowd size causes littering.","It lets her randomly assign shoppers to crowded or quiet food courts.","Behavior is recorded in a real-world setting, so it is less artificial than behavior in a laboratory.","It removes the need to define littering in measurable terms."],"answer":2,"rationale":"Naturalistic observation captures behavior as it normally happens, which reduces the artificiality of lab settings. Because nothing is manipulated or randomly assigned, it cannot establish cause and effect."},{"id":"U4-214","section":"0.3","skill":"Research Methods and Design","difficulty":2,"stem":"Psychology students test the bystander effect at their school. A confederate drops a large stack of papers in a hallway once when two students are nearby and again, at the same spot and time of day, when about fifteen students are nearby. Observers record how many seconds pass before someone helps. What is the independent variable?","options":["The number of seconds before someone helps","The number of students near the confederate","The hallway where the papers were dropped","The size of the stack of papers"],"answer":1,"rationale":"The independent variable is what the researchers vary on purpose, here the number of students present. The time until help is the dependent variable, and the location and papers are kept constant."},{"id":"U4-215","section":"0.3","skill":"Research Methods and Design","difficulty":1,"stem":"Dr. Ahn wants to know whether pianists make fewer mistakes when playing a well-practiced piece for an audience than when playing alone. In her experiment, the number of mistakes each pianist makes is the","options":["independent variable","confounding variable","experimental group","dependent variable"],"answer":3,"rationale":"The dependent variable is the outcome the researcher measures, here the number of mistakes. Whether an audience is present is the independent variable."},{"id":"U4-216","section":"0.2","skill":"Research Methods and Design","difficulty":2,"stem":"Dr. Okafor, an industrial-organizational psychologist, is hired by a delivery company to test the hypothesis that a two-day teamwork workshop increases the number of parcels warehouse teams sort per hour. Which plan would best allow her to conclude that the workshop itself causes any improvement?","options":["Randomly assign 20 warehouse teams either to attend the workshop or to continue normal work, then compare parcels sorted per hour","Ask the teams who volunteered for the workshop whether they feel they now work faster","Compare the company's average sorting speed with that of a rival delivery company","Interview the single fastest team in depth about how its members work together"],"answer":0,"rationale":"Only random assignment to a workshop group and a comparison group rules out preexisting differences, so a difference in sorting speed can be credited to the workshop. Volunteers' self-reports add selection and response bias, and the other plans have no proper comparison group."},{"id":"U4-217","section":"4.3","skill":"Concept Recall","difficulty":1,"stem":"Stanley Milgram's obedience studies are best summarized by which finding?","options":["Most participants refused to continue as soon as the learner first complained.","Only participants with aggressive personalities obeyed until the end.","Participants obeyed more when the experimenter gave orders by phone from another room.","A majority of participants followed an experimenter's instructions to give what they believed were dangerous shocks."],"answer":3,"rationale":"About two-thirds of participants in the original study continued to the highest shock level when instructed by an authority figure. Obedience actually dropped when the experimenter was not physically present."},{"id":"U4-218","section":"0.3","skill":"Research Methods and Design","difficulty":1,"stem":"A researcher follows the same 35 students from one school from age 10 to age 18, asking them each year how strongly they feel pressure to conform to their peers. The 35 students are best described as the study's","options":["sample","population","independent variable","experimental condition"],"answer":0,"rationale":"A sample is the group of people actually studied, drawn from a larger population. The population would be all the students the researcher hopes to draw conclusions about."},{"id":"U4-219","section":"0.3","skill":"Research Methods and Design","difficulty":2,"stem":"To learn how students at her 1,200-student school feel about peer pressure, Leila posts a survey only in the online group chat of the debate club, which she leads. Her results are most likely limited by","options":["the absence of a double-blind procedure","sampling bias","the use of operational definitions","an error in random assignment"],"answer":1,"rationale":"Surveying only debate club members means the sample does not represent the whole student body, which is sampling bias. Double-blind procedures and random assignment apply to experiments, not to a survey like this one."},{"id":"U4-220","section":"0.3","skill":"Research Methods and Design","difficulty":2,"stem":"A marketing team wants to know whether shoppers across the country are more persuaded by celebrity endorsements than by product facts. To generalize their results to all shoppers in the country, the team should","options":["test only shoppers at the largest mall in the capital city","use a double-blind procedure","select participants who reflect the age, region and income mix of the country's shoppers","conduct a case study of one very loyal customer"],"answer":2,"rationale":"Results can be generalized only when the sample is representative of the population of interest. A double-blind procedure reduces bias in an experiment but does not make a sample representative."},{"id":"U4-221","section":"0.3","skill":"Concept Recall","difficulty":1,"stem":"Dr. Kamau designed a kindness program that worked well at one university, and she wants to know whether it would work in the wider community. For findings from a sample to be generalized to a population, the sample must above all be","options":["representative of that population","larger than 100 people","made up of highly motivated volunteers","drawn from people who already behave prosocially"],"answer":0,"rationale":"A representative sample mirrors the important characteristics of the population, so results can be generalized. A large sample that is unrepresentative is still biased."},{"id":"U4-222","section":"0.2","skill":"Research Methods and Design","difficulty":1,"stem":"Before launching a campaign, the student council wants to find out what most of the school's 900 students think about a proposed change to the school uniform. Which method best fits this goal?","options":["An in-depth case study of the council president","A naturalistic observation of what students wear","A survey given to a random sample of students","An experiment in which half the students wear the new uniform"],"answer":2,"rationale":"Surveys are designed to collect opinions and attitudes from many people efficiently. Observing clothing would show behavior, not what students think about the change."},{"id":"U4-223","section":"0.4","skill":"Concept Recall","difficulty":2,"stem":"After controversy over mid-20th-century studies in which participants were misled about what was really happening to them, ethical guidelines for psychological research came to require which of the following?","options":["Participants must be paid for every hour they spend in a study.","Deception may never be used in any research study.","Only animals may be used in studies that involve stress.","If deception is used, participants must be debriefed about the true purpose as soon as possible."],"answer":3,"rationale":"Current guidelines allow deception only when it is justified and require a debriefing that reveals the true purpose afterward. Deception is not banned outright, which is why debriefing is so important."},{"id":"U4-224","section":"0.4","skill":"Concept Recall","difficulty":1,"stem":"Which statement best describes current American Psychological Association (APA) ethical standards for research?","options":["They apply only to research conducted in clinical settings.","They include guidelines that protect both human participants and animal subjects.","They protect human participants, but animal research is not addressed.","They allow any procedure as long as the results are published."],"answer":1,"rationale":"The APA has ethical codes covering research with human participants and separate standards for the humane care and use of animals. These guidelines apply to research in all settings."},{"id":"U4-225","section":"0.2","skill":"Research Methods and Design","difficulty":2,"stem":"With approval from an ethics board, Dr. Nkemelu's team studies honesty at a shopping mall. A team member dressed as an ordinary shopper walks ahead of a passerby and pretends to accidentally drop a wallet containing a contact card, while a hidden observer records whether the passerby picks up the wallet and returns it. In this study, the team member who drops the wallet is best described as","options":["a participant in the experimental group","the dependent variable","a confederate","a participant in the control group"],"answer":2,"rationale":"A confederate is a member of the research team who pretends to be an ordinary person to create the situation being studied. The passerby is the participant, and whether the wallet is returned is the dependent variable."},{"id":"U4-226","section":"0.4","skill":"Research Methods and Design","difficulty":2,"stem":"Dr. Chen runs a version of Asch's line-judgment task at a university. Participants are told they are in a study of visual perception, and they do not know that the other people in the room are confederates instructed to give wrong answers. Which ethical issue does this design most clearly raise?","options":["A risk of serious physical harm, which requires medical staff to be present","A conflict of interest, because the researcher is employed by the university","A lack of random assignment, which makes the study unethical","The use of deception, which requires a full debriefing at the end of the session"],"answer":3,"rationale":"Hiding the true purpose and the confederates' role is deception, which is allowed only when justified and must be followed by a debriefing. The task poses no physical risk, and random assignment is a design issue rather than an ethical one."},{"id":"U4-227","section":"0.4","skill":"Research Methods and Design","difficulty":2,"stem":"Dr. Farouk plans a study of obedience in which an experimenter instructs participants to keep reading loud, distracting announcements while another person, secretly a confederate, tries to finish a timed memory test, even after the confederate asks them to stop. Her goal is to identify which factors increase obedience to authority. Which element is required for the study to be ethical?","options":["A debriefing at the end that explains the real purpose and the confederate's role","A requirement that participants complete every trial once they begin","A plan to publish each participant's name alongside how long they obeyed","A rule that only psychology students may take part"],"answer":0,"rationale":"Because the study uses deception and may make participants uncomfortable, they must be debriefed afterward. Forcing participants to finish would violate their right to withdraw, and publishing names would break confidentiality."},{"id":"U4-228","section":"0.4","skill":"Research Methods and Design","difficulty":1,"stem":"Dr. Iqbal wants to test whether people wearing headphones are less likely to help a stranger who drops a bag of groceries at a busy outdoor market. While planning, Dr. Iqbal worries about whether participants have agreed to take part. A concern about informed consent relates most directly to which aspect of research?","options":["Reliability of the measurement","Operational definition of variables","Ethical treatment of participants","Random assignment to conditions"],"answer":2,"rationale":"Informed consent is an ethical requirement that protects participants' right to choose whether to take part. Reliability, operational definitions and random assignment concern the quality of the measures and design, not participant rights."},{"id":"U4-229","section":"0.4","skill":"Research Methods and Design","difficulty":2,"stem":"Dr. Santos invites volunteers at a community center to take what she calls a \"reaction-time test.\" In reality, she is measuring whether they keep doing a dull, repetitive card-sorting task for 30 minutes when an experimenter in a lab coat tells them, \"The study requires that you continue.\" Several participants ask to stop partway through. Which statement about the ethics of this study is most accurate?","options":["Participants must be allowed to stop when they ask, and everyone must be debriefed about the real purpose afterward.","Because the volunteers signed up willingly, the experimenter may require them to finish the task.","Because the task is boring rather than painful, informed consent is not needed.","Because the study involves deception, no ethics board could approve it."],"answer":0,"rationale":"Participants always keep the right to withdraw, and a study that misleads participants about its purpose must end with a debriefing. Deception can be approved when it is justified, and informed consent is required even for low-risk tasks."},{"id":"U4-230","section":"0.4","skill":"Research Methods and Design","difficulty":1,"stem":"Participants are told they are testing a new video game, but the researcher is actually measuring how they react when a confederate insults them. Which procedure, carried out after the study, is used to reduce problems caused by this deception?","options":["Counterbalancing","Debriefing","Replication with a new sample","Random assignment"],"answer":1,"rationale":"Debriefing reveals the true purpose of the study and the use of deception, and it helps address any distress participants felt. Counterbalancing, replication and random assignment strengthen a design but do not undo deception."},{"id":"U4-231","section":"4.3","skill":"Data Interpretation","difficulty":2,"stimulus":{"type":"bar","title":"Average Pulling Force per Person in a Rope-Pulling Task","xLabel":"Number of people pulling together","yLabel":"Force per person (% of solo effort)","categories":["1","2","4","8"],"series":[{"name":"Force per person","values":[100,89,76,64]}],"yMax":100},"stem":"In a study, blindfolded participants pulled on a rope either alone or in teams, and the researchers measured how hard each person pulled. Which concept does the pattern in the graph illustrate?","options":["Social loafing","Social facilitation","Deindividuation","Groupthink"],"answer":0,"rationale":"Each person's effort falls as the team grows, which is social loafing: people exert less effort when their individual contribution is pooled with others. Social facilitation would show improved performance in the presence of others."},{"id":"U4-232","section":"4.3","skill":"Data Interpretation","difficulty":2,"stimulus":{"type":"line","title":"Words Written per Person on a Shared Report","xLabel":"Number of people on the team","yLabel":"Average words written per person","series":[{"name":"Only team total credited","points":[[1,600],[2,540],[3,470],[4,420],[5,380],[6,350]]},{"name":"Each person's work credited","points":[[1,600],[2,595],[3,600],[4,590],[5,598],[6,594]]}]},"stem":"Students wrote sections of a shared report in teams of different sizes. In one condition, only the team's total was credited; in the other, each person's section was credited separately. Which principle is best represented in the graph?","options":["Deindividuation","Conformity","Social facilitation","Social loafing"],"answer":3,"rationale":"Effort per person drops as teams grow only when individual work is not identifiable, which is the hallmark of social loafing. When each person's contribution was credited, effort stayed level."},{"id":"U4-233","section":"4.3","skill":"Data Interpretation","difficulty":2,"stimulus":{"type":"line","title":"Conformity by Size of the Unanimous Majority (Hypothetical Replication)","xLabel":"Number of people giving the same wrong answer","yLabel":"Percentage of trials on which participant conformed","series":[{"name":"Conformity","points":[[1,3],[2,13],[3,31],[4,35],[5,34],[6,35],[7,34],[8,35]]}]},"stem":"A research team repeated a line-judgment conformity task, varying how many confederates unanimously gave the same wrong answer before the participant responded. Which is the best interpretation of the results in the graph?","options":["Conformity rises steadily with every additional person in the majority.","Conformity rises as the majority grows to about three or four people and then levels off.","Participants conformed on most trials once the majority reached four people.","Conformity decreases once the majority grows beyond two people."],"answer":1,"rationale":"The line climbs from one to about four confederates and then stays flat near 35 percent, so adding more people has little extra effect, and conformity never reaches a majority of trials. This matches the classic finding that conformity levels off after a small majority."},{"id":"U4-234","section":"4.3","skill":"Data Interpretation","difficulty":1,"stimulus":{"type":"bar","title":"Participants Who Helped Within Two Minutes","xLabel":"Number of other people present","yLabel":"Percentage who helped","categories":["0","1","4"],"series":[{"name":"Helped","values":[85,62,31]}],"yMax":100},"stem":"Participants heard someone in the next room fall and cry out in pain. The graph shows how often participants helped depending on how many other people were present. Which concept does the graph illustrate?","options":["Social facilitation","The reciprocity norm","The bystander effect","The false consensus effect"],"answer":2,"rationale":"Helping becomes less likely as more people are present, which is the bystander effect. The reciprocity norm concerns returning favors, not the number of onlookers."},{"id":"U4-235","section":"4.2","skill":"Data Interpretation","difficulty":2,"stimulus":{"type":"table","caption":"New Gym Memberships Signed per Month","headers":["Month","Reem's memberships","Pablo's memberships"],"rows":[["January","42","25"],["February","38","29"],["March","45","22"],["April","40","27"]]},"stem":"Reem and Pablo sell gym memberships. Reem first asks each visitor to commit to a two-year premium membership; when the visitor refuses, she offers a three-month basic plan. Pablo hangs posters of a famous athlete around the gym and lets visitors decide on their own. Based on the table, which persuasion technique was the more successful strategy?","options":["The foot-in-the-door technique","The peripheral route to persuasion","The door-in-the-face technique","The central route to persuasion"],"answer":2,"rationale":"Reem signed more members every month, and her method of following a large refused request with a smaller one is the door-in-the-face technique. Pablo's celebrity posters use the peripheral route, which produced fewer sign-ups."},{"id":"U4-236","section":"0.5","skill":"Data Interpretation","difficulty":2,"set":"u4b_helptime","stimulus":{"type":"table","caption":"Food Court Spill Observations","headers":["Trial","Diners seated nearby","Seconds until someone helped"],"rows":[["1","2","8"],["2","9","47"],["3","1","5"],["4","6","31"],["5","4","19"],["6","12","64"],["7","3","14"],["8","7","36"]]},"stem":"Psychology students carried out a field study in a mall food court. On each of eight trials, a team member \"accidentally\" spilled a tray of drinks beside a table, and observers counted how many diners were seated nearby and timed how long it took before any diner offered to help. The results are shown in the table. What is the range of the helping times across all eight trials?","options":["28 seconds","59 seconds","64 seconds","25 seconds"],"answer":1,"rationale":"The range is the highest value minus the lowest value: 64 minus 5 equals 59 seconds. Twenty-eight seconds is the mean helping time, and 25 seconds is the median."},{"id":"U4-237","section":"4.3","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"set":"u4b_helptime","stimulus":{"type":"table","caption":"Food Court Spill Observations","headers":["Trial","Diners seated nearby","Seconds until someone helped"],"rows":[["1","2","8"],["2","9","47"],["3","1","5"],["4","6","31"],["5","4","19"],["6","12","64"],["7","3","14"],["8","7","36"]]},"stem":"Psychology students carried out a field study in a mall food court. On each of eight trials, a team member \"accidentally\" spilled a tray of drinks beside a table, and observers counted how many diners were seated nearby and timed how long it took before any diner offered to help. The results are shown in the table. Based on these findings, in which situation is the person in need most likely to receive help quickly?","options":["A tourist drops a bag of belongings in the middle of a packed festival crowd.","A spectator feels faint in the stands during a sold-out football match.","A shopper's grocery bags tear open in a crowded checkout area during a holiday sale.","A cyclist's chain snaps on a quiet residential street where one neighbor is watering plants nearby."],"answer":3,"rationale":"In the data, help came faster when fewer diners were nearby, so a lone witness, who cannot assume someone else will act, is most likely to help quickly. In each crowded setting, onlookers can assume someone else will step in."},{"id":"U4-238","section":"4.3","skill":"Data Interpretation","difficulty":1,"set":"u4b_helptime","stimulus":{"type":"table","caption":"Food Court Spill Observations","headers":["Trial","Diners seated nearby","Seconds until someone helped"],"rows":[["1","2","8"],["2","9","47"],["3","1","5"],["4","6","31"],["5","4","19"],["6","12","64"],["7","3","14"],["8","7","36"]]},"stem":"Psychology students carried out a field study in a mall food court. On each of eight trials, a team member \"accidentally\" spilled a tray of drinks beside a table, and observers counted how many diners were seated nearby and timed how long it took before any diner offered to help. The results are shown in the table. Which concept best explains the pattern in the table?","options":["Deindividuation","Diffusion of responsibility","Social facilitation","The reciprocity norm"],"answer":1,"rationale":"As more diners were present, each one felt less personally responsible for acting, so help came more slowly. Social facilitation is improved performance on well-learned tasks in front of others, and deindividuation is a loss of self-awareness and restraint in a crowd, not delayed helping."},{"id":"U4-239","section":"0.5","skill":"Data Interpretation","difficulty":2,"set":"u4b_skew","stimulus":{"type":"bar","title":"Quiz Scores on Group Influence","xLabel":"Score (out of 10)","yLabel":"Number of students","categories":["3","4","5","6","7","8","9","10"],"series":[{"name":"Students","values":[1,1,2,3,5,8,10,12]}]},"stem":"The graph shows the scores of 42 students on a 10-point quiz about how groups influence individual behavior. Given the shape of this distribution, which measure of central tendency would best represent a typical score?","options":["Mean","Median","Mode","Range"],"answer":1,"rationale":"The few very low scores pull the mean down to about 8.2, while the median of 9 resists extreme scores, so it best represents a typical score in a skewed distribution. The mode of 10 sits at the very top of the scale, and the range measures variability, not central tendency."},{"id":"U4-240","section":"0.5","skill":"Data Interpretation","difficulty":2,"set":"u4b_skew","stimulus":{"type":"bar","title":"Quiz Scores on Group Influence","xLabel":"Score (out of 10)","yLabel":"Number of students","categories":["3","4","5","6","7","8","9","10"],"series":[{"name":"Students","values":[1,1,2,3,5,8,10,12]}]},"stem":"The graph shows the scores of 42 students on a 10-point quiz about how groups influence individual behavior. Which type of distribution is shown?","options":["Positively skewed","Normal","Negatively skewed","Bimodal"],"answer":2,"rationale":"Most scores cluster at the high end with a tail stretching toward the low scores, which is a negative skew. A positive skew would have its tail toward the high scores."},{"id":"U4-241","section":"0.6","skill":"Data Interpretation","difficulty":2,"stem":"In an obedience study, researchers report that participants obeyed significantly more often when the experimenter wore a uniform than when the experimenter wore casual clothes (p < .05). This means the difference","options":["is large enough to matter in everyday life","proves that uniforms cause obedience in all people","means most participants in the uniform condition obeyed","is unlikely to have occurred by chance alone"],"answer":3,"rationale":"Statistical significance means the result is unlikely to be due to chance. It does not prove a claim for everyone, it does not show that an effect is large or practically important, and it says nothing about what share of participants obeyed."},{"id":"U4-242","section":"0.6","skill":"Data Interpretation","difficulty":2,"stem":"An industrial-organizational psychologist finds a correlation of +.45 between employees' scores on a teamwork questionnaire and their supervisor ratings one year later. Which conclusion is justified?","options":["Employees with higher teamwork scores tend to receive higher supervisor ratings, though other factors could explain the link.","An employee with a low teamwork score will definitely receive a low supervisor rating.","Training that raises teamwork scores is guaranteed to raise supervisor ratings.","This correlation is stronger than a correlation of -.60 between absences and supervisor ratings."],"answer":0,"rationale":"A positive correlation means the two variables tend to rise together, but it does not establish cause or guarantee any individual's outcome. Strength depends on distance from zero, so -.60 is a stronger relationship than +.45."},{"id":"U4-243","section":"0.6","skill":"Data Interpretation","difficulty":2,"stem":"Dr. Nakamura compares conformity rates between participants who answered aloud and participants who answered privately. She then calculates whether the difference is large and reliable enough to generalize from her sample to the wider population. This type of analysis is","options":["descriptive","a measure of variability","qualitative","inferential"],"answer":3,"rationale":"Inferential statistics, such as significance tests and effect sizes, are used to draw conclusions beyond the sample about whether the independent variable affected the dependent variable. Descriptive statistics, including measures of variability, only summarize the data collected."},{"id":"U4-244","section":"4.3","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Before the robotics team votes on its competition design, captain Tomas assigns one member to argue against every proposal and invites a teacher from outside the team to critique the plan. These steps are most likely to","options":["increase social loafing","reduce the risk of groupthink","produce deindividuation among members","push the team toward the riskiest design through group polarization"],"answer":1,"rationale":"Groupthink occurs when the desire for harmony silences doubts, so assigning a critic and inviting outside opinions brings dissent into the open. These steps encourage careful evaluation rather than more extreme decisions."},{"id":"U4-245","section":"4.3","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":1,"stem":"According to research on the bystander effect, in which situation is Amira most likely to help a stranger who has fainted?","options":["She is one of about thirty people in a stadium walkway.","She notices that several onlookers are standing still and not reacting.","She is the only other person in a parking garage when the stranger collapses.","She is part of a large tour group that all saw the person fall."],"answer":2,"rationale":"People are most likely to help when they are the only witness, because responsibility cannot be shared. Seeing others fail to react also suggests the situation is not an emergency, which lowers helping."},{"id":"U4-246","section":"4.3","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":3,"stem":"Joon-ho takes part in a study in which he and five others judge which of three tones lasts longest. The others are confederates who sometimes all give the same wrong answer. The researchers run several versions of the study. In which version is Joon-ho likely to conform the LEAST?","options":["Five confederates, one of whom gives the correct answer before Joon-ho responds","Five confederates who all give the same wrong answer aloud","Eight confederates who all give the same wrong answer aloud","Five confederates who are introduced as experts in sound perception"],"answer":0,"rationale":"Breaking the majority's unanimity with even one dissenter sharply reduces conformity. Adding more confederates or presenting them as experts would keep conformity high or raise it."},{"id":"U4-247","section":"4.3","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which situation best illustrates informational social influence?","options":["Arjun wears a team jersey to a match he does not care about because he does not want his friends to tease him.","Bilal stops talking in the library after a librarian tells him to be quiet.","At a train station in a country she is visiting for the first time, Mei-Ling joins a long line of passengers at one ticket machine, figuring they know which machine sells the right tickets.","Sara agrees to help a neighbor for an afternoon after earlier agreeing to hold the neighbor's keys for a day."],"answer":2,"rationale":"Mei-Ling copies others because she believes they have accurate information in an unfamiliar situation, which is informational social influence. Arjun conforms to avoid rejection, which is normative influence, and Bilal is obeying a person in authority."},{"id":"U4-248","section":"4.3","skill":"Concept Recall","difficulty":2,"stem":"Groupthink is most likely to develop in a group whose members","options":["openly compete with one another for leadership","consult outside experts before every decision","hold strongly opposing views that they debate at length","value harmony and agreement so highly that they avoid voicing doubts"],"answer":3,"rationale":"Groupthink rests on members' desire for unity, which leads them to suppress disagreement and make poor decisions. Open debate and outside input are ways to prevent it."},{"id":"U4-249","section":"4.2","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which situation best illustrates the foot-in-the-door technique?","options":["A charity asks Omar to volunteer every weekend for a year and, after he refuses, asks him to help for one afternoon.","A neighbor asks Grace to water her plants for one day; after Grace agrees, the neighbor asks her to care for the garden for a whole month.","A student keeps raising his hand in class because the teacher praises every answer he gives.","Lina buys the same sneakers as her friends so that she will fit in with them."],"answer":1,"rationale":"The foot-in-the-door technique starts with a small request and follows it with a larger one, as with Grace. Omar's case is the opposite order, which is the door-in-the-face technique."},{"id":"U4-250","section":"4.2","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":3,"stem":"Two students are running for student council. Farah presents a detailed budget showing how her plan would pay for new library books, while Kwame plays upbeat music at his booth and hands out stickers showing a popular local athlete endorsing him. According to the elaboration likelihood model, which student voter is most likely to be persuaded mainly by Farah's approach?","options":["Priya, who is rushing between classes and glances at the booths for only a few seconds","Leo, who is a big fan of the athlete on Kwame's stickers","Yousef, who uses the library daily and has time to read both candidates' plans carefully before voting","Chiara, who is caught up in a loud conversation with friends as she passes the booths"],"answer":2,"rationale":"Farah's evidence-based message uses the central route, which works best on people who are motivated and able to think carefully about the arguments, as Yousef is. Hurried, distracted or celebrity-focused voters are more likely to be swayed by peripheral cues like Kwame's music and stickers."},{"id":"U4-251","section":"4.2","skill":"Concept Recall","difficulty":2,"stem":"According to the elaboration likelihood model, what mainly distinguishes the central route from the peripheral route to persuasion?","options":["Whether people are persuaded by carefully weighing the message's arguments or by surface cues such as a speaker's attractiveness","Whether the persuader makes a large request first or a small request first","Whether people conform out of a desire to be liked or a desire to be correct","Whether the message is delivered to a group or to one person"],"answer":0,"rationale":"The central route works through thoughtful processing of the content of the message, while the peripheral route works through cues unrelated to the argument's quality. Request order describes the foot-in-the-door and door-in-the-face techniques."},{"id":"U4-252","section":"4.2","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":3,"stem":"A hospital volunteer coordinator tests two approaches with visitors. Visitors in Group A are first asked to complete a two-question survey about the waiting room, and almost all agree; a few minutes later they are asked to volunteer for a two-hour shift at the information desk next month. Visitors in Group B are asked only about the volunteer shift. Which result is most likely, and why?","options":["Group A agrees less often, because people who have already helped once feel they have done enough.","Both groups agree at about the same rate, because the survey and the shift are unrelated tasks.","Group A agrees more often, because the long shift makes the earlier survey seem small by comparison.","Group A agrees more often, because agreeing to a small request makes people more willing to agree to a larger one."],"answer":3,"rationale":"This is the foot-in-the-door technique: people who agree to a small request become more likely to agree to a bigger one later. Making a request seem small by contrast with a larger one describes the door-in-the-face technique, which works in the opposite order."},{"id":"U4-253","section":"5.5","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":3,"stem":"A counselor notices that some clients raised in strongly collectivist cultures stop attending traditional one-on-one talk therapy after a single session. Which explanation is most consistent with research on collectivism?","options":["People from collectivist cultures experience fewer psychological disorders, so they need less therapy.","Collectivist cultures place little value on family opinions, so clients prefer not to involve anyone else.","Clients raised in collectivist cultures usually want longer and more frequent one-on-one sessions.","A format built around one person disclosing private feelings may clash with values of group harmony and keeping family matters within the family."],"answer":3,"rationale":"Collectivist cultures prioritize the group and family, so a therapy style focused on an individual's private self may feel unfamiliar or inappropriate. Collectivist cultures value family input highly, and nothing suggests clients want more individual sessions."},{"id":"U4-254","section":"4.2","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"A school fundraising team tests two scripts with parents. In Script 1, volunteers first ask parents to donate 1,000 dirhams and, when parents decline, ask for 100 dirhams instead. In Script 2, volunteers ask for 100 dirhams right away. Script 1 produces noticeably more 100-dirham donations. Which technique best explains the success of Script 1?","options":["The foot-in-the-door technique","The door-in-the-face technique","The halo effect","Normative social influence"],"answer":1,"rationale":"Starting with a large request that is likely to be refused and then making a smaller, reasonable one is the door-in-the-face technique. The foot-in-the-door technique works in the opposite order, starting small and building up."},{"id":"U4-255","section":"4.3","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Aiko already thought the school's new homework policy was somewhat unfair. After an hour-long discussion with classmates who shared her view, she now believes the policy is completely unacceptable and must be removed immediately. This change best illustrates","options":["group polarization","groupthink","cognitive dissonance","the false consensus effect"],"answer":0,"rationale":"Group polarization is the strengthening of a group's existing views after discussion among like-minded members. Groupthink concerns a group making a poor decision because members suppress dissent."},{"id":"U4-256","section":"4.3","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"At a late-night concert, audience members wearing identical glowing masks in a dark, packed arena begin throwing water bottles at security staff. Afterward, most say they would never do something like that on their own. Which concept best explains this behavior?","options":["Group polarization","The bystander effect","Social facilitation","Deindividuation"],"answer":3,"rationale":"Anonymity, darkness and the arousal of a crowd reduce self-awareness and accountability, which is deindividuation. Group polarization involves views becoming more extreme through discussion, not anonymous crowd behavior."},{"id":"U4-257","section":"4.3","skill":"Concept Recall","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which research finding is most directly explained by diffusion of responsibility?","options":["Cyclists rode faster when racing against other cyclists than when racing against the clock.","Participants who believed several others could hear someone having a seizure over an intercom were slower to seek help than those who believed they were the only listener.","Participants gave obviously wrong answers on a line-judgment task after hearing others give them.","Like-minded group members held more extreme opinions after discussing an issue together."],"answer":1,"rationale":"When responsibility is spread across many potential helpers, each feels less obligated to act, so help is slower. The cycling finding is social facilitation and the line-judgment finding is conformity."},{"id":"U4-258","section":"4.3","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"When her class votes on a field-trip destination by a show of hands, Daniela raises her hand for the museum that most classmates are cheering for, even though she would rather visit the aquarium. A week later, when the class votes again by secret ballot, she votes for the aquarium. Her show-of-hands vote is best explained by","options":["informational social influence","group polarization","normative social influence","the false consensus effect"],"answer":2,"rationale":"Daniela goes along with the group only when her choice is public, which shows she is conforming to be accepted rather than because she thinks the others are right. That is normative social influence; informational influence would change her private choice too."},{"id":"U4-259","section":"4.3","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":1,"stem":"On a crowded public beach, dozens of people notice a swimmer far from shore who seems to be struggling. Several minutes pass before anyone alerts the lifeguards, because each person assumes that someone else must already have done so. This is an example of","options":["deindividuation","the mere exposure effect","diffusion of responsibility","groupthink"],"answer":2,"rationale":"When many people could act, each feels less personally responsible, so action is delayed; that is diffusion of responsibility. Deindividuation explains antisocial behavior in anonymous crowds, and groupthink concerns poor group decisions made to keep harmony."},{"id":"U4-260","section":"4.3","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":1,"stem":"Tariq loves very spicy food and was genuinely shocked when his new roommate said he could barely eat anything with chili in it. Tariq had assumed that almost everyone enjoys spicy dishes as much as he does. Tariq's assumption best reflects","options":["the fundamental attribution error","the false consensus effect","the self-serving bias","the just-world phenomenon"],"answer":1,"rationale":"The false consensus effect is the tendency to overestimate how much other people share our own preferences and beliefs. The fundamental attribution error concerns explaining others' behavior through personality instead of the situation."},{"id":"U4-261","section":"4.3","skill":"Data Interpretation","difficulty":2,"stimulus":{"type":"bar","title":"Average Support for a Stricter School Phone Ban (1 = strongly oppose, 10 = strongly support)","xLabel":"Type of discussion group","yLabel":"Average support rating","categories":["Groups that leaned in favor","Groups that leaned against"],"series":[{"name":"Before discussion","values":[6.2,3.8]},{"name":"After discussion","values":[8.1,1.9]}],"yMax":10},"stem":"Students who mostly agreed with one another were placed in small discussion groups and rated their support for a stricter phone ban before and after a 30-minute discussion. Which concept is best illustrated by the results in the graph?","options":["Deindividuation","Groupthink","The just-world phenomenon","Group polarization"],"answer":3,"rationale":"After discussion, each group moved further in the direction it already leaned, which is group polarization. Groupthink concerns members suppressing doubts to reach a harmonious but poor decision, which these ratings do not show."},{"id":"U4-262","section":"4.3","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"A company's leadership team approves a risky product launch. Several members had serious doubts but stayed silent to keep the meeting friendly, and no one outside the team was asked for an opinion. Which term describes this kind of distorted decision process?","options":["Groupthink","Social loafing","Social facilitation","Diffusion of responsibility"],"answer":0,"rationale":"Groupthink occurs when the desire for harmony leads members to suppress dissenting views and avoid outside input, producing poor decisions. Social loafing concerns reduced effort, not silenced disagreement."},{"id":"U4-263","section":"4.3","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Five tournament judges each privately recommend a penalty for a player who broke a rule, and their average recommendation is a two-match ban. After discussing the case together for an hour, the panel agrees on a five-match ban. This shift best illustrates","options":["groupthink","deindividuation","group polarization","social loafing"],"answer":2,"rationale":"The judges already leaned toward a penalty, and discussion pushed the group to a more extreme position than their average starting view, which is group polarization. Groupthink is about suppressing dissent to keep harmony, not about a shift toward a stronger shared view."},{"id":"U4-264","section":"4.3","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Seo-yeon sees an elderly man drop his medication bottle and struggle to pick up the scattered pills. According to research on prosocial behavior, which circumstance would make Seo-yeon LEAST likely to help him?","options":["She is not in a hurry to get anywhere.","She is in a packed airport terminal surrounded by many other travelers.","She is in a good mood after hearing some good news.","She saw another traveler help someone earlier that day."],"answer":1,"rationale":"A large crowd triggers diffusion of responsibility, which lowers the chance that any one person helps. Having time to spare, being in a good mood and having just seen someone model helping all make helping more likely."},{"id":"U4-265","section":"4.3","skill":"Concept Recall","difficulty":1,"stem":"The tendency for each person in a large group of witnesses to feel less personal obligation to act in an emergency, because the obligation seems to be shared, is called","options":["diffusion of responsibility","altruism","the social responsibility norm","deindividuation"],"answer":0,"rationale":"Diffusion of responsibility explains why larger numbers of bystanders make helping less likely. The social responsibility norm is the expectation that we should help those who depend on us, which increases helping."},{"id":"U4-266","section":"3.6","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Reza's family recently moved from one country to another. In his new country, widely shared cultural values about independence, education and how young people address adults differ from those of his home country. In ecological systems theory, these broad cultural values and norms belong to which system?","options":["Microsystem","Mesosystem","Macrosystem","Exosystem"],"answer":2,"rationale":"The macrosystem contains the broad cultural values, beliefs and norms of a society. The microsystem includes people in direct contact with the child, such as family and classmates."},{"id":"U4-267","section":"4.3","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":1,"stem":"Yusuf is an experienced skateboarder who lands his tricks more consistently when a crowd gathers at the skate park than when he practices alone. Which concept best explains this?","options":["Deindividuation","Social facilitation","The self-serving bias","Social loafing"],"answer":1,"rationale":"Social facilitation is improved performance on well-learned tasks when others are watching. Social loafing is reduced effort when individual contributions are pooled in a group."},{"id":"U4-268","section":"4.3","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":3,"stem":"Social facilitation research predicts that an audience will most likely IMPROVE the performance of which person?","options":["Ben, giving his first speech in a language he started learning last month, to a large class","Lucia, trying a complex new gymnastics routine for the first time, in front of judges","Daniel, solving unfamiliar advanced math problems while classmates watch him","Chiara, performing a piano piece she has played hundreds of times, at a school recital"],"answer":3,"rationale":"The presence of others strengthens dominant responses, so it helps on well-learned tasks like Chiara's piece. On new or difficult tasks, the dominant response is often an error, so an audience tends to hurt performance."},{"id":"U4-269","section":"4.3","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":1,"stem":"A recruitment website lists four openings for psychologists. Which posting is most likely written for an industrial-organizational psychologist?","options":["Wanted: specialist to redesign our hiring interviews and run staff surveys on job satisfaction and burnout","Wanted: specialist to assess and treat children with anxiety at an outpatient clinic","Wanted: specialist to study how sleep loss changes brain activity using fMRI","Wanted: specialist to test students for learning difficulties and plan support at school"],"answer":0,"rationale":"Industrial-organizational psychologists apply psychology to the workplace, including hiring, job satisfaction and burnout. The other postings fit clinical, biological and school psychology."},{"id":"U4-270","section":"4.2","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"In Milgram's obedience studies, participants began by giving what they believed were mild 15-volt shocks and were then asked to raise the level in small steps. This gradual increase helps explain participants' obedience through which concept?","options":["The door-in-the-face technique","Deindividuation","The foot-in-the-door technique","The halo effect"],"answer":2,"rationale":"Agreeing to a small first step made it easier to agree to each slightly larger step, which is the foot-in-the-door effect. The door-in-the-face technique would begin with a large request."},{"id":"U4-271","section":"4.3","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"During an online exam, a student posts in a class chat of 200 people that she has been locked out of the exam portal and asks someone to alert the teacher. An hour passes before anyone responds. Which phenomenon best explains the delay?","options":["Social facilitation","The reciprocity norm","Group polarization","Diffusion of responsibility"],"answer":3,"rationale":"With so many people able to respond, each one assumes someone else will alert the teacher, so no one acts quickly. This is the same process behind the bystander effect."},{"id":"U4-272","section":"4.3","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":1,"stem":"Which situation is the clearest example of conformity rather than obedience?","options":["During a school assembly, Kalani starts clapping along to the music after noticing that everyone around her is doing it.","Kalani hands in her phone at the start of an exam because the invigilator tells every student to do so.","Kalani stays after school because her coach orders the whole team to attend an extra practice.","Kalani lowers her voice in a hospital corridor after a nurse asks her to be quiet."],"answer":0,"rationale":"Conformity is changing behavior to match a group without being directly told to, as when Kalani joins the clapping. In the other situations, a person in authority gives a direct instruction, which is obedience."},{"id":"U4-273","section":"4.3","skill":"Data Interpretation","difficulty":2,"stimulus":{"type":"table","caption":"Students' Estimates of How Many Classmates Skip Breakfast (actual rate: 25%)","headers":["Student group","Number of students","Average estimate of classmates who skip breakfast"],"rows":[["Students who usually skip breakfast","40","68%"],["Students who usually eat breakfast","120","22%"]]},"stem":"A school nurse asked 160 students whether they usually skip breakfast and what percentage of their classmates they thought skip it. The actual rate was 25 percent. The pattern in the table best illustrates","options":["the halo effect","the just-world phenomenon","the false consensus effect","the self-fulfilling prophecy"],"answer":2,"rationale":"Students who skip breakfast estimated that far more classmates do so than actually do, overestimating how common their own habit is; this is the false consensus effect. The halo effect involves letting one positive trait shape an overall impression of a person."},{"id":"U4-274","section":"4.3","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":3,"stem":"Hana has just joined a choir. At rehearsals, the director asks singers to say aloud whether they prefer a slow or fast tempo for each new song, and Hana always names whatever most of the other singers chose, mainly because she wants them to accept her. If her conformity is driven by normative social influence, which change would most likely reduce it?","options":["Telling Hana that the other singers all have years of musical training","Collecting tempo preferences on anonymous slips of paper","Increasing the choir from 12 to 20 singers","Having the most experienced singer explain her reasons before anyone else answers"],"answer":1,"rationale":"Normative influence depends on being seen by the group, so responding privately removes the pressure to fit in. Learning that others are experts or hearing an expert's reasons would add informational influence, and a larger majority would not reduce conformity."},{"id":"U4-275","section":"4.3","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Teenagers from two neighboring residential compounds keep arguing over who gets to use a shared sports field. Which strategy is best supported by research for reducing the conflict between the two groups?","options":["Scheduling the groups at different times so they never meet","Holding a tournament between the compounds to settle who controls the field","Having both groups work together to raise money for new floodlights that the field will receive only if both groups contribute","Encouraging each group to discuss its complaints only among its own members"],"answer":2,"rationale":"Superordinate goals, shared goals that require cooperation, unite rival groups and reduce hostility. Competition increases conflict, and discussing complaints only within each group would likely polarize views further."},{"id":"U4-276","section":"4.3","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"A first-aid instructor tells her class: \"If someone collapses in a crowded place, do not just shout 'Somebody call an ambulance!' Instead, point to one person and say, 'You in the green jacket, call an ambulance now.'\" Her advice is designed mainly to overcome which phenomenon?","options":["Social facilitation","The foot-in-the-door technique","The bystander effect","Group polarization"],"answer":2,"rationale":"In a crowd, each person tends to wait for someone else to act, which is the bystander effect. Singling out one person removes the shared responsibility, so that person is much more likely to act."},{"id":"U4-277","section":"4.3","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which finding is an example of social facilitation?","options":["Shoppers buy more after a store employee hands them a free sample.","Experienced basketball players make a higher percentage of free throws during games with spectators than during solo practice.","Employees grow to like a new office building more the longer they work there.","New club members adopt the group's style of dress so that they will be accepted."],"answer":1,"rationale":"Social facilitation is improved performance on a well-practiced skill in the presence of others. The free sample illustrates the reciprocity norm, and liking a building more over time illustrates the mere exposure effect."},{"id":"U4-278","section":"4.3","skill":"Concept Recall","difficulty":1,"stem":"A cultural orientation in which people give priority to the goals and well-being of their family and community over their own personal goals is called","options":["collectivism","individualism","ethnocentrism","conformity"],"answer":0,"rationale":"Collectivism places the group's needs and harmony above individual needs. Ethnocentrism is judging other cultures by the standards of one's own."},{"id":"U4-279","section":"4.2","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Malik is designing a campaign to persuade students to join the school's recycling club, and he wants to use the peripheral route to persuasion. Which strategy best fits?","options":["Handing out a report comparing the school's waste data with national averages","Hosting a 90-minute debate on the environmental costs of plastic","Posting a short video of a popular student athlete smiling while holding a recycling bin","Presenting a step-by-step cost analysis of recycling to the student council"],"answer":2,"rationale":"The peripheral route relies on surface cues such as a likable or attractive messenger rather than on the strength of the arguments. The data, debate and cost analysis all depend on careful thinking about the message, which is the central route."},{"id":"U4-280","section":"4.3","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":1,"stem":"A carpet seller in a traditional souq offers each customer a free cup of tea and some dates before showing any carpets. He notices that customers are more likely to buy something afterward. This technique relies mainly on","options":["the foot-in-the-door technique","normative social influence","the mere exposure effect","the reciprocity norm"],"answer":3,"rationale":"The reciprocity norm is the expectation that people should return favors, so receiving a gift makes customers feel they owe something back. Normative influence involves conforming to fit in with a group."},{"id":"U4-281","section":"4.2","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":1,"stem":"A phone company's advertisement presents battery test results, a chart comparing processor speeds and summaries of independent lab reviews to convince buyers to choose its new model. This approach best represents","options":["the peripheral route to persuasion","the halo effect","the central route to persuasion","the door-in-the-face technique"],"answer":2,"rationale":"The central route persuades through evidence and logical arguments that the audience thinks about carefully. The halo effect is an example of the peripheral route, which relies on surface cues."},{"id":"U4-282","section":"4.3","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Kofi is a competitive swimmer. His average time in the 100-meter freestyle is 61.2 seconds during solo training sessions but 59.4 seconds at meets where spectators fill the stands. Which concept best accounts for the difference?","options":["Social loafing","Social facilitation","The bystander effect","Deindividuation"],"answer":1,"rationale":"A skilled swimmer performing a well-practiced task swims faster with an audience, which is social facilitation. Social loafing would predict less effort, not faster times."},{"id":"U4-283","section":"4.3","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which situation best illustrates compliance?","options":["Rania's principal orders all students to leave the building during a fire drill, and she does.","At the library, a stranger asks Rania whether he may borrow her spare pen for a moment, and she hands it to him.","Rania starts wearing her watch on her right wrist after noticing that all her friends do.","Rania practices the violin longer each evening after her teacher praises her progress."],"answer":1,"rationale":"Compliance is going along with a direct request from someone who has no special authority, as with the stranger's request for a pen. Following the principal's order is obedience, and copying her friends without being asked is conformity."},{"id":"U4-284","section":"4.2","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Researchers asked homeowners to place a small sticker supporting safe driving in a window. Two weeks later, the same homeowners were asked to put a large, unattractive safe-driving sign on their front lawn, and most agreed. Homeowners who had not been asked about the sticker were much less likely to agree to the sign. This finding is best characterized as","options":["the door-in-the-face technique","the central route to persuasion","the reciprocity norm","the foot-in-the-door technique"],"answer":3,"rationale":"Agreeing to the small sticker request made homeowners more likely to agree to the larger sign request, which is the foot-in-the-door effect. The door-in-the-face technique starts with a large request that is refused."},{"id":"U4-285","section":"4.4","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":1,"stem":"A psychologist shows a client a card with a symmetrical, shapeless ink pattern and asks, \"What might this be?\" The client's answers are scored for themes that the psychologist believes reflect unconscious concerns. This is an example of which kind of assessment?","options":["A projective personality test","A self-report personality inventory","A behavioral observation checklist","An achievement test"],"answer":0,"rationale":"Projective tests present ambiguous stimuli, such as inkblots, so that people will project unconscious thoughts and feelings into their responses. A self-report inventory asks direct questions with fixed answer choices."},{"id":"U4-286","section":"4.4","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":1,"stem":"Ibrahim is furious with his coach for benching him during the final match, but he says nothing to the coach. On the bus ride back, he starts a loud argument with a younger player over a seat. Ibrahim's behavior illustrates which defense mechanism?","options":["Projection","Displacement","Rationalization","Sublimation"],"answer":1,"rationale":"Displacement is redirecting emotions, such as anger, from the true source to a safer target. Sublimation would channel the frustration into a socially valued activity instead."},{"id":"U4-287","section":"4.4","skill":"Concept Recall","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which belief most clearly separates humanistic psychologists, such as Carl Rogers, from psychoanalytic theorists, such as Sigmund Freud?","options":["Behavior is largely determined by unconscious conflicts from childhood.","Personality is best measured with projective tests.","Behavior is shaped mainly by reinforcement and punishment.","People have free will and a natural drive toward personal growth."],"answer":3,"rationale":"Humanistic psychologists emphasize free will, personal choice and the self-actualizing tendency. Unconscious determinism and projective testing belong to the psychoanalytic approach."},{"id":"U4-288","section":"4.4","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Mr. Okonkwo has received three official letters stating that his small cafe must close next month because the building will be demolished. He keeps ordering supplies for next year and tells his staff, \"This is not really going to happen.\" Which defense mechanism best describes his behavior?","options":["Reaction formation","Displacement","Denial","Projection"],"answer":2,"rationale":"Denial is refusing to accept a painful reality, even when there is clear evidence for it. Reaction formation would involve expressing the opposite of a hidden feeling."},{"id":"U4-289","section":"4.4","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":1,"stem":"Valentina constantly tries to outshine her teammates in the robotics club and takes credit for shared work, yet she sees herself as modest. She often complains that the other members \"only care about getting credit.\" Which defense mechanism is she most likely using?","options":["Projection","Denial","Sublimation","Displacement"],"answer":0,"rationale":"Projection is attributing one's own unacceptable traits to other people, so Valentina sees her own credit-seeking in her teammates. Displacement would involve redirecting an emotion onto a safer target."},{"id":"U4-290","section":"4.4","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which example best illustrates the defense mechanism of projection?","options":["After his manager criticizes him, an employee kicks the office vending machine.","A student who often copies homework is convinced that most of his classmates are cheaters.","A child who is anxious about starting at a new school begins sucking her thumb again.","A student who fails a test decides the test was poorly written anyway."],"answer":1,"rationale":"The student attributes his own dishonesty to others, which is projection. Kicking the machine is displacement, thumb sucking is regression and blaming the test is rationalization."},{"id":"U4-291","section":"4.4","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Ten-year-old Mariam accidentally breaks a vase while playing ball indoors. According to Carl Rogers, which response from her parent best shows unconditional positive regard?","options":["\"I'll be proud of you again once you show me you can follow the rules.\"","\"Let me show you the first step of gluing it, and then you try the next part on your own.\"","\"You will do as you are told because I am the parent.\"","\"I'm upset about the vase, and we'll talk about playing ball outside, but nothing you do changes how much I love you.\""],"answer":3,"rationale":"Unconditional positive regard means accepting and valuing a person fully, even while disapproving of a specific behavior. Making pride depend on good behavior creates conditions of worth, and the other responses describe scaffolding and an authoritarian style."},{"id":"U4-292","section":"4.4","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Nkechi feels strong resentment toward a teammate who was chosen as captain, but she believes such feelings are unacceptable and is not aware of them. Instead, she becomes convinced that the teammate secretly resents her. Which defense mechanism does this demonstrate?","options":["Displacement","Sublimation","Projection","Reaction formation"],"answer":2,"rationale":"Projection protects the ego by attributing one's own unacceptable feelings to someone else. Reaction formation would mean acting with exaggerated friendliness toward the teammate, and displacement would redirect the resentment onto a safer target."},{"id":"U4-293","section":"4.4","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":1,"stem":"Ali bought a new video game on impulse the day it came out, spending his entire month's allowance. When his older sister asks why, he explains with complete confidence that the game will \"improve my problem-solving skills for school\" and that buying it early actually saved money. Which defense mechanism is Ali relying on?","options":["Rationalization","Displacement","Reaction formation","Sublimation"],"answer":0,"rationale":"Rationalization is creating a reasonable-sounding explanation to justify behavior that was really driven by other motives. Reaction formation would involve acting in the opposite way to a hidden impulse."},{"id":"U4-294","section":"0.3","skill":"Research Methods and Design","difficulty":2,"stem":"Dr. Mwangi wants to study whether university students who rely on rationalization after a poor exam grade also report more test anxiety. Which is the best operational definition of \"use of rationalization\" for her study?","options":["The number of excuse-type statements, such as \"the test was unfair,\" a student writes when asked to explain a recent low grade, as scored by two trained raters","How strongly a student's ego unconsciously protects itself from anxiety","Whether a student is generally the kind of person who makes excuses","A student's score on a standardized test-anxiety scale"],"answer":0,"rationale":"An operational definition states exactly how a variable will be measured, here a countable number of excuse statements scored by raters. The test-anxiety score measures the other variable, and the remaining choices describe the concept without saying how to measure it."},{"id":"U4-295","section":"4.4","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"According to psychodynamic theory, a teenager with strong aggressive urges who channels them into becoming a disciplined, award-winning boxer is using which defense mechanism?","options":["Displacement","Repression","Reaction formation","Sublimation"],"answer":3,"rationale":"Sublimation transforms unacceptable impulses into socially valued activities. Displacement would redirect the aggression onto an innocent person or object rather than into an admired skill."},{"id":"U4-296","section":"4.4","skill":"Concept Recall","difficulty":1,"stem":"According to the psychodynamic perspective, what is the main purpose of defense mechanisms?","options":["To move the person toward self-actualization","To build self-efficacy through repeated success","To protect the ego from anxiety caused by unconscious conflicts","To strengthen habits that have been reinforced"],"answer":2,"rationale":"Defense mechanisms operate unconsciously to reduce anxiety and protect the ego from threats. Self-actualization is a humanistic idea, and self-efficacy is a social-cognitive idea."},{"id":"U4-297","section":"4.4","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":3,"stem":"Henrik is unconsciously envious of a coworker who was recently promoted, but he cannot accept this feeling in himself. Which behavior would be an example of Henrik using projection?","options":["Admitting his envy to a close friend and asking for advice","Telling others that his coworkers are all envious of his own success","Taking up long-distance running to release his frustration","Throwing a childlike tantrum when he loses a board game at home"],"answer":1,"rationale":"In projection, a person attributes his own unacceptable feeling, here envy, to other people. Openly admitting the feeling is not a defense mechanism at all, and the tantrum would be regression."},{"id":"U4-298","section":"4.4","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":3,"stem":"Rafael often complains that his lab partner is careless with equipment. Which additional piece of information would most strongly suggest that Rafael's complaint reflects the defense mechanism of projection?","options":["The lab partner is actually very careful, while Rafael has broken several beakers this term without ever acknowledging it.","The lab partner has broken several beakers this term, and the teacher has warned her about it.","Rafael is angry at his teacher but complains about his lab partner instead.","Rafael complains only after receiving low grades, and he blames the faulty lab equipment for them."],"answer":0,"rationale":"Projection means attributing one's own unacknowledged flaw to someone who does not have it, so the key evidence is that Rafael, not his partner, is the careless one. If the partner really is careless, the complaint is accurate, and redirecting anger from the teacher would be displacement."},{"id":"U4-299","section":"4.4","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"After Arnav's team loses the regional final because of his missed penalty kick, his coach sits down with him and says, \"You made a mistake today, and I'm not going to pretend it didn't matter. But my respect for you as a person hasn't changed one bit.\" According to Carl Rogers, the coach is showing","options":["unconditional positive regard","conditions of worth","reciprocal determinism","an external locus of control"],"answer":0,"rationale":"Unconditional positive regard is accepting a person fully, even when you disapprove of a specific behavior. Conditions of worth would mean the coach's respect depended on Arnav performing well."},{"id":"U4-300","section":"4.4","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which situation best illustrates the defense mechanism of denial?","options":["After failing his driving test, Pedro says he did not really want to drive yet because fuel is expensive.","After an argument with her coach, Aaliyah comes home and yells at her younger cousin.","Even after a severe storm has clearly destroyed the vegetable garden she spent a year building, Sami tells everyone it will be fine by tomorrow and refuses to look at the damage.","Feeling restless energy after exams, Wen spends hours painting a large mural for the school."],"answer":2,"rationale":"Denial is refusing to accept a threatening reality despite clear evidence. Pedro's excuse is rationalization, yelling at the cousin is displacement and painting the mural is closer to sublimation."},{"id":"U4-301","section":"4.4","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Dr. Rahman gives one client a 300-item questionnaire of true-false statements such as \"I enjoy large parties,\" and gives another client a sentence-completion task with stems such as \"When I am alone, I...\" and asks for the first thing that comes to mind. Which statement best contrasts the two assessments?","options":["The sentence-completion task is a projective test that uses open-ended prompts, while the questionnaire is an objective self-report inventory.","Both are projective tests, because both rely on the client describing themselves.","The questionnaire is a projective test because it has many items, while the sentence-completion task is an achievement test.","The sentence-completion task is an aptitude test of verbal ability, while the questionnaire is a projective test."],"answer":0,"rationale":"Projective tests give ambiguous or open-ended prompts so that people may reveal hidden thoughts and feelings, as in sentence completion. A questionnaire with fixed true-false answers is an objective self-report inventory, the kind used by trait psychologists."},{"id":"U4-302","section":"4.4","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":1,"stem":"Dr. Lindqvist argues that a client's constant fear of disappointing others stems from unresolved, unconscious conflicts that began in early childhood. Which perspective is Dr. Lindqvist using?","options":["Trait","Humanistic","Social-cognitive","Psychodynamic"],"answer":3,"rationale":"The psychodynamic perspective explains behavior through unconscious drives and conflicts, often rooted in childhood. The trait perspective would describe stable characteristics without tracing them to hidden conflicts."},{"id":"U4-303","section":"2.8","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"A school is considering using students' interpretations of inkblots to decide which students should be referred for counseling. A psychologist on the review panel objects to the plan. Which concern best supports her objection?","options":["The inkblot task asks too many direct true-false questions about everyday behavior.","Different scorers often interpret the same responses differently, and such tests show weak reliability and validity.","The inkblot task measures intelligence rather than personality.","The inkblot task can legally be given only by medical doctors."],"answer":1,"rationale":"Projective tests are criticized because scoring is subjective and the results are often inconsistent and poor at predicting behavior, so they are a weak basis for decisions about students. Direct true-false questions are a feature of personality inventories, not projective tests."},{"id":"U4-304","section":"4.4","skill":"Concept Recall","difficulty":2,"stem":"The main reasoning behind projective tests such as the Thematic Apperception Test (TAT) is that","options":["answering many objective questions reveals patterns of stable personality traits","scores can be compared directly with those of a large, representative norm group","a person's confidence in their abilities determines how they describe pictures","people will reveal unconscious motives and conflicts when they interpret ambiguous stimuli"],"answer":3,"rationale":"Projective tests assume that, when the stimulus is vague, people project their hidden thoughts and feelings onto it. Answering many objective questions describes trait-based personality inventories."},{"id":"U4-305","section":"4.4","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Kaito was not selected for the school debate team after skipping most of the practice sessions. He tells his friends that debate \"is a waste of time anyway\" and that he \"would rather focus on science this year.\" Which defense mechanism best fits Kaito's explanation?","options":["Reaction formation","Projection","Rationalization","Regression"],"answer":2,"rationale":"Kaito invents reasonable-sounding explanations to avoid facing the anxiety of not being chosen because of his own choices, which is rationalization. Reaction formation would involve acting in a way opposite to his true feelings."},{"id":"U4-306","section":"4.4","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Mr. Ortiz is secretly envious of his neighbor's large new house but cannot admit the feeling to himself. Which behavior would best illustrate reaction formation?","options":["He becomes convinced that the neighbor is envious of him.","He tells himself that a big house would only mean more cleaning anyway.","He becomes the neighbor's most enthusiastic admirer, praising the house to everyone on the street.","He comes home and snaps at his children over small things."],"answer":2,"rationale":"In reaction formation, a person unconsciously replaces an unacceptable feeling with exaggerated behavior showing the opposite feeling, here admiration in place of envy. Believing the neighbor is envious is projection, the cleaning excuse is rationalization and snapping at his children is displacement."},{"id":"U4-307","section":"2.7","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Psychodynamic theorists propose that a person may be unable to recall a frightening childhood event because of repression. In information-processing terms, repression would be described as a failure of","options":["encoding","sensory memory","retrieval","implicit memory"],"answer":2,"rationale":"Repression assumes the memory was stored but is blocked from being brought back to awareness, which is a retrieval problem. If it had never been encoded, there would be nothing to repress."},{"id":"U4-308","section":"4.4","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":1,"stem":"A counselor believes that every client has an inborn drive to grow, develop their talents and fulfill their potential. This counselor is drawing most directly on which approach to personality?","options":["Psychodynamic","Humanistic","Social-cognitive","Trait"],"answer":1,"rationale":"The humanistic approach centers on the self-actualizing tendency, the drive to reach one's full potential. The psychodynamic approach focuses on unconscious conflicts instead."},{"id":"U4-309","section":"4.4","skill":"Concept Recall","difficulty":1,"stem":"Which theorist's view of personality is most closely associated with the self-actualizing tendency and the importance of unconditional positive regard?","options":["Carl Rogers","Sigmund Freud","Albert Bandura","Paul Costa and Robert McCrae"],"answer":0,"rationale":"Carl Rogers, a humanistic psychologist, emphasized the self-actualizing tendency and unconditional positive regard. Bandura is associated with social-cognitive theory and self-efficacy, and Costa and McCrae with the Big Five traits."},{"id":"U4-310","section":"0.2","skill":"Research Methods and Design","difficulty":1,"stem":"Dr. Haddad surveys 400 adults about how many hours they volunteer each month and has them complete a self-actualization scale. He wants to see whether the two measures are related. Which research method is he using?","options":["An experiment","A meta-analysis","A case study","A correlational study"],"answer":3,"rationale":"Dr. Haddad measures two existing variables to see whether they are related without manipulating either one, which is a correlational study. An experiment would require assigning people to different levels of volunteering."},{"id":"U4-311","section":"4.4","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Without realizing it, Femi resents his younger cousin, who always gets the most attention at family gatherings. Yet Femi is excessively sweet to the cousin, constantly complimenting him and insisting that he is his favorite relative. Which defense mechanism is Femi most likely using?","options":["Projection","Reaction formation","Displacement","Denial"],"answer":1,"rationale":"Femi unconsciously converts his resentment into exaggerated affection, the opposite feeling, which is reaction formation. Projection would mean believing the cousin resents him."},{"id":"U4-312","section":"4.4","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":1,"stem":"A psychologist asks a client to draw a house, a tree and a person, believing the drawings will reveal hidden conflicts the client cannot describe directly. This psychologist most likely holds which perspective on personality?","options":["Social-cognitive","Trait","Psychodynamic","Biological"],"answer":2,"rationale":"Using open-ended drawings to uncover unconscious conflicts is a projective technique, which comes from the psychodynamic perspective. Trait psychologists rely on objective personality inventories instead."},{"id":"U4-313","section":"4.4","skill":"Concept Recall","difficulty":1,"stem":"During a projective personality assessment, a client would most likely be asked to","options":["describe what she sees in a series of ambiguous, symmetrical inkblots","rate how well each of 240 statements describes her on a scale from 1 to 5","answer timed questions about vocabulary and arithmetic","keep a daily log of situations in which she felt confident"],"answer":0,"rationale":"Projective techniques present ambiguous stimuli, such as inkblots, and invite open-ended responses. Rating many statements is a self-report personality inventory, and timed vocabulary questions are part of an intelligence test."},{"id":"U4-314","section":"4.4","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which example best illustrates the defense mechanism of regression?","options":["Told that his science project failed a safety inspection, Kwesi insists it passed and keeps preparing to present it.","Frustrated at losing her place on the team, Lin pours her energy into writing short stories that win a national prize.","Sanjay, who secretly dislikes a coworker, goes out of his way to praise her at every meeting.","Overwhelmed after moving to a new country, twelve-year-old Ana starts speaking in baby talk and asks her parents to dress her."],"answer":3,"rationale":"Regression is retreating to behavior typical of an earlier stage of development when under stress, as Ana does. Kwesi's insistence is denial, Lin's writing is sublimation and Sanjay's praise is reaction formation."},{"id":"U4-315","section":"0.5","skill":"Data Interpretation","difficulty":1,"set":"u4b_selfesteem","stem":"Ten participants in a confidence-building program rated their self-esteem on a scale from 1 (very low) to 10 (very high). Their ratings were: 2, 4, 4, 6, 6, 6, 7, 8, 9, 9. What is the mode of these ratings?","options":["4","6.1","6","9"],"answer":2,"rationale":"The mode is the score that occurs most often, and 6 appears three times. The ratings 4 and 9 each appear only twice, and 6.1 is the mean."},{"id":"U4-316","section":"0.5","skill":"Data Interpretation","difficulty":2,"set":"u4b_selfesteem","stem":"Ten participants in a confidence-building program rated their self-esteem on a scale from 1 (very low) to 10 (very high). Their ratings were: 2, 4, 4, 6, 6, 6, 7, 8, 9, 9. What is the mean of these ratings?","options":["6.1","6","6.5","5.5"],"answer":0,"rationale":"The ratings add up to 61, and 61 divided by 10 participants equals 6.1. The value 6 is both the median and the mode, not the mean."},{"id":"U4-317","section":"4.5","skill":"Concept Recall","difficulty":1,"stem":"A person's belief in their own ability to succeed at a particular task or to handle a particular situation is called","options":["self-esteem","self-efficacy","self-actualization","unconditional positive regard"],"answer":1,"rationale":"Self-efficacy, a social-cognitive concept from Albert Bandura, is confidence in one's ability to accomplish a specific task. Self-esteem is a broader sense of one's overall self-worth."},{"id":"U4-318","section":"4.5","skill":"Concept Recall","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which statement best reflects the five-factor (Big Five) model of personality?","options":["Personality is driven mainly by each person's inborn tendency toward self-actualization.","Each person belongs to exactly one of five fixed personality types.","Personality is shaped by the interaction of behavior, thoughts and the environment.","Personality can be described by where a person falls on five broad trait dimensions, such as conscientiousness and extraversion."],"answer":3,"rationale":"The Big Five model describes personality by a person's position on five broad trait dimensions, measured by personality inventories, rather than by sorting people into types. The interaction of behavior, thoughts and environment describes reciprocal determinism from social-cognitive theory."},{"id":"U4-319","section":"4.5","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":1,"stem":"Although she has never coached before, Imani volunteers to coach a youth basketball team because she feels confident she can learn the drills and lead the players well. A social-cognitive theorist would describe Imani's confidence as","options":["self-efficacy","reciprocal determinism","self-actualization","an external locus of control"],"answer":0,"rationale":"Self-efficacy is a person's belief that they can succeed at a specific task, such as coaching a team. Self-actualization is the humanistic idea of fulfilling one's full potential."},{"id":"U4-320","section":"4.5","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":1,"stem":"A psychologist's report describes a client as high in agreeableness, average in openness to experience and low in emotional stability, based on the client's scores on a standardized inventory. Which theory of personality is the psychologist most likely using?","options":["Psychodynamic theory","The five-factor (Big Five) trait theory","Humanistic theory","Social-cognitive theory"],"answer":1,"rationale":"Describing someone by scores on agreeableness, openness and emotional stability uses the Big Five trait dimensions. Psychodynamic theory would focus on unconscious conflicts instead of measured traits."},{"id":"U4-321","section":"4.5","skill":"Research Methods and Design","difficulty":2,"stem":"Researchers find that people's self-ratings on \"organized,\" \"dependable\" and \"self-disciplined\" are highly correlated and can be grouped into a single dimension labeled conscientiousness. Which statistical technique identifies clusters of related items like this?","options":["Meta-analysis","Inter-rater reliability","Standard deviation","Factor analysis"],"answer":3,"rationale":"Factor analysis finds groups of items that are highly correlated with each other, revealing underlying dimensions such as the Big Five traits. A meta-analysis combines the results of many separate studies."},{"id":"U4-501","section":"4.5","skill":"Concept Recall","difficulty":1,"stem":"Which statement best describes reciprocal determinism as used in the social-cognitive theory of personality?","options":["A person's behavior, internal factors such as thoughts and beliefs, and the environment all influence one another.","A person's personality is set by inherited traits that stay fixed regardless of the environment.","Unconscious conflicts from early childhood determine how a person behaves as an adult.","People naturally move toward fulfilling their potential when they receive unconditional positive regard."],"answer":0,"rationale":"Reciprocal determinism is the social-cognitive idea that behavior, personal factors (such as beliefs and self-efficacy) and the environment shape each other in every direction. The other statements describe trait, psychodynamic and humanistic views."},{"id":"U4-502","section":"4.5","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Kenji joins his school's robotics club because he already enjoys building things. Working on the team's robot makes him more confident with coding, so he volunteers for harder tasks. His enthusiasm leads the club advisor to start an advanced coding group, which gives Kenji even more chances to practice. A social-cognitive psychologist would most likely use which concept to explain this pattern?","options":["Observational learning","Reciprocal determinism","The self-actualizing tendency","Sublimation"],"answer":1,"rationale":"Kenji's interests and confidence (personal factors), his actions in the club (behavior) and the club setting (environment) keep influencing one another, which is reciprocal determinism. Observational learning involves imitating a model, which is not the focus here."},{"id":"U4-503","section":"4.5","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":1,"stem":"After losing in the final round of a chess tournament, Amara is disappointed with the result but still tells her brother, \"I am a good person, and I like who I am.\" Her general sense of her own worth and value as a person is best described as her","options":["self-efficacy","conscientiousness","self-esteem","reciprocal determinism"],"answer":2,"rationale":"Self-esteem is a person's overall evaluation of their own worth. Self-efficacy is narrower: the belief that one can succeed at a specific task, such as winning chess matches."},{"id":"U4-504","section":"4.5","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":3,"stem":"Lucas is confident he can solve any calculus problem his teacher assigns, and he volunteers to answer the hardest ones in class. However, he often tells his school counselor that he does not feel like a worthwhile person and that everyone else is better than him. Which statement best describes Lucas, according to social-cognitive theory?","options":["He has high self-esteem in calculus but low self-efficacy overall.","He has low self-efficacy for calculus and low self-esteem.","He has high self-efficacy and high self-esteem, which together form an inflated self-concept.","He has high self-efficacy for calculus but low self-esteem."],"answer":3,"rationale":"Self-efficacy is confidence in succeeding at a particular task, and Lucas clearly believes he can succeed in calculus. Self-esteem is his overall sense of personal worth, which is low. Both feed into his self-concept, but they can differ sharply."},{"id":"U4-505","section":"4.5","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"In a class activity, students complete the sentence \"I am...\" ten times. Aisha writes \"a big sister,\" \"a strong swimmer,\" \"quieter than most of my friends,\" \"someone who keeps promises\" and \"the funny one in my family.\" Taken together, her answers most directly describe Aisha's","options":["self-concept","self-efficacy","ideal self","level of extraversion"],"answer":0,"rationale":"Self-concept is how a person views themselves, including in relation to others (a big sister, quieter than friends). Self-efficacy refers only to confidence in succeeding at particular tasks, and the ideal self is who a person wishes to be rather than how they currently see themselves."},{"id":"U4-506","section":"4.5","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Ms. Fernandes notices that several of her piano students believe they could never perform at a public recital. Drawing on social-cognitive theory, which plan would be most likely to raise the students' self-efficacy for performing?","options":["Promise each student a cash prize if they agree to perform at the recital.","Have each student play first for one friend, then for a small group, then for the class, building a record of successful performances.","Ask each student to describe dreams and early memories that may explain the fear.","Tell the students that performing ability is an inborn trait that cannot be changed."],"answer":1,"rationale":"Self-efficacy grows most strongly from mastery experiences, repeated successes at gradually harder versions of a task. A cash prize might make students more willing to perform, but it does not build their belief that they can perform well."},{"id":"U4-507","section":"4.5","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":1,"stem":"Mateo loves trying dishes from cuisines he has never tasted, visits exhibitions of unusual abstract art, and enjoys long discussions about strange scientific ideas with his cousins. Which Big Five trait would Mateo most likely score high on?","options":["Conscientiousness","Extraversion","Openness to experience","Agreeableness"],"answer":2,"rationale":"Openness to experience reflects curiosity, imagination and a preference for variety and new ideas. Extraversion concerns sociability and energy around others, which is not the focus of the scenario."},{"id":"U4-508","section":"4.5","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":1,"stem":"Priya writes every deadline in a planner, starts her assignments as soon as they are given, and checks her work twice before submitting it. Her friends say she is the most dependable person they know. Priya would most likely score high on which Big Five trait?","options":["Emotional stability","Openness to experience","Agreeableness","Conscientiousness"],"answer":3,"rationale":"Conscientiousness describes being organized, careful, self-disciplined and dependable. Agreeableness relates to warmth and cooperation with others rather than organization."},{"id":"U4-509","section":"4.5","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Tomás's teammates describe him as trusting, kind and easy to work with. When others disagree with him, he looks for a compromise, and he forgives quickly when someone lets him down. He is not especially talkative and prefers spending time in small, quiet groups. Which pattern best fits Tomás?","options":["High agreeableness and lower extraversion","High extraversion and lower agreeableness","High conscientiousness and lower openness to experience","High openness to experience and lower emotional stability"],"answer":0,"rationale":"Trust, kindness, cooperation and forgiveness are markers of agreeableness. Being less talkative and preferring small, quiet groups suggests lower extraversion."},{"id":"U4-510","section":"4.5","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Hana's family notices that she worries about small things for days, feels very tense before ordinary events such as a short class presentation, and her mood shifts quickly whenever minor problems come up. On a Big Five personality inventory, Hana would most likely score","options":["low in agreeableness","low in openness to experience","low in emotional stability","low in conscientiousness"],"answer":2,"rationale":"Frequent worry, tension and quick mood shifts reflect low emotional stability (also called high neuroticism). Nothing in the scenario suggests unkindness, lack of curiosity or disorganization."},{"id":"U4-511","section":"4.5","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":3,"stem":"At a family gathering, Yusuf talks loudly to everyone, tells long stories to the whole room and says he feels most energized in big crowds. However, he frequently interrupts relatives, criticizes other people's opinions bluntly and rarely offers help when someone needs it. Which Big Five profile best describes Yusuf?","options":["High extraversion and high agreeableness","Low extraversion and low agreeableness","High openness to experience and low conscientiousness","High extraversion and low agreeableness"],"answer":3,"rationale":"Seeking attention and gaining energy from crowds point to high extraversion, while being blunt, critical and unhelpful points to low agreeableness. Being outgoing is not the same as being kind or cooperative, which is why the two traits are measured separately."},{"id":"U4-512","section":"4.5","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"A psychologist reviews ten years of records about Daniela and notes that she has been punctual, careful and reliable at school, at her part-time job and with her friends. The psychologist concludes that these are lasting characteristics that lead Daniela to respond in similar ways across many situations. This conclusion best reflects which approach to personality?","options":["Psychodynamic theory","Trait theory","Humanistic theory","Social-cognitive theory"],"answer":1,"rationale":"Trait theories describe personality as a set of enduring characteristics that lead to typical responses across situations. A social-cognitive theorist would focus more on how Daniela's beliefs, behavior and environment interact."},{"id":"U4-513","section":"4.5","skill":"Concept Recall","difficulty":1,"stem":"Psychologists who use the Big Five model typically measure a person's traits with which kind of assessment?","options":["A projective test in which the person describes what they see in ambiguous images","Free association, in which the person says whatever comes to mind","A personality inventory in which the person rates how well many statements describe them","An interview focused on whether the person received unconditional positive regard as a child"],"answer":2,"rationale":"Trait psychologists use personality inventories: questionnaires with many self-report items whose responses are organized into trait scores, often with the help of factor analysis. Projective tests and free association are psychodynamic methods."},{"id":"U4-514","section":"4.5","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"To understand why a client avoids speaking up at work, Dr. Abubakar watches the client during a realistic team meeting, asks what the client expects will happen if they share an idea, and asks how confident the client feels about speaking in groups. Dr. Abubakar's approach to assessing personality is most consistent with which perspective?","options":["Psychodynamic","Humanistic","Trait","Social-cognitive"],"answer":3,"rationale":"Social-cognitive psychologists assess personality by observing behavior in specific situations and asking about the person's expectations and self-efficacy, which interact with the environment. A trait psychologist would more likely use a standardized inventory."},{"id":"U4-515","section":"4.5","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":3,"stem":"Leila scores high in extraversion on a personality inventory. Her history teacher is surprised, because Leila rarely speaks in that class, where the teacher calls on students without warning. At family gatherings and with her football team, however, Leila is talkative and lively. Which explanation would a social-cognitive theorist most likely give?","options":["Leila's behavior depends on the interaction between her personal characteristics and the situation, so a trait score alone cannot predict how she acts in every setting.","The inventory must be invalid, because a person's traits should produce identical behavior in all situations.","Leila is unconsciously repressing anxiety about history class, which causes her silence.","Leila's silence in history class reveals that her true trait is low extraversion, so her behavior in other settings should be disregarded."],"answer":0,"rationale":"Social-cognitive theory emphasizes that personal factors, behavior and environment interact (reciprocal determinism), so behavior can vary across situations. Traits predict typical behavior on average, not identical behavior everywhere, so the inventory is not necessarily invalid."},{"id":"U4-516","section":"4.5","skill":"Data Interpretation","difficulty":2,"stimulus":{"type":"table","caption":"Factor loadings from a hypothetical personality questionnaire (higher numbers mean an item is more strongly related to that factor)","headers":["Questionnaire item","Factor 1","Factor 2"],"rows":[["I make plans and stick to them","0.12","0.81"],["I start conversations with strangers","0.79","0.08"],["I finish tasks on time","0.05","0.77"],["I feel energized at large events","0.83","0.10"],["I keep my belongings neat and organized","0.09","0.74"],["I enjoy being the center of attention","0.76","0.14"]]},"stem":"A researcher gave a personality questionnaire to 500 students and used factor analysis to group the items. Based on the table, which labels best fit the two factors?","options":["Factor 1 is conscientiousness, and Factor 2 is extraversion.","Factor 1 is extraversion, and Factor 2 is conscientiousness.","Factor 1 is agreeableness, and Factor 2 is openness to experience.","Factor 1 is extraversion, and Factor 2 is emotional stability."],"answer":1,"rationale":"Items about starting conversations, feeling energized at events and enjoying attention load highly on Factor 1, which fits extraversion. Items about planning, finishing on time and neatness load highly on Factor 2, which fits conscientiousness. Factor analysis groups items whose responses are strongly correlated."},{"id":"U4-517","section":"4.5","skill":"Research Methods and Design","difficulty":2,"stimulus":{"type":"passage","text":"Dr. Castillo gave a Big Five personality inventory to 240 high school seniors and collected each student's grade point average (GPA) from school records. She found a correlation of +0.42 between conscientiousness scores and GPA. She did not manipulate any variables."},"stem":"Which conclusion is best supported by Dr. Castillo's study?","options":["Being conscientious causes students to earn higher grades.","Students with higher conscientiousness scores tended to have higher GPAs, but the study cannot show that conscientiousness causes higher grades.","Earning high grades causes students to become more conscientious.","Because the correlation is less than +1.00, there is no meaningful relationship between conscientiousness and GPA."],"answer":1,"rationale":"No variables were manipulated, so this is a correlational study: it shows a positive relationship but cannot establish cause and effect in either direction. A correlation of +0.42 is a moderate positive relationship, not the absence of one."},{"id":"U4-518","section":"4.5","skill":"Data Interpretation","difficulty":3,"stimulus":{"type":"line","title":"Average trait scores by age group (hypothetical cross-sectional study)","xLabel":"Age group (years)","yLabel":"Average score (1 to 5 scale)","series":[{"name":"Emotional stability","points":[[20,3.0],[30,3.2],[40,3.4],[50,3.6],[60,3.7]]},{"name":"Openness to experience","points":[[20,3.8],[30,3.7],[40,3.6],[50,3.5],[60,3.4]]}]},"stem":"Researchers gave the same personality inventory, at one point in time, to adults in five different age groups. Which conclusion is best supported by the graph and the study design?","options":["Each participant's emotional stability rose by 0.7 points as they aged from 20 to 60.","Openness to experience dropped sharply after age 40, while emotional stability stayed the same.","Aging causes people to become less open to new experiences.","Older groups scored higher on emotional stability and slightly lower on openness, though these differences might partly reflect generational differences between the groups."],"answer":3,"rationale":"Across age groups, emotional stability rises from 3.0 to 3.7 and openness falls gradually from 3.8 to 3.4. Because this cross-sectional design compares different people of different ages, it cannot show how any individual changed, and the differences could partly be cohort effects."},{"id":"U4-519","section":"4.5","skill":"Research Methods and Design","difficulty":2,"stimulus":{"type":"passage","text":"Dr. Okafor randomly assigned 80 students to two groups. Group 1 completed a series of logic puzzles that started easy and became gradually harder, and every student succeeded on each puzzle. Group 2 spent the same amount of time reading a short article about logic puzzles. Afterward, all students rated their confidence in solving logic puzzles (1 to 10) and then attempted a very difficult puzzle. The researcher recorded how many minutes each student kept trying before giving up."},"stem":"In Dr. Okafor's experiment, which is the independent variable?","options":["Whether students completed the graduated puzzle series or read the article","Students' confidence ratings after the activity","The number of minutes students kept trying on the very difficult puzzle","The difficulty of the final puzzle"],"answer":0,"rationale":"The independent variable is the factor the researcher manipulates, here the type of activity each group received. Confidence ratings (a measure of self-efficacy) and persistence time are dependent variables, and the final puzzle was the same for everyone."},{"id":"U4-520","section":"4.5","skill":"Data Interpretation","difficulty":2,"stimulus":{"type":"bar","title":"Noor's Big Five inventory results","xLabel":"Trait","yLabel":"Percentile score","categories":["Openness","Conscientiousness","Extraversion","Agreeableness","Emotional stability"],"series":[{"name":"Noor","values":[88,22,85,60,55]}],"yMax":100},"stem":"Noor completed a Big Five personality inventory, and her percentile scores are shown in the graph. Based on these results, which description of Noor is most likely accurate?","options":["She is quiet and reserved, but very organized and careful with deadlines.","She worries constantly and has difficulty trusting other people.","She enjoys new ideas and lively social events, but she may have trouble staying organized and finishing tasks on time.","She prefers familiar routines and avoids unfamiliar activities, but she is very dependable."],"answer":2,"rationale":"High openness (88th percentile) and high extraversion (85th) suggest curiosity and enjoyment of social activity, while low conscientiousness (22nd) suggests difficulty with organization and deadlines. Her agreeableness and emotional stability are near average, so claims about distrust or constant worry are not supported."},{"id":"U4-601","section":"4.6","skill":"Concept Recall","difficulty":1,"stem":"According to drive-reduction theory, what is the main source of motivation?","options":["A physiological need creates an aroused tension state that pushes a person to act in ways that restore homeostasis.","People seek out rewards in the environment even when they have no physical need.","People try to keep their level of stimulation at an optimal point, neither too high nor too low.","People must resolve choices between goals that have attractive and unattractive features."],"answer":0,"rationale":"Drive-reduction theory says a physical need (such as a lack of water) produces a drive, and behavior that reduces the drive returns the body to homeostasis, a balanced internal state. The other statements describe incentive theory, arousal theory and Lewin's conflict theory."},{"id":"U4-602","section":"4.6","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"On a quiet Saturday, Mei has eaten, slept well and finished all her homework. Even so, she soon feels restless, so she starts a difficult jigsaw puzzle and then convinces her cousin to explore a new walking trail. Which theory best explains why Mei seeks activity even though none of her physical needs are unmet?","options":["Drive-reduction theory","Arousal theory","Instinct theory","Lewin's motivational conflicts theory"],"answer":1,"rationale":"Arousal theory proposes that people are motivated to maintain an optimal level of arousal, so when stimulation is too low they look for something interesting to do. Drive-reduction theory struggles to explain this because Mei has no physical need to reduce."},{"id":"U4-603","section":"4.6","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Coach Adeyemi tracks how his football goalkeeper performs in penalty shootouts. When the goalkeeper is very relaxed, he reacts slowly. When he is extremely anxious, he dives too early and misjudges the ball. He saves the most shots when he feels alert and moderately keyed up. This pattern is best explained by","options":["incentive theory","drive-reduction theory","the Yerkes-Dodson law","homeostasis"],"answer":2,"rationale":"The Yerkes-Dodson law, part of arousal theory, states that performance is best at a moderate level of arousal and declines when arousal is too low or too high."},{"id":"U4-604","section":"4.6","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":1,"stem":"Gabriel usually dislikes working weekend shifts at the cafe. When his manager announces a bonus for anyone who works three extra weekend shifts this month, Gabriel immediately signs up for all three. Which theory of motivation best explains Gabriel's decision?","options":["Drive-reduction theory","Sensation-seeking theory","Arousal theory","Incentive theory"],"answer":3,"rationale":"Incentive theory explains behavior as being pulled by external rewards, such as a bonus. Gabriel is not reducing a biological need or seeking stimulation; he is working for the reward."},{"id":"U4-605","section":"4.6","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":1,"stem":"Zara spends several hours each week drawing her own comic stories. She does not show them to anyone, they are not graded, and no one rewards her for them. She says she simply finds the process satisfying. According to self-determination theory, Zara's drawing is mainly driven by","options":["intrinsic motivation","extrinsic motivation","a fixed instinct","drive reduction"],"answer":0,"rationale":"Intrinsic motivation is doing an activity for its own sake because it is enjoyable or satisfying. Extrinsic motivation involves outside rewards or pressures, which are absent here."},{"id":"U4-606","section":"4.6","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":1,"stem":"A psychology teacher asks the class to choose the example that best fits the definition of an instinct: an innate and typically fixed pattern of behavior that appears in response to certain stimuli. Which example should the students choose?","options":["A child learns to say \"thank you\" after seeing her older brother praised for doing so.","Newly hatched sea turtles that have never seen the ocean crawl toward the bright horizon over the water.","A teenager checks his phone whenever it buzzes because messages have been rewarding in the past.","An adult feels hungry at 1:00 p.m. because that is when she usually eats lunch."],"answer":1,"rationale":"Instincts are unlearned, fixed patterns of behavior shown by members of a species, as seen in many non-human animals. The other examples are learned through observation, reinforcement or routine, and humans do not seem to show true instinctual behavior."},{"id":"U4-607","section":"4.6","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Rashid has been accepted to two summer programs that both excite him: a coding camp in Abu Dhabi and a marine biology program in Fujairah. The programs run on the same dates, so he must choose only one. According to Lewin's motivational conflicts theory, Rashid is facing","options":["an approach-avoidance conflict","an avoidance-avoidance conflict","an approach-approach conflict","a conflict between intrinsic and extrinsic motivation"],"answer":2,"rationale":"An approach-approach conflict occurs when a person must choose between two attractive options. Neither program has an unpleasant feature that Rashid wants to avoid."},{"id":"U4-608","section":"4.6","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"On Saturday morning, Ines's parents tell her she must either scrub the bathroom tiles or reorganize the cluttered garage before lunch. She dislikes both jobs equally and keeps putting off the decision. According to Lewin's theory, which type of conflict is Ines experiencing?","options":["Approach-approach","Approach-avoidance","Boredom susceptibility","Avoidance-avoidance"],"answer":3,"rationale":"An avoidance-avoidance conflict is a choice between two unattractive options. People in this kind of conflict often delay deciding, as Ines does."},{"id":"U4-609","section":"4.6","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":3,"stem":"Kofi has been invited to join the school's top debate team. He would love to compete and believes it would strengthen his university applications. However, the team practices at 6:00 a.m. three days a week, and members must complete long research assignments that Kofi finds tedious. Kofi keeps changing his mind about whether to join. Which conflict best describes his situation?","options":["Approach-avoidance, because one goal has both attractive and unattractive features","Avoidance-avoidance, because the early practices and the research assignments are both unpleasant","Approach-approach, because competing and strengthening his applications are both attractive","An arousal conflict, because the early practices would push his arousal above the optimal level"],"answer":0,"rationale":"Kofi is both drawn toward and pushed away from the same single goal, joining the team, which is an approach-avoidance conflict; wavering back and forth is typical of it. The two unpleasant features belong to the same option, so this is not a choice between two unwanted alternatives."},{"id":"U4-610","section":"4.6","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"During a family holiday, Dina asks to go skydiving, then wants to try high-speed dune bashing in the desert and an advanced rock-climbing route. She says the physical risk and the speed are exactly what make these activities exciting for her. Which type of sensation seeking does Dina most clearly show?","options":["Experience seeking","Thrill and adventure seeking","Disinhibition","Boredom susceptibility"],"answer":1,"rationale":"Thrill and adventure seeking is the desire for physically risky, fast or exciting activities like skydiving. Experience seeking is more about new sensations through travel, music, art and unconventional ways of living, not physical danger."},{"id":"U4-611","section":"4.6","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":3,"stem":"Carlos says he feels most alive at loud, crowded celebrations where he can let go of his usual social restraints. He jumps on stage to dance with the band, shouts jokes to strangers and acts on impulse without worrying what anyone thinks. He has little interest in dangerous sports or in traveling to unusual places. Which type of sensation seeking best describes Carlos?","options":["Thrill and adventure seeking","Experience seeking","Disinhibition","Boredom susceptibility"],"answer":2,"rationale":"Disinhibition is seeking stimulation through uninhibited, spontaneous social activity and dropping one's usual social restraints. Carlos is not drawn to physical danger (thrill and adventure seeking) or to new places and ideas (experience seeking)."},{"id":"U4-612","section":"4.6","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Arjun complains whenever his weekly routine repeats. He refuses to rewatch films, gets irritated in conversations with people who are predictable, and becomes restless after only a few minutes of any repetitive task. He does not especially enjoy risky activities. Which type of sensation seeking best describes Arjun?","options":["Thrill and adventure seeking","Disinhibition","Experience seeking","Boredom susceptibility"],"answer":3,"rationale":"Boredom susceptibility is a strong dislike of repetition, routine and predictable people, with restlessness when things stay the same. It does not require a taste for physical danger."},{"id":"U4-613","section":"4.6","skill":"Concept Recall","difficulty":1,"stem":"Which hormone is released by fat tissue and signals the hypothalamus that the body has enough stored energy, reducing feelings of hunger over time?","options":["Leptin","Ghrelin","Melatonin","Oxytocin"],"answer":0,"rationale":"Leptin, released by fat cells, acts on the hypothalamus to signal satiety. Ghrelin has the opposite effect: it is released mainly by an empty stomach and increases hunger."},{"id":"U4-614","section":"4.6","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Farid ate a large breakfast at 10:30 a.m. and was not hungry afterward. At 1:00 p.m., his family sat down together for their usual weekend lunch, with his favorite dishes on the table. Farid began to feel hungry and ate a full plate. Which explanation best accounts for Farid's eating?","options":["His stomach was empty, so it released leptin, which increased his hunger.","External cues such as the usual mealtime, the sight of food and the family gathering triggered his desire to eat.","His fat cells released ghrelin, which signaled that his energy stores were low.","Eating with family is an instinct that all humans display in the same fixed way."],"answer":1,"rationale":"Eating is influenced by external factors like the time of day, the presence of appealing food and social meals, not only by hormones. The hormone statements reverse the roles of ghrelin (from the stomach, increases hunger) and leptin (from fat tissue, reduces hunger)."},{"id":"U4-615","section":"4.6","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"After moving to a new school, Lina is left out when classmates form project groups and is not added to the class group chat. She feels hurt, so she starts sitting near a friendly group at lunch, laughing at their jokes and offering to help with their assignments. Lina's behavior is best explained by","options":["drive-reduction theory, because she is restoring a physiological balance","thrill and adventure seeking, because she enjoys risky social situations","the need to belong, a motivation to form and keep close social connections","incentive theory, because she expects to be paid for helping"],"answer":2,"rationale":"The need to belong is a strong motivation to form and maintain relationships, and being excluded is painful, so people often work harder to be accepted. Lina's actions aim at social acceptance, not a biological need or an outside reward."},{"id":"U4-616","section":"4.6","skill":"Data Interpretation","difficulty":2,"stimulus":{"type":"line","title":"Ghrelin levels across one day for a participant who ate at 8:00, 13:00 and 19:00 (hypothetical)","xLabel":"Time of day (hour, 24-hour clock)","yLabel":"Ghrelin level (relative units)","series":[{"name":"Ghrelin","points":[[6,55],[7,70],[8,82],[9,40],[10,45],[11,60],[12,75],[13,85],[14,42],[15,45],[16,55],[17,65],[18,78],[19,88],[20,45],[21,50]]}],"bands":[{"from":8,"to":9,"label":"Breakfast"},{"from":13,"to":14,"label":"Lunch"},{"from":19,"to":20,"label":"Dinner"}]},"stem":"A researcher measured a participant's blood ghrelin levels throughout one day. Which conclusion is best supported by the graph?","options":["Ghrelin was highest just after each meal, consistent with a role in signaling fullness.","Ghrelin stayed constant across the day, so time of day does not affect hunger signals.","Ghrelin rose steadily from morning to night and never decreased.","Ghrelin rose in the hours before each meal and dropped after eating, consistent with its role in signaling hunger."],"answer":3,"rationale":"The graph peaks at the start of each meal (8, 13 and 19 hours) and falls right after eating. Ghrelin, released mainly by the stomach, increases hunger, so rising before meals and dropping afterward fits that role."},{"id":"U4-617","section":"4.6","skill":"Data Interpretation","difficulty":3,"stimulus":{"type":"bar","title":"Average accuracy by arousal level and task difficulty (hypothetical)","xLabel":"Arousal level","yLabel":"Percentage correct","categories":["Low","Moderate","High"],"series":[{"name":"Simple task","values":[70,84,90]},{"name":"Difficult task","values":[60,80,48]}],"yMax":100},"stem":"Participants completed either a simple task (crossing out every letter E on a page) or a difficult task (solving multistep logic problems) under low, moderate or high arousal created by increasing time pressure. Which conclusion is best supported by the results?","options":["High arousal helped performance on the simple task but hurt performance on the difficult task, consistent with the Yerkes-Dodson law.","Higher arousal improved performance on both tasks, so arousal should always be as high as possible.","Arousal level had no effect on performance for either task.","Low arousal produced the best performance on both tasks, consistent with drive-reduction theory."],"answer":0,"rationale":"On the simple task, accuracy kept rising up to high arousal (90 percent), but on the difficult task it peaked at moderate arousal (80 percent) and fell at high arousal (48 percent). The Yerkes-Dodson law predicts that the best level of arousal is lower for difficult tasks than for simple ones."},{"id":"U4-618","section":"4.6","skill":"Data Interpretation","difficulty":3,"stimulus":{"type":"bar","title":"Average minutes spent on origami during free time (hypothetical)","xLabel":"Phase of study","yLabel":"Minutes","categories":["Week 1 (before any rewards)","Week 4 (after rewards ended)"],"series":[{"name":"Reward group","values":[18,8]},{"name":"No-reward group","values":[17,16]}],"yMax":20},"stem":"Dr. Haddad selected students who already enjoyed making origami models and randomly assigned them to two groups. During weeks 2 and 3, the reward group earned a gift card for each model completed, while the no-reward group received nothing. All rewards then stopped. Which conclusion is best supported by the results and self-determination theory?","options":["The rewards strengthened the reward group's interest, so they spent more free time on origami after rewards stopped.","The external rewards appear to have reduced the reward group's intrinsic motivation, so they spent less free time on origami once the rewards ended.","The no-reward group lost interest in origami because they had no incentive to continue.","Because both groups started at similar levels, the rewards had no effect on behavior."],"answer":1,"rationale":"Both groups started at about 17 to 18 minutes, but only the reward group dropped (to 8 minutes) after rewards stopped. Adding external rewards to an already enjoyable activity can shift the reason for doing it from intrinsic to extrinsic, so interest falls once the rewards are removed."},{"id":"U4-619","section":"4.6","skill":"Research Methods and Design","difficulty":2,"stimulus":{"type":"passage","text":"Dr. Tanaka had 60 participants join an online chat with three \"teammates\" to plan a group project. The teammates were actually scripted by the researchers. Participants were randomly assigned to one of two conditions. In the included condition, the teammates replied to and built on the participant's ideas. In the ignored condition, the teammates replied to one another but never to the participant. Afterward, participants rated their sense of belonging (1 to 10) and were asked whether they would volunteer for another group task."},"stem":"Which result would best support the hypothesis that being excluded motivates people to seek new social connections?","options":["Participants in both conditions reported the same sense of belonging and volunteered at the same rate.","Participants in the included condition reported a lower sense of belonging than those in the ignored condition.","Participants in the ignored condition reported a lower sense of belonging and were more likely to volunteer for another group task than those in the included condition.","Participants in the ignored condition reported a higher sense of belonging and were less likely to volunteer for another group task."],"answer":2,"rationale":"If exclusion threatens the need to belong, ignored participants should feel less belonging and try harder to reconnect, for example by volunteering for another group task. Because participants were randomly assigned, such a difference could be attributed to the condition."},{"id":"U4-620","section":"4.6","skill":"Data Interpretation","difficulty":2,"stimulus":{"type":"table","caption":"Rafael's scores on a sensation-seeking scale (each subscale scored 0 to 10)","headers":["Subscale","Score"],"rows":[["Thrill and adventure seeking","2"],["Experience seeking","9"],["Disinhibition","3"],["Boredom susceptibility","4"]]},"stem":"Rafael completed a sensation-seeking scale during a careers and interests workshop. Based on his scores in the table, which weekend plan would Rafael most likely find most appealing?","options":["Bungee jumping from a tall bridge with a group of friends","Rewatching his favorite film at home, as he does most weekends","A noisy, crowded celebration where he can drop his usual social restraints","Visiting an unfamiliar city to explore its street food, unusual music and local art"],"answer":3,"rationale":"Rafael scores highest on experience seeking, the desire for new sensations through travel, art, music and unfamiliar ways of living. His low thrill and adventure seeking and disinhibition scores make bungee jumping and an uninhibited celebration less appealing, and rewatching a familiar film offers no novelty."},{"id":"U4-701","section":"4.7","skill":"Concept Recall","difficulty":1,"stem":"Psychologists describe emotion, also called affect, as different from other mental processes. Which statement best describes how psychologists define emotion?","options":["A process of weighing evidence logically in order to reach a conclusion","A store of factual knowledge built up through education and experience","A complex psychological process, distinct from reasoning or knowledge, that reflects internal and external factors affecting a person","A simple reflex triggered by a stimulus without any involvement of thought or bodily change"],"answer":2,"rationale":"Emotion is treated as its own complex process, separate from reasoning and from knowledge, and shaped by internal factors (such as bodily arousal) and external factors (such as the situation). Because emotions involve both physiological and cognitive elements, they are not simple reflexes."},{"id":"U4-702","section":"4.7","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"While hiking in the mountains near Hatta, Farida steps on a loose rock that slides away beneath her. Her heart pounds and her legs start to shake. Later she explains, \"I only realized how frightened I was when I noticed my legs trembling.\" Farida's explanation best matches which view of emotion?","options":["The felt emotion and the bodily arousal occur at the same moment, and neither causes the other","Bodily arousal comes first, and the emotion is experienced as a result of perceiving that arousal","Positive and negative emotions change how widely a person attends to the environment","Emotions are shown or hidden according to the expectations of a person's culture"],"answer":1,"rationale":"Farida describes her body reacting first and her fear following from noticing that reaction, which is the sequence proposed by theories that place physiology before the emotional experience (often taught as the James-Lange view). A simultaneous view would say the trembling and the fear happened together."},{"id":"U4-703","section":"4.7","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Dr. Okafor describes a driver who swerves to avoid a camel crossing a desert road. She argues that the brain sends signals to the body, producing a racing heart, and to the cortex, producing the conscious feeling of fear, at the same instant. Dr. Okafor's explanation is most consistent with which claim?","options":["The physiological response must be cognitively labeled before any emotion can be felt","The feeling of fear causes the racing heart a moment later","The racing heart causes the feeling of fear a moment later","The physiological response and the subjective experience of emotion occur simultaneously"],"answer":3,"rationale":"Dr. Okafor says the bodily response and the conscious feeling are triggered at the same time, so neither causes the other. This simultaneous view (often taught as the Cannon-Bard view) contrasts with theories that place physiology first and the feeling second."},{"id":"U4-704","section":"4.7","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Backstage before the school talent show, Hessa's heart races and her stomach flutters. She glances at her friends in the wings, who are laughing and cheering for every act, and tells herself, \"This is excitement, not nerves.\" She walks on stage feeling thrilled. Which idea about emotion does Hessa's experience best illustrate?","options":["The same physiological arousal can be experienced as different emotions depending on how it is cognitively labeled","Smiling at her friends produced the physical arousal she felt in her body","Emotions are expressed in the same way by people in every culture","Negative emotions narrow attention and reduce the options a person considers"],"answer":0,"rationale":"Hessa's body was aroused either way; she used cues from the situation (her cheering friends) to label the arousal as excitement, and that label shaped the emotion she felt. Theories that require a cognitive label for arousal (often taught as the Schachter-Singer two-factor theory) predict exactly this."},{"id":"U4-705","section":"4.7","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":3,"stem":"Dr. Castillo is evaluating a claim made by an early theory of emotion: that a person feels an emotion only after sensing the body's physiological reaction to an event. Which of the following findings would most directly challenge this claim?","options":["Participants report feeling afraid only after they notice that their heart rate has risen","Participants whose bodily arousal is reduced by a medication still report strong emotions as quickly as other participants","Participants who hold a smile while watching cartoons rate them as funnier than participants who frown","Participants in a cheerful mood list more activities they would like to try than participants in a neutral mood"],"answer":1,"rationale":"If emotions depend on first sensing bodily arousal, then reducing that arousal should weaken or delay the emotion, so strong and quick emotions without the usual arousal challenge the claim. Feeling fear after noticing a racing heart and the smiling finding both fit the idea that physiology comes first."},{"id":"U4-706","section":"4.7","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":1,"stem":"Before his debate round, Rashid feels tense and gloomy. His coach tells him to stretch his mouth into a wide smile and hold it for a full minute, claiming that the smile itself will help him feel more positive. The coach's advice is based on which idea?","options":["Display rules","The broaden-and-build theory","The universality of emotional expression","The facial-feedback hypothesis"],"answer":3,"rationale":"The facial-feedback hypothesis proposes that facial expressions influence the emotion a person experiences, so holding a smile could make Rashid feel more positive. Research testing this idea has produced mixed results, so the effect may be small or inconsistent."},{"id":"U4-707","section":"4.7","skill":"Research Methods and Design","difficulty":1,"stimulus":{"type":"passage","text":"Dr. Ishikawa tells participants that she is testing a new sensor that records facial muscle activity. Participants are randomly assigned to one of two groups. One group is instructed to pull their cheeks up toward their eyes, as in a smile. The other group is instructed to pull their eyebrows down and together, as in a frown. While holding the position, every participant rates how pleasant they find the same set of 20 nature photographs on a scale from 1 to 10."},"stem":"Dr. Ishikawa is testing the facial-feedback hypothesis using the procedure described. What is the independent variable in her study?","options":["The pleasantness ratings participants give to the photographs","The facial muscle position participants are instructed to hold","The set of 20 nature photographs shown to every participant","The cover story about testing a facial muscle sensor"],"answer":1,"rationale":"The independent variable is the factor the researcher manipulates, here the smile-like or frown-like position assigned to each group. The pleasantness ratings are the dependent variable, and the photographs are held constant across groups."},{"id":"U4-708","section":"4.7","skill":"Data Interpretation","difficulty":3,"stimulus":{"type":"bar","title":"Mean Pleasantness Ratings of Photographs in Three Laboratories","xLabel":"Laboratory","yLabel":"Mean pleasantness rating (1 to 10)","categories":["Lab 1","Lab 2","Lab 3"],"series":[{"name":"Smile-like position","values":[6.9,6.1,5.8]},{"name":"Frown-like position","values":[5.3,5.9,6.0]}],"yMax":10},"stem":"Three laboratories ran the same study testing whether holding a smile-like or a frown-like facial position changes how pleasant people find a set of photographs. Their results are shown in the graph. Which conclusion is best supported by the data?","options":["Holding a smile-like position strongly raised pleasantness ratings in all three laboratories","Holding a frown-like position raised pleasantness ratings in every laboratory","Support for the facial-feedback hypothesis was inconsistent across laboratories, with a clear effect in only one","Facial position had no effect in any laboratory, so the facial-feedback hypothesis is disproven"],"answer":2,"rationale":"Lab 1 shows a clear difference (6.9 versus 5.3), Lab 2 shows only a tiny difference, and Lab 3 shows a slight reversal. This pattern matches the mixed results reported in research on the facial-feedback hypothesis, and one set of studies cannot fully disprove it."},{"id":"U4-709","section":"4.7","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":1,"stem":"Ms. Santos shows one class a short, joyful video before asking students to plan a charity event, and shows another class a tense, upsetting video before the same task. The first class suggests many more varied and creative ideas than the second. Which theory best predicts this difference?","options":["The facial-feedback hypothesis","The theory that arousal and emotion occur simultaneously","Display rules","The broaden-and-build theory"],"answer":3,"rationale":"The broaden-and-build theory proposes that positive emotions broaden awareness and encourage new thoughts and actions, while negative emotions narrow thinking. The joyful class was in a more positive emotional state, so its wider range of ideas fits the theory."},{"id":"U4-710","section":"4.7","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Kwame becomes very anxious halfway through a timed chemistry exam. He keeps rereading one problem he cannot solve, never considers skipping it, and does not think of the alternative method his teacher showed in class. According to the broaden-and-build theory, which explanation best accounts for his behavior?","options":["His anxiety broadened his awareness, so he was distracted by too many possible strategies","His frowning facial expression was the only cause of the anxiety he felt","Negative emotions such as anxiety tend to narrow a person's thinking and range of actions","His culture's display rules prevented him from showing anxiety in the exam hall"],"answer":2,"rationale":"The broaden-and-build theory states that negative emotions reduce awareness and narrow thinking and action, which fits Kwame's fixation on a single problem. Broadening of awareness is linked to positive emotions, not to anxiety."},{"id":"U4-711","section":"4.7","skill":"Data Interpretation","difficulty":2,"stimulus":{"type":"table","caption":"Mean number of different solutions listed after each film clip","headers":["Film clip condition","Mean number of different solutions"],"rows":[["Amusing clip","11.2"],["Neutral clip","8.4"],["Frightening clip","5.9"]]},"stem":"Students were randomly assigned to watch an amusing, a neutral or a frightening film clip. Each student then listed as many different solutions as possible to the problem of an overcrowded school cafeteria. The results are shown in the table. Which conclusion is best supported by the data?","options":["Compared with the neutral clip, the amusing clip was followed by more solutions and the frightening clip by fewer","The frightening clip produced the widest range of solutions because fear increases alertness","Emotion had no effect, because all three conditions produced roughly the same number of solutions","The amusing clip reduced the number of solutions compared with the neutral clip"],"answer":0,"rationale":"The amusing condition (11.2) is above the neutral condition (8.4), and the frightening condition (5.9) is below it. This pattern fits the broaden-and-build theory: positive emotions broaden thinking and negative emotions narrow it."},{"id":"U4-712","section":"4.7","skill":"Concept Recall","difficulty":1,"stem":"Research on the universality of emotion suggests that some emotions may be commonly experienced and expressed across cultures. Which list contains only emotions commonly identified in this research?","options":["Guilt, pride, embarrassment, envy, shame and boredom","Happiness, sadness, pride, curiosity, nostalgia and shame","Anger, disgust, sadness, happiness, surprise and fear","Fear, loneliness, gratitude, hope, guilt and interest"],"answer":2,"rationale":"Anger, disgust, sadness, happiness, surprise and fear are the emotions most often proposed as commonly experienced across cultures. Emotions such as pride, shame and guilt are not usually included in this list, and research on universality as a whole has produced mixed results."},{"id":"U4-713","section":"4.7","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Dr. Mensah visits a remote farming community with very little access to television or the internet. She reads short stories aloud, such as one about discovering that food has gone bad, and asks villagers to point to the photographed face that matches each story. Their choices closely match those of university students in a large city. These results best support which claim?","options":["Display rules for showing emotion are identical in every culture","Facial expressions of emotion are learned mainly by watching media","Emotions cannot be experienced until they are given a cognitive label","Some facial expressions of emotion may be recognized across very different cultures"],"answer":3,"rationale":"Because villagers with little media exposure matched faces to stories much as city students did, the findings suggest that some expressions, such as disgust at spoiled food, may be universally recognized. Recognizing expressions is different from following the same display rules, which do vary among cultures."},{"id":"U4-714","section":"4.7","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":3,"stem":"In a cross-cultural study, Dr. Petrova finds that participants in two communities recognize smiling faces as happy with nearly equal accuracy. However, participants in one community correctly identify fear and surprise faces far less often than participants in the other, often confusing the two expressions. Which conclusion is most defensible?","options":["All facial expressions are fully universal, so the lower accuracy must reflect a recording error","No facial expressions of emotion are shared across cultures","Fear and surprise are not genuine emotions, because people sometimes confuse them","Support for universal emotional expression is mixed, with some expressions recognized more consistently across cultures than others"],"answer":3,"rationale":"Happiness was recognized equally well in both communities, but fear and surprise were not, so the results neither fully support nor fully reject universality. This matches the mixed results found in research on whether emotional expression is universal."},{"id":"U4-715","section":"4.7","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Sofia, an exchange student, notices that when her host siblings receive a gift they do not like, they always smile and thank the giver warmly, even though they later admit feeling disappointed. In her home community, people often show mild disappointment openly in a joking way. The difference Sofia notices is best explained by","options":["the facial-feedback hypothesis","cultural display rules","the broaden-and-build theory","the universality of basic emotions"],"answer":1,"rationale":"Display rules are the cultural norms about when and how emotions should be shown. Both groups may feel similar disappointment, but they differ in how openly they are expected to express it."},{"id":"U4-716","section":"4.7","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":3,"stem":"Dr. Tan interviews teenagers from two communities about being praised in front of the whole class. Teenagers from one community say they genuinely feel embarrassed and uncomfortable when this happens. Teenagers from the other community say they genuinely feel proud and pleased. Which conclusion best fits these findings?","options":["The two communities feel the same emotion but follow different rules about hiding it","The two communities differ in which emotion the same situation tends to elicit","Facial expressions of pride and embarrassment produce the feelings the teenagers report","The teenagers in one community are unable to experience pride"],"answer":1,"rationale":"The teenagers report actually feeling different emotions, so the communities differ in the elicitors of emotion, meaning which situations trigger which feelings. Display rules would explain differences in how a shared feeling is shown, not differences in the feeling itself."},{"id":"U4-717","section":"4.7","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"At a large family wedding, Aditya notices that his younger cousins are allowed to cry loudly or stamp their feet when they are upset. The teenagers and adults, however, are expected to keep a calm face in front of the elders, even when they feel frustrated. This observation best illustrates that","options":["display rules within a culture can differ depending on a person's age","children experience emotions before cognitive labeling, but adults do not","frustration is not an emotion that people in different cultures experience","positive emotions broaden the range of actions people take"],"answer":0,"rationale":"Within the same family and culture, the expectations for showing emotion are different for young children than for teenagers and adults. Display rules can regulate expression by age, as well as by other factors such as socioeconomic class."},{"id":"U4-718","section":"4.7","skill":"Research Methods and Design","difficulty":2,"stimulus":{"type":"passage","text":"Dr. Rivera surveys 300 parents in one city and groups them by household income level. Parents in the higher-income group more often report teaching their children to hide anger in public places such as shops and restaurants. Parents in the lower-income group more often report that showing some anger in public is acceptable when a child has been treated unfairly."},"stem":"Based on the survey described, which conclusion is most appropriate?","options":["Household income causes children to feel less anger","Anger is not a universal emotion, because it is shown differently across income groups","Display rules for anger appear to differ by socioeconomic group in this city, but the survey cannot show that income itself causes the difference","Because the study is a survey, no differences between the groups can be described"],"answer":2,"rationale":"The survey describes differences in what parents teach about showing anger, which are display rules that can vary by socioeconomic class within a culture. Because income was not manipulated, the study cannot show that income causes the difference, and it measured rules for showing anger, not how much anger children feel."},{"id":"U4-719","section":"4.7","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Before a basketball final, Daniel drinks two strong cups of coffee and feels jittery, with a fast heartbeat. In the locker room, a teammate jokes about a shot Daniel missed last week. Daniel snaps back angrily and later says, \"I don't know why that annoyed me so much.\" Which explanation best fits Daniel's reaction?","options":["He interpreted the arousal caused by the coffee as anger because of the teasing in the situation","His angry facial expression was the only cause of his emotional reaction","His negative emotion broadened his awareness of his teammate's intentions","His team's display rules required him to show anger before a match"],"answer":0,"rationale":"Daniel's arousal actually came from caffeine, but the teasing gave him a reason to interpret that arousal as anger. Theories in which emotion depends on a cognitive label for arousal (often taught as the two-factor theory) predict this kind of misattribution."},{"id":"U4-720","section":"4.7","skill":"Research Methods and Design","difficulty":2,"stimulus":{"type":"passage","text":"To study how people label physical arousal, Dr. Khoury tells participants that in a few minutes they will give an unprepared speech in front of a panel of judges, which raises their heart rates. In reality, no speech will be given. After measuring their heart rates and asking them to describe their emotions, she thanks them and lets them leave without any further explanation."},"stem":"Which ethical problem is most clearly present in Dr. Khoury's study?","options":["Participants were not debriefed about the deception and the true purpose of the study","Any use of deception makes a study unethical, regardless of the procedures that follow","Heart rate is a physiological measure, which may not be collected in psychological research","Asking participants to describe their own emotions is a self-report method that ethics guidelines forbid"],"answer":0,"rationale":"Deception can be permitted when it is necessary and causes minimal harm, but participants must then be debriefed, told the truth about the study and given a chance to ask questions. Letting them leave still believing they might have to give a speech is the clear problem."},{"id":"U5-001","section":"0.1","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Two classmates, Layla and Joon-ho, both learn that their chemistry exam has been moved forward by a week. Layla sees the change as a chance to finish studying early and feels energized. Joon-ho believes the change means he will fail and feels overwhelmed. Which psychological perspective best explains why the same event produces such different levels of stress?","options":["Cognitive","Biological","Sociocultural","Evolutionary"],"answer":0,"rationale":"The cognitive perspective emphasizes appraisal: how a person interprets a stressor shapes how much stress it causes. The event is identical for both students, so the difference lies in their thinking, not in their biology or culture."},{"id":"U5-002","section":"5.1","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"After their football team loses a semifinal, Omar spends the evening reviewing match footage and writing down three drills to practice. His teammate Diego stays home, avoids talking about the match, and plays video games until late. Omar and Diego most clearly differ in which of the following?","options":["Fluid intelligence","Instincts","The fundamental attribution error","Coping strategies"],"answer":3,"rationale":"Coping strategies are the ways people respond to stressors. Omar tackles the problem directly, while Diego avoids it, so their coping differs; nothing in the scenario concerns intelligence, instincts or explanations of other people's behavior."},{"id":"U5-003","section":"5.1","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"For two years, Mrs. Santos has cared for her elderly father at home while also working night shifts at a hospital. She rarely sleeps more than five hours, and recently she has caught one infection after another and feels worn out most days. According to Selye's general adaptation syndrome, Mrs. Santos is most likely in which stage?","options":["Alarm","Exhaustion","Resistance","Recovery"],"answer":1,"rationale":"In the exhaustion stage, the body's resources are depleted after prolonged stress, and vulnerability to illness is greatest. Recovery is not one of Selye's stages, and resistance describes the earlier period when the body is still coping."},{"id":"U5-004","section":"0.3","skill":"Research Methods and Design","difficulty":2,"stem":"Dr. Nasser wants to know whether people who try to solve their problems directly also sleep better. She sends an online survey to 800 adults aged 25 to 50. The survey asks participants how many days in the past week they made a concrete plan to deal with a stressor, and it includes a standardized sleep-quality questionnaire. Which of the following best states the operational definitions of the variables in this study?","options":["Problem-focused coping and sleep","The number of days participants report making a concrete plan to deal with a stressor, and their scores on the sleep-quality questionnaire","The ages of the participants and the number of stressors they list","Whether participants experience stress and whether they feel rested"],"answer":1,"rationale":"An operational definition states exactly how a variable is measured. Reported days of planning and a questionnaire score are measurable; simply naming problem-focused coping and sleep identifies the concepts but not how they are measured."},{"id":"U5-005","section":"5.1","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Ayesha has just been selected to represent her school at an international robotics competition in Singapore. She feels nervous because the other teams are very experienced, but she is thrilled by the challenge and has started practicing every afternoon. Which concept best describes the stress Ayesha is experiencing?","options":["Distress","Diathesis-stress","Exhaustion","Eustress"],"answer":3,"rationale":"Eustress is stress that is viewed as a positive, motivating challenge. Ayesha feels pressure, but it pushes her to prepare and grow, so it is not the debilitating stress described as distress."},{"id":"U5-006","section":"5.1","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"While hiking in a wadi at dusk, Rashid hears a loud rustling in the rocks just beside the path. Instantly, his heart pounds, his breathing speeds up, and his muscles tense. Which of the following best describes Rashid's physiological response?","options":["Alarm reaction","Exhaustion stage","Parasympathetic rebound","Approach-avoidance conflict"],"answer":0,"rationale":"The alarm reaction is the first stage of the general adaptation syndrome, when a sudden stressor triggers the sympathetic fight-flight-freeze response. A parasympathetic response would calm the body, not speed it up."},{"id":"U5-007","section":"5.1","skill":"Concept Recall","difficulty":1,"stem":"Hans Selye's general adaptation syndrome was developed to describe which of the following?","options":["The order in which children develop language skills","The way traits combine to form a stable personality","The pattern of physical responses the body shows when it faces a stressor over time","The steps involved in solving a complex problem"],"answer":2,"rationale":"The general adaptation syndrome describes how the body responds to stress across three stages: alarm, resistance and exhaustion. It is not a theory of development, personality or problem solving."},{"id":"U5-008","section":"5.1","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Dr. Bello works in a hospital clinic for people recovering from heart surgery. She runs workshops on reducing stress, helps patients stick to exercise and diet plans, and studies how family support affects how quickly patients recover. Dr. Bello's work best reflects which field of psychology?","options":["Industrial-organizational psychology","Developmental psychology","Forensic psychology","Health psychology"],"answer":3,"rationale":"Health psychology examines how psychological, behavioral and social factors affect physical health and illness. Dr. Bello focuses on stress, health behaviors and social support in patients' physical recovery."},{"id":"U5-009","section":"0.1","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Before a lesson on stress, Maryam predicted that major life events, such as moving house, would harm health far more than small daily annoyances. Her teacher then explained that research shows small daily hassles, such as traffic and lost keys, can build up and also predict health problems. Maryam immediately told her friend, \"That is so obvious. I always knew little things add up.\" Maryam's reaction is most clearly an example of which of the following?","options":["Confirmation bias","Belief perseverance","Hindsight bias","The availability heuristic"],"answer":2,"rationale":"Hindsight bias is the tendency to believe, after learning an outcome, that one knew it all along. Maryam originally predicted the opposite, yet she now claims the finding was obvious; belief perseverance would mean holding on to her original view."},{"id":"U5-010","section":"5.1","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"For about fourteen months, Mateo has felt constant pressure: he works two part-time jobs, studies in the evenings, and worries about paying his family's rent. He rarely gets a break from this strain. Which of the following is most likely true of Mateo?","options":["His body has fully adapted, so he is now more resistant to infections than before.","His immune system is likely functioning less effectively because of the prolonged stress.","His long-term stress has most likely lowered his blood pressure.","His stress has most likely improved his ability to remember details."],"answer":1,"rationale":"Prolonged stress is linked to immune suppression, so Mateo is likely more vulnerable to illness. Chronic stress tends to raise blood pressure, not lower it, and it does not strengthen immunity."},{"id":"U5-011","section":"5.1","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Hiroshi spent three weeks studying late every night for his final exams. The day after his last exam, he came down with a bad cold and spent his first week of holiday in bed. Stress hormones most likely contributed to his illness by","options":["reducing the activity of lymphocytes, the white blood cells that fight infection","increasing the release of melatonin, which slows the immune system","shrinking the cerebellum, which controls the immune response","activating the parasympathetic nervous system for too long"],"answer":0,"rationale":"Stress hormones such as cortisol suppress lymphocytes, the white blood cells that help fight infection, which makes illness more likely. The cerebellum coordinates movement, and stress activates the sympathetic, not parasympathetic, system."},{"id":"U5-012","section":"5.1","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Nadia is a cashier at a busy supermarket during the week before a major holiday. For the third day in a row, long lines of customers keep forming, but Nadia has settled into a steady rhythm, stays focused, and handles each customer calmly while her body remains on alert. Which stage of the general adaptation syndrome is Nadia most likely experiencing?","options":["Resistance","Alarm","Exhaustion","Appraisal"],"answer":0,"rationale":"In the resistance stage, the body stays physiologically aroused while the person confronts and copes with a continuing stressor. Nadia is past the initial shock of alarm and is not yet depleted, as in exhaustion; appraisal is not a GAS stage."},{"id":"U5-013","section":"5.3","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":3,"stem":"Researchers compare two regions with similar populations. In Region 1, mental health problems are commonly discussed and people are encouraged to visit a counselor when they feel low. In Region 2, people are expected to keep personal struggles within the family, and few visit mental health professionals. Official records show far fewer diagnosed cases of depressive disorders in Region 2. Which concept provides the most likely explanation for the difference in recorded cases?","options":["Diffusion of responsibility","Cultural norms about expressing distress and seeking help","Eclectic treatment","Learned helplessness"],"answer":1,"rationale":"Cultural norms shape whether people report symptoms and seek diagnosis, so lower recorded rates in Region 2 may reflect fewer people coming forward rather than less depression. Learned helplessness explains depression in individuals, not differences in reporting between regions."},{"id":"U5-014","section":"0.3","skill":"Research Methods and Design","difficulty":2,"set":"u5a_breath","stimulus":{"type":"bar","title":"Mean Tension Ratings Before and After the 10-Minute Break","xLabel":"Group","yLabel":"Mean tension rating (0 to 10)","categories":["Breathing app group","Magazine group"],"series":[{"name":"Before break","values":[7.8,7.6]},{"name":"After break","values":[2.9,6.1]}],"yMax":10},"stem":"Dr. Wanjiru tests whether a guided-breathing phone app reduces tension. Twenty-four university students each give a short speech to a panel of strangers and then rate their tension from 0 (none) to 10 (extreme). Half of the students are randomly assigned to use the guided-breathing app for 10 minutes; the other half read a printed travel magazine for 10 minutes. All students then rate their tension again. The group means are shown in the graph. Which of the following is the independent variable in this experiment?","options":["The students' tension ratings","The speech given to the panel of strangers","Whether students used the guided-breathing app or read a magazine","The ages of the students"],"answer":2,"rationale":"The independent variable is what the researcher manipulates: use of the app versus reading a magazine. Tension ratings are the dependent variable, and the speech is the same stressor given to everyone."},{"id":"U5-015","section":"0.6","skill":"Data Interpretation","difficulty":2,"set":"u5a_breath","stimulus":{"type":"bar","title":"Mean Tension Ratings Before and After the 10-Minute Break","xLabel":"Group","yLabel":"Mean tension rating (0 to 10)","categories":["Breathing app group","Magazine group"],"series":[{"name":"Before break","values":[7.8,7.6]},{"name":"After break","values":[2.9,6.1]}],"yMax":10},"stem":"Dr. Wanjiru tests whether a guided-breathing phone app reduces tension. Twenty-four university students each give a short speech to a panel of strangers and then rate their tension from 0 (none) to 10 (extreme). Half of the students are randomly assigned to use the guided-breathing app for 10 minutes; the other half read a printed travel magazine for 10 minutes. All students then rate their tension again. The group means are shown in the graph. Which of the following statements is best supported by the results?","options":["The magazine group became more tense after the break than before it.","The breathing app group was less tense than the magazine group before the break began.","After the break, every student in the breathing app group rated tension below 3.","Both groups became less tense, but the breathing app group's drop was larger than the magazine group's."],"answer":3,"rationale":"The app group dropped from 7.8 to 2.9 (4.9 points), while the magazine group dropped from 7.6 to 6.1 (1.5 points), so both fell but the app group fell more. The graph shows only group means, so it cannot show that every individual student scored below 3."},{"id":"U5-016","section":"5.1","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Kwame and his younger brother had a serious argument over a borrowed laptop that was returned broken, and they have not spoken for days. Kwame decides that, although it will be awkward, he will sit down with his brother, talk through what happened, and agree on a plan to share the cost of the repair. Which approach to managing stress is most consistent with Kwame's decision?","options":["Emotion-focused coping","An avoidance-avoidance conflict","Problem-focused coping","Learned helplessness"],"answer":2,"rationale":"Problem-focused coping means treating the stressor as a problem to solve and acting on its source. Kwame plans to address the conflict directly rather than only managing his feelings about it."},{"id":"U5-017","section":"5.1","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Ji-woo has submitted all of her university applications, and decisions will not arrive for another six weeks. There is nothing more she can do to change the outcome, but she feels tense and keeps checking her email. To manage these feelings, she starts going for an evening run and writing in a journal about her worries each night. Which concept best describes Ji-woo's new routine?","options":["Emotion-focused coping","Problem-focused coping","Eustress","The alarm reaction"],"answer":0,"rationale":"Emotion-focused coping manages the emotional reaction to a stressor, which suits situations like Ji-woo's that cannot be changed. Running and journaling ease her tension without changing the admissions decision, so this is not problem-focused coping."},{"id":"U5-018","section":"5.1","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"set":"u5a_walk","stimulus":{"type":"passage","text":"Dr. Al Mansoori recruited 60 delivery drivers who regularly worked 12-hour shifts. Using a random number generator, she placed 30 drivers in a program of three 30-minute group exercise sessions per week and put the other 30 on a waiting list for the program. A nurse who did not know which group each driver was in measured every driver's resting systolic blood pressure (in mmHg) before the program began and again after 10 weeks. Average blood pressure fell significantly in the exercise group but stayed about the same in the waiting-list group."},"stem":"Felipe, one of the drivers in Dr. Al Mansoori's study, has lived with several ongoing stressors for more than a year: he works long shifts, is repaying a large debt, and helps care for a parent with a long-term illness. If these stressors continue, which health outcome is Felipe most likely to experience?","options":["Faster healing of minor cuts and scrapes","Catching colds and other infections more often","More regular sleep patterns","A lower resting heart rate"],"answer":1,"rationale":"Prolonged stress suppresses the immune system, so people under chronic stress tend to catch infections more often. Chronic stress also slows wound healing and disrupts sleep rather than improving them."},{"id":"U5-019","section":"0.2","skill":"Research Methods and Design","difficulty":1,"set":"u5a_walk","stimulus":{"type":"passage","text":"Dr. Al Mansoori recruited 60 delivery drivers who regularly worked 12-hour shifts. Using a random number generator, she placed 30 drivers in a program of three 30-minute group exercise sessions per week and put the other 30 on a waiting list for the program. A nurse who did not know which group each driver was in measured every driver's resting systolic blood pressure (in mmHg) before the program began and again after 10 weeks. Average blood pressure fell significantly in the exercise group but stayed about the same in the waiting-list group."},"stem":"Which research method did Dr. Al Mansoori use?","options":["A quasi-experiment","An experiment","A correlational study","A longitudinal study"],"answer":1,"rationale":"Dr. Al Mansoori manipulated a variable (the exercise program versus the waiting list) and randomly assigned drivers to groups, which makes this an experiment. A quasi-experiment would compare preexisting groups without random assignment."},{"id":"U5-020","section":"0.3","skill":"Research Methods and Design","difficulty":2,"set":"u5a_walk","stimulus":{"type":"passage","text":"Dr. Al Mansoori recruited 60 delivery drivers who regularly worked 12-hour shifts. Using a random number generator, she placed 30 drivers in a program of three 30-minute group exercise sessions per week and put the other 30 on a waiting list for the program. A nurse who did not know which group each driver was in measured every driver's resting systolic blood pressure (in mmHg) before the program began and again after 10 weeks. Average blood pressure fell significantly in the exercise group but stayed about the same in the waiting-list group."},"stem":"Which of the following best describes the operational definition of the dependent variable in Dr. Al Mansoori's study?","options":["Attending three 30-minute group exercise sessions per week","The nurse not knowing which group each driver was in","Each driver's resting systolic blood pressure, in mmHg, measured after 10 weeks","How much each driver's stress went down during the program"],"answer":2,"rationale":"An operational definition states exactly how a variable is measured, and the outcome here is resting systolic blood pressure recorded in mmHg after 10 weeks. Attending sessions is the independent variable, the nurse's lack of knowledge is a blinding control, and a general drop in stress is not stated in measurable terms."},{"id":"U5-021","section":"0.2","skill":"Research Methods and Design","difficulty":1,"set":"u5a_resid","stimulus":{"type":"passage","text":"A public health team studied 120 trainees at an air traffic control academy during the final month of their certification course, a period known for intense pressure. Each trainee completed a job-pressure scale scored from 0 to 50, and, with the trainees' consent, the team counted how many times each trainee had visited the academy clinic for minor illnesses such as colds or stomach upsets during that month. Trainees with higher job-pressure scores tended to have made more clinic visits. The team leader told a newspaper that certification pressure makes trainees sick."},"stem":"Which research method did the public health team use?","options":["An experiment","A case study","A longitudinal study","A correlational study"],"answer":3,"rationale":"The team measured two existing variables, job pressure and clinic visits, in the same people without manipulating anything, so the study is correlational. That also means the team leader's causal claim goes beyond the data."},{"id":"U5-022","section":"0.5","skill":"Data Interpretation","difficulty":3,"set":"u5a_resid","stimulus":{"type":"passage","text":"A public health team studied 120 trainees at an air traffic control academy during the final month of their certification course, a period known for intense pressure. Each trainee completed a job-pressure scale scored from 0 to 50, and, with the trainees' consent, the team counted how many times each trainee had visited the academy clinic for minor illnesses such as colds or stomach upsets during that month. Trainees with higher job-pressure scores tended to have made more clinic visits. The team leader told a newspaper that certification pressure makes trainees sick."},"stem":"If the team graphed the trainees' job-pressure scores, which prediction about the shape of the distribution is most reasonable?","options":["Most scores would cluster near the high end of the scale, with a tail of a few low scores (a negatively skewed distribution).","Scores would form a symmetrical, bell-shaped normal distribution.","Most scores would cluster near the low end of the scale, with a tail of a few high scores (a positively skewed distribution).","Scores would form two equal clusters, one at each end of the scale."],"answer":0,"rationale":"Trainees in the final, high-pressure month of certification would mostly report high pressure, so scores pile up at the top with a few low outliers, producing a negative skew. A positive skew would mean most trainees felt little pressure."},{"id":"U5-023","section":"0.4","skill":"Research Methods and Design","difficulty":2,"set":"u5a_resid","stimulus":{"type":"passage","text":"A public health team studied 120 trainees at an air traffic control academy during the final month of their certification course, a period known for intense pressure. Each trainee completed a job-pressure scale scored from 0 to 50, and, with the trainees' consent, the team counted how many times each trainee had visited the academy clinic for minor illnesses such as colds or stomach upsets during that month. Trainees with higher job-pressure scores tended to have made more clinic visits. The team leader told a newspaper that certification pressure makes trainees sick."},"stem":"Which of the following best explains why the team measured existing pressure and illness rather than designing an experiment?","options":["A correlational design allows stronger cause-and-effect conclusions than an experiment.","Clinic records can never be used as data in an experiment.","Deliberately exposing people to intense pressure that could make them ill would be unethical, so measuring pressure as it naturally occurs is the more ethical choice.","Experiments may be conducted only with university students."],"answer":2,"rationale":"Randomly assigning people to illness-causing stress would violate the ethical principle of avoiding harm, so researchers measure stress as it naturally occurs. The trade-off is that correlational data cannot establish cause and effect."},{"id":"U5-024","section":"5.1","skill":"Concept Recall","difficulty":1,"stem":"Kofi has just learned that his family must move to a new city at short notice. If his response follows Selye's general adaptation syndrome, in which order would he pass through its stages?","options":["Resistance, then alarm, then exhaustion","Alarm, then exhaustion, then resistance","Appraisal, then coping, then recovery","Alarm, then resistance, then exhaustion"],"answer":3,"rationale":"The general adaptation syndrome begins with the alarm reaction, continues with resistance as the stressor is confronted, and ends with exhaustion when resources are spent. Appraisal and coping belong to other stress models."},{"id":"U5-025","section":"5.1","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Priyanka has been dealing with a stressful legal dispute over her small business for many months. She has now reached the final stage of the general adaptation syndrome. Which of the following is she most likely to experience in this stage?","options":["A sudden burst of energy as her body first mobilizes its resources","A lasting boost in immune function as her body fully adapts to the stressor","Greater vulnerability to stress-related illness as her body's resources run low","A steady ability to cope while her body stays on alert"],"answer":2,"rationale":"The third stage, exhaustion, is when resources are depleted and susceptibility to illness is greatest. A sudden mobilization of resources describes alarm, steady coping while aroused describes resistance, and prolonged stress weakens rather than boosts immunity."},{"id":"U5-026","section":"5.4","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Dr. Rahman's client, Joaquin, feels intense fear whenever he is high above the ground. Standing on a balcony or looking down from a footbridge makes his heart race and his legs shake, so he refuses to visit friends who live above the third floor and takes long detours to avoid bridges. He has no other major fears. Which diagnosis best fits Joaquin's symptoms?","options":["Agoraphobia","Specific phobia","Panic disorder","Generalized anxiety disorder"],"answer":1,"rationale":"A specific phobia is intense fear and avoidance of a particular object or situation, here heights (acrophobia). Agoraphobia involves fear of several situations such as crowds, open spaces or leaving home, and generalized anxiety disorder involves broad, nonspecific worry."},{"id":"U5-027","section":"5.1","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Hamad is walking through a car park when a car suddenly reverses toward him, and he leaps out of the way. During this fight-flight-freeze reaction, which of the following is most likely to decrease?","options":["Digestive activity","Breathing rate","Pupil size","Blood glucose available to the muscles"],"answer":0,"rationale":"The sympathetic nervous system diverts energy away from nonurgent functions, so digestion slows. Breathing rate, pupil size and available blood glucose all increase to prepare the body for action."},{"id":"U5-028","section":"0.3","skill":"Research Methods and Design","difficulty":2,"stem":"Dr. Chua is testing whether a weekly relaxation class lowers blood pressure in office workers. She is concerned that differences in how much participants already exercise could affect the results. Which of the following would best reduce the influence of this possible confounding variable?","options":["Randomly assigning participants to the relaxation class or the control group","Reporting the mean and standard deviation of blood pressure","Conducting an in-depth case study of one worker","Asking participants to rate their own stress on a survey"],"answer":0,"rationale":"Random assignment spreads preexisting differences, such as exercise habits, evenly across groups so they are unlikely to explain any difference in outcomes. Descriptive statistics only summarize data and do not control confounds."},{"id":"U5-029","section":"5.1","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Ever since he missed a penalty that cost his school the national schools football final, Sung-min feels his stomach knot before every training session. He sleeps badly the night before matches, dreads being chosen to take penalties, and his passing accuracy has dropped sharply this season. Which concept best describes what Sung-min is experiencing?","options":["Eustress","Distress","Catharsis","An approach-approach conflict"],"answer":1,"rationale":"Distress is stress experienced as negative and harmful, and it is undermining Sung-min's sleep and performance. Eustress would feel like a motivating challenge that improves performance."},{"id":"U5-030","section":"0.5","skill":"Data Interpretation","difficulty":2,"stem":"A positive psychology class asks eight students to rate how satisfied they are with their lives this week on a scale from 1 to 10. Their ratings are 6, 2, 9, 4, 7, 9, 3, 8. What is the median rating?","options":["6","7","6.5","9"],"answer":2,"rationale":"Ordered, the ratings are 2, 3, 4, 6, 7, 8, 9, 9. With an even number of scores, the median is the average of the two middle scores, 6 and 7, which is 6.5. The mean is 6, and the mode is 9."},{"id":"U5-031","section":"5.2","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"At the end of each day, Ms. Abara asks her students to write down three things that went well and to thank, in a short note, someone who helped them. She explains that noticing and appreciating the good things in their lives can raise their sense of well-being. Which concept is Ms. Abara mainly trying to build in her students?","options":["Self-efficacy","Catharsis","Unconditional positive regard","Gratitude"],"answer":3,"rationale":"Gratitude is noticing and appreciating positive experiences and the people who contribute to them, and expressing it increases subjective well-being. Self-efficacy is belief in one's ability to succeed at a task, and catharsis is the release of pent-up emotion."},{"id":"U5-032","section":"0.2","skill":"Research Methods and Design","difficulty":2,"stem":"Dr. Kaya surveys 500 adults in her city and finds that those who volunteer at least once a week report higher happiness than those who never volunteer. In a public talk, she announces that volunteering makes people happier. Which of the following best explains why her claim is not justified by her data?","options":["A sample of 500 adults is too small to show any relationship between two variables.","Comparing volunteers with non-volunteers is not allowed in survey research.","Her survey shows only a correlation, so it cannot establish that volunteering causes happiness.","Happiness is a private feeling that cannot be measured by any questionnaire."],"answer":2,"rationale":"A survey that measures existing behavior is correlational, so it cannot show causation; happier people may be more likely to volunteer, or a third variable may affect both. Only an experiment with random assignment can support a causal claim."},{"id":"U5-033","section":"0.5","skill":"Data Interpretation","difficulty":2,"stimulus":{"type":"table","caption":"Weekly social contacts and stress scores for ten participants","headers":["Participant","Close social contacts per week","Stress score (out of 40)"],"rows":[["1","1","36"],["2","8","10"],["3","3","30"],["4","6","17"],["5","2","33"],["6","9","8"],["7","5","22"],["8","4","25"],["9","7","14"],["10","2","35"]]},"stem":"Researchers studying well-being asked ten adults to record how many times per week they spent meaningful time with close friends or family. Each participant also completed a stress inventory scored from 0 to 40, with higher scores meaning more stress. The results are shown in the table. Which statement most accurately describes these data?","options":["There is a strong negative correlation between social contacts and stress scores.","There is a strong positive correlation between social contacts and stress scores.","Social contacts and stress scores show no clear relationship.","Spending more time with friends and family causes stress to decrease."],"answer":0,"rationale":"Participants with more weekly contacts consistently have lower stress scores, so the variables move in opposite directions in a tight pattern: a strong negative correlation. Because the data are correlational, they cannot show that social contact causes lower stress."},{"id":"U5-034","section":"5.2","skill":"Concept Recall","difficulty":1,"stem":"Which area of psychology focuses on human strengths and on the factors that help people thrive, such as resilience, positive emotions and well-being?","options":["Psychodynamic psychology","Behavioral psychology","Clinical neuropsychology","Positive psychology"],"answer":3,"rationale":"Positive psychology studies what helps people flourish, including well-being, resilience and signature strengths, rather than focusing mainly on disorders."},{"id":"U5-035","section":"0.5","skill":"Data Interpretation","difficulty":2,"stem":"A study of hospital staff finds a negative correlation between scores on the character strength of perseverance and the number of workdays missed per year. Which conclusion is best supported by this finding?","options":["Developing perseverance causes staff to miss fewer workdays.","Staff with higher perseverance scores tend to miss fewer workdays.","Perseverance and missed workdays are unrelated, because a negative correlation means there is no relationship.","Staff with higher perseverance scores tend to miss more workdays."],"answer":1,"rationale":"A negative correlation means that as one variable increases, the other tends to decrease, which is still a relationship. It does not show which variable, if either, causes the other."},{"id":"U5-036","section":"5.2","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Dr. Haddad studies hospital nurses who work in intensive care. She wants to identify the personal strengths and workplace conditions that help some nurses bounce back from difficult shifts and stay healthy and engaged in their work. Dr. Haddad's research best fits which area of psychology?","options":["Cognitive psychology","Positive psychology","Psychodynamic psychology","Behavioral psychology"],"answer":1,"rationale":"Positive psychology seeks to identify factors that lead to well-being and resilience. Dr. Haddad is studying what helps nurses thrive, not how people process information or how behavior is conditioned."},{"id":"U5-037","section":"0.5","skill":"Data Interpretation","difficulty":2,"set":"u5a_points","stimulus":{"type":"table","caption":"Mei Ling's unforced errors in eight squash matches this season","headers":["Match","Unforced errors"],"rows":[["1","12"],["2","7"],["3","15"],["4","10"],["5","4"],["6","18"],["7","10"],["8","13"]]},"stem":"Mei Ling, a member of her school's squash team, has started practicing a pre-match breathing routine with the school counselor to manage her nerves. She records the number of unforced errors she makes in each match this season, as shown in the table. What is the median number of unforced errors?","options":["10","12","13","11"],"answer":3,"rationale":"Ordered, the scores are 4, 7, 10, 10, 12, 13, 15, 18. The two middle scores are 10 and 12, so the median is 11. The value 10 is the mode."},{"id":"U5-038","section":"0.5","skill":"Data Interpretation","difficulty":1,"set":"u5a_points","stimulus":{"type":"table","caption":"Mei Ling's unforced errors in eight squash matches this season","headers":["Match","Unforced errors"],"rows":[["1","12"],["2","7"],["3","15"],["4","10"],["5","4"],["6","18"],["7","10"],["8","13"]]},"stem":"Mei Ling, a member of her school's squash team, has started practicing a pre-match breathing routine with the school counselor to manage her nerves. She records the number of unforced errors she makes in each match this season, as shown in the table. What is the range of Mei Ling's unforced errors?","options":["14","11","18","8"],"answer":0,"rationale":"The range is the highest score minus the lowest score: 18 minus 4 equals 14. The value 18 is only the highest score, and 8 is the number of matches."},{"id":"U5-039","section":"4.1","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":3,"set":"u5a_drivers","stimulus":{"type":"table","caption":"Categories of stressors in baggage handlers' diary entries, ranked by number of entries","headers":["Rank","Category of stressor","Number of entries"],"rows":[["1","Shift end times pushed back by flight delays they cannot influence","48"],["2","Supervisors changing task assignments without asking them","37"],["3","Breakdowns of the automated sorting system that only technicians are allowed to fix","29"],["4","Work rosters released with only one day's notice","22"],["5","Break times dictated by flight arrival schedules","15"]]},"stem":"An airport asked Dr. Santos, a positive psychologist, to help reduce stress and staff turnover among its baggage handlers. She gave 30 handlers a small notebook and asked them, over two weeks, to write a short entry each time they felt stressed at work, describing what had happened. Her team then read every entry, sorted the entries into categories, and counted how many entries fell into each category, as shown in the table. Which concept best captures what the five most common categories have in common?","options":["Drive-reduction theory","Social loafing","A perceived lack of personal control (an external locus of control)","The self-serving bias"],"answer":2,"rationale":"Every category involves outcomes decided by flight schedules, supervisors or rules rather than by the handlers themselves, which reflects a sense that control lies outside oneself. A perceived lack of control is a well-known contributor to workplace stress."},{"id":"U5-040","section":"0.2","skill":"Research Methods and Design","difficulty":2,"set":"u5a_drivers","stimulus":{"type":"table","caption":"Categories of stressors in baggage handlers' diary entries, ranked by number of entries","headers":["Rank","Category of stressor","Number of entries"],"rows":[["1","Shift end times pushed back by flight delays they cannot influence","48"],["2","Supervisors changing task assignments without asking them","37"],["3","Breakdowns of the automated sorting system that only technicians are allowed to fix","29"],["4","Work rosters released with only one day's notice","22"],["5","Break times dictated by flight arrival schedules","15"]]},"stem":"An airport asked Dr. Santos, a positive psychologist, to help reduce stress and staff turnover among its baggage handlers. She gave 30 handlers a small notebook and asked them, over two weeks, to write a short entry each time they felt stressed at work, describing what had happened. Her team then read every entry, sorted the entries into categories, and counted how many entries fell into each category, as shown in the table. Which approach did Dr. Santos's team use to analyze the diary entries?","options":["A Likert scale","Test-retest reliability","A double-blind procedure","Qualitative analysis: sorting written descriptions into themes and counting them"],"answer":3,"rationale":"The team took open-ended written descriptions and coded them into categories, which is qualitative analysis. A Likert scale would require handlers to rate statements on a fixed numerical scale."},{"id":"U5-041","section":"2.2","skill":"Research Methods and Design","difficulty":2,"set":"u5a_drivers","stimulus":{"type":"table","caption":"Categories of stressors in baggage handlers' diary entries, ranked by number of entries","headers":["Rank","Category of stressor","Number of entries"],"rows":[["1","Shift end times pushed back by flight delays they cannot influence","48"],["2","Supervisors changing task assignments without asking them","37"],["3","Breakdowns of the automated sorting system that only technicians are allowed to fix","29"],["4","Work rosters released with only one day's notice","22"],["5","Break times dictated by flight arrival schedules","15"]]},"stem":"An airport asked Dr. Santos, a positive psychologist, to help reduce stress and staff turnover among its baggage handlers. She gave 30 handlers a small notebook and asked them, over two weeks, to write a short entry each time they felt stressed at work, describing what had happened. Her team then read every entry, sorted the entries into categories, and counted how many entries fell into each category, as shown in the table. The instructions printed inside each notebook read, \"Write about each moment this exhausting job wore you down.\" Which concept best describes how this wording could have biased the entries?","options":["Retroactive interference","Social facilitation","Framing","The mere exposure effect"],"answer":2,"rationale":"Framing is the way information or a question is worded, which can influence the responses people give. Describing the job as exhausting invites negative entries that may exaggerate stress."},{"id":"U5-042","section":"0.5","skill":"Data Interpretation","difficulty":1,"stem":"Over one school term, Aiko recorded the number of hours of homework assigned each day at her school and the average mood rating students gave in a quick daily poll. She found that on days with more homework, mood ratings tended to be lower. Which term best describes the relationship between these two variables?","options":["Positive correlation","Negative correlation","Zero correlation","Cause-and-effect relationship"],"answer":1,"rationale":"In a negative correlation, one variable tends to go up as the other goes down. Aiko only measured the variables, so she cannot claim that homework caused the lower moods."},{"id":"U5-043","section":"0.5","skill":"Data Interpretation","difficulty":2,"stem":"A team of researchers measures adults' scores on the signature strength of kindness and their scores on a hostility questionnaire. Which of the following correlation coefficients would indicate the strongest relationship between these two variables?","options":["-0.78","+0.61","-0.42","+0.09"],"answer":0,"rationale":"The strength of a correlation is shown by how close the coefficient is to 1 or -1, regardless of sign. At 0.78, the coefficient -0.78 is farther from zero than any of the others."},{"id":"U5-044","section":"5.3","skill":"Concept Recall","difficulty":1,"stem":"A psychologist taking a psychodynamic perspective would most likely explain the development of a psychological disorder in terms of","options":["unconscious conflicts and experiences, often from early childhood","maladaptive associations learned through conditioning","imbalances in neurotransmitters and other physiological factors","irrational beliefs about oneself and the world"],"answer":0,"rationale":"The psychodynamic perspective attributes disorders to unconscious thoughts and experiences, often developed in childhood. Learned associations reflect the behavioral perspective, and irrational beliefs reflect the cognitive perspective."},{"id":"U5-045","section":"5.3","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Rania's mother and uncle have both been diagnosed with schizophrenia, so Rania has a genetic predisposition for the disorder. According to the diathesis-stress model, which statement about Rania is most accurate?","options":["She will certainly develop schizophrenia by early adulthood.","Her chance of developing schizophrenia is raised, especially if she faces major stressful life experiences.","Her risk is the same as anyone else's, because only the environment determines whether the disorder appears.","She can develop schizophrenia only if both of her parents have the disorder."],"answer":1,"rationale":"The diathesis-stress model holds that a genetic vulnerability combines with stressful experiences to produce a disorder. A predisposition raises risk but does not guarantee the disorder, and genes do matter."},{"id":"U5-046","section":"5.3","skill":"Concept Recall","difficulty":1,"stem":"Which of the following is a central factor psychologists consider when deciding whether a pattern of behavior should be classified as a psychological disorder?","options":["Whether a close relative shows the same behavior","Whether the behavior is performed in front of other people","Whether it interferes with the person's ability to function in daily life","Whether the person can describe the behavior in detail"],"answer":2,"rationale":"Level of dysfunction, along with perception of distress and deviation from social norms, is used to identify psychological disorders. Family history, having an audience, or being able to describe a behavior does not determine whether it is disordered."},{"id":"U5-047","section":"5.3","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Tariq has always been a strong swimmer, but this season his race times have slipped badly and he has started skipping training. His psychologist explains that Tariq's difficulties come from beliefs such as \"If I do not win every race, I am a complete failure\" and from constantly expecting the worst. The psychologist's explanation is most typical of which perspective?","options":["Psychodynamic","Sociocultural","Biological","Cognitive"],"answer":3,"rationale":"The cognitive perspective attributes problems to maladaptive thoughts and beliefs, such as all-or-nothing thinking and expecting failure. A psychodynamic explanation would focus on unconscious childhood conflicts."},{"id":"U5-048","section":"0.2","skill":"Research Methods and Design","difficulty":2,"stem":"Clinicians at a hospital in Nairobi meet a patient whose combination of symptoms has never been documented in the medical literature. They want to understand this pattern as fully as possible before anyone attempts larger studies. Which research method is the best first step?","options":["An experiment with random assignment","A meta-analysis of published studies","A case study","A survey of a large random sample of adults"],"answer":2,"rationale":"A case study is an in-depth examination of one individual and is well suited to rare or newly observed conditions. With only one known patient there are no groups to assign, no published studies to combine, and a general survey would be unlikely to find similar cases."},{"id":"U5-049","section":"4.6","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":3,"stem":"On practice days, Farah feels a little nervous before her timed math quizzes and usually scores well. On the day of her final exam, she is so anxious that her hands shake and her mind goes blank, and she scores far below her usual level. Which theory best explains why a moderate level of anxiety helped Farah while a very high level hurt her performance?","options":["Arousal theory (the Yerkes-Dodson law)","Drive-reduction theory","Instinct theory","Tend-and-befriend theory"],"answer":0,"rationale":"Arousal theory, including the Yerkes-Dodson law, predicts that performance is best at a moderate level of arousal and declines when arousal becomes too high, especially on difficult tasks. Drive-reduction theory explains behavior as reducing biological needs."},{"id":"U5-050","section":"0.1","skill":"Concept Recall","difficulty":1,"stem":"Critics argue that some psychodynamic explanations of disorders are not falsifiable. Which of the following describes what would make a theory falsifiable?","options":["It agrees with theories that are already widely accepted.","It has been endorsed by many experienced clinicians.","It can explain any outcome, whatever happens.","It makes predictions that could be shown to be wrong by future evidence."],"answer":3,"rationale":"A falsifiable theory makes testable predictions that evidence could contradict. A theory that can explain every possible outcome cannot be tested, which is the criticism leveled at some psychodynamic ideas."},{"id":"U5-051","section":"5.5","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Four counselors at a university wellness center are each working with a student who avoids speaking up in seminars. Which counselor is using a behavioral approach to treatment?","options":["Counselor Okafor asks the student to write down the thought \"Everyone will think I am stupid\" and to weigh the evidence for and against it.","Counselor Reyes reflects the student's feelings back to him and offers unconditional positive regard as he talks about the person he hopes to become.","Counselor Lim sets up a plan in which the student earns points toward a chosen reward for each comment he makes in a seminar, and the number of comments required slowly increases each week.","Counselor Haider asks the student to describe his dreams and early family memories in search of unconscious conflicts behind his silence."],"answer":2,"rationale":"Rewarding gradually larger amounts of the target behavior applies operant conditioning (reinforcement and shaping), so it is behavioral. Weighing evidence for a thought is cognitive, unconditional positive regard is humanistic, and dream interpretation is psychodynamic."},{"id":"U5-052","section":"5.5","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"On a residential treatment unit for adolescents, staff award points each time a client completes a chore or attends a group session, and the points can be exchanged for extra phone time or outings. Behaviorally oriented therapists use this kind of system because they aim to change behavior by","options":["helping clients gain insight into their unconscious motives","changing the consequences that follow the behavior","linking clients' past experiences to their current behavior","challenging the irrational beliefs behind the behavior"],"answer":1,"rationale":"This is a token economy, which changes the contingencies of reinforcement so that desired behaviors are rewarded. Insight and childhood links are psychodynamic goals, and challenging beliefs is cognitive."},{"id":"U5-053","section":"0.1","skill":"Concept Recall","difficulty":1,"stem":"The idea that behavior and mental processes are strongly shaped by thoughts, wishes and memories that a person is not aware of is central to which approach?","options":["Humanistic","Behavioral","Evolutionary","Psychodynamic"],"answer":3,"rationale":"The psychodynamic approach emphasizes the influence of the unconscious mind on behavior. Behaviorists focus on observable, learned behavior rather than hidden mental content."},{"id":"U5-054","section":"5.5","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Dr. Mensah, a behavior therapist, begins working with a client who has stopped going to university lectures. In planning treatment, Dr. Mensah is most likely to emphasize","options":["the client's observable behavior and the reinforcers and associations that currently maintain it","the meaning the client finds in life and the client's potential for personal growth","unconscious motives revealed through free association","medication to correct a possible neurotransmitter imbalance"],"answer":0,"rationale":"Behavior therapists focus on observable behavior and the learning, especially reinforcement and conditioned associations, that keeps it going. Personal growth is a humanistic theme, free association is psychodynamic, and medication is biomedical."},{"id":"U5-055","section":"5.3","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Hana has several close relatives with anxiety disorders. She also tends to interpret a racing heart as a sign that something terrible is about to happen. Last year she moved to a new country for work, far from her friends and family. Hana has recently been diagnosed with panic disorder. A psychologist who explains her disorder by considering all three of these influences is using which model?","options":["Psychodynamic","Humanistic","Biopsychosocial","Cognitive"],"answer":2,"rationale":"The biopsychosocial model holds that disorders can involve biological (family history), psychological (catastrophic thinking) and sociocultural (loss of social support) factors together. A cognitive account, for example, would address only her catastrophic thinking."},{"id":"U5-056","section":"5.5","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Dr. Kimura's client, a newly promoted manager, says that leading a large group project is \"a trap that will expose me as incompetent.\" Over several sessions, Dr. Kimura helps the client notice this interpretation, question whether it is the only possible one, and practice viewing the project as a chance to build new skills. Dr. Kimura's approach is most consistent with which treatment orientation?","options":["Biological","Humanistic","Psychodynamic","Cognitive"],"answer":3,"rationale":"Cognitive therapy helps clients identify and restructure unhelpful interpretations, here turning a threat appraisal into a challenge appraisal. A humanistic therapist would offer empathy and unconditional positive regard rather than actively questioning the client's interpretation."},{"id":"U5-057","section":"5.3","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"A school counselor worries that once a student receives a formal diagnosis using the Diagnostic and Statistical Manual of Mental Disorders (DSM), teachers may begin to interpret everything the student does through that diagnosis. This concern best reflects which common criticism of diagnostic classification?","options":["The DSM is revised over time, so diagnostic criteria can change between editions.","The DSM describes symptoms but does not state which therapy must be used.","Diagnostic labels can lead others to see the person mainly through the label, creating stigma and biased expectations.","The DSM gives each disorder its own criteria so that clinicians can agree on diagnoses."],"answer":2,"rationale":"The counselor fears that teachers will interpret the student's behavior through the diagnosis, which is the labeling and stigma criticism. Regular revision and shared criteria are features of the DSM, not the concern described."},{"id":"U5-058","section":"5.3","skill":"Concept Recall","difficulty":1,"stem":"Psychologists consider several factors when deciding whether behavior should be considered a psychological disorder. Which of the following is NOT one of these factors?","options":["The level of dysfunction the behavior causes","Whether a relative has been diagnosed with the same disorder","Whether the person perceives distress","How far the behavior deviates from social norms"],"answer":1,"rationale":"Dysfunction, distress and deviation from social norms are used to identify disorders. A family history may raise a person's risk, but it is not a criterion for deciding whether that person's own behavior is disordered."},{"id":"U5-059","section":"0.2","skill":"Research Methods and Design","difficulty":2,"stem":"Dr. Sato is studying a single patient who, after surviving a serious flood, could not recall several years of her own life, although tests showed no brain injury. Over 18 months, Dr. Sato gives her memory tests, reviews her hospital records, looks through old photographs with her, interviews her family, and keeps detailed notes, hoping the findings will suggest hypotheses for larger studies of dissociative amnesia. Which research method is Dr. Sato using?","options":["Case study","Quasi-experiment","Cross-sectional study","Correlational study"],"answer":0,"rationale":"A case study is an in-depth investigation of one or a few individuals using many sources of information. Dr. Sato does not compare groups, measure people of different ages at one time, or correlate variables across a sample."},{"id":"U5-060","section":"5.3","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Many people with schizophrenia experience a reduction in hallucinations and delusions after taking antipsychotic medications that block dopamine receptors. This outcome most directly supports which approach to explaining schizophrenia?","options":["Biological","Cognitive","Psychodynamic","Humanistic"],"answer":0,"rationale":"When a drug that alters a neurotransmitter reduces symptoms, it supports a biological explanation, in this case the dopamine hypothesis. The other approaches focus on thoughts, unconscious conflicts or unmet potential."},{"id":"U5-061","section":"5.3","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":3,"stem":"Kofi and Kwesi are identical twins whose mother has bipolar I disorder. At age 24, Kofi goes through a year of severe stress, losing his job and caring for a seriously ill grandparent, and he experiences his first manic episode. Kwesi, whose year is calm, shows no symptoms. How would Dr. Ogunleye, who uses the diathesis-stress model, most likely explain the difference between the twins?","options":["Because the twins share the same genes, the difference shows that genes play no part in bipolar disorder.","Both twins share a genetic vulnerability, but the disorder emerged in Kofi because his vulnerability combined with major stress.","Kofi's disorder comes from unconscious childhood conflicts that Kwesi was able to resolve.","Kwesi cannot carry the genetic vulnerability, because anyone with the vulnerability always develops the disorder."],"answer":1,"rationale":"The diathesis-stress model holds that an inherited vulnerability raises risk but usually produces a disorder only when combined with stressful experiences. Identical twins share the diathesis, so a difference in stress can explain why only one twin is diagnosed."},{"id":"U5-062","section":"5.3","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which of the following cases best illustrates the diathesis-stress model of psychological disorders?","options":["Beatriz, who has no family history of anxiety, develops a fear of dogs after being bitten by one.","Dalia's psychologist explains her anxiety as the result of unconscious conflicts about her parents.","Amir, whose father and grandmother have major depressive disorder, develops depression for the first time after losing his job and his home in the same year.","Elif's psychologist explains her constant worry as the result of her habit of predicting the worst possible outcome."],"answer":2,"rationale":"Amir has a genetic vulnerability (family history) that appears after major life stress, which is exactly what the diathesis-stress model describes. The other cases rely on a single learned, unconscious or cognitive cause."},{"id":"U5-063","section":"5.5","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Dr. Petrov, a psychiatrist, believes that bipolar disorder results mainly from abnormal brain chemistry, and he treats his clients with the mood stabilizer lithium. Which treatment approach does Dr. Petrov most likely support?","options":["Psychodynamic","Humanistic","Behavioral","Biomedical"],"answer":3,"rationale":"Biomedical therapies treat disorders by changing the body's biology, for example with psychotropic medications such as lithium. Psychotherapies such as psychodynamic, humanistic and behavioral therapy work through talk or learning instead."},{"id":"U5-064","section":"5.3","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Leila has recently been diagnosed with major depressive disorder. Which of the following explanations of her depression is most consistent with the cognitive perspective?","options":["Lower than typical activity of serotonin in her brain","Repressed anger toward a parent that began in early childhood","A habit of interpreting every setback as proof that she is worthless and that her future is hopeless","A lack of acceptance from others that has kept her from fulfilling her potential"],"answer":2,"rationale":"The cognitive perspective attributes depression to maladaptive thinking, such as negative views of the self and the future. Serotonin is a biological explanation, repressed anger is psychodynamic, and unmet potential is humanistic."},{"id":"U5-065","section":"5.5","skill":"Concept Recall","difficulty":1,"stem":"A central goal of cognitive therapy is to help clients","options":["recognize and change distorted interpretations of their experiences","uncover the hidden meaning of their dreams","feel unconditionally accepted so they can grow toward their potential","extinguish conditioned fears through repeated exposure"],"answer":0,"rationale":"Cognitive therapists target the way clients think, using techniques such as cognitive restructuring to replace distorted interpretations. Dream analysis is psychodynamic, unconditional acceptance is humanistic, and exposure is behavioral."},{"id":"U5-066","section":"5.3","skill":"Data Interpretation","difficulty":2,"stimulus":{"type":"bar","title":"Adults Diagnosed With a Depressive Disorder in the Past Year, by Employment Status","xLabel":"Employment status","yLabel":"Percent diagnosed","categories":["Employed","Unemployed less than 1 year","Unemployed more than 1 year"],"series":[{"name":"Percent diagnosed","values":[6,11,19]}],"yMax":25},"stem":"A national health survey reports the percentage of adults diagnosed with a depressive disorder in the past year, grouped by employment status, as shown in the graph. These results most directly support which perspective on the origins of psychological disorders?","options":["Psychodynamic","Evolutionary","Biological","Sociocultural"],"answer":3,"rationale":"The graph links rates of depressive disorders to employment status, an economic and social circumstance, which is the focus of the sociocultural perspective. The data say nothing about unconscious conflicts, survival value or physiology."},{"id":"U5-067","section":"5.3","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Dr. Castillo's client, Ibrahim, has kept so many newspapers, boxes and broken appliances that he can no longer cook in his kitchen or sleep in his bed. He feels intense distress at the thought of throwing anything away, and he recently failed a fire-safety inspection that may cost him his apartment. Dr. Castillo is considering a diagnosis of hoarding disorder. Which information would give her the most objective evidence that Ibrahim's behavior is disordered?","options":["His neighbors' opinion that his collecting is strange","Records showing that the clutter prevents him from using his kitchen and bed and has put his housing at risk","The estimated value of the items he has saved","The age at which he first began collecting newspapers"],"answer":1,"rationale":"Dysfunction, here being unable to use his home and risking eviction, can be documented directly, so it is the most objective criterion. Neighbors' opinions depend on judgment, and the value of the items or when collecting began does not show whether the behavior is disordered."},{"id":"U5-068","section":"5.5","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Rahim, who has been feeling depressed, tells his therapist, \"Everyone at work thinks I am useless.\" The therapist asks him to list the evidence for and against this belief and then to write a more balanced statement based on that evidence. Which approach to therapy is most consistent with this technique?","options":["Humanistic","Cognitive","Behavioral","Sociocultural"],"answer":1,"rationale":"Testing the evidence for a belief and replacing it with a more realistic one is cognitive restructuring, a core technique of cognitive therapy. Humanistic therapy would focus on empathy and acceptance rather than disputing thoughts."},{"id":"U5-069","section":"5.3","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Suppose a survey of 2,000 adults in the UAE finds that many more people report intense fear of spiders and heights than of cars or electrical sockets, even though road accidents and electrical faults cause far more injuries in the country today. Which explanation is best supported by this pattern?","options":["People mainly fear the dangers they encounter most often in daily life.","Humans may be biologically prepared to develop fears of threats that endangered our ancestors.","People develop fears only of things that have directly harmed them in the past.","Fears are a result of cognitive dissonance about modern technology."],"answer":1,"rationale":"The pattern fits the evolutionary idea of preparedness: fears of ancestral threats such as spiders and heights are acquired easily, while modern hazards are feared far less than their actual danger suggests. If fear tracked everyday encounters or direct harm, cars and sockets would be feared more."},{"id":"U5-070","section":"5.3","skill":"Data Interpretation","difficulty":3,"stimulus":{"type":"line","title":"Mean Skin Conductance Response During Extinction Trials","xLabel":"Extinction trial","yLabel":"Mean skin conductance response (microsiemens)","series":[{"name":"Spider pictures","points":[[1,0.82],[2,0.78],[3,0.75],[4,0.71],[5,0.69],[6,0.66]]},{"name":"Flower pictures","points":[[1,0.8],[2,0.52],[3,0.31],[4,0.19],[5,0.13],[6,0.1]]}]},"stem":"In a conditioning study, Dr. Mwangi's participants first saw pictures of spiders and pictures of flowers, each followed by an unpleasant burst of loud noise, until both kinds of pictures produced similar fear responses. In the extinction phase shown in the graph, the pictures were presented with no noise. These results best support which perspective on the origins of phobias?","options":["Sociocultural","Humanistic","Evolutionary","Psychodynamic"],"answer":2,"rationale":"Fear of the spider pictures barely declines across trials, while fear of the flower pictures fades quickly, suggesting humans are prepared to learn and keep fears of ancestral threats. Ordinary conditioning alone would predict similar extinction for both kinds of pictures."},{"id":"U5-071","section":"0.1","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":1,"stem":"Which of the following research topics would a psychologist working from the cognitive perspective be most likely to study?","options":["How the presence of a group changes whether people conform","How reinforcement schedules affect how long a pigeon keeps pecking","How people use mental shortcuts when estimating the risks of everyday activities","How hormone levels change across adolescence"],"answer":2,"rationale":"Cognitive psychologists study mental processes such as thinking, judgment and problem solving, including heuristics. Conformity is a social topic, reinforcement schedules are behavioral, and hormones are biological."},{"id":"U5-072","section":"5.3","skill":"Concept Recall","difficulty":1,"stem":"A counselor believes that every person has an inborn drive to grow and fulfill their potential, and that psychological problems arise when this growth is blocked, for example by a lack of acceptance and support. Which perspective does the counselor hold?","options":["Behavioral","Evolutionary","Biological","Humanistic"],"answer":3,"rationale":"The humanistic perspective holds that all people can move toward self-actualization and that disorders arise from a lack of social support and an inability to fulfill one's potential."},{"id":"U5-073","section":"0.1","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Tomasz, a successful engineer with no psychological symptoms, signs up for a weekend workshop. The facilitator explains that the goal is not to fix problems but to help each participant become more authentic, accept themselves, and move closer to reaching their full potential. The workshop's emphasis is most typical of which perspective?","options":["Behavioral","Biological","Humanistic","Psychodynamic"],"answer":2,"rationale":"The humanistic perspective takes an optimistic view of people and emphasizes growth and self-actualization, not only the treatment of symptoms. A psychodynamic counselor would focus on uncovering unconscious conflicts."},{"id":"U5-074","section":"0.1","skill":"Concept Recall","difficulty":1,"stem":"Which perspective views the mind as an information-processing system and studies how people take in, store and use information from the senses?","options":["Behavioral","Cognitive","Humanistic","Sociocultural"],"answer":1,"rationale":"The cognitive perspective focuses on mental processes such as perceiving, encoding, storing and retrieving information. Behaviorists focus on observable behavior rather than internal processing."},{"id":"U5-075","section":"4.5","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"A psychologist gives clients a questionnaire to find out whether they believe their successes and failures are mostly caused by their own efforts or mostly by luck and other people. Psychologists from which perspective on personality are most interested in this kind of assessment?","options":["Social-cognitive","Psychoanalytic","Humanistic","Evolutionary"],"answer":0,"rationale":"The questionnaire measures locus of control, a concept from Julian Rotter's social-cognitive approach to personality. Psychoanalytic theory focuses on unconscious drives, and humanistic theory focuses on self-concept and growth."},{"id":"U5-076","section":"5.3","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":3,"stem":"Elena has been diagnosed with panic disorder. Dr. Lim believes that after Elena's first panic attack on a crowded train, trains became linked with fear for her, and that avoiding trains is maintained because it brings relief. Dr. Osei believes that Elena's attacks continue because she interprets normal bodily sensations, such as a fast heartbeat, as signs that she is about to collapse. Which perspective is each psychologist using?","options":["Dr. Lim is using a behavioral perspective, while Dr. Osei is using a cognitive perspective.","Dr. Lim is using a cognitive perspective, while Dr. Osei is using a behavioral perspective.","Dr. Lim is using a psychodynamic perspective, while Dr. Osei is using a biological perspective.","Dr. Lim is using a sociocultural perspective, while Dr. Osei is using a humanistic perspective."],"answer":0,"rationale":"Dr. Lim explains the disorder through learned associations and reinforcement, which is behavioral. Dr. Osei focuses on catastrophic interpretations of sensations, which is cognitive."},{"id":"U5-077","section":"4.4","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"During a debate, Sofia points out that if a client is openly angry, a psychoanalyst can say the anger reveals a repressed wish, and if the client is calm, the psychoanalyst can say the client is defending against that same wish. Sofia's point best illustrates which common criticism of psychodynamic theory?","options":["It places too little emphasis on the unconscious mind.","Its claims are difficult to falsify because almost any outcome can be explained.","It assumes that behavior is shaped mainly by rewards and punishments.","It relies too heavily on laboratory experiments with animals."],"answer":1,"rationale":"A theory that can explain both an outcome and its opposite cannot be tested or proven wrong, so it is not falsifiable. Psychodynamic theory actually emphasizes the unconscious heavily rather than ignoring it."},{"id":"U5-078","section":"5.3","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Yusuf feels intensely anxious around teachers, managers and other authority figures. His therapist believes that this anxiety stems from unresolved conflicts with a very strict parent during early childhood, conflicts that Yusuf is not consciously aware of. Which perspective does the therapist most likely hold?","options":["Behavioral","Cognitive","Psychodynamic","Biological"],"answer":2,"rationale":"The psychodynamic perspective explains disorders through unconscious thoughts and experiences rooted in childhood. A cognitive therapist would focus on Yusuf's current beliefs about authority figures."},{"id":"U5-079","section":"5.5","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Dr. Andersson believes that her client's long-lasting depression is linked to reduced activity in a region of the prefrontal cortex. Because several medications have not helped, she recommends repetitive transcranial magnetic stimulation (rTMS) aimed at that brain region. Dr. Andersson's treatment is most in line with which perspective?","options":["Psychodynamic","Humanistic","Cognitive","Biological"],"answer":3,"rationale":"rTMS is a biomedical treatment that uses magnetic pulses to change activity in targeted brain areas, so it reflects the biological perspective. It does not work through insight, personal growth or changing thoughts."},{"id":"U5-080","section":"5.5","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Lucia's therapist explains that her depression is kept going by three kinds of negative thoughts: about herself (\"I am a failure\"), about the world (\"People only let me down\") and about the future (\"Nothing will ever get better\"). Therapy will focus on changing these thoughts. The therapist is most likely a supporter of which perspective?","options":["Behavioral","Humanistic","Cognitive","Evolutionary"],"answer":2,"rationale":"Negative thoughts about the self, the world and the future form Aaron Beck's cognitive triad, a key idea in cognitive therapy. A behavioral therapist would target actions and their consequences rather than thoughts."},{"id":"U5-081","section":"5.5","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":1,"set":"u5a_flying","stimulus":{"type":"passage","text":"Mr. Delgado has an intense fear of flying that has kept him from visiting his family abroad for years. His psychologist first teaches him deep muscle relaxation. Then, while he practices relaxing, the psychologist guides him through the following steps in order, moving on only when he can stay calm at the current step. Step 1: looking at a photo of an airplane. Step 2: watching a video of a plane taking off. Step 3: walking through the airport departures hall. Step 4: sitting in a parked plane during a training session. Step 5: taking a 40-minute flight with the psychologist."},"stem":"Mr. Delgado's psychologist pairs relaxation with a series of increasingly challenging flight-related situations. Which type of therapy is most closely associated with this technique?","options":["Behavioral","Psychodynamic","Person-centered","Biomedical"],"answer":0,"rationale":"Gradually pairing relaxation with feared situations applies classical conditioning, which makes it a behavioral therapy. It does not involve uncovering unconscious conflicts, unconditional positive regard or medication."},{"id":"U5-082","section":"5.4","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":1,"set":"u5a_flying","stimulus":{"type":"passage","text":"Mr. Delgado has an intense fear of flying that has kept him from visiting his family abroad for years. His psychologist first teaches him deep muscle relaxation. Then, while he practices relaxing, the psychologist guides him through the following steps in order, moving on only when he can stay calm at the current step. Step 1: looking at a photo of an airplane. Step 2: watching a video of a plane taking off. Step 3: walking through the airport departures hall. Step 4: sitting in a parked plane during a training session. Step 5: taking a 40-minute flight with the psychologist."},"stem":"Based on the description of Mr. Delgado, which category of psychological disorder would his difficulty most likely fall into?","options":["Depressive disorders","Dissociative disorders","Obsessive-compulsive and related disorders","Anxiety disorders"],"answer":3,"rationale":"An intense, persistent fear of a specific situation such as flying, with avoidance, is a specific phobia, which belongs to the anxiety disorders category. Nothing in the description suggests low mood, memory or identity disruption, or obsessions."},{"id":"U5-083","section":"5.5","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"set":"u5a_flying","stimulus":{"type":"passage","text":"Mr. Delgado has an intense fear of flying that has kept him from visiting his family abroad for years. His psychologist first teaches him deep muscle relaxation. Then, while he practices relaxing, the psychologist guides him through the following steps in order, moving on only when he can stay calm at the current step. Step 1: looking at a photo of an airplane. Step 2: watching a video of a plane taking off. Step 3: walking through the airport departures hall. Step 4: sitting in a parked plane during a training session. Step 5: taking a 40-minute flight with the psychologist."},"stem":"Which treatment technique is Mr. Delgado's psychologist using?","options":["Flooding","Systematic desensitization","Cognitive restructuring","A token economy"],"answer":1,"rationale":"Systematic desensitization combines relaxation with a gradual hierarchy of feared situations. Flooding would expose him to the most feared situation all at once, and cognitive restructuring would target his thoughts about flying rather than pairing relaxation with exposure."},{"id":"U5-084","section":"2.2","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Dr. Varga suspects that her 10-year-old client, Leo, has attention-deficit/hyperactivity disorder. During a one-hour observation, she carefully records each time Leo fidgets or looks out of the window, but she does not record the long stretches when he works quietly and steadily on a puzzle. Dr. Varga is showing which of the following?","options":["Hindsight bias","Confirmation bias","Functional fixedness","The framing effect"],"answer":1,"rationale":"Confirmation bias is the tendency to notice and record information that supports an existing belief while overlooking information that contradicts it. Hindsight bias would involve believing, after the fact, that an outcome was predictable."},{"id":"U5-085","section":"5.3","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":3,"stem":"Researchers trying to compare rates of panic disorder across countries find that in some communities, episodes of intense distress are commonly expressed as ataque de nervios, which can include shouting, trembling and a feeling of losing control, and these episodes are often not reported as panic attacks. Which statement best explains why estimating how common a disorder is worldwide is difficult?","options":["Disorders exist only in cultures that have their own diagnostic manual.","Panic disorder is a purely genetic condition, so it cannot differ across cultures.","Every country applies diagnostic criteria in exactly the same way, so recorded rates are already directly comparable.","Cultures differ in how distress is expressed and what is considered normal, so applying diagnostic criteria consistently across cultures is difficult."],"answer":3,"rationale":"Social norms and cultural expressions of distress shape which behaviors are seen as symptoms, which makes consistent diagnosis across cultures difficult. Ataque de nervios is a culture-bound expression of panic-like distress."},{"id":"U5-086","section":"0.1","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":1,"stem":"Dr. Yamada's research examines how levels of cortisol and serotonin in the body relate to people's mood, memory and behavior. Dr. Yamada's approach is best described as","options":["biological","cognitive","humanistic","sociocultural"],"answer":0,"rationale":"Studying hormones and neurotransmitters such as cortisol and serotonin is the focus of the biological perspective. Although memory is involved, Dr. Yamada explains it through body chemistry rather than mental processes alone."},{"id":"U5-087","section":"0.1","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Dr. Okoye runs a sleep laboratory where participants' brain activity is recorded throughout the night. She notes that bursts of activity arise in the brainstem during REM sleep, and she believes dreams are simply the cortex's attempt to make sense of this random neural firing. Which perspective best supports Dr. Okoye's view of dreaming?","options":["Psychodynamic","Sociocultural","Biological","Humanistic"],"answer":2,"rationale":"Explaining dreams as the brain's interpretation of random brainstem activity (activation-synthesis theory) is a biological explanation. A psychodynamic view would treat dreams as disguised unconscious wishes."},{"id":"U5-088","section":"5.3","skill":"Data Interpretation","difficulty":2,"stimulus":{"type":"bar","title":"Hypothetical Rates of Schizophrenia Diagnosis by Household Income Group","xLabel":"Household income group","yLabel":"Percent diagnosed","categories":["Lowest","Lower middle","Middle","Upper middle","Highest"],"series":[{"name":"Percent diagnosed","values":[1.6,1.0,0.8,0.6,0.5]}],"yMax":2},"stem":"The graph shows hypothetical percentages of people diagnosed with schizophrenia in five household income groups. A researcher argues that the steady rise in diagnoses from the highest to the lowest income group suggests that social and economic conditions may contribute to the disorder. This argument most clearly reflects which perspective?","options":["Cognitive","Humanistic","Evolutionary","Sociocultural"],"answer":3,"rationale":"Income reflects socioeconomic status, a social and economic condition, which the sociocultural perspective examines as a factor in psychological disorders. The graph contains no information about thinking, personal growth or survival value."},{"id":"U5-089","section":"1.1","skill":"Research Methods and Design","difficulty":2,"stem":"A research team wants to estimate how strongly genetic factors contribute to schizophrenia by examining patterns within families. Which pair of variables would be of most direct interest for this correlational research?","options":["Each person's dopamine activity and the severity of his or her hallucinations","The age at which each person was diagnosed and the number of hospital stays he or she has had","How much genetic material each pair of relatives shares (for example, identical twins, fraternal twins or cousins) and how often both members of each pair are diagnosed","Whether each person grew up in a city or a rural area and whether he or she was diagnosed"],"answer":2,"rationale":"If the rate at which both relatives share a diagnosis rises as genetic relatedness increases, that pattern suggests a genetic contribution. The other pairs of variables do not measure genetic similarity between relatives."},{"id":"U5-090","section":"5.5","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"A weekly peer support group for people caring for family members with dementia meets at a community center. Members share their experiences in a warm, nonjudgmental atmosphere, listen to one another with empathy, and encourage each other to grow despite their challenges. This kind of group is most closely rooted in which therapeutic perspective?","options":["Psychoanalytic","Humanistic","Behavioral","Biomedical"],"answer":1,"rationale":"Support groups grew out of humanistic ideas, such as acceptance, empathy and the belief that social support helps people grow. Psychoanalytic groups would focus on unconscious conflicts, and behavioral groups on conditioning techniques."},{"id":"U5-091","section":"0.1","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":1,"stem":"Chiamaka is very talkative and outgoing. Her psychologist points out that, from early childhood, her parents and teachers praised her every time she spoke up in class or introduced herself to new people. This explanation of her outgoing personality reflects which perspective?","options":["Behavioral","Psychodynamic","Humanistic","Biological"],"answer":0,"rationale":"The behavioral perspective explains personality as a set of responses shaped by reinforcement. Here, repeated praise strengthened Chiamaka's outgoing behavior."},{"id":"U5-092","section":"5.3","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Rafael, 22, has followed the same routine for years: he wakes at 4:30 a.m., eats the same breakfast, and runs exactly 10 kilometers before sunrise, even on holidays. His classmates find this odd. Rafael enjoys the routine, earns high grades, works part-time, has a close group of friends, and adjusts easily when travel occasionally disrupts his schedule. Psychologists would be hesitant to diagnose Rafael with a psychological disorder mainly because his behavior","options":["does not appear to cause him distress or impair his daily functioning","is rare, and rare behaviors are never considered when identifying disorders","is shared by nearly all university students","clearly has a biological cause"],"answer":0,"rationale":"Diagnosis depends heavily on distress and dysfunction, and Rafael is content and functioning well in school, work and friendships. His routine does deviate from the norm, but deviation alone is not enough for a diagnosis."},{"id":"U5-093","section":"5.3","skill":"Data Interpretation","difficulty":2,"stimulus":{"type":"bar","title":"Hypothetical Concordance Rates for Bipolar Disorder in Twin Pairs","xLabel":"Type of twin pair","yLabel":"Percent of pairs in which both twins are diagnosed","categories":["Identical twins","Fraternal twins"],"series":[{"name":"Concordance rate","values":[55,8]}],"yMax":100},"stem":"The graph shows hypothetical concordance rates for bipolar disorder, meaning the percentage of twin pairs in which both twins are diagnosed. Identical twins share nearly all of their genes, while fraternal twins share about half, like ordinary siblings. These data are most consistent with which approach to explaining bipolar disorder?","options":["Humanistic","Biological","Behavioral","Sociocultural"],"answer":1,"rationale":"Identical twins, who share more genes, are far more likely to share the diagnosis than fraternal twins, which points to a genetic contribution emphasized by the biological approach. Both kinds of twins usually grow up in similar environments, so environment alone does not explain the gap."},{"id":"U5-094","section":"5.3","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Kenji, a hospital pharmacist, eats every meal standing at a tall counter and wears a wool hat indoors all year, habits that no one else he knows shares. He performs well at work, has close friends, and says his habits make him comfortable. Of the factors psychologists use to identify psychological disorders, which one does Kenji's behavior meet?","options":["Level of dysfunction","Perception of distress","Deviation from social norms","Danger to himself or others"],"answer":2,"rationale":"Kenji's habits are unusual compared with the people around him, so they deviate from social norms. He functions well, is not distressed and poses no danger, so deviation alone would not justify a diagnosis."},{"id":"U5-095","section":"5.5","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"A psychoanalyst argues that a certain therapy removes a client's observable symptoms, such as avoiding feared places, without addressing the inner conflicts that produced them, so new symptoms may appear later. The psychoanalyst is most likely criticizing which approach?","options":["Psychodynamic","Humanistic","Sociocultural","Behavioral"],"answer":3,"rationale":"Behavior therapy targets observable behavior directly, which led psychoanalysts to argue that it treats symptoms rather than underlying causes. Behavior therapists respond that the problem behavior itself is what needs to change."},{"id":"U5-096","section":"5.3","skill":"Concept Recall","difficulty":1,"stem":"Which perspective would explain depression as the result of anger toward a lost loved one that is turned inward and kept out of awareness, with roots in early childhood experiences?","options":["Behavioral","Cognitive","Psychodynamic","Evolutionary"],"answer":2,"rationale":"Explanations based on unconscious feelings and conflicts that began in childhood are psychodynamic. A cognitive explanation would emphasize conscious negative thinking patterns."},{"id":"U5-097","section":"5.5","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Nikhil avoids speaking in class because he believes, \"If I make one mistake, everyone will think I am stupid.\" His therapist helps him identify this belief, test it against evidence, and replace it with a more realistic one, expecting that once his thinking changes, his avoidance will decrease. Which type of therapy is Nikhil receiving?","options":["Cognitive therapy","Systematic desensitization","Person-centered therapy","Psychodynamic therapy"],"answer":0,"rationale":"Cognitive therapy aims to change behavior and emotion by changing maladaptive thoughts. Systematic desensitization would instead pair relaxation with gradual exposure to speaking situations."},{"id":"U5-098","section":"5.4","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Dr. Rahman's client, Adaeze, is 27. For more than six months she has worried on most days about many different things, including her job, her family's health, money and small everyday tasks. She finds the worry hard to control and feels restless and tense, and she often has trouble falling asleep. She does not have sudden panic attacks, and no single situation triggers her worry. Adaeze's symptoms are most consistent with which disorder?","options":["Panic disorder","Persistent depressive disorder","Social anxiety disorder","Generalized anxiety disorder"],"answer":3,"rationale":"Generalized anxiety disorder involves prolonged, hard-to-control anxiety about many nonspecific concerns. Panic disorder centers on unexpected panic attacks, and social anxiety disorder centers on fear of being judged by others."},{"id":"U5-201","section":"5.4","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Over the past year, Hamdan has stopped going to shopping malls, riding the metro, and waiting in long queues because he fears he would be trapped and unable to get help if he suddenly felt panicky. Recently he has started refusing to leave his home unless his brother goes with him. Hamdan's symptoms are most consistent with which disorder?","options":["Social anxiety disorder","Specific phobia","Generalized anxiety disorder","Agoraphobia"],"answer":3,"rationale":"Agoraphobia involves intense fear of situations such as crowds, public transportation, or being away from home, where escape or help might be hard to get. Social anxiety disorder centers on fear of being judged by others, which is not what Hamdan describes."},{"id":"U5-202","section":"5.4","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":3,"stem":"Dr. Nakamura tests volunteers in two sessions. In the first session, half of the volunteers, chosen at random, receive word puzzles that can all be solved. The other half receive puzzles that look similar but have no solution, although they are told the puzzles are solvable. In the second session, everyone receives the same set of easy, solvable puzzles. Volunteers who had the unsolvable puzzles give up much sooner and solve far fewer puzzles. Repeated everyday experiences like the one created for the unsolvable-puzzle volunteers are most often proposed as a contributor to which disorder?","options":["Schizophrenia","Hoarding disorder","Major depressive disorder","Avoidant personality disorder"],"answer":2,"rationale":"Group 2 learned that their efforts did not control the outcome, a pattern called learned helplessness. Learned helplessness has been proposed as a contributor to depression, because people stop trying even when they could succeed."},{"id":"U5-203","section":"5.4","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":1,"set":"u5b_kwame","stem":"Kwame, age 22, has recently begun speaking in ways others cannot follow, jumping between unrelated topics in the middle of sentences. He also reports hearing voices commenting on his actions when no one else is in the room. Which diagnosis best fits Kwame's symptoms?","options":["Dissociative identity disorder","Bipolar II disorder","Autism spectrum disorder","Schizophrenia"],"answer":3,"rationale":"Disorganized speech and auditory hallucinations are positive symptoms of schizophrenia. Dissociative identity disorder involves separate identities, not hallucinated voices and incoherent speech."},{"id":"U5-204","section":"5.5","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"set":"u5b_kwame","stem":"Kwame, age 22, has recently begun speaking in ways others cannot follow, jumping between unrelated topics in the middle of sentences. He also reports hearing voices commenting on his actions when no one else is in the room. Which treatment is most likely to be used as the primary treatment for Kwame's symptoms?","options":["Systematic desensitization","Antipsychotic medication","Lithium carbonate","Psychoanalytic dream interpretation"],"answer":1,"rationale":"Antipsychotic medications, which block dopamine receptors, are the main treatment for the hallucinations and disorganized thinking of schizophrenia. Lithium is a mood stabilizer used mainly for bipolar disorder."},{"id":"U5-205","section":"5.3","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Dr. Ortega argues that rates of bulimia nervosa rise in communities where social media influencers, fashion magazines, and peer groups constantly praise very thin bodies and share dieting tips. Which perspective on psychological disorders best matches Dr. Ortega's argument?","options":["Biological","Evolutionary","Cognitive","Sociocultural"],"answer":3,"rationale":"The sociocultural perspective explains disorders through the influence of cultural messages, media, and social relationships. A biological explanation would focus on genes or brain chemistry instead."},{"id":"U5-206","section":"5.4","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Eight months ago, a fire broke out in Ibrahim's apartment building. He escaped without injury, but he now has vivid flashbacks of the smoke-filled stairwell, startles at sudden noises, sleeps poorly, and avoids walking past the building. Which disorder is most consistent with Ibrahim's experience?","options":["Specific phobia","Panic disorder","Posttraumatic stress disorder","Generalized anxiety disorder"],"answer":2,"rationale":"Posttraumatic stress disorder follows exposure to a traumatic event and includes flashbacks, hypervigilance, insomnia, and avoidance of reminders. Generalized anxiety disorder involves broad, nonspecific worry that is not tied to a traumatic event."},{"id":"U5-207","section":"5.4","skill":"Concept Recall","difficulty":2,"stem":"In the current DSM classification, which of the following disorders is placed in the category of anxiety disorders?","options":["Social anxiety disorder","Posttraumatic stress disorder","Obsessive-compulsive disorder","Persistent depressive disorder"],"answer":0,"rationale":"Social anxiety disorder is one of the anxiety disorders. Obsessive-compulsive disorder and posttraumatic stress disorder were once grouped with anxiety disorders but now have their own categories (obsessive-compulsive and related disorders, and trauma- and stressor-related disorders)."},{"id":"U5-208","section":"5.4","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Four-year-old Lin rarely responds when her name is called, seldom makes back-and-forth conversation with other children, and lines up her toy cars in exact rows. She becomes very distressed if anyone moves them. Which disorder is most consistent with these behaviors?","options":["Attention-deficit/hyperactivity disorder","Obsessive-compulsive disorder","Autism spectrum disorder","Social anxiety disorder"],"answer":2,"rationale":"Autism spectrum disorder is a neurodevelopmental disorder marked by difficulties with social communication together with restricted, repetitive patterns of behavior, both of which Lin shows. ADHD centers on inattention, hyperactivity, and impulsivity rather than social communication and rigid routines."},{"id":"U5-209","section":"5.4","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"For the past month, Rania has lost interest in the hobbies she used to enjoy, sleeps far more than usual, has trouble concentrating at work, and describes herself as worthless. She has no history of periods of unusually elevated mood. Rania's symptoms are most consistent with which disorder?","options":["Generalized anxiety disorder","Bipolar I disorder","Major depressive disorder","Persistent depressive disorder"],"answer":2,"rationale":"Several weeks of loss of interest, sleep changes, poor concentration, and feelings of worthlessness fit major depressive disorder. Persistent depressive disorder requires a milder low mood lasting at least two years, and bipolar I disorder requires a manic episode."},{"id":"U5-210","section":"5.4","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"For about three years, Tomas has felt down on most days. He has low energy, a small appetite, and little confidence in himself, but he keeps going to work and has never been unable to function. Which disorder best fits Tomas's pattern of symptoms?","options":["Generalized anxiety disorder","Bipolar II disorder","Persistent depressive disorder","Avoidant personality disorder"],"answer":2,"rationale":"Persistent depressive disorder involves a chronic, often milder depressed mood lasting at least two years along with symptoms such as low energy and appetite changes. Bipolar II disorder would also require periods of hypomania."},{"id":"U5-211","section":"5.4","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Mariam, age 24, is convinced that her neighbors are using hidden devices to broadcast her thoughts. Over the past year her family has noticed that she speaks very little, shows almost no facial expression, and no longer washes or changes her clothes unless reminded. Mariam's symptoms are most consistent with which disorder?","options":["Major depressive disorder","Schizoid personality disorder","Schizophrenia","Bipolar I disorder"],"answer":2,"rationale":"Schizophrenia combines positive symptoms, such as delusions, with negative symptoms, such as flat affect and reduced speech, and causes major impairment. Schizoid personality disorder involves emotional detachment but not delusions, and nothing suggests the mood episodes of bipolar I disorder."},{"id":"U5-212","section":"5.4","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"After his family business collapsed, Rashid could not recall the weeks surrounding the closure, including meetings with his staff and the day he locked the shop for the last time. He still remembers general facts and skills, such as how to drive. A medical exam found no head injury or neurological illness, and he reports no flashbacks or nightmares. Which disorder is Rashid most likely experiencing?","options":["Dissociative amnesia","Posttraumatic stress disorder","Dissociative identity disorder","Schizophrenia"],"answer":0,"rationale":"Dissociative amnesia is an inability to recall important personal information, usually linked to stress or trauma, with no physical cause. Posttraumatic stress disorder would involve intrusive memories such as flashbacks rather than a simple gap in memory."},{"id":"U5-213","section":"5.4","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Last month, Sofia slept only two or three hours a night for over a week, talked so rapidly that friends could barely keep up, and spent most of her savings on several new business ideas. This week she has stayed in bed, feels hopeless, and has little appetite. Which diagnosis is Sofia most likely to receive?","options":["Bipolar I disorder","Major depressive disorder","Schizophrenia","Borderline personality disorder"],"answer":0,"rationale":"Bipolar I disorder involves at least one manic episode (little sleep, rapid speech, risky spending) and often periods of depression. Major depressive disorder alone does not include manic episodes."},{"id":"U5-214","section":"5.5","skill":"Concept Recall","difficulty":1,"stem":"A psychiatrist wants to prescribe a mood stabilizer to reduce the frequency and intensity of manic episodes in a patient with bipolar I disorder. Which medication is the psychiatrist most likely to choose?","options":["An SSRI antidepressant taken on its own","A beta blocker such as propranolol","A melatonin sleep supplement","Lithium"],"answer":3,"rationale":"Lithium is a classic mood stabilizer used to even out the highs and lows of bipolar disorder. An antidepressant taken alone can trigger manic symptoms, and beta blockers and melatonin do not stabilize mood."},{"id":"U5-215","section":"5.4","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Darius, age 30, regularly lies to his coworkers to take credit for their work, borrows money from friends with no intention of repaying it, and drives recklessly. When confronted, he shrugs and shows no remorse. Darius's behavior is most consistent with which personality disorder?","options":["Antisocial","Histrionic","Paranoid","Narcissistic"],"answer":0,"rationale":"Antisocial personality disorder involves a lasting pattern of deceit, exploitation, recklessness, and lack of remorse. Narcissistic personality disorder centers on grandiosity and a need for admiration rather than rule-breaking and manipulation without guilt."},{"id":"U5-216","section":"5.4","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Kofi, 28, is awaiting trial for setting up a fake online charity that collected money from hundreds of donors, whom he calls \"easy targets.\" School records show that from age 13 he was suspended again and again for fighting, shoplifting, and damaging property. Which diagnosis best fits Kofi's current behavior?","options":["Borderline personality disorder","Paranoid personality disorder","Antisocial personality disorder","Conduct disorder"],"answer":2,"rationale":"Antisocial personality disorder is diagnosed in adults with a lasting pattern of disregard for others' rights, usually preceded by conduct problems in childhood. Conduct disorder is the childhood diagnosis, so it does not describe his current adult behavior."},{"id":"U5-217","section":"5.3","skill":"Concept Recall","difficulty":1,"stem":"According to the cognitive perspective, depression is primarily caused by","options":["low levels of serotonin and norepinephrine in the brain","negative, distorted ways of interpreting oneself, the world, and the future","a lack of reinforcement for positive behaviors in the environment","unresolved unconscious conflicts from early childhood"],"answer":1,"rationale":"The cognitive perspective focuses on maladaptive thoughts, such as the negative cognitive triad about the self, world, and future. Low neurotransmitter levels describe a biological explanation, and a lack of reinforcement describes a behavioral one."},{"id":"U5-218","section":"5.5","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"A client with major depressive disorder begins working with a cognitive therapist. Which activity is the therapist most likely to include in the treatment plan?","options":["Rewarding the client with tokens for each household task completed","Asking the client to say whatever comes to mind so hidden conflicts can surface","Letting the client lead every session while the therapist offers unconditional positive regard","Having the client keep a log of automatic negative thoughts and test the evidence for each one"],"answer":3,"rationale":"Cognitive therapy uses cognitive restructuring: identifying automatic negative thoughts and challenging them with evidence. Free association is psychodynamic, unconditional positive regard is humanistic, and tokens are a behavioral technique."},{"id":"U5-219","section":"5.4","skill":"Concept Recall","difficulty":1,"stem":"Which characteristic is most central to narcissistic personality disorder?","options":["Intense fear of abandonment along with unstable relationships","An excessive need to be taken care of by others","Pervasive suspicion that others intend harm","A grandiose sense of self-importance and a strong need for admiration"],"answer":3,"rationale":"Narcissistic personality disorder is defined by grandiosity, a need for admiration, and limited empathy. Fear of abandonment points to borderline, suspicion to paranoid, and the need to be cared for to dependent personality disorder."},{"id":"U5-220","section":"5.4","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which situation best illustrates a compulsion, as the term is used in describing obsessive-compulsive disorder?","options":["Kenji has repeated, unwanted images of germs crawling on his hands.","Omar worries about how he will perform on his upcoming final exams.","Ana feels mildly uncomfortable when her desk is messy at the end of the day.","Leila washes her hands in a fixed sequence twenty times after touching a door handle, which often makes her late to class."],"answer":3,"rationale":"A compulsion is a repetitive behavior performed to reduce the anxiety caused by an obsession, and it interferes with daily life. Kenji's intrusive images are an obsession, which is a thought rather than an action."},{"id":"U5-221","section":"5.4","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"For more than a year, Nasser has felt tense and on edge most days. He worries about his health, his finances, and his children's grades even when nothing is going wrong, and he has constant muscle tension and trouble falling asleep. There is no single object or situation that triggers his anxiety. Nasser is most likely to be diagnosed with","options":["generalized anxiety disorder","specific phobia","major depressive disorder","obsessive-compulsive disorder"],"answer":0,"rationale":"Generalized anxiety disorder involves prolonged, nonspecific worry and physical tension across many areas of life. A specific phobia is tied to one object or situation, and nothing suggests the depressed mood or loss of interest of major depressive disorder."},{"id":"U5-222","section":"5.4","skill":"Concept Recall","difficulty":1,"stem":"A firmly held false belief that persists despite clear evidence against it, such as the belief that one is being followed by secret agents, is a hallmark symptom of which group of disorders?","options":["Obsessive-compulsive and related disorders","Dissociative disorders","Anxiety disorders","Schizophrenia spectrum disorders"],"answer":3,"rationale":"Delusions are positive symptoms of schizophrenia spectrum disorders. Dissociative disorders involve disruptions of memory or identity rather than fixed false beliefs."},{"id":"U5-223","section":"4.1","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Yusuf has just learned that he was not selected for his school's football team. Which explanation from Yusuf would most likely indicate the explanatory style associated with depression?","options":["\"The coach only picked his favorite players this year.\"","\"I had a bad day at tryouts, so I will train harder for next season.\"","\"I fail at everything I try, and it will always be that way.\"","\"A lot of strong players tried out, so the competition was tough.\""],"answer":2,"rationale":"A depressive explanatory style explains bad events as internal (my fault), stable (always), and global (everything). Blaming the coach or the competition is an external explanation."},{"id":"U5-224","section":"5.4","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which of the following examples best matches the symptoms of a dissociative disorder?","options":["After a frightening accident at sea, Mateo travels to another city and starts working under a new name, with no memory of his past life.","Halima has sudden attacks of pounding heart and dizziness while shopping and now avoids crowded markets.","Ben has vivid flashbacks of a car crash and jumps at every sound of screeching tires.","Priya hears voices criticizing her and believes strangers can read her mind."],"answer":0,"rationale":"Dissociative amnesia with fugue involves loss of memory for one's identity and past, sometimes with unplanned travel, usually after stress or trauma. Ben's flashbacks fit posttraumatic stress disorder, which does not involve losing one's identity."},{"id":"U5-225","section":"5.4","skill":"Concept Recall","difficulty":2,"stem":"A researcher reviews many case histories of people diagnosed with dissociative identity disorder. Which finding is she most likely to report?","options":["Symptoms usually first appear in late adulthood following a stroke or head injury.","People with the disorder report fewer stressful life events than the general population.","Most individuals report a history of severe, often repeated, trauma or stress.","Most cases can be traced to a single gene inherited from one parent."],"answer":2,"rationale":"Dissociative disorders are linked to the experience of severe trauma or stress, often in childhood, and dissociation is thought to serve as a way to cope. A head injury or stroke would point to a medical cause of memory problems rather than a dissociative disorder."},{"id":"U5-226","section":"5.4","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"For many years, Ji-ho has believed that his coworkers are secretly plotting against him. He reads hidden insults into harmless comments and refuses to share personal information because he expects it to be used against him. He has no hallucinations. Which personality disorder best fits this lasting pattern?","options":["Schizoid","Paranoid","Histrionic","Avoidant"],"answer":1,"rationale":"Paranoid personality disorder, in Cluster A, involves pervasive, unjustified distrust and suspicion of others. Avoidant personality disorder involves fear of rejection, not a belief that others are plotting harm."},{"id":"U5-227","section":"5.4","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":3,"stem":"Ayesha has had several periods lasting four or five days in which she felt unusually cheerful, slept little, and was much more productive than usual. Friends noticed the change, but it did not disrupt her job or require hospitalization. She has also had episodes of major depression lasting several weeks. Which diagnosis best fits Ayesha's history?","options":["Bipolar I disorder","Persistent depressive disorder","Bipolar II disorder","Borderline personality disorder"],"answer":2,"rationale":"Bipolar II disorder involves at least one hypomanic episode (milder elevated mood that does not cause severe impairment) plus major depressive episodes. Bipolar I disorder requires a full manic episode that seriously disrupts functioning."},{"id":"U5-228","section":"5.4","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Ms. Okafor, 41, did not pass her professional licensing exam for the second time. In the two months since, she has had little energy, has stopped meeting friends, wakes very early and cannot fall back asleep, and says she expects every plan she makes to fail. She has not experienced any life-threatening or traumatic event. She is most likely experiencing","options":["posttraumatic stress disorder","major depressive disorder","persistent depressive disorder","generalized anxiety disorder"],"answer":1,"rationale":"Two months of low energy, withdrawal, early waking, and pessimism fit major depressive disorder. Posttraumatic stress disorder requires exposure to a traumatic event, and persistent depressive disorder requires a low mood lasting at least two years."},{"id":"U5-229","section":"5.4","skill":"Concept Recall","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which disorder is defined by a period of at least two weeks of depressed mood or loss of interest or pleasure, along with symptoms such as changes in sleep, appetite, and concentration?","options":["Persistent depressive disorder","Bipolar II disorder","Major depressive disorder","Generalized anxiety disorder"],"answer":2,"rationale":"A major depressive episode lasts at least two weeks and includes depressed mood or loss of pleasure plus other symptoms. Persistent depressive disorder involves a longer-lasting, usually milder low mood of at least two years."},{"id":"U5-230","section":"5.4","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Arjun, a university student, avoids answering questions in seminars and refuses to eat in the cafeteria because he fears others will judge him as awkward. His heart pounds and his hands shake whenever he must speak in front of classmates. Arjun's symptoms are most consistent with which disorder?","options":["Agoraphobia","Specific phobia","Generalized anxiety disorder","Social anxiety disorder"],"answer":3,"rationale":"Social anxiety disorder is an intense fear of being watched or judged by others in social or performance situations. Agoraphobia involves fear of places where escape might be hard, not fear of evaluation."},{"id":"U5-231","section":"5.4","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Elena has repeated, unexpected surges of intense fear in which her heart races, her chest feels tight, and she feels dizzy. Between these episodes she worries constantly about when the next one will happen. In which category of the DSM would Elena's disorder most likely be classified?","options":["Trauma- and stressor-related disorders","Depressive disorders","Obsessive-compulsive and related disorders","Anxiety disorders"],"answer":3,"rationale":"Elena's unexpected panic attacks and worry about future attacks describe panic disorder, which is classified as an anxiety disorder. Trauma- and stressor-related disorders require a traumatic event as the trigger."},{"id":"U5-232","section":"5.4","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":1,"stem":"Gabriel feels intense dread whenever he has to cross a large open plaza or a wide parking lot, and he has started staying at home whenever possible to avoid these places. In which DSM category would Gabriel's symptoms most likely be classified?","options":["Personality disorder","Depressive disorder","Dissociative disorder","Anxiety disorder"],"answer":3,"rationale":"Gabriel's fear and avoidance of open spaces describe agoraphobia, which is classified as an anxiety disorder. Nothing in the case suggests memory loss, a depressed mood, or a lifelong personality pattern."},{"id":"U5-233","section":"5.4","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Zara, the captain of her basketball club, insists that teammates acknowledge she is by far the most talented player. She exaggerates her role in every win, presents teammates' strategy ideas as her own to impress the coach, and becomes furious at any criticism. Zara's behavior is most consistent with which disorder?","options":["Histrionic personality disorder","Avoidant personality disorder","Borderline personality disorder","Narcissistic personality disorder"],"answer":3,"rationale":"Narcissistic personality disorder involves grandiosity, exaggerated achievements, exploiting others to boost one's image, and a strong need for admiration. Histrionic personality disorder centers on attention-seeking emotional display rather than a belief in one's own superiority."},{"id":"U5-234","section":"5.4","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"For most of the past year, Lucas has spent hours each day worrying about worst-case scenarios: that his sister's flight might crash, that the economy will collapse and he will lose his savings, or that he has forgotten something important at work. He tries to stop worrying but cannot, and he feels restless and tired most of the time. He does not perform any repeated rituals. Which disorder is most consistent with Lucas's symptoms?","options":["Obsessive-compulsive disorder","Posttraumatic stress disorder","Panic disorder","Generalized anxiety disorder"],"answer":3,"rationale":"Generalized anxiety disorder involves prolonged, hard-to-control worry about many everyday topics, along with restlessness and fatigue. Without repetitive rituals performed to reduce intrusive thoughts, obsessive-compulsive disorder is not the best fit."},{"id":"U5-235","section":"4.7","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"In a study, students first ran up several flights of stairs, which raised their heart rates. Half then watched a very funny video, and the other half were told about an unfair grading decision. Students in the video condition described their racing hearts as excitement and amusement, while the others described the same arousal as anger. Which explanation of emotion do these results best support?","options":["An emotion depends on bodily arousal combined with a cognitive label drawn from the situation.","Bodily arousal and the felt emotion occur at the same time but independently of each other.","Each emotion is produced by its own distinct pattern of bodily arousal, which is felt first.","Changing one's facial expression is what produces the emotional experience."],"answer":0,"rationale":"The same racing heart was labeled as amusement or anger depending on the situation, so the emotion came from arousal plus a cognitive label (often taught as the two-factor view). If each emotion had its own arousal pattern, the identical arousal could not have produced two different emotions."},{"id":"U5-236","section":"5.4","skill":"Concept Recall","difficulty":1,"stem":"A person who feels driven to tap each light switch exactly ten times before leaving a room, because not doing so causes intense anxiety, is showing","options":["an obsession","a compulsion","a phobia","a delusion"],"answer":1,"rationale":"A compulsion is a repetitive behavior a person feels driven to perform to reduce anxiety. An obsession is the unwanted thought itself, not the action."},{"id":"U5-237","section":"5.4","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":1,"stem":"During an evaluation, a patient reports seeing small figures moving across the floor of an empty room and smelling smoke when nothing is burning. Medical tests rule out drug use and neurological illness. These experiences are most characteristic of which group of disorders?","options":["Schizophrenia spectrum and other psychotic disorders","Anxiety disorders","Dissociative disorders","Personality disorders"],"answer":0,"rationale":"Perceiving sights or smells that are not present are hallucinations, a positive symptom of schizophrenia spectrum and other psychotic disorders. Dissociative disorders involve disruptions of memory or identity rather than false perceptions."},{"id":"U5-238","section":"5.4","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"For the past eight months, Chen, 23, has refused to eat any meal his family prepares because he is certain they are slowly poisoning him, even though tests of the food show nothing. At night, while alone in his room, he hears two voices arguing about what he deserves. Chen's symptoms are most consistent with which disorder?","options":["Paranoid personality disorder","Schizophrenia","Obsessive-compulsive disorder","Social anxiety disorder"],"answer":1,"rationale":"Chen has both a persecutory delusion (being poisoned) and auditory hallucinations, which together point to schizophrenia. Paranoid personality disorder involves lasting distrust of others but not hallucinations or fixed delusions of this kind."},{"id":"U5-239","section":"5.4","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Mehmet, 35, has been fired from four jobs in five years for fights with coworkers and for using company cards for personal purchases. He has been fined repeatedly for speeding with his young nephews in the car and laughs off the danger. His older sister says that since about age 12 he has lied constantly and stolen from neighbors. These behaviors are most consistent with which disorder?","options":["Antisocial personality disorder","Borderline personality disorder","Narcissistic personality disorder","Obsessive-compulsive personality disorder"],"answer":0,"rationale":"Antisocial personality disorder involves a lasting pattern of deceit, aggression, reckless disregard for others' safety, and lack of remorse, with conduct problems beginning before age 15. Narcissistic personality disorder centers on grandiosity and a need for admiration rather than rule-breaking and recklessness."},{"id":"U5-240","section":"5.4","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Farida is certain that the presenters on the evening news are sending her personal coded messages telling her she has been chosen to lead a world peace mission. No evidence her family shows her changes her mind. Farida's symptoms point most strongly to which DSM category?","options":["Obsessive-compulsive and related disorders","Anxiety disorders","Dissociative disorders","Schizophrenia spectrum and other psychotic disorders"],"answer":3,"rationale":"A fixed false belief that broadcasts contain special messages meant for oneself is a delusion, a positive symptom of schizophrenia spectrum disorders. Obsessive-compulsive disorder involves intrusive thoughts that the person usually recognizes as excessive, not fixed false beliefs."},{"id":"U5-241","section":"5.4","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Rafael, a well-known chef, describes himself as a culinary genius whose talents only a few elite critics can truly appreciate. He expects his staff to praise his dishes constantly, demands special treatment from suppliers, and shows little concern when exhausted staff ask for a break. These characteristics are most associated with which personality disorder?","options":["Narcissistic","Avoidant","Borderline","Paranoid"],"answer":0,"rationale":"Narcissistic personality disorder involves grandiosity, a sense of entitlement, a constant need for admiration, and a lack of empathy. Borderline personality disorder centers on unstable emotions, relationships, and self-image rather than a stable sense of superiority."},{"id":"U5-242","section":"5.4","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"While waiting at a bus stop each morning, Ricardo watches the colors of passing cars. He is completely certain that the order of red and white cars is a coded instruction sent to him alone by a secret agency, and he spends hours writing down the colors to work out his orders. Ricardo's belief about the cars is best described as","options":["a hallucination","an obsession","a negative symptom","a delusion"],"answer":3,"rationale":"Ricardo sees real cars but holds a fixed false belief about what they mean, which is a delusion. A hallucination would involve perceiving something that is not there, and an obsession is an intrusive thought the person usually recognizes as excessive rather than fully believes."},{"id":"U5-243","section":"5.4","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Talia believes she has been personally chosen to solve every problem facing the world and that world leaders are waiting for her orders. She hears a voice giving her instructions when no one is present, and her speech often jumps between unrelated words that do not form sentences. She has no history of episodes of depression or of unusually elevated mood. Talia's symptoms are most consistent with which disorder?","options":["Schizophrenia","Narcissistic personality disorder","Dissociative identity disorder","Bipolar I disorder"],"answer":0,"rationale":"Delusions of grandeur, auditory hallucinations, and disorganized speech are positive symptoms of schizophrenia. Grandiosity can occur in mania or narcissistic personality disorder, but Talia has no mood episodes, and narcissistic personality disorder does not involve hallucinations."},{"id":"U5-244","section":"5.4","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Ten months after a strong earthquake damaged her school, Keiko, 17, refuses to enter buildings taller than two floors, jumps whenever a heavy truck rumbles past, sleeps poorly, and no longer enjoys time with friends. Several times a week she suddenly feels as if the shaking is happening all over again. Keiko is most likely experiencing","options":["a specific phobia","posttraumatic stress disorder","panic disorder","generalized anxiety disorder"],"answer":1,"rationale":"Exposure to a traumatic event followed by flashbacks, hypervigilance, insomnia, avoidance, and emotional detachment is the pattern of posttraumatic stress disorder. A specific phobia would involve fear of tall buildings without the reliving of a trauma and the other symptoms."},{"id":"U5-245","section":"5.4","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":1,"stem":"Every morning before school, Dalia empties and repacks her school bag five or six times, checking that each book is inside, because she cannot shake a nagging feeling that something is missing. She is often late as a result. Her repeated repacking would be classified as","options":["an obsession","a specific phobia","a compulsion","a delusion"],"answer":2,"rationale":"The repeated behavior Dalia performs to relieve her anxious feeling is a compulsion. The nagging thought that something is missing is the obsession that drives it."},{"id":"U5-246","section":"5.4","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"A clinician's notes describe a patient whose speech is a jumble of unrelated words that listeners cannot follow, and who holds a fixed false belief that the hospital staff are poisoning his food, despite clear evidence to the contrary. Which disorder is the patient most likely experiencing?","options":["Obsessive-compulsive disorder","Bipolar II disorder","Schizophrenia","Dissociative identity disorder"],"answer":2,"rationale":"Disorganized speech (word salad) and delusions are positive symptoms of schizophrenia. Bipolar II disorder involves hypomania, which by definition does not include psychotic symptoms, and dissociative identity disorder involves separate identities rather than word salad and delusions."},{"id":"U5-247","section":"5.4","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"At several birthday parties when he was a young child, Malik was badly startled when balloons burst loudly right next to him. Now, as a teenager, the mere sight of balloons makes his heart race, and he leaves any event where balloons are present. He has no other unusual fears. Malik's fear of balloons is classified as","options":["agoraphobia","a specific phobia","generalized anxiety disorder","social anxiety disorder"],"answer":1,"rationale":"A specific phobia is intense, irrational fear of a particular object or situation, here likely learned through classical conditioning (balloons paired with a startling bang). Generalized anxiety disorder involves broad worry that is not tied to one object."},{"id":"U5-248","section":"5.4","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":3,"stem":"Ngozi, 20, has never had an episode of depression. For the past nine days, however, she has slept about two hours a night, talked so fast that her family cannot follow her, and announced that she is destined to become the greatest architect in history. She spent all of her university fees on drawing equipment, and her family took her to hospital when she could not be calmed down. Which diagnosis will Ngozi most likely receive?","options":["Major depressive disorder","Bipolar I disorder","Bipolar II disorder","Generalized anxiety disorder"],"answer":1,"rationale":"A single full manic episode (a week or more of elevated mood, little sleep, rapid speech, grandiosity, and serious impairment) is enough for bipolar I disorder, even with no history of depression. Bipolar II disorder requires major depressive episodes and only milder hypomania."},{"id":"U5-249","section":"5.4","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Over several years, Pedro, 40, has taken out loans in his relatives' names without asking them, has left his young children alone for days while he travels, and was dismissed from his last two jobs for threatening managers. When his sister tells him how much he has hurt the family, he says it is their own fault for trusting him. Which diagnosis best accounts for Pedro's long-standing pattern?","options":["antisocial personality disorder","avoidant personality disorder","borderline personality disorder","paranoid personality disorder"],"answer":0,"rationale":"Antisocial personality disorder involves a lasting pattern of exploiting others, irresponsibility, aggression, and a lack of remorse. Borderline personality disorder centers on unstable emotions, relationships, and self-image, and paranoid personality disorder on distrust, not on exploiting others without guilt."},{"id":"U5-250","section":"5.4","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Hana, age 26, asks her mother to choose her clothes, her university courses, and which job offer to accept. She agrees with friends even when she disagrees, because she fears losing their support, and she feels helpless whenever she must be on her own. Which personality disorder best describes Hana's pattern?","options":["Dependent","Avoidant","Histrionic","Schizoid"],"answer":0,"rationale":"Dependent personality disorder, in Cluster C, involves an excessive need to be cared for, difficulty making decisions, and fear of disagreeing with others. Avoidant personality disorder involves avoiding social contact out of fear of rejection, not relying on others to make decisions."},{"id":"U5-251","section":"5.5","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"A psychiatrist prescribes a medication that increases the calming, inhibitory effect of the neurotransmitter GABA in the brain. This medication would most likely be used to treat which disorder?","options":["Panic disorder","Bipolar I disorder","Schizophrenia","Attention-deficit/hyperactivity disorder"],"answer":0,"rationale":"Antianxiety drugs such as benzodiazepines enhance GABA activity, which reduces nervous system arousal, so they are used for anxiety disorders such as panic disorder. Schizophrenia is treated with antipsychotics that act on dopamine."},{"id":"U5-252","section":"5.4","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Five-year-old Idris has delayed speech and often repeats phrases from cartoons instead of answering questions. He flaps his hands when excited, plays alone even when other children invite him, and screams when his bedtime routine is changed in any small way. Idris's behavior is most consistent with which disorder?","options":["Autism spectrum disorder","Social anxiety disorder","Attention-deficit/hyperactivity disorder","Persistent depressive disorder"],"answer":0,"rationale":"Difficulties with social communication combined with repetitive movements and strong insistence on routines are hallmarks of autism spectrum disorder. ADHD involves inattention and hyperactivity but not these social communication and routine-related patterns."},{"id":"U5-253","section":"5.4","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Police find a man sitting at a bus station hundreds of kilometers from his home. He carries a ticket but cannot say his name, where he lives, or how he got there, although he can read, count money, and hold a normal conversation. Doctors find no head injury or illness. His family later explains that two days earlier he had watched a flood destroy the family farm. He would most likely be diagnosed with","options":["dissociative identity disorder","schizophrenia","posttraumatic stress disorder","dissociative amnesia with fugue"],"answer":3,"rationale":"Dissociative amnesia with fugue involves loss of memory for one's identity, often with unplanned travel, following severe stress, while general knowledge and skills stay intact. Posttraumatic stress disorder involves reliving a trauma for more than a month, not forgetting who one is."},{"id":"U5-254","section":"5.4","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"A clinician concludes that Viktor, an engineer, meets the criteria for narcissistic personality disorder. Which observation from his coworkers best fits that diagnosis?","options":["He will not make any decision at work without first checking with a colleague.","He avoids team events because he expects others to reject him.","He insists on sitting with senior executives and sulks whenever his work is not singled out for praise.","He rapidly shifts between idealizing and devaluing the coworkers close to him."],"answer":2,"rationale":"A sense of entitlement and a constant need for admiration are central to narcissistic personality disorder. Needing others to make decisions fits dependent, expecting rejection fits avoidant, and idealizing then devaluing others fits borderline personality disorder."},{"id":"U5-255","section":"5.4","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"A nurse on a ward for people with schizophrenia records observations about four different patients. Which observation describes a negative symptom?","options":["One patient reports seeing shadowy figures in the corner of the room.","One patient insists that he is a famous inventor.","One patient speaks very little and gives only one-word answers.","One patient laughs loudly while describing a sad event."],"answer":2,"rationale":"Negative symptoms are the absence of typical behaviors, such as reduced speech or flat affect. Hallucinations, delusions, and inappropriate emotional responses are positive symptoms because they add something that is not usually present."},{"id":"U5-256","section":"5.5","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Some patients who take older antipsychotic medications develop tremors and stiff movements that resemble Parkinson's disease. This side effect most likely occurs because the medication reduces the activity of which neurotransmitter?","options":["Serotonin","Dopamine","Glutamate","Acetylcholine"],"answer":1,"rationale":"Parkinson's disease involves a loss of dopamine-producing neurons, and antipsychotics block dopamine receptors to reduce schizophrenia symptoms. Reducing dopamine can therefore produce Parkinson's-like movement problems."},{"id":"U5-257","section":"5.5","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Dr. Banerjee prescribes a medication to reduce the hallucinations and delusions experienced by her patient Tariq, who has schizophrenia. The medication most likely works by blocking receptor sites for","options":["GABA","dopamine","acetylcholine","endorphins"],"answer":1,"rationale":"Antipsychotic medications reduce positive symptoms of schizophrenia mainly by blocking dopamine receptors, consistent with the dopamine hypothesis. GABA is the target of antianxiety drugs."},{"id":"U5-258","section":"5.4","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":1,"stem":"Kareem tells his doctor that he secretly owns every airline in the world and that pilots take their orders from him, even though he works as a shop assistant and has no such connections. Which symptom is Kareem experiencing?","options":["Hallucination","Obsession","Flat affect","Delusion"],"answer":3,"rationale":"A delusion is a false belief held despite clear evidence against it; Kareem's belief is a delusion of grandeur. A hallucination is a false perception, such as hearing a voice that is not there."},{"id":"U5-259","section":"5.4","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"While riding the metro, Bilal is struck by a sudden, unwanted thought that the handrail has left dangerous germs on his skin. He knows the idea is exaggerated, but it keeps returning and makes him so anxious that he cannot concentrate on his book. When he gets home, he scrubs his hands for twenty minutes. The recurring, unwanted thought about germs is best described as","options":["a compulsion","an obsession","a delusion","a panic attack"],"answer":1,"rationale":"An obsession is an intrusive, unwanted thought that causes anxiety. Bilal's scrubbing is the compulsion, and because he recognizes the idea as exaggerated, it is not a delusion."},{"id":"U5-260","section":"5.4","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Researchers have observed that large doses of amphetamines, which increase dopamine activity, can produce hallucinations and paranoid delusions in people who do not have schizophrenia. This observation supports the idea that schizophrenia is linked to excess activity of which neurotransmitter?","options":["Serotonin","GABA","Dopamine","Endorphins"],"answer":2,"rationale":"Because drugs that boost dopamine can mimic positive symptoms, and antipsychotics that block dopamine reduce them, researchers proposed that excess dopamine activity contributes to schizophrenia."},{"id":"U5-261","section":"5.4","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":1,"stem":"For the past few months, whenever Ama walks into her family's empty kitchen, she hears a man's voice calling her name and laughing. She searches the house each time, but no one is there, no radio or phone is on, and her sister, who is sometimes with her, hears nothing. What is Ama experiencing?","options":["Illusions","Hallucinations","Obsessions","Flashbacks"],"answer":1,"rationale":"A hallucination is a sensory experience without any real external stimulus, such as hearing a voice no one else hears. An illusion is a misinterpretation of something that is actually present, and an obsession is an intrusive thought rather than a perception."},{"id":"U5-262","section":"5.4","skill":"Concept Recall","difficulty":1,"stem":"In its original form, the dopamine hypothesis proposed that schizophrenia is at least partly caused by","options":["underactivity of dopamine systems in the brain","overactivity of dopamine systems in the brain","excess dopamine released by the adrenal glands into the bloodstream","damage to the myelin sheaths of dopamine-releasing neurons"],"answer":1,"rationale":"The original dopamine hypothesis held that too much dopamine activity in the brain produces symptoms of schizophrenia. Too little dopamine in the brain is linked to Parkinson's disease."},{"id":"U5-263","section":"5.4","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Ravi, age 30, cannot make everyday choices, such as what to eat for lunch, without first asking his older brother. He goes along with plans he dislikes rather than risk upsetting anyone, and he panics at the thought of having to look after himself. Ravi's long-standing pattern best fits which personality disorder?","options":["Borderline","Dependent","Obsessive-compulsive","Paranoid"],"answer":1,"rationale":"Dependent personality disorder involves an excessive need to be taken care of, difficulty making decisions without reassurance, and reluctance to disagree. Borderline personality disorder centers on unstable emotions, relationships, and self-image rather than reliance on others for decisions."},{"id":"U5-264","section":"5.4","skill":"Concept Recall","difficulty":1,"stem":"Which description best captures obsessive-compulsive disorder?","options":["Alternating periods of mania and depression","Intense fear of being judged or watched in social situations","Recurring intrusive thoughts that cause anxiety, often paired with repetitive behaviors meant to reduce it","Loss of memory for important personal information after a stressful event"],"answer":2,"rationale":"Obsessive-compulsive disorder involves obsessions (intrusive, anxiety-provoking thoughts) and compulsions (repetitive behaviors performed to reduce that anxiety). Alternating mania and depression describes bipolar disorder."},{"id":"U5-265","section":"5.4","skill":"Concept Recall","difficulty":1,"stem":"Unwanted, intrusive thoughts, images, or urges that are not memories of past events, yet repeatedly enter a person's mind and cause distress, are called","options":["compulsions","flashbacks","obsessions","hallucinations"],"answer":2,"rationale":"Obsessions are intrusive, unwanted thoughts or urges that the person recognizes as their own and cannot control. Compulsions are the repetitive behaviors performed in response to them."},{"id":"U5-266","section":"5.4","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":1,"stem":"Felipe feels intense distress whenever he must enter a crowded stadium or a busy airport terminal, because he fears he could not get out quickly if he began to feel ill. Nothing bad has ever happened to him in these places, but he now turns down invitations to avoid them. Felipe's likely diagnosis belongs to which DSM category?","options":["Trauma- and stressor-related disorders","Bipolar and related disorders","Anxiety disorders","Dissociative disorders"],"answer":2,"rationale":"Fear and avoidance of crowded places where escape might be difficult is characteristic of agoraphobia, an anxiety disorder. Trauma- and stressor-related disorders require exposure to a traumatic event, which Felipe has not had."},{"id":"U5-267","section":"5.4","skill":"Concept Recall","difficulty":1,"stem":"A clinic runs a treatment program for clients with specific phobias, panic disorder, and social anxiety disorder. All three of these diagnoses belong to which category of disorders?","options":["Obsessive-compulsive and related disorders","Trauma- and stressor-related disorders","Anxiety disorders","Personality disorders"],"answer":2,"rationale":"Specific phobia, panic disorder, and social anxiety disorder are all anxiety disorders, marked by excessive fear or anxiety and related avoidance. Trauma- and stressor-related disorders, such as posttraumatic stress disorder, require exposure to a traumatic event."},{"id":"U5-268","section":"5.4","skill":"Concept Recall","difficulty":1,"stem":"A patient with schizophrenia reports feeling insects crawling under his skin, although a medical exam finds nothing there. Sensory experiences that occur without any real external stimulus, like this one, are called","options":["delusions","hallucinations","obsessions","illusions"],"answer":1,"rationale":"Hallucinations are false perceptions and can occur in any sense, including touch. Delusions are false beliefs rather than false sensory experiences."},{"id":"U5-269","section":"5.4","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Mr. Lindqvist repeatedly experiences unwanted, distressing thoughts that he might accidentally start a fire in his apartment building, even though he has never done anything careless. He recognizes the thoughts as irrational and tries hard to push them away, but they keep returning. This pattern is most consistent with","options":["generalized anxiety disorder","schizophrenia","panic disorder","obsessive-compulsive disorder"],"answer":3,"rationale":"Recurring, intrusive thoughts that the person finds unwanted and irrational are obsessions, a core feature of obsessive-compulsive disorder. Because Mr. Lindqvist recognizes the thoughts as irrational, they are not delusions, and generalized anxiety disorder involves worry about realistic everyday concerns."},{"id":"U5-270","section":"5.4","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":1,"stem":"Tran, 16, spends nearly two hours each evening arranging the items on his desk until they look \"just right,\" and he starts over if anyone bumps the desk. He says that if he stops before it feels right, he is flooded with anxiety that something bad will happen to his parents. Tran's problems are most consistent with which disorder?","options":["Panic disorder","Hoarding disorder","Generalized anxiety disorder","Obsessive-compulsive disorder"],"answer":3,"rationale":"Tran's intrusive fear that harm will come to his parents is an obsession, and his time-consuming arranging ritual is a compulsion performed to relieve it, the pattern of obsessive-compulsive disorder. Hoarding disorder involves difficulty discarding possessions, not ordering rituals."},{"id":"U5-271","section":"5.4","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":3,"stem":"Yasmin and Noura both avoid crowded shopping malls. Yasmin avoids them because she fears she might faint and be unable to get out or get help. Noura avoids them because she fears people will notice her blushing and think she is strange. Which statement best describes their most likely diagnoses?","options":["Yasmin's fear fits agoraphobia, while Noura's fear of being judged fits social anxiety disorder.","Yasmin's fear fits social anxiety disorder, while Noura's fear of being judged fits agoraphobia.","Both women have agoraphobia, because they avoid the same type of place.","Yasmin's fear fits panic disorder, while Noura's fear of being judged fits generalized anxiety disorder."],"answer":0,"rationale":"Agoraphobia is fear of situations where escape or help might be difficult, while social anxiety disorder is fear of being watched or judged by others. The diagnosis depends on the reason for the fear, not on the place that is avoided."},{"id":"U5-272","section":"5.4","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Faisal's older brother contacts a psychologist. He explains that over the past seven months, Faisal, 20, has stopped attending his engineering classes and often talks back to voices that no one else can hear. When his brother asked whether he wanted dinner, Faisal replied, \"Dinner is a blue triangle and the spoon remembers Tuesday.\" Faisal's symptoms are most consistent with which category of disorders?","options":["Schizophrenia spectrum and other psychotic disorders","Bipolar and related disorders","Anxiety disorders","Trauma- and stressor-related disorders"],"answer":0,"rationale":"Auditory hallucinations and nonsensical, disorganized speech (word salad) are positive symptoms of schizophrenia spectrum disorders. Nothing in the case suggests mania, excessive fear, or a traumatic event."},{"id":"U5-273","section":"5.3","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which of the following research findings best reflects the sociocultural perspective on psychological disorders?","options":["Identical twins are more likely than fraternal twins to share a diagnosis of bipolar disorder.","Brain scans show greater amygdala activity in people with social anxiety disorder when they view angry faces.","People with depression in some cultures mainly report headaches and fatigue, while people with depression in other cultures mainly report sadness and guilt.","People with depression report more automatic negative thoughts about themselves than people without depression."],"answer":2,"rationale":"Differences in how a disorder is expressed across cultures reflect social and cultural influences, the focus of the sociocultural perspective. Twin and brain-scan findings reflect the biological perspective, and negative thoughts reflect the cognitive perspective."},{"id":"U5-274","section":"5.4","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Ines feels intense fear only when she is near dogs, and she is calm in other situations. Paolo feels anxious and keyed up about almost everything in his life, with no single trigger. Which statement correctly distinguishes their likely diagnoses?","options":["Only Ines has an anxiety disorder, because generalized anxiety disorder is classified as a depressive disorder.","Ines's fear is nonspecific, which fits generalized anxiety disorder, while Paolo's worry is tied to a particular object, which fits specific phobia.","Only Paolo has an anxiety disorder, because specific phobias are classified as trauma- and stressor-related disorders.","Ines's fear is tied to a particular object, which fits specific phobia, while Paolo's nonspecific worry fits generalized anxiety disorder."],"answer":3,"rationale":"Specific phobia is fear linked to a particular object or situation, while generalized anxiety disorder is prolonged, nonspecific anxiety. Both are classified as anxiety disorders."},{"id":"U5-275","section":"5.4","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":3,"stem":"Lukas, age 34, was recently arrested for stealing people's identities online. A clinician is considering a diagnosis of antisocial personality disorder. Which additional information would most strongly support that diagnosis rather than an explanation based on a single criminal act?","options":["Lukas reports feeling intense guilt and shame after the arrest.","Lukas has shown a pattern of lying, fighting, and disregarding others' rights since early adolescence.","Lukas says a voice told him to commit the crime.","Lukas committed the crime only during a two-week period of severe depression."],"answer":1,"rationale":"Antisocial personality disorder is an enduring, pervasive pattern of deceit and disregard for others, usually beginning with conduct problems in adolescence. Intense guilt would argue against the lack of remorse typical of the disorder."},{"id":"U5-276","section":"5.4","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Mr. Haddad, a 45-year-old executive taking an evening course, tells his instructor that someone of his talent should not be graded by the same rubric as \"ordinary students.\" When the instructor refuses to raise his mark, he complains loudly to the department and says the instructor is too small-minded to recognize genius. If this is part of a lasting pattern, his behavior is most consistent with which disorder?","options":["Narcissistic personality disorder","Dependent personality disorder","Paranoid personality disorder","Schizotypal personality disorder"],"answer":0,"rationale":"A sense of entitlement, a belief that one is special, and anger when not treated as superior are features of narcissistic personality disorder. Paranoid personality disorder involves suspicion that others mean harm, not a belief in one's own superiority."},{"id":"U5-277","section":"5.4","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which of the following people is most likely experiencing symptoms of anorexia nervosa?","options":["Leila, who skips breakfast on busy school mornings but eats a full lunch and dinner","Andre, who repeatedly eats very large amounts of food in a short time and then vomits, while staying at an average weight","Kaito, whose weight has dropped far below a healthy level because he eats very little, yet who still sees himself as overweight and panics at the idea of gaining any weight","Grace, who feels nauseated and loses her appetite on the mornings of her oral exams"],"answer":2,"rationale":"Anorexia nervosa involves severe restriction of food intake, significantly low body weight, and an intense fear of gaining weight. Andre's pattern of binge eating and purging at an average weight fits bulimia nervosa instead."},{"id":"U5-278","section":"5.4","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Several times a month, without any warning or clear trigger, Soraya is overcome by waves of terror. Her chest hurts, she trembles and sweats, and she feels as if she is about to die. Each episode peaks within minutes. Between episodes she worries constantly about the next one. She has never been through a traumatic event. Soraya's experiences are most consistent with","options":["a specific phobia","generalized anxiety disorder","panic disorder","posttraumatic stress disorder"],"answer":2,"rationale":"Panic disorder involves repeated, unexpected panic attacks with intense physical and emotional fear, followed by worry about future attacks. A specific phobia would be triggered by a particular object or situation, and generalized anxiety disorder involves steady, nonspecific worry rather than sudden attacks."},{"id":"U5-279","section":"5.4","skill":"Concept Recall","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which pattern would a clinician most expect to see in a person with chronic schizophrenia?","options":["Disorganized thinking along with negative symptoms such as social withdrawal and flat affect","Alternating weeks of mania and depression","Intense fear of being judged in social situations","Repetitive hand-washing rituals performed to reduce anxiety"],"answer":0,"rationale":"Chronic schizophrenia commonly involves disorganized thinking and lasting negative symptoms. Alternating mania and depression describes bipolar disorder, and fear of being judged describes social anxiety disorder."},{"id":"U5-280","section":"5.4","skill":"Concept Recall","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which statement about personality disorders is most accurate?","options":["They are usually first diagnosed in early childhood, before age 5.","They involve enduring, inflexible patterns that typically begin by early adulthood, and many people with them still hold jobs and manage daily routines.","They are diagnosed mainly through brain scans that show frontal lobe damage.","They are a type of mood disorder marked by cycles of mania and depression."],"answer":1,"rationale":"Personality disorders are stable, pervasive patterns that begin in adolescence or early adulthood and cause distress or impairment, yet many people with them function reasonably well in everyday life. They are diagnosed from long-term patterns of behavior, not brain scans, and are distinct from bipolar disorder."},{"id":"U5-281","section":"5.4","skill":"Concept Recall","difficulty":1,"stem":"A psychiatrist lists several symptoms observed in a patient with schizophrenia. Which one is a positive symptom?","options":["Flat affect","Hearing voices that no one else hears","Little motivation to start or finish activities","Speaking very little"],"answer":1,"rationale":"Positive symptoms are added experiences or behaviors, such as hallucinations and delusions. Flat affect, low motivation, and reduced speech are negative symptoms because they reflect the absence of typical behavior."},{"id":"U5-282","section":"5.4","skill":"Concept Recall","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which disorder is correctly paired with its DSM category?","options":["Panic disorder: depressive disorders","Hoarding disorder: obsessive-compulsive and related disorders","Bipolar II disorder: personality disorders","Dissociative amnesia: anxiety disorders"],"answer":1,"rationale":"Hoarding disorder is grouped with obsessive-compulsive disorder in the obsessive-compulsive and related disorders category. Panic disorder is an anxiety disorder, bipolar II is a bipolar disorder, and dissociative amnesia is a dissociative disorder."},{"id":"U5-283","section":"5.4","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":1,"stem":"Which of the following people would most likely be diagnosed with social anxiety disorder?","options":["Mei, who panics whenever she has to ride in an elevator","Ingrid, who feels uneasy during thunderstorms","Tunde, who fears being laughed at and judged when he eats in front of his colleagues","Karim, who refuses to step onto high balconies"],"answer":2,"rationale":"Social anxiety disorder is an intense fear of being watched or judged by others. Fears of elevators, storms, or heights are specific phobias because they center on a particular object or situation rather than on evaluation by others."},{"id":"U5-284","section":"5.4","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"A psychologist explains that a compulsion can be a mental act as well as a visible behavior. Which example fits the definition of a compulsion?","options":["Silently repeating a set phrase seven times whenever an upsetting thought appears, to make the anxiety go away","Spending hours on a favorite hobby because it is enjoyable and relaxing","Having a sudden, unwanted image of shouting something rude during a quiet exam","Feeling a surge of fear when a large dog runs toward you in the park"],"answer":0,"rationale":"A compulsion is a repetitive behavior or mental act performed to reduce the anxiety caused by an obsession, so silently repeating a phrase to ease anxiety qualifies. The unwanted rude image is an obsession, and an enjoyable hobby is not driven by anxiety."},{"id":"U5-285","section":"5.4","skill":"Concept Recall","difficulty":1,"stem":"Which of the following is a core diagnostic feature of autism spectrum disorder?","options":["Delusions similar to those seen in schizophrenia","Cycling between manic and depressive moods","Persistent difficulties with social communication and interaction","Symptoms that first appear in late adulthood"],"answer":2,"rationale":"Autism spectrum disorder is a neurodevelopmental disorder defined by persistent difficulties with social communication and interaction plus restricted, repetitive behaviors, with onset in early development."},{"id":"U5-286","section":"5.4","skill":"Concept Recall","difficulty":1,"stem":"During a manic episode, a person with bipolar I disorder is most likely to show which of the following?","options":["Decreased need for sleep and inflated self-esteem","Long periods of oversleeping and fatigue","Loss of memory for important personal information","Flashbacks to a traumatic event"],"answer":0,"rationale":"Mania involves elevated mood, inflated self-esteem, rapid speech, and a reduced need for sleep. Oversleeping and fatigue are more typical of a depressive episode."},{"id":"U5-287","section":"5.4","skill":"Concept Recall","difficulty":1,"stem":"Researchers studying the biological basis of schizophrenia have focused most on which neurotransmitter?","options":["GABA","Endorphins","Melatonin","Dopamine"],"answer":3,"rationale":"The dopamine hypothesis links schizophrenia, especially its positive symptoms, to overactive dopamine systems. GABA is more closely tied to anxiety, and melatonin to sleep-wake cycles."},{"id":"U5-288","section":"5.4","skill":"Concept Recall","difficulty":1,"stem":"Which personality disorder is defined by a long-standing pattern of violating the rights of others, deceitfulness, and a lack of remorse?","options":["Histrionic","Antisocial","Avoidant","Schizotypal"],"answer":1,"rationale":"Antisocial personality disorder, a Cluster B disorder, involves disregard for others' rights, lying, impulsivity, and no remorse. Histrionic personality disorder involves excessive attention-seeking and emotional display."},{"id":"U5-289","section":"5.4","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"A classmate claims that schizophrenia means having a \"split personality.\" Which correction is most accurate?","options":["Schizophrenia mainly involves mood swings between mania and depression.","Schizophrenia involves a split from reality, with symptoms such as hallucinations and delusions, while having multiple identities describes dissociative identity disorder.","Schizophrenia mainly involves sudden memory loss after a traumatic event.","The classmate is correct, because schizophrenia and dissociative identity disorder are two names for the same condition."],"answer":1,"rationale":"Schizophrenia is a psychotic disorder involving hallucinations, delusions, and disorganized thinking, not multiple identities. Distinct alternating identities characterize dissociative identity disorder, a separate diagnosis."},{"id":"U5-290","section":"5.4","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"On most days for the past eight months, Mei Ling, 34, a hospital nurse, has worried about many different things: whether she made an error on a patient's chart, whether her parents will become ill, and whether her car will break down. She knows the worries are out of proportion but cannot switch them off. She has tense shoulders, tires easily, and lies awake going over the day. Her mood is otherwise steady, and she still enjoys weekends with her family. Mei Ling's problems are most consistent with which disorder?","options":["Major depressive disorder","Generalized anxiety disorder","Posttraumatic stress disorder","Panic disorder"],"answer":1,"rationale":"Persistent, hard-to-control worry about many everyday matters, with muscle tension, fatigue, and sleep problems, fits generalized anxiety disorder. She has no depressed mood or loss of pleasure, no traumatic event, and no sudden panic attacks."},{"id":"U5-291","section":"5.4","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"During a loud argument between relatives at a family celebration, Luisa, whose family is from Puerto Rico, suddenly begins shouting uncontrollably and trembling. She says she feels heat rising from her chest into her head and that she has lost control, and afterward she remembers little of the episode. Which condition best describes Luisa's experience?","options":["Taijin kyofusho","Ataque de nervios","Social anxiety disorder","Bipolar I disorder"],"answer":1,"rationale":"Ataque de nervios is a culture-bound expression of panic, reported mainly among people of Caribbean or Iberian descent, often triggered by family conflict and involving shouting, trembling, heat rising in the chest, and a sense of losing control. Taijin kyofusho is a Japanese culture-bound fear that others judge one's body as offensive."},{"id":"U5-292","section":"2.2","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"After a local football club announced that its star striker had been diagnosed with depression and was taking a break, several fans posted online, \"It was obvious he was struggling. I could tell months ago.\" Before the announcement, none of these fans had mentioned any concern. The fans' belief that they had seen it coming is best explained by which reasoning error?","options":["Hindsight bias","Confirmation bias","Belief perseverance","Framing effect"],"answer":0,"rationale":"Hindsight bias is the tendency to believe, after learning an outcome, that one knew it all along. Confirmation bias is searching for information that supports an existing belief, but the fans had expressed no belief before the announcement."},{"id":"U5-293","section":"0.2","skill":"Research Methods and Design","difficulty":2,"set":"u5b_panic_trial","stimulus":{"type":"table","caption":"Calmness ratings after eight weeks","headers":["Participant","Group 1 (new program)","Group 2 (standard program)"],"rows":[["1","8","5"],["2","4","3"],["3","9","6"],["4","7","4"],["5","8","5"],["6","5","6"],["7","9","2"],["8","6","5"],["9","8","4"],["10","7","6"]]},"stem":"Researchers randomly assigned 20 adults diagnosed with panic disorder to one of two conditions for eight weeks. Group 1 attended a new group program combining breathing training with exposure exercises. Group 2 attended the clinic's standard program. At the end, each participant rated how calm they felt in everyday situations on a scale from 1 (not at all calm) to 10 (completely calm). Which feature of this study indicates that it is an experiment?","options":["The researchers manipulated which program participants received.","Participants were randomly selected from the general population.","Data were collected using self-report ratings.","All participants shared a diagnosis of panic disorder."],"answer":0,"rationale":"An experiment requires the researcher to manipulate an independent variable, here the type of program, while random assignment balances the groups. The participants were not described as randomly selected from the population, and self-report can be used in any design."},{"id":"U5-294","section":"0.3","skill":"Research Methods and Design","difficulty":1,"set":"u5b_panic_trial","stimulus":{"type":"table","caption":"Calmness ratings after eight weeks","headers":["Participant","Group 1 (new program)","Group 2 (standard program)"],"rows":[["1","8","5"],["2","4","3"],["3","9","6"],["4","7","4"],["5","8","5"],["6","5","6"],["7","9","2"],["8","6","5"],["9","8","4"],["10","7","6"]]},"stem":"Researchers randomly assigned 20 adults diagnosed with panic disorder to one of two conditions for eight weeks. Group 1 attended a new group program combining breathing training with exposure exercises. Group 2 attended the clinic's standard program. At the end, each participant rated how calm they felt in everyday situations on a scale from 1 (not at all calm) to 10 (completely calm). What is the independent variable in this study?","options":["Whether participants attended the new program or the standard program","The eight-week length of the study","The calmness ratings at the end of eight weeks","The 1-to-10 rating scale"],"answer":0,"rationale":"The independent variable is the factor the researchers manipulated: which program each participant attended. The calmness ratings are the dependent variable, and the eight-week length was kept the same for everyone."},{"id":"U5-295","section":"0.5","skill":"Data Interpretation","difficulty":2,"set":"u5b_panic_trial","stimulus":{"type":"table","caption":"Calmness ratings after eight weeks","headers":["Participant","Group 1 (new program)","Group 2 (standard program)"],"rows":[["1","8","5"],["2","4","3"],["3","9","6"],["4","7","4"],["5","8","5"],["6","5","6"],["7","9","2"],["8","6","5"],["9","8","4"],["10","7","6"]]},"stem":"Researchers randomly assigned 20 adults diagnosed with panic disorder to one of two conditions for eight weeks. Group 1 attended a new group program combining breathing training with exposure exercises. Group 2 attended the clinic's standard program. At the end, each participant rated how calm they felt in everyday situations on a scale from 1 (not at all calm) to 10 (completely calm). What is the median calmness rating for Group 1?","options":["7.0","7.1","7.5","8.0"],"answer":2,"rationale":"Ordered, Group 1's ratings are 4, 5, 6, 7, 7, 8, 8, 8, 9, 9. With ten scores, the median is the mean of the fifth and sixth scores: (7 + 8) / 2 = 7.5. The mean is 7.1 and the mode is 8."},{"id":"U5-296","section":"0.2","skill":"Research Methods and Design","difficulty":1,"stem":"Dr. Mensah is deciding between a correlational survey and an experiment to study treatments for posttraumatic stress disorder. Which advantage would an experiment give her that a correlational study would not?","options":["The ability to draw cause-and-effect conclusions","The ability to study variables, such as past trauma, that cannot ethically be manipulated","The ability to avoid using operational definitions","The ability to skip review by an ethics board"],"answer":0,"rationale":"Because experiments manipulate an independent variable and use random assignment, they allow cause-and-effect conclusions. Studying variables that cannot ethically be manipulated is actually a strength of correlational research, and all research needs operational definitions and ethical review."},{"id":"U5-297","section":"0.3","skill":"Research Methods and Design","difficulty":2,"stem":"Dr. Kapoor is testing a new antidepressant. She worries that her own expectations about which participants are receiving the real drug might influence how she rates their mood. Which research procedure would best address this concern?","options":["A double-blind procedure","Random sampling from the population","Naturalistic observation","A single-blind procedure in which only participants are unaware of their condition"],"answer":0,"rationale":"In a double-blind procedure, neither participants nor the researchers interacting with them know who receives the drug, which reduces experimenter bias. A single-blind procedure controls participant expectations but not the researcher's."},{"id":"U5-298","section":"0.3","skill":"Research Methods and Design","difficulty":3,"stem":"Researchers are comparing an online cognitive-behavioral program with a support-group program for adults with social anxiety disorder. They worry that if participants choose their own program, the more motivated people might pick the online option, and that other differences they have not thought of could also affect the results. Which step would best prevent such participant differences from explaining the results?","options":["Matching the two groups on age only","Using a double-blind procedure","Randomly assigning participants to the two programs","Doubling the sample size while still letting participants choose their program"],"answer":2,"rationale":"Random assignment gives every participant an equal chance of being in either group, so motivation and unknown participant variables tend to be spread evenly. Matching on age controls only that one known variable, and a double-blind procedure controls expectations rather than who ends up in each group."},{"id":"U5-299","section":"0.3","skill":"Research Methods and Design","difficulty":2,"stem":"A researcher wants to estimate how many adults in her country have severe symptoms of social anxiety disorder. She posts an online survey only on a website that sells self-help books about shyness, and 40% of respondents report severe symptoms. Her estimate for the whole country is most likely limited because of","options":["the placebo effect","experimenter bias","sampling bias","the lack of an operational definition"],"answer":2,"rationale":"Sampling bias occurs when a sample does not represent the population of interest; visitors to a shyness self-help site are likely to have more social anxiety than adults in general. The placebo effect concerns expectations about a treatment, and no treatment was given."},{"id":"U5-300","section":"0.2","skill":"Research Methods and Design","difficulty":3,"stem":"Researchers measured hippocampus volume in 150 adults and gave each a questionnaire on depressive symptoms. They found a moderate negative correlation (r = -0.42): adults with smaller hippocampus volume tended to have higher depression scores. A news headline then announced, \"Small hippocampus causes depression.\" Why is the headline not justified by this study?","options":["Depression could reduce hippocampus volume, or a third factor such as long-term stress could affect both, so the direction of cause is unknown.","A negative correlation means there is no relationship between the two variables.","A correlation of -0.42 is too weak to show any relationship at all.","Because the correlation is negative, the headline should state that a large hippocampus causes depression."],"answer":0,"rationale":"A correlation shows that two variables are related but cannot show which one causes the other or rule out a third variable, such as chronic stress, that may shrink the hippocampus and raise depression risk. A negative correlation of -0.42 is a real, moderate relationship in which one variable decreases as the other increases."},{"id":"U5-301","section":"0.4","skill":"Research Methods and Design","difficulty":1,"stem":"Dr. Lim posts stories about the participants in her study of therapy for obsessive-compulsive disorder on a public online forum, including details that could identify them. Which ethical principle is Dr. Lim most likely violating?","options":["Informed consent","The right to withdraw","Approval by an institutional review board","Confidentiality"],"answer":3,"rationale":"Confidentiality requires researchers to protect participants' personal information and identities. Sharing identifiable details in public violates that principle."},{"id":"U5-302","section":"0.4","skill":"Research Methods and Design","difficulty":1,"stem":"A researcher plans a study comparing genetic and environmental influences on attention-deficit/hyperactivity disorder. Before recruiting any participants, which step should the researcher take?","options":["Obtain approval from an institutional review board","Collect a signed informed consent form from each participant","Debrief the participants","Present the findings at a conference"],"answer":0,"rationale":"An institutional review board must review and approve a study before anyone is recruited. Informed consent is collected from each person after recruitment begins, and debriefing and presenting findings come at the end of a study."},{"id":"U5-303","section":"0.5","skill":"Data Interpretation","difficulty":2,"stem":"A health psychologist records the resting heart rates (in beats per minute) of 50 employees before a stress-management workshop. She later learns that the monitor read every value exactly 4 beats per minute too low, so she adds 4 to every recorded value. Compared with the original data, the corrected data set will have","options":["a higher median and the same standard deviation","a higher median and a larger standard deviation","the same median and a larger standard deviation","the same median and the same standard deviation"],"answer":0,"rationale":"Adding the same constant to every score shifts measures of central tendency, such as the median and mean, up by that amount. The distance between scores does not change, so the standard deviation and range stay the same."},{"id":"U5-304","section":"0.5","skill":"Data Interpretation","difficulty":2,"stem":"Dr. Nkemelu finds a correlation of -0.62 between hours of weekly exercise and scores on a depression questionnaire in a sample of 300 adults. Which conclusion is supported by this finding?","options":["Exercising more causes people to become less depressed.","As hours of exercise increase, depression scores tend to decrease.","As hours of exercise increase, depression scores tend to increase.","The relationship is weak because the correlation is negative."],"answer":1,"rationale":"A negative correlation means that as one variable increases, the other tends to decrease, and -0.62 is a moderately strong relationship because strength depends on the size of the number, not its sign. A correlation alone cannot show that exercise causes lower depression."},{"id":"U5-305","section":"0.6","skill":"Data Interpretation","difficulty":2,"stem":"A school counselor compares students with test anxiety who completed biofeedback training with similar students who did not. The biofeedback group had lower average heart rates during exams, and the difference was statistically significant (p < .05). Which statement about this result is true?","options":["The difference is likely due to chance.","The difference is unlikely to be due to chance alone.","The result proves biofeedback will work for every student with test anxiety.","The result shows a positive correlation between biofeedback and heart rate."],"answer":1,"rationale":"Statistical significance means the observed difference is unlikely to have occurred by chance alone. It does not guarantee that the treatment works for every individual."},{"id":"U5-306","section":"0.5","skill":"Data Interpretation","difficulty":2,"stem":"A researcher creates four scatterplots relating adults' average nightly hours of sleep to the number of headaches they report each month. Which description shows the strongest relationship between the two variables?","options":["Points fall almost exactly along a straight line that slopes downward.","Points form a loose, wide cloud that slopes slightly upward.","Points are scattered with no visible pattern.","Points form a moderately tight band that slopes upward."],"answer":0,"rationale":"The strength of a correlation depends on how closely points cluster around a line, not on whether the line slopes up or down. Points falling almost exactly on a line show the strongest relationship, here a strong negative one."},{"id":"U5-307","section":"5.5","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Javier begins person-centered therapy to cope with feelings of emptiness. Which approach is his therapist most likely to take?","options":["Reflecting back Javier's feelings with genuine empathy and unconditional positive regard while letting Javier direct each session","Interpreting Javier's dreams to uncover unconscious childhood conflicts","Directly disputing Javier's irrational beliefs and assigning homework to change them","Setting up a schedule of rewards to reinforce healthier behaviors"],"answer":0,"rationale":"Person-centered therapy, a humanistic approach, relies on active listening, empathy, and unconditional positive regard so that the client's natural tendency toward growth can flourish. Disputing irrational beliefs describes rational-emotive behavior therapy."},{"id":"U5-308","section":"5.5","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Anan feels miserable because a close friend did not reply to his message for a whole day. A therapist using rational-emotive behavior therapy would most likely identify which of the following as the main source of Anan's distress?","options":["Anan's belief that the people he cares about must always respond to him immediately","The friend's failure to reply","An unconscious conflict from Anan's early childhood","A lack of positive reinforcement in Anan's environment"],"answer":0,"rationale":"In rational-emotive behavior therapy, emotional distress comes mainly from irrational beliefs about an event rather than the event itself. The therapist would challenge Anan's demanding belief."},{"id":"U5-309","section":"0.3","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"In a treatment study, adults with mild depression received a sugar pill that they were told was a new antidepressant. After four weeks, they reported clearly better mood than a similar group who received no pill. Their improvement is best explained by","options":["spontaneous remission","the placebo effect","cognitive restructuring","the therapeutic alliance"],"answer":1,"rationale":"The placebo effect is improvement caused by the expectation of benefit rather than by an active treatment. Spontaneous remission would be expected in both groups, so it cannot explain why only the pill group improved, and no therapy took place to build a therapeutic alliance."},{"id":"U5-310","section":"5.3","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"A psychologist believes that Mariana's anxiety comes from a gap between the person she believes she is and her actual experiences, a gap that grew because her parents valued her only when she earned top grades. The psychologist most likely follows which orientation?","options":["Psychodynamic","Behavioral","Biomedical","Humanistic (person-centered)"],"answer":3,"rationale":"Carl Rogers's humanistic theory explains distress as incongruence between self-concept and experience, often rooted in conditional positive regard. A psychodynamic view would focus on unconscious conflicts instead."},{"id":"U5-311","section":"0.3","skill":"Research Methods and Design","difficulty":2,"stem":"A double-blind procedure would be most important in which of the following studies?","options":["A survey of how many hours of sleep teenagers get on school nights","A naturalistic observation of children's play on a school playground","A case study of one patient with dissociative amnesia","An experiment testing whether a new antianxiety medication reduces panic attacks compared with a placebo"],"answer":3,"rationale":"A double-blind procedure controls both participant and researcher expectations, which matters most when testing whether a treatment works compared with a placebo. Surveys, observations, and case studies do not assign people to treatment conditions."},{"id":"U5-312","section":"5.5","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":1,"stem":"Samir joins a therapy group for people with social anxiety disorder. After the first session he says, \"Hearing others describe the same fears made me feel less alone.\" Which advantage of group therapy does Samir's comment illustrate?","options":["Clients can keep their concerns completely private from other people.","Clients avoid having to talk about their difficulties aloud.","Clients receive more one-on-one attention from the therapist.","Clients discover that other people share struggles similar to their own."],"answer":3,"rationale":"A key benefit of group therapy is learning that others share similar struggles, along with support and feedback from peers. Group therapy actually offers less privacy and less one-on-one therapist time than individual therapy."},{"id":"U5-313","section":"5.5","skill":"Concept Recall","difficulty":1,"stem":"A psychiatrist prescribes a selective serotonin reuptake inhibitor (SSRI) for a patient with major depressive disorder. How does this medication most likely ease the patient's symptoms?","options":["It speeds up the reuptake of serotonin, leaving less serotonin in the synapse.","It enhances the inhibitory effect of GABA.","It blocks dopamine receptors, reducing dopamine activity.","It blocks the reuptake of serotonin, leaving more serotonin available in the synapse."],"answer":3,"rationale":"SSRIs block the reabsorption of serotonin by the sending neuron, so more serotonin remains available in the synapse. Blocking dopamine receptors describes antipsychotic drugs, and enhancing GABA describes antianxiety drugs."},{"id":"U5-314","section":"5.5","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"A therapist teaches a client progressive muscle relaxation and then asks the client to imagine a ranked list of feared situations, from least to most frightening, while staying relaxed at each step. This treatment is best suited for which disorder?","options":["Bipolar I disorder","Antisocial personality disorder","A specific phobia of flying","Dissociative identity disorder"],"answer":2,"rationale":"Systematic desensitization pairs relaxation with a gradual fear hierarchy and is an exposure therapy designed for specific phobias. Bipolar I disorder is treated mainly with mood stabilizers such as lithium, and the technique does not target the personality patterns or identity disruptions of the other disorders."},{"id":"U5-315","section":"0.4","skill":"Research Methods and Design","difficulty":1,"stem":"In an experiment testing whether a new sleep medication reduces insomnia in people with depression, which step is most important for ensuring that the study follows appropriate ethical procedures?","options":["Using a double-blind procedure","Publishing the results in a peer-reviewed journal","Recruiting a very large sample","Obtaining informed consent from participants before they take part"],"answer":3,"rationale":"Informed consent means participants learn what the study involves, including possible risks, and agree before participating. A double-blind procedure, a large sample, and peer review improve scientific quality but are not the core ethical safeguard for participants."},{"id":"U5-316","section":"0.4","skill":"Concept Recall","difficulty":2,"stem":"Dr. Achebe treats teenagers with panic disorder. Which of the following actions best shows that he is following the ethical principle of nonmaleficence?","options":["Keeping the promises and commitments he makes to clients","Being honest and accurate when describing his qualifications","Avoiding techniques that are likely to cause his clients physical or psychological harm","Billing clients only for the sessions they actually attend"],"answer":2,"rationale":"Nonmaleficence means doing no harm, so a therapist avoids methods likely to injure or distress clients. Keeping commitments reflects fidelity, and honesty about qualifications reflects integrity."},{"id":"U5-317","section":"5.5","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Lerato, who has depression, writes \"I am boring\" in her diary after almost every conversation. Her psychologist plans weekly sessions in which Lerato first examines the evidence for and against that thought and then role-plays starting conversations, with homework to try one new conversation each day. This treatment plan is best described as","options":["psychodynamic therapy","person-centered therapy","cognitive-behavioral therapy","biomedical therapy"],"answer":2,"rationale":"Cognitive-behavioral therapy combines challenging maladaptive thoughts with practicing new behaviors, such as conversation skills, and often includes homework. Psychodynamic therapy focuses on unconscious conflicts, and person-centered therapy avoids directing the client with assignments."},{"id":"U5-501","section":"5.2","skill":"Concept Recall","difficulty":1,"stem":"Positive psychologists have developed a classification of character strengths organized into six broad categories of virtue. Which list correctly names these six virtues?","options":["Wisdom, courage, humanity, justice, temperance and transcendence","Openness, conscientiousness, extraversion, agreeableness, emotional stability and honesty","Kindness, bravery, honesty, gratitude, humor and self-control","Wisdom, ambition, loyalty, independence, patience and confidence"],"answer":0,"rationale":"The classification of character strengths groups specific strengths under six virtues: wisdom, courage, humanity, justice, temperance and transcendence. Kindness, bravery, gratitude and similar qualities are individual strengths within those virtues, not the six categories themselves, and the remaining lists mix in personality traits or other qualities."},{"id":"U5-502","section":"5.2","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Bao is captain of his school's futsal team. He makes sure every player gets fair playing time regardless of who his close friends are, organizes the team so that everyone has a clear role, and encourages players to work together rather than compete for attention. Bao's strengths fall mainly under which virtue?","options":["Temperance","Humanity","Justice","Wisdom"],"answer":2,"rationale":"Fairness, leadership and teamwork are strengths grouped under justice, the virtue concerned with healthy group and community life. Humanity covers close, one-to-one strengths such as kindness and love, which are not the focus here."},{"id":"U5-503","section":"5.2","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Priya's friends describe her as patient and careful. She thinks hard before spending her part-time job wages on anything expensive, forgives friends quickly when they apologize, and rarely mentions her own high marks. On a character strengths survey, her top strengths would most likely fall under which virtue?","options":["Courage","Temperance","Transcendence","Justice"],"answer":1,"rationale":"Prudence, forgiveness, humility and self-regulation are strengths grouped under temperance, the virtue that protects against excess. Courage involves strengths such as bravery and perseverance in the face of difficulty."},{"id":"U5-504","section":"5.2","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":3,"stem":"Lucia often stops to admire a desert sunset or a skillfully played piece of music. She stays hopeful about the future even after setbacks, and she uses gentle humor to lift her friends' spirits when a group project goes badly. Which virtue best groups the strengths Lucia shows?","options":["Humanity","Wisdom","Temperance","Transcendence"],"answer":3,"rationale":"Appreciation of beauty and excellence, hope and humor are strengths grouped under transcendence, the virtue linked to finding meaning beyond everyday concerns. Humor that cheers friends may look like kindness, but kindness, love and social intelligence are the strengths grouped under humanity."},{"id":"U5-505","section":"5.2","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Jun spends his free time reading about how bridges are designed, enjoys inventing new ways to solve problems, and carefully weighs evidence on both sides before forming an opinion. Friends often ask him for advice because he can see the bigger picture. Jun's signature strengths fall mostly under which virtue?","options":["Wisdom","Justice","Courage","Humanity"],"answer":0,"rationale":"Curiosity, love of learning, creativity, open-mindedness and perspective are strengths grouped under wisdom, the virtue concerned with gaining and using knowledge."},{"id":"U5-506","section":"5.2","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"A career counselor helps Hamdan identify his signature strengths, which are curiosity and creativity. Hamdan then changes how he works at his part-time job in a bookshop, designing new themed displays and researching titles that customers ask about. Based on positive psychology research, which outcome is most likely?","options":["Hamdan will report lower well-being because he is putting more effort into work","Hamdan will report higher happiness and subjective well-being","Hamdan's well-being will not change, because character strengths cannot be used intentionally","Hamdan will lose his curiosity and creativity because he is using them too often"],"answer":1,"rationale":"People who regularly exercise their signature strengths report higher levels of happiness and subjective well-being. Deliberately using strengths, as Hamdan does, is the kind of change positive psychology predicts will help."},{"id":"U5-507","section":"5.2","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":1,"stem":"Ms. Dela Cruz asks her students to complete a character strengths survey. She then asks each student to circle the three to five strengths that feel most like \"the real me,\" that they use naturally, and that leave them feeling energized. The strengths the students circle are best described as their","options":["resilience factors","posttraumatic growth","signature strengths","subjective well-being"],"answer":2,"rationale":"Signature strengths are the character strengths a person feels are most central to who they are and uses most naturally. Exercising them is linked to greater happiness and subjective well-being."},{"id":"U5-508","section":"5.2","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"For a class project, Kofi writes a detailed letter to his former football coach describing how the coach's encouragement helped him, then reads the letter to the coach in person. Kofi's teacher asks the class to predict how this activity will most likely affect Kofi. Based on positive psychology research, the best prediction is that Kofi will","options":["feel worse afterward, because thanking others reminds people of what they owe","experience posttraumatic growth, because the letter required recalling his past","show no change, because only the person receiving thanks benefits","show an increase in his subjective well-being"],"answer":3,"rationale":"Expressing gratitude is a positive subjective experience that increases the subjective well-being of the person doing the thanking. Posttraumatic growth refers to positive change after trauma or major stress, which is not described here."},{"id":"U5-509","section":"5.2","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":1,"stem":"A national survey asks adults to rate how satisfied they are with their lives overall and how often they have felt positive and negative emotions during the past month. Which concept is this survey most directly designed to measure?","options":["Subjective well-being","Posttraumatic growth","Signature strengths","Resilience"],"answer":0,"rationale":"Subjective well-being is a person's own evaluation of their life, usually measured by life satisfaction and the balance of positive and negative emotions. The survey does not ask about growth after trauma, recovery from adversity or specific character strengths."},{"id":"U5-510","section":"5.2","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":3,"stem":"When the restaurant her family owned closed, Ama felt stressed for several weeks. She talked with friends, made a budget and found a new job. Within two months she was sleeping well, enjoying her classes and feeling much as she had before the closure. She says she does not feel changed by the experience. Ama's response best illustrates","options":["posttraumatic growth","resilience","gratitude","learned helplessness"],"answer":1,"rationale":"Resilience is the ability to adapt to and recover from stress, returning to healthy functioning, which is what Ama did. Posttraumatic growth would involve positive change beyond her earlier level, such as new priorities or a greater appreciation of life, which Ama says she did not experience."},{"id":"U5-511","section":"5.2","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Two years after a serious car accident and a long recovery, Tariq says the experience was painful but changed him. He now values time with his family far more, has decided to study physiotherapy, and feels personally stronger than he did before the accident. Tariq's report best illustrates","options":["posttraumatic stress disorder","learned helplessness","resilience","posttraumatic growth"],"answer":3,"rationale":"Posttraumatic growth is positive psychological change, such as new priorities, closer relationships and a greater sense of personal strength, that may follow trauma or major stress. Resilience would mean returning to his earlier level of functioning, while Tariq describes growing beyond it."},{"id":"U5-512","section":"5.2","skill":"Concept Recall","difficulty":2,"stem":"A student is writing revision notes on positive psychology. Which statement about posttraumatic growth should she include because it is accurate?","options":["It happens automatically to nearly everyone who experiences a traumatic event","It is a disorder involving flashbacks, nightmares and avoidance after a traumatic event","It is positive psychological change that some people report after struggling with trauma or major stress","It means the traumatic event had no negative effects on the person"],"answer":2,"rationale":"Posttraumatic growth may result after trauma or major stress, but it does not happen to everyone and does not mean the event was harmless. Flashbacks, nightmares and avoidance describe posttraumatic stress disorder, a different concept."},{"id":"U5-513","section":"5.2","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":1,"stem":"Four psychologists at a university are each planning a new study. Which research question would a positive psychologist be most likely to investigate?","options":["Which brain abnormalities are most often found in people with schizophrenia?","Which daily habits are associated with higher life satisfaction and more frequent positive emotions among university students?","Which symptoms best distinguish bipolar disorder from major depressive disorder?","How does long-term exposure to loud noise affect blood pressure in factory workers?"],"answer":1,"rationale":"Positive psychology seeks to identify factors that lead to well-being, resilience, positive emotions and psychological health. The other questions focus on disorders or on stress and physical illness, which are the concerns of clinical and health psychology."},{"id":"U5-514","section":"5.2","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":2,"stem":"Nadia quickly notices when a classmate seems left out at lunch and invites them to join her table. She remembers her friends' worries and checks on them later, and she spends Saturday mornings helping her elderly neighbor carry groceries. Nadia's strengths are best classified under which virtue?","options":["Humanity","Justice","Temperance","Transcendence"],"answer":0,"rationale":"Kindness, love and social intelligence (reading others' feelings and needs) are strengths grouped under humanity, the virtue of caring for others in close relationships. Justice strengths, such as fairness and leadership, focus more on how groups and communities are run."},{"id":"U5-515","section":"5.2","skill":"Data Interpretation","difficulty":2,"set":"add_d_gratitude_aziz","stimulus":{"type":"bar","title":"Mean Life Satisfaction After Six Weeks","xLabel":"Condition","yLabel":"Mean life satisfaction score (1 to 10)","categories":["Weekly thank-you messages","Weekly log of daily tasks","No writing"],"series":[{"name":"Life satisfaction","values":[7.4,6.1,6.0]}],"yMax":10},"stem":"Students were randomly assigned to send one thank-you message each week to someone who had helped them, to write a weekly log of their daily tasks, or to do no writing. After six weeks, all students rated their life satisfaction. Which conclusion is best supported by the results in the graph?","options":["Any kind of weekly writing raised life satisfaction by about the same amount","Logging daily tasks produced the highest life satisfaction of the three conditions","Life satisfaction was nearly identical in all three conditions","Sending thank-you messages led to higher life satisfaction than either comparison condition, while logging daily tasks made little difference compared with no writing"],"answer":3,"rationale":"The thank-you group (7.4) scored well above the task-log group (6.1) and the no-writing group (6.0), which barely differed from each other. Because students were randomly assigned, the results support the claim that expressing gratitude increases subjective well-being."},{"id":"U5-516","section":"5.2","skill":"Research Methods and Design","difficulty":3,"set":"add_d_gratitude_aziz","stimulus":{"type":"passage","text":"Dr. Aziz randomly assigns 90 university students to one of three groups for six weeks. Group 1 sends one thank-you message each week to someone who helped them. Group 2 writes a weekly log describing their daily tasks, such as classes and chores. Group 3 does no writing. At the end of the six weeks, all students complete a life satisfaction questionnaire."},"stem":"In the study described, what is the main reason Dr. Aziz included Group 2 in addition to Group 3?","options":["To increase the sample size so that the results can be generalized to all adults","To serve as the experimental group that receives the gratitude treatment","To control for the effect of simply spending time writing each week, so that any difference can be linked to the gratitude content","To measure whether life satisfaction affects how much students choose to write"],"answer":2,"rationale":"Group 2 does a similar amount of writing as Group 1 but without expressing thanks, which rules out the possibility that the act of writing itself, rather than gratitude, raises well-being. Group 3 shows what happens with no task at all, and Group 1 is the gratitude group."},{"id":"U5-517","section":"5.2","skill":"Research Methods and Design","difficulty":3,"stimulus":{"type":"passage","text":"Dr. Oyelaran surveys 400 employees at several companies. Each employee rates how often they use their signature strengths at work and how happy they are overall. She finds a correlation of +0.42 between the two measures."},"stem":"Which of the following is the most plausible alternative explanation for Dr. Oyelaran's result?","options":["Employees with supportive managers may both have more chances to use their strengths and feel happier for other reasons","The positive correlation shows that employees who use their strengths more tend to be less happy","A correlation of +0.42 is too weak to show any relationship between the two variables","Because the survey used self-report, using strengths must have caused the higher happiness"],"answer":0,"rationale":"A correlation cannot establish causation, so a third variable, such as a supportive manager, could increase both strength use and happiness. A correlation of +0.42 is a moderate positive relationship, meaning more strength use goes with more happiness."},{"id":"U5-518","section":"5.2","skill":"Data Interpretation","difficulty":2,"stimulus":{"type":"table","caption":"Mean scores of adults recovering from serious injuries at three points after the injury","headers":["Time since injury","Mean growth score (0 to 100)","Mean distress score (0 to 40)"],"rows":[["3 months","22","28"],["12 months","38","19"],["24 months","47","15"]]},"stem":"Dr. Kowalski follows the same group of adults who are recovering from serious injuries and measures posttraumatic growth and psychological distress at three points in time. Which statement is best supported by the data in the table?","options":["Rising growth scores caused the drop in distress scores","Mean growth scores rose over time while distress fell, although some distress remained at 24 months","Growth scores were highest at 3 months and declined over time","Distress had completely disappeared by 24 months"],"answer":1,"rationale":"Growth rose from 22 to 47 while distress fell from 28 to 15, but a mean distress score of 15 shows that distress had not disappeared. Because nothing was manipulated, the data cannot show that growth caused distress to fall."},{"id":"U5-519","section":"5.2","skill":"Research Methods and Design","difficulty":2,"stem":"Dr. Al Shamsi wants to find out whether using one's signature strengths in a new way each day for a week causes an increase in happiness. Which research design would best allow her to draw a cause-and-effect conclusion?","options":["Survey a large sample about how often they use their strengths and correlate the answers with their happiness ratings","Ask only volunteers who already describe themselves as very happy to complete the strengths task","Conduct a detailed case study of one person who uses her strengths every day","Randomly assign volunteers to either the daily strengths task or a comparison task, then compare their happiness scores afterward"],"answer":3,"rationale":"Only an experiment with random assignment to conditions can show that the strengths task causes a change in happiness, because random assignment balances other differences between the groups. A correlational survey shows only a relationship, and a self-selected happy sample or a single case offers no fair comparison."},{"id":"U5-520","section":"5.2","skill":"Concept Application","difficulty":1,"stem":"After a knee injury, Omar keeps attending painful physical therapy sessions for months instead of giving up. He also volunteers to give the closing speech at his school's sports awards, even though public speaking frightens him. The strengths Omar shows are grouped under which virtue?","options":["Temperance","Wisdom","Courage","Justice"],"answer":2,"rationale":"Bravery and perseverance, meaning acting and persisting despite fear, pain or difficulty, are strengths grouped under courage. Temperance involves restraint, such as self-control and prudence, rather than facing fear."}];
